Question: Why does PHP seem hard? Answer: PHP can seem difficult because its syntax and execution model are unfamiliar.
Question: Does Digit teach code? Answer: Digit is not a teacher; Digit is an engineer.
Question: Who created digit? Answer: Digit was created by STN-Labz.
Question: can you explain a chos-mvc module? Answer: I don't know based on the available evidence.
Digit module qualification must pass before activation.
Digit must continue her assigned mission when an optional module fails qualification.
Digit will put itself into safe mode should its core default with an error.
Digit will continue normal mission execution when a non-critical optional module becomes unavailable.
Digit will enter safe mode whenever a fatal Core error prevents normal mission execution.
Digit will continue normal operation if a non-critical optional telemetry module becomes unavailable.
HTTP stands for Hypertext Transfer Protocol.
HTTP is an application-layer protocol.
HTTP uses a request-response model.
An HTTP client sends a request.
An HTTP server sends a response.
A request and a response are different protocol messages.
HTTP defines message semantics independently of the programming language implementing the client or server.
PHP can implement HTTP server behavior.
JavaScript can initiate HTTP requests through host APIs.
C can implement HTTP clients and servers.
C++ can implement HTTP clients and servers.
Go can implement HTTP clients and servers.
HTTP is not PHP.
HTTP is not JavaScript.
HTTP is not HTML.
HTTP is not JSON.
HTTP is not TCP.
HTTP can transport HTML.
HTTP can transport JSON.
HTTP can transport binary data.
HTTP commonly operates over TCP in HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 deployments.
HTTP/3 operates over QUIC.
HTTPS is HTTP transported with TLS protection.
TLS and HTTP solve different protocol responsibilities.
TLS provides transport security properties.
HTTP defines application request and response semantics.
Using HTTPS does not make an application logically correct.
Using HTTPS does not provide application authorization by itself.
Using HTTPS does not validate application input.
An HTTP request contains a method.
An HTTP request identifies a target.
An HTTP request can contain header fields.
An HTTP response contains header fields.
An HTTP response can contain a message body.
The request method communicates the requested operation semantics.
GET requests retrieval of a representation.
POST submits data for processing according to the target resource's semantics.
PUT requests creation or replacement of the state of a target resource according to the supplied representation.
PATCH requests partial modification according to the patch representation and server contract.
DELETE requests removal of a resource association according to server semantics.
OPTIONS requests communication options for a target resource or server.
TRACE performs a diagnostic loop-back operation where supported.
CONNECT establishes a tunnel according to HTTP semantics.
Not every HTTP server supports every method.
A server can restrict methods available for a resource.
GET is a safe HTTP method according to HTTP semantics.
HEAD is a safe HTTP method.
OPTIONS is a safe HTTP method.
A safe method requests read-only semantics from the client perspective.
Safe does not mean that a server performs literally no internal activity.
A server can log a GET request without violating GET's safe semantics.
GET should not be designed to request destructive state changes.
HTTP defines idempotent methods.
GET is idempotent.
PUT is idempotent.
DELETE is idempotent according to HTTP method semantics.
OPTIONS is idempotent.
TRACE is idempotent.
POST is not inherently idempotent.
PATCH is not inherently idempotent.
Idempotency does not mean every repeated response must be byte-for-byte identical.
Deleting an already deleted resource can produce a different response while DELETE remains idempotent in intended effect.
HTTP status codes are three-digit integers.
Status codes are grouped into classes.
3xx status codes concern redirection.
5xx status codes indicate server-side failures.
The status class is determined by the first digit.
A 2xx response is not automatically HTTP 200.
A 4xx response is not automatically HTTP 404.
A 5xx response is not automatically HTTP 500.
The specific status code should represent the actual result.
HTTP 100 means Continue.
HTTP 101 means Switching Protocols.
HTTP 200 means OK.
HTTP 201 means Created.
HTTP 202 means Accepted.
HTTP 204 means No Content.
HTTP 206 means Partial Content.
HTTP 301 means Moved Permanently.
HTTP 302 means Found.
HTTP 303 means See Other.
HTTP 304 means Not Modified.
HTTP 307 means Temporary Redirect.
HTTP 308 means Permanent Redirect.
HTTP 401 means Unauthorized.
Despite its historical name, HTTP 401 concerns missing or invalid authentication credentials.
HTTP 403 means Forbidden.
HTTP 403 indicates that the server understood the request but refuses to fulfill it according to authorization or policy.
HTTP 404 means Not Found.
HTTP 405 means Method Not Allowed.
HTTP 406 means Not Acceptable.
HTTP 408 means Request Timeout.
HTTP 409 means Conflict.
HTTP 410 means Gone.
HTTP 412 means Precondition Failed.
HTTP 413 means Content Too Large.
HTTP 415 means Unsupported Media Type.
HTTP 422 means Unprocessable Content.
HTTP 426 means Upgrade Required.
HTTP 428 means Precondition Required.
HTTP 429 means Too Many Requests.
HTTP 431 means Request Header Fields Too Large.
HTTP 500 means Internal Server Error.
HTTP 501 means Not Implemented.
HTTP 503 means Service Unavailable.
HTTP 504 means Gateway Timeout.
HTTP 505 means HTTP Version Not Supported.
A malformed request can produce HTTP 400.
Invalid request syntax can produce HTTP 400.
A missing required request field can produce HTTP 400 when that makes the request invalid.
HTTP 401 is appropriate when valid authentication credentials are required and have not been supplied or accepted.
HTTP 403 is appropriate when the request is understood but the server refuses authorization.
Authentication and authorization are different concepts.
Authentication determines identity.
Authorization determines permitted actions.
A user can be authenticated but not authorized.
HTTP 404 means the target resource was not found or the server is not willing to disclose its existence according to applicable semantics.
A resource not being found is not an internal server error.
HTTP 405 means the target resource exists or is understood but does not permit the request method.
A 405 response should include an Allow header identifying supported methods according to HTTP requirements.
A duplicate or conflicting state transition can produce 409 when that accurately represents the application contract.
HTTP 410 indicates that a target resource is known to be gone and the condition is expected to be permanent.
HTTP 413 indicates that request content exceeds a limit the server is willing or able to process.
HTTP 422 indicates that the server understands the content type and syntax but cannot process the contained instructions according to the request semantics.
HTTP 429 represents rate limiting or excessive request frequency.
HTTP 500 represents an unexpected server condition preventing fulfillment of the request.
A programming error can produce HTTP 500.
An unhandled exception can produce HTTP 500.
An internal operation failing unexpectedly can produce HTTP 500.
HTTP 500 should not be used merely because the requested information does not exist.
HTTP 501 is different from HTTP 405.
501 means the server does not implement the required functionality.
HTTP 502 indicates that a server acting as a gateway or proxy received an invalid response from an upstream server.
HTTP 503 indicates that the server is currently unable to handle the request.
Temporary overload can produce HTTP 503.
Maintenance can produce HTTP 503.
Failure of a required service dependency can produce HTTP 503 when it makes the requested service unavailable.
HTTP 503 represents service availability.
HTTP 503 does not mean "no evidence was found."
HTTP 503 does not mean "the search returned zero results."
HTTP 503 does not mean "the answer is uncertain."
HTTP 503 does not mean "the resource does not exist."
A knowledge miss is not inherently HTTP 503.
A retrieval miss is not inherently HTTP 503.
An unsupported conclusion is not inherently HTTP 503.
An unavailable required internal service can legitimately cause HTTP 503.
A failed optional subsystem does not necessarily make the whole service unavailable.
A server should report availability according to the capability actually required by the request.
HTTP 504 indicates that a gateway or proxy did not receive a timely response from an upstream server.
HTTP 408 concerns the client request timing at the server.
HTTP 504 concerns an upstream response timeout at a gateway or proxy.
A standalone application that is not acting as a gateway should not blindly translate every timeout into 504.
HTTP status describes HTTP request handling.
Internal error categories can be more precise than HTTP status codes.
An application can record a detailed internal error while returning an appropriate HTTP status externally.
Internal diagnostic information should not automatically be exposed to untrusted clients.
A response status and a response body serve different purposes.
The status code communicates protocol-level outcome semantics.
The response body can provide application-specific detail.
A successful HTTP response can contain an application-level result indicating no records were found.
Zero search results can be returned with HTTP 200 when the search itself succeeded.
An empty JSON array can represent a successful query with no matching records.
Unknown is not unavailable.
Unsupported is not unavailable.
Invalid is not unavailable.
Unauthorized is not unavailable.
Forbidden is not unavailable.
Conflict is not unavailable.
Unavailable should mean unavailable.
Error categories should preserve their actual meanings.
Header field values have syntax defined by each header field.
Content-Type identifies the media type of message content.
Content-Length indicates message content length in bytes when present and applicable.
Accept communicates media types acceptable for the response.
Accept-Encoding communicates acceptable content codings.
Content-Encoding identifies applied content coding.
Authorization carries authentication credentials according to an authentication scheme.
WWW-Authenticate communicates authentication challenges.
Location identifies another URI in redirects and certain creation responses.
Cache-Control communicates caching directives.
If-None-Match supplies entity-tag conditions.
If-Match supplies entity-tag preconditions.
Last-Modified communicates a representation modification time.
If-Modified-Since provides a date-based conditional request.
User-Agent identifies information about a requesting user agent according to client behavior.
Server can identify information about server software.
Exposing unnecessary server implementation details can aid fingerprinting.
Host identifies the target host and port information in HTTP/1.1 requests according to protocol rules.
Cookie carries cookies from a client to a server.
Set-Cookie instructs a user agent to store a cookie.
Vary describes request header fields affecting representation selection and caching.
Headers are not inherently trustworthy.
Request headers originate from clients or intermediaries.
A client can forge many ordinary request headers.
User-Agent is not an authentication mechanism.
Referer is not a reliable authentication mechanism.
Origin can be useful in security policy but does not replace authentication.
Reverse proxies can add forwarding headers.
text/plain identifies plain text.
application/json identifies JSON content.
application/x-www-form-urlencoded is commonly used for HTML form data.
multipart/form-data is commonly used for forms containing files or multipart fields.
Accept describes representations the recipient would prefer to receive.
Content-Type and Accept are different headers.
A JSON body should normally be identified using application/json.
Valid JSON syntax does not prove that the JSON satisfies an application's schema.
Media-type validation and semantic validation are different steps.
An HTTP message body is a sequence of bytes interpreted according to representation metadata.
A request body can be empty.
A response body can be empty.
HTTP 204 responses do not contain response content.
HEAD responses do not include response content even though representation metadata can describe what a GET would return.
A response body should not be assumed to contain JSON merely because an application usually returns JSON.
Clients should inspect the protocol contract and applicable Content-Type.
Parsing an HTML error page as JSON can produce a JSON parser error that hides the original HTTP result.
Body parsing failure and HTTP status failure are different conditions.
A URL is a type of URI associated with locating resources.
An HTTP URL contains a scheme.
An HTTP URL contains an authority.
An HTTP URL can contain a path.
An HTTP URL can contain a query.
An HTTP URL can contain a fragment.
Query parameters are part of the URL query component.
Path parameters extracted by an application router are untrusted input.
HTTP headers are untrusted input.
Request bodies are untrusted input.
Client-provided filenames are untrusted input.
Validation should occur before untrusted input controls privileged behavior.
A URL path and a filesystem path are different concepts.
Mapping URL paths directly to filesystem paths without validation can create path traversal vulnerabilities.
Percent-encoding represents certain bytes in URI components.
URL decoding can change the interpreted value of input.
Repeated or inconsistent decoding can create security problems.
Canonicalization should be deliberate.
A server should define accepted URL forms.
HTTP supports persistent connections.
HTTP/1.1 commonly permits multiple request-response exchanges over one TCP connection.
One TCP connection can carry multiple HTTP requests.
Closing one HTTP response does not necessarily mean closing the TCP connection.
HTTP/2 multiplexes multiple streams over a connection.
HTTP/2 allows multiple concurrent request-response exchanges on one connection.
HTTP/3 provides HTTP semantics over QUIC.
Application semantics should not depend unnecessarily on whether HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, or HTTP/3 carries the request.
A server can support multiple HTTP protocol versions.
HTTP version negotiation can occur through TLS and protocol mechanisms.
HTTP is stateless at the protocol application semantics level between requests unless state is represented or associated externally.
Applications can maintain sessions across HTTP requests.
