Buffered writes can remain in memory.
Storage devices can contain volatile write caches.
Databases can buffer operations.
Durability requirements determine which boundary matters.
Do not claim durable persistence without evidence that the required durability boundary was reached.
A flush requests that buffered data move toward a defined persistence boundary according to the API.
Flush semantics depend on the storage system.
A language runtime flush can move data to the operating system.
An operating-system synchronization call can request filesystem or device persistence.
These are not interchangeable.
Know the contract of the storage interface being used.
Closing a file is not universally equivalent to durable synchronization.
A successful file close means the close operation succeeded according to the platform API.
Do not infer stronger guarantees without documentation.
Storage behavior is platform dependent.
Operating systems differ.
Storage devices differ.
Network filesystems differ.
Cloud storage systems differ.
Database engines differ.
Portable storage code should depend only on guarantees common to supported platforms or isolate platform-specific behavior behind defined interfaces.
A filesystem organizes persistent data as files, directories, metadata, and other filesystem-defined structures.
A file is a named or referenced sequence of data according to filesystem semantics.
A directory organizes filesystem entries.
A path identifies a location according to filesystem naming rules.
Path syntax differs between platforms.
Path separators can differ.
Drive semantics can differ.
Case sensitivity can differ.
Permission models can differ.
Do not construct paths through assumptions that are not portable.
Use appropriate path handling.
A relative path is interpreted relative to a defined working or base location.
An absolute path identifies a location from a filesystem root or platform-defined absolute namespace.
Relative paths can become ambiguous when working directory changes.
Critical persistent state should use a known base location.
Do not depend accidentally on the process working directory.
Normalize paths only according to safe platform semantics.
Path normalization can resolve selected redundant components.
A path inside an allowed directory before symbolic-link resolution can resolve elsewhere.
Filesystem security requires more than string prefix checks.
Symbolic links can redirect path resolution.
Junctions and other platform-specific mechanisms can alter resolution.
Do not assume path text uniquely identifies underlying storage.
File identity and path identity are different concepts.
A file can be renamed.
A file can have multiple links.
A path can later refer to a different file.
Permissions control filesystem access according to operating-system rules.
Read permission permits selected reading.
Write permission permits selected modification.
Execute permission has platform-specific semantics.
Directory permissions have directory-specific semantics.
Ownership affects permission evaluation.
Access-control lists can provide additional rules.
Application storage should use least privilege.
A process needing read access does not automatically need write access.
A process needing one writable directory does not automatically need write access to the entire application tree.
Writable code directories increase risk.
Separate code from writable state where practical.
Configuration can be read-only during normal operation.
Runtime data can use dedicated writable locations.
Logs can use dedicated writable locations.
Temporary files can use controlled temporary locations.
Permissions should match actual responsibilities.
Storage errors should be handled.
A file can be missing.
A file can be unreadable.
A directory can be missing.
A directory can be unwritable.
A device can be full.
A quota can be exceeded.
A path can be invalid.
A network filesystem can become unreachable.
An operation can be interrupted.
A file can change during access.
Do not assume storage operations succeed.
Check results.
Preserve useful error information.
Do not convert every storage error into "file not found."
Different failures require different recovery.
Missing optional cache data can permit regeneration.
Missing authoritative data can be critical.
Permission denied is not the same as missing data.
Corruption is not the same as absence.
Classify failures accurately.
A file can exist and be empty.
A file can exist and contain malformed data.
A file can exist and contain an unsupported version.
A file can exist and contain corrupted data.
A file can exist and contain valid but stale data.
A storage format defines how data is represented.
Formats can be fixed-width.
Formats can be variable-width.
Formats can be self-describing.
Format choice should reflect requirements.
Human readability can be valuable.
Compactness can be valuable.
Parsing speed can be valuable.
Forward compatibility can be valuable.
Backward compatibility can be valuable.
Determinism can be valuable.
No format is universally best.
JSON is a structured text format.
JSON can represent objects.
JSON can represent arrays.
JSON can represent strings.
JSON can represent booleans.
JSON can represent null.
JSON numbers require careful handling when exact integer range matters.
A parser can represent numbers differently depending on language and implementation.
