It does not necessarily prove the new data survives sudden power loss.
Atomicity and durability are separate.
Atomicity is one common transaction property.
Consistency is one common transaction property.
Durability is one common transaction property.
These are commonly summarized as ACID.
Exact guarantees depend on the storage engine and configuration.
Do not claim ACID behavior merely because a system uses the word transaction.
Read the actual contract.
Transaction consistency means defined invariants are preserved when transactions execute correctly according to the system model.
Transaction durability controls persistence of committed state according to the storage guarantee.
Isolation has levels.
Different isolation levels permit different concurrent observations.
Lost updates can occur.
Dirty reads can occur where permitted.
Non-repeatable reads can occur where permitted.
Phantom reads can occur where permitted.
Write skew can occur under selected isolation models.
Database semantics should be understood before relying on them.
A lock coordinates access to shared state.
Locks can protect files.
Locks can protect database rows or tables.
Locks can be mandatory according to selected systems.
Lock semantics vary.
A lock existing does not automatically mean every participant honors it.
Lock ownership should be clear.
Locks should be released.
Crash behavior should be understood.
Stale lock files are not the same as active operating-system locks.
A lock file can remain after a process dies.
A lock file should contain enough information to support safe stale-lock determination if that mechanism is used.
Do not delete a lock merely because it appears old.
The owning operation might legitimately be long-running.
Time alone can be insufficient evidence.
Concurrent readers can often share immutable state safely.
Concurrent writers require coordination.
A writer can race with another writer.
Atomic replacement can let readers observe either old complete state or new complete state according to filesystem semantics.
It can reduce partial-read risk.
It does not solve every multi-writer conflict.
State versioning can detect concurrent modification.
A version number can increment with each accepted modification.
Optimistic concurrency assumes conflicts are uncommon and detects them before commit.
Pessimistic concurrency prevents selected conflicts through locking.
Neither is universally superior.
Choose according to workload and correctness requirements.
Last-write-wins is a conflict resolution policy.
Last-write-wins can discard earlier concurrent changes.
It is not automatically correct.
Timestamp-based last-write-wins depends on time assumptions.
Conflict resolution should reflect domain semantics.
Append-only storage adds new records without modifying previous records under normal operation.
Append-only designs can preserve history.
Append-only does not mean immutable under every storage or administrative capability.
Access controls and integrity mechanisms can strengthen historical protection.
An event log can represent state changes.
Current state can be reconstructed by replaying events when event semantics are sufficient.
Event sourcing adds complexity.
It is useful when complete state transition history is required.
It is unnecessary when ordinary state storage satisfies requirements.
A journal records changes used for recovery or history according to its design.
A write-ahead log records information before selected state changes so recovery can replay or roll back operations according to the system.
Databases commonly use write-ahead logging.
Filesystems can use journaling.
Application-level journals can also be implemented.
Journal semantics should be understood.
A journal is not automatically a backup.
If both primary state and journal are lost, recovery can still fail.
If corruption is faithfully journaled, the journal can contain the same logical defect.
A backup is a recoverable copy of data created according to backup policy.
A backup should identify what it contains.
A backup should identify when it was created.
A backup should be protected.
A backup should be validated.
A backup should be restorable.
Restore testing provides evidence.
Backups can be full.
Backups can be incremental.
Backups can be differential.
A full backup contains the complete selected backup set according to policy.
An incremental backup contains selected changes since a previous backup point according to its scheme.
A differential backup contains selected changes relative to a defined base according to its scheme.
Incremental backup chains can increase restore dependencies.
Backup architecture should account for this.
Backup retention defines how long backups remain available.
Retention can use generations.
Retention can use time windows.
Retention can preserve selected long-term checkpoints.
More backups consume more storage.
Too little retention can make historical recovery impossible.
Backup storage should not share every failure domain with primary storage.
A backup on the same failing disk does not protect against disk loss.
A backup on the same machine does not protect against machine loss.
Independent failure domains improve recovery.
Offline or logically isolated backups can reduce selected risks.
Backup encryption can protect confidentiality.
Encryption keys become required recovery material.
Losing the key can make the backup unusable.
Key backup and recovery should therefore be designed.
Recovery dependencies matter.
Checksums detect selected accidental changes.
A checksum maps data to a fixed-size value according to an algorithm.
A matching checksum provides evidence that data matches the expected checksum under the algorithm's properties.
A checksum does not prove data is authorized.
A cryptographic hash provides stronger collision resistance than ordinary non-cryptographic checksums according to the algorithm.
A digital signature can establish that data was signed by a holder of the relevant private key according to the signature system.
A signature does not prove the data is correct.
Integrity and authenticity are different.
Checksums should cover clearly defined bytes.
Canonical representation matters when logically equivalent data can have multiple byte representations.
Hashing parsed objects without canonical serialization can produce inconsistent results.
Whitespace can change a file hash.
Field ordering can change a file hash.
Line endings can change a file hash.
Encoding can change a file hash.
A content hash identifies bytes, not abstract meaning, unless canonicalization defines the bytes.
Text encoding should be defined.
UTF-8 is a common text encoding.
ASCII is a subset of UTF-8 for standard ASCII characters.
Other encodings exist.
Do not assume arbitrary bytes are UTF-8.
A byte order mark can appear in selected Unicode encodings.
Parsers should follow format rules.
Line endings can differ by platform.
LF is commonly used on Unix-like systems.
CRLF is commonly used on Windows text files.
Software handling cross-platform text should account for line-ending differences where semantics permit.
Protocol-defined line endings should follow the protocol exactly.
Storage normalization should not silently alter protocol-significant bytes.
Binary formats operate on bytes.
Binary serialization should define byte order.
Binary serialization should define signedness.
Binary serialization should define lengths.
Binary serialization should define alignment if alignment is serialized.
Do not serialize native C structures directly as portable persistent formats without defining representation.
Compiler padding can differ.
Architecture alignment can differ.
Endianness can differ.
Explicit serialization improves portability.
Network byte order is conventionally big-endian for many network protocols.
Persistent formats can choose any defined byte order.
The choice matters less than consistency and specification.
Readers should validate lengths before allocation.
A stored length can be malicious.
Do not allocate arbitrary memory solely because a file claims an enormous length.
Bounds protect storage parsers.
Integer overflow can occur when calculating storage sizes.
Validate before multiplication and addition.
A count multiplied by element size can overflow.
An offset plus length can overflow.
Overflow can bypass bounds checks.
Storage parsing is a security boundary when data is untrusted.
Treat persistent input as input.
Do not trust a file merely because your own application created it previously.
It can be truncated.
It can be modified.
It can be from an older version.
It can be restored from backup.
It can be copied from another system.
Validate on load.
Validation should be proportional to risk.
Magic values can identify file type.
Version fields can identify format.
Length fields can identify expected size.
Checksums can detect corruption.
Signatures can authenticate content.
Schemas can validate structure.
Semantic checks can validate relationships.
No one check replaces all others.
Corruption should have defined handling.
If a cache is corrupted, discard and rebuild when safe.
If derived indexes are corrupted, rebuild from authoritative state when safe.
If authoritative state is corrupted, stop unsafe processing and recover from a trusted source according to policy.
Do not silently reset authoritative state to empty.
An empty database is not equivalent to a corrupted database.
A default configuration is not equivalent to missing required configuration.
Fail clearly.
