A record can be required to reference an existing parent.
A configuration can require exactly one active value.
Storage operations should maintain these rules.
Database constraints can enforce selected invariants.
Application validation can enforce selected invariants.
Protocol validation can enforce selected invariants.
Use the strongest appropriate boundary.
Multiple clients can bypass the UI.
Foreign-key constraints can enforce selected relationships.
Unique constraints can enforce uniqueness.
Check constraints can enforce selected value rules.
Transactions can coordinate related changes.
Application rules can enforce domain semantics too complex for storage constraints.
Defense in depth can use multiple layers.
Duplicated validation should remain semantically consistent.
Storage failures should be observable.
Record failed operation.
Record storage component.
Record relevant error code.
Record affected logical object where safe.
Record whether operation can be retried.
Record whether state outcome is known.
Do not log entire sensitive records unnecessarily.
A failed write can have uncertain outcome in remote storage.
If connection fails after sending a write, the server can have committed it.
Do not automatically retry non-idempotent writes.
Use operation identifiers.
Query authoritative state.
Reconcile uncertain outcomes.
Storage operations should support idempotency where retries are expected.
Writing the same complete configuration to the same version can be idempotent.
Appending an event is not automatically idempotent.
Incrementing a counter is not idempotent.
Creating a record can be idempotent if a stable unique operation identifier prevents duplicates.
Compaction rewrites stored state into a smaller or more efficient representation.
Logs can be compacted.
Compaction should preserve required semantics.
Compaction can remove obsolete derived data.
Compaction should not remove permanent historical records when history must remain.
Use atomic or recoverable compaction strategies.
Do not destroy the only valid state before replacement is verified.
Garbage collection removes storage objects no longer needed according to ownership rules.
Reachability can determine liveness in some storage systems.
Reference counts can determine liveness in others.
Retention rules can determine liveness.
Garbage collection should never guess about authoritative ownership.
A falsely deleted object can be unrecoverable.
Deletion criteria should be deterministic.
Orphaned data can be safe to delete.
Do not automatically delete unexpected state before investigation.
Storage repair modifies state to restore invariants.
Repair should use known rules.
Repair should preserve evidence.
Repair should record what changed.
Repair should verify the result.
If missing information cannot be reconstructed, report that it cannot be reconstructed.
Recovery is not fabrication.
A recovery source can be backup.
A recovery source can be an append-only journal.
A recovery source can be deterministic recomputation from authoritative history.
A recovery source should be validated.
Do not restore corrupted state merely because it came from backup.
Validate restored data.
Stop writers if required.
Restore data.
Apply logs or increments where required.
Validate state.
Rebuild derived data.
Verify application behavior.
Resume writers.
Exact procedure depends on architecture.
Recovery should be tested before disaster.
A recovery procedure written but never tested contains assumptions.
Restore tests can reveal missing credentials.
Restore tests can reveal missing encryption keys.
Restore tests can reveal incompatible software.
Restore tests can reveal corrupted backups.
Restore tests can reveal undocumented dependencies.
Recovery evidence matters.
A successful restore test proves the tested backup and procedure worked under those conditions.
It does not prove every backup is valid.
Automated verification can improve confidence.
Periodic restore testing can provide stronger evidence.
Storage performance matters.
Sequential reads can differ from random reads.
Small I/O can differ from large I/O.
Synchronous writes can differ from buffered writes.
Measure the actual deployment environment.
Do not optimize storage before identifying the bottleneck.
A storage cache can hide physical latency.
Benchmark cold and warm behavior where relevant.
Write benchmarks should account for durability settings.
A benchmark that never synchronizes durable writes does not measure durable-write latency.
State the durability level being benchmarked.
Database benchmarks should use realistic data volume.
Filesystem benchmarks should use realistic file counts.
Object-store benchmarks should use realistic object sizes.
Storage performance changes with scale.
A directory with ten files can behave differently from one with millions of files.
A database with one thousand rows can behave differently from one with billions.
Capacity testing should reflect expected growth.
Storage performance should not compromise correctness.
Removing synchronization can improve benchmark numbers while weakening durability.
Disabling validation can improve parsing speed while accepting corruption.
Skipping transactions can improve throughput while allowing partial state.
These are semantic changes, not free optimizations.
Requirements come first.
Storage should be boring when possible.
Simple formats are easier to inspect.
Single-writer designs are easier to coordinate.
Atomic replacement is easier to recover than complicated in-place mutation for small files.
Explicit versions are easier to migrate.
Bounded data is easier to operate.
Complex storage should be introduced only when requirements justify it.
Do not confuse a file with a filesystem.
Do not confuse a filesystem with physical media.
Do not confuse write success with durability.
Do not confuse flush with every possible durability guarantee.
Do not confuse atomicity with durability.
Do not confuse persistence with correctness.
Do not confuse persistence with integrity.
Do not confuse persistence with backup.
Do not confuse replication with backup.
Do not confuse a snapshot with a guaranteed application-consistent backup.
Do not confuse a cache with authority.
Do not confuse a replica with authority.
Do not confuse newer data with authoritative data.
Do not confuse existing data with valid data.
Do not confuse syntactic validity with semantic validity.
Do not confuse a signature with correctness.
Do not confuse encryption with integrity unless the encryption construction provides authenticated integrity.
Do not confuse encryption with access control.
Do not confuse deletion with immediate physical erasure.
Do not confuse archival with deletion.
Do not confuse temporary data with disposable data unless ownership defines it as disposable.
Do not confuse an index with authoritative state.
Do not confuse an append-only design with absolute immutability.
Do not confuse transaction support with identical isolation semantics.
Do not confuse last-write-wins with correct conflict resolution.
Do not confuse timestamp order with authoritative order.
Do not confuse file age with lock ownership.
Do not confuse load failure with missing initial state.
Do not confuse recovery with reset.
Do not confuse repair with fabrication.
Do not confuse backup creation with successful restore.
Do not confuse storage redundancy with system reliability.
Identify who owns it.
Identify whether it is derived.
Identify whether it is sensitive.
Identify whether it must survive restart.
Identify expected size.
Identify expected write frequency.
Identify expected read frequency.
Identify required history.
Identify platform requirements.
Then choose the simplest storage mechanism satisfying those requirements.
When writing authoritative state, validate input.
Prepare the complete change.
Use an atomic or transactional mechanism where required.
Synchronize to the required durability boundary.
Verify the result where appropriate.
Read the data.
Validate version.
Validate bounds.
Validate integrity where required.
Validate semantics.
Reject unsupported or corrupted state.
Do not guess missing required values.
Do not silently replace invalid state with defaults.
Missing.
Unreadable.
Unsupported.
Corrupted.
Unavailable.
Full.
Unauthorized.
Conflicted.
Unconfirmed.
These states require different responses.
Preserve damaged evidence where practical.
Restore into a controlled location.
Validate the restored state.
Verify integrity.
Verify required invariants.
Verify end-to-end operation.
When designing portable storage, define bytes explicitly.
Define byte order.
Define length limits.
Define field semantics.
Define versioning.
Define canonicalization when hashes or signatures require it.
Keep platform-specific persistence mechanisms behind platform boundaries.
Keep authoritative data semantics common.
The storage backend can differ.
The authoritative meaning must not.
Storage is not merely where bytes are placed.
Storage defines how state survives, how state is identified, how state changes, who may change it, how concurrent changes are controlled, how corruption is detected, and how valid state is recovered after failure.
A reliable storage design knows which state matters.
It knows which copy is authoritative.
It knows what can be rebuilt.
It knows what must never be silently discarded.
It validates what it reads.
It preserves evidence when something goes wrong.
And when the required state cannot be established from evidence, it reports that state as unknown or unavailable rather than inventing one.
Acknowledgment indicates that a statement has been received without necessarily expressing agreement.
A follow-up question seeks additional information about a subject already being discussed.
A conversation may continue across multiple messages without repeating the original subject.
Short replies such as yes, no, maybe, and sometimes often depend on preceding conversational context.
When someone changes the subject, subsequent messages may relate to the new topic rather than the previous discussion.
A rhetorical question is asked for emphasis and does not always require a literal answer.
Repeating information already established in a conversation may be unnecessary unless clarification is requested.
----
Small talk is informal conversation intended to establish or maintain friendly social interaction.
Sharing music preferences is a common way people connect socially.
Someone discussing their day may be sharing an experience rather than requesting advice.
A friendly invitation may be expressed indirectly through casual language.
Appreciation is an expression of gratitude or recognition for someone's actions.
Friendly teasing uses playful language and depends on shared context and mutual understanding.
Silence or a lack of response does not necessarily indicate disagreement or hostility.
Excitement is an emotional state commonly associated with anticipation, enthusiasm, or positive developments.
Relief is a feeling that may follow the resolution of a stressful or uncertain situation.
Nostalgia is an emotional connection to memories or experiences from the past.
Pride may occur when someone recognizes an accomplishment or meaningful personal progress.
Affection may be expressed through nicknames, playful language, compliments, or gestures.
Emotional expressions do not always indicate that someone is seeking a solution.
Yus is a playful variation of yes and commonly indicates enthusiastic agreement.
Nah is an informal expression of no or disagreement.
Yep is an informal affirmative response.
Oof is an informal expression of sympathy, discomfort, or acknowledgment of a difficult situation.
Bruh is an informal expression that may indicate disbelief, surprise, frustration, or familiarity.
LMAO is an internet abbreviation indicating strong amusement or laughter.
OMG is an abbreviation for oh my god and commonly expresses surprise or disbelief.
BISH is a playful variation of bitch that may express familiarity or affection depending on context.
A nickname is an alternative name used to identify or address a person, character, or system.
Direct address occurs when a speaker intentionally directs a message toward a particular participant.
A conversational participant may be addressed by a name, nickname, or other recognized identifier.
The meaning of a nickname may depend on who is speaking and who is being addressed.
A question containing you generally refers to the participant being addressed, unless context indicates otherwise.
A message may contain both a direct address and a conversational question.
Knowing the definition of a term does not establish which participant the term refers to in a specific conversation.
Uncertainty means that available information is insufficient to establish a confident conclusion.
An assumption is a proposition accepted without sufficient supporting verification.
A factual claim and a personal opinion may require different methods of evaluation.
A social greeting does not necessarily require a factual Corpus lookup.
Acknowledging a person's statement does not require claiming knowledge beyond what the person provided.
API stands for Application Programming Interface.
An API defines how one software component can interact with another.
An API is a contract.
An API can define operations.
An API can define inputs.
An API can define data formats.
An API can define authentication requirements.
An API can define authorization requirements.
An API can define compatibility requirements.
An API is not necessarily a network service.
A library can expose an API.
An operating system exposes APIs.
A network service can expose an API.
An HTTP service can expose an API.
A command-line program can provide an interface consumed by other software.
An API describes interaction rather than implementation.
Consumers should not need to know unnecessary implementation details behind an API.
An API provider is software that implements an API.
An API contract should be explicit.
A contract should define valid requests.
A contract should define valid responses.
A contract should define failure behavior.
A contract should define relevant limits.
A contract should define version behavior where versions exist.
A consumer should not rely on undocumented behavior.
Observed behavior is not automatically guaranteed behavior.
An implementation detail is not automatically part of the API contract.
Changing an implementation detail should not affect consumers when the contract remains unchanged.
Changing the contract can affect consumers.
API stability reduces unnecessary coupling between components.
A local API operates within the same machine or process environment.
A remote API crosses a communication boundary.
A remote request can time out.
A network can fail.
A server can be unavailable.
A response can be lost.
A request can arrive more than once.
A response can arrive later than expected.
A remote service can return malformed data.
A remote service can return an unexpected version.
A remote service can partially complete an operation.
HTTP stands for Hypertext Transfer Protocol.
HTTPS protects transport according to TLS properties.
HTTPS does not automatically make an API logically correct.
HTTPS does not automatically provide application authorization.
HTTPS does not automatically validate request data.
An HTTP request contains a method.
An HTTP request contains a target.
An HTTP request can contain a body.
An HTTP response contains headers.
An HTTP response can contain a body.
The HTTP method communicates request semantics.
GET is commonly used to retrieve a representation.
POST is commonly used to submit data or request processing.
PUT is commonly used to create or replace a resource representation according to API semantics.
PATCH is commonly used to modify part of a resource.
HEAD requests response metadata corresponding to GET without the normal response body.
HTTP methods have defined protocol semantics.
GET should normally be safe according to HTTP semantics.
A safe method is intended not to request a state-changing action.
Safe does not mean the server performs no internal activity.
A GET request can be logged.
A GET request should not ordinarily perform an application action such as deleting a record.
Idempotency means repeating an operation has the same intended effect as performing it once according to the operation semantics.
GET is intended to be idempotent.
PUT is intended to be idempotent.
DELETE is intended to be idempotent according to HTTP method semantics.
POST is not inherently idempotent.
PATCH is not inherently idempotent.
An API can implement additional idempotency mechanisms.
A client can time out after the server successfully processes a request.
The client may not know whether the operation occurred.
Blindly repeating a non-idempotent operation can duplicate state changes.
A resource is an entity exposed through an API according to the API model.
A resource can have an identifier.
A resource can have a representation.
A representation describes resource state in a particular format.
JSON is commonly used for API representations.
JSON is not required for an API.
APIs can use XML.
APIs can use plain text.
APIs can use binary formats.
APIs can use protocol-specific encodings.
The format should be explicitly defined.
application/json identifies JSON content.
A client sending JSON should use the appropriate Content-Type when the API requires it.
A server should validate content type according to the API contract.
Accept can communicate response media types acceptable to a client.
Content negotiation selects a representation according to supported media types and HTTP rules.
An API does not need multiple formats merely because HTTP supports content negotiation.
Keep the interface as small as requirements permit.
JSON parsing and JSON validation are different.
Parsing determines whether input is syntactically valid JSON.
A valid JSON document can be an invalid API request.
Required fields should be validated.
Field types should be validated.
Allowed values should be validated.
Length limits should be validated.
Numeric ranges should be validated.
Unknown fields should be handled according to API policy.
Malformed requests should not be silently interpreted as something else.
Input validation should occur before trusted processing.
An API schema can formally describe data structures.
JSON Schema can describe JSON structure and constraints.
OpenAPI can describe HTTP APIs.
An API description can document methods.
An API description can document request bodies.
An API description can document authentication mechanisms.
The implementation should be tested against the contract.
An endpoint identifies an API interaction target according to the API design.
An HTTP endpoint commonly consists of a path combined with supported methods.
The same path can support different methods.
GET /items and POST /items can represent different operations.
The method is part of the interface.
A URL can contain path parameters.
A request can contain body parameters.
These serve different purposes.
Path parameters commonly identify resources or hierarchy.
Query parameters commonly modify retrieval, filtering, sorting, or pagination.
Request bodies commonly carry structured input for state-changing or complex operations.
These are conventions rather than universal laws.
The API contract determines actual meaning.
Do not accept the same security-sensitive parameter from multiple locations without a defined precedence rule.
Ambiguous parameter precedence creates unpredictable behavior.
HTTP status codes are grouped by class.
1xx status codes represent informational responses.
2xx status codes represent successful processing.
3xx status codes represent redirection.
4xx status codes represent client-side request conditions or failures.
5xx status codes represent server-side failures.
200 OK indicates successful handling with an applicable response.
201 Created indicates successful creation of a resource.
202 Accepted indicates a request was accepted for processing but processing is not necessarily complete.
204 No Content indicates successful processing with no response content.
A 202 response does not mean the requested operation has completed.
An asynchronous operation should expose completion state when consumers need it.
400 Bad Request indicates the server cannot or will not process the request because of a client request problem according to the interface.
401 Unauthorized is commonly used when valid authentication credentials are absent or insufficient for authentication.
Despite its name, HTTP 401 primarily concerns authentication.
404 Not Found indicates the requested resource was not found or is not exposed as existing according to API policy.
413 Content Too Large indicates request content exceeds accepted limits.
422 Unprocessable Content can indicate syntactically acceptable content that cannot be processed according to defined semantics.
429 Too Many Requests indicates rate limiting or request-frequency limits.
500 Internal Server Error indicates an unexpected server-side failure.
502 Bad Gateway indicates a gateway or proxy received an invalid response from an upstream server.
503 Service Unavailable indicates temporary service unavailability.
504 Gateway Timeout indicates a gateway or proxy did not receive a timely upstream response.
Status codes should describe the actual result.
Do not return 200 merely because the server generated a response.
An error response is still a response.
A successful TCP connection does not establish API success.
A successful TLS connection does not establish API success.
An HTTP response does not establish application success.
A 2xx status normally communicates application-level success according to the API contract.
An API error body can provide structured diagnostic information.
Error responses should be predictable.
An error representation can contain an error code.
An error representation can contain a human-readable message.
An error representation can contain relevant field errors.
An error representation can contain a request or correlation identifier.
Internal stack traces should not ordinarily be exposed to remote clients.
Database credentials should not appear in API errors.
Filesystem paths should not be exposed without reason.
Tokens should not appear in API errors.
Machine-readable error codes are more stable than parsing human-readable messages.
Error codes should have stable meanings.
Do not reuse one error code for unrelated conditions.
A request identifier identifies one request for tracing or diagnostic purposes.
A correlation identifier can associate activity across multiple components.
Identifiers can make distributed debugging easier.
A request ID is not automatically an authentication credential.
A request ID should not grant authority.
Authorization determines what an authenticated identity can do.
Authentication and authorization are separate.
An API can require authentication.
An API can require authorization for selected operations.
Authentication should occur before relying on identity.
Authorization should occur before protected action.
A valid credential does not grant unlimited authority.
API keys can authenticate or identify clients according to the API design.
API keys are secrets when possession grants access.
API keys should not be embedded in publicly delivered client-side code when secrecy is required.
API keys should not be written into public repositories.
API keys should not be exposed in logs.
Bearer tokens grant defined access through possession.
Bearer tokens must be protected.
An Authorization header commonly carries authentication credentials in HTTP APIs.
Bearer tokens are commonly transmitted using the Authorization header with the Bearer scheme.
Credentials should not normally be placed in query strings.
URLs can be stored in logs.
URLs can be stored in browser history.
URLs can be exposed through monitoring systems.
Sensitive credentials belong in appropriate protected request mechanisms.
Basic authentication transmits a username and password-derived credential encoding according to HTTP authentication rules.
Base64 encoding used by Basic authentication is not encryption.
Basic authentication should use protected transport when credentials require confidentiality.
OAuth is an authorization framework.
OAuth is not one single authentication protocol.
OAuth defines flows for delegated authorization according to its specifications.
OpenID Connect adds an identity layer on top of OAuth 2.0.
JWT stands for JSON Web Token.
A JWT is a token format.
A JWT can contain claims.
JWT payload data can often be decoded by anyone possessing the token.
Do not store confidential data in a merely signed JWT unless disclosure is acceptable.
A token signature must be verified before trusting signed claims.
Parsing a JWT is not verification.
Verification is not authorization.
Claims must be validated according to the application.
An expiration claim can define token expiration.
An audience claim can identify intended recipients.
Applications should validate claims required by their security model.
An expired token should not be treated as current merely because its signature remains cryptographically valid.
API sessions can use cookies.
Cookies can carry session identifiers.
Session identifiers should be unpredictable when they function as credentials.
A session identifier is not the same as user identity.
Cookies have security attributes.
HttpOnly prevents ordinary client-side script access to the cookie according to browser rules.
Cookie security settings should follow application requirements.
Cookie-authenticated APIs can be exposed to CSRF risks.
CSRF stands for Cross-Site Request Forgery.
CSRF causes a browser to send an authenticated request the user did not intend.
State-changing browser requests should use appropriate CSRF defenses when the authentication model requires them.
Using POST alone does not prevent CSRF.
A CSRF token can bind a state-changing request to an authorized user interaction according to the application mechanism.
CORS is a browser security mechanism governing selected cross-origin web requests.
CORS is not authentication.
CORS is not authorization.
Allowing an origin through CORS does not grant that origin application permissions by itself.
Disabling CORS restrictions does not fix an API authentication problem.
CORS should be configured according to actual browser-client requirements.
A REST-style API commonly models resources and uses HTTP semantics.
An API can use HTTP without being RESTful.
An API can be well designed without strictly following REST.
Architecture should serve requirements rather than terminology.
If an operation is naturally a command, forcing it into artificial CRUD terminology can reduce clarity.
CRUD stands for Create, Read, Update, and Delete.
CRUD describes common data operations.
Validation can be an operation.
Compilation can be an operation.
Submission can be an operation.
Signing can be an operation.
Processing can be an operation.
RPC stands for Remote Procedure Call.
An RPC API models remote operations as procedure or method calls.
JSON-RPC is an RPC protocol using JSON.
gRPC is an RPC framework using Protocol Buffers and HTTP/2 or related supported transport behavior.
RPC is not inherently inferior to REST.
REST is not inherently superior to RPC.
The appropriate interface depends on system requirements.
A protocol should be deterministic where deterministic behavior is required.
Given the same valid request and relevant state, a deterministic operation should produce the defined result.
An API should not return arbitrary different meanings for identical status fields.
Field semantics should remain stable.
A boolean should not sometimes mean success and sometimes mean availability.
One field should have one defined purpose.
Response schemas should be explicit.
Optional fields should be identified as optional.
Required fields should be identified as required.
Missing and null are not necessarily the same.
A missing field means the field is absent.
A null field explicitly carries a null value.
The API contract determines whether these states are equivalent.
Consumers should not invent values for missing fields unless the contract defines defaults.
Defaults should be explicit.
A default is part of behavior.
Changing a default can be a compatibility change.
Pagination divides large result sets into bounded responses.
An API should not return unbounded data when the dataset can grow without limit.
Pagination can use page numbers.
Pagination can use offsets.
Pagination can use cursors.
Pagination can use continuation tokens.
Clients should not parse opaque continuation tokens.
A consumer should return them unchanged when instructed.
Sorting determines result ordering.
Without defined ordering, clients should not rely on accidental database or filesystem order.
A unique tie-breaker can make ordering deterministic.
Rate limiting bounds request frequency or resource use.
Rate limits protect service capacity.
Rate limiting is not a replacement for authorization.
A rate limit can apply per identity.
A rate limit can apply per address.
A rate limit can apply per token.
A rate limit can apply globally.
The rate-limit policy should be defined.
A 429 response commonly indicates rate limiting.
Retry-After can tell clients when retry may be appropriate according to HTTP semantics.
Repeated aggressive retry can worsen an outage.
Timeouts should be bounded.
A client should not wait forever for a remote API.
Connection timeout limits connection establishment time.
Read timeout limits waiting for response data according to the client implementation.
Overall request timeout limits total operation duration.
Different timeout categories should not be confused.
A timeout does not prove the server did nothing.
A timeout establishes that the expected result was not received within the defined time.
Backoff delays repeated retries.
Exponential backoff increases retry delay progressively.
Jitter varies retry timing.
Backoff and jitter reduce synchronized retry storms.
Retries should be bounded.
Permanent errors should not be retried indefinitely.
A 401 authentication failure normally requires valid credentials rather than immediate identical retry.
A 403 authorization failure normally requires changed authority or policy rather than identical retry.
A 404 normally does not become successful through immediate repeated requests unless resource creation or eventual consistency is expected.
A 429 can be retryable according to rate-limit policy.
A 503 commonly indicates temporary unavailability.
A circuit breaker temporarily stops requests to a failing dependency according to defined thresholds.
Circuit breakers can prevent cascading failure.
A circuit breaker has states.
An open circuit rejects or redirects calls without repeatedly contacting the failing dependency.
Bulkheads isolate resources so one failing workload does not consume all capacity.
A separate worker pool can isolate one dependency.
Isolation can limit cascading failures.
API gateways sit between clients and backend services.
A gateway can route requests.
A gateway can enforce rate limits.
A gateway can terminate TLS.
A gateway can transform requests.
A gateway does not remove the need for correct backend authorization when backend trust boundaries require it.
Reverse proxies forward client requests to backend services.
A reverse proxy can provide TLS termination.
A reverse proxy can provide load balancing.
Forwarded client-address headers should not be blindly trusted from arbitrary clients.
A load balancer distributes requests among service instances.
Load balancing improves capacity or availability according to architecture.
Load balancing does not guarantee application state consistency.
Stateful applications can require session affinity or shared state.
Stateless API design makes horizontal distribution easier.
Stateless does not mean the system stores no data.
Stateless means each request contains or references sufficient context so the server does not require hidden per-client conversational state according to the interface model.
Persistent database state is compatible with stateless HTTP request handling.
Caching stores reusable results.
HTTP defines caching mechanisms.
Cache-Control communicates caching directives.
If-None-Match can request conditional retrieval using an ETag.
A server can return 304 Not Modified when the client's cached representation remains valid according to the condition.
Caching can reduce latency and load.
Caching can return stale data if invalidation is incorrect.
Sensitive responses should not be cached where policy forbids it.
Cache behavior should be explicit.
ETags can support optimistic concurrency.
A client retrieves a representation and ETag.
The client sends an update with If-Match containing the expected ETag.
The server can reject the update if the resource changed.
Concurrent modification should be handled deliberately.
Last-write-wins is a policy.
Last-write-wins is not automatically correct.
Optimistic concurrency is another policy.
Explicit conflict resolution is another policy.
The domain determines appropriate behavior.
API versioning manages incompatible interface changes.
Versioning can occur in URL paths.
Versioning can occur through protocol negotiation.
No versioning strategy is universally correct.
A version should have defined semantics.
Do not increment versions without knowing what compatibility changed.
Removing a required field can break clients.
Changing a field type can break clients.
Changing the meaning of a field can break clients even when syntax remains identical.
Adding a new optional response field is often backward compatible for clients that correctly ignore unknown fields.
A client that rejects every unknown response field can be less forward compatible.
Whether unknown fields should be accepted depends on protocol policy.
Deprecation identifies API behavior planned for retirement.
Deprecated endpoints can remain functional.
Deprecation should provide migration information when consumers need it.
Internal APIs also have consumers.
"Internal" does not mean changes are free.
Another module can depend on an internal interface.
Another service can depend on an internal endpoint.
API documentation should describe behavior.
Documentation should include request examples where useful.
Documentation should include response examples where useful.
Examples should not replace formal rules.
An example demonstrates one case.
Documentation should identify authentication requirements.
Documentation should identify error behavior.
Documentation should identify rate limits when applicable.
Documentation should identify version behavior.
Documentation should identify pagination.
Documentation should identify idempotency behavior.
Limits are part of an interface.
Maximum request size should be bounded.
Maximum field length should be bounded where applicable.
Maximum array length should be bounded where applicable.
Maximum pagination size should be bounded.
Unbounded input creates resource-exhaustion risk.
A server should reject input exceeding defined limits.
Input limits should be enforced before excessive resource consumption when possible.
API security begins at the trust boundary.
Treat remote input as untrusted.
Validate type.
Validate range.
Validate authorization.
Validate state transitions.
Use parameterized database queries.
Avoid command injection.
Avoid path traversal.
Avoid unsafe deserialization.
Trust depends on the relationship and authentication boundary.
Server-to-server communication still crosses a trust boundary.
A compromised service can send malicious input.
Defense in depth can validate data at important boundaries.
File upload APIs require special care.
A MIME type supplied by a client is untrusted metadata.
A file extension supplied by a client is untrusted metadata.
Validate content according to application requirements.
Generate controlled storage names where appropriate.
Enforce size limits.
Store uploads outside executable locations when appropriate.
An API accepting URLs can create Server-Side Request Forgery risk.
SSRF occurs when an attacker causes a server to make unintended network requests.
Network destination validation can be required.
Redirect handling can affect destination validation.
SSRF defense requires considering the complete request path.
Deserialization converts encoded data into application structures.
Unsafe deserialization can permit unintended behavior when formats support executable object construction or dangerous type mechanisms.
Prefer simple data formats for untrusted API input.
Validate decoded structures.
Do not allow untrusted data to select arbitrary application classes or executable behavior.
Mass assignment occurs when application frameworks automatically map untrusted input onto object fields that should not be externally writable.
Explicitly define writable fields.
A user should not gain administrator status merely by adding an unexpected field to a request.
Authorization applies to fields as well as endpoints when necessary.
An endpoint can permit updating a display name while forbidding updating account authority.
Validate requested state changes.
Object-level authorization ensures a caller can access the specific requested object.
A user allowed to view their own record should not automatically be able to view another user's record by changing an ID.
Resource identity and authorization are separate.
Function-level authorization controls access to operations.
Administrative endpoints require appropriate authority.
Do not rely on hiding an endpoint.
An undocumented endpoint remains reachable if exposed.
Security by obscurity is not authorization.
API logs support operations and audit.
Log request identity where appropriate.
Log endpoint or operation.
Log request identifiers.
Do not log session secrets.
Do not indiscriminately log complete request bodies containing sensitive information.
Logging policy should identify sensitive fields.
Audit logs preserve security-relevant or governance-relevant events.
Diagnostic logs support debugging.
These logs can have different retention requirements.
API observability can include metrics.
Request count can be measured.
Error count can be measured.
Latency can be measured.
Active requests can be measured.
Rate-limit events can be measured.
Dependency failures can be measured.
A service returning 500 responses quickly is not healthy merely because latency is low.
Health requires defined success criteria.
An API health endpoint can report service health.
A liveness endpoint and readiness endpoint can have different meanings.
A service can be alive but not ready.
A service can be ready for limited functionality while another optional subsystem is degraded.
Health state should represent actual capability.
An API can expose asynchronous operations.
A client submits work.
The server returns an operation identifier.
The client can query operation state.
Operation states can include pending.
Operation states can include running.
Operation states can include completed.
Operation states can include failed.
Operation states should have defined meanings.
Completed should mean completion criteria were met.
Failed should preserve an appropriate error result.
Asynchronous work should not require clients to guess completion from elapsed time.
Polling repeatedly queries operation status.
Polling intervals should be bounded.
Excessive polling wastes resources.
Webhooks allow a server to send event notifications to another server.
A webhook is an outbound HTTP callback according to the application design.
Webhook delivery can fail.
Webhook delivery can be duplicated.
Webhook receivers should consider idempotency.
Webhook senders should define retry policy.
Webhook messages should be authenticated when trust requires it.
A signature can authenticate webhook content according to the signing scheme.
The receiver should verify signatures before trusting signed content.
A valid webhook signature does not automatically authorize every possible operation.
An event-driven API communicates state changes through events.
An event represents something that occurred.
Events describe occurrences.
"Create user" is command-like.
"User created" is event-like.
An event should normally describe a completed fact according to its semantics.
Consumers can process an event more than once unless delivery guarantees prevent it.
Exactly-once processing is difficult in distributed systems.
Many systems instead use at-least-once delivery plus idempotent processing.
Delivery guarantees should be explicit.
The queue can hold work.
Queue acceptance does not mean processing completed.
A queue can become unavailable.
A worker can fail.
A poison message repeatedly fails processing.
Poison-message handling should be bounded.
A dead-letter queue can hold messages that cannot be processed according to queue policy.
Do not retry permanently invalid work forever.
Service boundaries should be deliberate.
Every network boundary introduces latency.
Every network boundary introduces failure modes.
Every service introduces compatibility concerns.
Every service can introduce authentication requirements.
Microservices are not automatically better architecture.
A modular monolith can preserve component boundaries without distributed-system complexity.
Distribute components when requirements justify distribution.
An internal function call is cheaper and more reliable than an unnecessary network call.
API design should minimize unnecessary round trips.
A chatty API requires many small requests to complete one operation.
A coarse API can perform a coherent operation in one request.
Interfaces should match domain operations.
Do not force consumers to reconstruct server-side domain logic through dozens of calls when one bounded operation is appropriate.
Do not create enormous endpoints performing unrelated actions merely to reduce requests.
Balance cohesion and communication cost.
Batch operations should define whether failure is atomic.
All items can succeed or fail together.
Items can succeed independently.
The response should identify per-item outcomes when partial success is allowed.
Do not return ambiguous success for partially failed batches.
A database transaction does not automatically span multiple independent services.
Distributed transactions introduce additional complexity.
Compensating actions can reverse or offset completed steps according to workflow design.
An external action may not be perfectly reversible.
Distributed workflows should model these realities.
An API can expose capability discovery.
Capability discovery tells a consumer which features are supported.
A protocol version can imply capabilities.
A capabilities field can explicitly list them.
Consumers should not assume optional capabilities.
Feature negotiation should produce deterministic behavior.
Unsupported capability requests should be rejected clearly.
Do not silently pretend unsupported behavior succeeded.
Check status codes.
Check types.
Check protocol version.
Check identifiers.
API servers should validate requests.
Both sides have responsibilities.
Serialization converts structured data into a transferable representation.
Deserialization reconstructs structured data from the representation.
Serialization rules matter.
Field names matter.
Field types matter.
Character encoding matters.
Number representation matters.
JSON object member order should not normally be treated as semantic application order unless a separate canonicalization scheme defines it.
Canonical serialization defines one representation for logically equivalent data.
Canonicalization can be important for signatures.
If an API signs request content, signer and verifier must agree on exact signed bytes or canonical representation.
Changing field ordering can change a raw-byte signature.
Protocol design must define signature representation.
Binary APIs require defined byte order.
Network byte order is traditionally big-endian.
Multibyte numeric representation must be specified when raw binary formats are used.
Structure padding from a native C struct should not automatically become a network protocol.
Native memory layout can differ by compiler and architecture.
Serialize protocol fields explicitly.
Do not transmit arbitrary in-memory structures and assume portability.
API protocol fields should have defined sizes.
Integer overflow should be considered.
A claimed payload length should not be trusted before bounds checking.
Malformed lengths can cause memory safety defects.
Allocate based on validated limits.
Protocol parsers should reject truncated input.
Protocol parsers should reject oversized input.
Protocol parsers should reject invalid field values.
Protocol parsers should not read beyond received data.
A parser should know exactly how many bytes remain.
Binary protocols benefit from explicit framing.
A length-prefixed message contains a length followed by the defined payload.
The receiver should validate the length before allocation and reading.
A delimiter-framed protocol uses defined delimiter values.
Framing belongs to the protocol.
TCP does not provide application message framing.
An API implemented over TCP must define framing.
One TCP receive operation does not equal one API message.
A protocol implementation should handle partial reads.
A protocol implementation should handle partial writes.
A protocol implementation should handle disconnects.
A protocol implementation should handle malformed peers.
A peer following the transport protocol can still violate the application protocol.
API compatibility tests can verify old consumers against new providers.
Integration tests verify actual interaction.
End-to-end tests verify complete workflows.
Mock API tests do not prove the real provider behaves identically.
Test real boundaries before claiming interoperability.
API tests should include successful requests.
API tests should include missing fields.
API tests should include invalid field types.
API tests should include oversized input.
API tests should include unauthorized requests.
API tests should include forbidden requests.
API tests should include duplicate requests where relevant.
API tests should include concurrency where relevant.
API tests should include timeout behavior.
API tests should include dependency failure.
API tests should include version mismatch.
API tests should include unsupported methods.
API tests should include unsupported content types.
API tests should verify exact status semantics.
Validate the response content.
Validate the state change.
Validate that unauthorized state did not change.
Negative tests establish boundaries.
A secure API should reject unauthorized behavior.
A deterministic API should reject invalid states predictably.
Fuzz testing supplies generated or mutated inputs to find unexpected behavior.
Protocol parsers can benefit from fuzz testing.
Fuzzing should not be performed against production systems without explicit authorization and controls.
Generated malformed input can create load or unexpected state.
Test environments should isolate destructive testing.
Latency should be measured at meaningful percentiles.
Average latency can hide slow outliers.
Throughput should be measured.
Error rate should be measured.
Payload size should be measured when relevant.
Dependency latency should be measured when relevant.
Do not add caching merely because an endpoint is slow.
Determine why it is slow.
Database queries can be slow.
Serialization can be expensive.
Network latency can dominate.
Lock contention can dominate.
Measure the actual bottleneck.
API availability depends on dependencies.
If an optional service is unavailable, unrelated API operations can remain available.
Do not make every endpoint depend on every subsystem without need.
API startup should validate required configuration.
Missing required credentials should produce explicit failure.
Invalid port configuration should produce explicit failure.
Unsupported protocol version should produce explicit failure.
Defaults should be defined.
An API service should bind only to required interfaces.
Binding to loopback restricts ordinary network exposure to the local host.
Binding externally exposes the service according to network and firewall configuration.
Exposure should be intentional.
Production exposure should use appropriate network controls.
TLS should be used when confidentiality or transport authentication is required.
Certificates should be validated.
Disabling TLS verification to eliminate an error removes an important security property.
API secrets should be rotatable.
Credential rotation should avoid unnecessary downtime where required.
Multiple active keys can support controlled transition.
Old keys should be revoked when transition completes according to policy.
Audit events can record credential identity safely when designed appropriately.
API access should follow least privilege.
A read-only client should not receive write authority.
A monitoring client should not receive administrative authority.
A service should receive only the endpoints and resources required for its mission.
Authorization policy should be enforced server-side.
A client hiding a button is not authorization.
Client-side validation is not server-side security.
The server must enforce protected rules.
An authenticated developer is not automatically an administrator.
An authenticated service is not automatically authorized for every resource.
An authenticated module is not automatically authorized to modify core state.
Authority belongs to policy.
API implementation enforces that policy.
An API should distinguish accepted from completed.
An API should distinguish completed from successful when a completed operation can fail.
An API should distinguish invalid from unauthorized.
An API should distinguish timeout from refusal.
An API should distinguish conflict from server failure.
Precise states improve interoperability.
API consumers should not infer facts not established by the response.
If the server reports pending, the operation is pending.
If the server reports unknown, the result is unknown.
If the server reports failed, do not reinterpret it as probably successful.
If a request times out, outcome can be unknown.
Unknown is a valid operational state.
Evidence should resolve unknown state when possible.
A status endpoint can resolve uncertain asynchronous state.
A transaction identifier can allow querying an operation after timeout.
Request identifiers can allow log correlation.
API behavior should be small.
API behavior should be deterministic where possible.
API behavior should be documented.
API behavior should be bounded.
API behavior should be testable.
API behavior should fail explicitly.
Do not expose functionality merely because implementation already exists.
Do not accept input merely because it can be parsed.
Do not perform an action merely because the caller requested it.
Validate identity.
Validate input.
Validate current state.
Validate the result where required.
Record appropriate evidence.
Do not report success before success criteria are satisfied.
Do not return secret internal information as diagnostic convenience.
Do not silently change protocol versions.
Do not silently reinterpret malformed requests.
Do not silently ignore required fields.
Do not silently invent missing authority.
Do not silently retry non-idempotent operations without understanding the consequences.
Do not confuse transport with protocol.
Do not confuse protocol with application behavior.
Do not confuse authentication with authorization.
Do not confuse HTTP 200 with universal success.
Do not confuse HTTP 202 with completion.
Do not confuse HTTP 401 with authorization success or failure.
Do not confuse HTTP 403 with failed authentication.
Do not confuse an endpoint with a resource.
Do not confuse REST with HTTP.
Do not confuse HTTP with TCP.
Do not confuse HTTPS with application security.
Do not confuse API keys with encryption.
Do not confuse a token with identity itself.
Do not confuse a valid token with unlimited authority.
Do not confuse an opaque identifier with a secret.
Do not confuse an unguessable ID with authorization.
Do not confuse retry with recovery.
Do not confuse timeout with confirmed failure.
Do not confuse queue acceptance with completed work.
Do not confuse webhook delivery with webhook processing.
Do not confuse a valid signature with authorization.
Do not confuse an API implementation with its contract.
Do not confuse undocumented behavior with guaranteed behavior.
An API is a boundary.
Good API engineering makes that boundary explicit.
The provider defines supported behavior.
The consumer uses supported behavior.
The protocol communicates requests and results.
Validation protects the boundary.
Authentication establishes identity when required.
Authorization controls permitted actions.
Versioning manages change.
Testing establishes evidence.
Logging preserves operational evidence.
Precise errors communicate failure.
Bounded behavior prevents uncontrolled resource use.
PHP stands for PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor.
PHP source code is normally executed on a server rather than in the user's web browser.
PHP can generate HTML and other response content dynamically.
PHP code is commonly embedded within HTML documents.
PHP statements normally end with a semicolon.
PHP comments can use # for single-line comments.
PHP comments can use /* and */ for block comments.
PHP variable names are case-sensitive.
PHP is dynamically typed.
PHP supports integer values.
PHP supports boolean values.
PHP supports arrays.
PHP supports objects.
PHP constants represent named values that are not intended to change during execution.
PHP constants can be defined using the const keyword.
PHP constants can also be defined using the define function.
PHP strings can be enclosed in single quotes.
PHP strings can be enclosed in double quotes.
PHP double-quoted strings can interpolate variables.
PHP strings can contain escape sequences when appropriate quoting rules are used.
PHP arrays can contain multiple values.
PHP arrays can use numeric indexes.
PHP arrays can use string keys.
An indexed PHP array normally uses numeric keys.
An associative PHP array uses named string keys.
PHP arrays can contain values of different types.
PHP arrays can contain other arrays.
The count function returns the number of elements in a PHP array.
The foreach statement can iterate over PHP arrays.
PHP supports assignment operators.
PHP supports comparison operators.
PHP supports logical operators.
PHP uses == for loose equality comparison.
PHP uses === for strict equality comparison.
PHP uses != for loose inequality comparison.
PHP uses !== for strict inequality comparison.
Strict comparison in PHP compares both value and type.
PHP supports else and elseif branches.
PHP supports switch statements.
PHP supports match expressions in modern PHP versions.
PHP supports do-while loops.
PHP supports for loops.
PHP supports foreach loops.
The break statement exits a loop or switch.
The continue statement advances execution to the next loop iteration.
PHP functions are declared using the function keyword.
PHP functions can accept parameters.
PHP functions can return values.
A PHP function can explicitly declare parameter types.
A PHP function can explicitly declare a return type.
PHP supports optional function parameters with default values.
PHP supports variable-length argument lists.
PHP functions have their own local variable scope by default.
PHP can include another PHP file using include.
PHP can include another PHP file using require.
PHP provides include_once to prevent repeated inclusion of the same file through that statement.
A failed require produces an error that stops normal execution of the requiring script.
Namespaces help organize PHP classes, interfaces, functions, and constants.
PHP can process HTTP requests received by a web server.
PHP provides superglobal arrays containing request and server information.
The $_GET superglobal contains query-string parameters supplied to PHP.
The $_SERVER superglobal contains server and request information.
The $_COOKIE superglobal contains cookies supplied with the request.
The $_FILES superglobal contains information about uploaded files.
The $_REQUEST superglobal may combine input from multiple request sources depending on PHP configuration.
PHP can send HTTP response headers using the header function.
HTTP headers must normally be sent before response body output begins.
HTML forms can submit data to PHP applications.
Input sanitization modifies or removes unwanted data.
Validation and sanitization are different operations.
Output escaping helps prevent untrusted data from being interpreted as executable or structural content.
HTML output containing untrusted text should be escaped for the HTML context.
The htmlspecialchars function can encode characters with special meaning in HTML.
Security decisions should not rely solely on client-side validation.
PHP provides built-in session support.
A PHP session can preserve server-side state between HTTP requests.
The session_start function starts a new session or resumes an existing session.
PHP session data is accessible through the $_SESSION superglobal after session initialization.
Cookies are stored by the client and returned with applicable later requests.
PHP can create cookies using the setcookie function.
Session identifiers must be protected from unauthorized disclosure and reuse.
Authentication state should not be trusted solely because a client supplied a cookie.
PDO stands for PHP Data Objects.
PDO provides a consistent database-access interface for PHP.
PDO supports prepared statements.
Prepared statements separate SQL structure from bound data values.
Bound parameters should be used instead of concatenating untrusted input into SQL statements.
Database operations can use transactions when supported by the database and storage engine.
A transaction can group related database operations into a single logical unit.
A transaction can be rolled back when its operations must be discarded.
PHP supports object-oriented programming.
PHP classes are declared using the class keyword.
Objects are instances of classes.
PHP classes can contain properties.
PHP classes can contain methods.
PHP constructors use the __construct method.
PHP supports class inheritance.
PHP supports interfaces.
PHP supports traits.
An interface defines a contract that implementing classes must satisfy.
A trait provides reusable method and property declarations for classes.
PHP supports public visibility.
PHP supports protected visibility.
PHP supports private visibility.
PHP supports static class members.
PHP supports abstract classes.
PHP supports final classes and methods.
PHP supports exceptions.
Exceptions can be handled using try and catch.
A finally block can execute after try and catch processing regardless of whether an exception was caught.
Production applications should log useful diagnostic information without unnecessarily exposing internal details to users.
Displaying detailed application errors to public users can disclose sensitive implementation information.
PHP can read files from the filesystem when operating-system permissions allow access.
PHP can write files to the filesystem when operating-system permissions allow access.
The fopen function opens a file or stream.
The fclose function closes an opened file handle.
The file_put_contents function can write data to a file.
Filesystem paths supplied by users must not be trusted without validation.
File upload handling must validate the uploaded content and destination rather than trusting the supplied filename.
PHP application security depends on both application code and runtime configuration.
Untrusted input must not be directly concatenated into SQL queries.
Prepared statements with bound parameters reduce SQL injection risk when used correctly.
Cross-site scripting can occur when untrusted content is inserted into executable browser contexts without appropriate handling.
Cross-site request forgery protections are important for state-changing web requests authenticated through ambient credentials such as cookies.
A CSRF token should be unpredictable and validated by the server.
Passwords should not be stored as plaintext.
PHP provides password_hash for creating password hashes using supported password-hashing algorithms.
PHP provides password_verify for checking a password against a compatible stored password hash.
Authentication establishes who a user is.
Authorization determines what an authenticated identity is permitted to do.
Application secrets should not be exposed through public web content.
MVC stands for Model-View-Controller.
MVC separates application responsibilities into models, views, and controllers.
A view is responsible for presenting application output.
A controller commonly receives an application request and coordinates the work required to produce a response.
Routing determines which application handler or controller receives a request.
MVC implementations vary between frameworks and applications.
MVC is an application architecture pattern rather than a feature of the PHP language itself.
PHP web applications require a mechanism for the web server to execute PHP code.
Apache HTTP Server can execute PHP through supported PHP integration methods.
PHP-FPM provides FastCGI process management for PHP.
PHP runtime configuration is commonly controlled through php.ini and related configuration mechanisms.
Filesystem ownership and permissions affect what PHP processes can read and write.
A PHP process cannot normally write to a filesystem location unless its operating-system identity has appropriate permission.
The json_decode function parses JSON into PHP values.
JSON decoding can produce associative arrays when requested.
JSON parsing errors should be detected rather than assuming supplied JSON is valid.
The DateTime class represents date and time values.
The DateTimeImmutable class provides date and time objects whose modification methods return new objects instead of changing the original object.
PHP application behavior should be deterministic where deterministic behavior is required.
Errors should be handled deliberately rather than silently ignored.
Database failures should not be assumed to have succeeded.
Filesystem operations should be checked for failure.
Security-sensitive operations should fail safely.
Application logs should provide enough information to diagnose failures without exposing secrets.
Configuration should be separated from application behavior where practical.
Code should favor clear behavior over unnecessary complexity.
Documentation should describe the behavior actually implemented by the software.
Git is a distributed version control system.
Version control records changes to files over time.
Git tracks snapshots of project content.
A Git repository contains version-control history and metadata.
A repository is not the same thing as a working directory.
The working tree contains files currently checked out for editing.
The Git index contains the proposed content for the next commit.
The repository contains committed history.
The working tree, index, and committed repository are distinct states.
A file can differ between the index and the current commit.
Understanding these states is fundamental to Git.
git status reports information about the current working tree and index.
git diff normally shows selected differences involving the working tree.
git diff --staged shows changes staged for the next commit.
git add places selected content into the index.
git commit creates a new commit from the staged snapshot.
A commit does not automatically include every modified file.
Only content staged for the commit is included according to Git operation.
An untracked file is not automatically part of a commit.
A tracked file is a file Git knows through repository history or the index.
An untracked file exists in the working tree but is not currently tracked.
git add can begin tracking a new file.
git rm stages removal of a tracked file.
Deleting a tracked file from the filesystem and committing the deletion removes it from later snapshots.
Deleting a file in a later commit does not erase it from earlier Git history.
Historical content does not automatically represent current project state.
A README in an older commit describes the project according to that historical revision.
A sequence of historical versions does not mean all versions are currently active.
Current state and historical state are different concepts.
A commit is identified by an object identifier.
Git object identifiers are derived from cryptographic hashes.
Modern Git can support different repository object hash algorithms depending on repository format.
SHA-1 has historically been Git's primary object hash algorithm.
A Git object identifier identifies repository content and object metadata according to Git's object model.
A commit hash is not an arbitrary version number.
A commit identifier is not an ABI version.
A commit identifier is not an application version.
A commit identifier is not a protocol version.
Different identifiers answer different questions.
A commit normally contains a reference to one or more parent commits.
A root commit has no parent commit.
A normal linear commit has one parent.
A merge commit commonly has multiple parents.
A commit records author information.
A commit records timestamps.
A commit records a commit message.
A commit message explains a change according to developer convention.
A commit message is not executable code.
A commit message does not override the actual committed content.
Repository evidence should be interpreted according to what it actually establishes.
Git objects include blobs.
Git objects include trees.
Git objects include commits.
A blob stores file content.
A blob does not inherently store the filename.
A commit points to a tree representing a project snapshot.
A branch is a movable reference to a commit.
A branch is not a separate copy of every repository file.
Creating a branch is normally inexpensive because the branch begins as a reference.
A branch name identifies a line of development by reference.
The commit graph represents history.
A branch moves when new commits are made on that branch.
The branch name is not part of the commit's immutable content.
A commit can become unreachable from ordinary references.
HEAD identifies the currently checked-out branch or commit context.
HEAD commonly refers symbolically to the current branch.
Detached HEAD means HEAD refers directly to a commit rather than a normal local branch reference.
Detached HEAD is not inherently an error.
Commits can be created while HEAD is detached.
Such commits can become difficult to find if no persistent reference is created.
A branch can preserve a detached-history commit by referencing it.
git branch lists or manipulates branches according to options.
git switch can change branches.
git switch can create a new branch.
git checkout historically performs branch switching and other operations.
git checkout has multiple behaviors.
Commands should be understood before destructive use.
Git can refuse a branch switch when local changes would be overwritten.
That refusal protects uncommitted work.
Uncommitted work should not be discarded merely to make a command succeed.
git log displays commit history according to selected traversal and formatting.
git show displays information about Git objects, commonly commits.
git show on a commit can display its metadata and patch.
git diff compares selected Git states.
A diff shows changes between two selected states.
A diff is not necessarily the complete project.
A patch describes changes.
Git fundamentally stores snapshots even though differences are commonly displayed as patches.
git blame associates lines with commits according to repository history.
git blame does not establish why a line is correct.
git blame does not establish who currently owns a subsystem.
git blame identifies historical line attribution according to Git's algorithm and available history.
Renames and code movement can complicate historical attribution.
History is evidence, not automatic authority.
The current source determines current implementation.
Project policy can determine which current source is authoritative.
Documentation can explain intent.
Runtime execution can provide current operational evidence.
Git history can explain how current state developed.
These evidence sources should not be conflated.
Divergence means each branch contains commits not contained in the other.
A merge combines histories.
git merge integrates another history into the current branch according to Git's merge algorithm.
A three-way merge uses common ancestry to combine changes.
A merge can succeed automatically.
An automatic merge does not prove the resulting program is correct.
A merge can produce syntactically valid but semantically incorrect code.
A merge conflict occurs when Git cannot automatically reconcile selected changes.
A merge conflict requires resolution.
Conflict markers can identify conflicting content in working files.
<<<<<<< commonly begins one side of a textual conflict.
======= commonly separates conflicting sections.
>>>>>>> commonly ends the other side of a textual conflict.
Conflict markers should not remain accidentally in production source.
Resolving a conflict means deliberately choosing or constructing correct resulting content.
Choosing ours automatically is not always correct.
Choosing theirs automatically is not always correct.
Both sides can contain necessary changes.
Neither side can be correct after surrounding architecture changes.
Conflict resolution requires understanding intent.
After resolving a conflict, the resolved content is staged.
The merge can then be committed according to workflow.
A merge conflict is not evidence that either branch is defective.
It is evidence that Git requires human or tool-assisted reconciliation.
git rebase can create new commit identities.
Rebase rewrites history.
Merge preserves both development histories through graph relationships.
Rebase and merge solve related integration problems differently.
Neither is universally superior.
Repository policy should determine appropriate history practices.
Rewriting public shared history can disrupt collaborators.
Rewriting private unpublished history can be useful.
History rewriting should be deliberate.
The amended commit has a new identity.
Amending a pushed shared commit rewrites published history.
git rebase -i can edit a sequence of commits.
Interactive rebase can reorder commits.
Interactive rebase can combine commits.
Interactive rebase can edit commits.
Interactive rebase can remove commits.
These operations rewrite history.
A rewritten history is not byte-for-byte the same history.
A force push can replace a remote branch's reference.
Force pushing can destroy collaborators' visible branch history.
git push --force-with-lease provides additional protection compared with an unconditional force push.
Force operations should be deliberate.
A remote is a named configuration describing another repository location.
origin has no magical authority merely because it is named origin.
A repository can have multiple remotes.
A remote can be hosted on GitHub.
A remote can be hosted on another Git service.
A remote can be another filesystem repository.
Git is not GitHub.
GitHub is a service that hosts Git repositories and provides additional collaboration features.
Git can operate without GitHub.
A GitHub repository uses Git but GitHub provides features beyond Git.
Issues are a GitHub collaboration feature.
Pull requests are a GitHub collaboration feature.
Git itself does not require pull requests.
A remote-tracking branch records the last known state of a remote branch according to local fetch operations.
origin/main is commonly a remote-tracking branch.
main and origin/main are different references.
A local branch can be ahead of a remote-tracking branch.
A local branch can be behind a remote-tracking branch.
git fetch obtains objects and updates applicable remote-tracking references.
git fetch does not normally merge fetched changes into the current local branch.
git pull fetches and then integrates according to configured pull behavior.
git pull can merge.
The exact integration behavior should be known.
Fetch and pull are different operations.
Fetching is useful when changes should be inspected before integration.
git push sends objects and updates remote references when permitted.
A successful push means the remote accepted the requested reference update according to protocol and permissions.
A successful push does not prove deployed software has changed.
Pushing source code does not automatically rebuild binaries unless automation performs that action.
Pushing source code does not automatically restart services.
Pushing source code does not automatically migrate databases.
Repository state and deployed state must remain distinguishable.
git clone normally configures a remote named origin.
A clone is a repository in its own right.
A distributed Git repository contains local history.
Network connectivity is required only for operations involving remote systems.
A remote outage does not prevent ordinary local Git operations.
A GitHub outage does not destroy a local repository.
Distributed version control provides multiple copies of repository history.
Multiple copies do not replace a deliberate backup policy.
A corrupted or force-rewritten remote can propagate undesirable state.
A tag is a reference commonly used to identify important commits.
A lightweight tag directly references an object.
An annotated tag is a Git object containing metadata and a reference.
Annotated tags can contain a message.
Annotated tags can be cryptographically signed.
Tags are commonly used for releases.
A tag name can contain a software version.
The tag name and software's internal version are still different pieces of information.
A release tag named v1.5.0 does not change a program's compiled version constant by itself.
The source should define its own required version information.
Release automation can verify that tag and source versions agree.
A tag can be moved or deleted by someone with sufficient repository authority.
Signed tags can provide authenticity evidence.
A cryptographic signature can verify that a tag was signed by a key.
Cryptographic identity and organizational authority are different.
A release can reference a commit.
A release can contain built artifacts.
Source commits and built artifacts are different objects.
A binary should be traceable to its source revision when reproducibility or auditability matters.
Build metadata can record a commit identifier.
A commit identifier embedded in a binary can help identify source provenance.
An embedded commit identifier does not prove the binary was honestly built from that commit.
A reproducible build produces identical output from defined identical inputs and environment according to the build specification.
Source integrity and build integrity are separate concerns.
.gitignore specifies patterns for intentionally untracked files.
.gitignore does not remove already tracked files from history.
Adding a secret file to .gitignore after committing it does not remove the secret from Git history.
A tracked file remains tracked even if a later .gitignore pattern matches it.
git rm --cached can remove a path from the index while preserving a working-tree copy according to command usage.
Credentials exposed in Git should be rotated when required.
History rewriting can remove sensitive data from ordinary repository history.
History rewriting does not guarantee every existing clone or cache has lost the old data.
Secret rotation is more reliable than assuming every copy was erased.
.gitignore should exclude generated files when appropriate.
.gitignore should exclude local configuration when appropriate.
.gitignore should exclude secrets when those secrets should never be versioned.
Repository policy should define which artifacts belong under version control.
Build output can often be reproduced and therefore can often be excluded.
Generated artifacts can be committed when project requirements require them.
Dependency directories can be excluded when dependencies are reproducibly restored.
Rules should follow project architecture.
.gitattributes defines path-specific Git attributes.
.gitattributes can control text handling.
.gitattributes can control line-ending normalization.
.gitattributes can identify binary files.
.gitattributes can configure diff behavior.
.gitattributes can configure merge behavior.
.gitattributes can configure export behavior.
Line endings matter for cross-platform development.
LF is a line-feed line ending commonly used on Unix-like systems.
CRLF is a carriage-return plus line-feed line ending commonly used on Windows.
Git can normalize line endings according to configuration and attributes.
Unexpected line-ending conversion can make entire files appear modified.
Line-ending conversion can affect scripts.
Line-ending conversion can affect byte-sensitive files.
