A hotspot occurs when disproportionate workload targets one partition or node.
Range partitioning assigns key ranges.
Consistent hashing can reduce remapping when nodes change according to its algorithm.
Measure actual workload.
Replication and partitioning are different.
Partitioning divides data.
Replication copies data.
Rebalancing moves ownership or copies when topology changes.
Rebalancing consumes resources.
Rebalancing can affect latency.
Rebalancing should preserve consistency guarantees.
Do not treat node addition as free capacity immediately.
Data can require transfer before the node is useful.
A cluster is a group of cooperating nodes.
Cluster health is not simply the sum of process health.
A cluster can have all processes running while lacking quorum.
A cluster can have quorum while one replica is unhealthy.
A cluster can be operational in degraded state.
Health should describe actual capability.
A node can be alive.
A node can be reachable.
A node can be qualified.
A node can be synchronized.
A node can be voting.
A node can be leader.
These are different properties.
Do not collapse them into one boolean without reason.
Heartbeats are periodic messages used to indicate continuing communication or liveness according to protocol semantics.
Missing one heartbeat does not necessarily mean a node failed.
Network delay can delay heartbeats.
Scheduling delay can delay heartbeats.
Failure detectors operate with uncertainty.
A failure detector can suspect a node.
Distributed systems often cannot perfectly distinguish a failed node from an unreachable node.
Failure detection thresholds trade detection speed against false suspicion.
Very long timeouts reduce false suspicion but delay recovery.
Choose thresholds according to requirements and observed network conditions.
Health checks should be bounded.
Health checks should not create significant load.
A health check should test the capability it claims to test.
TCP connect success proves a listener accepted or completed transport establishment according to the observed result.
TCP connect success does not prove application protocol correctness.
Application operation success provides stronger evidence for that operation.
Qualification should be staged.
Discovery can use configuration.
Discovery can use a registry.
Discovery information can become stale.
Clients must still handle connection failure.
Weighted distribution can represent unequal capacity.
Consistent hashing can preserve affinity for selected keys.
One request can be much more expensive than another.
A load balancer can become a single point of failure if not made resilient according to requirements.
Redundancy should follow availability requirements.
Redundancy can itself fail.
Two identical redundant systems can share the same defect.
Shared dependencies can defeat redundancy.
Examples include shared power.
Examples include shared network infrastructure.
Examples include shared software defects.
Examples include shared configuration.
Redundancy should consider failure independence.
Availability requires a definition of successful service.
A server returning incorrect responses is not meaningfully available for the affected operation.
High availability requires architecture and operations.
Replication alone does not create high availability.
Failover moves responsibility from one component to another.
Automatic failover should have defined authority rules.
A resource accepts operations only from the current valid ownership epoch according to the design.
Fencing is stronger than merely asking the old owner to stop.
The old owner can be partitioned and unable to receive the stop request.
Leases provide temporary authority.
A lease expires unless renewed.
Clock assumptions matter to lease correctness.
A node whose lease expired should stop acting under that lease.
A partitioned node might not know immediately that another node obtained newer authority.
Fencing can protect the shared resource from stale owners.
Messages from an older term can be rejected.
Terms help prevent stale leaders from influencing current state.
Monotonically increasing generations are useful for distinguishing newer authority.
A generation number should come from the authority mechanism, not arbitrary local clocks.
Snapshots capture system state at a defined point or logical position.
Snapshots can accelerate recovery.
A recovering node can load a snapshot and replay later log entries.
Snapshot creation should correspond to a defined committed state.
A snapshot should not silently include uncommitted state when protocol semantics forbid it.
Snapshots require integrity validation.
Snapshots can become corrupted.
Snapshots can become incompatible with newer software versions.
Snapshot format should be versioned where necessary.
Compaction reduces historical storage according to protocol requirements.
A consensus log can be compacted while a separate audit system preserves required historical records.
Do not delete governance or historical records merely because operational state no longer needs them.
Checkpointing records recoverable progress.
A checkpoint should identify exactly what state has been durably completed.
Reporting a checkpoint before durable completion can cause skipped work after restart.
Reporting it too late can cause repeated work.
Repeated work should be safe where possible.
A node can terminate between any two operations.
A node can terminate after writing data but before acknowledging it.
A node can terminate after sending a message but before recording that it sent it.
A node can terminate after receiving a message but before processing it.
A node can terminate after processing but before acknowledging it.
Each boundary can create duplicate or uncertain work.
Persistent state should establish recovery facts.
Write-ahead logging records intended or completed changes in an order supporting recovery according to the storage design.
Durability should be established before reporting durable success.
Buffering can delay physical persistence.
A successful write system call does not universally mean bytes are safely persisted to nonvolatile media.
Storage durability semantics depend on operating system, filesystem, hardware, and synchronization operations.
Define what committed means.
Committed can mean written to one process memory.
Committed can mean written to local filesystem cache.
Committed can mean replicated to quorum.
These are very different guarantees.
Use precise terminology.
A distributed transaction identifier can correlate one logical operation across components.
Correlation improves observability.
Tracing records the path of requests through distributed components.
A trace can contain spans.
A span represents one timed operation within a trace.
Tracing provides evidence about execution.
Distributed logs can be difficult to order using timestamps alone.
Clock skew can make later causal events appear earlier in wall-clock logs.
Correlation identifiers and causal context improve reconstruction.
A timeline should distinguish observed local time from established causal order.
Observability should include component identity.
Observability should include result.
Observability should include relevant latency.
Observability should include retries where useful.
Observability should include peer state transitions where useful.
Do not log secrets merely to improve observability.
Distributed failures require precise classification.
DNS failure means something different from protocol rejection.
Protocol rejection means something different from authentication failure.
Authentication failure means something different from authorization failure.
Replication lag means something different from corruption.
Classify failures accurately.
A cascading failure occurs when one component failure causes increased load or failure in other components.
Retries can cause cascading failure.
Shared resource exhaustion can cause cascading failure.
Synchronous dependency chains can cause cascading failure.
Circuit breakers can limit repeated calls to failing dependencies.
Bulkheads can isolate resource pools.
Load shedding can reject excess work deliberately.
Failing a bounded amount of work can preserve overall service.
Load shedding should follow priority and policy.
Do not silently discard important work.
A bounded queue should define behavior when full.
It can block producers.
It can reject new work.
It can drop selected work where permitted.
It can persist overflow.
The correct policy depends on workload semantics.
Backpressure communicates that downstream capacity is exhausted.
Ignoring backpressure can cause memory exhaustion.
A distributed system should have bounded resources.
Bound queue size.
Bound retries.
Bound connection count.
Bound worker count.
Bound message size.
Bound timeouts.
Unbounded resource use becomes an availability risk.
A service-level objective is commonly abbreviated SLO.
An SLO defines a target level of service performance or reliability.
An SLI is a Service Level Indicator.
An SLI is a measured property used to evaluate service behavior.
An SLA is a Service Level Agreement.
An SLA is an agreement containing service commitments.
SLI, SLO, and SLA are different concepts.
Do not build complex high-availability machinery for a system that does not require it.
Do not omit redundancy where failure would violate required availability.
Complexity should be proportional to requirements.
Network location is not sufficient authentication.
An internal network should not automatically be treated as fully trusted.
Nodes should authenticate when the threat model requires it.
Confidentiality should be provided when required.
Authorization should restrict node operations.
A valid peer should not automatically have every capability.
Protocol parsers should treat peer input as untrusted.
Authenticated peers can be defective.
Replay attacks repeat previously valid messages.
Protocols should prevent harmful replay where required.
Sequence numbers can help.
Nonces can help.
Timestamps can help when clock assumptions are acceptable.
Persistent replay state can help.
Signatures alone do not automatically prevent replay.
A previously valid signed message remains cryptographically valid unless the protocol also establishes freshness or uniqueness.
Message identity should be defined.
A message hash can identify exact message bytes.
A request ID can identify logical requests.
A transaction ID can identify state transitions.
Identifiers should not be reused ambiguously.
A valid signature establishes only what the signing scheme and trusted key establish.
It does not prove the content is authorized.
Authorization and protocol validation still apply.
Distributed systems should reject malformed state transitions even when messages are correctly signed.
A crash-fault-tolerant protocol should not be described as Byzantine tolerant unless it actually provides those guarantees.
Failure assumptions are part of protocol correctness.
The number of failures a protocol can tolerate depends on its design and membership size.
Do not invent fault-tolerance thresholds.
Derive them from the protocol.
A majority-based crash-fault-tolerant consensus group generally requires a majority of voting members to make progress.
Losing quorum prevents progress.
Preventing progress can preserve safety.
Restoring quorum should follow membership and recovery procedures.
Do not create a fake quorum by simply lowering the required count during an outage unless the protocol explicitly defines a safe reconfiguration procedure.
Membership is consensus state in many consensus systems.
Reconfiguration therefore requires care.
Joint consensus or equivalent mechanisms can safely transition membership in protocols that define them.
Changing configuration files independently on nodes does not automatically create a coherent cluster membership change.
Distributed configuration should have authority.
Configuration drift occurs when nodes expected to share configuration differ.
Configuration hashes can help detect drift.
Version reporting can help detect drift.
Startup qualification can reject incompatible configuration.
A node should not silently join a network with the wrong network identifier.
A development network should not accidentally communicate with a production network.
Network identifiers should be explicit.
Genesis identifiers can distinguish independently initialized chains or replicated histories where that architecture uses genesis state.
Protocol identity should be validated before exchanging authoritative state.
Capability negotiation allows nodes to identify supported optional features.
A capability should have stable semantics.
Optional capabilities should not change consensus-visible behavior unless the protocol defines how all required participants agree on the behavior.
Consensus rules should not depend on which local optional module happens to be installed.
Consensus-visible behavior must be common.
Platform-specific implementations must produce identical consensus-visible results when platform independence is required.
Different operating systems can use different system APIs.
Different compilers can generate different machine code.
Different hardware can execute differently internally.
Those differences must not change protocol-required results.
Serialization should be platform independent.
Integer sizes should be explicit.
Byte order should be explicit.
String encoding should be explicit.
Floating-point behavior should be avoided or tightly defined where exact cross-platform consensus requires it.
Filesystem path syntax should not enter consensus-visible data unless normalized by protocol definition.
Local timezones should not enter consensus-visible calculations.
Environment-specific values should not enter consensus-visible calculations.
Consensus should consume agreed protocol data.
Consensus output should be deterministic.
Distributed systems can use gossip protocols.
Gossip disseminates information through peer-to-peer exchanges.
Each node communicates selected information to other nodes.
Information propagates through the network over time.
Gossip can scale well.
Gossip does not inherently provide immediate global consistency.
Gossip can distribute membership information.
Gossip can distribute state summaries.
Gossip can distribute events.
Gossip data should still be validated.
A peer saying another peer exists does not establish that peer's trustworthiness.
Peer reputation can influence connection decisions according to protocol policy.
Reputation should not override cryptographic or consensus validation.
Probabilistic routing and deterministic validation can coexist.
Discovery can be probabilistic.
Acceptance can remain deterministic.
Bloom filters provide probabilistic membership tests.
A probabilistic data structure should not be used where false positives violate correctness unless a definitive validation follows.
Probable is not determinate.
Heuristics can guide work.
Heuristics should not replace required proof.
Caching can reduce distributed load.
Cache invalidation can occur through expiration.
Cache invalidation can occur through explicit events.
Cache invalidation can occur through version checks.
A cache hit should not be treated as authoritative if the cache is permitted to be stale.
Negative caching stores the absence of a result.
Negative caches can hide newly created data until expiration.
Use negative caching only when its staleness is acceptable.
Content delivery networks distribute cached content geographically.
A CDN is not automatically an authoritative application datastore.
Edge caches can return stale content according to cache policy.
Distributed storage can use erasure coding.
Erasure coding stores redundant encoded fragments allowing recovery from selected fragment loss.
Erasure coding and replication are different redundancy techniques.
Erasure coding stores encoded fragments.
Tradeoffs include storage overhead, repair cost, and read complexity.
Happy-path testing is insufficient.
Disconnect peers.
Duplicate messages.
Reorder messages where the transport or protocol can permit it.
Restart nodes.
Restart leaders.
Exhaust connections.
Introduce stale replicas.
Restore quorum.
Test incompatible protocol versions.
Test malformed messages.
Test duplicate requests.
Test replay.
Test partial writes where applicable.
Test recovery from persistent state.
Test deterministic results across supported platforms.
A chaos test deliberately introduces failures to observe system behavior.
Chaos testing should be controlled.
Chaos testing should not be performed against critical production systems without explicit authorization and safeguards.
Fault injection should test defined hypotheses.
For consensus, test safety.
For recovery, test durable state restoration.
For replication, test convergence.
For retries, test idempotency.
For versioning, test compatibility.
A distributed soak test runs the system for an extended period.
Soak testing can reveal reconnect problems.
Soak testing can reveal accumulated queue growth.
Soak testing can reveal state drift.
Soak testing can reveal timing-dependent failures.
A long-running test is useful only when relevant evidence is collected.
Monitor connections.
Monitor retries.
Monitor memory.
Monitor queue depth.
Monitor consensus state.
Monitor rejected messages.
Monitor state divergence.
End-to-end testing follows an operation across all required distributed boundaries.
Define the final success condition.
Observe each required stage.
Submission can pass.
Admission can pass.
Replication can pass.
Commit can pass.
Report exactly which stage failed.
Requests have states.
Transactions have states.
Jobs have states.
Explicit states improve diagnosis.
State transitions should be logged where operationally useful.
Invalid transitions should be rejected.
A reconnect should not silently duplicate logical state.
Connection identity and logical peer identity are different.
A peer can disconnect and reconnect.
The new connection can represent the same logical peer.
Persistent peer state should be keyed by stable identity where required, not transient socket identity.
Connection churn consumes resources.
Connection churn can increase latency.
Connection churn can fill logs.
Connection churn can create duplicate handshakes.
Connection churn can degrade user experience.
Persistent connections can reduce repeated setup cost.
Persistent connections require liveness detection and cleanup.
Reconnect behavior should use bounded backoff.
A healthy persistent connection should not be destroyed without reason.
A connection pool can reuse network connections.
Stale pooled connections must be detected.
Pool capacity should be bounded.
A pool can become exhausted.
Leaked connections can exhaust the pool.
DNS is a distributed naming system.
DNS names can resolve to multiple addresses.
DNS responses can be cached.
DNS TTL values influence caching.
A hostname should not be assumed to map permanently to one machine.
Resolve failures precisely.
Service names provide logical addressing.
Logical names reduce hard-coded physical addresses.
Failover order should be deterministic where required.
Selection policy should be explicit.
A fallback endpoint should not silently become authoritative merely because the preferred endpoint is unavailable.
Authority and reachability are different.
A distributed system should degrade explicitly.
Healthy means defined required capabilities are operational.
Degraded means the system remains operational with defined capability loss.
Unavailable means required service cannot currently be provided.
Unknown means sufficient evidence is unavailable.
These states should not be collapsed unnecessarily.
A degraded node can still serve safe operations.
A node without quorum can potentially serve non-authoritative diagnostic information while refusing authoritative writes.
Do not permit state-changing operations merely to appear available when authority cannot be established.
Distributed systems should preserve evidence.
Record peer transitions.
Record leadership transitions.
Record term or epoch changes.
Record quorum restoration.
Record incompatible versions.
Record committed state transitions.
Record recovery events.
Record repeated failures.
Operational logs can have bounded retention.
Audit history and operational logs have different purposes.
Do not erase historical facts merely because current state changed.
A node that was previously leader remains historically the leader for that period even after leadership changes.
Recovery should reconstruct current state from valid persistent evidence.
Recovery should not invent missing committed state.
If required state cannot be established, report the uncertainty.
Do not guess.
A distributed system can be correct while unavailable.
A distributed system can be available while incorrect.
Correctness and availability are different.
A distributed system can be fast while unsafe.
Performance and safety are different.
A distributed system can have many replicas while having poor durability.
Replica count and durability guarantees are different.
A distributed system can have encryption while having broken authorization.
Transport security and authority are different.
A distributed system can have consensus while application logic remains wrong.
Consensus agrees on values.
Consensus does not establish that the agreed application decision is factually correct unless correctness is encoded in validation rules.
Consensus does not replace authorization.
Consensus does not replace deterministic execution.
Consensus does not replace backups.
Consensus does not replace monitoring.
Consensus does not replace governance.
Do not confuse discovery with admission.
Do not confuse authentication with authorization.
Do not confuse replication with consensus.
Do not confuse replication with backup.
Do not confuse consensus with voting in general.
Do not confuse quorum with every node.
Do not confuse leader with permanent owner.
Do not confuse network address with identity.
Do not confuse newest timestamp with authority.
Do not confuse wall-clock order with causal order.
Do not confuse timeout with confirmed failure.
Do not confuse retry with recovery.
Do not confuse receipt with completion.
Do not confuse replication with commitment.
Do not confuse queue acceptance with processing.
Do not confuse asynchronous with unreliable.
Do not confuse stale data with corrupted data.
Do not confuse split brain with ordinary replication lag.
Do not confuse a heartbeat with proof of complete health.
Do not confuse process liveness with cluster health.
Do not confuse all processes running with quorum.
Do not confuse a majority of currently reachable nodes with a valid quorum unless membership defines them as voters.
Do not confuse configuration discovery with consensus membership.
Do not confuse cryptographic validity with authorization.
Do not confuse nondeterministic scheduling with permission for nondeterministic consensus output.
Do not confuse probabilistic evidence with deterministic validation.
Identify the protocol.
Identify the persistent state.
Identify the success condition.
Identify the failure conditions.
Identify what happens if a message is lost.
Identify what happens if a message is duplicated.
Identify what happens if a message is delayed.
Identify what happens if quorum is lost.
Identify what happens if quorum returns.
Identify what happens if two participants claim authority.
Identify what happens if persistent state is stale.
Identify what happens if the operation is retried.
Then test those conditions.
When a distributed failure occurs, establish facts.
Which node observed the failure?
At what local time?
Which connection was involved?
Which protocol stage was active?
Which request identifier was involved?
Which state existed afterward?
Was the operation submitted?
Was it received?
Was it committed?
Was it applied?
Was the response received?
Do not collapse these questions into "did it work?"
Distributed systems require precise boundaries.
Distributed systems require precise authority.
Distributed systems require bounded retries.
Distributed systems require deterministic validation.
Distributed systems require evidence.
Keep the protocol small.
Keep messages bounded.
Keep states explicit.
Keep authority explicit.
Keep membership explicit.
Keep persistence explicit.
Keep retries bounded.
Keep failure visible.
Keep platform-specific implementation outside common protocol semantics.
Do not add distribution merely because multiple machines are available.
A single machine is simpler than a cluster.
Local communication is simpler than network communication.
Distribution should solve a real requirement.
Once distribution is required, design for partial failure from the beginning.
The network is not guaranteed to deliver the result when expected.
The remote machine is not guaranteed to remain available.
Messages are not guaranteed to arrive when expected.
Participants are not guaranteed to observe state simultaneously.
Correct distributed software does not pretend these conditions do not exist.
C# is a general-purpose programming language.
C# is pronounced C sharp.
C# is a statically typed programming language.
C# is commonly used with the .NET platform.
C# and .NET are related but are not the same thing.
C# is the programming language.
.NET provides runtimes, libraries, tools, and application frameworks used by C# and other languages.
C# source files commonly use the .cs filename extension.
C# is case-sensitive.
C# supports procedural programming.
C# supports generic programming.
Modern .NET normally compiles C# source into Common Intermediate Language and associated metadata.
Common Intermediate Language is commonly abbreviated CIL.
The .NET runtime can use just-in-time compilation to translate CIL into native machine code.
Just-in-time compilation is commonly abbreviated JIT.
.NET can also support ahead-of-time compilation in applicable deployment models.
Ahead-of-time compilation is commonly abbreviated AOT.
The Common Language Runtime is commonly abbreviated CLR.
The CLR is the execution environment used by .NET implementations that provide the CLR.
The runtime provides services including managed memory, type handling, exception handling, and execution support.
Managed code executes under services provided by the .NET runtime.
Managed code does not mean that application logic is automatically correct or secure.
A C# application can still use unmanaged resources.
C# can interoperate with native code.
C# blocks are enclosed by braces.
C# line comments begin with //.
C# block comments begin with /* and end with */.
XML documentation comments commonly begin with ///.
An identifier names a program entity.
C# identifiers are case-sensitive.
A variable stores a value according to its type.
A local variable must be definitely assigned before its value is read.
C# is statically typed.
The type of an expression is determined according to C# language rules.
The var keyword requests local variable type inference.
A variable declared with var still has a statically determined type.
The dynamic type defers certain operations to runtime binding.
dynamic and var have different meanings.
bool represents Boolean values.
sbyte represents a signed 8-bit integer.
short represents a signed 16-bit integer.
int represents a signed 32-bit integer.
long represents a signed 64-bit integer.
ulong represents an unsigned 64-bit integer.
char represents a UTF-16 code unit.
decimal represents a decimal floating-point value designed for high-precision decimal calculations.
string represents a sequence of characters.
object is the root type from which other .NET types ultimately derive according to the .NET type system.
C# distinguishes value types from reference types.
A reference type variable holds a reference to an object or can hold null where nullable.
Enumerations are value types.
Classes are reference types.
Arrays are reference types.
Delegates are reference types.
Strings are reference types.
A class defines a reference type.
A struct defines a value type.
The class keyword declares a class.
The struct keyword declares a structure.
The enum keyword declares an enumeration.
An enum defines a set of named constants backed by an integral type.
A namespace organizes related types.
Namespaces help prevent naming collisions.
The namespace keyword declares a namespace.
A using directive does not copy a library into a program.
Types can contain fields.
Types can contain properties.
Types can contain methods.
Types can contain events.
A field stores data associated with a type or object.
A property exposes value access through accessors.
A property can define a get accessor.
A property can define a set accessor.
An init accessor permits initialization in applicable object initialization contexts while restricting later assignment.
A method defines executable behavior.
A method can accept parameters.
A method can return a value.
C# passes arguments by value by default.
Passing a reference-type variable by value passes the reference value by value.
Passing a reference value by value does not copy the referenced object.
The ref modifier supports passing an argument by reference.
The out modifier supports passing a variable by reference for output.
The in modifier supports readonly reference passing in applicable contexts.
A ref parameter and an ordinary value parameter have different semantics.
A constructor has the same name as its containing type.
A class can define multiple constructors.
The new operator can create an object or value according to the target type.
Creating a managed object normally allocates storage managed by the runtime.
C# uses automatic garbage collection for managed memory.
Garbage collection reclaims managed objects that are no longer reachable according to runtime rules.
Garbage collection does not automatically release every type of unmanaged resource promptly.
IDisposable defines a standard pattern for releasing resources.
The Dispose method releases resources according to an object's implementation.
The using statement can ensure IDisposable resources are disposed when control leaves the applicable scope.
The using declaration can associate disposal with the enclosing scope.
Garbage collection and deterministic resource disposal solve different problems.
A finalizer can participate in cleanup of unmanaged resources when a type requires one.
Finalizers are not a substitute for deterministic disposal.
A finalizer's execution time is not deterministic.
The public access modifier allows access according to public accessibility rules.
The private access modifier restricts access to the containing type.
The internal access modifier provides access within the same assembly according to C# accessibility rules.
protected internal and private protected provide combined accessibility forms.
Class members are private by default when no applicable access modifier is supplied.
A namespace-level type is internal by default when no applicable access modifier is supplied.
C# supports inheritance.
A class can derive from another class.
A class can have only one direct base class.
A class can implement multiple interfaces.
Inheritance and interface implementation are different mechanisms.
The base keyword accesses members of the base class in applicable contexts.
The virtual modifier permits applicable members to be overridden.
The override modifier provides a new implementation of an inherited virtual member.
The abstract modifier can declare incomplete behavior requiring implementation by a derived non-abstract type.
An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly.
A sealed override prevents further overriding of that member.
Polymorphism allows code to operate through a base type or interface while runtime dispatch selects applicable overridden behavior.
An interface defines a contract that implementing types can satisfy.
The interface keyword declares an interface.
Interfaces are commonly used to separate contracts from implementations.
Composition allows one object to contain or reference other objects.
Composition and inheritance model different relationships.
Composition can reduce coupling when inheritance is not required.
C# supports method overloading.
Method overloading allows methods to share a name when their parameter signatures distinguish the overloads.
C# does not permit arbitrary new operator symbols to be invented.
The assignment operator is =.
The equality operator is ==.
The inequality operator is !=.
The behavior of == depends on the types being compared and any applicable overloaded operator.
ReferenceEquals can determine whether two reference variables refer to the same object.
Value equality and reference identity are different concepts.
The Equals method participates in value equality conventions.
Types overriding Equals should follow the associated equality contract.
GetHashCode should be consistent with Equals when objects are used in hash-based collections.
The relational operators include <, <=, >, and >= where supported.
The logical AND operator is &&.
The logical OR operator is ||.
The logical NOT operator is !.
The bitwise AND operator is &.
The bitwise XOR operator is ^.
The bitwise complement operator is ~.
&& uses short-circuit evaluation.
|| uses short-circuit evaluation.
The null-coalescing operator is ??.
The null-coalescing assignment operator is ??=.
The member access operator is a period.
The null-forgiving operator is ! in applicable nullable-reference contexts.
A nullable value type can represent either a value or null.
The syntax int? represents a nullable int.
Nullable reference types provide compile-time annotations and analysis concerning possible null references.
Nullable reference type analysis does not make null reference errors impossible.
A non-nullable reference annotation expresses an intended nullability contract to compiler analysis.
A nullable reference annotation such as string? indicates that null is an expected value according to nullable analysis.
A NullReferenceException can occur when code dereferences a null reference.
Null input should be validated where an interface does not permit it.
The is operator can test type and pattern relationships.
A switch expression can produce a value according to matching arms.
The if statement conditionally executes code.
The else clause provides an alternative branch.
The switch statement selects behavior among cases.
The foreach statement iterates over an enumerable sequence.
The while statement repeats while its condition remains true.
The continue statement advances to the next applicable loop iteration.
The return statement exits a method and can return a value.
The throw statement raises an exception.
C# supports exception handling.
A catch block handles matching exceptions.
A finally block executes cleanup behavior when control leaves the associated try construct according to language rules.
Exceptions can propagate through multiple method calls.
System.Exception is the base type for standard exception objects.
Exception messages can provide diagnostic information.
Sensitive information should not be exposed through exception messages presented to untrusted users.
An exception can contain an inner exception.
C# supports generics.
Generics allow types and methods to operate with type parameters.
A generic type can be reused with different type arguments.
List<T> is a generic collection type.
Dictionary<TKey,TValue> is a generic key-value collection.
Queue<T> is a generic first-in-first-out collection.
Generic constraints restrict the types that can be supplied as type arguments.
Generics differ from storing all values as object.
Boxing converts a value type to an object or implemented interface representation.
Unboxing extracts a value type from a compatible boxed representation.
Unboxing requires compatible type information.
Unnecessary boxing can create allocations and overhead.
Accessing an array outside its valid bounds throws an IndexOutOfRangeException.
The Length property reports an array's number of elements.
List<T> provides a dynamically sized sequence.
The Count property reports the number of elements in many collection types.
Count and capacity are different concepts.
A collection's capacity can exceed its current element count.
Dictionary<TKey,TValue> associates keys with values.
Dictionary keys should have stable equality and hash behavior while used as keys.
Collections are not automatically thread-safe.
System.Collections.Concurrent provides concurrent collection types.
C# supports delegates.
A delegate represents references to methods with a compatible signature.
Delegates can reference static or instance methods.
Action represents a delegate that returns no value.
Func represents a delegate that returns a value.
Predicate<T> represents a delegate returning a Boolean result for a value.
Lambda expressions use the => syntax.
A lambda can capture variables from its surrounding scope.
Captured variables can outlive the immediate execution of the declaring method depending on the resulting closure.
Closures can introduce additional object lifetimes and allocations.
C# events provide a publish-and-subscribe mechanism based on delegates.
The event keyword restricts direct invocation and assignment according to event semantics.
Subscribers register handlers with an event.
Publishers raise events.
C# supports Language Integrated Query.
Language Integrated Query is commonly abbreviated LINQ.
LINQ operations can be written using method syntax.
Where filters a sequence.
Select projects elements into another form.
OrderBy orders elements according to a key.
GroupBy groups elements according to a key.
Any determines whether a sequence contains an element satisfying applicable conditions.
All determines whether all applicable elements satisfy a condition.
First returns the first applicable element and throws when no element exists according to its contract.
FirstOrDefault returns the first applicable element or a default value when none exists.
Single requires exactly one applicable element according to its contract.
SingleOrDefault permits zero or one applicable element according to its contract.
LINQ queries can use deferred execution.
Deferred execution means evaluation can occur when results are enumerated rather than when the query is constructed.
Not every LINQ operation uses deferred execution.
ToList materializes a sequence into a List<T>.
ToArray materializes a sequence into an array.
Repeated enumeration can repeat underlying work for deferred queries.
A LINQ expression used with a database provider is not necessarily executed as ordinary in-memory C# code.
The async modifier identifies methods that can contain await according to language rules.
async and await do not automatically create a new thread.
Task represents an asynchronous operation.
Task<T> represents an asynchronous operation that produces a value.
An async method commonly returns Task, Task<T>, ValueTask, or ValueTask<T> according to its requirements.
An async void method should generally be limited to scenarios such as event handlers where the contract requires void.
Blocking synchronously on asynchronous operations can cause performance or synchronization problems.
Asynchronous I/O can allow a thread to perform other work while waiting for external operations.
CancellationToken communicates cancellation requests to cooperating operations.
Cancellation is cooperative.
A CancellationToken does not forcibly terminate arbitrary executing code.
CancellationTokenSource creates and controls cancellation tokens.
Timeouts and cancellation are related but distinct concepts.
C# supports multithreading.
Thread represents an operating-system thread abstraction provided by .NET.
The thread pool manages reusable worker threads.
Task-based programming commonly uses the thread pool for CPU-bound scheduled work.
lock provides mutual exclusion around a critical section.
The lock statement uses a synchronization object.
Code should not normally lock on publicly accessible objects.
Deadlock can occur when execution paths wait indefinitely for resources held by one another.
Shared mutable state requires synchronization when accessed concurrently.
Interlocked provides atomic operations for supported scenarios.
volatile affects certain memory-access semantics but is not a replacement for all synchronization.
A race condition occurs when program results depend improperly on timing or ordering between operations.
Repeated string concatenation can create multiple intermediate strings.
StringBuilder provides mutable string-building functionality.
String interpolation can embed expressions into formatted strings.
An interpolated string commonly begins with $.
The syntax $"Hello {name}" is an interpolated string.
String comparisons should use an appropriate comparison rule.
StringComparison can specify ordinal or culture-sensitive comparison behavior.
Ordinal comparison compares string data according to ordinal rules.
Culture-sensitive comparisons can vary according to cultural rules.
Security-sensitive identifiers should not casually depend on culture-sensitive string comparison.
C# supports records.
A record is a type designed to support value-oriented data modeling.
Records can provide compiler-generated value equality behavior.
Record classes are reference types.
Records do not make contained mutable objects immutable.
with expressions can create modified copies of records and other supported types.
C# supports tuples.
Tuple elements can have names.
Tuples are useful for small grouped return values when a dedicated domain type is unnecessary.
C# supports extension methods.
Extension methods do not actually add instance members to the extended type.
Extension method resolution follows compile-time rules.
Attributes attach metadata to program elements.
Attributes derive from System.Attribute.
Reflection can inspect metadata and type information at runtime.
Reflection can discover types and members.
Heavy reflection can increase complexity and runtime cost.
Assembly metadata describes compiled .NET components.
An assembly is a deployment and versioning unit containing .NET code and metadata.
A .NET .dll is not necessarily equivalent in binary interface behavior to an unmanaged native DLL.
Managed assemblies contain metadata understood by the .NET runtime.
Namespaces and assemblies are different concepts.
An assembly can contain types from multiple namespaces.
NuGet is a package-management system commonly used by .NET projects.
A NuGet package can contain libraries, build assets, analyzers, tools, or other package resources.
Dependencies should be selected deliberately.
The .NET SDK provides command-line development tools.
dotnet is the primary command-line driver for modern .NET SDK workflows.
dotnet run builds and runs an applicable project.
dotnet publish prepares an application for deployment.
A .csproj file describes a C# project using MSBuild project format.
MSBuild is the build system used by .NET project tooling.
Project files can define target frameworks.
A target framework identifies the .NET API surface and runtime family targeted by a project.
Target frameworks are commonly represented using target framework monikers.
A project can target more than one framework.
Targeting a framework does not mean every machine has that runtime installed.
Framework-dependent deployment requires an appropriate runtime on the target system.
Single-file deployment can package application components into a reduced number of deployment files according to .NET publishing behavior.
Trimming can remove code considered unused during publishing.
Reflection and dynamic loading can complicate trimming because required code may not be statically obvious.
Native AOT can compile applicable .NET applications ahead of time to native code.
AOT deployment has different compatibility and runtime characteristics from ordinary JIT deployment.
C# can be used for command-line applications.
C# can be used for web applications.
C# can be used for web APIs.
C# can be used for libraries.
C# can be used for cloud services.
C# can be used for games with frameworks and engines that support C#.
C# can be used for mobile and cross-platform applications with applicable .NET frameworks.
ASP.NET Core is a framework for building web applications and services using .NET.
ASP.NET Core can be used with C#.
A web application receives HTTP requests and produces HTTP responses.
Middleware forms a processing pipeline for HTTP requests and responses in ASP.NET Core.
Middleware can inspect, modify, handle, or pass requests to later middleware.
An endpoint performs application behavior associated with a matched request.
Dependency injection is built into common ASP.NET Core application patterns.
Dependency injection supplies dependencies to components rather than requiring those components to construct every dependency themselves.
Dependency injection and interfaces can reduce coupling when used appropriately.
Dependency injection does not automatically make a design correct.
Configuration provides application settings.
Secrets should not be committed directly into source code.
Environment variables can provide configuration values.
Logging records application events and diagnostics.
Logs should not unnecessarily contain passwords, tokens, private keys, or other sensitive data.
Structured logging records fields in a machine-processable form.
Database access can be performed using database providers or libraries.
ADO.NET provides core .NET database-access abstractions.
DbConnection represents a database connection abstraction.
DbDataReader provides forward-only reading of query results according to provider behavior.
Database commands should use parameters rather than constructing SQL by concatenating untrusted input.
Parameterized SQL does not automatically make dynamically constructed SQL identifiers safe.
Database input remains untrusted input.
Entity Framework Core is an object-relational mapping framework for .NET.
Entity Framework Core is commonly abbreviated EF Core.
An object-relational mapper is commonly abbreviated ORM.
Using an ORM does not eliminate the need to understand database behavior.
Database transactions group operations into an atomic unit according to database transaction semantics.
Applications should handle database failures explicitly.
Database connections are resources and should be released appropriately.
Connection pooling can reuse underlying database connections.
Disposing a pooled connection object normally returns its underlying connection to the pool according to provider behavior rather than necessarily closing the physical connection immediately.
C# applications can interact with MySQL through an appropriate .NET database provider.
C# applications can interact with MariaDB through an appropriate compatible provider.
C# applications can interact with PostgreSQL through an appropriate .NET provider.
C# applications can interact with SQLite through an appropriate .NET provider.
Database-provider behavior is not defined by the C# language.
C# supports file operations through .NET libraries.
System.IO provides common file and stream APIs.
File provides static convenience methods for common file operations.
FileStream provides stream-based file access.
StreamReader reads characters from a stream using text encoding.
File operations can fail.
Filesystem permissions can prevent an otherwise correct C# program from reading or writing a file.
Path.Combine combines path components using platform-aware rules.
User-controlled paths require validation when they can influence filesystem access.
Canonicalization and authorization of filesystem targets require careful design.
System.Text.Json provides JSON serialization and deserialization facilities.
Serialization converts an object representation into a transferable or stored representation.
Deserialization reconstructs values from serialized data.
A deserialized object should not automatically be trusted merely because parsing succeeded.
Serialization formats should be defined when data is exchanged between independently developed systems.
Binary compatibility and serialized-data compatibility are different concerns.
A C# object should not be treated as a portable binary protocol merely because its in-memory fields appear suitable.
Explicit serialization defines transferable representation independently of managed object layout.
C# supports network programming through .NET libraries.
TcpClient provides a higher-level TCP client abstraction.
TcpListener provides a TCP listener abstraction.
TCP does not preserve application message boundaries.
Network reads can return fewer bytes than the complete application message requires.
Network writes and reads must handle connection failures.
Network operations can block unless asynchronous or timeout behavior is used appropriately.
HttpClient instances are intended to be reused according to common .NET usage patterns rather than unnecessarily created and destroyed for every request.
HTTP success should be determined from the actual response status and application contract.
A 404 HTTP status indicates that a requested resource was not found.
A 503 HTTP status indicates that a service is unavailable.
A 503 response should represent actual service unavailability rather than uncertainty about an answer.
HTTP behavior is provided through protocols and .NET libraries.
Security boundaries should be explicit.
Input from network peers is untrusted unless established otherwise by a trusted protocol and validation.
Input from files can be untrusted.
Client-side validation is not a server-side security boundary.
Server-side applications must validate untrusted input.
Validation determines whether input satisfies expected requirements.
Sanitization modifies input according to a defined policy.
Output encoding prepares data for a particular output context.
Validation, sanitization, and output encoding solve different problems.
SQL values should be supplied through parameters where applicable.
Authentication determines who or what an actor is.
Authorization determines what an authenticated actor is permitted to do.
Authentication and authorization are different security responsibilities.
A successful login does not imply permission to perform every operation.
Authorization should be enforced by trusted server-side code.
Passwords should not be stored as plaintext.
Password storage should use an appropriate password hashing algorithm.
Password hashing and encryption are different concepts.
Secrets should not be hard-coded into publicly distributed client software when secrecy depends on users being unable to recover them.
TLS protects network traffic between communicating endpoints when correctly configured.
Cryptographic algorithms should be used through established cryptographic libraries rather than invented casually.
System.Security.Cryptography provides cryptographic facilities in .NET.
RandomNumberGenerator provides cryptographically suitable random data for applicable security uses.
Cryptographic keys require secure generation, storage, access control, rotation, and disposal according to system requirements.
The unsafe keyword permits operations involving pointers and other unsafe constructs.
Unsafe code requires an appropriate compilation context.
Unsafe C# can bypass some protections normally provided by managed execution.
Pointer arithmetic can be used within unsafe contexts.
Unsafe code should be bounded and justified when used.
Span<T> is a ref struct.
Span<T> cannot be stored in ordinary heap objects in ways prohibited by ref struct lifetime rules.
Memory<T> provides a memory abstraction that can be stored and used across asynchronous boundaries in scenarios where Span<T> cannot.
Bounds checking remains part of ordinary safe span access.
Reference equality asks whether references identify the same object.
Value equality asks whether values should be considered equal according to the type's equality semantics.
For strings, == performs value equality according to string operator semantics.
The === operator does not exist in C#.
C# and PHP have different equality operators and semantics.
C# and JavaScript have different equality operators and semantics.
C# and C are distinct languages.
C# and C++ are distinct languages.
C# code does not compile as C merely because some syntax looks similar.
C# code does not compile as C++ merely because some syntax looks similar.
C# normally depends on .NET runtime and library facilities.
C normally uses its implementation and native platform facilities.
A C# assembly and a native shared library use different execution and metadata models.
Native interoperability must define a compatible boundary.
Platform Invocation Services are commonly called P/Invoke.
P/Invoke allows managed code to call functions exported by compatible native libraries.
The DllImport attribute can declare native functions for P/Invoke in established .NET patterns.
LibraryImport can provide source-generated interop declarations in applicable modern .NET versions.
Managed and native code must agree on calling conventions and data representation at an interop boundary.
Structure layout can be controlled for interoperability using applicable interop attributes.
Native ownership rules must specify who allocates and frees memory.
An ABI is an Application Binary Interface.
An ABI defines binary-level conventions between compiled components.
C# source-level APIs and native ABIs are different concepts.
A C-compatible ABI can simplify interoperability with multiple languages.
Version compatibility should be checked explicitly when independently deployed components communicate.
A module should not assume implementation details that are absent from its declared contract.
A module boundary should define ownership.
A module boundary should define lifetime.
A module boundary should define version compatibility.
A module boundary should define error behavior.
A module boundary should validate untrusted input.
C# attributes can provide metadata used by frameworks and tools.
An attribute does not necessarily execute behavior by itself.
Frameworks can inspect attributes and change behavior based on their metadata.
Dependency injection containers can use reflection or generated metadata to construct objects.
A source generator participates in compilation.
Generated code should be treated as part of the resulting program and reviewed according to project requirements.
Roslyn is the .NET compiler platform used for C# and Visual Basic tooling.
The C# compiler can expose analyzers.
Code fixes can suggest transformations for analyzer findings.
Compiler warnings and analyzers provide evidence but do not prove correctness.
Unit tests exercise bounded program behavior.
Negative tests verify correct handling of invalid or prohibited conditions.
A passing test demonstrates that the tested condition passed for that execution.
A passing test does not prove that every possible defect is absent.
A warning-free build does not prove that software is correct.
Static analysis can identify classes of defects without ordinary execution.
Dynamic testing observes behavior during execution.
Code review complements automated analysis.
Assertions can verify assumptions during development.
Debug.Assert provides diagnostic assertions according to its runtime and build behavior.
Assertions should not replace validation of untrusted production input.
Logging can provide operational evidence.
Logs should provide enough context for diagnosis without exposing unnecessary secrets.
Structured error handling is preferable to silently ignoring failures.
An operation that fails should return or report failure according to its interface contract.
Catching Exception indiscriminately can hide important failures when used carelessly.
A catch block should handle exceptions it can meaningfully address or report.
An empty catch block can conceal defects and operational failures.
Cancellation should not automatically be reported as an unexpected failure when cancellation was requested and is part of the interface contract.
A deterministic operation produces the same required result for the same defined input and state.
Hash-based collection enumeration order should not be used as a protocol-defined ordering unless explicitly guaranteed by the applicable contract.
Current observed iteration order should not be mistaken for a permanent cross-runtime protocol guarantee.
Culture-sensitive formatting can produce different text under different culture settings.
InvariantCulture provides culture-independent formatting behavior for applicable conversions.
Date and time serialization should use explicitly defined formats.
Time zones should not be inferred when protocol semantics require an explicit time basis.
DateTime and DateTimeOffset represent different time concepts.
UTC is commonly used for interoperable timestamps.
A deterministic protocol should specify timestamp representation.
A deterministic protocol should specify string encoding.
C# strings use UTF-16 code units internally according to .NET string representation semantics.
Internal string representation and network encoding are different concepts.
BinaryWriter and BinaryReader can read and write primitive values but protocol byte order and format must still be deliberately specified.
BitConverter behavior can depend on machine endianness for applicable conversions.
BinaryPrimitives provides explicit endian-aware operations for primitive values.
Cross-platform protocols should explicitly define byte order.
Cross-platform behavior should not depend on filesystem path separators.
Cross-platform behavior should not assume Windows-only drive letters.
Cross-platform behavior should not assume Unix-only root paths.
Path manipulation should use platform-aware facilities where filesystem semantics are intended to be platform dependent.
Protocol paths and filesystem paths are different concepts.
A protocol can define its own platform-independent path syntax.
.NET supports multiple operating systems.
C# applications can run on Windows when supported by their target runtime and dependencies.
C# applications can run on Linux when supported by their target runtime and dependencies.
C# applications can run on macOS when supported by their target runtime and dependencies.
Cross-platform .NET code can still contain platform-specific dependencies.
Platform-specific behavior should be isolated when portability is required.
OperatingSystem provides runtime operating-system checks in applicable modern .NET versions.
Conditional compilation can select code according to defined compilation symbols.
Conditional compilation and runtime platform selection solve different problems.
Platform-specific implementations should preserve common required behavior.
Platform independence concerns behavior, not merely whether code compiles on multiple systems.
The partial modifier allows one type definition to be split across multiple source declarations.
Partial methods provide related compile-time extension mechanisms according to C# version rules.
Generated code commonly uses partial types to cooperate with developer-written code.
C# supports indexers.
An indexer allows instances of a type to be accessed using indexing syntax.
The base keyword refers to the base-class portion or members in applicable contexts.
A static class cannot be instantiated.
A static class contains only static members according to C# rules.
Static mutable state is shared across applicable callers within its runtime context.
readonly fields restrict assignment according to readonly field rules.
A const field represents a compile-time constant.
const and readonly have different initialization and runtime semantics.
C# supports local functions.
A local function is declared within another member.
Local functions can help keep bounded helper behavior close to its caller.
Expression-bodied syntax can make simple members concise.
Concise syntax should not be used when it makes behavior harder to understand.
Named arguments identify parameters by name at a call site.
C# supports optional parameters.
Optional parameter default values become part of the calling contract.
Changing an optional parameter default can have versioning implications for already compiled callers.
The params modifier permits a variable number of arguments for an applicable parameter.
params can cause an array or compatible collection representation to be created according to the language and runtime behavior.
C# supports ref returns and ref locals.
Reference-return features expose aliases to storage and require careful lifetime reasoning.
ref struct types have restricted lifetimes designed to prevent unsafe escape of stack-associated data.
C# supports pattern-based disposal and enumeration in addition to interface-based mechanisms in applicable language versions.
await using supports asynchronous disposal.
IAsyncDisposable defines asynchronous cleanup.
await foreach supports asynchronous enumeration.
IAsyncEnumerable<T> represents an asynchronous sequence.
Asynchronous enumeration can produce values over time without materializing the entire sequence first.
Asynchronous code should propagate cancellation when its interface supports cancellation.
