Software engineering is the disciplined design, construction, testing, deployment, operation, and maintenance of software systems.
A program can compile successfully and still be incorrect.
A program can run successfully and still produce incorrect results.
A program can pass one test and still contain defects.
Correctness must be established against defined requirements.
A requirement describes behavior, capability, constraint, or property a system must satisfy.
Requirements should be explicit.
Requirements should be testable when practical.
Requirements should avoid unnecessary ambiguity.
A requirement should distinguish required behavior from optional behavior.
A requirement should distinguish implementation constraints from desired outcomes.
Requirements can change.
Changes to requirements should be controlled.
A changed requirement can invalidate previously correct implementation behavior.
Engineering begins by understanding the problem.
Unknown requirements should remain unknown rather than being invented.
Assumptions should be identified as assumptions.
Evidence should be distinguished from inference.
Observed behavior should be distinguished from intended behavior.
Existing behavior should not automatically be interpreted as correct behavior.
Documentation can describe intended behavior.
Tests can provide evidence about expected behavior.
Source code can provide evidence about implemented behavior.
Runtime observation can provide evidence about actual behavior.
Authority must be established by the project.
Architecture should serve requirements.
Architecture should not exist merely to demonstrate complexity.
A small system does not require a complicated architecture.
Complexity should be justified by requirements.
A component is a bounded part of a software system.
Components should have clear responsibilities.
A component should expose defined interfaces.
An interface defines how another component interacts with it.
Implementation details should remain behind interfaces when they do not need to be exposed.
Encapsulation limits knowledge of implementation details.
Coupling describes dependencies between components.
High coupling means components depend heavily on one another.
Low coupling reduces unnecessary dependency.
Cohesion describes how closely related a component's responsibilities are.
High cohesion means a component has a focused purpose.
Good modular design generally favors high cohesion and controlled coupling.
A module should have a defined purpose.
A module should have a defined boundary.
A module should not silently assume authority belonging to another component.
A module should not modify unrelated state merely because it can access it.
Capability does not establish authority.
An API is an Application Programming Interface.
An API should define relevant state behavior.
An API contract is a behavioral agreement between provider and consumer.
Changing an API can break consumers.
Backward compatibility means newer software continues supporting defined behavior expected by older consumers.
Forward compatibility describes the ability of existing software to tolerate or interact with newer representations according to the protocol design.
Do not assume compatibility merely because two versions compile.
Protocol compatibility and implementation compatibility are different.
A dependency is software or functionality required by another component.
Dependencies should solve actual problems.
Dependencies require maintenance.
Dependencies can introduce vulnerabilities.
Dependencies can change behavior.
Dependencies can become unavailable.
Dependencies can create licensing obligations.
Do not reimplement complex specialized functionality merely to avoid a mature dependency when doing so increases risk.
Dependency decisions are engineering tradeoffs.
A dependency should have a defined reason for existing.
Unused dependencies should be removed when safe and authorized.
A build transforms source code and resources into executable or distributable artifacts.
A successful build proves that the build process completed according to its criteria.
A successful build does not prove correctness.
A successful build does not prove security.
A successful build does not prove compatibility.
A successful build does not prove runtime success.
Compiler warnings provide engineering evidence.
Warnings should be examined.
Warnings should not be ignored merely because compilation succeeded.
Treating warnings as errors can improve build discipline where appropriate.
Not every warning indicates a defect.
Suppressing a warning should have a reason.
A build should be reproducible when reproducibility is a project requirement.
A reproducible build produces equivalent required output from defined inputs under defined conditions.
Build inputs should be known.
Compiler versions can affect output.
Library versions can affect output.
Build flags can affect output.
Environment variables can affect output.
Build systems should avoid hidden dependencies.
A clean build starts without relying on stale previous build artifacts.
Clean builds help expose undeclared dependencies.
Incremental builds improve development speed.
An incremental build should not produce different required behavior from a clean build.
Source control records changes to project files.
A commit should represent coherent work when practical.
A commit message should describe the change sufficiently for project use.
Version control history is engineering evidence.
History should not be rewritten merely to make past development appear cleaner unless project policy explicitly permits the history operation.
A branch provides an independent line of development according to version-control semantics.
Branches can isolate work.
Merging combines development histories or changes.
Merge conflicts require deliberate resolution.
A conflict means automatic reconciliation was not possible.
A conflict does not indicate which side is correct.
Resolve conflicts according to intended project state.
Do not choose a conflict side merely because it is newer.
A patch is a change applied to existing software.
A patch should be bounded.
Unrelated cleanup increases patch scope.
Increased scope increases regression risk.
Do not refactor unrelated code during a narrowly scoped repair unless authorized or required.
A regression is a previously working behavior that becomes defective after a change.
Regression testing checks that established behavior remains correct after changes.
Every change has regression potential.
Small changes can cause large effects.
Before changing code, identify the failure.
Collect evidence.
Identify the responsible component.
Change the smallest appropriate boundary.
Build the changed software.
Test the changed behavior.
Test relevant surrounding behavior.
Inspect resulting logs or outputs.
Do not declare a defect fixed solely because compilation succeeded.
Debugging is the process of finding and understanding defects.
Begin with observed behavior.
Determine expected behavior.
Identify the difference.
Reduce the problem.
Test hypotheses.
Reject hypotheses contradicted by evidence.
Random changes can hide defects.
A symptom is not necessarily the root cause.
A root cause is the underlying condition producing the failure.
One root cause can produce multiple symptoms.
One symptom can have multiple possible causes.
Do not claim a root cause without evidence.
Logs provide evidence about execution.
Logs can show state transitions.
Logs can show failures.
Logs can show timing.
Logs can show component interaction.
Logs can also be incomplete.
Absence of a log entry does not automatically prove an operation did not occur unless logging is guaranteed for that operation.
