Fail fast means stopping when continuing would violate required assumptions.
Fail safe means failure leaves the system in an acceptable state.
Fail fast and fail safe describe different properties.
A system can fail fast while failing unsafely.
A system can fail safely without terminating the entire process.
Failure policy should match the component's responsibility.
Unrecoverable failures should be reported accurately.
Retry can recover transient failures.
Retry is not appropriate for every failure.
Invalid input generally does not become valid through repetition.
Permission denial generally does not disappear through immediate repeated attempts.
Retries should be bounded.
Retries should have defined conditions.
Repeated retry without limits can create an infinite failure loop.
Backoff increases delay between retry attempts according to a defined strategy.
A retry storm occurs when many clients repeatedly retry failing operations and increase system load.
Jitter can distribute retry timing.
State-changing operations require careful retry semantics.
An operation is idempotent when repeating it produces the same intended state according to its defined semantics.
Setting a value to a specific value can be idempotent.
Creating a payment twice is not equivalent to creating it once.
Retry safety should be established before automatically repeating state-changing operations.
Testing evaluates software behavior.
A test should have a defined purpose.
A test should establish conditions.
A test should perform an operation.
A passing test is evidence for the behavior covered by that test.
A failing test is evidence that expected and actual behavior differ according to the test.
When a test fails, examine both implementation and test assumptions.
A unit test evaluates a small bounded component.
An integration test evaluates interaction between components.
An acceptance test evaluates behavior against acceptance requirements.
A regression test protects previously established behavior.
A smoke test checks basic viability after a build or deployment.
A load test evaluates behavior under defined workload.
A stress test evaluates behavior under conditions approaching or exceeding expected capacity.
A soak test evaluates behavior over extended operation.
A security test evaluates security properties.
Different tests answer different questions.
Do not treat a unit test as an end-to-end test.
Do not treat a successful smoke test as proof of production readiness.
