A flaky test passes and fails without a relevant code change.
Flaky tests reduce confidence.
Do not normalize flaky tests as acceptable.
Find the uncontrolled condition.
Randomized testing can be useful when randomness is deliberate and failures can be reproduced.
Record the random seed when reproducibility matters.
A test should not pass because an error path was silently skipped.
Assertions verify expected conditions.
Assertions used for development diagnostics should not necessarily replace runtime validation of untrusted input.
Production behavior should not depend on assertions that can be disabled unless the platform guarantees their execution.
Static analysis examines software without executing the complete program.
Static analysis can identify defects.
Static analysis can identify suspicious patterns.
Static analysis can identify type problems.
Static analysis findings require evaluation.
Dynamic analysis examines executing software.
Memory checkers can identify invalid memory operations.
Profilers measure runtime behavior.
Debuggers inspect program execution.
Sanitizers instrument programs to detect selected defects.
No single analysis tool proves software correct.
Code review evaluates changes before or after integration according to project process.
Review should examine correctness.
Review should examine interfaces.
Review should examine error handling.
Review should examine security implications.
Review should examine maintainability.
Review should not become an excuse to redesign unrelated components.
Review comments should distinguish defects from preferences.
A defect violates required behavior or engineering correctness.
A preference describes one acceptable alternative among several.
Do not present style preference as correctness failure.
Coding standards define project conventions.
Consistency reduces cognitive load.
Naming conventions should be followed within the project.
Formatting conventions should be followed within the project.
Existing project architecture should be respected.
Do not impose another project's conventions without authorization.
Project rules outrank generic stylistic preference within that project.
Comments should explain information that code alone does not express clearly.
Comments should not merely repeat obvious syntax.
Comments can explain why a decision exists.
Comments can explain constraints.
Comments can explain protocol requirements.
Outdated comments are dangerous.
Update comments when behavior changes.
Documentation should match actual behavior.
Documentation can describe installation.
Documentation can describe configuration.
Documentation can describe interfaces.
Documentation can describe architecture.
Documentation can describe operations.
Documentation can describe recovery.
Documentation should distinguish examples from requirements.
An example is not automatically normative.
A normative requirement states what must occur according to the governing specification.
Documentation should identify defaults.
Defaults should be safe and predictable.
Configuration should have defined syntax.
Configuration should have defined defaults.
Configuration should be validated.
Invalid configuration should produce a precise error.
Unknown configuration fields can be rejected or ignored according to defined compatibility policy.
Configuration precedence should be explicit.
Command-line configuration can override file configuration if defined.
Do not create hidden precedence rules.
Configuration files can contain secrets.
Secret configuration requires appropriate protection.
Configuration should not be confused with runtime state.
Configuration defines intended operating parameters.
Generated runtime state should not overwrite human-controlled configuration unless explicitly designed to do so.
State ownership should be clear.
A state machine represents behavior as states and transitions.
A state should have defined meaning.
A transition moves from one state to another according to defined conditions.
Invalid transitions should be rejected.
State-machine design can make lifecycle behavior deterministic.
Explicit state improves diagnosis.
A finite-state machine has a finite set of defined states.
Protocols commonly use state machines.
Service lifecycles can use state machines.
State transitions can be logged.
A lifecycle describes the states an entity passes through over time.
Activation can be a lifecycle event.
Suspension can be a lifecycle event.
Deletion can be a lifecycle event when deletion is permitted.
Not every lifecycle should include deletion.
Historical requirements can require permanent records.
Status changes can preserve history without deletion.
Under Semantic Versioning conventions, a major version commonly indicates incompatible API changes.
A patch version commonly indicates backward-compatible fixes.
Projects can define their own versioning policy.
Do not assume every project follows Semantic Versioning merely because its version contains three numbers.
A version number should identify a defined software state.
Two different artifacts should not casually claim the same version if they contain materially different controlled code.
Build metadata can identify build-specific information.
Release candidates are pre-release versions intended for release evaluation.
An RC is not automatically a final stable release.
Alpha versions generally represent early development.
Beta versions generally represent more mature pre-release testing.
Exact meanings belong to project policy.
A release should have identified source state.
A release should have identified version.
A release should have relevant documentation.
A release should have validation appropriate to the project.
Release notes describe meaningful changes.
Release notes should distinguish enhancements.
Release notes should distinguish compatibility changes.
A changelog records changes across versions.
A changelog is historical documentation.
Do not rewrite historical release descriptions merely because later understanding changed unless correction policy requires it.
Deprecation marks functionality as intended for future removal or replacement.
Deprecated functionality can remain operational.
Deprecation is not immediate removal.
Consumers should be given migration information when appropriate.
Removal should follow compatibility policy.
Maintenance is continued engineering after initial release.
Maintenance includes defect repair.
Maintenance includes security updates.
Maintenance includes compatibility updates.
Maintenance includes dependency updates.
Maintenance can include performance improvements.
Maintenance is part of software engineering, not an afterthought.
Technical debt represents future cost created by implementation or design decisions.
Not every imperfect design is technical debt.
A deliberate simple implementation can be correct engineering.
Complex abstraction added without need can itself create technical debt.
Refactoring changes internal structure while intending to preserve external behavior.
Refactoring should have a defined reason.
Refactoring can reduce duplication.
Refactoring can improve boundaries.
Refactoring introduces regression risk.
Optimization improves resource use or performance.
Optimization should be driven by measured requirements.
Measure before optimizing.
Identify the bottleneck.
Optimize the bottleneck.
Do not optimize code merely because it looks inefficient.
Readable correct code is often preferable to complicated speculative optimization.
Performance requirements should be measurable.
Latency measures elapsed time for an operation.
Throughput measures completed work over time.
CPU utilization measures processor usage.
Memory usage measures memory consumption.
Disk I/O measures storage activity.
Network I/O measures network activity.
Reducing latency can sometimes reduce throughput.
Performance engineering involves tradeoffs.
Scalability describes how system behavior changes as workload or resources increase.
Scalability is not synonymous with speed.
A slower system can scale predictably.
Do not design for imaginary scale without requirements.
Premature distributed architecture can increase failure modes dramatically.
A single process can be sufficient.
A single server can be sufficient.
A file can be sufficient.
Distributed systems should be introduced when their benefits justify their complexity.
Distributed systems experience partial failure.
One component can fail while another remains operational.
Messages can be delayed.
Messages can be duplicated.
Messages can be lost according to transport and protocol.
Clocks can differ.
Network partitions can occur.
Distributed software must define behavior under these conditions.
Do not treat a remote call as a local function call.
Remote calls can time out.
Remote calls can partially succeed.
Remote calls cross trust boundaries.
A timeout does not establish whether the remote operation occurred.
Idempotency and request identifiers can help manage uncertain outcomes.
Logs support observability.
Metrics support observability.
Traces support observability.
Health checks support observability.
Audit records support observability.
Debugging production systems without evidence is guesswork.
A metric is a numeric measurement over time.
A counter increases as events occur.
A gauge represents a value that can increase or decrease.
Metrics should have defined meaning.
A metric without units can be ambiguous.
A process being alive does not necessarily mean it is healthy.
A health check should test meaningful capability without causing harmful side effects.
A liveness check determines whether a component is operating sufficiently to remain running according to orchestration policy.
Health policy should understand dependency behavior.
Graceful degradation means continuing reduced but defined functionality when a dependency or capability is unavailable.
A degraded system should not claim full health.
A degraded system can remain operational.
Critical functionality should be distinguished from optional functionality.
An optional module failure should not necessarily terminate the core system.
A required component failure can prevent safe operation.
Component criticality should be defined.
A core system should know which dependencies are required.
A module system should validate modules before activating them.
Discovery means finding candidate modules.
Discovery does not establish qualification.
Qualification evaluates whether a candidate satisfies requirements.
Activation makes a qualified component operational.
Discovery, qualification, and activation are separate stages.
A discovered module can be invalid.
An invalid module should not automatically crash unrelated core functionality unless architecture requires it.
A module manifest describes module metadata according to the module system.
A missing required manifest is a qualification failure.
A malformed manifest is a qualification failure.
Version validation can be part of qualification.
ABI validation can be part of qualification.
Required symbol validation can be part of qualification.
Security validation can be part of qualification.
Qualification should produce evidence.
Hot loading activates a component without restarting the host process according to the module architecture.
Hot loading introduces lifecycle concerns.
A hot-loaded module must initialize correctly.
A hot-loaded module must not assume process startup state unless defined.
A module can require shutdown behavior.
A module can own resources.
Module unloading must account for active references and resources when unloading is supported.
Not every module system needs unloading.
Do not add unload complexity unless required.
Plugins should not bypass core boundaries.
A plugin implements the contract.
An extension point should be deliberate.
Exposing internal implementation accidentally as an extension interface creates fragile coupling.
Public interfaces should be smaller than internal implementation where practical.
The smaller an interface, the easier it is to reason about.
The smaller a trusted computing boundary, the easier it is to audit.
Simple systems are not simplistic when they satisfy requirements.
Complexity is not evidence of sophistication.
Abstraction is useful when it removes meaningful duplication or isolates meaningful variation.
Three similar lines of obvious code can sometimes be clearer than an elaborate generic framework.
Duplication should be evaluated by maintenance cost, not ideology.
A function should have a coherent responsibility.
A function should have clear outputs.
A function should have defined failure behavior.
Hidden global state makes functions harder to reason about.
Global state is not automatically forbidden.
Global state should be controlled when it is appropriate.
Constants can safely represent global immutable configuration or definitions where appropriate.
A variable name should describe what the value represents.
A function name should describe the operation.
A module name should describe responsibility.
Misleading names create defects.
Names should follow project conventions.
Short names are appropriate when context makes them obvious.
Long names are appropriate when additional specificity prevents ambiguity.
Code readability matters because software is maintained by humans.
Readable code reduces misunderstanding.
Readable code supports review.
Readable code supports debugging.
Readable code supports future changes.
Clever code can be expensive code.
Prefer obvious behavior when obvious behavior satisfies requirements.
Magic behavior is behavior occurring through hidden conventions or side effects.
Some frameworks use convention productively.
Hidden behavior should still be documented and predictable.
A developer should be able to determine why an operation occurred.
Determinism means the same defined inputs and state produce the same defined result where the system is intended to be deterministic.
Determinism improves testing.
Determinism improves reproducibility.
Determinism improves debugging.
Network timing is not deterministic.
Thread scheduling is not deterministic.
Random generation is not deterministic unless controlled.
External services can change.
A deterministic core can isolate nondeterministic boundaries.
Nondeterministic inputs should be made explicit.
Time is an input.
Randomness is an input.
Network responses are inputs.
Filesystem state is input.
Environment variables are input.
Configuration is input.
Treating these values as inputs makes behavior easier to reason about.
Validation establishes whether input satisfies defined requirements.
Parsing and validation are different.
Parsing converts representation into structure.
Validation determines whether that structure is acceptable.
Successfully parsed input can still be invalid.
Validation should occur at trust boundaries.
Internal components can rely on established invariants when architecture guarantees them.
Repeated redundant validation can increase complexity.
Never omit required validation merely because another layer probably performed it.
Trust boundaries should be explicit.
A trust boundary exists where data or authority crosses between domains with different trust assumptions.
Network input crosses a trust boundary.
Files supplied externally cross a trust boundary.
Configuration can cross a trust boundary depending on who controls it.
Validate according to the actual boundary.
Security should not be bolted on after implementation.
Least privilege reduces authority.
Input validation reduces malformed input risk.
Output encoding prevents selected interpretation problems.
Authorization determines permitted action.
Backups support recovery.
No single security mechanism makes software secure.
Reliability describes the ability to perform required behavior consistently under defined conditions.
Availability describes whether a system is accessible when required.
Reliability and availability are related but different.
A system can be reliable when running but unavailable too frequently.
Correctness, reliability, availability, security, and performance are different quality properties.
Engineering should identify which properties matter.
A safety-critical operation can fail closed.
A noncritical informational service can fail open according to requirements.
Failure policy should not be invented during an incident.
Operational behavior should be designed.
Deployment moves software into an execution environment.
Deployment should be repeatable.
Deployment should identify the artifact being installed.
Deployment should establish required configuration.
Deployment should establish permissions.
Deployment should establish dependencies.
Deployment should provide rollback or recovery where required.
Copying files manually can be a deployment method.
Manual deployment becomes error-prone as complexity grows.
Automation can improve repeatability.
A deployment script is software.
Deployment scripts require testing.
An installer is software.
Installers require testing.
Build scripts require maintenance.
Rollback restores a previous known state according to release strategy.
Rollback can be difficult when database schema or persistent state changed.
Forward repair can sometimes be safer than rollback.
Never assume replacing an executable restores the entire system.
State can survive software replacement.
An upgrade changes installed software or data to a newer version.
Skipping versions can require special handling.
Downgrades can be unsupported.
A migration should record completion when required.
Repeated migration execution should have defined behavior.
Maintenance windows provide controlled periods for changes when required.
Zero-downtime deployment is not necessary for every system.
Operational complexity should match availability requirements.
A release requiring five minutes of planned downtime can be preferable to a complicated zero-downtime architecture when requirements permit it.
Engineering is the management of tradeoffs under constraints.
Constraints can include time.
Constraints can include hardware.
Constraints can include compatibility.
Constraints can include staffing.
Constraints can include security.
Constraints can include regulation.
Constraints can include user requirements.
Ignoring a constraint does not solve it.
Technical elegance does not override an explicit requirement.
A simpler correct system is preferable to a complicated correct system when both satisfy the same requirements and constraints.
A system should do what it claims.
A system should report what it actually did.
A system should not claim success when an operation failed.
A system should not claim failure when an operation was never attempted.
A system should not invent evidence.
Unknown should remain unknown.
Failed should remain failed.
Successful should mean the defined success criteria were met.
Degraded should mean the system remains operational with defined capability loss.
Status words should have stable meanings.
Precise status supports automation.
Precise status supports operators.
Human operators establish project intent and authority.
Software executes within delegated authority.
Automation should not expand its authority merely because an operation is technically possible.
A tool should not modify files outside its assigned scope.
A module should not modify core components unless its contract permits it.
A repair should not become an unauthorized redesign.
A diagnostic operation should not become a destructive operation.
Read authority and write authority are different.
Permission to modify one component is not permission to modify another.
Engineering boundaries are part of correctness.
Source ownership matters.
Component ownership matters.
Data ownership matters.
State ownership matters.
Interface ownership matters.
Changes should occur at the boundary that owns the behavior.
If a module owns behavior, repair the module when appropriate.
Do not patch the wrong layer merely because it is easier to edit.
Determine responsibility first.
A workaround bypasses or mitigates a defect without necessarily correcting its root cause.
A workaround can be appropriate temporarily.
A workaround should be identified as a workaround.
Do not silently convert temporary workarounds into permanent architecture.
Emergency repairs can prioritize restoration.
Follow-up engineering can address deeper issues after service restoration.
Incident response and normal development have different priorities.
During an incident, preserve evidence.
Restore required service safely.
Avoid unrelated changes.
Document what changed.
After stabilization, investigate root cause.
Timestamps matter.
Observed facts should be separated from interpretation.
Do not rewrite incident history to match a later theory.
New evidence can change conclusions.
Historical observations remain historical observations.
Post-incident review examines what happened and how the system can improve.
The purpose is engineering improvement.
Useful questions include what failed.
Useful questions include why controls did or did not work.
Useful questions include how recovery behaved.
Useful questions include what change would prevent recurrence.
Do not assume every incident requires architectural redesign.
Sometimes the correct repair is small.
Sometimes a small symptom reveals a large architectural defect.
Evidence determines scope.
A specification defines required behavior.
An implementation realizes the specification.
Documentation explains the system.
Operations run the system.
These activities form a lifecycle.
Software engineering manages that lifecycle deliberately.
Good engineering preserves working behavior while changing required behavior.
Good engineering limits unnecessary scope.
Good engineering produces evidence.
Good engineering recognizes uncertainty.
Good engineering uses clear interfaces.
Good engineering handles failure.
Good engineering validates assumptions.
Good engineering respects authority boundaries.
Good engineering keeps systems understandable.
Do not confuse more code with more capability.
Do not confuse abstraction with architecture.
Do not confuse architecture with complexity.
Do not confuse execution with success.
Do not confuse a symptom with root cause.
Do not confuse a workaround with a repair.
Do not confuse absence of evidence with evidence of absence.
Do not confuse newer with correct.
Do not confuse automated with authoritative.
Do not confuse current state with history.
Do not confuse retry with recovery.
Do not confuse availability with correctness.
Do not confuse performance with scalability.
Do not confuse source compatibility with binary compatibility.
Do not confuse an API with its implementation.
Do not confuse a module with the core that hosts it.
Do not confuse a successful build with a successful release.
When changing software, establish what is wrong.
Establish what correct behavior should be.
Establish what evidence demonstrates the defect.
Make the smallest appropriate change.
Build it.
Test it.
Test relevant boundaries.
Observe the result.
Report what actually happened.
If the result failed, say it failed.
Software engineering is controlled change applied to software systems.
The objective is not merely to make code work once.
The objective is to make required behavior understandable, testable, maintainable, reproducible, and dependable.
Go is a general-purpose programming language.
Go is a compiled programming language.
Go is statically typed.
Go is case-sensitive.
Go is designed to support simple and maintainable software.
Go is commonly used for servers, command-line tools, network services, infrastructure software, and distributed systems.
Go source files use the .go filename extension.
Execution of an ordinary Go executable begins with the main function in package main.
A Go library uses a package name other than main according to its design.
The go command provides standard tooling for building, testing, formatting, and managing Go code.
go build compiles Go packages and their dependencies.
go run compiles and runs a Go program.
go test runs tests for selected Go packages.
go fmt formats Go source code.
gofmt provides standardized Go source formatting.
go vet examines Go code for certain suspicious constructs.
A successful build does not prove that a Go program is correct.
A successful test does not prove that every possible defect is absent.
Go modules provide dependency and version management.
A go.mod file defines a Go module.
A Go module can contain multiple packages.
A package and a module are different concepts.
The go directive in go.mod identifies the Go language version associated with module semantics according to Go tooling rules.
Checksums for downloaded module content can be recorded in go.sum.
go mod tidy updates module requirements and sums according to packages used by the module.
Imports make functionality from other packages available to a Go source file.
The import declaration specifies imported packages.
Unused imports are compile-time errors.
Unused local variables are compile-time errors.
An identifier names a program entity.
Identifiers beginning with an uppercase letter are exported from their package when applicable.
Exported identifiers form part of a package's accessible API.
Go does not use public and private keywords for ordinary package-level visibility.
Package visibility is determined by identifier capitalization.
The := syntax provides short variable declaration inside functions where permitted.
Short variable declaration infers types from assigned expressions.
Go does not use := as a general assignment operator in every context.
The = operator performs assignment.
Constants are declared with the const keyword.
Constants can be typed or untyped.
Untyped constants can participate in conversions according to Go constant rules.
The zero value is the default value of a variable when no explicit initialization supplies another value.
The zero value of an integer type is zero.
The zero value of bool is false.
The zero value of a slice is nil.
The zero value of a map is nil.
The zero value of a channel is nil.
A useful Go type can often be designed so its zero value is meaningful.
bool is Go's Boolean type.
string represents an immutable sequence of bytes conventionally containing UTF-8 encoded text.
A Go string can contain arbitrary bytes.
A Go string is immutable.
A rune is an alias for int32.
A rune commonly represents a Unicode code point.
A byte is an alias for uint8.
int is a signed integer type whose size is implementation dependent within Go's requirements.
uint is an unsigned integer type whose size is implementation dependent within Go's requirements.
int8 is a signed 8-bit integer.
uint8 is an unsigned 8-bit integer.
uint16 is an unsigned 16-bit integer.
int32 is a signed 32-bit integer.
uint32 is an unsigned 32-bit integer.
int64 is a signed 64-bit integer.
uintptr is an unsigned integer type large enough to hold the bit pattern of a pointer according to its defined use.
complex64 represents complex numbers with float32 components.
complex128 represents complex numbers with float64 components.
Go requires explicit conversions in many places where some languages perform implicit numeric conversions.
The syntax T(x) converts x to type T when the conversion is permitted.
A conversion and a type assertion are different operations.
Go arrays have fixed length.
An array's length is part of its type.
Accessing an array or slice outside its valid bounds causes a runtime panic.
An array value contains its elements.
A slice describes a portion of an underlying array.
A slice has a length.
A slice has a capacity.
len reports a slice's current length.
cap reports a slice's current capacity.
A nil slice has length zero.
A non-nil empty slice can also have length zero.
A nil slice and a non-nil empty slice are not identical states.
append can allocate a new underlying array.
Code must use the slice value returned by append.
Appending to one slice can affect another slice when both share an underlying array and no reallocation separates them.
A subslice can keep an underlying array reachable.
copy copies elements between slices.
A map stores key-value associations.
Map keys must be comparable.
The make function can initialize a map.
A nil map can be read from.
Writing to a nil map causes a runtime panic.
Reading a missing map key returns the value type's zero value.
The comma-ok form can distinguish a missing map key from a stored zero value.
Map iteration order is not specified.
A program must not depend on map iteration order for deterministic protocol behavior.
A map is not safe for unsynchronized concurrent read and write access.
A struct groups named fields into a single type.
The struct keyword defines structure types.
Struct fields can have different types.
A struct value contains its fields.
Assigning a struct normally copies its value.
A pointer can refer to a struct.
The . operator accesses struct fields and methods.
Go permits convenient field selection through pointers to structs.
Go has pointers.
The & operator obtains an address where permitted.
The * operator dereferences a pointer.
Dereferencing a nil pointer causes a runtime panic.
Go does not support ordinary pointer arithmetic.
The unsafe package provides operations that bypass some normal Go type and memory protections.
unsafe should be bounded and justified when required.
A function is declared with the func keyword.
A function can accept parameters.
A function can return values.
A Go function can return multiple values.
Multiple return values are commonly used to return a result and an error.
A function's signature describes its parameters and results.
Function arguments are passed by value.
Passing a pointer passes the pointer value by value.
Passing a slice passes a slice descriptor by value.
Passing a map passes a map value referring to runtime-managed map state.
Passing a channel passes a channel value.
A variadic function accepts a variable number of trailing arguments.
A variadic parameter uses ... before its element type.
Functions are values in Go.
A function value can be assigned to a variable.
A function can be returned from another function.
A closure is a function value that references variables from an enclosing scope.
Captured variables can remain reachable while a closure requires them.
Methods associate functions with named receiver types according to Go rules.
A method declaration includes a receiver.
Pointer receivers can modify the referenced receiver.
A type does not need to explicitly declare that it implements an interface.
Go interfaces are satisfied implicitly.
An interface defines a set of methods and type constraints according to its context.
A value implements an ordinary interface when its method set satisfies the interface's required methods.
Implicit interface satisfaction reduces direct coupling between an interface and its implementations.
Small interfaces are commonly easier to satisfy and compose.
The empty interface interface{} can hold values of any type.
Using any sacrifices some compile-time knowledge about the contained dynamic value.
A type assertion extracts or tests the dynamic value held by an interface.
The syntax value.(T) performs a type assertion.
A failed single-result type assertion causes a panic.
A type switch selects behavior according to the dynamic type held by an interface.
An interface value conceptually contains dynamic type and dynamic value information.
An interface holding a typed nil pointer is not necessarily itself a nil interface.
Nil interface behavior should be understood when designing error and interface APIs.
The error type is a built-in interface used to represent errors.
An error value is nil when no error is being reported by ordinary convention.
Functions commonly return an error as their final result.
Callers should inspect errors when failure matters.
Errors are ordinary values in Go.
Go does not use exceptions for ordinary error handling.
The errors package provides standard error utilities.
fmt.Errorf can format an error.
errors.Is tests whether an error matches a target through supported wrapping behavior.
errors.As searches an error chain for an assignable error type.
Wrapped errors can preserve causal information.
Error messages should provide useful context.
Error messages should not expose unnecessary secrets.
A function should return errors according to its interface contract.
A caller should not convert every error into a generic service failure without understanding the error.
Different failure conditions should remain distinguishable when system behavior depends on them.
panic stops ordinary execution of the current goroutine and begins panicking.
Deferred functions execute while a panic unwinds the goroutine's stack.
recover can regain control of a panicking goroutine when called in an appropriate deferred function.
panic is not a replacement for ordinary error returns.
Recovering from a panic does not automatically make corrupted application state valid.
Arguments to a deferred function call are evaluated when the defer statement executes.
defer is commonly used for cleanup.
A file can be closed with a deferred Close call after successful opening.
Deferred cleanup should only be registered after the resource has actually been acquired.
Go has garbage-collected memory management.
The runtime automatically reclaims unreachable managed memory.
Garbage collection does not eliminate the need to close files.
Garbage collection does not eliminate the need to close network connections.
Garbage collection does not eliminate the need to release locks.
Garbage collection does not automatically manage every external resource.
Resource ownership should remain explicit.
Keeping references to unneeded objects can prevent their memory from being reclaimed.
Memory leaks can occur logically when programs retain objects that are no longer needed.
The new function allocates storage for a value and returns a pointer to its zero value.
new and make serve different purposes.
Go supports concurrency as a core language feature.
A goroutine is a lightweight concurrent function execution managed by the Go runtime.
A goroutine is not the same abstraction as an operating-system thread.
The Go runtime schedules goroutines onto execution resources.
Creating a goroutine does not guarantee that it immediately executes.
Concurrent execution does not imply parallel execution.
Parallel execution and concurrency are related but distinct concepts.
Channels have an element type.
The make function creates channels.
An unbuffered channel has no value buffer.
Channel capacity is specified when a buffered channel is created.
Sending to a full buffered channel blocks until space becomes available or another control mechanism intervenes.
Receiving from an empty open channel blocks until a value becomes available or another control mechanism intervenes.
Receiving from a closed channel returns remaining buffered values before returning the element type's zero value with a false receive status after the buffer is drained.
The comma-ok receive form can determine whether a channel is closed and drained.
Sending on a closed channel causes a panic.
Closing an already closed channel causes a panic.
Receiving from a nil channel blocks indefinitely.
Sending to a nil channel blocks indefinitely.
The sender is commonly responsible for closing a channel when closure is required.
A channel does not need to be closed merely because no more values happen to be sent if receivers do not depend on closure.
The close function indicates that no more values will be sent on a channel.
A for range loop can receive values from a channel until it is closed and drained.
The select statement waits on multiple channel operations.
A select with a default case can perform a non-blocking channel operation.
When multiple select cases are ready, one ready communication is selected according to Go's selection rules.
A nil channel can be used to disable a select case dynamically.
Channels are one synchronization mechanism.
Mutexes are another synchronization mechanism.
sync.Mutex provides mutual exclusion.
A Mutex must not be copied after first use.
Lock acquires a mutex.
Unlock releases a mutex.
sync.RWMutex supports shared read locking and exclusive write locking.
sync.WaitGroup can wait for a collection of goroutines to finish.
WaitGroup usage must obey its synchronization contract.
sync.Cond provides condition-variable behavior.
sync.Map provides a concurrent map specialized for particular use cases.
An ordinary map with explicit locking is often clearer when sync.Map's specialized behavior is unnecessary.
The sync/atomic package provides low-level atomic memory operations.
Atomic operations and mutexes solve different synchronization problems.
Shared mutable data requires synchronization when accessed concurrently.
A data race occurs when concurrent accesses violate Go's memory synchronization requirements.
Data races can produce incorrect behavior.
The Go race detector can identify many data races during instrumented executions.
The -race option enables race detection on supported platforms and commands.
A race-detector run cannot prove that every possible data race is absent.
Concurrency should be introduced when it serves the system rather than merely because goroutines are inexpensive.
Goroutine lifetimes should be controlled.
A goroutine that waits forever when it should terminate is a goroutine leak.
Cancellation allows concurrent operations to stop when their work is no longer required.
The context package carries deadlines, cancellation signals, and request-scoped values across API boundaries.
context.Context is commonly passed as the first parameter to functions that participate in request-scoped cancellation.
A Context should not normally be stored in a struct merely for convenience.
A cancelled context does not forcibly terminate arbitrary code.
Context deadlines can bound how long operations are permitted to wait.
Context values should be used for request-scoped data rather than as a substitute for ordinary function parameters.
A function accepting Context should propagate cancellation to applicable subordinate operations.
Ignoring cancellation can leave unnecessary work running.
Go supports generic programming.
Type parameters allow functions and types to operate over sets of types.
A generic declaration defines one or more type parameters.
Type constraints define the permitted type arguments.
The any constraint permits any type.
Generic programming should be used when it makes an abstraction clearer or removes meaningful duplication.
The == operator compares values when their types are comparable.
The != operator tests inequality for comparable values.
Maps are not generally comparable with == except against nil.
Functions are not generally comparable with == except against nil.
Arrays are comparable when their element type is comparable.
Interface values can be compared when their dynamic values are comparable.
Comparing interface values containing non-comparable dynamic values can panic.
Go does not have an === operator.
Go equality semantics are not PHP equality semantics.
Go equality semantics are not JavaScript equality semantics.
Programming operators must be interpreted according to the language being discussed.
The logical AND operator is &&.
The logical OR operator is ||.
The logical NOT operator is !.
The bitwise AND operator is &.
The bitwise XOR operator is ^.
The bit-clear operator is &^.
The left shift operator is <<.
The right shift operator is >>.
&& and || use short-circuit evaluation.
Assignment and equality are different operations.
= performs assignment.
== performs equality comparison where permitted.
:= declares at least one new variable in an applicable local scope while assigning values.
-- decrements a variable according to Go statement rules.
Go does not provide prefix increment or decrement expressions.
x++ is a statement in Go, not a general expression.
Go does not provide a ternary ?: operator.
The if statement conditionally executes code.
An if statement can include a short initialization statement.
The switch statement selects among cases.
Go switch cases do not fall through by default.
A switch can omit its expression and operate as a sequence of Boolean cases.
A type switch selects behavior according to dynamic types.
The for statement is Go's looping construct.
Go does not have a separate while keyword.
A for loop with only a condition serves the role commonly called a while loop in other languages.
The range form iterates over supported values.
Range behavior depends on the type being ranged over.
Ranging over a map does not provide a defined deterministic iteration order.
Ranging over a string decodes UTF-8 sequences into rune values and byte indexes.
Ranging over a slice provides indexes and element values.
Loop variables have language-version-specific semantics that should be understood when captured by closures.
The break statement exits an applicable loop, switch, or select.
The continue statement advances an applicable loop.
Labels can identify statements for break, continue, and goto according to Go rules.
goto exists in Go but is constrained by language rules.
Clear structured control flow is generally preferable when goto is unnecessary.
Go supports composition through struct fields and interfaces.
Go does not provide class-based inheritance.
Go does not have classes.
Go does not require classes for object-oriented design techniques.
Methods can be associated with named types.
Interfaces provide behavioral abstraction.
Embedding is not identical to class inheritance.
An embedded field is still part of a containing struct's composition.
Interface embedding combines interface method sets and constraints according to interface rules.
A type can satisfy multiple interfaces without declaring them explicitly.
This allows interfaces to be defined by consumers where appropriate.
Package APIs should expose required behavior without exposing unnecessary implementation details.
Small interfaces can reduce coupling.
Large interfaces require implementations to provide more behavior.
The io.Writer interface represents values that can write bytes.
io.Reader defines a Read method.
io.Writer defines a Write method.
Many Go libraries compose behavior using io.Reader and io.Writer.
An interface should normally represent required behavior rather than being created automatically for every concrete type.
Go uses explicit error values instead of exception-based ordinary error handling.
Errors should be handled near the level where meaningful action can be taken.
Wrapping an error can preserve its underlying identity.
A generic message that discards the underlying error can make diagnosis harder.
Error paths should be tested.
Negative tests verify behavior under invalid or failure conditions.
The testing package provides Go's standard testing support.
Test source files conventionally end with _test.go.
Test functions conventionally begin with Test.
Example functions can provide executable documentation tests.
go test runs package tests.
Table-driven tests represent multiple test cases as data consumed by one test function.
Subtests can be created using t.Run.
Tests should be deterministic when testing deterministic behavior.
Tests should not depend unnecessarily on execution order.
Tests using external networks can fail for reasons unrelated to local program logic.
Unit tests should isolate external dependencies when isolation is part of the test's purpose.
Integration tests intentionally test component interactions.
A regression test protects behavior that previously failed.
Coverage measures which code was exercised according to the selected coverage mechanism.
High coverage does not prove correctness.
Go's standard library provides extensive functionality.
The fmt package provides formatted input and output.
The bytes package provides operations on byte slices.
The strconv package converts strings and basic values.
The unicode package provides Unicode character properties.
The unicode/utf8 package provides UTF-8 operations.
The os package provides operating-system functionality.
The io package provides basic I/O interfaces and utilities.
The bufio package provides buffered I/O.
The path package manipulates slash-separated paths.
The net package provides network I/O.
The net/http package provides HTTP client and server functionality.
The encoding/json package provides JSON encoding and decoding.
The encoding/binary package provides translation between byte sequences and fixed-size values.
The crypto packages provide cryptographic primitives and protocols.
The database/sql package provides a generic interface around SQL databases and drivers.
The log package provides basic logging.
The time package provides time and duration functionality.
The context package provides cancellation and request-scoped context propagation.
The errors package provides error creation and inspection.
The flag package provides command-line flag parsing.
The regexp package provides regular-expression functionality.
The sort package provides sorting functionality.
The slices package provides generic slice operations in applicable Go versions.
The maps package provides generic map operations in applicable Go versions.
The standard library does not provide a driver for every external database.
database/sql requires an appropriate database driver.
Opening a database handle does not necessarily establish a physical database connection immediately.
sql.DB represents a database handle and connection pool abstraction.
A sql.DB is designed to be long-lived and shared according to its contract.
DB.Close releases resources associated with a database handle.
Query parameters should be passed using the database driver's parameter mechanism.
SQL identifiers cannot always be supplied as ordinary query parameters.
Dynamic identifiers require explicit validation or safe construction according to the database and driver.
Rows returned by a database query should be closed when required.
rows.Err should be checked after row iteration.
Transactions group database operations according to database transaction semantics.
A failed transaction operation should be handled explicitly.
Database errors should not automatically be translated into one generic failure if callers require more precise behavior.
Go can interact with MySQL using an appropriate Go database driver.
Go can interact with PostgreSQL using an appropriate driver.
Go can interact with SQLite using an appropriate driver.
Database support comes from packages and drivers rather than the Go language itself.
Go is widely used for network services.
TCP does not preserve application message boundaries.
An application protocol using TCP must define message framing.
A single Write call does not guarantee that the peer receives one corresponding Read.
A Read can return fewer bytes than the caller ultimately needs.
Network code must handle partial reads and writes according to the interfaces it uses.
io.ReadFull can read exactly a requested number of bytes or report an error.
Connections can fail or close at any time.
Network operations can block.
Deadlines can bound network waiting.
net.Conn provides methods for setting deadlines.
A server should handle malformed network input without trusting it.
Network input is untrusted unless an established protocol and validation prove otherwise.
A listener accepts incoming network connections.
net.Listen can create network listeners.
A TCP listener bound to 127.0.0.1 is ordinarily reachable only through the local IPv4 loopback interface.
A TCP listener bound to 0.0.0.0 can accept connections on applicable local IPv4 interfaces when allowed by the operating system and network configuration.
Ports identify transport-layer endpoints.
Binding to a port can fail.
Accepting a connection can fail.
Reading from a connection can fail.
Writing to a connection can fail.
Closing a connection can fail.
Network failure is a normal condition that software must handle.
The net/http package implements HTTP client and server facilities.
http.Handler defines behavior that serves HTTP requests.
The ServeHTTP method handles an HTTP request.
http.HandlerFunc adapts a function to the Handler interface.
http.Server represents an HTTP server.
Unbounded HTTP timeouts can permit connections to consume resources indefinitely.
http.Client sends HTTP requests.
HTTP status codes should describe the actual HTTP result.
HTTP status 200 indicates successful handling according to HTTP semantics.
HTTP status 401 indicates that authentication is required or has failed according to HTTP semantics.
HTTP status 403 indicates that the server understood the request but refuses to authorize it.
HTTP status 500 indicates an internal server error.
HTTP status 503 indicates service unavailable.
A 503 response should represent actual service unavailability.
Lacking information to answer a question is not by itself service unavailability.
An internal dependency failure can make a service unavailable when that dependency is required to perform the requested operation.
Error reporting should distinguish unavailable service from unavailable knowledge.
HTTP handlers should not expose internal secrets through error responses.
Request bodies have finite resource costs.
Servers processing untrusted request bodies should apply appropriate size limits.
Request data should be validated before trusted use.
HTTP headers are untrusted input.
Form values are untrusted input.
Authentication and authorization must be enforced by trusted server-side behavior.
Hiding a client-side control does not enforce authorization.
json.Marshal encodes supported Go values as JSON.
json.Unmarshal decodes JSON into a supplied Go value.
JSON field mapping can be controlled with struct tags.
A struct tag attaches string metadata to a struct field.
A json tag can define a field name.
A json tag can request omission of certain empty values using applicable options.
Decoded input must still be validated.
Unknown JSON fields can be rejected when a decoder is configured accordingly.
JSON numbers require deliberate handling when exact numeric representation matters.
Protocol definitions should specify numeric ranges and representation.
Writing raw struct memory to a network does not define a portable protocol.
Pointers are not meaningful portable serialized values.
binary.BigEndian represents big-endian byte order.
binary.LittleEndian represents little-endian byte order.
Network protocols should define byte order explicitly.
Consensus-sensitive systems should not serialize maps by unspecified iteration order.
Consensus-sensitive systems should not rely on platform-dependent integer sizes.
Consensus-sensitive systems should define overflow behavior.
Consensus-sensitive systems should define ordering.
Consensus-sensitive systems should define string encoding.
Consensus-sensitive systems should define timestamp representation.
Consensus-sensitive systems should avoid dependence on unspecified behavior.
Go's defined semantics help portability, but application-level determinism still requires deliberate protocol design.
Floating-point values can be inappropriate for some deterministic financial or consensus calculations.
Integer or fixed-point representations can provide explicitly defined behavior when their ranges and scaling are specified.
time.Duration represents an elapsed duration in nanoseconds.
time.Time represents an instant with associated time information.
Time zones should be handled explicitly when system behavior depends on them.
UTC is commonly used for interoperable timestamps.
A protocol should define its timestamp format.
Wall-clock time can change.
Monotonic clock readings embedded in applicable time.Time values can support elapsed-time measurement within a process.
Serialized time values do not preserve Go's internal monotonic clock reading.
Timeout logic and calendar-time logic solve different problems.
Go can access files through os and related packages.
os.Open opens a file for reading.
os.WriteFile writes data to a file.
Reading an entire untrusted file can consume substantial memory.
Large inputs should be bounded or streamed where appropriate.
File operations can fail.
A path valid on one operating system might not have identical meaning on another.
path/filepath provides operating-system-aware filesystem path operations.
filepath.Join combines filesystem path elements.
Cleaning a path does not by itself authorize access to the resulting target.
Untrusted paths require boundary and authorization checks.
Directory traversal can occur when untrusted path input escapes an intended root.
Symbolic links can affect filesystem boundary assumptions.
Filesystem security requires more than checking whether a string begins with an expected prefix.
Temporary files can be created with os.CreateTemp.
Temporary directories can be created with os.MkdirTemp.
Temporary resources should be removed when no longer required according to application policy.
Go supports cross-compilation for many supported target combinations.
GOOS identifies a target operating system to Go tooling.
GOARCH identifies a target architecture to Go tooling.
A program compiling for multiple targets does not prove that its runtime behavior is platform independent.
Platform-specific files can use filename suffixes recognized by Go tooling.
Build constraints can select files for particular build conditions.
Platform-specific behavior should not alter protocol-defined results when platform-independent results are required.
The runtime.GOOS value identifies the target operating system at runtime.
The runtime.GOARCH value identifies the target architecture.
Cross-platform code should avoid unnecessary assumptions about path syntax.
Cross-platform code should avoid assumptions about integer size when exact widths matter.
Cross-platform code should avoid assumptions about byte order when protocols define an order.
Go's standard library provides many cross-platform abstractions but cannot erase every operating-system difference.
cgo is not required for ordinary pure-Go programs.
Using cgo introduces native compiler and platform dependencies.
cgo can complicate cross-compilation.
Calls across the Go and C boundary have additional rules and costs.
Go pointers passed to C must obey cgo pointer rules.
C resources must be released according to the C interface contract.
A stable C ABI can provide a language-neutral native boundary.
Go itself does not define one universal dynamic-library plugin ABI across all platforms.
The plugin standard-library package provides dynamic loading on supported systems with significant compatibility constraints.
Go plugins require closely compatible toolchains and dependencies.
A general cross-platform module system should not assume Go's plugin package is universally available.
A process boundary can provide stronger language and ABI separation than in-process dynamic loading.
Protocols can connect components written in different programming languages.
An ABI and a network protocol are different contracts.
An ABI defines binary interaction inside compatible process environments.
A protocol defines communication representation and behavior between components.
A stable protocol can allow a Go component to communicate with components written in C, C++, C#, PHP, or other languages.
Protocol compatibility requires defined serialization and behavior rather than shared source language.
Validation and sanitization are different operations.
Output encoding depends on the destination context.
HTML output requires HTML-appropriate encoding.
SQL data should use parameterized query facilities.
File paths derived from users require boundary validation.
Authentication determines identity.
Authorization determines permitted actions.
Authentication and authorization are different responsibilities.
Authorization must be enforced by trusted service behavior.
Passwords should not be stored as plaintext.
Passwords should use an appropriate password-hashing algorithm.
The crypto/rand package provides cryptographically secure random bytes according to its contract.
The math/rand package is not a substitute for cryptographic randomness.
TLS protects network traffic between endpoints when correctly configured.
The crypto/tls package provides TLS functionality.
TLS does not make unsafe application logic automatically secure.
HTTPS is HTTP transported over TLS.
A server should validate TLS configuration according to its security requirements.
Certificates establish properties according to the trust model and validation performed.
Go does not eliminate all security vulnerabilities.
Logic errors remain possible.
Authorization failures remain possible.
Resource exhaustion remains possible.
Unbounded allocations remain possible.
Unbounded goroutines remain possible.
Denial-of-service vulnerabilities remain possible.
Security requires deliberate system design.
A context timeout can help bound external operations.
Input-size limits can help bound resource consumption.
Worker limits can help bound concurrency.
Connection limits can help bound service resource use.
Backpressure prevents producers from indefinitely overwhelming consumers.
Buffered channels provide finite queues, not unlimited capacity.
A bounded system should define what happens when capacity is reached.
Dropping work, blocking producers, rejecting requests, and applying backpressure are distinct policies.
The correct capacity policy depends on the system's contract.
Go encourages explicit dependencies through ordinary values and interfaces.
Package-level global mutable state can make testing and reasoning harder.
Initialization behavior can be provided by init functions.
An init function runs automatically during package initialization.
A package can contain multiple init functions according to Go rules.
init functions cannot be called directly.
Excessive hidden initialization can make startup behavior difficult to reason about.
Explicit initialization is easier to control when failure must be reported.
An init function cannot return an error.
Operations that can fail and require operator handling are often better represented by explicit initialization functions.
Package initialization order follows Go's defined dependency rules.
Import cycles are not permitted.
A package cannot directly or indirectly import itself.
Avoiding import cycles encourages directional package dependencies.
Packages should have bounded responsibilities.
A package's exported API should be smaller than its internal implementation when practical.
The cmd directory is a common project convention for executable commands but is not a language requirement.
The internal directory behavior is enforced by Go tooling.
Project layout conventions are not all language rules.
Go does not require a src directory for modern module-based projects.
Go does not require object-oriented directory structures.
Simple project layouts are preferable when additional hierarchy provides no useful boundary.
Build automation should remain repeatable.
go build resolves and compiles package dependencies according to module configuration.
go test can build test binaries automatically.
The -count option can control test caching behavior in applicable go test usage.
The -run option selects tests by name pattern.
Benchmarks can measure performance under controlled test conditions.
Benchmark results depend on hardware, environment, workload, and compiler/runtime versions.
Optimization should be based on measured requirements rather than assumption.
pprof provides profiling support for Go programs.
Profiles can identify CPU and memory behavior.
A profile describes observed execution and does not prove all possible performance behavior.
Allocations can create garbage-collection work.
Reducing unnecessary allocations can improve some workloads.
Avoiding every allocation is not automatically a useful optimization.
Clear code should not be sacrificed without evidence that complexity is justified.
Compiler optimizations can change across Go versions.
Go binaries commonly contain substantial runtime support.
A Go executable can often be deployed as a single native binary when its dependencies and build mode permit.
DNS resolution behavior can vary according to build configuration and platform.
Deployment requirements should be verified from the actual build.
Environment variables can configure Go applications.
os.Getenv reads an environment variable.
os.LookupEnv distinguishes an unset variable from a variable set to an empty string.
Environment variables are not automatically secret.
Secrets require appropriate access control regardless of configuration mechanism.
Command-line arguments are available through os.Args.
Sensitive secrets should not automatically be placed on command lines.
The flag package provides basic command-line option parsing.
Configuration should have defined precedence when multiple sources can provide values.
Configuration parsing should reject invalid critical values rather than silently inventing unsafe defaults.
Defaults should be deliberate.
Operational errors should be observable.
Logs provide evidence about application events.
Logs should not contain unnecessary secrets.
Structured logs can make machine processing easier.
A log entry should distinguish informational operation from warning and failure conditions.
Logging an error does not handle the error by itself.
Returning an error and logging an error are separate actions.
Logging the same error repeatedly at multiple layers can create noisy duplicate records.
The layer responsible for operational reporting should be defined.
Graceful shutdown allows a service to stop accepting new work and finish or cancel active work according to policy.
Operating-system signals can request process termination.
os/signal provides signal handling facilities.
Shutdown should have a bounded deadline when indefinite waiting is unacceptable.
A service should report degraded operation according to actual capability rather than generic failure wording.
An unavailable optional dependency does not necessarily make every service capability unavailable.
A required dependency failure can prevent the dependent capability from operating.
System status should reflect actual system state.
A dispatcher coordinates work among components according to defined responsibilities.
A dispatcher should distinguish routing failure from execution failure.
A dispatcher should preserve useful error information returned by downstream components.
A dispatcher should not transform every downstream failure into one generic error.
A module should have a bounded responsibility.
A module should not depend on undocumented internals of another module.
A module's successful qualification establishes only the conditions tested by qualification.
Hot loading a module does not prove every runtime operation will succeed.
Module discovery, qualification, activation, dispatch, and execution are distinct lifecycle stages.
Failures should identify the lifecycle stage in which they occurred.
A system that continues after a nonfatal module failure should report degraded capability accurately.
A system should not claim total service unavailability when only one bounded capability failed.
A response should be grounded in available information.
A system should distinguish not knowing an answer from being technically unavailable.
A knowledge retrieval miss is not inherently an HTTP service failure.
A reasoning failure is not inherently a network failure.
A persistence failure is not inherently a knowledge failure.
Retrieved information should be ranked according to the actual question rather than merely broad topic overlap.
Programming-language operators are meaningful retrieval terms.
Go uses == for equality on comparable values.
Go does not use ===.
PHP defines == and === with different comparison semantics.
C# uses == but does not use === as an equality operator.
A question about an operator should retain enough language context to avoid mixing semantics from different languages.
Knowledge about multiple languages should remain distinguishable by language.
Go favors explicit control flow and explicit error handling.
Go favors composition through packages, structs, and interfaces rather than class inheritance.
Go provides garbage collection but still requires explicit management of external resources.
Go provides goroutines and channels but concurrency still requires deliberate lifecycle and synchronization design.
Go supports cross-platform compilation but cross-platform behavior still requires deliberate engineering.
Go can interoperate with C, but native interoperability introduces additional ownership and portability constraints.
Go can build small command-line programs as well as long-running network services.
Simple Go code is preferable to unnecessary abstraction when both satisfy the same requirements.
Deterministic behavior requires explicit control of inputs, state, ordering, serialization, and external dependencies.
Correctness is more important than merely producing output.
A filesystem organizes and provides access to stored data.
A filesystem commonly organizes data as files and directories.
A filesystem defines naming rules.
A filesystem defines path semantics.
A filesystem defines metadata.
A filesystem defines operations for creating, reading, modifying, renaming, and removing filesystem objects.
A filesystem can provide permissions.
A filesystem can provide links.
A filesystem can provide allocation and durability mechanisms.
Exact filesystem behavior depends on the filesystem and operating system.
A filesystem is not the same thing as a storage device.
A storage device can contain one filesystem.
A storage device can contain multiple filesystems.
A filesystem can span storage mechanisms according to its implementation.
A filesystem can exist entirely in memory.
A file is a filesystem object used to represent stored data according to filesystem semantics.
A file can contain text.
A file can contain binary data.
A file can contain structured data.
A file can contain executable code.
The filesystem does not necessarily understand the application's interpretation of file contents.
A directory organizes filesystem entries.
A directory is not merely a text list of filenames.
Directory representation is controlled by the filesystem.
A path identifies a filesystem location according to path-resolution rules.
A path is not the filesystem object itself.
A path can refer to a file.
A path can refer to a directory.
A path can refer to a symbolic link.
A path can fail to resolve.
A path can resolve differently in different namespaces.
A path can resolve differently depending on the working directory.
Do not confuse a path with permanent object identity.
An absolute path begins from a defined filesystem root or platform-specific absolute location.
A relative path is interpreted relative to a base location.
The process working directory is commonly the base for unresolved relative paths.
Relative paths are context dependent.
Critical state should not depend accidentally on an unknown working directory.
Path syntax differs between operating systems.
Unix-like systems commonly use forward slash as a path separator.
Windows commonly uses backslash in native path syntax and supports additional path forms.
Windows can use drive letters.
Windows can use UNC paths.
Unix-like systems generally use one rooted namespace.
Portable software should not construct paths solely through assumptions about separators or drive syntax.
Use platform-appropriate path facilities.
The visible filesystem root does not necessarily correspond to one physical storage device.
Mounts can attach additional filesystems into the namespace.
Containers can expose different filesystem roots.
Chroot-like mechanisms can alter visible roots.
The visible namespace is not necessarily the physical storage topology.
A mount attaches a filesystem or filesystem view to a location in a namespace.
Crossing a mount point can move an operation onto a different filesystem.
Different mounted filesystems can have different semantics.
Different filesystems can have different capacity.
Different filesystems can have different durability guarantees.
Different filesystems can have different case behavior.
An operation that is atomic within one filesystem might not be atomic across filesystems.
Do not assume a rename across filesystem boundaries has the same semantics as a rename within one filesystem.
A path component is one part of a path.
Special path components can exist.
On many systems, "." represents the current directory.
On many systems, ".." represents the parent directory.
Untrusted path components require validation.
A user-supplied filename should not automatically be concatenated onto a privileged base path.
Input containing ".." can escape an intended directory under naive validation.
Absolute path input can ignore an intended base path depending on path construction.
Path separators embedded in input can create additional components.
A path beginning with an allowed text prefix can still resolve outside an intended directory.
Symbolic links can redirect resolution.
Mount points can alter resolution.
Case rules can alter comparisons.
Normalization can alter representation.
Path normalization simplifies selected path syntax according to platform rules.
Normalization can remove redundant separators.
Normalization can resolve selected "." components.
Normalization does not prove a path is authorized.
Normalization does not necessarily resolve symbolic links.
Normalization does not prove the target exists.
Normalization does not prove the target remains unchanged.
Do not confuse normalized path text with validated filesystem identity.
Canonicalization can resolve a path into a standardized or fully resolved representation according to a platform API.
A canonical path can still become stale after resolution.
Time-of-check to time-of-use races can occur.
A path can be checked.
Another process can replace part of the path.
The original process can then operate on a different object.
Security-sensitive code should use filesystem APIs designed to maintain object identity where possible.
An open file handle or descriptor can refer to an already resolved object.
Operations through that handle can avoid selected path re-resolution races.
A filename identifies an entry within a directory according to filesystem naming rules.
Filename rules differ.
Some filesystems are case-sensitive.
Some filesystems are case-insensitive.
Some preserve original case while comparing names case-insensitively.
Do not assume they identify the same file.
The filesystem determines the behavior.
Portable applications should avoid creating names differing only by case when cross-platform compatibility matters.
Filesystems can restrict characters in names.
Filesystems can reserve names.
Operating systems can impose additional restrictions.
Unix-like filesystems generally permit many bytes in filenames except filesystem-defined restrictions.
Portable naming conventions should use a conservative set when files move between platforms.
Path length can be bounded.
Do not assume one universal maximum path length.
Applications should handle path construction failure.
A file extension is part of a filename convention.
A file extension does not prove file content.
A file named data.json can contain invalid JSON.
A file named image.png can contain unrelated bytes.
A file named program.exe can be invalid.
Validate contents when content type matters.
Metadata can include timestamps.
Metadata can include ownership.
Metadata can include permissions.
Metadata can include filesystem attributes.
Metadata can include link information.
Metadata semantics vary.
File size does not necessarily equal physical storage consumption.
Sparse files can have large logical size while consuming less physical storage.
Filesystem metadata consumes storage.
Allocation units can cause physical consumption to exceed logical file size.
Do not equate logical size with exact disk consumption.
A sparse file contains logical regions that do not require ordinary physical allocation according to filesystem semantics.
Writing into a sparse region can allocate storage.
Copying a sparse file with an unaware tool can expand its physical storage use.
Timestamps can include creation time where supported.
Timestamps can include access time.
Timestamps can include metadata-change time.
Timestamp precision differs.
Timestamp update policy differs.
Timestamps can be modified.
Timestamps are not secure proof of history.
A modification timestamp indicates filesystem metadata according to its semantics.
It does not prove who changed the file.
It does not prove the clock was correct.
Do not use timestamps alone as strong authenticity evidence.
File ownership identifies operating-system ownership according to filesystem and security semantics.
Ownership does not necessarily identify the human who created the contents.
A service account can create files.
An administrator can change ownership.
A copied file can acquire new ownership.
Ownership is one security attribute.
Permissions control allowed operations according to the filesystem and operating-system security model.
Unix-like systems commonly provide owner, group, and other permission classes.
Read permission allows selected reading.
Write permission allows selected modification.
Directory permissions have different operational consequences from regular-file permissions.
Execute permission on a directory commonly permits traversal or lookup.
Directory permissions can permit removal or replacement of directory entries.
A read-only file inside a writable directory can sometimes be replaced through directory operations according to platform semantics.
Do not evaluate file security from file mode alone.
Windows commonly uses ACL-based security.
Unix-like systems can also support ACLs.
Least privilege applies to filesystem access.
A process needing read access should not receive write access unnecessarily.
Executable code can be read-only during normal operation.
Configuration can be read-only when runtime modification is unnecessary.
Mutable application data can use dedicated writable directories.
Logs can use dedicated writable directories.
Temporary files can use dedicated temporary directories.
Separating writable state from executable code reduces risk.
A hard link creates another directory entry referring to the same underlying file object where supported.
Two hard-link paths can refer to the same file.
Changing the file through one hard link changes the same underlying content visible through the other.
Deleting one hard link does not necessarily delete the underlying file while other links remain.
A file's lifetime can depend on references rather than one filename.
Directories are commonly restricted from ordinary hard linking.
Cross-filesystem hard links are generally not supported because the underlying object belongs to one filesystem.
A symbolic link is distinct from its target.
Such a link is commonly called dangling or broken.
A symbolic link can point to a directory.
Recursive traversal should account for links.
Do not follow symbolic links blindly during deletion, copying, indexing, or security-sensitive traversal.
A recursive operation can escape an intended directory if links are followed without policy.
A recursive traversal can loop if cycles are possible.
Track identity or apply link policy.
A shortcut used by a graphical environment is not necessarily a filesystem symbolic link.
A junction on Windows provides filesystem redirection semantics distinct from ordinary shortcut files.
Platform-specific link types should be handled according to their actual semantics.
Unix-like systems commonly represent open files using file descriptors.
A descriptor or handle is not the file contents.
It is a process-visible reference to an operating-system resource.
Descriptors and handles have lifetimes.
They should be closed when no longer required.
A file can remain open after its pathname changes.
A file can remain open after its directory entry is removed on systems whose semantics permit it.
Therefore path existence and open-object existence can differ.
On Unix-like filesystems, unlinking an open file commonly removes a directory entry while the underlying object remains accessible through open descriptors until references are released.
Windows behavior can differ depending on sharing and deletion semantics.
Do not assume one platform's open-file deletion behavior applies everywhere.
An open operation can specify access mode.
A file can be opened for reading.
A file can be opened for writing.
A file can be opened for creation.
A file can be opened for exclusive creation.
A file can be opened for truncation.
Exact flags and combinations depend on API.
Some modes truncate immediately.
Exclusive creation can create a file only when the target does not already exist according to the API's atomic semantics.
This can prevent selected creation races.
Checking existence and then creating in two separate operations can race.
Another process can create the file between the check and creation.
A read request for N bytes can return fewer than N bytes.
This does not always indicate failure.
End-of-file can produce fewer bytes.
Streams and special files can produce partial reads.
Signals or platform conditions can affect operations.
Code should follow the API's exact read contract.
File writing can be partial.
A write request can write fewer bytes than requested according to the API and resource type.
Robust code should handle partial writes where the interface permits them.
A successful partial write is not complete operation success.
Track remaining bytes.
Disk-full conditions can occur after some bytes were written.
This can leave partial files.
Use atomic replacement strategies for important complete-file updates.
Appending writes data at the end according to append semantics.
Concurrent append behavior depends on filesystem and API guarantees.
Do not assume multiple writes from multiple processes become one indivisible record.
A logical record can require one atomic write or explicit locking according to the system.
Log files written concurrently require deliberate design.
A file offset identifies a position for sequential or random access according to file API semantics.
Multiple descriptors can have independent offsets.
Concurrent operations can change offsets.
Random access reads or writes at explicit offsets can avoid shared-position races.
File locking coordinates access according to locking semantics.
Locks can be mandatory in selected environments.
Advisory locks require cooperating processes.
