Perform network identity validation according to the protocol.
Discovery and trust are separate.
After resolution, the application should establish the peer's actual protocol identity.
DNS compromise should not automatically become protocol authority.
Use cryptographic or protocol identity where required.
DNS changes can be operationally audited.
Record the requested change.
Record the actor or authorized system.
Record the old state.
Record the new state.
Record the time.
Record provider acceptance.
Record authoritative verification.
These are separate events.
A requested DNS change is not the same as an observed authoritative change.
Do not report deployment complete before required verification.
DNS automation should be idempotent where practical.
Setting an RRset to a desired value repeatedly should converge on that desired state.
Appending duplicate records repeatedly is not equivalent.
Represent desired state explicitly.
Read current state.
Calculate required change.
Apply bounded change.
Verify.
Do not delete unrelated records accidentally.
One automation system should not overwrite records owned by another system without coordination.
TXT records are especially prone to multiple independent application uses.
Replacing the entire TXT RRset can delete unrelated verification or policy records.
Modify the intended RRset carefully.
Understand provider APIs.
Some APIs replace an entire RRset.
Some operate on individual records.
Do not assume.
A DNS record can remain after the application using it is gone.
Stale DNS can direct clients to retired infrastructure.
Stale DNS can leak architecture.
Remove obsolete records according to lifecycle policy.
Operational state and historical record are separate.
Domain registration and DNS hosting are separate functions.
A registrar manages domain registration with the relevant registry.
Changing registrar does not necessarily require changing authoritative DNS.
Changing DNS hosting does not necessarily change domain registration.
Nameserver delegation at the registrar or registry connects the registered domain to authoritative DNS.
Do not confuse registrar failure with authoritative DNS failure.
DNS itself does not establish legal ownership.
WHOIS or registration data is not DNS resolution.
Keep administrative systems distinct.
Domain expiration can ultimately affect DNS availability.
A healthy authoritative server cannot compensate indefinitely for loss of delegation caused by registration state.
Operational monitoring can include registration status separately from DNS health.
DNSSEC key management requires lifecycle planning.
Keys can be introduced.
Keys can be retired.
Parent DS records can need coordinated changes.
Incorrect sequencing can break validation.
Do not rotate DNSSEC keys by guessing.
Follow the defined DNSSEC key-rollover procedure used by the implementation and policy.
Monitor signature validity.
Do not assume signed zones remain valid forever without maintenance.
Clock correctness matters for cryptographic validity.
Severely incorrect time can affect DNSSEC validation.
Time problems can therefore appear as DNSSEC failures.
Verify system time when signature validation fails unexpectedly.
Fix the actual cause.
A DNS resolver can use QNAME minimization to reduce unnecessary disclosure during iterative resolution.
This changes which portions of the requested name are sent to selected authoritative levels.
It is a privacy improvement.
Implementation details should not be mistaken for application semantics.
DNS cookies and other protocol mechanisms can improve selected security or operational properties.
They do not replace application authentication.
DNS has protocol standards.
Provider-specific DNS features can extend operational behavior.
Do not describe proprietary flattening, traffic steering, failover, or proxying as universal DNS behavior.
State whether behavior comes from DNS itself or a provider.
A proxied DNS provider can return provider addresses instead of origin addresses.
The DNS answer can therefore intentionally identify an intermediary.
This does not mean the origin server moved.
CDNs frequently use DNS as part of traffic steering.
A hostname can resolve differently by region.
Use the relevant resolver and vantage point.
Do not treat geographic variance as corruption without evidence.
Anycast can allow the same IP address to be announced from multiple network locations.
DNS can return one address while routing directs different clients to different physical infrastructure.
Therefore one IP address does not necessarily mean one physical server.
DNS answers alone cannot reveal complete physical topology.
DNS should remain small and boring where requirements are simple.
If one hostname needs one address, an A or AAAA record can be sufficient.
Do not introduce complex traffic steering without need.
Use the smallest configuration satisfying requirements.
A reliable DNS design has clear authority.
A reliable DNS design has redundant authoritative service appropriate to its requirements.
A reliable DNS design has deliberate TTLs.
A reliable DNS design has valid delegation.
A reliable DNS design has correct glue where required.
A reliable DNS design validates DNSSEC if DNSSEC is used.
A reliable DNS design does not expose open recursion without reason.
A reliable DNS design monitors authoritative answers.
A reliable DNS design monitors client-visible resolution where required.
A reliable DNS design distinguishes name resolution from service health.
A reliable DNS design does not use DNS as unverified application identity.
A reliable DNS design plans changes around cache lifetime.
A reliable DNS design preserves evidence during incidents.
Do not confuse a DNS name with a URL.
Do not confuse a DNS name with an IP address.
Do not confuse a zone with a physical server.
Do not confuse an authoritative server with a recursive resolver.
Do not confuse a recursive resolver with a stub resolver.
Do not confuse a referral with a final answer.
Do not confuse glue with ordinary authoritative child-zone data.
Do not confuse an A record with an AAAA record.
Do not confuse CNAME with an IP address.
Do not confuse CNAME aliasing with application identity.
Do not confuse MX priority with generic record ordering.
Do not confuse TXT storage with one universal TXT meaning.
Do not confuse cache expiration with one global propagation event.
Do not confuse SERVFAIL with NXDOMAIN.
Do not confuse timeout with NXDOMAIN.
Do not confuse DNS success with service availability.
Do not confuse DNS failure with server failure.
Do not confuse port 53 availability with correct DNS data.
Do not confuse process existence with DNS health.
Do not confuse one successful authoritative server with healthy delegation.
Do not confuse one recursive resolver's cache with authoritative state.
Do not confuse local hosts-file resolution with public DNS.
Do not confuse reverse DNS with authentication.
Do not confuse DNSSEC with DNS encryption.
Do not confuse DoH with DNSSEC.
Do not confuse a DNSSEC signature's presence with successful validation.
Do not confuse DNS discovery with protocol trust.
Do not confuse DNS record publication with application authorization.
Do not confuse a provider API accepting a change with authoritative verification.
Do not confuse domain registration with DNS hosting.
Do not confuse DNS control with permanent application ownership.
Do not confuse an IP address with one physical machine.
Do not confuse a resolver's current answer with permanent truth.
When diagnosing a hostname that does not resolve, identify the exact name.
Identify the requested record type.
Query the local resolver if application behavior is being investigated.
Query the configured recursive resolver.
Query authoritative DNS.
Inspect the response code.
Inspect delegation.
Inspect all authoritative servers.
Inspect local overrides.
Inspect split-DNS behavior.
Do not guess from one failed application request.
When diagnosing a hostname that resolves to the wrong address, determine which resolver supplied the answer.
Inspect its TTL.
Determine whether the answer is cached.
Query authority directly.
Compare current authoritative data.
Determine whether multiple DNS views exist.
Do not immediately edit authoritative DNS if authority already contains the intended value.
The problem can be cache state.
When changing a service address, inspect the existing TTL before the migration.
Lower TTL in advance when faster cache turnover is required.
Wait for older high-TTL cached records to expire where the migration plan requires it.
Change the authoritative address.
Verify every authoritative server or provider-defined authoritative endpoint.
Observe recursive resolvers.
Do not assume all clients move instantly.
When a DNS query succeeds but an application fails, stop treating DNS as the only suspect.
Test address reachability.
Test the required transport port.
Test application protocol.
Test application authentication.
Identify the actual failing stage.
DNS has already supplied evidence that name resolution succeeded.
When a DNS query returns SERVFAIL, do not create an A record blindly.
Inspect authoritative reachability.
Inspect DNSSEC.
Inspect server responses.
Inspect resolver behavior.
It does not identify one universal cause.
If authority returns the name, a recursive resolver can hold a negative cache.
If authority also returns NXDOMAIN, the name is absent according to current authoritative DNS.
If the name should exist, repair authoritative state.
Then account for negative cache lifetime.
When authoritative servers disagree, query them individually.
Compare SOA and record data.
Determine whether replication is incomplete.
Determine whether one server serves stale configuration.
Determine whether one server is incorrectly authoritative.
Do not average the answers.
DNS correctness is not determined by majority vote unless a specific architecture defines such policy outside DNS.
When DNSSEC validation fails, preserve validation.
Identify the failing chain.
Inspect DS.
Inspect DNSKEY.
Inspect signatures.
Inspect validity times.
Inspect algorithms.
Inspect parent-child coordination.
Do not disable DNSSEC merely to make the error disappear unless the authorized operational decision is to remove DNSSEC correctly through its defined process.
When building software that accepts a hostname, validate the hostname syntax required by the application.
Resolve it through the intended resolver mechanism.
Treat returned addresses as current resolution results.
If network policy restricts destinations, validate the actual destination addresses.
Handle IPv4 and IPv6 according to requirements.
Do not convert resolution failure into a fabricated address.
DNS can discover candidate endpoints.
The protocol can then validate candidate identity.
Discovery can vary.
What DNS information is currently available for this name according to the resolver, authority, cache state, query type, and DNS policy involved?
It does not answer:
Is the application running?
Is the destination trustworthy?
Is the service healthy?
Will every resolver return the same answer right now?
Will this address remain the same?
Those are different questions.
Use DNS for naming.
Use network tests for connectivity.
Use application protocols for service behavior.
And when DNS evidence is incomplete, do not turn a resolver's uncertainty into a fabricated answer.
C is a general-purpose programming language.
C is a compiled programming language.
C was designed for efficient systems programming.
C provides relatively direct access to memory and machine-level resources.
C source files commonly use the .c filename extension.
C header files commonly use the .h filename extension.
ISO C refers to standardized versions of the C programming language.
Portable C code avoids unnecessary dependence on implementation-specific behavior.
The main function returns an int.
Returning zero from main conventionally indicates successful program termination.
A nonzero program exit status conventionally indicates some form of failure.
C is case-sensitive.
Uppercase and lowercase identifiers are distinct in C.
Statements in C commonly end with a semicolon.
A block of C statements is enclosed by braces.
Whitespace generally separates tokens but is otherwise flexible in many contexts.
C comments can use /* and */.
Modern C also supports // line comments.
An identifier names an object, function, type, label, macro, or other program entity.
C identifiers can contain letters, digits, and underscores subject to language rules.
C has reserved keywords that cannot be used as ordinary identifiers.
A variable is a named object whose stored value can change during its lifetime.
A declaration introduces information about an identifier.
A definition provides the entity described by a declaration where required.
A function declaration describes a function's interface.
A function definition provides a function's implementation.
A function prototype declares the types of a function's parameters and return value.
Calling a function with an incompatible declaration can produce incorrect behavior or a diagnostic.
C is statically typed.
Every object and expression in C has a type.
The short type is an integer type.
The int type is an integer type.
The long type is an integer type.
The long long type is an integer type.
Unsigned integer arithmetic has defined modulo behavior.
Signed integer overflow has undefined behavior.
The float type represents floating-point values.
The double type represents floating-point values with at least the precision requirements specified for double.
The long double type provides an implementation-defined extended floating-point type.
The _Bool type represents boolean values in C.
The stdbool.h header provides the bool, true, and false convenience macros in versions of C where they are defined by that header.
The sizeof operator reports the size of an object or type in bytes.
sizeof returns a value of type size_t.
A C byte is the size of char.
C does not require a byte to contain exactly eight bits.
uint8_t is an exact-width unsigned 8-bit integer type when provided.
int16_t and uint16_t represent exact-width 16-bit integer types when provided.
int32_t and uint32_t represent exact-width 32-bit integer types when provided.
int64_t and uint64_t represent exact-width 64-bit integer types when provided.
size_t is an unsigned integer type suitable for representing object sizes.
ptrdiff_t is a signed integer type suitable for representing pointer differences.
A constant is a value that does not change during evaluation.
Floating-point literals represent floating-point constants.
Character constants represent integer values associated with characters.
A string literal normally includes an implicit terminating null character.
A C string is conventionally a sequence of characters terminated by a null character.
The null character has numeric value zero.
The null character is commonly written as '\\0'.
A C string does not intrinsically store its own length.
Functions processing C strings commonly search for the terminating null character.
strlen returns the number of characters before the terminating null character.
strlen does not count the terminating null character.
strlen requires a valid null-terminated string.
A character array is not necessarily a valid C string.
A pointer stores an address or pointer value referring to an object, function, or permitted special location.
The address-of operator & obtains the address of an object where permitted.
The dereference operator * accesses the object designated by a pointer where permitted.
A null pointer does not designate an object or function.
The NULL macro can represent a null pointer constant in appropriate contexts.
Using a pointer after the lifetime of its referenced object has ended can have undefined behavior.
Pointer arithmetic is defined only within constrained relationships to arrays and objects.
A pointer can point to an element of an array.
Adding one to a pointer to an array element advances it to the next element.
Pointer arithmetic is scaled according to the pointed-to type.
C arrays contain a fixed number of elements once defined.
Array elements have the same element type.
The expression array[index] accesses an array element when the index is valid.
Array indexing outside the permitted bounds has undefined behavior.
C does not automatically perform array bounds checking.
An array expression often converts to a pointer to its first element.
An array and a pointer are not the same type of object.
After an array parameter is adjusted to a pointer parameter, sizeof no longer reports the original array size.
A multidimensional array is an array whose elements are themselves arrays.
C stores ordinary multidimensional arrays in row-major order.
A structure groups multiple named members into one type.
The struct keyword declares or defines structure types.
The . operator accesses a member of a structure object.
The -> operator accesses a structure member through a pointer to a structure.
The expression ptr->member is equivalent to (*ptr).member when ptr validly points to the structure.
A union allows multiple members to share overlapping storage.
Only appropriate access to a union's stored representation should be relied upon according to C rules.
An enumeration defines a set of named integer constants.
The enum keyword declares enumeration types.
typedef creates an alias for an existing type.
typedef does not create a fundamentally new distinct type.
The const qualifier indicates that an object should not be modified through that qualified access path.
Attempting to modify an object defined as const through an inappropriate cast can have undefined behavior.
The volatile qualifier indicates that accesses to an object have observable behavior relevant to the implementation.
volatile is not a general thread-synchronization mechanism.
The restrict qualifier can express pointer aliasing assumptions for optimization and semantics where applicable.
The static keyword has different meanings depending on context.
A static local variable retains its stored value between function calls.
A file-scope identifier declared static has internal linkage where applicable.
A static function at file scope is visible by name only within its translation unit.
The extern keyword can declare an identifier whose definition exists elsewhere.
External linkage allows appropriately declared entities to be referenced across translation units.
A translation unit is the source processed by the compiler after preprocessing.
A C project can contain multiple translation units.
Header files commonly contain declarations shared between translation units.
Function definitions are normally placed in source files.
Header guards prevent repeated inclusion from causing unwanted duplicate declarations or definitions.
A conventional header guard uses #ifndef, #define, and #endif.
The C preprocessor operates before ordinary compilation.
Preprocessor directives begin with #.
A macro performs preprocessing substitution rather than behaving exactly like a C function.
Function-like macros can evaluate their arguments more than once if written carelessly.
Macro parameters should often be parenthesized appropriately when used in expressions.
Conditional compilation uses directives such as #if, #ifdef, #ifndef, #elif, #else, and #endif.
Conditional compilation can select platform-specific implementation code.
Platform-specific implementation code can be isolated behind a common interface.
Portable interfaces allow different platform implementations to provide equivalent behavior.
The compiler translates C source into lower-level program representation or object code.
A compile error prevents successful translation of a translation unit.
A linker error occurs when required definitions or symbol relationships cannot be resolved during linking.
A runtime error occurs while an executing program is running.
Warnings should be reviewed rather than automatically ignored.
-Wextra enables additional warnings in GCC and compatible compilers.
The -std option can select a particular C language standard mode in compilers that support that option.
Optimization options can affect generated machine code without changing the intended defined behavior of a correct program.
Undefined behavior permits the C implementation to impose no requirements on the resulting behavior.
A program containing undefined behavior cannot rely on a particular result from that operation.
Implementation-defined behavior is behavior where the implementation selects and documents a permitted choice.
Unspecified behavior permits one of multiple possibilities without requiring the implementation to document which occurs.
Portable C programs distinguish defined behavior from undefined, implementation-defined, and unspecified behavior.
The assignment operator = stores a value into an object where permitted.
The equality operator == compares two values for equality according to their types.
The inequality operator != compares two values for inequality.
The relational operators <, <=, >, and >= compare values according to C rules.
The logical AND operator is &&.
The logical OR operator is ||.
The logical NOT operator is !.
The bitwise AND operator is &.
The bitwise XOR operator is ^.
The bitwise complement operator is ~.
The left shift operator is <<.
The right shift operator is >>.
Logical operators and bitwise operators are not interchangeable.
The && operator uses short-circuit evaluation.
The || operator uses short-circuit evaluation.
The conditional operator ?: selects between two expressions according to a condition.
The increment operator ++ increases an applicable scalar value by one.
The decrement operator -- decreases an applicable scalar value by one.
Prefix and postfix increment have different value computation timing.
Operator precedence determines how expressions are grouped when parentheses do not override that grouping.
Parentheses can make intended expression grouping explicit.
The if statement conditionally executes code.
The switch statement selects among cases based on an integer or enumeration expression.
A case label identifies a value handled by a switch statement.
The break statement can terminate the nearest enclosing loop or switch.
Missing break statements in switch cases can intentionally or accidentally allow fallthrough.
Intentional fallthrough should be made clear to maintainers.
The for statement provides a looping construct.
The do statement executes its body before testing its loop condition.
The continue statement skips to the next applicable loop iteration.
A function can accept parameters.
C passes function arguments by value.
Passing a pointer allows a function to access an object owned by its caller.
Passing a pointer is still passing the pointer value by value.
C does not provide pass-by-reference semantics as a separate language mechanism.
A function returning void does not return a value.
A void pointer can hold a converted pointer to an object type.
void * is commonly used for generic object pointers.
A void pointer must be converted or assigned appropriately before dereferencing as a typed object.
Function pointers can be used to implement callbacks.
A callback allows one component to invoke behavior supplied by another component through an agreed function interface.
An ABI is an Application Binary Interface.
An ABI defines binary-level conventions required for independently compiled components to interact correctly.
An ABI can define calling conventions.
An ABI can define symbol naming and binary interface expectations.
A source-level API and a binary ABI are related but distinct concepts.
An API defines interfaces used by source code.
An ABI defines compatibility at the compiled binary level.
Changing a public structure layout can break ABI compatibility.
Changing a function signature can break API and ABI compatibility.
Modules loaded dynamically must satisfy the binary interface expected by their host.
A dynamically loaded module can expose a known entry point used by its host.
The host and module must agree on structures passed across their binary boundary.
Versioned ABIs can prevent incompatible modules from being accepted.
ABI validation should occur before an incompatible module is allowed to operate.
Dynamic libraries contain code that can be loaded and linked at runtime or program startup depending on the platform.
Shared libraries on Unix-like systems commonly use the .so filename extension.
Dynamic libraries on Windows commonly use the .dll filename extension.
Platform-specific dynamic loading APIs differ.
C itself does not define a standard dynamic-library loading API.
Memory for ordinary local automatic objects is managed according to their scope and lifetime.
Dynamic memory can be requested using malloc.
malloc returns a pointer to allocated storage or a null pointer when allocation fails.
calloc allocates storage and initializes its bytes to zero.
free releases dynamically allocated storage obtained through compatible allocation functions.
Passing an invalid pointer to free has undefined behavior.
Freeing the same allocation more than once has undefined behavior.
Failing to release no-longer-needed dynamic allocations can cause memory leaks.
A dangling pointer refers to storage whose lifetime has ended or that is otherwise no longer valid.
Setting a pointer to NULL after free can help prevent accidental reuse of that particular pointer but does not repair other aliases.
Every successful allocation should have clearly defined ownership and lifetime.
Memory ownership conventions make C systems easier to reason about.
A buffer has a finite capacity.
Writing beyond the bounds of a buffer has undefined behavior and can create security vulnerabilities.
Buffer sizes must be tracked explicitly when the program cannot otherwise derive them safely.
A destination buffer must have enough capacity for data written into it.
strcpy does not know the capacity of its destination buffer.
strcat does not know the capacity of its destination buffer.
Unbounded string copying can cause buffer overflows.
snprintf accepts a destination size and can be used for bounded formatted output.
The return value of snprintf should be checked when truncation matters.
memcpy copies a specified number of bytes between non-overlapping memory regions.
Using memcpy with overlapping source and destination regions has undefined behavior.
memmove supports overlapping source and destination regions.
memcmp compares byte sequences.
strcmp compares null-terminated strings lexicographically.
strncmp compares strings up to a specified maximum number of characters.
String comparison using == compares pointer values rather than string contents.
strcmp should be used when ordinary C string contents need to be compared.
stdio.h declares standard input and output facilities.
stdlib.h declares general utility functions including memory allocation and conversions.
stddef.h declares common types and macros such as size_t and ptrdiff_t.
limits.h declares implementation limits for integer types.
float.h declares characteristics of floating-point types.
ctype.h declares character classification and conversion functions.
time.h declares date and time facilities.
errno.h provides access to errno and related error macros.
assert.h provides the assert diagnostic macro.
FILE represents a stream used by standard I/O facilities.
fopen opens a file stream.
fclose closes a file stream.
fread reads binary data from a stream.
fwrite writes binary data to a stream.
fgets reads a bounded line or character sequence from a stream.
fputs writes a string to a stream.
fprintf writes formatted output to a stream.
printf writes formatted output to standard output.
stderr is the standard error stream.
stdout is the standard output stream.
stdin is the standard input stream.
File operations can fail.
Programs should check the results of operations that can fail.
fopen returns a null pointer when it cannot open the requested file.
Binary and text stream behavior can differ across platforms.
Opening a file with mode "rb" requests binary reading.
Writing a file is not guaranteed to place all data permanently on physical storage immediately.
Buffered I/O can delay underlying writes.
fflush requests that buffered output for an applicable stream be transmitted to its host environment.
Closing a writable stream flushes buffered output as part of normal stream closure.
Error handling should be explicit.
A function can return a status value indicating success or failure.
A caller should check error status when failure matters.
errno can provide additional error information for functions documented to set it.
errno should only be interpreted according to the contract of the function that failed.
perror can print a message associated with the current errno value.
Defensive C programming validates assumptions at boundaries.
Lengths received from external input should be validated before being used for memory operations.
Integer overflow can cause size calculations to produce unsafe results.
Network protocols should define serialization explicitly.
Serialization converts structured data into a defined transferable representation.
A protocol should define field sizes and encoding rather than depending on compiler-specific structure layout.
Writing a C struct directly to a network connection does not automatically create a portable protocol.
Structure padding can vary according to implementation and ABI requirements.
Integer byte order can vary between systems.
Network byte order conventionally uses big-endian ordering for many Internet protocols.
Portable protocol implementations explicitly encode and decode multibyte fields.
Deterministic serialization produces the same defined byte representation for the same logical input.
Consensus-sensitive software should not depend on unspecified or undefined behavior.
Consensus-sensitive software should define serialization precisely.
Consensus-sensitive calculations should produce identical required results across supported platforms.
Floating-point behavior can vary in ways that make it unsuitable for some deterministic consensus calculations.
Fixed-width integer arithmetic is often easier to specify deterministically than platform-dependent integer representations.
Platform independence does not require every implementation file to be identical.
Compile-time platform selection can choose an implementation backend.
Runtime platform detection can choose an implementation backend when architecture permits.
Common code should avoid unnecessary operating-system dependencies.
A module interface should define ownership of memory passed across module boundaries.
A module interface should define the lifetime of pointers passed across module boundaries.
A module interface should validate version compatibility.
A module should not assume host internals that are not part of its declared interface.
A host should reject a module that fails required qualification or compatibility checks.
A test verifies expected program behavior.
A negative test verifies correct handling of invalid or prohibited input or behavior.
Regression tests preserve previously established behavior against future changes.
Tests should be deterministic when testing deterministic behavior.
A passing test demonstrates that the tested condition passed for that execution.
A passing test does not prove the absence of every possible defect.
Compiler warnings and tests provide different kinds of evidence.
A clean compilation does not prove runtime correctness.
Static analysis can identify classes of defects without executing the program.
Dynamic analysis examines behavior while a program executes.
Code review can identify design and implementation problems not captured by automated tests.
Small functions with clear responsibilities are generally easier to test.
Initialization should establish valid program state before dependent operations execute.
Cleanup should release resources acquired during execution.
Resource acquisition and release paths should account for intermediate failures.
A program should not continue using a resource after acquisition failed.
The ISO C standard library does not define sockets.
Socket programming requires platform-specific networking facilities or an abstraction.
TCP does not preserve application message boundaries.
An application protocol running over TCP must define its own message framing.
A single send operation does not guarantee a single corresponding receive operation.
Network timeouts prevent some operations from waiting indefinitely.
Ports identify transport-layer service endpoints.
Binding a server socket to a port can fail if the address is unavailable or permission is denied.
HTTP is an application protocol commonly transported over TCP.
A C program can implement an HTTP server using appropriate networking facilities.
HTTP requests contain a request method, target, headers, and optionally a body according to protocol rules.
HTTP responses contain a status, headers, and optionally a body according to protocol rules.
A 404 HTTP status indicates that the requested resource was not found.
A 503 HTTP status indicates that a service is unavailable.
An application should use HTTP status codes according to the actual result of request handling.
A JSON response is text formatted according to JSON syntax.
C does not include JSON processing in the ISO C standard library.
JSON support requires program code or an additional library.
External libraries add dependencies that must be understood and managed.
Minimizing dependencies can reduce deployment complexity.
Avoiding unnecessary dependencies can make systems easier to audit and maintain.
make is not part of the ISO C language.
A Windows batch file can invoke a C compiler and linker directly.
Build scripts should make compilation steps repeatable.
Repeatable builds reduce accidental differences between development runs.
Compiler options should be documented as part of a project's build procedure.
Comments should explain information that is not already obvious from the code.
Comments that contradict the implementation are harmful.
Consistent formatting improves readability.
Braces can make control flow clearer even when the language permits an unbraced statement.
Combining multiple unrelated statements on one line can reduce readability.
C permits highly compact code but compact code is not automatically better code.
Correctness is more important than cleverness.
Defined behavior is more reliable than behavior that merely happens to work on one compiler.
Portable behavior is more reliable across supported platforms than undocumented assumptions.
A deterministic system should make important behavior explicit.
C provides mechanisms rather than automatic safety.
The programmer is responsible for many memory, lifetime, bounds, and resource-management decisions.
Careful validation is essential when C processes untrusted input.
C can provide efficient and predictable systems when its interfaces and invariants are carefully defined.
Debugging seeks to identify the cause of an observed problem.
Debugging is not guessing.
Debugging is not randomly changing code until symptoms disappear.
A symptom is observable incorrect behavior.
A cause is the condition responsible for producing that behavior.
An error message is evidence.
A warning is evidence.
A warning is not automatically the root cause.
A log entry is not automatically the root cause.
A crash location is evidence.
Debugging begins with observation.
Record what actually happened.
Record what was expected to happen.
Identify the difference between expected and observed behavior.
Preserve relevant timestamps.
Preserve relevant logs.
Preserve relevant configuration.
Preserve the software version.
Preserve the source revision when known.
Preserve the operating environment when relevant.
Do not replace observed facts with assumptions.
A reproducible failure is easier to debug than an intermittent failure.
Reproduction means producing the same relevant failure under defined conditions.
A reproduction should identify required initial state.
A reproduction should identify inputs.
A reproduction should identify expected results.
A minimal reproduction removes unrelated conditions while preserving the failure.
Reducing a reproduction can identify the failing boundary.
Change one relevant variable at a time when practical.
Changing many variables simultaneously weakens causal conclusions.
If five things are changed and the problem disappears, the responsible change is unknown.
A hypothesis is a proposed explanation.
A hypothesis is not evidence.
A hypothesis should produce testable predictions.
Reject hypotheses contradicted by evidence.
Prefer explanations supported by observed behavior.
Do not become attached to a hypothesis.
The first plausible explanation is not necessarily correct.
The most complicated explanation is not necessarily correct.
The simplest explanation is not automatically correct.
Evidence determines which explanation survives.
Two events occurring together does not prove one caused the other.
A network attack occurring during an outage makes the attack relevant evidence.
It does not automatically prove the attack caused the outage.
Causation requires evidence connecting cause and effect.
Debugging should identify the failing layer.
A user-interface failure can originate in the interface.
A user-interface failure can originate in application logic.
A user-interface failure can originate in a network request.
A user-interface failure can originate in a remote service.
A user-interface failure can originate in persistence.
Trace execution through boundaries.
Module interfaces create boundaries.
Process communication creates boundaries.
Network protocols create boundaries.
Database interfaces create boundaries.
Filesystem interfaces create boundaries.
Platform abstractions create boundaries.
Each boundary provides an opportunity to inspect inputs and outputs.
If correct data enters a component and incorrect data leaves it, that component becomes relevant.
If incorrect data already enters a component, investigate upstream.
If correct data leaves a component but the next component receives incorrect data, investigate the boundary or transport.
Debugging can be treated as narrowing a search space.
Begin with the largest known failing path.
Identify a midpoint.
Determine whether behavior is correct before or after that midpoint.
Continue narrowing.
This resembles binary search when the investigation permits it.
git bisect applies this idea across source history.
Logging can expose execution state.
Logs should identify important transitions.
Logs should identify failures.
Logs should preserve useful context.
Logs should not expose unnecessary secrets.
Adding targeted diagnostic logging can test a hypothesis.
Diagnostic logging should be removed or reduced when it is no longer appropriate.
Excessive logging can hide important events.
Useful logging is structured and purposeful.
A log saying "network error" is weaker than a log identifying timeout, refusal, DNS failure, TLS failure, or protocol failure.
Specific errors narrow investigation.
Error propagation should preserve useful cause information.
Wrapping an error can add context.
Wrapping an error should not destroy the underlying cause.
An internal error can contain a platform-specific code.
A higher layer can translate that code into a stable application error category.
Both can be useful for debugging.
Windows error codes are platform-specific.
Linux errno values are platform-specific.
Connection refusal is different from DNS failure.
DNS failure is different from malformed protocol data.
Empty data is different from service failure.
Do not collapse distinct failures during debugging.
A debugger can set breakpoints.
A debugger can inspect variables.
A debugger can inspect call stacks.
A debugger can step through instructions or source lines according to available symbols.
Conditional breakpoints are useful for failures occurring only under specific state.
A watchpoint can pause when memory changes according to debugger capability.
A call stack records active function calls.
The top stack frame identifies the current execution location.
Earlier frames show how execution arrived there.
A stack trace is valuable crash evidence.
A stack trace should be preserved before restarting a failed process when practical.
The crashing function is not necessarily the function that corrupted state.
Memory corruption can occur earlier and become visible later.
A buffer overflow can corrupt memory.
The crash can occur far from the original overflow.
Use-after-free can corrupt or read invalid state.
The visible failure can depend on memory layout.
Uninitialized memory can produce inconsistent behavior.
Undefined behavior can change when optimization changes.
A bug appearing only in a release build can indicate undefined behavior, timing, optimization, or another build-dependent condition.
Compare build settings.
Compare compiler versions.
Compare optimization settings.
Compare preprocessor definitions.
Compare linked libraries.
Build configuration is part of the debugging environment.
Stale object files can create misleading behavior.
A successful incremental build does not prove every source file was rebuilt.
When build-state uncertainty exists, perform a clean build.
Do not make clean builds a ritual without reason.
Use them when stale artifacts are plausible.
Compiler warnings can identify defects.
Do not suppress warnings merely to obtain a clean build.
A warning can reveal implicit conversions.
A warning can reveal uninitialized variables.
A warning can reveal incompatible types.
Not every warning is a defect.
A crash should be reproduced when possible.
Record the stack trace.
Record the binary version.
Record the source revision.
Record whether the crash is deterministic.
A deterministic crash is easier to isolate.
Intermittent failures can result from uninitialized state.
Intermittent failures can result from timing.
Intermittent failures can result from resource exhaustion.
Intermittent does not mean random.
A race condition depends on relative timing between operations.
Adding logging can alter timing.
A race can disappear when observed.
A debugger can also alter timing.
Timing-sensitive defects require careful evidence.
Thread sanitizers can help identify data races.
Stress testing can increase occurrence frequency.
Controlled delays can test timing hypotheses.
Synchronization should use defined coordination mechanisms.
Deadlocks occur when execution participants wait indefinitely on one another according to a dependency cycle or equivalent condition.
A deadlocked process can remain alive.
A process being alive does not prove it is responsive.
CPU usage can provide evidence.
Zero CPU usage does not prove deadlock.
High CPU usage does not prove useful work.
A busy loop can consume CPU while making no progress.
Progress should be measured by meaningful state transitions.
A watchdog can detect lack of expected progress.
A heartbeat does not prove complete functional health.
Debugging should test the capability that failed.
If a database query fails, test database behavior.
If DNS fails, test DNS.
If TCP connection fails, test TCP connectivity.
If TLS fails, test TLS.
If protocol qualification fails, test the protocol handshake.
Do not use ping as proof that an application protocol works.
Do not use a successful TCP connection as proof that the application works.
Do not use application startup as proof that every module works.
A network timeout can occur because packets were dropped.
A network timeout can occur because a service was overloaded.
Inspect both endpoints when possible.
Inspect server logs.
Inspect packet behavior when necessary.
Inspect timing.
Do not immediately increase the timeout.
A larger timeout is appropriate only when the required operation legitimately needs more time.
A connection repeatedly disconnecting and reconnecting should be investigated as a lifecycle problem.
Record why each disconnect occurs.
Determine which side closes the connection.
Determine whether closure is graceful.
Determine whether a timeout triggers closure.
Determine whether protocol validation triggers closure.
Determine whether an intermediary triggers closure.
Determine whether the application intentionally reconnects.
Do not assume the network is unstable merely because reconnect messages appear.
The reconnect logic itself can be defective.
A reconnect loop can amplify a minor failure.
A retry loop should record its failure category.
A retry loop should have bounded timing.
Authentication failure is not normally repaired by reconnecting repeatedly.
Wrong-network identity is not repaired by reconnecting repeatedly.
Transient connection refusal can justify retry according to policy.
Transient timeout can justify retry according to policy.
Failure classification determines recovery behavior.
Database debugging should separate connection from query execution.
Can the application reach the database server?
Can the application authenticate?
Does it have permission?
Does the SQL parse?
Does it return expected rows?
Does the application interpret those rows correctly?
Each question identifies a different stage.
Zero rows is not a database failure.
A successful query returning zero rows should be debugged as data or query-condition behavior when zero rows are unexpected.
Inspect data types.
Inspect NULL behavior.
Inspect collation when relevant.
Inspect transaction visibility.
Do not assume the application connected to the intended database merely because connection succeeded.
Configuration can point to the wrong database.
Development and production databases can contain different data.
A query can be correct against the wrong database and still produce an unexpected result.
Record connection target during diagnosis without exposing secrets.
Prepared statements should be inspected with their parameter values represented safely.
Do not reconstruct SQL by unsafe concatenation merely for debugging.
The executed SQL structure and bound values can be logged separately.
Database transactions can hide state from other connections according to isolation.
An uncommitted insert can exist in one transaction but remain invisible elsewhere.
A rollback can remove changes that appeared temporarily successful.
A COMMIT failure or ambiguous connection loss requires careful investigation.
Do not infer persistence merely because an INSERT call initially succeeded.
Filesystem debugging should use the actual runtime identity.
A file being writable by root does not prove it is writable by the service.
Inspect permissions.
Inspect parent-directory permissions.
Inspect filesystem mount options where relevant.
Inspect access-control mechanisms where relevant.
Test using the actual service identity.
Permission denied is not repaired by blindly granting everyone write access.
Determine which identity requires access.
Grant the minimum required access according to policy.
Directory write permission and file write permission are different.
Modifying an existing file requires appropriate file and path permissions.
Renaming files requires directory permissions.
Atomic replacement often requires directory write permission.
A program can read a file but be unable to replace it.
Exact operation matters.
Path bugs can result from incorrect working directories.
Relative paths depend on process working directory.
Interactive shell working directory can differ from service working directory.
A path working from a terminal does not prove it works from a service.
Log resolved paths when diagnosing path problems.
Absolute paths can remove some ambiguity.
Hard-coded absolute paths reduce portability.
Architecture should determine path strategy.
Module debugging should preserve loader stages.
Discovery identifies a candidate.
Manifest validation validates metadata.
Qualification evaluates required properties.
Loading maps or attaches executable module code.
Initialization establishes module state.
Activation makes module functionality available according to architecture.
Failure at one stage should not be mislabeled as failure at another.
"module.conf missing" is a manifest-stage failure.
A missing export is a loading or interface-validation failure according to architecture.
Initialization failure occurs after loading when initialization does not complete.
Functional failure can occur after successful activation.
A manifest error should first be investigated at the manifest boundary.
Do not modify a valid module manifest merely because another component failed.
Do not touch unrelated configuration without evidence.
A module's module.conf can be authoritative configuration.
If module.conf is known correct and evidence points elsewhere, preserve it.
Changing known-good configuration introduces a new variable.
It does not prove every module command works.
Test the module's actual interface.
If "ingest php" fails, inspect command routing.
If routing succeeds, inspect lesson lookup.
If lookup succeeds, inspect file access.
If file access succeeds, inspect parsing.
If parsing succeeds, inspect storage.
If storage succeeds, inspect reporting.
The failing stage should be isolated.
A narrow defect should receive a narrow repair when architecture permits.
A broad rewrite increases regression risk.
Response debugging should distinguish retrieval from generation.
Did the request reach the response component?
Was the question parsed?
Was relevant knowledge retrieved?
Was irrelevant knowledge retrieved?
Was source authority applied?
Did an internal dependency fail?
Each stage can fail independently.
A wrong answer is not necessarily a generation defect.
The wrong evidence may have been retrieved.
Correct evidence can be retrieved but ranked below irrelevant evidence.
Correct output can be produced but lost during transport.
Trace the pipeline.
If Digit answers an unrelated PHP fact to a specific PHP question, inspect retrieval evidence before changing the PHP lesson.
The lesson can contain the correct fact.
A retrieval failure does not mean the lesson is wrong.
A reasoning failure does not mean ingestion failed.
A successful retrieval does not prove response quality.
Keep stages separate.
Source provenance is useful debugging information.
Record which source produced a retrieved fact.
Record source type.
Record current versus historical status when relevant.
This can explain why an answer was produced.
If an old README outranks a current interface header, the retrieval problem is source precedence.
Deleting historical evidence is one possible workaround but can destroy useful history.
When current state is requested, current authoritative sources should win according to architecture.
When history is requested, historical sources become relevant.
Debugging retrieval requires understanding question intent.
A current-state question and a historical question are not equivalent.
"What ABI do your modules use?" requests current state.
"What ABI versions have your modules used?" requests history.
A retrieval system should not answer all three with the same evidence selection.
A debugging test should include all three.
If the first returns "1.3 -> 1.4 -> 1.5," source-role handling is defective.
If the second returns only "1.5," historical retrieval may be incomplete.
If the third invents differences not present in evidence, grounding is defective.
Different failures require different repairs.
Configuration debugging should establish actual loaded configuration.
The configuration file on disk is not necessarily the configuration currently in memory.
Changing a file does not guarantee a running process reloaded it.
Some systems support live reload.
Some systems require restart.
Some systems watch files.
Some systems cache configuration.
Determine actual behavior.
Log configuration source where safe.
Do not log secrets.
Environment variables can override files.
Command-line options can override defaults.
A stale environment variable can override a corrected file.
A service manager can provide environment variables different from an interactive shell.
A service can run under a different working directory.
A service can run under a different user.
A service can have different PATH settings.
A program working interactively but failing as a service often indicates environment differences.
Compare environments systematically.
Do not immediately rewrite the application.
Deployment debugging should identify the actual executing binary.
The source directory can contain new code while an old binary remains running.
A binary can be rebuilt but not deployed.
A binary can be deployed but the service not restarted.
A service can restart but execute a different path.
Multiple copies of an executable can exist.
Determine the process executable path.
Determine version output.
Determine process start time.
Determine source revision associated with the binary when available.
"Source is fixed" does not mean "running service is fixed."
"Build succeeded" does not mean "deployment succeeded."
"Deployment succeeded" does not mean "runtime behavior is correct."
Each stage requires evidence.
Version strings are useful diagnostic evidence.
Build identifiers are useful diagnostic evidence.
ABI versions are useful diagnostic evidence.
Do not confuse them.
Application version 2.0 can use ABI 1.5.
Protocol version 1 can coexist with application version 2.0.
A commit hash does not replace any of these semantic versions.
Record identifiers with labels.
Unlabeled version numbers create ambiguity.
A client log shows client observations.
A proxy log shows proxy observations.
A packet capture shows traffic visible at the capture point.
No single perspective necessarily contains the complete event.
Correlate using timestamps and identifiers.
Request identifiers can connect events across components.
Transaction identifiers can connect related operations.
Peer identifiers can connect protocol events.
Alert identifiers can connect monitoring events.
Clock synchronization improves timeline reconstruction.
Clock drift can make event order appear incorrect.
Use monotonic clocks for measuring elapsed duration when appropriate.
Monotonic time is useful for durations because it is not affected by ordinary wall-clock adjustments.
Time source matters.
Performance debugging should measure before optimizing.
Identify where time is spent.
Measure DNS time.
Measure connection time.
Measure TLS time.
Measure server processing.
Measure database time.
Measure serialization.
Measure response transfer.
The bottleneck may be external.
A memory profiler identifies allocation behavior.
An I/O profiler can identify blocking or heavy input-output behavior.
Profiling provides evidence.
Optimize measured bottlenecks.
Premature optimization can increase complexity without solving the actual problem.
A high CPU process should be profiled before assuming a loop is defective.
High CPU can represent legitimate computation.
Low CPU can represent deadlock.
Memory debugging should observe growth over time.
Caches intentionally retain memory.
A memory leak occurs when memory that is no longer needed remains unreachable for reuse or is otherwise improperly retained according to the program's ownership model.
Measure the same operation repeatedly.
Observe whether memory stabilizes.
Use memory-analysis tools where available.
A leak can exist in a library.
File descriptor exhaustion can look like unrelated I/O failures.
Socket exhaustion can look like network failure.
Thread exhaustion can prevent new work.
Disk exhaustion can cause write failures.
Disk-full errors should remain disk-full errors.
Permission errors should remain permission errors.
Resource exhaustion should remain resource exhaustion.
Generic "internal service unavailable" messages can obscure actionable causes.
A core dump captures process state around a crash according to operating-system configuration.
Core dumps can contain sensitive information.
A core dump can be examined with a debugger.
Debug symbols improve stack traces.
Debug symbols map machine addresses to source information.
Stripped binaries can be harder to debug.
Production binaries can keep separate debug-symbol files.
Debug information does not need to change program semantics.
Debug builds and production builds can differ.
Reproduce against the actual failing build where possible.
A repair should address the identified cause.
A workaround changes conditions so the symptom does not occur without necessarily correcting the cause.
Workarounds can be legitimate temporary measures.
A workaround should not be mislabeled as a root-cause repair.
Increasing a timeout can be a workaround.
Restarting a process can be a workaround.
Clearing a cache can be a workaround.
Deleting state can be a workaround.
Reinstalling software can be a workaround.
These actions can restore operation while destroying evidence about the cause.
Preserve evidence before destructive recovery when investigation matters.
Operational urgency can require recovery before complete investigation.
When recovery must occur, capture available evidence first when practical.
Incident response and debugging have overlapping but different priorities.
Debugging prioritizes understanding defects.
After service restoration, root-cause analysis can continue using preserved evidence.
A root cause is a condition whose correction prevents recurrence within the defined causal model.
There can be multiple contributing causes.
Complex failures can have more than one necessary condition.
Identify contributing factors when evidence supports them.
A root-cause analysis should distinguish trigger from underlying weakness.
Unsafe parser behavior can be the underlying defect.
The request is not necessarily the software defect.
A network outage can trigger reconnect behavior.
Unbounded reconnect logic can amplify the outage.
A regression is behavior that previously worked and became incorrect after change.
Find a known-good revision.
Find a known-bad revision.
Compare them.
git bisect can narrow the change boundary.
The first bad commit identifies where the tested behavior changed.
Inspect that change.
Debugging concerns cause, not blame.
Blame does not repair software.
A patch should be as narrow as practical.
A patch should preserve unrelated known-good behavior.
A patch should not modify files unrelated to the identified defect without reason.
A patch should not modify a module manifest when the manifest is known-good and the defect lies elsewhere.
A patch should not introduce speculative architectural changes.
Fix the demonstrated problem.
Test the repair.
Run the original reproduction.
Run related regression tests.
Verify that neighboring behavior still works.
A repaired symptom without regression testing can conceal new defects.
A fix should include a regression test when practical.
The regression test should fail before the fix.
The regression test should pass after the fix.
This provides evidence that the patch addresses the observed defect.
If the test passed before the patch, it does not demonstrate the patch repaired that failure.
Do not merely chase green indicators.
Debugging can use assertions.
Assertions expose violated assumptions.
An assertion failure identifies an assumption that was false.
The assertion itself can be wrong.
Investigate both the state and the assumption.
Do not simply delete an assertion because it fired.
If the assertion expresses a valid invariant, the state-producing defect should be repaired.
An invariant is a condition expected to remain true during defined system states.
Examples include a pointer being non-null before dereference.
Examples include an active module having completed qualification.
A state machine makes legal transitions explicit.
An impossible transition indicates either corrupted state, a defective transition, or an incorrect state model.
Inspect the evidence.
Silent coercion hides defects.
Production behavior can require controlled degradation.
Development and production failure policies can differ while preserving the same underlying correctness rules.
Defensive programming anticipates invalid conditions.
Defensive programming does not mean hiding every defect.
Returning a safe error can protect production state.
Logging the invariant violation can preserve debugging evidence.
When state validity is uncertain, fail according to defined policy.
Do not continue blindly.
Do not immediately rewrite the corrupted file.
Make a copy.
Compare with known-good examples.
Identify the first invalid field or region.
Determine which writer could produce that region.
Trace write paths.
A corrupted file can be caused by partial writes.
A corrupted file can be caused by incorrect serialization.
Do not assume malicious modification without evidence.
Write temporary content.
Flush according to durability requirements.
Rename atomically where filesystem semantics permit.
The exact durability requirements determine whether additional synchronization is required.
Serialization debugging compares encoded and decoded representations.
Serialize a known value.
Inspect exact bytes.
Deserialize the bytes.
Compare the result.
Test boundary values.
Test zero.
Test maximum values.
Test signed values when permitted.
Byte-order defects often appear only for values larger than one byte.
Never assume raw C structure memory is portable protocol serialization.
Inspect bytes.
Protocol debugging should use known messages.
Capture received bytes.
Compare against protocol specification.
Verify version.
Verify identifiers.
Verify checksums or hashes.
Verify state-machine position.
A peer sending 80 bytes does not prove those 80 bytes are valid.
Decode success does not prove semantic validation.
When a protocol handshake succeeds but later operations fail, do not debug handshake code first without evidence.
The successful handshake narrows the failure to later behavior.
Use successful stages to eliminate portions of the search space.
If discovery passed, investigate after discovery.
If ABI validation passed, investigate after ABI validation.
If loading passed, investigate initialization or later behavior.
Do not repeatedly repair stages already proven correct unless new evidence contradicts that proof.
A stage proven correct for one input may fail for another.
Be precise about what was proven.
A test result applies to its tested conditions.
Debugging should avoid absolute conclusions beyond those conditions.
A debugger should ask: what remained the same?
A debugger should ask: where is the first observable divergence?
A debugger should ask: what evidence supports this hypothesis?
A debugger should ask: what experiment can falsify this hypothesis?
A debugger should ask: what is the smallest change that repairs the demonstrated cause?
A debugger should ask: how will the repair be verified?
Do not ask unrelated questions merely because they are easy to answer.
Do not modify unrelated components merely because they are accessible.
Do not replace architecture merely because one implementation has a defect.
Do not introduce a database to repair a parser.
Do not introduce networking to repair local state.
Do not rewrite a module to repair incorrect source ranking unless the module owns ranking.
The component that displays a symptom is not automatically the component that should be patched.
Trace ownership.
A debugger should know when evidence is insufficient.
"I do not yet know" is a valid debugging state.
Unknown is better than invented certainty.
Collect additional evidence.
Instrument the relevant boundary.
Reproduce again.
Compare states.
Narrow the problem.
A failed experiment is useful when it eliminates a hypothesis.
Debugging progress includes proving what is not causing the problem.
This prevents repeatedly investigating the same disproven explanation.
Do not treat a disproven hypothesis as current merely because it remains in historical notes.
Historical debugging notes record investigation history.
Current conclusions should reflect current evidence.
History and current state are different.
A previous suspected cause is not the current root cause after evidence disproves it.
A previous software version is not the current version.
A previous ABI is not the current ABI.
A previous configuration is not the current configuration.
A previous failure can still provide useful historical evidence.
Words such as suspected, candidate, confirmed, rejected, fixed, and current have operational meaning.
Preserve status.
A candidate cause is not confirmed.
A fixed defect remains historical fact but is not necessarily a current defect.
A reopened defect indicates new evidence or recurrence.
Status transitions preserve history without rewriting it.
Debugging documentation should record evidence.
Record diagnosis.
Record patch.
Record verification.
Record remaining uncertainty.
Do not rewrite history to make the investigation appear cleaner than it was.
Incorrect early hypotheses can remain part of the record when useful.
They should be labeled as superseded or rejected.
This preserves evidence without confusing current conclusions.
A debug session ends when the required problem has been explained or repaired according to scope.
Repair and explanation are separate accomplishments.
A problem can be repaired without fully understanding the cause.
A problem can be understood before a repair is implemented.
Engineering should distinguish these states.
A workaround can restore operation.
A root-cause repair can prevent recurrence.
A regression test can preserve the repair.
Monitoring can detect recurrence.
Together these create stronger confidence.
Debugging is an evidence-driven process.
Observe.
Reproduce.
Localize.
Verify.
Regression test.
Document.
Do not guess when evidence can be collected.
Do not alter known-good state without reason.
Do not destroy evidence unnecessarily.
Do not confuse history with current state.
Do not confuse symptoms with causes.
Do not confuse correlation with causation.
Do not confuse a successful intermediate stage with successful completion.
Do not confuse an error message with the root cause.
Do not confuse a hypothesis with a finding.
Do not confuse empty data with service failure.
Do not confuse one layer's failure with another layer's failure.
Precise observations produce precise diagnoses.
Precise diagnoses permit small repairs.
Small repairs reduce regression risk.
When evidence establishes the cause, stop speculating and repair that cause.
Encoding is the representation of information according to defined rules.
An encoding maps information from one representation into another representation.
Encoding does not inherently provide authenticity.
Encoding does not inherently provide integrity.
Encoding is not hashing.
Encoding is not compression.
These operations can be combined, but they solve different problems.
Text encoding defines how textual information is represented as bytes.
Computers store and transmit bytes.
Human-readable text consists of characters interpreted according to a character model.
The encoding must be known, defined, detected through a reliable mechanism, or otherwise established by the surrounding format or protocol.
ASCII is a character encoding.
ASCII defines numeric values for a limited set of characters.
ASCII includes English letters.
ASCII includes control characters.
ASCII does not represent the full range of human writing systems.
Unicode is a character standard.
Unicode assigns code points to characters and other textual elements.
Unicode is not itself one single byte encoding.
UTF-8 is a Unicode encoding.
UTF-16 is a Unicode encoding.
UTF-32 is a Unicode encoding.
Do not confuse a Unicode code point with its UTF-8 bytes.
A Unicode code point is an abstract numeric value assigned by Unicode.
Code points are commonly written using notation such as U+0041.
U+0041 represents the character LATIN CAPITAL LETTER A.
The UTF-8 representation of a code point can contain one or more bytes.
The UTF-16 representation of a code point can contain one or more 16-bit code units.
Code point and code unit are different concepts.
UTF-8 uses 8-bit code units.
UTF-16 uses 16-bit code units.
One character is not necessarily one code unit.
One character is not necessarily one byte.
One visible symbol is not necessarily one Unicode code point.
These distinctions matter.
ASCII characters use the same byte values in UTF-8 as they do in ASCII.
This makes UTF-8 compatible with ASCII for the ASCII range.
Characters outside the ASCII range require multiple bytes in UTF-8.
Do not truncate UTF-8 arbitrarily at a byte boundary if valid text must be preserved.
A truncation point can fall inside a multi-byte sequence.
Invalid UTF-8 can result.
UTF-8 has defined valid byte sequences.
Not every arbitrary byte sequence is valid UTF-8.
A UTF-8 decoder should reject or explicitly handle malformed sequences according to application policy.
UTF-16 represents Unicode using 16-bit code units.
Some Unicode code points require one UTF-16 code unit.
Others require a surrogate pair consisting of two code units.
Therefore one UTF-16 code unit does not always represent one code point.
Splitting a surrogate pair creates invalid Unicode representation.
UTF-16 has byte-order considerations when represented as bytes.
UTF-16BE uses big-endian byte order.
UTF-32 represents Unicode code points using fixed-width 32-bit code units.
Fixed-width code units do not mean user-visible characters have fixed width.
A visible grapheme can still contain multiple code points.
A byte order mark is commonly abbreviated BOM.
A BOM is a Unicode character that can be used at the beginning of selected encoded text to indicate encoding or byte order.
UTF-8 can contain the byte sequence representing U+FEFF as a signature at the beginning of a file.
UTF-8 does not require a BOM.
Some software expects a UTF-8 BOM.
Some software does not.
Some formats discourage or prohibit it.
The surrounding format determines acceptable behavior.
Do not automatically add a BOM to every UTF-8 file.
Do not automatically remove a BOM when exact bytes matter.
For example, a parser expecting the first bytes to contain a particular marker can fail if unexpected BOM bytes precede it.
Encoding rules belong to the format.
Unicode characters can have multiple equivalent representations.
A character with an accent can sometimes be represented as one precomposed code point.
It can sometimes be represented as a base character followed by a combining mark.
Their bytes can differ.
Their code-point sequences can differ.
Common normalization forms include NFC.
Common normalization forms include NFD.
Common normalization forms include NFKC.
Do not apply normalization blindly.
Compatibility normalization can change distinctions that an application considers meaningful.
If exact user input must be preserved, retain the original representation.
If canonical comparison requires normalization, define the required normalization form.
Normalization is not case folding.
Normalization is not encoding conversion.
Normalization is not transliteration.
These are separate operations.
Case conversion can be language-sensitive.
Uppercase and lowercase transformations are not always one-to-one.
Do not assume converting to lowercase preserves string length.
Do not assume ASCII case rules apply to all Unicode text.
Case-insensitive comparison requires defined rules.
Filesystem case comparison and Unicode application comparison are not automatically identical.
Locale-sensitive comparison can produce different results in different locales.
Canonical protocol ordering should not depend on unspecified locale behavior.
Use explicitly defined byte or code-point comparison when protocol determinism requires it.
A grapheme cluster represents a user-perceived textual unit according to Unicode text segmentation rules.
One grapheme cluster can contain multiple code points.
A displayed symbol can therefore contain multiple code points.
A code-point count is not necessarily a user-visible character count.
A byte count is not necessarily a code-point count.
A code-unit count is not necessarily a grapheme count.
If a protocol specifies byte length, count bytes.
Do not use one measurement accidentally for another.
Text width is not determined solely by character count.
Some characters occupy different display widths.
Combining marks can occupy no independent width.
Emoji can involve multiple code points.
Rendering depends on fonts and rendering systems.
Encoding establishes representation.
It does not establish visual appearance.
The same Unicode text can render differently using different fonts.
Do not use visual appearance as exact data identity.
Different Unicode characters can look similar.
These are sometimes called homoglyphs.
Homoglyphs can create security problems.
Security-sensitive identifiers should use defined character policies.
Display names and machine identifiers can use different rules.
Do not infer identity from visual similarity.
Text comparison can operate on code points.
Text comparison can operate on normalized code points.
Text comparison can use locale-aware collation.
These operations answer different questions.
Code-point equality proves the decoded code-point sequences are equal.
Normalized equality can treat selected equivalent sequences as equivalent.
Locale-aware comparison can apply language-specific ordering rules.
Choose the comparison matching the requirement.
Do not use locale-sensitive comparison for cryptographic identity.
Cryptographic operations operate on bytes.
Hashes operate on bytes.
If text is hashed or signed, the text must first have a defined byte representation.
Encoding therefore becomes part of the cryptographic protocol.
The same visible text encoded differently produces different bytes.
Different bytes generally produce different hashes.
Different bytes produce different signatures.
Define character encoding before hashing text.
Define normalization before hashing text if normalization is part of the protocol.
Do not normalize after hashing.
Do not guess encoding during verification.
Canonical text representation must be established before cryptographic processing.
Text files are byte sequences interpreted according to an encoding and format.
A file being readable in one editor does not establish its encoding conclusively.
Editors can detect or guess encodings.
Different editors can make different guesses.
A file can contain only ASCII-range bytes and therefore be valid under several compatible encodings.
Detection can be ambiguous.
If a system controls file generation, explicitly define the encoding.
UTF-8 is commonly appropriate for portable text.
Do not rely on operating-system legacy defaults when interoperability matters.
Default encoding can vary by operating system.
Default encoding can vary by runtime.
Default encoding can vary by application.
Compilers and interpreters must interpret source bytes.
Exact source-encoding rules depend on language and toolchain.
Do not assume every compiler interprets arbitrary source files identically.
JSON exchanged between systems should follow the encoding requirements of JSON and the surrounding protocol.
XML can declare encoding.
HTTP can communicate media type and character-set information where applicable.
Format and protocol metadata can establish encoding.
Use defined metadata rather than guessing.
The original bytes can be valid.
The decoder can still produce incorrect characters because the wrong encoding was selected.
It can be an interpretation error.
Repeated incorrect conversions can cause actual irreversible corruption.
Do not repeatedly convert text until it "looks right."
Determine the source encoding.
Decode once correctly.
Then encode into the target encoding.
Transcoding converts text from one character encoding to another.
Correct transcoding consists conceptually of decoding source bytes into textual code points and encoding those code points using the target encoding.
Do not reinterpret bytes directly as another encoding and call that conversion.
Reinterpretation and transcoding are different.
A byte value has no universal character meaning.
