Memory is storage used by a computer to hold data and instructions.
Computer systems contain multiple forms of storage with different properties.
CPU caches store frequently or recently used data close to processor execution units.
Main memory commonly refers to RAM.
RAM stands for Random Access Memory.
Persistent storage includes devices such as solid-state drives and hard drives.
Registers, cache, RAM, and persistent storage have different latency, capacity, and persistence characteristics.
Faster storage is generally smaller and more expensive per unit of capacity.
A memory hierarchy places frequently needed data closer to execution resources.
Registers are generally faster to access than main memory.
Main memory is generally faster to access than persistent storage.
These are architectural tendencies rather than universal timing guarantees.
RAM normally loses its contents when power is removed.
Persistent storage is designed to retain data without continuous power.
RAM and persistent storage serve different purposes.
A running program requires memory for data.
A running program can require memory for stacks.
A running program can require dynamically allocated memory.
The operating system manages memory resources available to processes.
A process normally operates within a virtual address space.
A virtual address is an address used by software within a virtual-memory system.
A physical address refers to a location in physical memory according to the hardware memory architecture.
The operating system and processor memory-management hardware translate virtual addresses to physical memory mappings.
Virtual memory allows different processes to use identical virtual address values without necessarily referring to the same physical memory.
Virtual memory supports process isolation.
Virtual memory can allow memory regions to be mapped with different permissions.
A memory region can be readable.
A memory region can be writable.
A memory region can be executable.
Permissions depend on the mapping and platform.
Memory protection prevents selected invalid accesses.
Memory protection does not make every program memory-safe.
A process can still misuse pointers.
A process can still access memory outside an intended object while remaining inside a mapped page.
Hardware memory protection and language memory safety are different concepts.
Virtual memory is commonly divided into pages.
A page is a fixed-size unit used by virtual-memory systems.
Physical memory can be organized into page frames.
A page table records virtual-to-physical mappings and associated properties according to the architecture.
Page size depends on the platform.
Four-kibibyte pages are common on many systems.
Larger page sizes can also exist.
Do not assume one page size universally.
A page fault is not necessarily an application defect.
A page fault can occur because a valid page has not yet been mapped into physical memory.
A page fault can occur during copy-on-write processing.
An invalid access can also produce a fault.
Valid and invalid page faults are different situations.
Demand paging loads or establishes pages when they are required rather than necessarily loading everything immediately.
Virtual memory can allow total mapped address space to exceed physical RAM.
Operating systems can reclaim memory.
Operating systems can evict cached pages.
Operating systems can move selected memory contents to secondary storage.
Swap space provides storage used by an operating system for selected memory-management operations.
Paging memory to storage is much slower than accessing RAM.
Heavy paging can severely reduce performance.
Thrashing occurs when a system spends excessive time moving pages rather than performing useful work.
More virtual address space does not create more physical RAM.
Address space and physical memory are different resources.
A 64-bit process can address a very large theoretical address range.
A 32-bit process has a much smaller address space than a typical 64-bit process.
A 32-bit pointer and a 64-bit pointer commonly differ in size.
A pointer stores an address or pointer representation according to the language and implementation.
A pointer can refer to an object.
A pointer can refer to a function where the language permits.
A pointer can be null.
A pointer can become invalid.
A pointer can be uninitialized.
A pointer value alone does not establish that dereferencing it is valid.
Dereferencing means accessing the object or function referenced by a pointer according to language semantics.
A null pointer does not point to a valid object.
Dereferencing a null pointer is invalid.
A dangling pointer refers to storage whose valid lifetime has ended.
Dereferencing a dangling pointer can produce undefined behavior in languages such as C and C++.
A wild pointer is an informal term for a pointer containing an invalid or unintended address.
Uninitialized pointers can behave as wild pointers.
Pointers should be initialized before use.
Pointer validity depends on object lifetime.
Pointer validity depends on bounds.
Pointer validity depends on access permissions.
Pointer validity depends on language rules.
An address being mapped does not prove a pointer access is valid according to the language.
An object has a lifetime.
Object lifetime determines when storage contains a valid object according to language rules.
Using an object before its lifetime begins can be invalid.
Using an object after its lifetime ends can be invalid.
Storage lifetime and variable scope are related but different concepts.
Lifetime determines when the object exists.
A local automatic variable commonly has automatic storage duration.
That pointer becomes invalid for dereferencing.
A static-storage object exists for the duration defined by the language.
A dynamically allocated object exists until its storage is released according to the allocation model.
Dynamic allocation allows runtime control of storage lifetime.
realloc changes the size of an allocated block according to C semantics.
free releases storage previously obtained through compatible allocation functions.
malloc returns a pointer to allocated storage or a null pointer when allocation fails.
Allocation failure must be handled where it is possible.
malloc does not initialize the allocated bytes.
Reading an uninitialized value can be invalid or indeterminate according to language rules.
calloc performs zero initialization of allocated bytes.
All-bits-zero does not universally represent every possible language value in every historical or theoretical implementation.
On common modern systems, zeroed storage commonly represents integer zero and null-like bit patterns where applicable, but portable code should follow language guarantees.
free does not erase the pointer variable.
free releases the allocation.
A pointer variable can still contain the old address after free.
That pointer is then dangling.
Setting a pointer to null after free can reduce accidental reuse through that particular pointer.
Aliasing occurs when multiple references or pointers can refer to the same storage.
Aliasing complicates ownership.
One pointer freeing an allocation can leave other aliases dangling.
Ownership defines responsibility for a resource.
A borrowed pointer or reference accesses a resource without owning its lifetime.
Ambiguous ownership causes leaks and premature release.
Shared ownership requires defined lifetime management.
Reference counting is one shared-ownership technique.
A reference count tracks how many owners retain a resource.
The resource can be released when the count reaches zero according to the ownership model.
Reference counting can leak cycles.
Weak references can break selected ownership cycles in systems that support them.
Garbage collection automatically identifies selected objects that are no longer reachable according to the runtime's model.
Garbage collection reduces explicit deallocation requirements.
Garbage collection does not eliminate all memory leaks.
A program can retain references to objects it no longer needs.
Those objects remain reachable.
Sockets can still require closing.
A memory leak occurs when allocated memory remains unavailable for reuse even though the program no longer needs it.
A small leak in a short-running program can have limited operational impact.
A small repeated leak in a long-running service can eventually exhaust resources.
Memory leaks are correctness and reliability concerns.
A leak does not necessarily cause immediate failure.
Memory growth does not automatically prove a leak.
Caches can intentionally grow.
Buffers can grow.
Workload can increase.
Memory allocators can retain freed memory for reuse.
Measure and inspect before diagnosing a leak.
Use-after-free occurs when code accesses memory after its valid allocation lifetime ended.
The freed storage can be reused for another object.
The old pointer can then access unrelated data.
Use-after-free can cause crashes.
Use-after-free can cause silent corruption.
Double free occurs when the same allocation is released more than once without an intervening valid reallocation ownership transition.
Double free can corrupt allocator state.
Double free can cause crashes.
Double free can create security vulnerabilities.
Ownership discipline helps prevent double free.
Buffer over-read occurs when code reads beyond the bounds of a buffer.
A program not crashing does not make an out-of-bounds access valid.
Memory corruption can damage nearby objects.
Memory corruption can surface much later than the original invalid write.
An earlier memory write can corrupt state that fails later.
Memory debugging should therefore consider preceding operations.
A thread normally has its own stack.
Stack frames can contain return information.
Stack frames can contain saved registers.
Stack frames can contain local variables.
Exact stack-frame layout depends on ABI, compiler, architecture, and optimization.
A compiler can keep values in registers.
A compiler can optimize values away.
A stack normally grows within a bounded memory region.
Stack overflow occurs when stack usage exceeds available stack capacity.
Deep recursion can cause stack overflow.
Very large automatic arrays can cause stack overflow.
Unbounded recursion is dangerous.
Stack size is not unlimited.
A pointer to stack-local storage should not be used after that storage's lifetime ends.
Heap is a common term for memory used for dynamic allocation.
Heap allocation allows objects to outlive the function that allocated them when ownership is transferred appropriately.
Heap allocation has management overhead.
Heap allocation can fragment.
Heap allocation is not automatically slower than every stack operation in every context, but it generally involves more allocator machinery.
Use storage appropriate to the required lifetime and size.
Do not allocate dynamically merely because dynamic allocation appears more flexible.
Do not place arbitrarily large objects on the stack merely because automatic lifetime is convenient.
Requirements determine the storage strategy.
Memory fragmentation occurs when available memory is divided into regions that are difficult to use efficiently.
Allocators use strategies to manage fragmentation.
A memory allocator manages dynamic allocation.
An allocator can maintain free lists.
An allocator can use arenas.
An allocator can request larger memory regions from the operating system and subdivide them.
free does not necessarily return memory immediately to the operating system.
An allocator can retain freed memory for future allocations.
Process resident memory therefore does not necessarily decrease immediately after free.
Allocator behavior should be distinguished from leaks.
RSS commonly means Resident Set Size.
RSS estimates memory pages currently resident for a process according to operating-system reporting semantics.
RSS and virtual size are different.
Shared pages can complicate memory accounting.
Memory metrics should be interpreted according to the operating system.
A memory map describes mapped regions in a process address space.
Mappings can contain executable code.
Mappings can contain shared libraries.
Mappings can contain stacks.
Mappings can contain anonymous memory.
mmap is a POSIX interface commonly used to create memory mappings.
Memory mapping can allow file data to be accessed through memory operations.
Memory mapping does not mean the entire file must be loaded into physical RAM immediately.
Pages can be loaded on demand.
Changes to mapped memory can be propagated to files according to mapping type and synchronization semantics.
Memory-mapped I/O requires careful error handling.
A mapped file can change externally.
Storage errors can occur while mapped pages are being accessed or written.
Mapping semantics depend on the operating system.
Shared memory allows multiple processes to access a common memory region.
Shared memory can provide efficient interprocess communication.
Process isolation is intentionally reduced for the shared region.
A pointer value from one process is generally not a portable reference for another process.
The same shared memory can be mapped at different virtual addresses in different processes.
Shared-memory data structures should not rely on ordinary absolute pointers unless the mapping design guarantees them.
Shared memory does not automatically provide persistence.
Shared memory does not automatically provide synchronization.
Shared memory does not automatically provide message boundaries.
Those properties must be designed separately.
Memory alignment determines address constraints for objects.
Some types require addresses aligned to particular boundaries.
Misaligned access can be slower.
Misaligned access can fault on some architectures.
Do not assume arbitrary byte buffers are correctly aligned for every type.
Structure padding can exist between members.
Structure padding can exist at the end of structures.
sizeof includes padding.
Two structures containing equivalent logical fields can have different binary layouts under different ABIs.
Do not use native structure memory as a portable serialized representation.
Serialization should define field sizes.
Serialization should define byte order.
Serialization should define encoding.
Serialization should define alignment independently of native memory layout.
Endianness determines byte order for multibyte values.
Little-endian and big-endian systems represent multibyte values differently in memory.
A portable protocol should define byte order.
Raw memory copies between different architectures can produce different numeric interpretation.
Byte order is a representation issue.
Alignment is a placement issue.
They are different.
Memory safety means preventing invalid memory operations according to the programming model.
Examples of memory-safety defects include out-of-bounds access.
Examples include use-after-free.
Examples include invalid pointer dereference.
Examples include double free.
Examples include selected uninitialized-memory use.
Memory safety does not mean logical correctness.
A memory-safe program can calculate the wrong answer.
A memory-safe program can authorize the wrong user.
A memory-safe program can delete the wrong record.
Logical correctness and memory safety are separate requirements.
A logically correct algorithm can still contain memory-safety defects in an unsafe implementation.
Both must be correct.
Languages differ in memory-safety models.
C gives programmers direct control over memory.
C requires programmers to maintain many memory invariants explicitly.
C++ provides low-level memory control plus higher-level ownership abstractions.
Rust uses ownership and borrowing rules to prevent many classes of memory defects at compile time.
Language choice changes which classes of defects are easier or harder to create.
Bounds checking validates that an index or access lies within a valid region.
Some languages perform automatic bounds checking.
The programmer must maintain valid bounds.
A buffer should travel with its length where possible.
A pointer without known bounds is difficult to validate safely.
APIs should include capacity information when writing into caller-provided buffers.
Do not infer buffer capacity from the pointer.
A pointer contains an address representation, not general allocation-size metadata.
sizeof applied to an array can produce the array's size while the array expression remains an actual array in the applicable context.
When an array parameter is adjusted to a pointer in a C function declaration, sizeof on that parameter does not recover the caller's array length.
Arrays and pointers are related in C but are not identical.
An array object contains elements.
Array expressions can decay to pointers in many contexts.
Decay does not make arrays and pointers the same type of object.
That technique does not work on a pointer as though the pointer contained the original array length.
Array length should be passed explicitly across function boundaries when required.
The null terminator is part of the storage requirement.
A string containing N visible characters normally requires at least N plus one bytes when characters occupy one byte each in the representation.
Failing to reserve space for the terminator can overflow a buffer.
strlen does not include the terminating null byte.
strlen requires a valid null-terminated string.
Calling strlen on memory without a reachable terminator within valid accessible object bounds is invalid.
Binary data is not necessarily a C string.
Binary buffers can contain zero bytes.
Use explicit lengths for binary data.
Do not use string functions on arbitrary binary data.
memcpy copies a specified number of bytes between non-overlapping memory regions according to C semantics.
memmove copies bytes and supports overlapping source and destination regions.
memcpy should not be used when source and destination overlap.
memset operates on bytes.
memset does not assign arbitrary typed values element by element.
memset(ptr, 1, size) fills each byte with 0x01.
It does not generally initialize each integer object to numeric value 1.
Memory operations should use correct byte counts.
Confusing element count with byte count can cause under-copying or overflow.
Multiplying element count by element size can overflow.
Allocation-size calculations should detect arithmetic overflow.
If count multiplied by element size exceeds representable size, the resulting allocation can be smaller than intended.
Writing the expected number of elements can then overflow the allocation.
Validate size arithmetic before allocation.
size_t is an unsigned integer type used to represent object sizes according to C.
sizeof produces a value of type size_t.
Do not assume size_t has the same width as unsigned int.
On common 64-bit platforms, size_t is commonly 64 bits.
Conversion between signed and unsigned size values requires care.
A negative signed value converted to size_t can become a very large positive value.
Validate ranges before conversion.
Memory safety therefore depends partly on arithmetic correctness.
A zero-length allocation has implementation-defined or specified special semantics depending on the allocation function and language version.
Do not assume malloc(0) behaves identically on every implementation.
Design code so zero-length cases are handled explicitly.
realloc requires careful ownership handling.
If realloc fails, the original allocation remains valid according to ordinary C semantics.
Assigning realloc directly to the only pointer can lose access to the original allocation when realloc returns null.
Use a temporary pointer when preservation is required.
For example, request the new allocation into a temporary variable.
If the request succeeds, update the owning pointer.
If it fails, retain the original pointer and handle failure.
realloc can move an allocation.
Pointers into the old allocation can become invalid after successful relocation.
If a function accepts a pointer, specify whether it borrows or takes ownership.
If a function stores a pointer after returning, specify the required lifetime.
If a function copies input data, specify that ownership remains with the caller.
Ambiguous API ownership creates defects.
Allocation and deallocation mechanisms must be compatible.
Memory allocated with malloc should be released using free.
C++ memory allocated with new should be released according to matching C++ deallocation rules.
Memory allocated with new[] should use the matching array deallocation mechanism.
Do not assume memory allocated by one allocator can be safely released by another allocator.
Cross-module memory ownership can be especially sensitive on platforms with multiple runtime heaps.
A module interface can avoid allocator mismatch by requiring the allocating component to release its own allocations.
An API can expose a release function.
An API can use caller-provided buffers.
An API can use fixed-layout values instead of transferred ownership.
ABI design should define memory ownership.
A DLL boundary is also a memory-ownership boundary when pointers cross it.
A host should not free plugin-owned memory using an incompatible allocator.
Memory pools preallocate or manage groups of objects for repeated use.
Pools can reduce allocation overhead.
Pools can improve locality.
Pools can bound resource use.
Pools introduce their own lifetime rules.
A pointer to a pooled object can become invalid when the object is reused.
Pool reuse can make stale-pointer bugs difficult to detect.
Arenas simplify ownership when many objects share one lifetime.
Choose allocation strategy based on lifetime patterns.
Object lifetime is often more important than individual allocation speed.
Memory locality describes how closely related data is arranged in memory and accessed over time.
Temporal locality occurs when the same data is accessed repeatedly within a short period.
CPU caches benefit from locality.
Contiguous arrays often have good spatial locality.
Pointer-heavy structures can have poorer locality.
Data structure choice affects memory performance.
Algorithmic complexity and memory locality both affect performance.
Measure actual workloads.
Cache-line size depends on processor architecture.
False sharing occurs when different threads modify independent data located on the same cache line.
The cache coherence system can repeatedly transfer ownership of the cache line.
False sharing can reduce parallel performance.
False sharing does not necessarily violate correctness.
CPU caches are normally hardware-managed.
Application caches are software-managed.
They are different concepts.
A CPU cache does not replace an application cache.
An application cache does not control processor cache coherence directly.
Cache coherence keeps processor caches sufficiently coordinated according to the hardware memory model.
Cache coherence does not replace language synchronization.
Two threads accessing shared mutable state still require synchronization according to the programming language memory model.
volatile is not a general replacement for synchronization in C or C++.
Atomic operations provide defined concurrent access for atomic objects.
Mutexes provide mutual exclusion.
One thread can hold a pointer while another frees the object.
A lock protecting object contents does not automatically protect object lifetime unless lifetime is part of the locking protocol.
Reference counting can coordinate shared lifetime.
Epoch-based reclamation can delay freeing objects until no relevant readers can reference them.
Concurrent memory reclamation is difficult.
Atomic does not mean memory-safe by itself.
An atomic pointer can still point to an object that has been freed.
Atomic access protects the pointer operation according to its memory semantics.
It does not automatically protect the pointed object's lifetime.
Memory barriers constrain ordering and visibility of memory operations according to architecture or language semantics.
High-level synchronization primitives provide required ordering according to their contracts.
Do not insert low-level memory barriers without understanding the language and processor memory model.
A barrier that is correct for one architecture can be unnecessary or insufficient in another context.
Prefer standard synchronization abstractions.
Memory corruption can affect security boundaries.
A buffer overflow can overwrite control information.
An out-of-bounds read can disclose sensitive data.
Uninitialized memory can disclose previous contents.
Memory-safety testing is therefore part of security engineering.
Address Space Layout Randomization is commonly abbreviated ASLR.
ASLR does not repair memory-safety defects.
NX is a common name for hardware support marking pages non-executable.
Executable-space protections make selected exploitation techniques more difficult.
They do not make invalid writes safe.
Stack canaries can detect selected stack-buffer corruption before a function returns.
Stack canaries are mitigations.
They do not prevent every buffer overflow.
Compiler hardening complements correct memory handling.
Memory-safe design remains preferable to relying on exploitation mitigations.
Securely clearing memory can be required for secrets.
Ordinary compiler optimization can remove a memory-clearing operation if the language considers the write unobservable.
Copies of secret data can exist elsewhere.
Registers can contain copies.
Swap can contain copies.
Crash dumps can contain copies.
Logs can contain copies.
Avoid unnecessary copies of secrets.
Minimize secret lifetime.
Do not log secret memory.
Memory locking is a specialized security mechanism.
It consumes constrained operating-system resources.
Copy-on-write allows multiple mappings or processes to initially share the same physical data.
Process creation mechanisms such as fork commonly use copy-on-write on Unix-like systems.
Copy-on-write reduces immediate duplication cost.
Writing many pages after fork can remove much of that benefit.
Copy-on-write also appears in data structures and storage systems.
The exact semantics depend on the implementation.
Memory-mapped executable code can be shared between processes.
Sharing physical code pages does not mean process writable data is automatically shared.
Virtual memory mappings determine sharing.
Executable files contain sections or segments used to establish process memory.
Writable global data is mapped writable.
BSS commonly describes zero-initialized static-storage data in executable formats.
BSS does not normally require storing all zero bytes explicitly in the executable file.
Static storage is not the same as stack storage.
File-scope static variables commonly have static storage duration.
Function-local static variables also have static storage duration.
Their names and linkage rules differ, but their lifetime extends for the program duration according to C semantics.
Thread-local storage provides separate instances associated with threads.
Thread-local storage consumes memory per applicable thread.
Large thread-local objects can multiply memory usage across many threads.
Thread stacks also multiply memory reservation or commitment across threads.
Concurrency design affects memory requirements.
A worker pool can bound thread count.
Bounded queues can bound queued memory.
Unbounded queues can become memory exhaustion mechanisms.
Backpressure protects memory as well as downstream processing capacity.
Memory exhaustion can become an availability failure.
Allocation failure should not automatically cause undefined behavior.
Some applications can reject work.
Some applications must terminate safely.
The correct behavior depends on requirements.
Continuing with partially initialized state after allocation failure can be worse than controlled failure.
Acquire required resources.
Validate them.
Cleanup paths matter.
Error handling should not leak resources.
C programs commonly use structured cleanup sections for functions acquiring multiple resources.
RAII stands for Resource Acquisition Is Initialization.
RAII binds resource lifetime to object lifetime.
Destructors release resources when owning objects leave scope according to C++ semantics.
RAII can manage memory.
RAII can manage locks.
RAII can manage files.
RAII can manage sockets.
Smart pointers implement selected ownership models in C++.
unique_ptr represents exclusive ownership.
shared_ptr represents shared reference-counted ownership.
weak_ptr represents non-owning observation associated with shared ownership.
Smart pointers reduce manual lifetime mistakes when used correctly.
Shared ownership has overhead and can obscure actual ownership.
Prefer the simplest ownership model that matches the design.
Rust ownership assigns responsibility for values according to compile-time rules.
Borrowing permits temporary access without transferring ownership.
Rust lifetimes describe relationships required for references to remain valid.
Rust can prevent many dangling-reference defects during compilation.
Unsafe Rust permits operations outside ordinary compiler-enforced safety guarantees.
Memory-safe languages can still call unsafe native libraries.
Foreign-function interfaces create memory-safety boundaries.
FFI stands for Foreign Function Interface.
FFI boundaries must agree on structure layout.
FFI boundaries must agree on allocation and release.
A memory-safe language cannot guarantee safety inside arbitrary unsafe foreign code.
Validate boundaries carefully.
Memory serialization copies logical state into a representation suitable for storage or communication.
Serialization is not the same as dumping process memory.
Process addresses should not be stored as durable object references.
Use identifiers.
Use offsets where appropriate.
Use explicit relationships.
Persistent data requires a representation independent of transient memory addresses.
Restarting a process can map objects at different addresses.
ASLR can deliberately change addresses.
Addresses are runtime implementation details unless a specialized architecture defines otherwise.
A memory dump captures selected process memory for diagnosis.
Memory dumps can contain sensitive data.
Crash dumps should be protected.
Dump retention should follow security policy.
Sharing a dump can disclose credentials, keys, personal data, or proprietary information.
Diagnostic value does not remove confidentiality requirements.
Memory inspection can reveal actual runtime state.
A debugger can inspect pointers.
A debugger can inspect stack frames.
A debugger can inspect registers.
A debugger can set watchpoints.
A watchpoint can stop execution when selected memory is accessed or modified where supported.
Debugging changes timing.
Concurrent memory defects can disappear under a debugger.
Absence of failure under debugging does not prove correctness.
Valgrind is a tool suite commonly used on supported systems for memory analysis.
Memcheck can detect selected invalid memory accesses and leaks.
Static analyzers can identify selected memory defects without executing the program.
No tool detects every memory defect.
Use multiple forms of evidence.
A sanitizer report should be investigated from the first invalid operation.
Later reports can be consequences of earlier corruption.
Do not repair only the final crash.
Reproduce with the smallest relevant test when possible.
Verify ownership.
Verify lifetime.
Verify bounds.
Verify allocation size.
Verify initialization.
Verify synchronization.
Memory debugging should distinguish leak from corruption.
A leak loses usable access to needed allocation ownership.
Corruption writes invalid data.
Use-after-free accesses expired storage.
Double free releases storage incorrectly.
Uninitialized read consumes data before valid initialization.
Out-of-memory exhausts available allocation resources.
These failures require different fixes.
Do not label every memory failure a leak.
A segmentation fault is an operating-system signal or failure commonly associated with invalid memory access on Unix-like systems.
A segmentation fault does not identify the root cause by itself.
Null dereference can cause a segmentation fault.
Use-after-free can cause a segmentation fault.
Stack overflow can cause a segmentation fault.
Executing non-executable memory can cause a segmentation fault.
Memory corruption can cause a later segmentation fault.
Inspect evidence.
The underlying engineering question remains which invalid access occurred and why.
An access violation contains information about the faulting operation and address.
The faulting address can provide clues.
A near-zero address can suggest a null pointer plus offset.
Confirm with debugging evidence.
Memory poisoning fills memory with recognizable values to help detect invalid use.
Debug allocators can place guard regions around allocations.
Red zones can detect selected boundary violations.
Diagnostic techniques deliberately increase memory usage.
They are useful during testing.
Canary values can detect selected overwrites.
A canary proves corruption only when its defined value unexpectedly changes under controlled semantics.
A canary does not prevent corruption.
Heap corruption can make allocator failures appear unrelated.
An invalid write can damage allocator metadata.
A later malloc or free can then detect corruption.
Trace backward.
Memory errors can be nondeterministic in appearance.
Allocator layout can change between runs.
Thread scheduling can change timing.
ASLR can change addresses.
Optimization can change object placement.
Different input can change allocation order.
A defect disappearing after adding a log statement can indicate timing or layout sensitivity.
It does not mean the logging fixed the defect.
Heisenbug is an informal term for a defect whose behavior changes when observed or instrumented.
Memory and concurrency defects commonly exhibit this behavior.
Testing should verify memory behavior under realistic builds.
Debug allocators can mask layout-sensitive defects.
Optimized builds can expose undefined behavior.
Memory limits can be deliberate.
Containers can impose memory limits.
Operating systems can impose process limits.
Job-control systems can impose memory limits.
A program can run successfully on a developer machine and fail under a constrained deployment.
Test within expected resource limits.
A memory budget defines how much memory a component is expected to consume.
Estimate fixed memory.
Estimate per-connection memory.
Estimate per-request memory.
Estimate queue memory.
Estimate cache memory.
Estimate peak temporary memory.
Many simultaneous operations can multiply temporary memory.
Memory complexity describes how storage requirements grow with input size.
An algorithm using O(1) auxiliary memory uses bounded additional storage independent of input size under the model.
Big-O notation describes growth behavior.
Constant factors still matter.
Measure when memory matters.
Data representation strongly affects memory usage.
A linked list stores nodes connected through references.
Linked lists require pointer storage in addition to element data.
Arrays often provide better locality.
Linked lists can support efficient insertion when the insertion location is already known and allocation costs are acceptable.
Choose structures according to actual operations.
A hash table trades memory for expected fast lookup.
A sparse representation stores only selected nonzero or present values.
Sparse structures can save memory when data contains many absent values.
Dense structures can be simpler and faster when most values are present.
Representation should follow data characteristics.
Memory ownership can follow module boundaries.
A module should own its internal allocations.
A module can expose copied results.
A module can expose caller-provided output buffers.
A module can expose opaque handles.
Opaque handles hide internal memory representation.
An opaque handle should be used only through its defining API.
Consumers should not assume the internal structure behind an opaque handle.
This preserves ABI flexibility.
A host can change internal representation without changing consumers when the public ABI remains stable.
Opaque interfaces can improve encapsulation.
Encapsulation also improves memory safety by reducing uncontrolled access.
Global mutable memory increases coupling.
Any component able to modify global state can affect other components.
Pass required state explicitly where practical.
Immutable shared configuration can be safer than mutable global configuration.
If configuration must change dynamically, define synchronization and version semantics.
Memory state should not silently become authority.
A value existing in process memory does not prove it is current.
Persistent authoritative state can change elsewhere.
Distributed systems should distinguish local memory from authoritative distributed state.
Memory is local unless explicitly shared or replicated.
A process restart destroys ordinary volatile state.
Required state must be persisted if it must survive restart.
Before acknowledging durable success, establish required persistence.
In-memory completion is not durable completion.
A queue stored only in RAM disappears on process termination.
That can be acceptable for disposable work.
It is not acceptable when work must survive restart.
Requirements determine persistence.
Reconstruction should be deterministic where required.
Do not depend on stale memory after restart.
Startup should initialize state from defined sources.
Uninitialized memory must never be treated as recovered state.
Zero initialization is not equivalent to valid application initialization unless zero is the defined initial state.
Initialization should establish invariants.
Zeroed bytes are not necessarily a semantically valid object.
Deserialized bytes are not valid until validated.
Memory safety and data validation are different.
Untrusted input should be validated before being used to control memory allocation.
A length field can request huge allocation.
A count field can overflow size calculations.
An offset can point outside a buffer.
A nested structure can cause excessive recursion.
A compressed payload can expand dramatically.
Resource limits are part of parser security.
Validate length before allocation.
Validate nesting depth.
Validate total decoded size.
Do not trust a buffer merely because it arrived from a known peer.
Known peers can malfunction.
Protocol parsers should operate on bounded memory.
Failure should not leak previously allocated resources.
Failure should not leave partially accepted state.
Parsing and validation should complete before authoritative state is changed where architecture permits.
Memory safety should fail closed.
When bounds cannot be established, do not perform the access.
When ownership cannot be established, do not free the resource arbitrarily.
When lifetime cannot be established, do not retain a borrowed pointer.
When allocation size cannot be represented safely, reject the allocation request.
When data length is unknown, do not guess.
Unknown capacity is not sufficient evidence of capacity.
Memory correctness requires evidence about size, lifetime, ownership, and access.
Do not confuse an address with an object.
Do not confuse a pointer with ownership.
Do not confuse a pointer with a length.
Do not confuse scope with lifetime.
Do not confuse heap with all dynamically changing data.
Do not confuse virtual memory with physical RAM.
Do not confuse address space with available memory.
Do not confuse mapped memory with valid object access.
Do not confuse a page fault with a program defect.
Do not confuse free with zeroing memory.
Do not confuse setting one pointer to null with eliminating every dangling alias.
Do not confuse reference counting with cycle collection.
Do not confuse memory safety with logical correctness.
Do not confuse memory protection with language safety.
Do not confuse allocation success with valid initialization.
Do not confuse zero-filled storage with valid application state.
Do not confuse resident memory growth with proof of a leak.
Do not confuse allocator retention with leaked ownership.
Do not confuse a sanitizer-clean run with proof of safety.
Do not confuse shared memory with synchronized memory.
Do not confuse executable mitigations with repaired source defects.
Do not confuse serialization with copying native memory.
Do not confuse runtime addresses with persistent identifiers.
Do not confuse memory cache with authoritative state.
Do not confuse volatile memory with durable state.
Do not confuse successful processing in RAM with durable completion.
Check arithmetic.
Check allocation success.
Establish ownership.
Use only within valid bounds.
Release according to the correct allocator.
When accepting a pointer, determine whether it can be null.
Determine its required alignment.
Determine the accessible length.
Determine whether the function borrows it.
Determine whether ownership transfers.
Document those rules.
Define length.
Define lifetime.
Define release mechanism.
Define mutability.
Do not force callers to infer these properties.
When debugging memory corruption, find the earliest invalid operation.
Check ownership.
Check initialization.
Check allocation sizes.
Check concurrent access.
Check module boundaries.
Use tools to establish evidence.
Do not guess from the final crash alone.
Bound workers.
Bound queues.
Bound request sizes.
Bound message sizes.
Bound parser depth.
Bound retries that retain memory.
Bound retained history where policy permits.
Measure peak memory.
Test sustained operation.
Memory is finite.
Address space is finite.
Allocator metadata consumes memory.
Threads consume memory.
Queues consume memory.
Connections consume memory.
Caches consume memory.
Logs consume memory.
Temporary operations consume memory.
Every unbounded structure is a potential resource-exhaustion path.
Simple ownership reduces memory defects.
Explicit lengths reduce bounds defects.
Explicit lifetimes reduce dangling references.
Explicit allocation limits reduce exhaustion.
Explicit serialization reduces platform dependence.
Explicit synchronization reduces shared-memory races.
Memory should be treated as a controlled resource.
Allocate deliberately.
Own explicitly.
Bound precisely.
Synchronize when shared.
Release deterministically when the ownership model requires it.
Persist only what must survive.
Epistemic reasoning is the practice of distinguishing what is known, how it is known, and what remains uncertain.
A claim is not evidence for itself; an assertion and its supporting observations are different objects.
An observation records what was directly perceived or measured, including its source, time, and limitations.
An assumption is a proposition temporarily accepted for reasoning without independent verification.
A hypothesis is a testable proposed explanation; a hypothesis should not be reported as an established fact.
A verified fact is a claim supported by appropriate evidence under an explicitly stated verification standard.
A source may be accurate about one claim and unreliable about another; evaluate claims individually.
Source provenance identifies where information originated and how it reached the current system.
Evidence freshness matters when conditions can change; an old observation may not describe the present state.
Independent corroboration is stronger than repeated copies of a single original report.
Two reports derived from the same source are not independent confirmation.
Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence unless the observation process was capable of detecting the event.
A failed search establishes that nothing was found by that search, not that the searched-for thing does not exist.
A successful process exit establishes only the conditions tested by that process; it does not prove untested runtime behavior.
A passing unit test supports the tested assertion, not every property of the program.
Compilation confirms that a build completed, not that a module behaves correctly after activation.
Module activation confirms that a module entered the active state, not that end-to-end delivery succeeded.
Delivery to one transport does not establish delivery to another transport.
A search result confirms retrieval, not the truth of the retrieved statement.
A source citation helps identify provenance; it does not automatically establish correctness.
When sources conflict, preserve each claim's provenance and identify the disagreement rather than silently choosing one.
Prefer a more direct and recent observation when evaluating rapidly changing operational state, while retaining contradictory evidence for review.
Do not fabricate measurements, logs, timestamps, command output, test results, citations, or operational status.
Distinguish activation from human acceptance of observed behavior.
Confidence expresses strength of support, not certainty; avoid false numerical precision without a calibrated model.
Confidence should decrease when supporting evidence depends on unverified assumptions or correlated sources.
Updating a belief after new evidence is a correction, not an inconsistency to conceal.
A counterexample can refute a universal claim when the counterexample satisfies the claim's stated conditions.
A single successful example does not prove a universal claim.
Distinguish necessary conditions from sufficient conditions when evaluating explanations and operational gates.
Correlation alone does not establish a causal relationship.
A causal explanation should identify a plausible mechanism and rule out relevant alternatives where possible.
Operational diagnosis begins with observed symptoms, then tests competing explanations using discriminating evidence.
Record what was expected and what actually occurred before concluding whether a change worked.
When an action is reversible, a narrowly scoped experiment may provide stronger evidence than speculation, subject to operator authorization.
Preserve source material during ingestion so interpretation errors can be detected and corrected later.
Deduplication should distinguish exact textual duplicates from semantically related but materially different claims.
A rejected parser line may contain useful information; track exclusion reasons before treating exclusions as harmless.
A report of zero changes is meaningful only when the detection mechanism and its monitored scope are known.
Knowledge retrieval should distinguish relevance ranking from evidence reliability; high-ranked text can still be wrong.
Prefer answers that expose assumptions and verification boundaries over answers that merely sound confident.
State machines describe systems whose behavior depends on their current state and on defined transitions between states.
A state represents a meaningful condition of a system.
An event is something that can cause a transition to be considered.
Keep these concepts separate.
A basic state machine can be represented as:
Current State + Event + Conditions -> Next State
DISCONNECTED + CONNECT -> CONNECTING
CONNECTING + CONNECTION_SUCCESS -> CONNECTED
CONNECTING + CONNECTION_FAILURE -> DISCONNECTED
CONNECTED + DISCONNECT -> DISCONNECTED
The current state matters.
The same event can produce different behavior in different states.
CONNECT while DISCONNECTED can begin a connection.
CONNECT while CONNECTED can be rejected or ignored according to policy.
State defines context.
A finite-state machine has a finite set of defined states.
STOPPED
RUNNING
STOPPING
The system should know which states exist.
Multiple Boolean flags can accidentally represent impossible conditions.
is_starting = true
What state is the system actually in?
A single explicit state can make the invariant clearer.
state = RUNNING
Use explicit states when behavior is fundamentally state-dependent.
Not every Boolean requires a state machine.
A feature that is simply enabled or disabled can remain a Boolean.
Do not introduce state-machine complexity where two independent values are sufficient.
States should represent meaningful system conditions.
Avoid vague states such as:
BUSY
OTHER
unless their semantics are explicitly defined.
A state name should communicate what condition exists.
UNINITIALIZED
PROCESSING
SHUTTING_DOWN
Choose them deliberately.
