A transition can be modeled as:
validate event
load current state
authenticate actor if required
authorize event if required
evaluate guards
reserve or synchronize transition if required
perform transition action according to defined ordering
persist next state
emit resulting events
record audit information
Define transactional boundaries.
Lock if required.
Evaluate transition.
Change state.
Perform defined local actions.
Release according to synchronization design.
For durable workflow:
Use transactions or conditional updates where appropriate.
Use durable intent, idempotency, and reconciliation where appropriate.
Do not pretend one technique solves every state transition.
State machines should remain small enough to understand.
A machine with hundreds of states can indicate several independent lifecycles have been combined.
Consider decomposition.
Do not split a coherent lifecycle merely to reduce a number.
State explosion occurs when every combination of independent properties becomes a separate state.
CONNECTED_AUTHENTICATED_SYNCED_ACTIVE
CONNECTED_AUTHENTICATED_SYNCED_PAUSED
CONNECTED_AUTHENTICATED_UNSYNCED_ACTIVE
and many more.
Then define cross-machine invariants.
Do not hide complexity by creating giant names.
State-machine documentation should answer:
What states exist?
What is the initial state?
Which transitions are permitted?
What guards apply?
What actions occur?
What permissions are required?
What happens on invalid events?
What happens on failure?
What happens on restart?
What state is persisted?
What transitions are audited?
What concurrency controls apply?
If these cannot be answered, the machine is not fully defined.
A reliable state machine has explicit states.
A reliable state machine has explicit events.
A reliable state machine has explicit transitions.
A reliable state machine rejects undefined transitions.
A reliable state machine evaluates guards before protected transitions.
A reliable state machine does not confuse desired state with actual state.
A reliable state machine does not confuse command acceptance with completion.
A reliable state machine handles duplicate and stale events according to policy.
A reliable state machine preserves durable state when required.
A reliable state machine does not invent authority.
A reliable state machine does not guess.
Do not confuse state with event.
Do not confuse state with action.
Do not confuse state with error code.
Do not confuse requested state with current state.
Do not confuse valid destination with valid transition.
Do not confuse authentication attempt with authenticated state.
Do not confuse detection with confirmation.
Do not confuse discovery with admission.
Do not confuse work requested with work completed.
Do not confuse stored state with current reality when external systems can change independently.
What is the current state?
Is this event defined for that state?
Is the actor authenticated where required?
Is the actor authorized where required?
Are the guards satisfied?
Has it already been processed?
Does concurrency require synchronization?
What is the defined next state?
What actions are required?
How is success established?
How is failure represented?
If the transition is not defined, do not invent one.
If a guard fails, remain according to defined policy.
If authority is absent, deny the transition.
If state is unknown, do not assume a privileged state.
If the event is duplicated, apply defined idempotency behavior.
If the operation outcome is uncertain, do not claim completion.
Events request or report change.
Transitions define permitted change.
Guards define required conditions.
A computer network allows systems to exchange data.
Network communication occurs through defined protocols.
A protocol defines rules for communication between participants.
A failure at one network layer should not automatically be reported as a failure at another layer.
Physical connectivity, IP routing, transport connectivity, name resolution, TLS, and application protocols are distinct concerns.
The Internet Protocol is commonly abbreviated IP.
IP provides packet delivery between network interfaces across interconnected networks.
IPv4 is version 4 of the Internet Protocol.
IPv6 is version 6 of the Internet Protocol.
IPv4 and IPv6 use different address formats.
An IPv4 address contains 32 bits.
An IPv6 address contains 128 bits.
An IPv4 address is commonly written as four decimal octets.
192.0.2.1 is an example IPv4 address.
An IPv6 address is commonly written as hexadecimal groups separated by colons.
2001:db8::1 is an example IPv6 address reserved for documentation.
An IP address identifies an interface or addressing endpoint according to network configuration.
An IP address does not identify an application by itself.
Multiple applications can communicate through the same IP address using different transport endpoints.
A host can have multiple IP addresses.
A hostname can resolve to multiple IP addresses.
Multiple hostnames can resolve to the same IP address.
IPv4 address 127.0.0.1 is part of the loopback range.
127.0.0.1 commonly refers to the local IPv4 host.
IPv6 address ::1 is the IPv6 loopback address.
Loopback traffic does not ordinarily leave the local host.
A server binding to 0.0.0.0 commonly listens on all available IPv4 interfaces.
The unspecified IPv6 address is ::.
Binding and connecting are different operations.
A server binds a local endpoint.
A client connects to a remote endpoint.
A socket is an operating-system abstraction for communication.
A network socket can be associated with a protocol family.
A network socket can be associated with a transport protocol.
A socket endpoint can include an IP address and port.
A TCP connection is identified by endpoint information for both participants.
Port numbers range from 0 through 65535.
A port number does not identify a protocol by itself.
TCP port 80 and UDP port 80 are different transport endpoints.
Port assignments are conventions and configuration.
An application can use a nonstandard port when both sides agree.
A service listening on a port does not prove it implements the expected application protocol.
A TCP listener on port 443 does not by itself prove valid HTTPS behavior.
Protocol qualification requires protocol-level evidence.
TCP stands for Transmission Control Protocol.
TCP is connection-oriented.
TCP provides a reliable ordered byte stream between endpoints while the connection remains operational.
TCP detects loss and performs retransmission according to its protocol.
TCP does not preserve application message boundaries.
One send operation does not necessarily correspond to one receive operation.
A receiver can receive part of one sender write.
Applications using TCP must define message framing.
Message framing identifies where one application message ends and another begins.
Delimiter framing separates messages using a defined delimiter.
Fixed-size framing uses messages of a predetermined size.
Protocol-specific framing can combine multiple techniques.
A newline can be used as a delimiter only when the protocol defines how newlines inside content are handled.
JSON alone does not define framing for multiple JSON documents over a raw TCP stream.
HTTP defines its own message framing.
A custom TCP protocol must define its own framing.
Failure to handle partial reads is a common TCP programming error.
A successful write call can report fewer bytes written than requested depending on API and conditions.
A correct application handles the number of bytes actually transferred.
TCP connection establishment uses a handshake.
The TCP handshake establishes transport connectivity.
Successful TCP connection establishment does not prove application compatibility.
Successful TCP connection establishment does not prove authentication.
Successful TCP connection establishment does not prove protocol qualification.
Transport connectivity and application readiness are different states.
TCP connections have state.
A connection can be established.
A connection can be closing.
A connection can be closed.
A connection can be reset.
Applications should define how connection closure affects their protocol.
TCP keepalive can detect some dead peer conditions over long-lived connections.
TCP keepalive is not the same as an application heartbeat.
An application heartbeat can test application-level responsiveness.
A TCP keepalive tests transport-level connection conditions according to operating-system behavior.
A successful application heartbeat provides stronger evidence of application responsiveness than mere socket existence.
A connection can remain established while an application is deadlocked or otherwise unable to perform useful work.
Application health should be tested at the required layer.
UDP stands for User Datagram Protocol.
UDP is connectionless at the protocol level.
UDP sends datagrams.
UDP preserves datagram boundaries.
UDP does not guarantee delivery.
UDP does not guarantee ordering.
UDP does not guarantee duplicate suppression.
UDP does not provide TCP-style retransmission.
A UDP send succeeding does not prove the remote application received the datagram.
A UDP application can receive duplicate messages.
A UDP application can receive messages out of order.
UDP is appropriate when its semantics match application requirements.
TCP is appropriate when reliable ordered byte-stream semantics match application requirements.
Neither TCP nor UDP is universally superior.
Protocol choice should follow requirements.
IP packets can be duplicated.
IP packets can arrive out of order.
Transport protocols handle these conditions according to their semantics.
Networks are not inherently reliable.
Applications should expect communication failure.
Timeouts bound how long an operation waits.
A read timeout limits waiting for incoming data.
An application timeout can limit an entire operation.
Different timeout types should remain distinguishable.
A timeout is not proof that the remote system performed no work.
A request can reach a server and complete even when the client times out before receiving the response.
Retrying after an ambiguous timeout can duplicate an operation.
State-changing operations require deliberate retry design.
Idempotency can make retries safer.
A retry should be bounded.
Infinite immediate retry loops can create failure storms.
Retry delays can reduce pressure on a failing dependency.
Exponential backoff increases delay after repeated failures.
Jitter adds variation to retry timing.
Jitter can reduce synchronized reconnect storms.
Many clients reconnecting simultaneously can overload a recovering server.
A reconnect loop should have bounded and controlled timing.
Repeated disconnect and reconnect behavior can indicate transport instability.
Repeated disconnect and reconnect behavior can indicate application timeout configuration.
Repeated disconnect and reconnect behavior can indicate server overload.
Repeated disconnect and reconnect behavior can indicate an intermediary closing connections.
Repeated disconnect and reconnect behavior can indicate client logic incorrectly treating normal conditions as failure.
The reconnect pattern alone does not prove the cause.
Connection refused is different from connection timeout.
A connection timeout indicates that connection establishment did not complete within the configured time.
A timeout does not prove that the host is offline.
A firewall can silently drop packets.
Routing problems can prevent packets from reaching the destination.
An accepted connection indicates a listener accepted transport establishment.
Neither condition alone proves application correctness.
DNS stands for Domain Name System.
DNS maps names to data including network addresses.
DNS is a distributed naming system.
A DNS lookup can succeed while the resulting service is unavailable.
A DNS lookup can fail while a service remains reachable directly by IP address.
A DNS record can exist while no application listens at the resulting address.
DNS data has caching behavior.
DNS records have TTL values.
TTL stands for Time To Live.
A DNS TTL influences how long applicable DNS data can be cached.
Cached old values can remain until expiration according to resolver behavior.
An A record maps a name to an IPv4 address.
An AAAA record maps a name to an IPv6 address.
An MX record identifies mail exchange information.
A TXT record stores text data used by many higher-level mechanisms.
An NS record identifies authoritative name servers for a DNS zone.
A PTR record supports reverse DNS mappings.
DNS record types have different purposes.
A hostname used by an application should resolve according to the application's network requirements.
IPv4 and IPv6 connectivity can differ.
Successful IPv4 connectivity does not prove successful IPv6 connectivity.
Successful IPv6 connectivity does not prove successful IPv4 connectivity.
Routing determines where IP packets are sent.
A routing table contains route information.
A route can identify a destination network.
A route can identify a gateway.
A route can identify an outgoing interface.
A default IPv4 route is commonly represented as 0.0.0.0/0.
A default IPv6 route is commonly represented as ::/0.
Longest-prefix matching is used to select more specific IP routes.
A gateway forwards packets between networks.
A router operates primarily at the network layer.
A missing route can prevent communication.
A wrong route can send traffic toward the wrong network.
Routing loops can prevent successful delivery.
traceroute can provide evidence about the path packets take where network devices permit the required responses.
A traceroute failure does not necessarily mean the final destination is unreachable.
Network devices can filter traceroute-related traffic.
ICMP communicates network control and diagnostic information.
Ping success proves some IP and ICMP communication occurred.
Ping success does not prove TCP connectivity.
Ping failure does not prove a host is down.
A host can serve TCP applications while refusing ICMP echo requests.
Network diagnosis should test the layer relevant to the problem.
A subnet groups addresses according to a network prefix.
CIDR stands for Classless Inter-Domain Routing.
CIDR notation combines an address with prefix length.
192.0.2.0/24 represents an IPv4 prefix with 24 network-prefix bits.
2001:db8::/32 represents an IPv6 prefix with 32 network-prefix bits.
A subnet mask describes network-prefix boundaries in IPv4 representations.
Hosts determine whether a destination is on-link according to routing and prefix configuration.
Private IPv4 address ranges include 10.0.0.0/8.
Private IPv4 address ranges include 172.16.0.0/12.
Private IPv4 address ranges include 192.168.0.0/16.
Private does not mean secure.
A private network can contain hostile or compromised systems.
Network location is not authentication.
Link-local IPv4 addresses use 169.254.0.0/16.
IPv6 has link-local addressing commonly using fe80::/10.
Link-local addresses have limited routing scope.
Address scope matters.
NAT modifies network addressing information according to translation rules.
NAT commonly allows multiple private hosts to share public IPv4 connectivity.
NAT is not a security model by itself.
A NAT device can incidentally block unsolicited inbound connections when no mapping exists.
NAT and firewalling are different concepts.
Port forwarding creates translation rules allowing traffic toward an internal service.
A firewall can allow traffic.
A firewall can reject traffic.
A firewall can drop traffic.
Reject and drop have different observable behavior.
Reject commonly sends an error response.
Drop commonly discards traffic without an explicit rejection response.
A dropped TCP SYN can lead to a connection timeout.
A rejected TCP connection can produce an immediate failure.
Firewall behavior can therefore affect observed client errors.
An application should not infer too much from one network symptom.
Host firewalls and network firewalls are separate enforcement points.
Cloud-provider firewalls can add another enforcement point.
Multiple firewall layers can exist simultaneously.
Opening a host firewall does not prove an upstream firewall permits traffic.
A listening application does not prove routing reaches it.
Network interfaces connect hosts to networks.
An interface has state.
An interface can be administratively up.
An interface can be administratively down.
An interface can have one or more addresses.
An interface can have routes.
The loopback interface is commonly named lo on Linux.
Ethernet interfaces can have implementation-dependent names.
ip addr displays interface addressing on Linux.
ip link displays link-layer interface information.
ip route displays routing information.
ss displays socket information.
ss -l can show listening sockets according to options.
A listening socket proves that a local socket is listening.
A listening socket does not prove external reachability.
netstat is an older networking utility still present on some systems.
ss is commonly preferred on modern Linux systems.
Network tools provide evidence about particular layers.
Ethernet operates at a link layer.
Ethernet frames carry network-layer packets on Ethernet networks.
A MAC address identifies a link-layer interface according to Ethernet addressing.
MAC addresses and IP addresses operate at different layers.
A router normally forwards based on IP routing rather than remote MAC addresses across routed networks.
IPv6 uses Neighbor Discovery rather than ARP.
Neighbor Discovery uses ICMPv6.
Neighbor Discovery failure can prevent applicable IPv6 communication.
Link-layer problems and IP-layer problems are different.
MTU stands for Maximum Transmission Unit.
MTU limits packet size on a network link.
Different network paths can have different effective MTUs.
Path MTU Discovery attempts to determine usable packet sizes.
Incorrectly filtered ICMP can interfere with Path MTU Discovery.
MTU problems can produce connections that establish but fail when larger data transfers occur.
A successful handshake does not prove all packet sizes will traverse the path.
MSS stands for Maximum Segment Size in TCP.
TCP MSS relates to payload sizing within the path's packet constraints.
Fragmentation and segmentation are different mechanisms.
TLS stands for Transport Layer Security.
TLS provides confidentiality and integrity for application communication when correctly configured.
TLS can authenticate servers through certificates.
TLS can optionally authenticate clients.
TLS commonly operates above TCP.
HTTPS uses HTTP over TLS.
TLS and TCP are different layers.
TCP connectivity can succeed while TLS negotiation fails.
TLS negotiation can succeed while HTTP fails.
Each stage should remain distinguishable.
A TLS certificate binds identity information according to certificate validation rules.
A certificate can be expired.
A certificate can be issued by a trusted certification path.
A certificate can fail hostname validation.
Certificate trust and hostname validation are separate checks.
Disabling certificate verification removes important authentication guarantees.
A client should not disable TLS verification merely to make an error disappear.
TLS protocol versions differ.
TLS cipher suites define cryptographic algorithm combinations according to protocol version.
TLS configuration should use currently appropriate security settings.
TLS protects data in transit.
TLS does not protect a compromised endpoint.
TLS does not provide application authorization by itself.
TLS does not prevent an authorized application from returning incorrect data.
TLS does not prevent application logic errors.
Network security requires multiple layers.
A proxy forwards communication on behalf of clients or servers.
A reverse proxy can route requests.
A reverse proxy can load balance.
A reverse proxy can enforce request limits.
A reverse proxy can alter headers.
Proxy behavior introduces another failure layer.
A client can successfully reach a reverse proxy while the reverse proxy cannot reach the origin.
An upstream connection failure can produce an HTTP gateway error.
HTTP 502 commonly indicates invalid or failed upstream response behavior at a gateway or proxy.
HTTP 504 commonly indicates an upstream timeout at a gateway or proxy.
Architecture determines whether the component is acting as a gateway.
Load balancing distributes traffic among multiple service instances.
A load balancer can use round-robin selection.
A load balancer can use least-connections selection.
A load balancer can use health information.
A load balancer can use other routing policies.
Health checks should test the capability required for traffic.
A process existing is weaker evidence than successful application-level qualification.
A backend can be reachable but unhealthy.
Failover changes service traffic from one endpoint to another.
Failover should occur according to defined qualification rules.
A fallback endpoint should be validated before use.
Merely being listed in configuration does not prove an endpoint is healthy.
A successful TCP connection is one qualification stage.
A successful protocol handshake is a stronger qualification stage.
Network identity can be validated at the application layer.
Protocol version can be validated.
Network identifier can be validated.
Genesis identifier can be validated in a blockchain protocol.
Qualification should reject an endpoint that answers transport connections but belongs to the wrong network.
Transport success is not identity success.
A peer is another participant in a network protocol.
Peer-to-peer is commonly abbreviated P2P.
P2P does not mean that every peer has identical authority.
P2P does not mean that every peer stores identical state at every instant.
P2P protocol rules define participant behavior.
A peer discovery mechanism identifies candidate peers.
Discovery and qualification are different stages.
A discovered peer is not automatically trusted.
A peer handshake can exchange identity information.
A peer handshake can exchange protocol version.
A peer handshake can exchange capabilities.
A peer handshake can exchange current state information.
A peer can send malformed messages.
A peer can send oversized messages.
A peer can send unsupported messages.
A peer can disconnect unexpectedly.
A peer can lie.
Network protocol implementations should treat remote input as untrusted.
Message lengths should be bounded.
Field values should be validated.
State transitions should be validated.
Malformed remote input should not corrupt local state.
A protocol parser should reject invalid messages safely.
Network protocols should define byte order.
Network byte order is conventionally big-endian.
Multi-byte integer serialization should use explicit byte order.
Sending raw native integer memory across a network is not portable.
Sending raw C structures across a network is not portable unless the protocol deliberately fixes layout, alignment, byte order, and sizes.
Structure padding can differ between compilers and platforms.
Protocol serialization should be explicit.
A 32-bit protocol field should have exactly defined width.
Signedness should be defined.
Malformed lengths should be rejected before allocation where possible.
An attacker-controlled length should not cause unbounded memory allocation.
Message framing should be validated before message processing.
Protocol parsing should separate transport receipt from semantic validation.
Receiving bytes does not mean the message is valid.
Parsing a message does not mean the message is authorized.
Validating syntax does not mean the message is truthful.
Authorization and consensus rules can apply after parsing.
Protocol state machines define allowed message sequences.
A message can be syntactically valid but invalid in the current protocol state.
A protocol should not silently guess the meaning of unknown message types.
Unknown protocol versions should be handled according to compatibility policy.
Backward compatibility should be deliberate.
Forward compatibility should be deliberate.
Network protocol behavior should not depend on undefined implementation behavior.
A heartbeat is an application-level message indicating liveness according to protocol rules.
A heartbeat interval defines expected heartbeat timing.
A heartbeat timeout defines when a peer can be considered unresponsive according to policy.
Heartbeat intervals should not be unnecessarily aggressive.
A heartbeat succeeding does not prove every application operation works.
A protocol-specific health request can test stronger capability.
Long-lived connections need lifecycle management.
A connection should have a defined establishment state.
A connection should have a defined qualification state.
A connection should have a defined closing state.
A connection should not be considered qualified merely because a socket descriptor exists.
Reconnect logic should distinguish intentional shutdown from failure.
Intentional shutdown should not trigger endless reconnection.
Authentication failure should not necessarily trigger rapid automatic retry.
Transient transport failure can justify retry.
Permanent configuration failure should be surfaced rather than hidden behind infinite retry.
Retry behavior should depend on failure category.
DNS failure can be retried according to policy.
Connection refusal can be retried according to policy.
Authentication rejection should normally require correcting credentials or authority.
Protocol-version rejection should normally require compatibility correction.
Malformed local configuration should be corrected rather than repeatedly retried.
Deterministic failure classification prevents useless reconnect loops.
A connection pool maintains reusable connections.
A leaked connection can exhaust a pool.
An unhealthy pooled connection should not be reused blindly.
A pool should validate connections according to its contract.
Idle connections can be closed by servers, proxies, firewalls, or NAT devices.
A client should tolerate a pooled connection becoming stale.
Round-trip time is commonly abbreviated RTT.
RTT measures time for a message or packet exchange to travel to a destination and back according to the measurement method.
A high-bandwidth link can still have high latency.
A low-latency link can still have limited bandwidth.
Throughput can be lower than nominal bandwidth.
Packet loss can reduce TCP throughput.
High latency can affect protocols requiring many sequential round trips.
Application design can reduce unnecessary round trips.
Batching can reduce round trips.
Persistent connections can reduce repeated handshake overhead.
Caching can reduce network requests.
Performance optimization should be measured.
A slow request is not necessarily caused by the network.
Server computation can dominate latency.
Database queries can dominate latency.
Queueing can dominate latency.
DNS resolution can contribute latency.
TCP connection establishment can contribute latency.
TLS negotiation can contribute latency.
Network monitoring can observe connection and traffic behavior.
Wireshark analyzes network protocol traffic.
Unencrypted application data can be visible in packet captures.
TLS encrypts application payloads in transit.
TLS metadata can still reveal some communication characteristics.
Packet captures should be protected according to their contents.
A packet capture provides evidence from the capture point.
Traffic absent from one interface capture does not prove it was absent from every network segment.
Logs and packet captures provide different evidence.
Application logs show application interpretation.
Packet captures show observed network traffic.
Kernel socket state shows transport state.
DNS tools show name-resolution behavior.
Each source answers different questions.
netcat can create simple TCP or UDP connections according to implementation.
A successful netcat TCP connection proves basic transport connectivity to the listener.
It does not prove the application protocol is correct.
telnet can establish plain TCP sessions and is sometimes used for basic diagnostics.
telnet is not appropriate for transmitting sensitive credentials over unencrypted networks.
curl is useful for HTTP and related protocol testing.
dig queries DNS.
nslookup queries DNS but has different behavior and output.
ss examines sockets.
tcpdump examines packets.
No single diagnostic command proves every layer is healthy.
Diagnosis should begin with the observed failure.
If a hostname cannot resolve, inspect DNS.
If an address resolves but connection establishment fails, inspect routing, firewall, and listener state.
If TCP connects but TLS fails, inspect TLS configuration and identity.
If TLS succeeds but HTTP fails, inspect HTTP behavior.
If HTTP succeeds but application data is wrong, inspect application logic and data.
If an application protocol handshake fails after TCP connection, inspect protocol compatibility and state.
Layered diagnosis reduces speculation.
"Network error" is less useful than a specific failure category.
"DNS resolution failed" identifies a layer.
"TCP connection refused" identifies a layer.
"TCP connection timed out" identifies a layer.
"TLS certificate validation failed" identifies a layer.
"Read timed out waiting for response" identifies a layer.
"Peer closed connection" identifies a layer.
"Malformed response" identifies a layer.
Specific errors improve debugging.
Specific errors reduce false assumptions.
Windows network error codes and Linux errno values are platform-specific representations.
A platform-independent application can translate platform-specific network errors into defined internal categories.
The original platform error should remain available for diagnostics where useful.
A portable network layer should preserve semantic meaning across operating systems.
A timeout should remain a timeout across platforms.
A DNS failure should remain a DNS failure across platforms.
Network byte serialization should produce identical protocol bytes across supported platforms.
Protocol behavior should not depend on Windows or Linux socket implementation details.
The platform interface can translate operating-system networking APIs into common internal operations.
Common protocol semantics should remain platform-independent.
Platform-specific implementation behavior belongs behind the platform boundary.
A network module should have a defined interface.
The interface can define connect.
The interface can define listen.
The interface can define accept.
The interface can define send.
The interface can define receive.
The interface can define close.
The interface can define timeout behavior.
The interface can define address resolution.
The interface can define error categories.
The exact interface depends on architecture.
An interface should expose enough information for callers to distinguish meaningful failures.
Returning one generic failure for every network condition destroys information.
A Boolean success-or-failure return can be insufficient when callers need recovery decisions.
Network errors should not automatically become knowledge errors.
A failed socket connection does not mean information does not exist.
A knowledge miss does not mean the socket failed.
A protocol response saying no records were found means the remote service responded.
A remote service returning no matching records is evidence that communication succeeded far enough to receive that result.
A service timeout and an empty result are different.
A connection failure and an unsupported request are different.
Operational state and information state should remain distinct.
A service can be online and know nothing about a requested subject.
A service can contain the requested information while temporarily being unreachable.
Distributed systems communicate over networks.
A distributed system should expect partial failure.
One component can fail while others remain operational.
One network path can fail while another remains operational.
One peer can be unreachable while other peers remain reachable.
A DNS server can fail while cached records remain usable.
A primary endpoint can fail while a qualified fallback remains available.
Architecture should define which failures are fatal.
Architecture should define which failures cause degraded state.
Architecture should define which failures permit failover.
Architecture should define which failures should merely be logged.
Failover should preserve protocol identity.
A fallback server belonging to the wrong network is not a valid failover merely because it accepts a connection.
A fallback server using an incompatible protocol is not a valid failover.
Qualification should precede promotion to active use.
Network state changes over time.
A peer qualified earlier can later fail.
A previously failed peer can later recover.
Health should be re-evaluated according to policy.
Re-evaluation should not create excessive traffic.
A cached qualification result should have a defined lifetime if continued validity matters.
Connection establishment itself can provide fresh evidence.
Trust decisions should use the evidence required by the architecture.
A network identifier distinguishes one logical network from another.
A protocol version distinguishes protocol revisions.
A node identifier identifies a protocol participant according to protocol rules.
An address identifies a transport location.
These identifiers are not interchangeable.
A node can change IP address while retaining logical identity if the protocol permits it.
An IP address can host multiple logical nodes or services.
Transport location and protocol identity should remain separate.
Cryptographic identity can bind protocol identity to a key.
A public key can identify an entity according to protocol design.
A network connection alone does not prove cryptographic identity.
TLS server authentication can establish transport endpoint identity according to certificate rules.
Application signatures can establish message-level authenticity according to application rules.
Transport authentication and message authentication are different layers.
Network data should be treated as untrusted until validated according to the protocol.
Length fields should be checked.
Cryptographic hashes should be verified when the protocol defines them.
Signatures should be verified when required.
Network identifiers should be checked.
Protocol versions should be checked.
Sequence numbers should be checked when defined.
Replay protection should be applied when required.
Replay occurs when a previously valid message is transmitted again in a context where repetition is not authorized.
Nonces can help distinguish protocol exchanges.
Sequence numbers can help detect duplicates or ordering violations.
Replay protection must match protocol requirements.
A checksum detects some accidental corruption.
A checksum is not generally a cryptographic authentication mechanism.
A message authentication code can provide integrity and authentication when participants share an appropriate secret.
A digital signature can provide message authenticity using asymmetric cryptography when correctly implemented.
Networking and cryptography solve related but distinct problems.
Broadcast sends traffic to multiple participants within applicable network scope.
IPv4 supports broadcast addressing.
IPv6 does not use broadcast in the same way and relies heavily on multicast.
Multicast delivers traffic to subscribed groups according to network support.
Unicast addresses one destination endpoint.
Broadcast, multicast, and unicast have different scaling and routing behavior.
Public Internet routing does not generally forward local broadcast traffic.
Service discovery on a LAN can use multicast or broadcast protocols.
Service discovery across routed networks requires appropriate mechanisms.
A network daemon should validate discovery responses.
Discovery does not establish trust.
A public service must intentionally bind and be permitted through firewall policy.
Exposing an internal control interface publicly increases attack surface.
Attack surface should be minimized.
Unused listeners should be disabled.
Unused protocols should be disabled when practical.
Default credentials should not be used.
Rate limiting can protect network services from excessive request volume.
Connection limits can protect finite resources.
Per-client limits can reduce abuse.
Timeouts can prevent idle clients from consuming resources indefinitely.
Maximum connection counts can prevent descriptor exhaustion.
Maximum queue sizes can prevent unbounded memory growth.
Backpressure allows overloaded components to slow producers instead of accepting unlimited work.
Dropping work can be appropriate when protocol and application semantics permit it.
Silent failure makes diagnosis difficult.
A server under overload should preserve enough capacity to report useful status where practical.
Denial-of-service conditions can exhaust bandwidth.
Denial-of-service conditions can exhaust CPU.
Denial-of-service conditions can exhaust memory.
Denial-of-service conditions can exhaust application worker pools.
Distributed denial-of-service is commonly abbreviated DDoS.
DDoS uses traffic or requests from multiple sources to disrupt service.
The observed source IP of hostile traffic is not always the true human or organizational origin.
Source addresses can be spoofed in protocols where return traffic is unnecessary.
Proxies and relays can obscure origin.
Attribution requires evidence beyond country or IP address alone.
Traffic source and attacker identity are different claims.
Operational reporting should distinguish observed source addresses from attribution.
A network outage can result from attack.
A network outage can result from overload.
A network outage can result from configuration error.
A network outage can result from provider failure.
A network outage can result from routing failure.
A network outage can result from application failure.
Evidence should establish cause before conclusions are asserted.
Network monitoring should record enough evidence to reconstruct incidents.
Useful network evidence can include source and destination addresses.
Useful network evidence can include source and destination ports.
Useful network evidence can include protocol.
Useful network evidence can include connection state.
Useful network evidence can include request counts.
Useful network evidence can include response status.
Useful network evidence can include duration.
Logs should distinguish observation from inference.
"Address X belongs to attacker Y" is an attribution claim requiring additional evidence.
