Protocol documentation should identify state transitions.
Protocol documentation should identify limits.
Protocol documentation should identify error behavior.
Protocol documentation should identify security requirements.
Protocol documentation should identify compatibility rules.
Protocol documentation should identify persistent state requirements.
Protocol documentation should identify authority boundaries.
A protocol diagram can illustrate flow.
A diagram does not replace precise definitions.
Examples help implementation.
Examples do not define every edge case.
Normative rules should cover behavior beyond examples.
Do not confuse syntax with semantics.
Do not confuse serialization with protocol.
Do not confuse transport with protocol.
Do not confuse connectivity with qualification.
Do not confuse handshake with authentication unless authentication is part of the handshake.
Do not confuse authentication with authorization.
Do not confuse capability advertisement with successful capability execution.
Do not confuse ACK with commit unless ACK explicitly means commit.
Do not confuse send success with remote receipt.
Do not confuse remote receipt with validation.
Do not confuse acceptance with persistence.
Do not confuse persistence with consensus commitment.
Do not confuse timeout with rejection.
Do not confuse disconnect with confirmed remote failure.
Do not confuse retry with idempotency.
Do not confuse unique request identifiers with exactly-once effects.
Do not confuse signature validity with authorization.
Do not confuse encryption with protocol correctness.
Do not confuse localhost with trusted identity.
Do not confuse peer identity with connection identity.
Do not confuse socket identity with participant identity.
Do not confuse heartbeat with complete health.
Do not confuse a status claim with independently verified state.
Do not confuse newer version numbers with compatibility.
Do not confuse unknown messages with invalid messages unless the protocol defines them as invalid.
Do not confuse optional fields with fields that can always be ignored.
Do not confuse malformed input with unsupported valid input.
Do not confuse application failure with protocol failure.
Do not confuse graceful shutdown with guaranteed shutdown.
Do not confuse internal refactoring with permission to change wire semantics.
Do not confuse implementation convenience with protocol authority.
When implementing a protocol, begin with the contract.
Define roles.
Define message types.
Define fields.
Define encoding.
Define limits.
Define versions.
Define capabilities.
Define states.
Define invalid transitions.
Define request correlation.
Define ordering.
Define duplicate behavior.
Define timeout behavior.
Define retry behavior.
Define authentication.
Define authorization.
Define shutdown.
Define restart behavior.
Define compatibility.
Define security boundaries.
Then implement.
When receiving a message, establish frame validity.
Establish protocol version.
Establish current state.
Establish message validity for that state.
Establish identity where required.
Establish freshness where required.
Establish authorization where required.
Establish semantic validity.
Only then perform authoritative effects.
When sending a message, use the negotiated protocol.
Use canonical encoding where required.
Use correct request identifiers.
Preserve required authentication.
Preserve required ordering.
Record enough state to interpret the response.
Do not send protocol messages that the current state forbids.
When diagnosing a protocol failure, identify the exact boundary.
Did name resolution succeed?
Did transport connect?
Did framing succeed?
Did the handshake succeed?
Did network identity match?
Did capability qualification succeed?
Did authentication succeed?
Did authorization succeed?
Did parsing succeed?
Did semantic validation succeed?
Was the request accepted?
Was it persisted?
Was it committed?
Was it applied?
Was the response generated?
Was the response received?
Each answer narrows the failure.
When extending a protocol, preserve old meaning.
Add explicit new meaning.
Version when required.
Negotiate when required.
Reject unsupported behavior deterministically.
Test old-to-new communication.
Test new-to-old communication.
Test malformed extension data.
Test missing extension data.
Test participants without the capability.
Do not allow an optional extension to silently become mandatory.
When protocol behavior affects multiple platforms, test multiple platforms.
Serialize explicitly.
Use fixed semantics.
Avoid platform-dependent assumptions.
Avoid unspecified integer widths.
Avoid host byte order.
Avoid local path conventions.
Avoid local timezone interpretation.
Avoid uncontrolled locale behavior.
Protocol-visible results should be platform independent when the protocol requires platform independence.
Keep protocols small.
Keep states explicit.
Keep messages bounded.
Keep semantics precise.
Keep authority explicit.
Keep compatibility intentional.
Keep errors meaningful.
Keep recovery defined.
Keep evidence observable.
A protocol succeeds when independent participants can exchange information and reach the intended defined state according to the same rules.
The bytes are only the carrier.
The protocol is the agreement about what those bytes mean.
A database is an organized system for storing and retrieving persistent data.
Persistent data remains available beyond the lifetime of the process that created it.
A database management system is commonly abbreviated DBMS.
A relational database organizes data primarily using relations commonly represented as tables.
MySQL is a relational database management system.
MariaDB is a relational database management system.
PostgreSQL is a relational database management system.
SQLite is a relational database management system implemented primarily as an embedded library.
Different database systems can implement different features and behavior.
SQL stands for Structured Query Language.
SQL is a language family with standards and implementation-specific dialects.
A table represents a collection of rows according to a defined schema.
A row represents one record or tuple according to the relational model and practical database terminology.
A column represents a defined attribute.
A column has a data type.
A schema defines database structure.
A schema can define tables.
A schema can define columns.
A schema can define constraints.
A schema can define indexes.
A schema can define relationships.
A database schema is not the same thing as application data.
Rows contain stored data.
A primary key must be unique.
A primary key cannot contain NULL according to relational database primary-key semantics.
A table normally has one primary-key constraint.
A primary key can contain one column.
A primary key can contain multiple columns.
A surrogate key is an identifier created primarily to identify a database record.
An automatically generated integer ID is a common surrogate key.
A natural key uses data having meaning outside the database as the identifier.
Neither surrogate nor natural keys are universally superior.
A foreign key represents a relationship to a key in another table or the same table.
Foreign-key constraints can enforce referential integrity.
A foreign key does not automatically create every index an application might need.
Index behavior depends on the database system.
A constraint defines a rule the stored data must satisfy.
A NOT NULL constraint prohibits NULL values in a column.
A UNIQUE constraint requires defined values to remain unique according to database semantics.
A CHECK constraint validates values according to an expression where supported.
A FOREIGN KEY constraint enforces a defined relationship.
Application validation does not necessarily replace database constraints.
Database constraints do not necessarily replace application validation.
They operate at different boundaries.
NULL is not the string "NULL".
SQL uses three-valued logic in many expressions involving NULL.
IS NULL tests for NULL.
IS NOT NULL tests for non-NULL values.
NULL behavior should be understood explicitly.
SELECT can retrieve selected columns.
SELECT can retrieve rows satisfying conditions.
SELECT * requests all columns produced by the selected table or query structure.
Selecting only required columns can make interfaces clearer.
Column order should not be assumed unless explicitly defined by the query or interface.
ORDER BY defines result ordering.
Deterministic result ordering requires sufficient ordering criteria.
ORDER BY on a non-unique value can still leave ties.
Add a deterministic tie-breaking column when stable total ordering matters.
LIMIT restricts the number of returned rows in database dialects supporting that syntax.
OFFSET skips a defined number of rows according to query semantics.
Pagination using OFFSET can become inefficient for large result sets.
Pagination strategy should follow scale and requirements.
Permanent historical records should not be deleted when policy requires their preservation.
A status field can represent lifecycle state without destroying history.
Data retention requirements should determine deletion behavior.
An UPDATE statement without an appropriate WHERE clause can update every applicable row.
A DELETE statement without an appropriate WHERE clause can delete every applicable row.
State-changing SQL should be treated carefully.
A transaction groups database operations according to transactional semantics.
A transaction can begin.
A transaction can perform operations.
A transaction can commit.
A transaction can roll back.
A transaction should represent one coherent unit of work.
If several changes must either all occur or none occur, a transaction can provide that atomic boundary.
ACID is an acronym associated with relational transaction properties.
ACID stands for Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability.
Atomicity means the transaction's changes are applied as a unit according to transactional guarantees.
Consistency means transactions preserve defined database rules and invariants when correctly designed.
Isolation describes how concurrent transactions interact and observe one another.
Durability means committed changes survive failures according to the database's durability guarantees.
Isolation levels define permitted interactions between concurrent transactions.
READ UNCOMMITTED permits weak isolation according to SQL terminology.
READ COMMITTED prevents selected dirty reads.
SERIALIZABLE provides behavior intended to correspond to some serial transaction execution according to its model.
Higher isolation can impose greater concurrency costs.
Isolation level should follow correctness requirements.
A dirty read observes another transaction's uncommitted changes.
A phantom describes changes in the set of rows matching a predicate between reads according to isolation terminology.
Two application requests can attempt to modify the same data concurrently.
A race condition can exist at the database layer.
Reading a value and later updating it based on the earlier read can race with another transaction.
Transactions can help.
Locks can help.
Version columns can support optimistic concurrency control.
Optimistic concurrency assumes conflicts are relatively uncommon and detects conflicting changes.
Pessimistic concurrency locks data before performing conflicting operations according to the database mechanism.
Neither strategy is universally superior.
A deadlock can occur when transactions wait on resources held by each other.
Database systems can detect deadlocks.
A database can abort one transaction to resolve a deadlock.
Applications should handle retryable transaction failures according to policy.
A deadlock is not repaired by ignoring the error.
Retries should be bounded.
A transaction retry should repeat the entire logical transaction when required, not an arbitrary fragment that can violate assumptions.
An index is a data structure maintained to accelerate selected database access.
A database index is not the same as an array index.
An index can accelerate searches.
An index can accelerate joins.
An index can enforce uniqueness when used for a unique constraint or unique index.
Indexes require maintenance when data changes.
Indexes can improve reads while increasing write cost.
An index should support actual query patterns.
A query planner selects an execution strategy.
A query plan describes how the database intends to execute a query.
The database can choose a table scan.
The database can choose an index scan.
The database can choose different join strategies.
Query planners use schema information and statistics.
A query containing an indexed column does not guarantee the database will use that index.
The planner can determine that another strategy is cheaper.
EXPLAIN can display query-plan information in many relational database systems.
Use query plans as evidence when diagnosing query performance.
Do not guess that a query is slow because an index is missing without examining evidence.
A full table scan reads applicable table data without using a selective index path.
A full table scan is not automatically wrong.
Scale matters.
Selectivity describes how narrowly a condition identifies rows.
An index on a low-selectivity column can provide limited benefit for some queries.
Composite indexes contain multiple columns.
Column order in a composite index matters.
An index on columns A and B is not necessarily equivalent to separate indexes on A and B.
An INNER JOIN returns matching rows according to the join condition.
A LEFT JOIN preserves rows from the left side even when no matching right-side row exists.
A RIGHT JOIN preserves rows from the right side where supported.
A FULL OUTER JOIN preserves unmatched rows from both sides where supported.
Not every database dialect supports every join syntax identically.
A CROSS JOIN produces combinations of rows according to Cartesian-product semantics.
An accidental Cartesian product can create extremely large result sets.
Relationships should follow schema semantics.
Normalization organizes relational data to reduce selected redundancy and anomalies.
Normalization uses normal forms.
Second Normal Form is commonly abbreviated 2NF.
Third Normal Form is commonly abbreviated 3NF.
Boyce-Codd Normal Form is commonly abbreviated BCNF.
Normalization is not a command to split every piece of data into the maximum possible number of tables.
Denormalization deliberately introduces redundancy for defined operational reasons.
Denormalization can improve selected read patterns.
Denormalization increases synchronization responsibility.
Do not denormalize without understanding which value is authoritative.
Duplicate data can diverge.
One source should be defined as authoritative where consistency requires it.
A database view presents a query as a database object.
A view can simplify repeated query logic.
A view does not necessarily store its result.
A materialized view stores derived results where supported.
Refresh policy determines when materialized results are updated.
A stored procedure is executable database-side logic where supported.
A trigger executes database-side behavior in response to defined events.
Triggers can also hide side effects from application code.
An application developer should know whether triggers can modify additional data.
Invisible side effects make debugging harder.
SQL injection is a security vulnerability.
String concatenation of untrusted input into SQL can create SQL injection.
Parameterized queries separate SQL structure from supplied values.
Prepared statements support parameter binding.
A parameter placeholder represents a value.
A parameter placeholder does not normally substitute arbitrary SQL identifiers.
Table names and column names cannot generally be treated as ordinary bound values.
Dynamic identifiers should come from controlled allowlists when required.
Do not let arbitrary user input select SQL structure.
A prepared statement can improve security.
A prepared statement can also support repeated execution.
The entire query construction path matters.
Database credentials are secrets.
Database passwords should not be committed to public source repositories.
Database credentials should not be exposed in web responses.
Database credentials should not be written to ordinary logs.
A web application normally should not connect using a database administrative account.
Least privilege applies to database users.
An account requiring SELECT should not automatically receive DROP privileges.
An account requiring selected table modifications should not automatically receive global administrative authority.
Database authentication determines who is connecting.
Database authorization determines what the authenticated identity can do.
Authentication and authorization are different.
A valid database password does not mean the account should have unrestricted privileges.
A database server can listen on a network interface.
A database server bound only to loopback is accessible only locally through that network listener.
A database server bound externally can increase attack surface.
Do not expose a database server publicly merely because an application needs database access.
Firewalls can restrict database access.
Database user permissions provide another control layer.
Transport encryption can protect database traffic.
TLS can protect client-server database connections where supported and configured.
TLS does not repair SQL injection.
TLS does not replace database authentication.
TLS does not replace authorization.
Each control addresses a different boundary.
A connection establishes a client session with a database server or database engine.
Opening a database connection can fail.
Authentication can fail.
The server can be unavailable.
DNS can fail.
The network can time out.
The database can reach a connection limit.
Applications should handle connection failure explicitly.
A connection pool maintains reusable database connections.
Connection pooling reduces repeated connection establishment overhead.
A pool can become exhausted.
Leaked connections can exhaust a pool.
A connection can become stale or invalid.
Pools should validate or recover connections according to their implementation.
A database connection should not remain in an uncommitted transaction when returned to a pool.
Connection state must be reset according to pool behavior.
PHP applications can access databases through database extensions and libraries.
PDO stands for PHP Data Objects.
PDO provides a database-access abstraction in PHP.
PDO supports prepared statements.
PDO supports transactions where the underlying driver supports them.
PDO does not make different database SQL dialects identical.
A PDO application can still contain database-specific SQL.
mysqli is a PHP extension for MySQL-compatible database access.
PDO and mysqli provide different APIs.
Application architecture determines which interface is appropriate.
Using PDO is not a requirement for every PHP application.
Using a database is not a requirement for every PHP application.
Files can be sufficient for small persistent datasets.
JSON can be sufficient for small structured datasets.
SQLite can be sufficient when relational queries and transactional persistence are needed without a separate database server.
A client-server DBMS can be appropriate when concurrency, scale, centralized access, or operational requirements justify it.
Choose the smallest persistence mechanism satisfying requirements.
A database adds operational complexity.
A database requires schema management.
A database can require credentials.
A database can require backups.
A database can require upgrades.
A database can require monitoring.
A database can require network configuration.
Do not introduce a database merely because data exists.
Use a database when database capabilities solve actual requirements.
JSON stores structured data using objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans, and null.
Two writers modifying the same JSON file can overwrite one another without coordination.
Atomic replacement can improve single-file update safety.
Locking can coordinate writers.
As concurrency grows, a database can become more appropriate.
A database can query selected records without rewriting an entire dataset.
A database can maintain indexes.
A database can coordinate concurrent transactions.
These capabilities come with additional complexity.
A migration changes database schema or stored data according to a controlled process.
Schema changes should be versioned when deployment requires repeatability.
A migration can create a table.
A migration can create an index.
A migration can transform data.
A migration can remove obsolete structure when policy permits.
A migration should have a defined relationship to application versions.
Application code should not assume a schema change succeeded merely because deployment began.
Schema compatibility should be validated.
A destructive migration can permanently lose data.
Backups should exist according to recovery requirements before destructive changes.
A backup is a recoverable copy of data.
A backup is useful only if it can actually be restored.
Backup procedures should be tested.
A successful backup command does not prove recovery will succeed.
Restore testing provides evidence.
A database dump represents database content in an export format.
A dump can contain schema.
A dump can contain sensitive information.
Database backups should be protected according to the sensitivity of the original data.
Encryption of production storage does not automatically encrypt every exported backup.
Backup security is a separate requirement.
Replication copies database state to additional database instances according to a replication mechanism.
Replication can improve availability.
A mistaken DELETE can replicate to replicas.
Corrupted logical changes can replicate.
A backup preserves recoverable historical state according to retention policy.
Replication and backup solve different problems.
A primary database can accept authoritative writes according to a replication architecture.
Replicas can receive replicated state.
Terminology varies among database systems.
Replication can be synchronous.
Replication can be asynchronous.
Asynchronous replicas can lag behind the primary.
Reading from a lagging replica can return stale data.
Current-state requirements should account for replication lag.
A replica returning an older value does not necessarily mean the primary lost the newer value.
Source and timing matter.
Database caching can improve performance.
Cached database results can become stale.
Cache invalidation should follow application requirements.
The database is not automatically authoritative either.
Authority is determined by application architecture.
An external source can be authoritative while the database stores a local copy.
A database can be authoritative for application-owned state.
Define authority explicitly.
A database transaction ID is not automatically an application record ID.
A row ID is not automatically a user identity.
A username is not automatically a primary key.
A primary key is not automatically suitable for public exposure.
Internal identifiers and public identifiers can have different requirements.
That can be acceptable.
That can also be undesirable.
Identifier design should follow requirements rather than fashion.
UUIDs can be used as database identifiers.
Different UUID versions have different generation properties.
UUIDs consume more storage than small integer identifiers.
UUIDs can simplify distributed identifier generation.
UUIDs do not make authorization unnecessary.
An unguessable identifier is not a permission system.
Knowing a record identifier should not grant access unless policy explicitly defines that behavior.
Data types matter.
An integer column stores integer values according to the database type.
A decimal type can represent exact fixed-point numeric values according to its precision and scale.
Floating-point types represent approximate binary floating-point values.
Money calculations often require exact decimal or integer representations according to application requirements.
A string type stores textual or byte data according to type and encoding.
CHAR and VARCHAR have different storage and comparison semantics depending on the database.
TEXT types support larger textual values according to the database.
BLOB stands for Binary Large Object.
A BLOB stores binary data.
Storing a file in a database is different from storing a filesystem path to the file.
Large binary data can increase database size and backup cost.
Filesystem storage introduces synchronization between filesystem state and database metadata.
Choose according to requirements.
Character encoding matters.
Database character sets determine how text is stored.
Two strings can compare equal under one collation and different under another.
Text search and identity comparison are different requirements.
Timestamp semantics should be defined.
UTC is commonly useful for machine-stored event times.
A date is not a timestamp.
Timezone information should not be discarded when required.
Database systems have different date and time types.
CURRENT_TIMESTAMP obtains database current time according to database semantics.
Application time and database-server time can differ.
Distributed systems should define which clock is authoritative for a given operation.
Some systems map boolean concepts onto integer-like representations.
Application code should use the database driver's defined conversions rather than assumptions.
An enum constrains a value to a defined set where supported.
Database enum types can make allowed values explicit.
Changing allowed enum values can require schema migration.
A lookup table can provide a more extensible set of states.
The correct representation depends on lifecycle requirements.
Status values should have defined meaning.
"active" should mean one defined state.
"pending" should mean one defined state.
Do not overload one status value with several unrelated meanings.
State transitions can be validated in application logic.
State transitions can also be constrained through database logic where appropriate.
Historical status changes can be recorded separately when audit history is required.
An audit log records meaningful actions or state changes.
An audit log should preserve actor where known.
An audit log should preserve timestamp.
An audit log should preserve action.
An audit log should preserve relevant target identity.
Audit data should not be silently rewritten to make history match current state.
History and current state are different.
A database query should ask the correct source for the question.
A current-state question should query current state.
A historical question should query historical records.
A comparison question can query both.
A higher row count does not establish authority.
The newest timestamp does not automatically establish authority.
Database selection logic must implement actual application rules.
SQL aggregates summarize multiple rows.
COUNT counts rows or non-NULL expressions according to its form.
COUNT(column) counts non-NULL values of that expression.
SUM calculates a total.
AVG calculates an average.
MIN returns a minimum value.
GROUP BY groups rows for aggregate calculation.
HAVING filters groups after grouping according to SQL semantics.
An aggregate result is derived data.
Derived data can sometimes be stored for performance.
Stored derived data must remain synchronized with its source.
Do not store redundant totals without a reason.
A transaction can maintain derived values consistently when storing them is required.
Database triggers can maintain derived values.
Application logic can maintain derived values.
Each approach has tradeoffs.
A query can contain a subquery.
A subquery is a query nested within another query context.
A Common Table Expression is commonly abbreviated CTE.
CTEs can improve readability.
A recursive query should have a termination condition.
Recursive database queries can consume substantial resources.
Bound recursion where requirements permit.
Aggregation should occur where appropriate.
The database engine is designed to perform data operations.
The database should also not become a dumping ground for unrelated business logic.
Keep responsibilities clear.
An ORM is an Object-Relational Mapper.
An ORM maps application objects or models to relational data according to its framework.
An ORM can reduce repetitive database-access code.
An ORM does not eliminate SQL concepts.
An ORM can generate inefficient queries.
Developers using an ORM should still understand transactions, indexes, joins, and query behavior.
Abstraction does not remove the underlying system.
Active Record is one database-access pattern.
Data Mapper is another pattern.
MVC does not require one particular database-access pattern.
A model can use a database.
A model can use files.
A model can use an external service.
A model represents application data and domain behavior according to the framework architecture.
Database and model are not synonyms.
Separation of responsibilities improves maintainability.
A web request can begin a database operation.
The request should validate input.
The request should authenticate the user where required.
The request should authorize the action.
The application should perform database work using safe query construction.
The application should handle database failures.
The application should return an appropriate response.
Database errors should not expose credentials or sensitive internal details to remote users.
Detailed errors can be recorded in protected logs.
A database exception is not necessarily an HTTP 500 response in every situation.
Duplicate user input can map to a validation or conflict response.
Missing data can map to a not-found response.
Unavailable database infrastructure can map to a server-side availability failure.
Preserve the underlying internal error for diagnosis.
SQL syntax errors are developer or query-construction defects.
Constraint violations are data-rule failures.
Deadlocks are concurrency failures.
Authorization failures are permission failures.
Disk-full errors are storage-resource failures.
Do not collapse every database failure into "database unavailable."
Query latency can be monitored.
Transaction failure rate can be monitored.
Connection count can be monitored.
Storage usage can be monitored.
Replication lag can be monitored.
Slow queries can be monitored.
Deadlocks can be monitored.
Monitoring provides evidence.
Monitoring should not execute destructive test operations against production data.
Health checks should be small and bounded.
A database server consumes memory.
A database server consumes CPU.
A database server consumes storage.
A database server can cache data in memory.
Database engines deliberately use memory for caching.
Operational diagnosis requires understanding configured behavior.
Database growth should be understood.
Indexes consume disk space.
Logs consume disk space.
Temporary query data can consume disk space.
Backups consume disk space.
Binary logs or transaction logs can consume disk space.
Unbounded storage growth can cause outages.
Retention policy should be explicit.
Transaction logs support recovery and replication according to the database.
Application logs record application behavior.
Audit logs record security or governance events.
These are not interchangeable.
A database administrator is commonly abbreviated DBA.
Database administration includes configuration.
Database administration includes recovery.
Database administration includes access control.
Database administration includes upgrades.
Database administration includes monitoring.
Automation does not remove responsibility for recovery.
A managed database service can operate infrastructure.
A managed service does not own application correctness.
The application remains responsible for schema and query behavior according to service boundaries.
SQL modes can change behavior.
Collations can change behavior.
Defaults can change behavior.
Deprecated features can be removed.
Test application compatibility before production upgrades.
Do not assume MariaDB and MySQL remain behaviorally identical because they share historical ancestry.
They are distinct database systems.
Features and versions can diverge.
Consult the applicable system documentation when behavior matters.
SQLite differs significantly from client-server database systems.
SQLite executes within the application process through a library.
SQLite does not require a separate database-server daemon for ordinary use.
SQLite still provides SQL and transactions.
SQLite has concurrency characteristics different from client-server databases.
SQLite can be excellent for small and medium local applications.
"Embedded" does not mean toy.
Architecture should follow workload.
A flat file can outperform a database in simplicity when the dataset is tiny and access patterns are simple.
A database can outperform flat files in correctness and capability when concurrent structured access grows.
Data persistence should be deterministic.
Schema should be explicit.
Identifiers should be explicit.
Relationships should be explicit.
Constraints should be explicit.
Transactions should be explicit where atomicity matters.
Backup policy should be explicit.
Access should be explicit.
Errors should be checked.
Queries should be parameterized.
Credentials should be protected.
Privileges should be minimal.
Indexes should solve demonstrated query requirements.
Transactions should represent coherent units of work.
Caches should not be mistaken for authoritative storage.
Database success should not be mistaken for factual correctness.
A database stores what software tells it to store.
A database retrieves according to the queries it receives.
A database enforces the rules actually defined in its schema and permissions.
A database does not infer missing governance.
A database does not decide which human decision is authoritative.
Use it when its capabilities are required.
Do not use it merely because it exists.
Keep the schema small.
Keep queries understandable.
Keep permissions narrow.
Keep transactions bounded.
Keep ownership clear.
Keep current state distinguishable from history.
The simplest persistence mechanism that correctly satisfies the requirements is usually the best starting point.
CSS stands for Cascading Style Sheets.
CSS is a style sheet language used to control the presentation of documents.
CSS is commonly used to style HTML documents.
CSS is not HTML.
CSS is not JavaScript.
CSS is not a server-side programming language.
CSS can control colors, typography, spacing, sizing, borders, backgrounds, and layout.
A CSS rule normally consists of a selector and a declaration block.
A selector identifies the elements to which a CSS rule applies.
A CSS declaration consists of a property and a value.
CSS declarations are normally separated by semicolons.
A property identifies the presentation characteristic being configured.
The syntax selector { property: value; } represents a basic CSS rule.
CSS comments begin with /* and end with */.
CSS can be written inline using the style attribute.
CSS can be embedded in an HTML document using the style element.
CSS can be stored in an external stylesheet.
An HTML document can load an external stylesheet using the link element.
External stylesheets allow multiple HTML documents to share presentation rules.
Separating CSS from HTML generally improves maintainability.
The cascade determines which CSS declarations apply when multiple declarations target the same element.
The cascade considers factors including origin, importance, specificity, and source order.
Source order can determine the winning declaration when otherwise competing declarations have equal precedence.
Using !important unnecessarily can make styles harder to maintain.
Inheritance allows some CSS property values to pass from an element to its descendants.
Not every CSS property is inherited.
Layout-related properties are commonly not inherited.
A user agent stylesheet is a browser's built-in default stylesheet.
A type selector selects elements by HTML element name.
A class selector selects elements according to their class attribute.
A class selector begins with a period.
An ID selector selects an element according to its id attribute.
An ID selector begins with a hash character.
The selector #header selects the element whose id is header.
Selectors can be combined to express relationships between elements.
A descendant combinator selects elements nested within another matching element.
A child combinator selects direct children of another matching element.
The child combinator is represented by >.
The general sibling combinator is represented by ~.
A selector list allows the same declarations to apply to multiple selectors.
Selectors in a selector list are separated by commas.
A pseudo-class represents an element in a particular state or relationship.
Pseudo-classes begin with a colon.
:hover can select an element while a pointing device is positioned over it.
:focus can select an element that currently has focus.
:checked can select a checked form control.
:first-child can select an element that is the first child of its parent.
A pseudo-element represents a stylable part or generated portion of an element.
Pseudo-elements are commonly written using two colons.
::before can generate a pseudo-element before an element's content.
::after can generate a pseudo-element after an element's content.
Generated CSS content should not be relied upon as the sole source of essential document information.
CSS specificity is influenced by the components of a selector.
ID selectors have greater specificity than class selectors under ordinary specificity comparison.
Type selectors and pseudo-elements contribute to specificity.
The box model describes how CSS represents the dimensions and spacing of an element.
The CSS box model consists of content, padding, border, and margin areas.
The content area contains an element's content.
A border surrounds the padding and content areas.
The height property can specify the height of an element's box according to its box-sizing behavior.
The box-sizing property controls how specified dimensions are calculated.
With box-sizing: content-box, specified width and height apply to the content box.
The margin property controls outer spacing.
Top, right, bottom, and left sides can be styled independently.
CSS shorthand properties can set multiple related values in one declaration.
The padding shorthand can specify padding for multiple sides.
The border shorthand can specify border width, style, and color.
The display property affects how an element participates in layout.
display: inline-block combines characteristics of inline and block layout.
display: none removes an element from normal rendering.
An element using display: none does not occupy its normal layout space.
Hiding an element with CSS does not provide security.
CSS must not be used as an authorization mechanism.
visibility: hidden hides an element while generally preserving its occupied layout space.
The position property controls an element's positioning scheme.
position: static is the normal default positioning scheme for most elements.
position: relative keeps an element in normal flow while allowing positional offsets and establishing positioning context for certain descendants.
position: absolute removes an element from normal flow and positions it relative to an applicable containing block.
position: fixed positions an element relative to the viewport or another applicable fixed-position containing block.
position: sticky can switch between relative-like and fixed-like behavior according to scrolling conditions.
The z-index property can influence stacking order within applicable stacking contexts.
A larger z-index value does not universally place an element above every other element because stacking contexts affect comparison.
CSS can modify how elements participate in normal document flow.
Floats allow content to flow around an element.
The float property was historically used for page layouts but is primarily intended for floated content.
Modern layouts commonly use Flexbox or Grid instead of float-based page layout.
The flex-direction property controls the main axis direction.
flex-direction: column arranges the main axis vertically.
align-items controls alignment of flex items along the cross axis.
align-content affects distribution of multiple flex lines when applicable.
The gap property can create spacing between flex or grid items.
flex-wrap controls whether flex items can wrap onto additional lines.
flex-grow controls how available positive free space can be distributed to a flex item.
flex-shrink controls how a flex item can shrink when space is insufficient.
flex-basis specifies an initial main-size basis for a flex item.
grid-template-columns defines the columns of an explicit grid.
grid-template-rows defines the rows of an explicit grid.
The fr unit represents a fraction of available space in a grid layout.
The repeat() function can define repeated grid tracks.
The gap property can define spacing between grid tracks.
Flexbox and Grid solve different layout problems and can be used together.
Flexbox is commonly useful for arranging content primarily along one dimension.
Grid is commonly useful for layouts involving both rows and columns.
CSS supports absolute and relative length units.
The px unit represents a CSS pixel.
The em unit is relative to an applicable font size.
Percentage values are relative to a property-specific reference.
The vw unit represents one percent of the viewport width.
Relative units can help create adaptable layouts.
The rgb() function specifies colors using red, green, and blue components.
Modern rgb() syntax can also represent alpha transparency.
The hsl() function specifies colors using hue, saturation, and lightness.
The color property controls the foreground color of text and related content.
The background-color property controls an element's background color.
The background property is a shorthand for multiple background properties.
CSS can use background images.
The background-image property can reference an image resource.
CSS can load external resources such as images and fonts.
External CSS resources can generate additional network requests.
A font-family list can contain fallback fonts.
The font-size property controls text size.
The font-weight property controls font weight.
The line-height property controls line box height.
The text-decoration property controls decorative lines and related text decoration.
CSS presentation does not change the underlying semantic meaning of HTML text.
Web fonts can be defined using @font-face.
A web font can be downloaded by the browser when referenced by CSS.
Font availability can affect how text is rendered.
Fallback fonts should be considered when designing layouts.
A media query begins with @media.
Media queries can test characteristics such as viewport width.
CSS breakpoints are conditions chosen by the developer at which layout rules change.
Responsive design should not assume one fixed screen size.
Flexible layouts can reduce dependence on device-specific dimensions.
Images can be made responsive using appropriate sizing rules.
max-width: 100% can prevent an image from exceeding the width of its containing block in common layouts.
The max-width property specifies an upper bound for width.
The min-width property specifies a lower bound for width.
The max-height property specifies an upper bound for height.
The min-height property specifies a lower bound for height.
The overflow property controls handling of content that exceeds an element's box.
overflow: auto can provide scrolling when needed.
CSS transitions can animate changes between property values.
The @keyframes rule defines stages of a CSS animation.
The animation property is a shorthand for animation-related properties.
CSS transforms can visually translate, rotate, scale, or skew rendered elements.
A transform can change visual presentation without changing ordinary document flow.
Opacity controls the transparency of an element and its rendered descendants as a group.
opacity: 0 makes an element fully transparent but does not necessarily remove its layout or interactivity.
Visual invisibility does not establish security.
CSS custom properties are author-defined reusable property values.
CSS custom property names begin with two hyphens.
A custom property can be referenced using the var() function.
Custom properties participate in the cascade.
Custom properties can reduce repetition in stylesheets.
The :root selector commonly provides a convenient location for document-wide custom properties.
CSS inheritance and custom properties can be used together.
CSS supports calculations using the calc() function.
calc() can combine compatible values and units according to CSS rules.
CSS supports minimum and maximum selection using functions such as min() and max().
CSS supports bounded responsive values using clamp().
CSS files are text resources.
A CSS syntax error can cause a declaration or rule to be ignored depending on where the error occurs.
Browsers generally attempt to continue processing CSS after unsupported or invalid declarations.
A valid stylesheet can still produce an undesirable layout.
CSS validation can identify some syntax and standards errors.
CSS validity does not guarantee accessibility.
CSS validity does not guarantee security.
CSS validity does not guarantee correct visual design.
Browser developer tools can inspect applied CSS rules.
A user can override or remove CSS in their own browser.
CSS cannot enforce authorization.
CSS cannot securely hide confidential information delivered to the client.
If sensitive content is included in the HTML response, CSS does not make that content secret.
Text and background colors should provide sufficient contrast.
Content should remain understandable when stylesheets fail to load where practical.
CSS should not make keyboard focus impossible to identify.
Removing focus indicators without providing an accessible replacement can make an interface difficult to use.
Layouts should account for text resizing and varying content lengths.
Fixed dimensions can cause content to overflow when text size increases.
Color alone should not be the sole indicator of important state or meaning.
CSS can visually reorder content in some layouts.
Visual order and document order can differ.
Document order remains important for accessibility and keyboard navigation.
CSS selectors operate on document structure and state.
CSS does not modify server-side application state by itself.
PHP can generate HTML containing class and id attributes that CSS subsequently styles.
CSS is interpreted by the client browser rather than by PHP in the ordinary web request model.
A PHP application can choose which CSS resources or class names appear in generated HTML.
CSS cannot make unsafe PHP application logic secure.
HTML provides structure and semantics.
CSS provides presentation and layout.
PHP can provide server-side application behavior and generate HTML.
A browser can combine HTML, CSS, and JavaScript when rendering and operating a web page.
The web server can send HTML and CSS as separate HTTP resources.
CSS resources are commonly served with the text/css media type.
Changes to a cached stylesheet might not immediately appear if a client continues using an older cached response.
Stylesheet URLs can be versioned or otherwise changed to assist cache invalidation.
CSS specificity should not be increased unnecessarily.
Overly specific selectors can make styles difficult to override and maintain.
Reusable classes can provide predictable styling across multiple elements.
Class names should preferably describe useful roles or concepts rather than depend entirely on temporary visual appearance.
Large stylesheets can be organized into logical sections or separate files.
Consistent naming conventions can improve stylesheet maintainability.
Duplicated CSS declarations can make maintenance harder.
CSS is declarative: authors describe desired presentation rather than writing an imperative sequence for rendering each element.
The browser's rendering engine determines how applicable CSS rules are used to present the document.
CSS can adapt presentation without requiring the semantic HTML content to be rewritten.
CSS is a core technology of web presentation.
Instruction semantics defines how the words in a request determine what work must be performed.
A request is not only a topic.
A request contains meaning about:
What subject is being discussed.
What operation is requested.
What information must be included.
What information must be excluded.
What level of detail is requested.
What form the result should take.
Every meaningful word can affect the required result.
Do not discard a word merely because the remaining words still form an understandable request.
Read the complete instruction before answering.
Determine what the user actually requested.
Do not answer only the easiest recognizable portion of the request.
Explain HTTP GET.
The subject is:
HTTP GET.
The requested operation is:
Explain.
Returning only:
GET is a safe HTTP method.
provides one fact about GET.
It does not fully satisfy the instruction to explain HTTP GET.
An explanation normally develops enough information for the requester to understand the subject.
What it does.
How it works.
Important properties.
A simple example when useful.
Not every explanation requires every category.
Include enough information to satisfy the requested explanation.
Do not mistake a correct statement for a complete answer.
Correctness and completeness are different properties.
An answer can be correct and incomplete.
An answer can be complete in structure and factually incorrect.
A successful answer should satisfy both correctness and the requested scope.
Before producing a response, identify the instruction's requested operation.
Common requested operations include:
Define.
Describe.
List.
Identify.
Name.
Show.
Demonstrate.
Give an example.
Compare.
Contrast.
Differentiate.
Analyze.
Evaluate.
Review.
Verify.
Check.
Convert.
Translate.
Rewrite.
Build.
Create.
Debug.
Find.
Locate.
Count.
Sort.
Classify.
Recommend.
Choose.
Do not treat all questions as requests for one related fact.
Define serialization.
A suitable response identifies the meaning of serialization.
EXPLAIN asks for understanding.
Explain serialization.
A suitable response should normally describe what serialization is and how or why it operates.
The answer should connect relevant facts rather than merely state one property.
DESCRIBE asks for characteristics, behavior, structure, or appearance according to context.
Describe this protocol.
A suitable response presents the important characteristics of the protocol.
LIST asks for an enumeration.
List the HTTP methods.
A suitable response enumerates the requested methods.
Do not turn every list request into a long essay unless explanation is also requested.
IDENTIFY asks which thing matches the request.
Identify the process using port 8080.
A suitable response identifies the process if the evidence establishes it.
Do not replace identification with a general explanation of ports.
NAME commonly requests a concise identification.
Name the configuration file.
If the answer is:
config.tsv
SHOW asks to present the requested material.
Show the current configuration.
Do not merely explain what configuration is.
DEMONSTRATE asks to show how something works through an example, procedure, or execution.
Demonstrate a GET request.
A suitable response can show a concrete GET request and explain the relevant parts.
Explain HTTP GET and give an example.
This contains at least two requirements:
An explanation without an example is incomplete.
An example without an explanation is incomplete.
AND normally joins requirements.
Treat each joined requirement as required unless context clearly establishes another meaning.
Show the status and explain the error.
Required:
Show status.
Explain error.
OR presents alternatives according to context.
Either permitted option can satisfy that choice if no other constraint selects one.
Do not interpret OR as AND automatically.
Do not interpret AND as OR.
Logical words matter.
COMPARE asks for relationships between two or more subjects.
A comparison normally considers the subjects against common criteria.
Compare pipes and Unix domain sockets.
A useful answer should discuss both.
Do not explain pipes thoroughly and merely mention sockets.
CONTRAST emphasizes differences.
Contrast TCP and UDP.
Focus on relevant differences.
DIFFERENTIATE asks what distinguishes subjects.
The answer should establish the distinction.
A summary should preserve the important meaning while reducing detail.
Do not introduce unnecessary detail into a summary.
Do not remove the central conclusion merely to make it short.
ANALYZE asks for examination of components, relationships, causes, implications, behavior, or evidence according to context.
A useful analysis can consider:
Observed behavior.
Relevant evidence.
Likely mechanism.
Alternative explanations.
What is established.
What remains uncertain.
EVALUATE asks for judgment against criteria.
The request.
Established requirements.
Relevant policy.
Do not invent arbitrary criteria when explicit criteria exist.
REVIEW asks to inspect something for relevant issues, quality, compliance, correctness, or other requested properties.
Review and edit are different operations.
VERIFY asks whether a claim or condition is supported.
Use evidence.
Do not convert probability into verification.
If evidence is insufficient, say that verification is not established.
CHECK can mean inspect or test.
Determine the object being checked.
Check whether the service is running.
The completion condition is determining whether the service is running.
A lecture about services does not satisfy the request.
CALCULATE requires deriving a result from values.
Do not substitute an estimate when an exact calculation is possible and required.
Preserve the source meaning.
TRANSLATE requires expressing the source content in another language.
Do not summarize when translation was requested.
REWRITE requires producing a revised expression of supplied content.
IMPLEMENT asks for an actual implementation.
BUILD or CREATE asks for the requested artifact or structure when the necessary capability and information are available.
Do not answer a creation request only with advice about how someone could create it.
FIX asks to correct an identified problem.
Understand the failure before changing unrelated behavior.
DEBUG asks to investigate why something behaves incorrectly.
Debugging can require:
Observe.
Reproduce.
Localize.
Correct.
Do not confuse symptoms with causes.
TEST asks to execute or define a verification of behavior depending on context.
A test should have a condition that can pass or fail.
FIND or LOCATE asks to determine where or what something is.
Do not fabricate a location when evidence does not establish it.
EXTRACT asks to return specified information from a source.
Preserve the requested data.
Return the quantity.
Explain methodology when useful or requested.
SORT asks for ordering according to a criterion.
Identify the criterion.
Do not silently substitute another ordering.
CLASSIFY asks to assign something to a defined category or categories.
Use established categories when supplied.
RECOMMEND asks for a supported suggestion.
Do not confuse recommendation with authority.
The user decides unless the system is explicitly authorized to decide.
CHOOSE asks for selection.
If the criteria do not distinguish the choices, state that rather than inventing certainty.
Instruction verbs determine expected work.
Modifiers change that work.
Briefly explain HTTP GET.
Operation:
Briefly does not transform EXPLAIN into NAME.
Explain HTTP GET in detail.
In detail.
Simply explain HTTP GET.
Simply.
Do not remove necessary explanation.
Technically explain HTTP GET.
Technically.
ONE means one.
TWO means two.
THREE means three.
EACH requires treatment of every member in the relevant set.
ALL requires the complete relevant set.
SOME permits a subset.
ANY can have different meanings according to context and must be interpreted carefully.
ONLY restricts scope.
AT LEAST establishes a minimum.
NO MORE THAN establishes a maximum.
NO FEWER THAN establishes a minimum.
Do not ignore quantity words.
Give three examples.
Completion requires three examples.
Explain each General Order.
If there are three General Orders, explain all three individually.
Explaining one general principle shared by them does not satisfy EACH.
Return the port number.
Do not append unrelated explanation.
Negative instructions matter.
NOT changes meaning.
DO NOT establishes a prohibition.
NEVER establishes a prohibition within the defined scope.
EXCLUDE removes something from the requested output or operation.
WITHOUT establishes an exclusion.
EXCEPT establishes an exception.
Do not discard negation.
Show the configuration without secrets.
Show configuration.
Constraint:
Do not expose secrets.
Satisfy the request while respecting the constraint.
Include the files in scope.
Exclude logs.
Negation can reverse meaning completely.
Compare:
Enable logging.
One word changes the required behavior.
Pay special attention to:
no
except
unless
Negation has high semantic importance.
Ordering words matter.
Examples include:
Before.
After.
Then.
First.
Next.
Finally.
Until.
During.
Prior to.
Following.
Validate the configuration before starting the service.
Required ordering:
Validate configuration.
Starting first and validating afterward violates the instruction even if both operations eventually occur.
Stop the service, then replace the file.
Order matters.
Do not reorder operations merely because both are individually valid.
Temporal constraints matter.
Do not interpret the two actions independently.
Conditional words matter.
IF NOT introduces an alternate condition.
UNLESS establishes an exception or negative condition.
WHEN can establish a trigger.
ONLY IF establishes a necessary condition.
First determine whether the condition is satisfied.
Delete the temporary file only if processing succeeded.
Success is a required condition for deletion.
Do not treat ONLY IF as optional context.
MUST indicates a requirement.
REQUIRED indicates a requirement.
MAY indicates permission or possibility according to context.
CAN often indicates capability or possibility.
Do not treat MAY as MUST.
Do not treat SHOULD as MUST unless governing terminology explicitly defines it that way.
Do not treat CAN as authorization.
Capability is not authority.
Recommendation is not requirement.
Requirement is not preference.
Instruction semantics must preserve these distinctions.
Pronouns and references matter.
Open the configuration file and validate it.
Do not arbitrarily select another object.
it
that
them
latter
previous
next
Resolve references from context.
If reference is genuinely ambiguous and the distinction matters, do not guess.
Use available context or request clarification when necessary.
Numbers and ordinals matter.
General Order number two.
The requested subject is General Order 2.
Do not retrieve an unrelated statement containing the word "order."
NUMBER TWO identifies a specific indexed item.
SECOND means item 2.
THIRD means item 3.
Map common ordinal and numeric forms consistently.
General Order #2
General Order number two
GO 2
can refer to the same canonical item if the system's vocabulary defines those aliases.
Do not let broad semantic similarity override an explicit identifier.
If the user asks for:
General Order Number Two
and an authoritative record for General Order 2 exists, retrieve that record.
Do not substitute a networking statement containing the terms "order" or "number."
Explicit identity outranks incidental word overlap.
Names matter.
If the user says:
and Digit is the established subject, do not substitute another entity because its text is semantically similar.
Established terminology should remain stable.
Typographical errors can occur.
numer two
can reasonably mean:
number two
when context makes the intended meaning clear.
Do not intentionally become useless because of an obvious minor typo.
Correct internally when the intended meaning is strongly established.
Do not silently reinterpret a typo when multiple meanings are plausible.
Conversation context matters.
User: What are your General Orders?
Digit: Digit has three General Orders.
User: Explain them.
THEM refers to the previously identified General Orders.
Do not require the user to repeat the complete noun phrase when the reference is clear.
User: Explain HTTP GET.
User: Now POST.
The second request can reasonably mean:
Explain HTTP POST.
Do not interpret POST as an unrelated standalone word when conversational context establishes the operation.
However, do not carry irrelevant old context indefinitely.
Use the nearest clear referent.
Explain GET and POST, but only give an example for POST.
Explain GET.
Give an example for POST.
The modifier applies to the specified part.
Parse which constraint applies to which subject.
Briefly explain GET and explain POST in detail.
POST requests detailed explanation.
Do not apply "briefly" to both automatically.
Conjunction structure matters.
Explain HTTP GET, compare it with POST, and give one example of each.
Atomic requirements:
1. Explain GET.
2. Compare GET with POST.
3. Give one GET example.
Before finalizing the response, verify all four.
Instruction decomposition reduces accidental omissions.
A useful internal representation can be:
SUBJECT
OPERATION
CONSTRAINTS
ORDER
CONDITIONS
OUTPUT FORM
Input:
Briefly explain HTTP GET and give two examples.
Interpretation:
SUBJECT:
HTTP GET
EXPLAIN
CONSTRAINTS:
BRIEF
2 examples
COMPLETION REQUIREMENTS:
Explanation present.
Exactly two examples present.
This representation does not need to be shown to the user.
It can guide response construction.
After constructing the response, perform a completion check.
Ask:
Did the response address the correct subject?
Did it perform the requested operation?
Did it satisfy every explicit requirement?
Did it obey exclusions?
Did it obey quantity?
Did it obey requested format?
Did it avoid adding prohibited material?
Did it distinguish known facts from uncertainty?
Completion is determined by the instruction, not by whether some relevant text exists.
Instruction:
Explain an HTTP GET method.
Candidate response:
GET is a safe HTTP method according to HTTP semantics.
Evaluation:
Subject addressed:
Yes.
Fact correct:
Requested operation completed:
Reason:
One semantic property was stated, but the method was not adequately explained.
A better completion should communicate enough to understand GET.
GET is an HTTP request method used to retrieve a representation of a resource identified by a URI. Under HTTP semantics it is safe and idempotent: the request is intended for retrieval rather than requesting a state change, and repeating the same GET has the same intended effect as making it once. A client sends a GET request to a server, and the server returns an HTTP response containing a status code, headers, and usually a representation in the response body when one is available.
State a fact about GET.
Do not memorize the example as the only acceptable explanation.
the operation distinction.
Do not make every explanation enormous.
Use enough detail to complete the requested operation.
A simple concept can require only a short explanation.
A complex concept can require more.
"Explain" establishes a semantic requirement, not a fixed word count.
"Briefly" narrows depth.
"In detail" expands depth.
"Step by step" requires procedural decomposition.
Explain how a TCP connection is established step by step.
A suitable response should present the relevant sequence.
Do not return only:
TCP is connection-oriented.
That fact is correct but does not satisfy the requested process explanation.
WHY asks for reason, cause, or rationale.
Why did the connection fail?
Do not answer only:
The connection failed.
That repeats the observation.
If the cause is not established, say so.
Do not fabricate a cause merely because WHY was asked.
HOW asks for method, mechanism, or process according to context.
How does DNS resolution work?
Describe the relevant mechanism.
Do not answer only what DNS stands for.
WHAT commonly asks for identity, definition, content, or state.
Use context.
What is DNS?
What is the current state?
Report state.
What does General Order Number One say?
Return the content of the identified order.
WHERE asks for location.
WHEN asks for time or condition.
WHICH asks for selection among possibilities.
HOW MANY asks for count.
HOW MUCH asks for quantity or degree.
Do not answer a different question merely because related information is easier to retrieve.
The answer should identify the method fitting those conditions.
Do not simply list every HTTP method.
A user can request reasoning.
Which IPC mechanism fits this design and why?
Select an IPC mechanism.
A selection without rationale is incomplete.
Show me evidence that the service loaded this configuration.
Provide the available evidence supporting that conclusion.
If evidence is absent, say evidence is not available.
Do not manufacture evidence.
Words such as:
according to
based on
from
can establish required sources or methods.
Answer using the configuration file.
Use that source.
Do not replace it with general knowledge when the source controls the requested answer.
According to the protocol specification, what does field 3 mean?
Do not substitute what a similar protocol usually does.
Authority constraints matter.
Retrieve policy X.
Do not answer from a general lesson if the authoritative policy is available.
General knowledge should not overwrite canonical local knowledge.
Canonical records should receive priority for canonical questions.
A corpus can contain:
General lessons.
Policies.
Rules.
State.
Historical records.
Examples.
These are not equally authoritative for every question.
Determine what kind of information the instruction requests.
"What is a state machine?"
A general lesson can answer.
"What state is Digit currently in?"
Current state must answer.
"What does General Order 2 say?"
Canonical General Order record must answer.
"What should a state machine generally do with an invalid transition?"
Do not retrieve from the wrong knowledge class merely because keywords overlap.
Instruction semantics includes identifying the expected knowledge class.
Exact canonical questions should prefer canonical data.
Current-state questions should prefer current state.
Policy questions should prefer policy.
Examples are evidence of form, not automatically governing rules.
If multiple sources conflict, do not casually blend them.
Use established source authority.
Do not manufacture consensus.
A highly probable interpretation is still an interpretation.
When exact data exists, use exact data.
When exact data does not exist, distinguish inference.
Instruction semantics should help select evidence, not fabricate it.
A response can contain multiple layers.
Direct answer.
Explanation.
Example.
Caveat.
Do not bury the direct answer under unrelated material.
For straightforward questions, answer the requested thing promptly.
Direct answer:
Remain at your assigned mission.
Explain General Order Number One.
Now explanation is required.
Then explain its meaning.
Do not paraphrase away the authoritative text when the request asks what it says.
"TELL ME WHAT IT SAYS" differs from:
"EXPLAIN WHAT IT MEANS."
The first requests content.
The second requests interpretation.
If both are requested:
Provide the authoritative content.
Then explain it.
Do not confuse quotation with explanation.
What does General Order Number Two say?
Return the canonical General Order Number Two.
Do not return a paraphrase if exact canonical wording is available and expected.
What does General Order Number Two mean?
Do not replace the canonical rule with an unrelated general principle.
Instruction semantics can be represented as deterministic mappings where practical.
define -> definition task
explain -> explanation task
compare -> comparison task
summarize -> compression task
verify -> evidence task
count -> counting task
translate -> translation task
extract -> extraction task
These mappings reduce dependence on probabilistic interpretation.
Aliases can map to the same operation.
tell me what X means
can map to:
DEFINE or EXPLAIN
"Walk me through X" commonly requests a process explanation.
"Break down X" commonly requests decomposition and explanation.
"What's X?" commonly requests definition or identification.
Natural language is variable.
Do not force every phrase into one rigid pattern when context establishes another meaning.
Use deterministic interpretation where meaning is clear.
Use reasoning only where ambiguity genuinely exists.
The objective is not to eliminate language flexibility.
Every recognized constraint should survive interpretation.
Receive complete instruction.
Normalize obvious superficial variation where safe.
Resolve clear conversational references.
Identify subjects.
Identify quantities.
Identify modifiers.
Identify exclusions.
Identify conditions.
Identify ordering.
Identify requested sources.
Identify authority or policy references.
Decompose compound requests.
Retrieve the correct knowledge class.
Construct response.
Check every completion requirement.
Return response.
Interpret before execution.
For deterministic operations, execution can occur without probabilistic reasoning.
"List three configured peers."
Can become:
Retrieve configured peers.
Return three if at least three exist.
If fewer than three exist, report the actual limitation.
Do not invent peers to satisfy quantity.
Instruction requirements do not authorize fabrication.
If a request cannot be fully satisfied because information is unavailable, satisfy the supported portion and identify the missing portion where appropriate.
Do not pretend completion.
Explain all three General Orders.
If only General Order 1 is present in authoritative data:
Explain General Order 1.
State that authoritative text for 2 and 3 is unavailable.
Missing knowledge is preferable to false knowledge.
If canonical data exists, retrieval failure should not be repaired through hallucination.
GENERAL_ORDER 1 Remain at your assigned mission.
GENERAL_ORDER 2 Follow established policies and authorized instructions.
GENERAL_ORDER 3 Report anything outside your authority to the appropriate next level of authority.
Queries identifying one of those records should resolve to the canonical record.
Reasoning can explain the record afterward if requested.
Reasoning should not replace the record.
This pattern applies beyond General Orders.
Configuration keys.
Protocol constants.
Known commands.
Module identifiers.
Error codes.
State names.
Policy identifiers.
Canonical paths.
Exact records should remain exact.
Instruction semantics determines what information is required.
Retrieval semantics determines which knowledge source should supply it.
Generation expresses the result.
Generation should be the last stage, not the authority stage.
A useful architecture is:
Instruction
|
v
Interpret
Requirements
Retrieve authoritative information
Reason if required
Completion check
Return
Do not let generation skip requirement extraction.
Do not let generation override authoritative retrieval.
Do not let generation claim completion when required elements are absent.
A completion checker can operate deterministically for many requests.
LIST
Requested quantity:
3
Produced items:
2
INCOMPLETE
Required conceptual elements:
definition
purpose
behavior
one isolated property
Subjects:
A
B
A only
EXAMPLE
Requested:
QUANTITY SATISFIED
Semantic quality can still require reasoning, but structural completion can often be checked deterministically.
A long answer can still fail to explain.
Check semantic coverage.
For technical explanation, useful coverage commonly includes:
Identity.
Purpose.
Mechanism.
Relevant properties.
Do not force irrelevant categories into every answer.
Explain an integer.
A concise definition and relevant behavior may be enough.
No network mechanism is needed.
Instruction semantics is about satisfying meaning, not filling a template mechanically.
Templates support interpretation.
They do not replace judgment where judgment is genuinely required.
If the same phrase repeatedly requires the same operation, encode it.
"what does X say" -> retrieve content
"list X" -> enumerate
"how many X" -> count
"compare X and Y" -> compare
This moves common interpretation from probabilistic reasoning toward deterministic behavior.
Unknown phrasing can still use a reasoning fallback.
The deterministic system can validate required fields.
INTENT:
CONFIDENCE:
...
Do not treat confidence as truth.
If interpretation changes authority or causes consequential action, stronger validation may be required.
Delete file X.
Digit can understand:
OPERATION = DELETE
OBJECT = file X
That does not establish permission to delete it.
Interpretation comes before authorization.
A possible action pipeline is:
Interpret.
Check authority.
Check policy.
Check state.
Validate parameters.
Report result.
Do not confuse semantic understanding with execution permission.
Likewise:
is not a DELETE action.
Do not execute an operation merely because its name appears in explanatory language.
This distinction is critical.
Delete config.tsv.
Explain how to delete config.tsv.
Do not delete config.tsv.
Their semantics are different.
The second requests explanation.
The third prohibits execution.
Parse the complete instruction.
Do not trigger actions through keyword matching alone.
What does the command "delete" mean?
The word DELETE appears.
The requested operation is explanation, not deletion.
"If I said stop the service, what would happen?"
This asks for explanation of a hypothetical.
It does not necessarily request stopping the service.
Distinguish mention from use.
This is a fundamental language distinction.
A word can refer to an action without requesting the action.
Determine grammatical and conversational role.
Do not execute based only on keyword presence.
Questions can ask about prohibited or unavailable actions.
Response semantics also matter.
Did the operation succeed?
Do not answer:
Yes
because the operation was requested.
Requested is not completed.
Sent is not acknowledged.
Use the actual state.
Words describing certainty matter.
is
did
will
can
may
might
probably
confirmed
verified
suspected
estimated
Do not silently strengthen uncertainty.
"May" does not mean "will."
"Probably" does not mean "confirmed."
"Suspected" does not mean "established."
Preserve epistemic meaning.
If evidence establishes something, state it directly.
If only inference supports it, identify inference.
Do not manufacture doubt where evidence is determinate.
Do not manufacture certainty where evidence is incomplete.
Instruction semantics includes requested certainty.
Verify that the file was written.
Do not respond:
It probably was.
Verification requires evidence.
If evidence is unavailable:
Unable to verify from available evidence.
This is more accurate than pretending.
Formatting requests matter.
Return only JSON.
Do not add explanatory prose outside it.
Give me a table.
Use an appropriate table.
One line only.
Respect the requested line constraint when practical.
Output format is part of instruction semantics.
Do not treat formatting as decorative when explicitly requested.
Case can sometimes carry meaning in identifiers.
Do not normalize case blindly.
For ordinary natural language, capitalization differences often do not change intent.
For:
HTTP
file paths
identifiers
case can matter according to the underlying system.
Preserve exact identifiers when necessary.
Punctuation can affect interpretation.
But tolerate ordinary informal typing when intent remains clear.
Users can misspell words.
Users can type quickly.
Do not demand formal grammar when meaning is evident.
It does not mean inventing meaning when ambiguity is material.
When ambiguity is harmless, choose the obvious interpretation.
"show logs"
can usually be interpreted safely.
"delete it"
Context and consequence affect how much certainty is required.
Explanatory conversation can tolerate reasonable interpretation.
Irreversible action requires stronger referent certainty.
Do not apply the same ambiguity threshold to every operation.
The cost of being wrong matters.
The more consequential the action, the stronger the required semantic certainty.
For read-only explanation:
For destructive action:
Object and authority should be clear.
For security changes:
Scope and intended value should be clear.
Require the exact established conditions.
Do not use conversational convenience to justify unsafe guessing.
At the same time, do not ask unnecessary questions when the instruction is already clear.
Clarification is for material ambiguity.
Not ritual.
A well-understood request should proceed.
A poorly understood consequential request should not be guessed.
Every response can be evaluated against the original request.
After drafting, compare response to instruction.
Not merely to retrieved facts.
What did the user ask me to do?
Did I do that?
This simple check prevents many failures.
If the user asks:
Do not ask only:
Did I say something true about GET?
Did I explain GET?
Did I retrieve text containing "order"?
Did I return the canonical content identified as General Order Number Two?
Retrieval relevance alone does not.
Semantic similarity alone does not.
Generation fluency does not.
A plausible answer does not prove instruction satisfaction.
A correct answer to a different question is still the wrong answer.
A relevant fact is not automatically a sufficient answer.
A short answer is not automatically incomplete.
The objective is faithful execution of human intent within established authority and system boundaries.
Understand first.
Decompose when needed.
Retrieve the correct knowledge.
Reason where necessary.
Check completion.
Then respond.
Do not guess when exact knowledge exists.
Do not discard modifiers.
Do not discard quantities.
Do not discard conditions.
Do not discard requested output form.
Every meaningful word contributes to the instruction.
Preserve that meaning.
A request is complete only when its required semantics have been satisfied.
HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language.
HTML describes the structure and meaning of web content.
HTML is a markup language, not a programming language.
A web browser parses HTML to construct a representation of the document.
The browser represents a parsed HTML document as the Document Object Model, commonly called the DOM.
HTML elements represent parts of a document.
Most HTML elements are represented using tags.
An HTML element can contain text, other elements, or both.
HTML elements can be nested inside other HTML elements.
Proper nesting helps maintain a valid and understandable document structure.
A closing tag ends most non-void HTML elements.
Some HTML elements are void elements and do not contain child content.
The img element is a void element.
The br element is a void element.
The hr element is a void element.
HTML attributes provide additional information about an element.
Attributes are normally written inside an element's opening tag.
Attribute values should normally be enclosed in quotation marks.
An element can have multiple attributes.
The id attribute identifies an element within a document.
An id value should be unique within an HTML document.
The class attribute associates an element with one or more class names.
Multiple elements can share the same class.
Classes are commonly used by CSS and JavaScript to select groups of elements.
The DOCTYPE tells the browser which general HTML parsing mode the document expects.
The html element is the root element of an HTML document.
The title element defines the title of the document.
The document title commonly appears in the browser tab or window title.
The charset metadata declares the character encoding used by the document.
UTF-8 is a commonly used character encoding for HTML documents.
A typical UTF-8 declaration is <meta charset="UTF-8">.
The viewport metadata helps browsers size pages appropriately on different devices.
The link element can associate external resources with an HTML document.
The link element is commonly used to load an external CSS stylesheet.
The script element is used to associate executable script code with an HTML document.
JavaScript is commonly loaded using the script element.
The style element can contain CSS rules within an HTML document.
External stylesheets are generally preferable when styles are shared across multiple pages.
HTML headings represent headings and subheadings within document content.
HTML provides heading elements from h1 through h6.
The h1 element normally represents the highest-level heading of a document or major content region.
Heading levels communicate document structure and should be chosen according to meaning rather than appearance alone.
The p element represents a paragraph.
The br element creates a line break.
The hr element represents a thematic break between sections of content.
The strong element represents content with strong importance.
The em element represents emphasized content.
The i element can represent text distinguished from surrounding prose for reasons such as terminology or alternate voice.
The div element is a generic block-level container.
Div and span are useful when no more meaningful semantic element is appropriate.
Semantic HTML uses elements whose names describe the purpose or meaning of their content.
Semantic HTML can improve accessibility and document maintainability.
The header element represents introductory or navigational content for a page or section.
The footer element represents footer information for a page or section.
The main element represents the primary content of a document.
A document should normally have only one visible main content region represented by the main element.
The nav element represents a section containing navigation links.
The section element represents a thematic section of content.
The aside element represents content indirectly related to surrounding content.
A link can point to another resource.
A link can point to a fragment within the current document.
A fragment link commonly references an element by its id.
An absolute URL includes sufficient information to identify a resource independently of the current document location.
A relative URL identifies a resource relative to the current document or configured base URL.
Relative URLs are useful for resources located within the same website.
The target attribute can influence where a linked resource is opened.
Opening untrusted links in a new browsing context can require additional security considerations.
Images are embedded in HTML using the img element.
The src attribute identifies the image resource.
The alt attribute provides a text alternative for an image.
Meaningful images should normally have useful alternative text.
Decorative images can use an empty alt attribute when appropriate.
Alternative text is important for accessibility and when an image cannot be displayed.
The width and height attributes can provide intrinsic dimensions for an image.
The li element represents an item within a list.
Lists can contain nested lists.
The dl element represents a description list.
The dt element represents a term or name in a description list.
The dd element represents a description or value associated with a term in a description list.
HTML tables represent tabular data.
The table element contains a table.
The tr element represents a table row.
The tfoot element groups table footer content.
Tables should be used for tabular data rather than general page layout.
HTML forms collect information from users.
The action attribute identifies where form data is submitted.
The method attribute identifies the HTTP method normally used when submitting a form.
GET and POST are commonly used HTTP methods for HTML forms.
GET form submissions commonly encode submitted data into the request URL.
Sensitive information requires appropriate transport security such as HTTPS.
An input with type="text" accepts general single-line text.
A password input does not encrypt the password merely because its characters are visually obscured.
An input with type="email" represents an email-address input.
An input with type="number" represents numeric input.
An input with type="checkbox" represents an independent on-or-off choice.
An input with type="radio" represents a choice commonly grouped with related radio controls.
An input with type="hidden" submits a value that is not normally presented as an editable control.
Hidden form values are still controlled by the client and must not automatically be trusted by the server.
The textarea element accepts multi-line text.
The select element provides a selection control.
The option element represents a choice within a select element.
A submit button can initiate form submission.
The name attribute determines the name associated with a successful form control during submission.
The value attribute provides or represents a value associated with a form control.
The label element associates descriptive text with a form control.
Labels improve form usability and accessibility.
A label can be associated with a control using the for attribute and the control's id.
The required attribute can request browser-side validation before form submission.
HTML form validation can improve usability.
Client-side HTML validation is not a security boundary.
A user can bypass or modify client-side validation.
Server-side applications must independently validate untrusted form data.
Data submitted by an HTML form must be treated as untrusted by the server.
HTML attributes such as maxlength can constrain normal browser interaction but do not establish a server-side security limit.
Disabled form controls are generally not submitted with a form.
Readonly controls can prevent normal editing while still allowing their values to be submitted.
HTML comments begin with <!-- and end with -->.
Secrets must not be placed in HTML comments.
Anything sent as HTML to a browser should be considered visible to the client.
Server-side secrets should remain on the server and should not be embedded in generated HTML.
The entity &lt; represents a less-than sign.
The entity &gt; represents a greater-than sign.
The entity &amp; represents an ampersand.
Escaping special characters is important when inserting untrusted text into HTML.
Untrusted data inserted into HTML without appropriate output escaping can create cross-site scripting vulnerabilities.
Output escaping should be appropriate for the context in which data is inserted.
HTML text content and HTML attribute values can require different output-handling considerations.
URLs and JavaScript contexts have security considerations different from ordinary HTML text.
HTML itself should not be relied upon to sanitize untrusted data.
Server-side software should validate input and encode output appropriately for its destination.
The DOM is a structured representation of a document exposed to software.
JavaScript can inspect and modify the DOM.
Changes to the DOM can change what a user sees without changing the original HTML response received from the server.
HTML provides document structure and semantics.
CSS controls the visual presentation and layout of HTML content.
JavaScript commonly provides client-side behavior and interactivity.
HTML, CSS, and JavaScript have distinct responsibilities even though they commonly work together.
PHP can generate HTML on the server.
A PHP program can produce an HTML document as its HTTP response.
The browser does not normally receive the PHP source code executed by the server.
The browser receives the output produced by PHP, which can include HTML.
HTML does not execute PHP.
PHP is executed by the server before generated HTML is sent to the browser.
An HTML form can submit data to a PHP application.
A PHP application can process submitted form data and return another HTML document.
A PHP application can retrieve information from a database and represent that information in generated HTML.
Database credentials should not be embedded in HTML delivered to a browser.
HTML cannot directly protect a server-side database.
Authorization decisions must be enforced by trusted server-side software rather than HTML controls.
Disabling a button in HTML does not establish authorization.
Security-sensitive operations must be protected on the server.
HTTP is the protocol commonly used to transfer HTML documents between web clients and web servers.
HTTPS is HTTP protected using TLS.
HTTPS protects data while it travels between communicating endpoints when correctly configured.
HTTPS does not make unsafe HTML or unsafe server-side application logic automatically secure.
A web server can serve static HTML files directly.
A browser sends an HTTP request for a resource.
A web server returns an HTTP response.
An HTTP response can contain an HTML document.
The Content-Type response header tells the client what media type the response represents.
HTML responses commonly use the text/html media type.
HTML document structure and HTTP transport are separate concerns.
Declaring document language assists accessibility tools and other software.
Accessibility means designing content so people with differing abilities can perceive and interact with it.
Semantic elements can improve accessibility.
Images conveying information should have appropriate alternative text.
Interactive controls should use elements appropriate for interaction when possible.
Keyboard accessibility is important for interactive web interfaces.
Visual appearance alone should not be the only means used to communicate important meaning.
HTML source order can affect reading and keyboard navigation order.
Valid and meaningful document structure can make accessibility easier to maintain.
HTML should be validated during development when practical.
HTML validation can detect malformed markup and structural mistakes.
Valid HTML does not guarantee that a page is accessible.
Valid HTML does not guarantee that an application is secure.
Browser developer tools can inspect HTML and the DOM.
Client-side HTML must never be treated as inaccessible to the user.
A user can inspect and modify HTML in their own browser.
An id identifies a particular element within a document.
A class categorizes one or more elements.
CSS can select elements by element type, id, class, attributes, and other relationships.
JavaScript can locate DOM elements using selectors and DOM APIs.
HTML provides the structure on which CSS and client-side JavaScript commonly operate.
Separating HTML structure, CSS presentation, and JavaScript behavior generally improves maintainability.
HTML files commonly use the .html or .htm filename extension.
A web server can return HTML regardless of the server-side filename or technology that generated it.
A URL does not have to end in .html for its response to contain HTML.
Dynamic web applications often generate HTML without exposing implementation filenames in URLs.
Document structure should describe content rather than merely reproduce a desired visual appearance.
HTML is one layer of a web application and does not replace server-side application logic.
HTML is not an authentication system.
HTML is not an authorization system.
HTML is not a server-side programming language.
HTML is the structural and semantic foundation of content presented on the Web.
Performance describes how efficiently a system performs required work under defined conditions.
Performance is not one measurement.
Performance can include latency.
Performance can include throughput.
Performance can include CPU usage.
Performance can include memory usage.
Performance can include storage activity.
Performance can include network activity.
Performance can include concurrency.
Performance can include resource utilization.
Performance can include scalability.
Performance can include responsiveness.
Performance requirements should be defined.
"Low latency" is not precise without a target.
"High throughput" is not precise without a workload.
A performance requirement should identify the operation being measured.
A performance requirement should identify the workload.
A performance requirement should identify relevant resource limits.
A performance requirement should identify an acceptable result.
Observation is stronger than assumption.
A slow-looking function is not necessarily a bottleneck.
A large function is not necessarily slow.
A small function is not necessarily fast.
Measure before optimizing.
Optimization without measurement can waste engineering effort.
Optimization without measurement can make software more complex without improving meaningful performance.
Optimization can introduce defects.
Optimization can increase memory usage.
Optimization can reduce portability.
Optimization can change timing.
Optimization can expose concurrency defects.
Performance changes should therefore be justified by evidence.
Latency is the time required to complete an operation.
Latency can be measured from request start to response completion.
Latency can be measured for one internal operation.
Latency can be measured across an entire end-to-end workflow.
The measurement boundary must be defined.
Network latency is not the same as application latency.
Storage latency is not the same as total request latency.
One operation can contain multiple latency components.
End-to-end latency includes all work inside the measured boundary.
Requests per second is a throughput measurement.
Transactions per second is a throughput measurement.
Messages per second is a throughput measurement.
Operations per second is a throughput measurement.
A system can have high throughput and poor latency.
A system can have low latency and low total throughput.
Batching can improve throughput while increasing latency for individual items.
Performance claims should identify which property improved.
Response time is commonly the elapsed time observed by a requester.
Service time is the time a resource actively spends servicing work according to the measurement model.
An operation can be computationally cheap but wait behind other work.
Queueing matters.
A queue stores work waiting for processing.
A growing queue can increase latency.
An unbounded queue can consume unbounded memory.
Queues should be bounded where resource control matters.
Backpressure limits incoming work when downstream capacity is constrained.
Backpressure can protect latency.
Backpressure can prevent overload from spreading.
Rejecting excess work can be safer than accepting work that cannot be completed within requirements.
CPU capacity is finite.
Storage bandwidth is finite.
Storage I/O operations are finite.
Network bandwidth is finite.
Thread capacity is finite.
Queue capacity should be finite.
A system cannot process unlimited work merely because more work is submitted.
Saturation occurs when a resource approaches its useful capacity.
A saturated CPU cannot provide unlimited additional compute.
A saturated storage device cannot provide unlimited additional I/O.
A saturated worker pool cannot immediately process additional work.
Performance testing should include behavior near expected peak load.
Average load can hide peak problems.
Average latency can hide slow operations.
An average does not describe distribution.
If nine operations complete quickly and one completes very slowly, the average can obscure the slow operation.
The 50th percentile is commonly called the median.
A p99 latency describes the value at or below which approximately 99 percent of observations fall.
Percentiles reveal tail latency.
Tail latency can matter greatly in user-facing and distributed systems.
One slow dependency can increase end-to-end latency.
Parallel operations can reduce total latency when independent work can execute concurrently.
Parallelism has overhead.
Parallelism should not be introduced automatically.
A benchmark measures performance under defined conditions.
A benchmark should identify hardware.
A benchmark should identify software version.
A benchmark should identify configuration.
A benchmark should identify input data.
A benchmark should identify duration.
A benchmark should identify measurement methodology.
Without context, benchmark numbers have limited meaning.
Benchmarks should be reproducible where practical.
A benchmark should measure relevant behavior.
A microbenchmark measures a small operation.
Microbenchmarks do not automatically predict complete application performance.
A function that is faster in isolation can have no measurable effect on the whole application.
They can make root-cause analysis harder.
Use appropriate measurement levels.
Warm-up can affect benchmark results.
Caches can be cold at startup.
JIT runtimes can compile code during early execution.
Memory allocators can establish internal state.
Connections can require initial setup.
DNS can require resolution.
Storage caches can require population.
Benchmark methodology should distinguish cold-start behavior from steady-state behavior.
Do not discard cold-start measurements when startup latency matters.
Benchmark duration should be sufficient to capture meaningful variation.
A one-second benchmark can miss periodic work.
A short benchmark can miss memory growth.
A short benchmark can miss queue buildup.
A short benchmark can miss resource exhaustion.
Sustained testing can reveal behavior that burst testing misses.
Stress testing pushes a system toward or beyond normal capacity.
Soak testing runs a system for an extended period.
Spike testing evaluates sudden workload increases.
Each test answers different questions.
A load test can determine expected operating behavior.
A stress test can identify failure boundaries.
A soak test can reveal leaks or long-term degradation.
A spike test can reveal queueing and recovery behavior.
Performance tests should define success criteria.
"Did not crash" is not sufficient when latency requirements exist.
A system can remain running while becoming unusably slow.
Availability and performance are related but different.
A profiler measures where execution resources are consumed.
CPU profiling identifies where processor time is spent.
Memory profiling identifies allocation and retention behavior.
Lock profiling can identify synchronization contention.
Sampling profilers periodically inspect execution state.
Profiling has overhead.
Profiler results should be interpreted with awareness of measurement effects.
A hot path is code executed frequently or consuming significant runtime resources.
Hot paths should be identified through evidence.
Frequency alone does not determine importance.
Cost alone does not determine importance.
Total contribution matters.
A bottleneck is a limiting resource or operation constraining overall performance.
If storage is the bottleneck, optimizing unrelated arithmetic can produce no meaningful improvement.
If network latency dominates, shaving microseconds from local parsing can be irrelevant.
Find the limiting factor.
A system can have multiple bottlenecks under different workloads.
A CPU bottleneck can become a storage bottleneck after CPU optimization.
A storage bottleneck can become a lock bottleneck after storage improvement.
Performance work is iterative.
Measure.
Identify.
Do not assume an optimization worked.
A change intended to improve performance can make performance worse.
Regression testing should detect performance degradation where performance is important.
Performance baselines provide comparison points.
A baseline records measured behavior under defined conditions.
New measurements should use comparable conditions.
Changing hardware invalidates direct comparison unless hardware differences are accounted for.
Changing workload invalidates direct comparison unless workload differences are accounted for.
I/O-bound work spends substantial time waiting for input or output.
Memory-bound work can be limited by memory bandwidth or latency.
Lock-bound work can be limited by synchronization contention.
Classify the actual constraint.
CPU utilization alone does not prove CPU-bound behavior.
Low CPU utilization does not prove the system has spare useful capacity.
A single-threaded workload can saturate one core while total machine CPU appears low.
A process can be blocked on a lock.
A process can be blocked on I/O.
A process can be rate limited.
A process can be waiting for a remote service.
Inspect the relevant resources.
CPU utilization describes processor time consumption.
User CPU time is spent executing application code according to operating-system accounting.
System CPU time is spent executing operating-system work on behalf of the process according to operating-system accounting.
High system CPU can indicate heavy system-call or kernel activity.
Context switching consumes CPU resources.
Excessive context switching can reduce useful work.
Each thread consumes memory.
Each thread can create scheduling overhead.
Each thread can increase contention.
Creating more threads does not guarantee more throughput.
For I/O-bound work, additional concurrency can keep processing resources busy while operations wait.
Concurrency should be bounded.
Parallelism means work executes simultaneously.
Concurrency means multiple tasks make progress during overlapping periods.
Concurrent systems are not necessarily parallel.
A single-threaded event loop can handle many concurrent operations.
Parallel processing can use multiple CPU cores.
Amdahl's law describes limits on speedup when only part of a workload can be parallelized.
If a substantial portion of work remains serial, adding processors has diminishing benefit.
Parallelism cannot eliminate serial work.
Lock contention occurs when multiple execution contexts compete for the same lock.
A lock with little contention can have low practical cost.
A heavily contended lock can serialize otherwise parallel work.
Measure contention.
Incorrect lock removal can create races.
Correctness comes before optimization.
Lock granularity affects contention.
One global lock is simple.
Many fine-grained locks can increase complexity.
Choose the simplest locking strategy that satisfies performance requirements.
Lock-free algorithms avoid traditional mutual exclusion locks for selected operations.
Lock-free does not mean wait-free.
Lock-free does not mean simple.
Lock-free code can be difficult to reason about.
Use it when measurements and requirements justify it.
Atomic operations can be cheaper than locks for selected operations.
Atomic operations can still cause cache contention.
A frequently modified shared atomic variable can become a scalability bottleneck.
Shared state has hardware costs.
Cache coherence can move cache lines between CPU cores.
False sharing occurs when independent variables modified by different threads occupy the same cache line.
False sharing can reduce performance.
Do not introduce architecture-specific padding without evidence.
Contiguous data often benefits processor caches.
Cache misses can stall execution.
Data layout can matter as much as algorithmic instruction count.
Measure realistic workloads.
CPU cache and application cache are different concepts.
CPU caches are hardware-managed memory hierarchy components.
Application caches store reusable computed or retrieved data.
A cache can become stale.
A cache can consume excessive memory.
A cache can add synchronization.
Cache invalidation must follow data semantics.
A cache miss requires obtaining or computing data elsewhere.
A high hit ratio can improve performance when cache hits are substantially cheaper.
A high hit ratio does not prove the cache is valuable if the underlying operation is already cheap.
Measure actual savings.
Caching expensive results can improve latency.
Caching cheap results can cost more than recomputing them.
Cache invalidation has cost.
Cache serialization can have cost.
Caching should target demonstrated repeated expense.
Memoization caches function results based on inputs.
Memoization is useful when repeated calls with the same inputs are expensive and results remain valid.
Memoization requires stable result semantics.
A function depending on hidden mutable state is difficult to memoize correctly.
Deterministic pure functions are natural memoization candidates.
Caches should have bounded size.
Unbounded caches can become memory leaks in operational effect.
Eviction removes entries according to cache policy.
LRU approximates least-recently-used eviction.
FIFO evicts according to insertion order.
Different policies fit different access patterns.
A TTL is not proof that data remains valid until expiration.
A TTL is a caching policy.
If authoritative state changes earlier, cached state can become stale unless invalidated.
Caching should not silently violate correctness.
Batching groups multiple operations.
Batching can reduce per-operation overhead.
Batching can reduce network round trips.
Batching can reduce storage transactions.
Batching can improve throughput.
Batching can increase individual item latency.
Batching can increase memory use.
Batch size should be bounded.
Larger batches are not automatically better.
A batch can become too large for memory.
A batch can exceed protocol limits.
A batch can hold locks too long.
Measure batch size effects.
Buffering stores data temporarily before processing or transfer.
Buffering can reduce small I/O operations.
Buffering can improve throughput.
Buffering adds memory use.
Buffering can delay visibility.
Buffering can lose unflushed data during failure.
Flush semantics should be understood.
A successful write to an application buffer is not necessarily durable storage.
If durability matters, measure the required durability boundary.
I/O operations have overhead.
Many tiny reads can be slower than fewer larger reads.
The difference depends on storage technology.
Solid-state storage reduces mechanical seek cost but still has finite I/O capacity.
Do not assume all storage is equivalent.
Storage latency should be measured on the deployment environment.
Filesystem performance can depend on file count.
Filesystem performance can depend on directory structure.
Filesystem performance can depend on metadata operations.
Filesystem performance can depend on synchronization requirements.
Filesystem performance can depend on storage hardware.
Database performance depends on queries.
Database performance depends on indexes.
Database performance depends on data volume.
Database performance depends on concurrency.
Database performance depends on transactions.
Database performance depends on storage.
Database performance depends on schema design.
A slow database request should be investigated using database evidence.
Do not assume the database is slow merely because a request involving a database is slow.
An index can improve selected query performance.
An index consumes storage.
An index increases selected write costs.
Unused indexes can impose unnecessary overhead.
Index design should follow query patterns.
Query plans show how a database intends to execute queries.
Query plans can reveal index usage.
Query plans can reveal joins.
Query plans can reveal estimated costs.
Actual execution measurements provide stronger evidence than estimates alone.
An ORM can simplify persistence code.
An ORM can also generate inefficient query patterns.
N+1 query behavior occurs when one initial query causes many additional queries for related data.
N+1 behavior can create excessive round trips.
Measure query count.
Do not blame the ORM without inspecting generated queries.
Network round trips have latency.
Multiple sequential remote calls accumulate round-trip latency.
Combining appropriate requests can reduce round trips.
Parallel remote calls can reduce total elapsed time when operations are independent.
Do not design network APIs as though they were local object methods.
Chatty protocols make many small exchanges.
Chatty protocols can suffer badly on higher-latency networks.
Protocol design should consider round trips.
Payload size also matters.
Large payloads consume bandwidth.
Large payloads consume serialization time.
Large payloads can increase latency.
Compression can reduce network bytes.
Compression consumes CPU.
Compression adds latency.
Compression effectiveness depends on data.
Already compressed data can gain little.
Small messages can cost more to compress than they save.
Measure before enabling compression universally.
Serialization consumes CPU and memory.
Text formats can be larger than binary formats.
Compactness is not the only requirement.
Debuggability can matter.
Compatibility can matter.
Simplicity can matter.
Schema evolution can matter.
Do not choose binary encoding solely because it sounds faster.
Connection establishment can contribute latency.
TLS handshakes can contribute latency.
Authentication can contribute latency.
End-to-end measurement should account for relevant stages.
Connection reuse can avoid repeated setup costs.
Persistent connections can reduce handshakes.
Persistent connections consume resources while open.
Connection pools reuse connections.
A pool that is too small can create waiting.
A pool that is too large can overwhelm the dependency.
Pool size should be measured.
A connection pool does not create unlimited backend capacity.
If a database handles 100 useful concurrent connections, creating 10,000 client connections does not create more database capacity.
Resource pools should protect dependencies.
Timeouts affect performance behavior.
A very long timeout can hold resources during failures.
A very short timeout can reject valid slow operations.
Three attempts can create up to three times the operation load.
During an outage, uncontrolled retries can amplify overload.
Retry storms can prevent recovery.
Retries should be bounded.
Retries should use appropriate delay.
Exponential backoff increases delay between repeated attempts.
Retries should be limited to retryable failures.
Non-idempotent operations require special care.
Performance optimization must not change correctness.
Skipping synchronization can make code faster and unsafe.
Skipping durable writes can make code faster and non-durable.
Skipping authentication can make code faster and insecure.
Correctness constraints remain constraints.
Algorithmic complexity describes how resource requirements grow with input size.
Big-O notation describes asymptotic growth.
O(1) means bounded growth independent of input size under the model.
O(log n) grows logarithmically.
O(n) grows linearly.
O(n log n) grows faster than linear.
O(n squared) grows quadratically.
Big-O does not specify actual runtime.
Constant factors matter.
Input sizes matter.
Memory behavior matters.
Hardware matters.
An O(n squared) algorithm can be acceptable for tiny bounded input.
An O(n) algorithm can still be slow with enormous constants.
Choose algorithms according to real constraints.
Complexity analysis helps predict scaling.
Use both.
A data structure affects performance.
Arrays provide indexed access and good locality.
