Cryptography is the study and application of techniques for protecting information and establishing security properties.
Cryptography can provide confidentiality.
Cryptography can provide integrity.
Cryptography can provide authenticity.
Cryptography can support identity verification.
Cryptography does not automatically provide every security property.
Different cryptographic mechanisms solve different problems.
A cryptographic algorithm is not secure merely because it is complicated.
A cryptographic algorithm is not secure merely because its implementation is secret.
Do not invent custom cryptographic algorithms for production security.
Use established algorithms and established implementations according to system requirements.
Cryptographic security depends on correct implementation.
Cryptographic security depends on correct parameter selection.
Cryptographic security depends on correct protocol composition.
Cryptographic security depends on correct randomness where randomness is required.
Cryptographic security depends on validating inputs.
Compression is not encryption.
Hashing is not encryption.
Base64 is not encryption.
Hexadecimal encoding is not encryption.
Changing a filename extension is not encryption.
Encryption transforms plaintext into ciphertext using a cryptographic algorithm and key material.
Plaintext is the original readable or interpretable information supplied to an encryption operation.
Ciphertext is the encrypted representation.
A cipher is an algorithm used for encryption and decryption.
A key is cryptographic input controlling the transformation performed by an algorithm.
The secrecy of protected data should depend on the secrecy of the key rather than secrecy of the algorithm.
Key length is measured in bits.
Larger key length does not automatically mean a cryptographic system is better.
Security depends on the algorithm and effective security strength.
Key sizes from different algorithms cannot be compared solely by their numeric bit length.
A 256-bit symmetric key and a 256-bit asymmetric key do not imply equivalent security.
Symmetric encryption is generally efficient for encrypting substantial amounts of data.
AES is a standardized symmetric block cipher.
AES supports defined key sizes.
AES itself is a block cipher rather than a complete application protocol.
A block cipher transforms fixed-size blocks according to its algorithm.
Using a block cipher securely requires an appropriate mode or construction.
ECB is a block cipher mode.
ECB exposes repeated plaintext block patterns.
CBC is a block cipher mode.
CBC encryption by itself does not provide authenticated encryption.
CTR requires unique counter or nonce handling according to its security requirements.
Reusing required-unique nonce or counter material can catastrophically weaken security.
GCM is an authenticated-encryption mode commonly used with AES.
Authenticated encryption is commonly abbreviated AE.
Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data is commonly abbreviated AEAD.
AEAD protects ciphertext integrity while permitting selected associated data to remain unencrypted but authenticated.
Associated data is authenticated but not encrypted.
Protocol headers can sometimes be associated data when the protocol requires their integrity without confidentiality.
Authenticated encryption should generally be preferred over unauthenticated encryption when both confidentiality and integrity are required.
Encryption without integrity protection can permit dangerous ciphertext modification attacks depending on the construction.
Confidentiality and integrity are different properties.
Asymmetric cryptography uses mathematically related public and private keys.
A private key must remain protected.
Knowledge of a public key should not reveal the corresponding private key under the security assumptions of the algorithm.
Public-key cryptography can support encryption.
Public-key cryptography can support digital signatures.
Encryption and signing are different operations.
A public key does not need to be secret.
A private key does need protection.
A private key should not be transmitted merely because the public key is transmitted.
Public-key distribution requires authenticity.
A certificate can bind identity information to a public key according to a certificate system.
A certificate is not automatically trustworthy merely because it is syntactically valid.
Certificate validation requires trust-chain and policy checks according to the applicable system.
A CA signs certificates according to its authority and validation procedures.
A root certificate represents a trust anchor according to the applicable trust store.
A trust anchor is trusted because the system's policy establishes it as trusted.
Cryptography does not decide which authority should be trusted.
Trust policy determines which keys or certificates are authoritative.
A mathematically valid signature from an unauthorized key remains unauthorized.
RSA is an asymmetric cryptographic algorithm family.
Raw RSA operations should not be used as application cryptographic protocols.
Padding and encoding schemes are essential to secure RSA use.
RSA key size affects security.
RSA-2048 and RSA-3072 refer to RSA modulus sizes.
An RSA public key can verify signatures generated by its corresponding private key under the signature scheme.
A public key cannot generate a valid private-key signature merely because it can verify one.
Elliptic-curve cryptography is commonly abbreviated ECC.
ECDSA is an elliptic-curve digital signature algorithm.
Ed25519 is a digital signature system based on Edwards-curve cryptography.
Ed25519 and X25519 serve different purposes.
A signing key and a key-agreement key should not be treated as interchangeable merely because both use related elliptic-curve technology.
Signing uses private key material.
A valid digital signature establishes cryptographic validity relative to the verified data and public key.
A valid signature does not by itself establish that the signer was authorized to perform an action.
A valid signature does not prove that the signed statement is factually true.
A signer can cryptographically sign false information.
A signature proves the cryptographic relationship between the signed data and signing key according to the algorithm.
Authority requires determining what that identity is permitted to do.
Truth requires evidence beyond signature validity when the signed content makes factual claims.
The exact bytes being verified matter.
Changing signed data causes signature verification to fail under a secure signature scheme except under cryptographically negligible or otherwise defined exceptional conditions.
Serialization therefore matters for signatures.
If two systems serialize logically identical structures differently, their signatures can differ.
Canonical serialization can ensure that logically equivalent data has one defined byte representation.
A signature should cover the exact fields requiring integrity and authenticity.
Unsigned fields can be modified without invalidating the signature.
Signature scope must therefore be explicit.
A digital signature is not encryption.
Signed data can remain completely readable.
A digital signature does not provide confidentiality.
Hashing transforms arbitrary input into a fixed-size digest according to the hash algorithm.
Identical input produces identical digest using the same hash algorithm.
Different input should normally produce different digest values, but collisions are mathematically possible.
A collision occurs when two different inputs produce the same hash digest.
Cryptographic hash algorithms are designed to make finding useful collisions computationally difficult according to their security properties.
Preimage resistance makes it computationally difficult to find an input producing a specified digest.
Collision resistance makes it computationally difficult to find any two different inputs producing the same digest.
These are related but distinct cryptographic properties.
SHA-256 is a cryptographic hash function.
A SHA-256 digest is commonly represented as 64 hexadecimal characters.
The hexadecimal representation is an encoding of the digest.
The hexadecimal string is not the hash algorithm itself.
SHA-256 does not encrypt data.
SHA-256 cannot be decrypted.
Hashing is one-way according to its security design.
A hash digest can be recomputed from candidate input and compared.
This is different from decrypting the digest.
Hashes can verify file integrity.
Compute the hash of the received file.
Matching hashes provide strong evidence that the bytes match the expected hashed content according to the hash assumptions.
A matching hash does not establish where the expected hash came from.
If an attacker can replace both a file and its published hash, hash comparison alone does not establish authenticity.
Integrity requires a trusted reference.
Authenticity can require signatures, authenticated channels, or another trusted mechanism.
MD5 is a cryptographic hash function with serious collision weaknesses.
MD5 should not be selected for modern cryptographic integrity security.
Legacy hashes can still appear in non-security contexts.
A password should not normally be stored using a simple fast general-purpose hash.
Password hashing has different requirements from ordinary file hashing.
Password hashing algorithms deliberately make guessing expensive.
Argon2 is a password-hashing algorithm family.
Argon2id is commonly recommended for password hashing when supported and appropriately configured.
bcrypt is a password-hashing algorithm designed for password storage.
scrypt is a password-based key derivation function designed to require substantial resources.
PBKDF2 is a password-based key derivation function using repeated pseudorandom-function operations.
Password-hashing parameters affect resistance to guessing attacks.
A password hash should use a unique salt.
A salt is non-secret random or unique data incorporated into password hashing.
A salt prevents identical passwords from necessarily producing identical stored hashes under the same password-hashing construction.
A salt does not need to be secret.
A salt is not an encryption key.
A salt does not replace a strong password-hashing algorithm.
A pepper is an additional secret value sometimes incorporated into password protection designs.
A pepper must be protected as secret.
A pepper and salt serve different purposes.
Compromise of a password database containing salts does not automatically compromise a separately protected pepper.
Pepper management introduces key-management responsibilities.
Password verification hashes or derives from the supplied password using the stored parameters and compares according to the password-hashing scheme.
Applications should not need to recover user passwords from password storage.
Passwords should not be logged.
Passwords should not be sent by email in plaintext.
A MAC provides integrity and authenticity using secret key material according to its construction.
HMAC is a widely used MAC construction based on a cryptographic hash function.
HMAC is not the same thing as simply hashing secret data together with a message arbitrarily.
Use the defined HMAC construction.
Both sender and verifier normally need the shared secret for HMAC.
Because both parties know the same secret, HMAC does not provide the same third-party verification property as a public-key digital signature.
An HMAC verifier possessing the shared secret can generally generate valid HMAC values too.
This distinction matters.
A checksum detects accidental changes according to its design.
CRC is commonly used for accidental error detection.
CRC is not a cryptographic integrity mechanism against an attacker.
Use cryptographic authentication when malicious modification is part of the threat model.
Randomness is important in cryptography.
Cryptographic randomness should be generated using a cryptographically secure random number generator.
A cryptographically secure pseudorandom number generator is commonly abbreviated CSPRNG.
A CSPRNG is designed to make future or previous output difficult to predict under its security model.
Ordinary programming-language random functions are not automatically cryptographically secure.
Do not use rand() in C for cryptographic key generation.
Do not use predictable timestamps as cryptographic keys.
Do not use process IDs as cryptographic keys.
Do not use usernames as cryptographic keys.
Do not construct cryptographic keys from easily predictable values.
Cryptographic keys should have sufficient entropy.
Entropy describes uncertainty in a value according to the relevant information model.
Operating systems provide cryptographically secure randomness facilities.
Applications should use established system or cryptographic-library interfaces rather than inventing entropy collection.
A nonce is a value intended to be used according to a cryptographic protocol's uniqueness or unpredictability requirements.
Nonce means number used once.
Some constructions require a nonce to be unique.
Some constructions require randomness.
Never assume nonce requirements.
Follow the algorithm specification.
An initialization vector is commonly abbreviated IV.
An IV initializes a cryptographic mode according to its requirements.
An IV is not necessarily secret.
An IV can require unpredictability.
An IV can require uniqueness.
Requirements depend on the cryptographic construction.
Do not replace an IV with a fixed constant unless the construction explicitly permits it.
A cryptographic key and IV are different values with different roles.
HKDF is a standardized key derivation function based on HMAC.
Password-based KDFs are designed for low-entropy password input.
General KDFs and password KDFs solve different problems.
Do not replace a password-hashing algorithm with a single SHA-256 operation merely because SHA-256 produces 256 bits.
Key separation means using distinct keys for distinct cryptographic purposes when required.
Context information can bind derived keys to specific purposes.
Key separation limits cross-protocol interactions and compromise scope.
Key management is part of cryptographic security.
Keys must be generated.
Keys must be stored.
Keys can require distribution.
Keys can require rotation.
Keys can require revocation.
Keys can require expiration.
Keys can require backup according to system needs.
Keys can require destruction according to system needs.
A secure algorithm with exposed keys is not secure.
A private key stored world-readable defeats private-key confidentiality.
Removing a committed secret from the latest source revision does not necessarily remove it from repository history.
Secret rotation may be required after exposure.
Key rotation replaces active key material according to policy.
Rotation should account for data or signatures created using previous keys.
Old verification keys can remain necessary for historical signature verification.
A retired signing key should not necessarily be deleted immediately if historical verification depends on it.
Retired and revoked are different states.
A retired key is no longer used for new operations according to policy.
A revoked key is explicitly no longer trusted for applicable operations according to policy.
A key identifier identifies key material according to system rules.
A key identifier is not the secret key.
A fingerprint can identify a public key using a digest.
A fingerprint should be verified through a trusted channel when used for identity verification.
Matching fingerprints do not independently establish who owns the key.
Trust must come from somewhere.
A trust store contains trusted keys or certificates according to system policy.
Adding a key to a trust store grants it some defined trust.
That is an authority decision.
Cryptographic software should not autonomously trust unknown keys merely because their signatures are mathematically valid.
Trust on first use is commonly abbreviated TOFU.
TOFU accepts an initial key and detects later changes according to the system model.
TOFU cannot independently prove the first observed key was legitimate.
Different trust models have different assumptions.
TLS is a cryptographic protocol used to protect network communication.
TLS can provide integrity.
TLS can authenticate servers using certificates according to ordinary deployments.
TLS can optionally authenticate clients according to configuration.
HTTPS is HTTP carried over TLS.
HTTPS does not mean the application itself is secure.
HTTPS protects communication according to TLS properties.
HTTPS does not fix SQL injection.
HTTPS does not fix command injection.
HTTPS does not fix broken authorization.
HTTPS does not validate application business logic.
Disabling certificate verification defeats important TLS authentication properties.
A successful TLS connection to an unverified certificate does not establish the intended server identity.
Hostname verification checks whether the certificate is valid for the requested hostname according to certificate rules.
Certificate expiration should be validated according to the system's time and policy.
System time therefore matters for certificate validation.
A certificate can be cryptographically valid but expired.
"Certificate expired" is different from "unknown issuer."
"Key missing" is different from "key malformed."
Public interfaces should not expose secrets.
Internal logs should not record key material.
A digital signature workflow begins with defined data.
The data is serialized according to the required representation.
The signer applies the signature algorithm using private key material.
The signature accompanies or is associated with the data.
The verifier obtains the corresponding public key through a trusted mechanism.
The verifier reconstructs the exact signed representation.
The verifier applies the verification algorithm.
Verification either succeeds or fails according to the algorithm.
A signed software module can be cryptographically valid but still incompatible.
A signed software module can be cryptographically valid but still unauthorized.
A signed software module can be cryptographically valid but still contain defects.
A signed software module can be cryptographically valid but still contain malicious behavior if an authorized signing key was misused or compromised.
A signature establishes origin relative to a key and integrity of signed bytes.
It does not establish software quality.
Module qualification and signature validation should remain separate checks.
A module loader can validate ABI compatibility.
A module loader can validate required exports.
A module loader can enforce authorization policy.
Passing one validation does not imply passing the others.
Cryptographic identity can be useful for module provenance.
A developer public key can identify a signing identity according to policy.
A module signature can bind module bytes to that signing identity.
Only authorized keys should satisfy authorization policy.
Unknown valid signatures can be reported as valid but unauthorized.
Do not report an unauthorized valid signature as invalid merely because authorization failed.
Preserve the distinction.
File integrity can be checked with hashes.
Suppose a file has an expected SHA-256 digest.
Read the exact file bytes.
Compute SHA-256.
Compare the computed digest to the expected digest.
Do not modify the expected digest to make a changed file pass validation unless an authorized change process requires establishing a new expected digest.
Changing document content changes its hash.
Changing whitespace changes the raw-byte hash.
Changing encoding can change the raw-byte hash.
If stable semantic hashing is required, canonicalization rules must be explicitly defined.
Without canonicalization, hash exact bytes.
Do not silently canonicalize data before hashing unless the specification requires it.
A hash placeholder is not a completed integrity value.
The actual digest must be calculated from the finalized content according to the governing process.
SHA-256 and SHA-512 are different algorithms.
AES-GCM and AES-CBC are different modes.
Ed25519 and ECDSA are different signature systems.
Protocol fields should identify algorithm choices when algorithm agility is required.
Algorithm agility can introduce complexity and downgrade risks.
A protocol should not accept weaker algorithms merely because a peer requests them unless policy permits them.
A downgrade attack causes participants to use weaker security than intended.
Version and algorithm negotiation should protect against unauthorized downgrade where required.
Cryptographic libraries can expose many options.
A library supporting MD5 does not mean MD5 is appropriate for new cryptographic security.
Algorithm implementation should use established cryptographic libraries.
Security updates matter.
Implementation bugs can compromise mathematically sound algorithms.
Memory safety matters when handling keys.
Bounds checking matters.
Error checking matters.
Return values matter.
Random-source failures matter.
Do not continue with predictable fallback randomness when secure randomness fails.
If secure key generation cannot obtain required entropy, fail according to security policy.
Failure is safer than silently generating weak keys.
Constant-time operations reduce information leakage through timing for selected secret-dependent computations.
Timing side channels can reveal information about secrets.
Cache behavior can create side channels.
Memory access patterns can create side channels.
Power consumption can create side channels in physical environments.
Electromagnetic emissions can create side channels in physical environments.
Cryptographic side-channel resistance is an implementation property as well as an algorithmic concern.
Use established implementations rather than attempting casual constant-time code.
A comparison of authentication tags or sensitive values can require constant-time comparison.
Early termination can leak position information through timing.
Whether that leakage is exploitable depends on environment and threat model.
Use cryptographic-library comparison functions when provided for sensitive values.
Memory containing secrets can remain after logical use.
Secret material can require explicit clearing according to security requirements.
Compilers can remove ordinary memory-clearing operations if they appear unnecessary.
Use platform or library facilities designed for secure clearing where required.
Modern filesystems, SSD wear leveling, snapshots, backups, and journaling can preserve previous data.
Full-disk encryption protects storage under its threat model.
Full-disk encryption does not protect data from an already authenticated running system with access to decrypted files.
Application-level encryption can protect selected data with different key boundaries.
Threat model determines required protection.
A threat model identifies adversaries.
A threat model identifies capabilities.
A threat model identifies attack surfaces.
Cryptographic design should follow the threat model.
Poor key management can make encryption useless.
Unnecessary cryptography can increase complexity.
Do not use cryptography as decoration.
Integrity means detecting unauthorized or accidental modification according to the mechanism and threat model.
Authenticity means establishing that data or an entity originates from the claimed source according to the authentication mechanism.
Authorization means determining whether an authenticated identity is permitted to perform an action.
Availability means systems and data remain accessible as required.
Cryptography can support confidentiality, integrity, and authenticity.
Cryptography alone does not guarantee availability.
Cryptography alone does not define authorization policy.
Authentication and authorization are different.
Identification and authentication are different.
A password can participate in authentication.
Authorization determines permitted actions.
A signature can authenticate a message relative to a key.
The application still needs to determine whether that key is authorized.
A hash can establish content identity relative to an expected digest.
The application still needs to determine whether the expected digest is trusted.
The application still needs to determine whether that identity is authorized.
Policy supplies authority.
A cryptographic protocol is more than a list of algorithms.
Order matters.
Freshness matters.
A replay attack retransmits a previously valid message in a context where repeating it should not be accepted.
A valid signature alone does not prevent replay.
A previously signed request remains correctly signed when replayed.
Replay prevention can use nonces.
Replay prevention can use sequence numbers.
Replay prevention can use timestamps with defined acceptance windows.
Replay prevention can use unique transaction identifiers.
The correct mechanism depends on protocol requirements.
A timestamp requires trusted time assumptions.
A nonce requires state or another mechanism preventing reuse according to the protocol.
A sequence number requires tracking expected sequence state.
Cryptographic validity and freshness are different properties.
A signed message can be authentic but stale.
A signed message can be authentic but already processed.
State matters.
A signature can include network identifier.
A signature can include intended recipient.
A signature can include transaction identifier.
The exact context fields depend on protocol design.
Domain separation distinguishes cryptographic uses of related data or keys.
A domain-separation label can identify the purpose of a hash or signature input.
The same bytes used in different protocol contexts should not necessarily produce interchangeable authenticated objects.
Cryptographic formats should be versioned when future changes are expected.
Version fields should be authenticated when changing them could affect interpretation.
Unauthenticated metadata can be modified.
If metadata changes security semantics, protect it.
Cryptographic parsing should reject malformed lengths.
Cryptographic parsing should reject unsupported algorithms.
Cryptographic parsing should reject unsupported versions.
Cryptographic parsing should reject invalid key encodings.
Cryptographic parsing should reject invalid signatures.
Cryptographic parsing should reject authentication failures.
Do not attempt to recover plaintext after authenticated decryption reports authentication failure.
Authentication failure means the ciphertext or associated data did not verify under the supplied key and parameters.
Authenticated decryption should succeed completely before plaintext is trusted.
Error handling should avoid oracle vulnerabilities.
An oracle reveals information through observable responses.
A padding oracle reveals information about decrypted padding validity.
Protocol error behavior should follow secure design.
Do not invent detailed external cryptographic errors without considering information leakage.
Internal diagnostics can preserve more detail under protected logging policy.
Passwords should be transmitted only through appropriately protected channels.
TLS protects passwords in transit according to the TLS connection.
These protections solve different stages.
Hashing a password in browser JavaScript does not automatically replace TLS.
The resulting hash can itself become a reusable credential if the server accepts it directly.
Credential protocol design requires more than transforming a password.
API keys are secrets.
API keys should not be embedded in public client-side code when secrecy is required.
API keys should not be committed to public repositories.
API keys should be scoped according to least privilege when the provider supports scope.
API keys should be rotated after confirmed exposure according to policy.
Tokens can be bearer credentials.
Possession of a bearer token can grant its defined authority.
Bearer tokens therefore require protection.
JWT is a token format and framework.
JWT can be signed.
JWT can be encrypted using applicable JOSE mechanisms.
A common signed JWT is not encrypted.
Base64url-encoded JWT fields are readable.
JWT validation should verify signature according to the expected algorithm and key.
JWT validation should validate relevant claims according to application policy.
Expiration can matter.
Audience can matter.
Issuer can matter.
Subject can matter.
Scope can matter.
Revocation or session state can matter.
Claims should be interpreted according to protocol.
Do not trust a token merely because it parses successfully.
Parsing is not verification.
Verification is not authorization.
A build can publish a SHA-256 digest.
A release can publish a digital signature.
The digest verifies exact artifact content against the expected digest.
The signature can authenticate the digest or artifact according to the signing-key trust model.
Together they can provide integrity and provenance.
Neither proves the software is free of defects.
A signed malicious release remains malicious if an authorized key signed it.
Software signing protects provenance, not correctness.
Reproducible builds allow independent parties to build software and compare resulting artifacts under defined build conditions.
Reproducible builds can strengthen confidence that published binaries correspond to source.
Reproducible builds require deterministic build inputs and environment control.
A signature and reproducible build answer different questions.
Reproducibility asks whether the artifact corresponds to reproducible build inputs according to the build process.
Cryptographic hashes are useful in content-addressed systems.
An object's identifier can be derived from its content.
Changing content changes the identifier according to the hash.
Content addressing can detect accidental or unauthorized modification when trusted references exist.
Content addressing does not establish access control.
Knowing an object's hash can identify it.
It does not necessarily authorize retrieving it.
Hash chains connect records cryptographically.
A record can contain the hash of a previous record.
Changing an earlier record changes its hash.
That change can invalidate later hash links.
Hash chaining does not physically prevent data modification.
An attacker controlling all records and trust anchors can potentially replace the entire chain.
Digital signatures can authenticate checkpoints.
Replication can preserve independent observations.
A Merkle tree hashes data hierarchically.
Leaf values represent data or hashes according to the construction.
Parent hashes commit to child values.
A Merkle root commits to the represented tree contents according to the construction.
Merkle proofs can demonstrate inclusion of data relative to a trusted root.
A Merkle proof does not prove the semantic truth of the included data.
It proves cryptographic relationship to the committed root.
Digital signatures do not create consensus.
Cryptographic primitives support integrity, identity, and verification within that system.
Do not attribute consensus properties solely to SHA-256 or signatures.
Proof of work is not encryption.
Mining is not encryption.
Hashing during proof of work does not decrypt anything.
A wallet address is not necessarily a public key.
Address construction depends on the system.
A private key is not an address.
A transaction identifier is not necessarily a signature.
Terminology must follow the protocol.
Cryptographic engineering requires exact terminology because similar-looking values have different security properties.
A key is not a password.
A password is not a salt.
A salt is not an IV.
A nonce is not necessarily random in every construction.
A certificate is not a private key.
A public key is not a certificate.
A signature is not a hash.
A signature can internally use hashing according to its algorithm.
An encrypted message is not necessarily authenticated.
An authenticated message is not necessarily encrypted.
A signed message is not necessarily secret.
A secret message is not necessarily signed.
A trusted source can still make mistakes.
Cryptography establishes defined mathematical properties.
When evaluating a cryptographic system, ask what property is required.
Ask what algorithm provides that property.
Ask where keys originate.
Ask how keys are protected.
Ask how public keys become trusted.
Ask what exact bytes are protected.
Ask whether serialization is deterministic.
Ask whether replay is possible.
Ask whether nonces or IVs can repeat.
Ask whether algorithms are current and approved.
Ask whether the implementation is maintained.
Ask whether errors are checked.
Ask whether verification occurs before trust.
Ask whether authorization occurs after authentication.
Ask whether old keys can be revoked.
Ask whether key rotation is supported.
Ask whether historical signatures remain verifiable.
Ask what happens if randomness fails.
Ask what happens if verification fails.
Ask whether failure leaves unsafe partial state.
Cryptographic design should fail safely.
Signature verification failure should reject the signature-dependent trust claim.
Certificate-validation failure should reject authenticated connection establishment according to policy.
Missing required key material should not silently disable protection.
Unavailable secure randomness should not silently substitute insecure randomness.
Unknown cryptographic state should not be represented as verified.
Unverified means verification has not established validity.
Invalid means verification was performed and failed according to the mechanism.
Unknown, unverified, and invalid are different states.
Preserve those distinctions.
Cryptographic algorithms should be treated as specialized engineering components.
Do not modify them casually.
Do not implement them from memory when a vetted library exists.
Do not remove validation merely to make an operation succeed.
Do not disable certificate checks merely to eliminate TLS errors.
Do not regenerate identity keys merely because another subsystem failed.
Do not replace trusted keys merely because verification rejected modified content.
Investigate why verification failed.
A cryptographic failure can be evidence of corruption.
A cryptographic failure can be evidence of wrong key selection.
A cryptographic failure can be evidence of wrong data serialization.
A cryptographic failure can be evidence of incompatible algorithms.
Debug using evidence.
Preserve failing artifacts where appropriate.
Record algorithm identifiers.
Record signature-validation result.
Record expected and actual digest where policy permits.
Record exact version and serialization rules.
Do not log seed material.
Observability must preserve confidentiality.
Cryptography should use secure randomness where the algorithm requires randomness.
These are not contradictory requirements.
Hashing is deterministic.
Signature algorithms can be deterministic or randomized depending on construction.
Encryption commonly uses nonce, IV, or random material according to construction.
Key generation requires secure randomness.
Protocol design must follow each primitive's requirements.
Never replace required randomness with determinism merely to make testing easier.
Testing can inject controlled cryptographic test vectors into test environments.
A test vector contains known input and expected cryptographic output.
Test vectors verify implementation behavior.
Known-answer tests can verify hash implementations.
Known-answer tests can verify signature implementations.
Passing test vectors is useful evidence.
Passing test vectors does not prove an implementation has no side-channel or memory-safety defects.
Interoperability requires exact byte-level agreement.
Algorithm name alone is insufficient.
Parameters matter.
Padding matters.
Byte order matters.
Signature encoding matters.
Nonce format matters.
A signature represented as raw components can differ from one encoded using ASN.1 DER.
Both can represent related mathematical values but are not byte-identical formats.
Interfaces must specify representation.
PEM is a textual encoding format commonly used to represent cryptographic objects.
PEM commonly contains Base64-encoded DER data between labeled boundaries.
PEM is not encryption by itself.
A PEM private key can be encrypted or unencrypted depending on its format.
DER is a binary encoding based on ASN.1 rules.
File extension alone does not establish cryptographic object type.
Inspect format according to specification.
A key file named .pem is not automatically a public key.
A key file named .key is not automatically private.
Do not infer security properties from filenames.
OpenSSL is a widely used cryptographic and TLS toolkit and library.
Correct API usage matters.
Correct verification matters.
Correct memory handling matters.
Correct algorithm selection matters.
Libraries provide mechanisms.
Engineering determines how they are used.
Predictable is good.
Established primitives are good.
Explicit algorithms are good.
Explicit key ownership is good.
Explicit trust anchors are good.
Explicit failure is good.
Hidden cryptographic behavior is dangerous.
Custom cleverness is dangerous.
Silent downgrade is dangerous.
Ignored verification errors are dangerous.
Secret reuse is dangerous.
Nonce reuse can be catastrophic.
Weak randomness can be catastrophic.
Private-key exposure can invalidate identity assumptions.
Cryptography is not magic.
Cryptography is mathematics implemented by software under explicit assumptions.
If the assumptions fail, the security claim can fail.
If the implementation fails, the security claim can fail.
If authority policy fails, cryptographic validity can still permit unauthorized behavior.
Authenticate before authorization.
Authorize before protected action.
Preserve exact bytes when signatures depend on exact bytes.
Preserve provenance when keys establish identity.
Preserve historical keys when historical verification requires them.
Revoke compromised authority according to policy.
Use cryptographically secure randomness where required.
Use established libraries.
Use defined protocols.
Do not confuse confidentiality with integrity.
Do not confuse integrity with authenticity.
Do not confuse authenticity with authorization.
Do not confuse hashing with encryption.
Do not confuse signatures with encryption.
Do not confuse certificates with authority.
Do not confuse a trusted identity with permission for every action.
Do not confuse mathematical correctness with secure implementation.
Do not confuse successful decryption with authenticated data unless the construction provides authentication.
Do not confuse a matching checksum with adversarial integrity.
Do not confuse key length with effective security strength.
Do not confuse possession of a credential with legitimate authority outside the credential's defined scope.
Cryptographic systems should establish exactly the security properties they claim.
A compiler translates source code from one representation into another representation.
Compilation commonly transforms human-readable source code into lower-level code.
A compiler does not necessarily produce a complete executable directly.
A typical native build can include preprocessing.
A typical native build can include compilation.
A typical native build can include assembly.
A typical native build can include linking.
The exact build stages depend on the language and toolchain.
A toolchain is a collection of tools used to transform source code into executable or distributable software.
A toolchain can include a preprocessor.
A toolchain can include a librarian or archiver.
A toolchain can include a debugger.
A toolchain can include binary inspection tools.
GCC is the GNU Compiler Collection.
Clang is a compiler front end associated with the LLVM project.
MSVC is Microsoft's C and C++ compiler toolchain.
Different compilers can implement the same language standard.
Equivalent machine code is not required for equivalent program behavior.
Compiler choice can affect diagnostics.
Compiler choice can affect optimization.
Compiler choice can affect supported extensions.
Source code is the human-maintained program representation supplied to the language toolchain.
C source files commonly use the .c extension.
C++ source files commonly use extensions such as .cpp, .cc, or .cxx.
Header files commonly use .h or .hpp extensions.
File extensions are conventions.
The compiler mode and build configuration determine how a file is interpreted.
A translation unit is the source presented to the compiler after preprocessing according to C and C++ terminology.
The linker later combines required object files and libraries.
A header file normally contains declarations and definitions intended for inclusion by source files.
The C preprocessor processes preprocessing directives.
Preprocessor directives begin with # in ordinary C and C++ source syntax.
Preprocessing occurs before ordinary C compilation.
Function-like macros can resemble functions syntactically.
Macro arguments can be evaluated in unexpected ways when macro definitions duplicate their arguments.
A macro argument with side effects can be dangerous when referenced more than once by the macro expansion.
Parenthesize macro parameters appropriately when macros form expressions.
Parenthesize complete macro expressions appropriately.
Macros do not obey ordinary function type checking in the same way functions do.
Enumerations can represent integer constants.
const-qualified objects can represent values that should not be modified through that object.
Language features are often preferable to complex macros when they satisfy the requirement.
Conditional compilation allows source to include or exclude code based on preprocessing conditions.
Conditional compilation can support platform-specific implementation.
Conditional compilation can support compiler-specific behavior.
Platform-specific behavior should remain bounded when architecture requires platform independence.
Common behavior should remain common.
Platform-specific implementations can exist behind defined interfaces.
A header guard prevents repeated processing of header contents within a translation unit.
A traditional header guard uses #ifndef, #define, and #endif.
Repeated inclusion can otherwise cause duplicate declarations or definitions where not permitted.
A definition provides the entity according to language rules.
A variable declaration can state that a variable exists elsewhere.
A variable definition allocates or defines the object according to language semantics.
A function declaration describes a function interface.
Declaration and definition are different concepts.
A declaration can occur multiple times where the language permits compatible redeclarations.
An object or function requiring one definition must satisfy applicable language rules.
extern can declare an object or function with external linkage according to C semantics.
static has multiple meanings depending on context.
Within a function, static gives an object static storage duration.
Do not infer one meaning of static without considering context.
External linkage allows an entity to be referenced across translation units according to language rules.
Internal linkage restricts the entity to its translation unit.
No linkage applies to selected local identifiers.
Linkage is a language concept.
Linking is a toolchain stage.
They are related but not identical concepts.
Lexical analysis recognizes tokens.
Tokens include keywords.
Tokens include literals.
Tokens include operators.
Parsing analyzes token structure according to grammar.
A parser can build an abstract syntax tree.
An abstract syntax tree is commonly abbreviated AST.
An AST represents program structure.
An AST is not machine code.
Semantic analysis can include type checking.
Semantic analysis can include name resolution.
Semantic analysis can include constraint checking.
Syntactically valid code can still contain semantic errors.
A compiler diagnostic reports a detected condition.
A compiler error normally prevents successful compilation.
A compiler warning reports a condition that may deserve attention but does not necessarily prevent output.
A warning is not automatically a defect.
A warning should be examined.
Do not suppress warnings merely to obtain a clean build.
Fix the cause when the warning identifies a real problem.
Suppress a warning only when the condition is understood and suppression is justified.
Warning levels control categories or quantities of diagnostics according to the compiler.
Different compilers have different warning options.
Strict warning settings can reveal portability problems.
Strict warning settings can reveal suspicious conversions.
Strict warning settings can reveal unused values.
Strict warning settings can reveal questionable control flow.
Compiler warnings provide evidence.
They do not replace testing.
A compiler accepting source does not prove the source is correct.
A compiler rejecting source does not necessarily mean the intended algorithm is wrong.
Compilation correctness and application correctness are different.
Intermediate representation is commonly abbreviated IR.
A compiler can translate source into an intermediate representation.
An IR provides a form suitable for analysis and optimization.
LLVM IR is one example of an intermediate representation.
The compiler can perform optimization.
Optimization attempts to improve generated code while preserving required observable program behavior according to language rules.
Optimization can reduce execution time.
Optimization can reduce code size.
Optimization can eliminate unnecessary calculations.
Optimization can rearrange operations where language semantics permit.
Optimization levels control optimization strategy according to the compiler.
GCC and Clang commonly provide options such as -O0, -O1, -O2, and -O3.
Exact optimization behavior is compiler and version dependent.
-O0 commonly minimizes optimization and is useful for straightforward debugging.
-O2 commonly enables a broad set of optimizations.
-O3 commonly enables additional aggressive optimizations.
Higher optimization levels do not guarantee faster software.
Optimization can increase code size.
Optimization can expose undefined behavior that appeared harmless in an unoptimized build.
Do not assume the optimizer is wrong without evidence.
Debug builds commonly include debugging information.
These are project conventions rather than language requirements.
A release should be tested using the configuration actually intended for release.
Source-code instruction order is not necessarily machine instruction order.
Compiler optimization can inline functions.
Inlining replaces or integrates a function call with equivalent generated behavior according to optimization decisions.
The inline keyword does not simply command the compiler to inline a function.
inline also participates in language linkage and definition rules.
The compiler can inline functions not marked inline.
Constant folding evaluates constant expressions during compilation when permitted.
Dead-code elimination removes code whose effects cannot affect required observable behavior.
Common-subexpression elimination can avoid repeated equivalent calculations.
Loop optimizations can transform iteration.
Vectorization can use SIMD instructions for suitable operations.
Optimization should preserve defined program semantics.
Undefined behavior is commonly abbreviated UB.
Undefined behavior is not the same as an ordinary runtime error.
Undefined behavior means the language standard imposes no requirements for that situation.
Accessing outside valid array bounds can produce undefined behavior.
Using an object after its lifetime ended can produce undefined behavior.
Undefined behavior should be removed rather than depended upon.
Behavior observed from one compiler does not define undefined behavior.
Behavior observed from one optimization level does not define undefined behavior.
Behavior observed repeatedly does not make undefined behavior valid.
Implementation-defined behavior is behavior where the implementation chooses among permitted possibilities and documents the choice.
Unspecified behavior allows an implementation to choose among permitted possibilities without necessarily documenting the choice.
Undefined behavior imposes no requirements.
These categories are different.
Portability requires understanding which behavior the language guarantees.
Compiler extensions provide behavior beyond the language standard.
Extensions can reduce portability.
If ISO C portability is required, compiler-specific extensions should be avoided or isolated.
A compiler flag can select a language standard.
Examples include -std=c17.
Compiler support for a standard can vary by version.
Language standard and platform API are different.
ISO C defines the C language and standard library.
POSIX defines additional operating-system interfaces.
Windows defines platform-specific APIs.
A program using Windows APIs is not strictly platform-independent C merely because it compiles with a C compiler.
Separate language portability from platform portability.
After compilation, an assembler can translate assembly language into machine-code object representation.
A compiler can invoke the assembler automatically.
Developers do not always invoke the assembler directly.
Assembly language represents processor instructions and related directives.
