A C byte is CHAR_BIT bits.
Portable code should distinguish common platform behavior from language guarantees.
Structure padding can add bytes between members.
Structure padding can add bytes after members.
Padding supports alignment requirements.
sizeof a structure can therefore exceed the sum of member sizes.
Do not serialize a native structure by writing its raw memory unless the format explicitly depends on that exact ABI and platform representation.
Compiler, architecture, packing, alignment, and endianness can change raw structure representation.
Serialize fields explicitly for portable formats.
Alignment constrains where objects can be stored efficiently or legally according to the platform.
Misaligned access can be slower.
Misaligned access can fault on some architectures.
The compiler normally handles alignment for ordinary objects.
Manual binary parsing must respect alignment when converting raw byte buffers into typed objects.
Copying bytes into properly aligned objects can be safer than casting arbitrary byte pointers.
Strict aliasing rules constrain how stored objects can be accessed through different types in C and C++.
