A build timestamp and runtime timestamp are different.
A compiler version can be part of provenance.
Rebuilding identical source with a different compiler version can produce different binaries.
Semantic equivalence does not automatically mean binary identity.
Hashes establish byte identity.
Tests establish selected behavioral evidence.
A checksum verifies data integrity against an expected value.
A cryptographic hash can identify an artifact.
A digital signature can authenticate an artifact according to the signing trust model.
Compilation does not authenticate source provenance.
Successful linking does not authenticate a module.
Build pipelines can sign approved artifacts.
Runtime loaders can verify signatures when architecture requires it.
Trust policy is separate from compiler behavior.
Compiler hardening options can add runtime protections.
Position-independent executables can support address-space randomization.
