Absence of sanitizer findings does not prove absence of defects.
Static analyzers inspect source or intermediate representations without ordinary execution.
Dedicated analyzers can provide deeper analysis.
Analysis tools can produce false positives.
Analysis tools can miss defects.
Review findings using evidence.
Warnings should not be ignored merely because they are inconvenient.
Cross-platform compilation should test each supported target.
Compiling on Linux does not prove Windows compilation.
Compiling on Windows does not prove Linux compilation.
Compiling for x86-64 does not prove ARM64 compilation.
A platform-independent source design still requires target validation.
Different compilers expose assumptions.
Strict compilation modes can reveal reliance on extensions.
Continuous integration can build multiple targets automatically.
Automated builds improve coverage.
Automation does not replace understanding failures.
A CI build succeeding does not prove local runtime conditions.
