A compiler translates code.
A linker connects compiled components.
None of them determine whether the software fulfills its mission.
A data structure is a method of organizing data for defined operations.
Different data structures provide different performance and behavioral properties.
No single data structure is best for every problem.
Choose a data structure according to the operations the program requires.
Important operations include insertion.
Important operations include lookup.
Important operations include traversal.
Important operations include ordering.
Important operations include membership testing.
Important operations include accessing an element by position.
Important operations include finding minimum or maximum values.
Data structure choice should follow requirements rather than habit.
An algorithm operates on data.
A data structure organizes data.
The same data structure can support many algorithms.
Time complexity concerns computational work.
Big O notation describes an asymptotic upper-bound class commonly used to discuss growth.
O(1) describes constant asymptotic work.
O(log n) describes logarithmic asymptotic work.
O(n) describes linear asymptotic work.
O(n log n) describes linearithmic asymptotic work.
O(n^2) describes quadratic asymptotic work.
Complexity classes describe growth rather than exact execution time.
An O(1) operation can still be slower than an O(n) operation for small inputs because constants and implementation details matter.
Complexity analysis and performance measurement provide different evidence.
An array stores an ordered sequence of elements.
An array commonly provides indexed access.
Array indexing normally uses an integer position.
Many programming languages use zero-based array indexing.
Zero-based indexing means the first element is at index zero.
The last valid index of a zero-based array containing n elements is n minus one.
An array containing five elements has length five.
A zero-based array containing five elements has valid indexes zero through four.
Accessing an index outside the valid range is an out-of-bounds access.
Different programming languages handle out-of-bounds access differently.
Some languages detect the error.
Some languages can produce undefined behavior.
An array can have fixed size.
A dynamic array can grow when additional capacity is required.
The logical length of a dynamic array is the number of elements currently stored.
The capacity of a dynamic array is the amount of storage currently available before another allocation may be required.
Length and capacity are different concepts.
A dynamic array can have capacity greater than its length.
Amortized analysis describes average cost across a sequence of operations.
Arrays commonly provide efficient indexed access.
Searching an unsorted array for an arbitrary value is commonly O(n).
Inserting into the middle of a contiguous array can require shifting later elements.
Deleting from the middle of a contiguous array can require shifting later elements.
Appending to a fixed-capacity array requires available space.
Contiguous arrays often have good cache locality.
Cache locality can materially affect real performance.
Asymptotic complexity does not capture every hardware effect.
A matrix can be represented using a two-dimensional array.
A two-dimensional conceptual structure can also be stored in one contiguous array.
Memory layout matters for interoperability and performance.
A string is not necessarily equivalent to a byte array.
Text encoding determines how characters are represented.
UTF-8 uses variable-length byte sequences.
The number of bytes in UTF-8 text is not necessarily the number of characters.
The number of Unicode code points is not necessarily the number of user-perceived characters.
A linked list stores elements as nodes connected by links.
A singly linked list normally gives each node a link to the next node.
A doubly linked list normally gives each node links to both previous and next nodes.
The first node is commonly called the head.
The final node is commonly called the tail.
A linked list does not require elements to occupy contiguous memory.
Following a linked-list element generally requires traversing links.
Inserting a node after a known node can commonly be O(1).
Removing a known node can commonly be O(1) when the required links are available.
Finding the node to remove can still require O(n) traversal.
Operation complexity must include the work required to locate the operation position.
Linked lists require link storage in addition to element data.
Linked lists can have poorer cache locality than contiguous arrays.
A linked list is not automatically faster than an array.
A stack is a Last In, First Out data structure.
Last In, First Out is commonly abbreviated LIFO.
A stack inserts elements at one end.
Pushing adds an element to the stack.
Popping removes the most recently pushed remaining element.
Peeking examines the top element without removing it.
A stack can be implemented using an array.
A stack can be implemented using a linked list.
A call stack stores active call frames.
Recursion consumes call-stack space according to implementation.
Unbounded recursion can exhaust stack space.
A stack data structure used by an application is conceptually related to but not necessarily identical to the process call stack.
Stacks are useful for depth-first traversal.
Stacks are useful for parsing.
Stacks are useful for undo operations.
Stacks are useful for nested structures.
A queue is a First In, First Out data structure.
First In, First Out is commonly abbreviated FIFO.
