Dequeue removes the oldest remaining queued element according to FIFO behavior.
A queue can be implemented using a linked structure.
A queue can be implemented using a circular array.
Removing the first element of a simple contiguous array by shifting every remaining element can be inefficient.
A circular buffer can avoid repeated shifting.
A circular buffer uses storage positions cyclically.
Head and tail indexes identify queue positions according to implementation.
Index arithmetic can wrap around the storage capacity.
Queues are useful for work scheduling.
Queues are useful for message processing.
Queues are useful for breadth-first traversal.
A deque is a double-ended queue.
A deque supports insertion at both ends.
A deque supports removal at both ends.
A deque can behave as a stack.
The name deque is commonly pronounced like "deck."
A priority queue processes elements according to priority rather than simple insertion order.
A priority queue can be implemented using a heap.
A priority queue can return the highest-priority element.
A priority queue can return the lowest-priority element depending on its ordering definition.
Equal-priority ordering should not be assumed unless the implementation defines it.
A stable priority queue preserves defined relative order among equal-priority elements.
Not every priority queue is stable.
A set stores unique elements according to its equality semantics.
A set answers membership questions.
A set does not inherently require duplicate elements.
Adding an element already present in a mathematical set does not create another distinct member.
Set implementations can preserve order.
Set implementations can leave order unspecified.
Do not assume set iteration order unless the implementation guarantees it.
Set operations include union.
Set operations include intersection.
Set operations include membership.
Sets are useful when uniqueness matters.
A hash set can provide average-case efficient membership testing.
A tree-based set can maintain ordering.
Different set implementations provide different guarantees.
A map associates keys with values.
A map is also commonly called a dictionary or associative array depending on language and context.
Each key identifies an associated value according to map semantics.
Keys are commonly unique within a map.
Assigning another value to an existing key commonly replaces or updates the associated value.
A map is useful when data should be retrieved by a meaningful key rather than numeric position.
A hash table is a common implementation of a map.
A hash function maps a key into a hash value.
The hash value helps determine where the key is stored.
Collisions are expected in finite hash tables.
Collision handling can use chaining.
Collision handling can use open addressing.
Collision strategy affects behavior and performance.
A cryptographic hash function and a hash-table hash function serve different requirements.
A hash-table hash function does not necessarily need cryptographic security.
Hash tables commonly provide average-case O(1) lookup.
