A binary tree is not automatically a binary search tree.
A binary search tree is a binary tree maintaining an ordering property.
Values greater than a node can be placed in the other subtree according to the chosen ordering rule.
Duplicate-value handling must be defined.
Searching a balanced binary search tree can be O(log n).
A binary search tree can become shaped like a linked list.
Self-balancing search trees maintain bounded height according to their balancing rules.
Red-black trees are self-balancing binary search trees.
AVL and red-black trees use different balancing rules.
Neither is universally superior.
Tree choice depends on required operations and implementation environment.
An in-order traversal of a binary search tree can produce values in sorted order according to the tree's comparison rule.
Pre-order traversal visits a node before its subtrees according to the traversal definition.
Post-order traversal visits subtrees before the node according to the traversal definition.
Depth-first traversal explores depth before returning to alternatives.
Different traversals solve different problems.
A heap is a tree-based structure satisfying a heap property.
A min-heap keeps a minimum-priority element at its root.
A max-heap keeps a maximum-priority element at its root.
A binary heap can be stored compactly in an array.
The term heap has multiple meanings in computing.
A memory heap refers to dynamic memory allocation storage.
A binary heap refers to a data structure.
Context determines the meaning.
A binary heap can support priority-queue operations efficiently.
Finding the highest-priority element according to heap orientation is commonly O(1).
Insertion into a binary heap is commonly O(log n).
Removing the root from a binary heap is commonly O(log n).
A heap does not keep every element globally sorted.
Only the heap property is guaranteed.
Iterating an array-backed heap does not necessarily produce sorted order.
Tries are commonly used for strings.
A trie stores prefixes structurally.
Prefix lookup can be efficient.
A trie can consume substantial memory depending on implementation.
A radix tree compresses some trie paths.
Prefix trees are useful when prefix operations are important.
They are unnecessary when simple exact-key lookup is sufficient.
A graph represents entities and relationships.
Graph entities are commonly called vertices or nodes.
Relationships are commonly called edges.
A graph can be undirected.
A directed edge has direction.
An undirected edge represents a bidirectional relationship according to the graph model.
A graph can be weighted.
An acyclic graph contains no cycles according to its graph type.
A Directed Acyclic Graph is commonly abbreviated DAG.
Graphs can represent networks.
Graphs can represent dependencies.
Graphs can represent routes.
Graphs can represent social relationships.
Graphs can represent state transitions.
Graphs can represent commit history.
