A concurrent queue can allow safe producer-consumer operations.
Using a concurrent collection does not automatically make compound application logic atomic.
Use atomic collection operations when the logical action requires atomicity.
For example, "insert only if absent" should use an atomic operation when multiple workers can perform it concurrently.
Separately checking absence and then inserting can race.
Database unique constraints can enforce uniqueness across concurrent application processes.
Application-only checks are insufficient when another process can modify the same database concurrently.
Enforce important invariants at the boundary capable of observing all relevant writers.
File locking can coordinate processes.
All cooperating processes must follow the locking protocol.
A lock file and a file lock are different concepts.
Creating a lock file can implement a locking protocol when creation is atomic and ownership rules are defined.
Merely checking whether a lock file exists can race.
Atomic creation primitives should be used when lock-file designs require exclusivity.
Stale lock handling must be defined.
Process crashes can leave ordinary lock files behind.
Shared filesystem locking can have different semantics across filesystems.
Do not assume local filesystem locking semantics automatically apply to network filesystems.
Once independent machines communicate over a network, additional distributed-system problems appear.
Those problems include message delay.
Those problems include network partitions.
Those problems include independent clocks.
Those problems belong to distributed-system design in addition to concurrency.
Concurrency knowledge remains necessary.
A server can process multiple client requests concurrently.
Two requests can modify the same application state.
HTTP requests should not be assumed to execute sequentially.
Two users can submit operations simultaneously.
One user can submit multiple requests simultaneously.
Browser behavior can create concurrent requests.
Automated clients can create concurrent requests.
Application state must remain correct under concurrent requests.
A web session can experience concurrent updates.
Reading session state, modifying it, and writing it back can lose another concurrent update.
Session storage concurrency semantics matter.
A mutex protects only participants sharing that mutex.
A mutex inside one process does not automatically protect another process.
A mutex inside one server does not protect another server.
Choose synchronization at the boundary containing all writers.
If multiple processes modify one database, database-level constraints or transactions may be required.
If multiple machines modify shared authoritative state, local locks alone are insufficient.
Scope matters.
A lock has a scope.
A thread mutex usually has process-local scope.
An operating-system named synchronization object can have broader local scope.
A database lock has database transaction scope.
Never assume a lock covers participants outside its defined scope.
A critical section should protect an invariant.
An invariant is a condition that must remain true.
For example, an identifier may be required to remain unique.
For example, only one active owner may be permitted.
Define the invariant before choosing synchronization.
The goal is correctness, not merely preventing simultaneous execution.
Over-locking can reduce concurrency unnecessarily.
Under-locking can violate correctness.
Correct lock granularity protects required state while permitting safe independent work.
Coarse-grained locking protects large amounts of state with fewer locks.
Coarse locks are simpler.
Coarse locks can create contention.
Fine-grained locking uses more locks over smaller state regions.
Fine-grained locks can increase concurrency.
Increase granularity complexity only when evidence shows it is necessary.
Contention occurs when multiple participants compete for the same synchronization resource.
Contention can increase latency.
Contention can reduce throughput.
Lock wait time can be measured.
Queue wait time can be measured.
Thread utilization can be measured.
Measure contention before redesigning synchronization.
False sharing can reduce parallel performance.
False sharing is a performance issue, not necessarily a correctness defect.
Do not optimize cache-line layout without evidence that it matters.
If part of a workload cannot be parallelized, adding processors eventually provides diminishing returns.
Real systems also encounter memory bandwidth, cache, scheduling, and I/O constraints.
Ordering matters in concurrent systems.
Program order describes operations within one execution context.
Synchronization order establishes relationships between execution contexts according to the memory model.
Completion order can differ from submission order.
Observation order can differ among participants without synchronization guarantees.
If ordering is required, encode it.
Do not rely on timing.
Sleeping is not synchronization.
Adding a delay can make a race harder to reproduce.
Adding a delay does not establish correctness.
Use futures.
Use condition variables.
Use events.
Use defined protocol acknowledgments.
Synchronization should establish facts rather than guess elapsed time.
Joining a thread waits for that thread to terminate according to the threading API.
A successful join establishes that the joined thread finished.
Thread creation does not establish completion.
Starting work does not establish completion.
Submission to a queue does not establish processing.
Receiving an acknowledgment should mean exactly what the protocol defines.
An acknowledgment of receipt is not necessarily an acknowledgment of completion.
Define stages precisely.
Concurrent state machines should have explicit transitions.
Two workers should not both claim an exclusive state transition unless the system permits it.
Compare-and-set operations can enforce conditional state transitions.
Database updates can include current-state predicates.
The number of affected rows can provide evidence whether the transition occurred.
Conditional transitions reduce race windows.
Work claiming is a common concurrent operation.
Multiple workers can inspect available work.
Exactly one worker should claim a work item when exclusive processing is required.
Claiming should be atomic.
Selecting work and later separately marking it claimed can race.
Duplicate processing can still require idempotency when failures occur after claiming.
Concurrency correctness and failure recovery interact.
The system needs a defined recovery policy.
A lease grants ownership for a bounded period.
If the worker fails to renew or complete the lease, work can become eligible for another worker.
Leases require time semantics.
Leases can result in overlapping work if an old worker continues after its lease expires.
Fencing tokens can distinguish newer ownership from stale ownership in appropriate designs.
Distributed leases introduce distributed-system concerns.
Within one process, simpler ownership mechanisms are usually preferable.
Two threads can both observe uninitialized state.
Use language or runtime primitives designed for one-time initialization.
Singleton patterns do not automatically solve concurrency.
A singleton can contain unsafe mutable state.
Global uniqueness and thread safety are separate properties.
Reference counting tracks the number of references to a resource.
Concurrent reference counting requires atomic or otherwise synchronized count updates.
An incorrect reference count can leak a resource.
A resource should not be destroyed while another execution context can still use it.
Use joins, reference ownership, synchronization, or lifecycle protocols appropriate to the design.
Use-after-free defects can be concurrency defects.
One thread can free memory while another accesses it.
Synchronization must protect lifetime as well as data contents.
Protecting a field while allowing its containing object to disappear is insufficient.
Stop accepting new work.
Signal workers to stop.
Allow bounded completion or cancellation.
Join workers where required.
Flush required state.
Release resources.
Destroying synchronization objects while workers still use them is unsafe.
Closing shared resources while workers still use them is unsafe.
Shutdown order matters.
Startup order can also matter.
Do not expose a component as ready before required shared state is initialized.
Initialization should establish invariants before concurrent access begins.
Publishing an object means making it visible to other execution contexts.
Establish synchronization.
Safe publication ensures other threads observe initialized state according to the memory model.
Concurrent error handling should preserve the responsible operation.
One worker failing does not automatically mean all workers must terminate.
An optional worker failure can permit degraded operation.
Do not silently restart a repeatedly failing worker forever.
Bound restart behavior.
Log the original failure.
A worker crash loop can consume CPU and fill logs.
Supervision should detect repeated failure.
Backoff can limit restart rate.
A restarted worker should begin from defined state.
Do not assume abandoned in-memory work survived a worker restart.
Persistent queues can preserve queued work.
In-memory queues disappear with the process.
Sequential tests cannot establish all concurrent behavior.
A race can require thousands of iterations to reproduce.
Memory sanitizers can detect selected memory defects.
Deadlock detection can identify selected lock cycles.
Instrumentation changes timing.
A race disappearing under instrumentation does not prove it was repaired.
Tests should verify invariants after concurrent execution.
For example, run many increments and verify the exact final count.
For example, run simultaneous unique inserts and verify uniqueness.
Test the property that matters.
A concurrency test passing once is limited evidence.
Repeat tests.
Vary scheduling pressure.
Vary processor count where practical.
Vary workload.
Test cancellation.
Test resource exhaustion.
Test error paths while work overlaps.
Concurrency failures often occur during exceptional conditions.
Fault injection can test failures at controlled points.
A dependency can be delayed.
A queue can be filled.
A write can fail.
Observe whether invariants remain correct.
Fault injection should be controlled.
Do not perform destructive concurrency experiments against production systems without authorization.
Determinism and concurrency can coexist when externally required results are controlled.
For example, independent calculations can execute in parallel and then be combined using a defined ordering.
Sort data before serialization when canonical ordering is required.
Floating-point parallel reductions can produce different rounding depending on operation order.
If exact deterministic numeric output is required, define the reduction method.
Parallel optimization must not change required semantics.
A faster nondeterministic result is not acceptable when deterministic behavior is required.
Concurrency is an implementation technique.
It does not override the system contract.
Concurrency should not leak accidental ordering into public behavior.
Concurrency should not weaken validation.
Concurrency should not expand authority.
A worker has only the authority delegated to the operation it performs.
Synchronization protects state.
Authorization protects actions.
They solve different problems.
An authorization check does not synchronize shared state.
Both may be required.
Concurrency should preserve auditability.
When multiple workers log simultaneously, log entries should remain individually valid.
Logs can interleave.
Structured logging can preserve event boundaries.
Include timestamps where useful.
Include worker or request identifiers where useful.
Do not infer causal order solely from coarse timestamps.
Clock readings can have limited resolution.
Within-process sequence numbers can provide stronger local ordering when required.
Tracing can associate concurrent work across components.
Correlation identifiers can distinguish overlapping requests.
Without identifiers, concurrent logs can become difficult to interpret.
Observability is especially important in concurrent systems because execution paths overlap.
Shared counters used for metrics require thread-safe updates.
Metrics themselves should not introduce significant contention.
Approximate metrics can be acceptable when exactness is unnecessary.
Business state requiring exactness should not be treated like approximate telemetry.
Know which values require exact correctness.
Locking every metric update globally can damage performance.
Using approximate counters for authoritative financial or security state can damage correctness.
Requirements determine synchronization strength.
Priority inversion occurs when a high-priority task waits for a resource held by a lower-priority task while intermediate-priority work prevents the lower-priority task from running.
Some operating systems provide priority-inheritance mechanisms.
Priority inversion matters particularly in real-time systems.
Ordinary applications should not invent real-time assumptions.
Real-time concurrency has timing guarantees beyond ordinary multithreading.
Fast is not the same as real-time.
Low average latency is not a real-time guarantee.
Two sequential operations have limited ordering possibilities.
Many concurrent operations can have many possible interleavings.
Reducing shared mutable state reduces the number of problematic interleavings.
Reducing concurrency reduces reasoning complexity.
Do not make software concurrent without a reason.
Concurrency can improve responsiveness.
Concurrency can hide I/O latency.
Concurrency can use multiple processors.
Start with the simplest correct execution model.
Add concurrency where requirements justify it.
A single-threaded design can be excellent engineering.
A multi-threaded design can be excellent engineering.
The correct choice depends on requirements.
Do not equate asynchronous code with better performance.
Do not equate a mutex with thread safety.
Do not equate atomic variables with an atomic algorithm.
Do not equate concurrent collections with race-free application logic.
Do not equate sleeping with synchronization.
Do not equate worker activity with useful progress.
Do not equate lack of crashes with concurrency correctness.
Do not equate local locks with cross-process protection.
Do not equate process locks with distributed coordination.
Do not equate a timeout with a repaired deadlock.
Do not equate parallelism with unlimited speedup.
Do not equate nondeterministic execution order with permission for nondeterministic results.
Identify every participant that can access it.
Define the invariant.
Define the synchronization mechanism.
Define the lock or coordination scope.
Define failure behavior.
Define shutdown behavior.
Then test the invariant under overlapping execution.
When work can execute independently, avoid unnecessary shared state.
When exclusivity matters, enforce exclusivity atomically.
When queues can grow, provide backpressure.
When workers can fail, define recovery.
When shutdown occurs, coordinate lifecycle.
When deterministic output matters, isolate nondeterministic scheduling from observable results.
Correct concurrent software does not depend on luck.
Correct concurrent software does not depend on one observed execution order.
Correct concurrent software establishes the relationships required for correctness explicitly.
Concurrency is controlled overlap of work.
Synchronization establishes required relationships between that work.
The goal is to permit useful overlap while preserving required behavior.
An operating system manages computer hardware and provides services to programs.
An operating system is commonly abbreviated OS.
An operating system manages processor execution.
An operating system manages memory.
An operating system manages files.
An operating system manages devices.
An operating system manages processes.
An operating system manages users and permissions.
An operating system provides interfaces through which applications request system services.
Windows is an operating system family.
Linux is an operating system family.
macOS is an operating system.
Applications can hide operating-system differences behind platform abstractions.
Portable software should distinguish common behavior from operating-system-specific implementation.
The kernel manages hardware resources.
The kernel manages process execution.
The kernel manages virtual memory.
The kernel provides device access through drivers and system interfaces.
The kernel enforces many security boundaries.
User mode provides restricted execution for ordinary applications.
Kernel mode provides privileged access to system resources.
A system call transfers a request from user-space software to the operating-system kernel.
Opening a file ultimately requires operating-system services.
Network communication requires operating-system services.
Applications should not assume they directly control hardware merely because they requested an operation.
The operating system mediates access.
A process is a running instance of a program.
A program stored on disk is not itself a running process.
Starting a program creates or participates in creating a process according to the operating system.
A process has execution state.
A process has operating-system resources.
A process has an identifier.
A process identifier is commonly abbreviated PID.
A PID can be reused after a process terminates.
A PID is therefore not a permanent identity.
A parent process can create child processes.
Parent and child relationships depend on operating-system process semantics.
On Unix-like systems, process creation commonly involves fork and exec mechanisms.
fork creates a new process based on the calling process according to Unix semantics.
fork and exec are distinct operations.
Windows uses different native process-creation mechanisms.
Portable application logic should not require Unix process creation semantics unless Unix is an explicit requirement.
A process has an exit status.
An exit status communicates process completion information.
By common command-line convention, exit status zero indicates success.
Nonzero exit status commonly indicates failure or another defined condition.
Exact exit-code meanings belong to the program interface.
Do not assume every nonzero value means the same failure.
A parent process can inspect a child process's exit status.
Ignoring exit status discards evidence about execution.
A successful process start does not prove the process completed successfully.
A process can start and later fail.
A process can crash.
A process can be terminated.
A process can exit normally.
A process can become blocked waiting for a resource.
A process can consume excessive resources.
Process lifecycle should be distinguished from program installation state.
A process can contain multiple threads.
Threads can share global data.
Threads can share heap memory.
A race condition occurs when program behavior depends incorrectly on execution timing or ordering.
Thread scheduling is not generally deterministic.
A mutex provides mutual exclusion.
A mutex can protect shared state.
Only one thread should hold a particular mutex at a time according to normal mutex semantics.
A mutex does not automatically make an entire program thread-safe.
The correct shared state must be protected consistently.
A deadlock occurs when execution participants wait indefinitely for resources held in a way that prevents progress.
Two threads can deadlock when each waits for a lock held by the other.
Consistent lock ordering can reduce deadlock risk.
Holding locks longer than necessary can increase contention.
A semaphore maintains a count controlling access according to its synchronization semantics.
A condition variable allows threads to wait for a condition associated with shared state.
Atomic does not mean every sequence involving an atomic variable is itself atomic.
Concurrency correctness requires understanding the applicable memory model.
Threads are not processes.
Processes provide stronger memory isolation than threads under ordinary operating-system models.
Named pipes are an IPC mechanism.
Shared memory is an IPC mechanism.
Each IPC mechanism has different synchronization and lifetime properties.
Shared memory allows processes to access a shared memory region.
Shared memory requires synchronization when multiple processes modify shared state.
Sharing memory does not automatically make access safe.
Standard input is commonly abbreviated stdin.
Standard output is commonly abbreviated stdout.
Standard error is commonly abbreviated stderr.
Command-line processes commonly receive three standard streams.
stdin normally provides input.
stdout normally provides ordinary output.
stdout and stderr are separate streams.
A shell can redirect stdout.
A shell can redirect stderr.
A shell can connect one program's output to another program's input.
A pipeline connects commands through streams.
The shell and the operating system are different things.
A shell is a command interpreter and execution environment.
PowerShell is a shell and automation environment.
A terminal is not the same thing as a shell.
A terminal provides an interface through which interactive programs can communicate with a user.
A shell can run inside a terminal.
A shell can also run noninteractively.
An environment variable associates a name with a value in a process environment.
Processes can inherit environment variables.
Environment variables can configure application behavior.
Environment variables are not automatically secret.
Secrets placed in environment variables can be exposed through process inspection, debugging, logging, or inherited environments depending on the system.
Environment variables should not be treated as magically secure storage.
PATH ordering matters.
Executing a command by name can run a different executable if PATH resolves unexpectedly.
Security-sensitive software should understand how executable resolution occurs.
A working directory is the directory used as the base for relative filesystem paths.
Changing the working directory can change what a relative path references.
Services should not rely on an interactive user's working directory unless explicitly configured.
Paths differ between operating systems.
Windows commonly uses drive-letter paths such as C:\directory\file.
Path separators differ by platform conventions and APIs.
Do not construct paths by blindly concatenating separators when a platform abstraction exists.
A filesystem organizes stored files and directories.
A file is a named or otherwise referenced sequence of data according to filesystem semantics.
A directory contains filesystem entries.
A directory can contain files.
A directory can contain subdirectories.
A symbolic link refers to another filesystem path.
Symbolic links and hard links are different.
Deleting a symbolic link does not normally delete its target.
Hard-link behavior depends on filesystem reference semantics.
A filename is not necessarily a unique permanent identity for file content.
A file can be renamed.
A file can be replaced.
File metadata can include timestamps.
File metadata can include permissions.
Filesystem timestamps have different meanings.
Creation time, modification time, metadata-change time, and access time are different concepts where supported.
Do not assume every filesystem exposes the same timestamps.
Do not assume timestamp precision is identical across filesystems.
A file extension is a naming convention.
A .txt filename conventionally suggests text.
The operating system does not universally guarantee file content from the extension.
A file can contain binary data despite a .txt extension.
A file can contain text despite having no extension.
File formats should be validated according to their actual requirements.
Unix-like systems commonly represent open resources using file descriptors.
A file descriptor is an integer identifying an open resource within a process.
File descriptor two is conventionally stderr.
Windows uses handles and socket abstractions with different native semantics.
A file descriptor is not a filename.
A handle is not the underlying resource itself.
Closing a handle releases that process's reference according to operating-system semantics.
A process has finite resource limits.
Too many open files can prevent additional files or sockets from being opened.
Resource cleanup matters.
Virtual memory gives processes an address-space abstraction.
Virtual addresses are not necessarily physical memory addresses.
The operating system and hardware translate virtual memory according to the memory-management architecture.
Different processes can use identical virtual addresses referring to different physical memory.
Virtual memory provides isolation.
Virtual memory can permit memory to be backed by physical RAM.
A page is a fixed-size unit used by virtual-memory systems.
Page size depends on architecture and operating-system configuration.
A page fault occurs when memory access requires operating-system intervention according to virtual-memory state.
A page fault is not automatically an application error.
Some page faults are part of normal memory operation.
An invalid memory access can result in a process fault or exception.
A segmentation fault is a common Unix-like result of invalid memory access.
Terminology differs while the underlying class of invalid memory access can be similar.
A process address space commonly contains thread stacks.
Exact layout depends on platform and executable format.
The stack commonly stores function-call state and local automatic variables.
The heap commonly provides dynamically allocated memory.
A stack data structure and a process call stack are related concepts but are not identical abstractions.
A heap data structure and process heap memory are different concepts.
Dynamic memory in C can be allocated using malloc.
Dynamic memory in C can be released using free.
Calling malloc does not necessarily cause a new operating-system allocation for every call.
Memory allocators can manage larger regions internally.
A use-after-free occurs when code accesses memory after its lifetime ended.
A double free occurs when memory is released more than once contrary to allocator requirements.
A buffer overflow occurs when writes exceed a valid buffer boundary.
These are application memory defects.
The operating system can mitigate some consequences.
The operating system cannot make incorrect memory handling correct.
Address Space Layout Randomization is commonly abbreviated ASLR.
ASLR randomizes selected memory locations to make some exploitation techniques more difficult.
Data Execution Prevention is commonly abbreviated DEP.
DEP prevents execution from selected memory regions according to platform security mechanisms.
Security mitigations reduce exploitability.
Security mitigations do not replace correct software.
Memory permissions can distinguish readable memory.
Memory permissions can distinguish writable memory.
Memory permissions can distinguish executable memory.
Executable memory does not ordinarily need to be writable for ordinary code.
The principle of least privilege also applies to memory permissions.
An executable file contains machine code and metadata according to an executable format.
Windows commonly uses the Portable Executable format.
Portable Executable is commonly abbreviated PE.
Linux commonly uses the Executable and Linkable Format.
Executable and Linkable Format is commonly abbreviated ELF.
PE and ELF are different binary formats.
A Windows executable commonly uses the .exe extension.
Executable permission and executable format are different concepts.
A file can have executable permission but contain invalid executable data.
A static library contains code linked into a program during the build process according to toolchain behavior.
Windows dynamic libraries commonly use the .dll extension.
DLL stands for Dynamic-Link Library.
Unix-like shared libraries commonly use .so files.
A shared object is commonly abbreviated SO.
Dynamic libraries allow executable code to be separated into loadable components.
A host program can resolve exported symbols.
A host program can call compatible exported functions.
A library's presence on disk does not mean it has been loaded.
Successful loading does not prove compatibility.
An ABI is an Application Binary Interface.
An ABI defines binary-level interaction requirements.
ABI requirements can include calling conventions.
ABI requirements can include structure layouts.
ABI requirements can include symbol names.
ABI requirements can include type sizes.
ABI requirements can include alignment.
ABI requirements can include function signatures.
ABI compatibility matters when independently compiled components interact.
An API and an ABI are different.
An API defines a programming interface at the source or conceptual level.
An ABI defines binary interaction.
Portable module systems should isolate platform-specific loading mechanisms from common module semantics.
Windows can load DLLs using Windows loader APIs.
Unix-like systems can load shared objects using dynamic-loading APIs.
The common application behavior can remain the same despite different loader implementations.
A service is a long-running managed program.
Linux commonly manages services using service-management systems.
systemd is a widely used Linux service manager.
A systemd service is defined by a unit configuration.
systemctl controls systemd-managed units.
Starting a service requests that the service manager activate it.
Stopping a service requests deactivation.
Enabling a service normally configures it to start automatically according to systemd rules.
A service can be enabled but currently stopped.
Service state should be queried rather than inferred.
A daemon can run without systemd.
systemd is not the Linux kernel.
systemd is not the operating system itself.
A service manager supervises processes according to its configuration.
Supervision can restart failed services.
Automatic restart should be bounded according to operational policy.
A repeatedly crashing service can enter a restart loop.
Restart loops can consume resources.
Restart loops can hide the original failure under repeated secondary errors.
Logs should preserve the first relevant failure.
A service can run under a dedicated user account.
Dedicated service accounts limit authority.
A network service generally should not run as an administrator or root user unless required.
Root is the traditional Unix superuser.
Unix-like systems identify users using numeric UIDs.
Unix-like systems identify groups using numeric GIDs.
A process runs with security credentials.
Those credentials determine access according to operating-system rules.
Traditional Unix permissions include read.
Traditional Unix permissions include write.
Traditional Unix permissions include execute.
Permissions are represented for file owner.
Permissions are represented for group.
Permissions are represented for others.
chmod changes Unix permission bits.
chown changes ownership.
chgrp changes group ownership.
The permission string rwxr-xr-x represents different permission sets for owner, group, and others.
Read permission on a regular file allows reading file content according to applicable access checks.
Write permission on a regular file allows modification according to applicable access checks.
Execute permission on a regular file permits execution when the file is a valid executable or script according to system rules.
Directory permissions have different practical semantics from regular-file permissions.
Read permission on a directory allows listing directory entries according to Unix semantics.
Execute permission on a directory allows traversal or access through that directory according to Unix semantics.
Write permission on a directory permits modifying directory entries according to applicable permissions.
A process can be unable to modify a file even when the file appears writable if directory or additional security controls prevent the operation.
A process can be unable to create a file when the target directory is not writable.
The user running a web server determines the permissions available to web application code.
PHP running through a web server does not automatically execute as the filesystem owner.
Apache commonly runs worker processes under a configured service account.
Changing filesystem permissions changes the operating-system security boundary.
Do not make an entire application tree world-writable merely to fix one required writable directory.
Least privilege means granting only the permissions necessary to perform authorized work.
A web application that needs to update a data directory requires write access to that directory.
A web application that does not need to modify application code should not require write access to application code.
Separating writable data from executable application code improves security.
Unix permissions are not the only Linux access-control mechanism.
Access Control Lists can provide additional permissions.
Access Control List is commonly abbreviated ACL.
SELinux can enforce mandatory access-control rules.
AppArmor can enforce application security profiles.
A file can have apparently sufficient Unix mode bits while an additional security mechanism still denies access.
Diagnose the actual security layer.
Do not disable security mechanisms merely because access was denied without first determining why.
Windows uses Access Control Lists extensively for filesystem security.
Portable applications should express required access conceptually and let platform-specific installation logic configure it appropriately.
An installer can create directories.
An installer can establish required ownership.
An installer can establish required permissions.
An installer can create configuration files.
An installer can register services.
An installer can validate prerequisites.
An installer should not grant broader access than required.
Installation and runtime are different lifecycle stages.
Privileges required during installation do not imply the application should run permanently with installation privileges.
A privileged installer can configure resources for an unprivileged runtime account.
Privilege separation reduces risk.
A signal is an asynchronous notification used by Unix-like operating systems.
SIGTERM requests process termination according to signal semantics.
SIGKILL forcibly terminates a process and cannot be caught or ignored according to Unix semantics.
SIGHUP is historically associated with terminal hangup and is commonly repurposed by daemons for configuration reload.
Signal behavior depends on application handling.
SIGTERM and SIGKILL are different.
A process receiving SIGTERM can perform cleanup if it handles the signal appropriately.
A process receiving SIGKILL cannot perform application cleanup before termination.
Windows has different native process-control mechanisms.
Portable software should map common shutdown behavior onto platform-specific mechanisms.
A service should define graceful shutdown behavior.
Graceful shutdown can stop accepting new work.
Graceful shutdown can persist required state.
Graceful shutdown can close network connections.
Graceful shutdown should have a bounded completion time according to operational requirements.
A hung process may require forced termination.
Forced termination can leave partially written state.
Persistent state should therefore use designs resilient to unexpected process termination.
Atomic file replacement can reduce partial configuration files.
Write a new file.
Replace the old file atomically when the filesystem supports the required operation.
The exact durability guarantees depend on operating-system and filesystem semantics.
A successful write call does not necessarily mean data has reached permanent physical storage.
Flush or synchronization operations can request stronger persistence.
Hardware can also cache writes.
Durability requirements should be explicit.
A filesystem cache improves performance.
Cached data is not a second authoritative version of the file.
The operating system can retain file data in memory and later write it to storage.
Applications should not assume every file read causes physical disk access.
Applications should not assume every file write immediately reaches physical media.
Filesystem locking coordinates access according to operating-system and filesystem mechanisms.
Advisory locks require cooperating applications to honor them.
Mandatory locking behavior differs and is not universally available.
A lock does not replace atomic update design.
A process can crash while holding application state.
Application lock files can remain after a crash if they are ordinary files.
Stale lock files require defined handling.
Do not delete a lock file merely because it exists.
Determine whether it represents an active owner according to the locking design.
A socket is an operating-system communication endpoint.
Sockets can support network communication.
TCP sockets provide TCP communication.
UDP sockets provide UDP communication.
Unix-domain sockets provide local IPC on Unix-like systems.
Opening a socket consumes an operating-system resource.
Sockets should be closed when no longer needed.
A listening socket waits for incoming connection requests.
An accepted socket represents a connected peer according to TCP server semantics.
Listening and connected sockets have different roles.
A timeout means the expected operation did not complete within the configured time.
The network may be delayed.
The remote operation may have completed while its response was lost.
Timeout handling should respect operation semantics.
Retrying a read can be safe according to protocol.
Retrying a state-changing request can duplicate the operation unless the protocol provides idempotency.
The operating system can report errors through platform-specific error mechanisms.
Unix-like APIs commonly use errno for many failures.
Windows APIs commonly provide Windows error codes.
Winsock uses socket-specific error reporting conventions.
Error numbers from one operating system should not be interpreted using another operating system's definitions.
Platform abstraction should translate native failures into common application error categories when appropriate.
Do not discard native error information needed for diagnosis.
An error code is evidence.
An error message is diagnostic information.
If an operation reports timeout, report timeout unless additional evidence establishes the cause.
If an operation reports permission denied, investigate permissions and security policy.
If an operation reports connection refused, a network path reached a rejecting endpoint or equivalent condition according to network semantics.
Connection refused and timeout are different.
Invalid argument and unsupported operation are different.
Logging records operational information.
Operating systems provide logging mechanisms.
Linux services managed by systemd can write to the journal.
journalctl reads systemd journal data.
Applications can maintain their own log files.
Windows provides Event Log facilities.
Application logs and operating-system logs provide different evidence.
Logs should include timestamps.
Logs should identify meaningful state transitions.
Logs should identify failures.
Logs should avoid exposing secrets.
Logs should not contain secret authentication tokens.
A long-running service should have a bounded logging strategy.
Disk exhaustion can become an availability failure.
Operating systems enforce resource limits.
A process can have file-descriptor limits.
A process can have memory limits.
A process can have CPU scheduling constraints.
A service manager can impose resource controls.
Containers can impose resource limits.
Applications should behave predictably when resource allocation fails.
malloc can fail.
File open can fail.
Thread creation can fail.
Process creation can fail.
Disk writes can fail.
Do not assume operating-system resources are unlimited.
CPU stands for Central Processing Unit.
A core can support one or more hardware execution threads depending on processor architecture.
Operating-system threads are scheduled onto available processor execution resources.
A single-threaded CPU-bound process generally executes on one logical processor at a time.
Multiple threads can execute concurrently on multiple cores.
Concurrency and parallelism are different concepts.
Concurrency means multiple tasks can make progress over overlapping periods.
A concurrent program is not necessarily parallel.
A parallel program is concurrent.
More threads do not automatically improve performance.
Workload determines useful parallelism.
CPU usage is a measured resource value.
CPU percentages depend on measurement convention and number of logical processors.
I/O-bound programs can use little CPU while performing substantial work.
CPU-bound programs spend substantial time performing computation.
I/O-bound programs spend substantial time waiting for input or output.
Optimization should identify the actual bottleneck.
Adding memory does not automatically solve a network latency problem.
Memory usage provides evidence.
CPU usage provides evidence.
Network I/O provides evidence.
Open handles provide evidence.
Process state provides evidence.
A scheduler decides which runnable execution context receives processor time.
Scheduling policy belongs to the operating system.
Application priority can influence scheduling.
Priority does not guarantee immediate execution.
Real-time scheduling has stronger timing requirements and different policies.
A clock measures time.
Operating systems expose multiple clock types.
Wall-clock time represents civil time.
Monotonic time measures elapsed progression unaffected by ordinary wall-clock adjustments.
Timeout measurement should generally use a monotonic clock when available.
Wall-clock time can change.
A user can change system time.
Daylight-saving transitions affect civil time representation.
Elapsed-time calculations should not assume wall-clock time always increases smoothly.
UTC is a time standard commonly used for machine timestamps.
Local time applies timezone rules to represent civil time.
Store and compare time according to application requirements.
A timestamp without timezone context can be ambiguous.
Operating systems expose clocks through platform APIs.
Portable code should use an appropriate abstraction when clock semantics must remain consistent.
A boot is the process of starting the operating system.
Firmware initializes hardware before or during the boot process according to platform architecture.
BIOS is an older firmware interface.
UEFI is a modern firmware interface.
UEFI stands for Unified Extensible Firmware Interface.
The bootloader loads or starts the operating-system kernel according to platform design.
User-space initialization starts system services.
Boot stages should not be confused.
A bootloader is not the kernel.
The kernel is not systemd.
systemd is not the shell.
The shell is not the terminal.
The terminal is not the operating system.
Keeping layers distinct makes debugging easier.
A device driver allows the operating system to control hardware according to the device interface.
Drivers commonly execute with elevated privilege.
A defective kernel-mode driver can destabilize the entire operating system.
User-space application failure is normally more isolated.
Different operating systems expose hardware information differently.
Portable hardware-detection logic should separate common classification from platform-specific enumeration.
A GPU is a Graphics Processing Unit.
A GPU being present does not mean a particular compute API is available.
A driver being installed does not prove a workload can execute successfully.
Hardware detection, capability detection, and successful execution are different stages.
USB stands for Universal Serial Bus.
USB devices are enumerated by the operating system.
USB device identity can include vendor and product identifiers.
A USB device's presence does not establish its intended application role.
Application logic should qualify devices according to required capabilities.
Storage devices are exposed through operating-system block or filesystem abstractions.
A block device provides addressable blocks according to storage interfaces.
A filesystem provides files and directories above storage.
Formatting creates a filesystem or storage structure according to the selected tool and format.
Mounting attaches a filesystem into the accessible filesystem namespace on Unix-like systems.
Windows commonly exposes mounted volumes through drive letters or mount points.
A mounted filesystem is not necessarily local physical storage.
Filesystem semantics can differ.
Atomic operations on one filesystem may not have identical guarantees on another.
Portable software should not assume every filesystem behaves identically.
Case sensitivity differs among filesystems.
Linux filesystems commonly distinguish filename case.
Windows filesystems commonly provide case-insensitive filename lookup by default while preserving case.
file.txt and File.txt can be distinct files on a case-sensitive filesystem.
Line endings differ by platform convention.
Windows text files traditionally use CRLF.
Protocol serialization should specify line endings independently from local operating-system conventions.
Network protocols should not inherit arbitrary local newline behavior.
Text encoding is also independent of operating-system identity.
UTF-8 can be used on Windows.
Locale affects language and formatting behavior.
Locale can affect sorting.
Locale can affect numeric formatting.
Machine protocols should avoid unintended dependence on user locale.
A decimal point in a machine protocol should not become a comma merely because the host locale changed unless the protocol explicitly defines locale behavior.
Deterministic systems should control locale-sensitive behavior where it affects outputs.
Containers provide an additional isolation mechanism using operating-system features.
A container is not necessarily a virtual machine.
Containers commonly share the host kernel.
Virtual machines normally run a guest operating system using virtualized hardware.
A container image contains application filesystem and configuration layers according to its container system.
A container does not provide a separate physical computer.
Security assumptions should reflect the actual isolation mechanism.
Privilege escalation means obtaining greater authority than currently permitted.
Applications should not require privilege escalation for routine operations unless necessary.
Running as Administrator to avoid configuring Windows ACLs is usually excessive.
Correct installation should grant the runtime identity exactly the access it requires.
Service identities should be explicit.
File ownership should be explicit.
Privileged operations should be explicit.
Operating-system interaction should be bounded.
A program should know which files it owns.
A program should know which ports it may bind.
A program should know which subprocesses it may launch.
A program should know which environment configuration it consumes.
A program should know which privileges it requires.
Implicit operating-system dependencies make deployment fragile.
A platform abstraction provides a common interface over operating-system-specific implementation.
The Linux implementation satisfies that behavior using Linux or POSIX interfaces as applicable.
Externally required results should remain consistent where architecture requires consistency.
Platform abstraction should not erase meaningful native errors.
Platform abstraction should normalize behavior that is intended to be common.
Do not put Windows-specific behavior into common semantics unless it is actually a common requirement.
Do not put Linux-specific behavior into common semantics unless it is actually a common requirement.
Platform independence means common system behavior is not determined by one operating system.
Platform independence does not mean platform-specific code cannot exist.
Platform-specific code is often necessary.
Runtime detection can select platform backends where architecture supports it.
Build-time and runtime platform selection are different strategies.
A single source tree can support multiple platforms.
Separate platform directories can isolate native implementation.
Shared headers can define common interfaces.
The linker can select the required implementation during build.
A runtime loader can select implementations dynamically when required.
The correct design depends on deployment requirements.
Do not hard-code one operating system when runtime portability is required.
Operating-system APIs return evidence.
Check return values.
Check error codes.
Check resource limits.
Close resources.
Bound retries.
Protect shared state.
Use least privilege.
Separate common behavior from platform implementation.
Do not infer success from silence.
Do not infer file content from extension alone.
Do not infer process success from process creation alone.
Do not infer remote success from a local network send.
Do not infer durability from a buffered write alone.
Do not infer portability because code compiled on two machines.
Do not infer platform independence when observable results differ where they are required to match.
The operating system is an execution environment and resource manager.
Applications operate within the boundaries it establishes.
Small operating-system interfaces are easier to reason about.
Explicit resource ownership is easier to reason about.
Explicit platform boundaries are easier to maintain.
Explicit permissions are easier to audit.
Explicit process lifecycle is easier to operate.
Explicit failure handling is easier to debug.
The application defines its required behavior.
Correct engineering keeps those responsibilities distinct.
C++ is a compiled programming language.
C++ was originally developed as an extension of ideas and capabilities associated with C.
C++ and C are distinct programming languages.
Valid C code is not necessarily valid C++ code.
Valid C++ code is not necessarily valid C code.
C++ supports procedural programming.
C++ supports object-oriented programming.
C++ supports generic programming.
C++ supports functional programming techniques.
C++ is case-sensitive.
C++ source files commonly use the .cpp, .cc, or .cxx filename extensions.
C++ header files commonly use the .h, .hpp, .hh, or .hxx filename extensions.
A C++ implementation includes a compiler and supporting implementation facilities.
The linker combines object files and libraries into an executable or library.
A translation unit is source code processed after preprocessing.
A C++ program can consist of multiple translation units.
ISO C++ refers to standardized versions of the C++ programming language.
Portable C++ avoids unnecessary dependence on implementation-specific behavior.
The -std option can select a C++ standard mode in GCC and compatible compilers.
Returning zero from main conventionally indicates successful program termination.
Statements commonly end with a semicolon.
Blocks are enclosed by braces.
Comments can use /* and */ for a block comment.
An identifier names a program entity.
A definition provides an entity where a definition is required.
A variable is an object with a name.
An object is a region of storage associated with a type and lifetime according to C++ rules.
Every expression has a type and a value category.
C++ provides fundamental types.
bool represents boolean values.
char is a character and integer type.
short is an integer type.
int is an integer type.
long is an integer type.
long long is an integer type.
float is a floating-point type.
double is a floating-point type.
long double is a floating-point type.
void represents the absence of a value in applicable contexts.
std::size_t is an unsigned integer type suitable for representing object sizes.
The sizeof operator reports the size of an object or type in bytes.
Fixed-width integer types such as std::uint32_t are provided through <cstdint> when supported.
Signed integer overflow has undefined behavior.
Unsigned integer arithmetic has defined modulo behavior.
C++ does not automatically make arithmetic immune to overflow.
auto can request type deduction from an initializer.
auto does not mean that an object has no type.
constexpr indicates that an entity can participate in constant evaluation when its requirements are satisfied.
consteval requires a function invocation to produce a compile-time constant where the language requires immediate invocation.
A reference provides an alias to another object or function.
An rvalue reference is written using && in a type declaration.
A reference must be initialized.
A reference is not normally reseated to refer to a different object after initialization.
Pointers and references are different language mechanisms.
A pointer stores a pointer value.
The address-of operator & can obtain the address of an object where permitted.
The dereference operator * accesses an object through a valid pointer.
nullptr is the C++ null pointer literal.
nullptr should generally be preferred over integer zero or NULL for null pointer values in C++.
Using an object after its lifetime has ended can have undefined behavior.
An array contains a fixed number of elements once its bound is established.
Out-of-bounds array access has undefined behavior.
Built-in arrays do not automatically perform bounds checking.
std::string manages its own storage.
std::string knows its current size.
std::string is different from a null-terminated C string.
A C-style string is conventionally a null-terminated sequence of characters.
std::string can interoperate with C-style strings when required.
The c_str member function provides access to a null-terminated character representation of a std::string.
The lifetime of pointers obtained from a std::string must obey the string object's invalidation rules.
C++ provides namespaces.
A namespace groups names and helps prevent naming collisions.
std::cin is a standard input stream.
std::cerr is a standard error stream.
The insertion operator << is commonly used with output streams.
The extraction operator >> is commonly used with input streams.
std::getline can read a line from an input stream into a string.
<iostream> declares standard stream facilities.
<string> declares std::string.
<vector> declares std::vector.
<memory> declares standard smart pointer facilities.
<algorithm> declares many standard algorithms.
<filesystem> declares standard filesystem facilities in applicable C++ versions.
<thread> declares standard thread facilities.
<mutex> declares mutual exclusion facilities.
<optional> declares std::optional.
<map> declares std::map.
<unordered_map> declares std::unordered_map.
C++ classes define user-defined types.
The class keyword defines a class type.
The struct keyword can also define a class type in C++.
Class members are private by default when declared using class.
Class members are public by default when declared using struct.
The public access specifier makes members accessible according to public access rules.
The protected access specifier provides access associated with inheritance relationships.
Encapsulation combines data and operations behind a defined interface.
Encapsulation can prevent callers from depending directly on internal representation.
A constructor initializes an object.
A constructor has the same name as its class.
A destructor performs cleanup associated with the end of an object's lifetime.
A destructor is named using a tilde followed by the class name.
An object can have multiple constructors with different parameter lists.
Function overloading allows multiple functions with the same name when their parameter declarations distinguish the overloads.
The compiler performs overload resolution to determine which applicable function is called.
A default constructor can construct an object without caller-supplied arguments.
A copy constructor constructs an object from another object of the same type.
A move constructor can transfer resources from an eligible source object.
A move assignment operator can transfer resources during assignment.
The Rule of Zero recommends designing types so resource management is handled by members that already manage their own resources.
The Rule of Three describes the relationship among destructor, copy constructor, and copy assignment in resource-managing classes.
RAII stands for Resource Acquisition Is Initialization.
RAII associates resource ownership with object lifetime.
An RAII object acquires or represents a resource through its lifetime and releases it during destruction.
RAII can manage memory.
RAII can manage files.
RAII can manage locks.
RAII can manage sockets through suitable wrapper types.
RAII reduces the need for manually paired cleanup operations.
Stack unwinding destroys applicable automatic objects when an exception propagates through their scopes.
Destructors are therefore important to exception-safe resource management.
C++ supports dynamic memory allocation.
The new expression can allocate and construct an object dynamically.
The delete expression can destroy and release an object allocated by a compatible new expression.
new[] allocates an array dynamically.
delete[] releases an array allocated by a compatible new[] expression.
Using mismatched allocation and deallocation mechanisms has undefined behavior.
Manual new and delete are often unnecessary in modern C++ application code.
Standard containers and smart pointers can manage dynamic storage automatically.
std::unique_ptr represents exclusive ownership of a dynamically managed object.
A std::unique_ptr is normally not copyable.
A std::unique_ptr can be moved.
std::make_unique constructs an object managed by a std::unique_ptr.
A dynamically managed object owned by shared_ptr objects remains alive while qualifying shared ownership remains.
std::weak_ptr provides a non-owning relationship to an object managed by std::shared_ptr.
std::weak_ptr can help break shared ownership cycles.
std::make_shared constructs an object managed through shared ownership.
Shared ownership should be used when shared lifetime is actually required.
A raw pointer does not inherently express ownership.
A reference does not inherently express ownership.
Resource ownership should be clear at interface boundaries.
C++ supports inheritance.
Public inheritance commonly represents an is-a relationship.
Inheritance should be used when the modeled relationship warrants it.
Composition allows an object to contain objects of other types.
Composition is often preferable when the relationship is has-a rather than is-a.
A virtual member function supports dynamic dispatch.
Dynamic dispatch can select an overriding function according to the runtime type of an object.
The virtual keyword declares applicable virtual behavior.
A derived class can override a virtual function.
The override specifier asks the compiler to verify that a member function overrides a virtual base-class function.
The final specifier can prevent further overriding or derivation in applicable contexts.
A pure virtual function is declared using = 0.
A class containing an unimplemented pure virtual function is abstract.
An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly.
A base class intended for polymorphic deletion should generally provide an appropriate virtual destructor.
Deleting a derived object through a base pointer without an appropriate virtual destructor can produce undefined behavior.
C++ supports templates.
A function template describes a family of functions.
A class template describes a family of classes.
std::vector is a class template.
std::array is a class template.
Template code is instantiated for applicable template arguments.
A template is not the same mechanism as a preprocessor macro.
Templates participate in C++ type checking and language semantics.
A concept expresses requirements on template arguments.
The C++ standard library provides containers.
A container manages a collection of objects.
std::array stores a fixed-size contiguous sequence.
std::deque provides a double-ended sequence container.
std::list provides a doubly linked list.
std::forward_list provides a singly linked list.
std::map stores key-value pairs ordered by key according to its ordering relationship.
std::unordered_map stores key-value pairs using hashing.
std::set stores unique keys in ordered form.
std::unordered_set stores unique keys using hashing.
Container choice should reflect required operations and behavior rather than habit.
std::vector is often a useful default sequence container when contiguous dynamic storage is appropriate.
Standard containers manage their own storage.
Iterators can be invalidated by container operations.
Using an invalidated iterator can produce undefined behavior.
A range-based for loop can iterate through a standard container.
Using a reference in a range-based for loop can avoid copying each element.
Using const references can provide read-only iteration without unnecessary copies.
The standard library provides algorithms.
std::sort sorts an applicable range.
std::find searches an applicable range.
std::copy copies elements between appropriate ranges.
std::transform applies an operation while transforming elements.
Algorithms commonly operate using iterators or ranges.
Modern C++ ranges provide additional mechanisms for composing operations in applicable standards.
A lambda expression creates an unnamed function object.
Lambdas can capture values or references from their surrounding scope.
Capture behavior should be chosen deliberately.
Capturing by reference requires the referenced object's lifetime to remain valid when the lambda uses it.
C++ supports exceptions.
The throw expression raises an exception.
A try block defines code for which exceptions can be handled.
A catch clause handles matching exceptions.
Exceptions can propagate through multiple function calls.
Exception propagation performs stack unwinding.
Not every C++ project uses exceptions.
Error handling policy should be consistent within an interface.
std::exception is a standard base class used by many standard exceptions.
C++ also supports error reporting through return values.
std::optional can represent the presence or absence of a value.
std::variant can represent one value selected from a defined set of alternative types.
An interface should make failure behavior clear.
C++ has deterministic object lifetime rules for many scoped objects.
Automatic objects are destroyed when their lifetime ends according to language rules.
RAII uses deterministic destruction to manage resources.
Garbage collection is not the normal automatic memory-management model of C++.
A memory leak occurs when allocated resources become unreachable or are otherwise never released when they should be.
A dangling pointer or reference refers to an object whose lifetime has ended or an invalid location.
Use-after-free is an invalid access to storage after its lifetime has ended.
Buffer overflows can occur in C++ when unsafe memory access exceeds valid bounds.
Using C++ does not automatically prevent memory-safety defects.
Bounds-checked access is available through facilities such as the at member function of standard containers.
std::array::at provides checked element access.
Undefined behavior means the C++ standard imposes no requirements on the behavior of that operation.
Out-of-bounds memory access can have undefined behavior.
Dereferencing invalid pointers can have undefined behavior.
Data races can have undefined behavior.
Unspecified behavior allows one of several possibilities without requiring a particular choice.
Portable C++ distinguishes defined behavior from implementation-dependent assumptions.
C++ supports function overloading.
Operator overloading cannot create arbitrary new operator symbols.
Operator overloading should preserve understandable semantics.
The == operator represents equality comparison according to the applicable built-in or overloaded semantics.
The = operator represents assignment according to the applicable semantics.
Assignment and equality comparison are different operations.
The === operator is not a C++ operator.
C++ does not use === for strict equality.
The != operator represents inequality comparison.
The logical AND operator is &&.
The logical OR operator is ||.
The logical NOT operator is !.
The bitwise AND operator is &.
The bitwise XOR operator is ^.
The bitwise complement operator is ~.
Logical and bitwise operators are not interchangeable.
&& uses short-circuit evaluation.
|| uses short-circuit evaluation.
The -> operator accesses a member through a pointer-like expression according to applicable language rules.
The . operator accesses a member through an object expression.
The expression std::string uses the scope-resolution operator to identify string within namespace std.
C++ supports references in function parameters.
A parameter declared T& receives an lvalue reference to an object of type T.
Passing large objects by const reference can avoid copying when ownership is not transferred.
Passing by value can be appropriate when a function needs its own copy.
std::move performs a cast that enables move semantics where the target operation supports moving.
std::move does not itself move bytes or resources.
Code should not assume a specific value for a moved-from object unless its type guarantees one.
Perfect forwarding preserves relevant value-category information through generic interfaces.
C++ supports compile-time programming.
Templates can perform compile-time computation.
Concepts can express compile-time interface constraints.
static_assert checks a condition during compilation.
A failed static_assert prevents successful compilation.
Compile-time validation can prevent invalid configurations from reaching runtime.
Macros are not type-safe language functions.
Macros can cause surprising behavior through textual substitution.
constexpr variables and inline functions can often replace simple constant or function-like macros.
A conventional header guard uses #ifndef, #define, and #endif.
Templates are commonly defined in headers because their definitions must be available where required for instantiation.
The inline specifier participates in language linkage and definition rules and does not guarantee machine-code inlining.
Internal linkage restricts applicable names to a translation unit.
External linkage permits applicable names to refer to entities across translation units.
Namespaces organize source-level names.
An ABI can define calling conventions.
An ABI can define object layout.
An ABI can define symbol naming and name mangling conventions.
An ABI can define exception and runtime conventions.
C++ source compatibility does not guarantee C++ binary compatibility.
C++ ABI compatibility can depend on compiler, compiler version, standard library, build options, architecture, and platform ABI.
Changing the layout of a class exposed across a binary boundary can break ABI compatibility.
Changing function signatures can break binary compatibility.
C++ name mangling encodes information about entities into linker symbol names according to an implementation ABI.
extern "C" can request C language linkage for applicable declarations.
extern "C" is commonly used when exposing a C-compatible interface from C++.
A stable C interface can sometimes provide a simpler binary boundary between independently built components.
C++ classes should not be exposed across an ABI boundary without deliberately controlling compiler and ABI compatibility.
C++ standard library types should not be assumed binary-compatible across arbitrary compilers or runtimes.
A module system should define its binary contract explicitly.
Dynamically loaded modules must satisfy the ABI expected by their host.
ABI version validation can prevent incompatible modules from loading.
C++ itself does not define a universal operating-system dynamic-library API.
Dynamic loading commonly requires platform facilities or an abstraction layer.
Unix-like systems commonly use shared objects with the .so extension.
Windows commonly uses dynamic-link libraries with the .dll extension.
Platform-specific loading code can be isolated behind a common interface.
Platform independence does not require every implementation file to be identical.
Platform-independent behavior requires defined externally observable behavior to remain consistent where the system requires consistency.
std::filesystem::path represents filesystem paths.
std::thread represents a thread of execution.
std::jthread provides a joining thread abstraction in applicable modern C++ standards.
std::mutex provides mutual exclusion.
std::lock_guard manages a lock using RAII.
std::unique_lock provides a more flexible lock ownership wrapper.
A data race occurs when conflicting unsynchronized accesses meet the language's data-race conditions.
Mutexes and atomic operations serve different synchronization purposes.
Thread-safe design requires explicit synchronization of shared mutable state.
Immutable data can reduce synchronization requirements.
Concurrency does not automatically improve performance.
Thread creation and synchronization have costs.
C++ provides time utilities through std::chrono.
std::chrono provides clocks, durations, and time points.
System wall-clock time can change while a program is running.
Network programming commonly uses operating-system APIs or additional libraries.
TCP does not preserve application message boundaries.
C++ network code using TCP must define application-level framing.
A send operation does not imply one matching receive operation.
Partial reads and writes must be handled.
Connections can fail or close.
Network operations can block.
Timeouts can bound waiting behavior.
Protocol serialization should be explicitly defined.
Writing the raw bytes of a C++ object to a network connection does not automatically create a portable protocol.
Object layout can include padding.
Object layout can depend on ABI rules.
Byte order can differ across platforms.
Pointer values are not portable serialized data.
Virtual table pointers are not portable serialized data.
std::string's internal representation is not a portable serialization format.
std::vector's internal representation is not a portable serialization format.
Portable serialization defines the bytes independently of in-memory implementation details.
Deserialization must validate untrusted input before using lengths, indexes, or allocations derived from it.
Integer overflow should be considered when calculating buffer and allocation sizes.
Consensus-sensitive behavior should avoid undefined behavior.
Consensus-sensitive behavior should not depend on unspecified iteration order unless the protocol explicitly accounts for it.
std::unordered_map iteration order should not be treated as a portable deterministic ordering.
An ordered representation should be explicitly defined when deterministic ordering is required.
Floating-point calculations can have properties that make them unsuitable for some cross-platform consensus calculations.
Fixed-width integer representations can be easier to define precisely for deterministic protocols.
C++ exceptions crossing arbitrary module or ABI boundaries can create compatibility problems.
Memory allocated by one runtime should not automatically be freed by an incompatible runtime.
Ownership across binary boundaries should be explicitly specified.
An ABI should define who allocates and who releases transferred resources.
Opaque handles can hide C++ implementation details behind a stable interface.
A C-compatible ABI can use opaque pointers or handles to represent internal C++ objects.
Implementation details can remain private behind an ABI boundary.
A compiler warning identifies a condition the compiler considers noteworthy.
Warnings do not necessarily indicate an error.
Warnings should be reviewed rather than ignored automatically.
-Wall enables a useful set of warnings in GCC and compatible C++ compilers.
-Wextra enables additional warnings.
-Wpedantic requests diagnostics concerning strict language conformance.
Treating warnings as errors can be useful in controlled build environments.
A warning-free build does not prove that software is correct.
Tests provide evidence about tested behavior.
A unit test exercises a bounded unit of functionality.
A regression test protects previously established behavior.
A negative test verifies correct handling of invalid or prohibited conditions.
Tests should be deterministic when the behavior being tested is deterministic.
A passing test does not prove that every possible defect is absent.
Static analysis examines source or program properties without ordinary execution.
AddressSanitizer can detect classes of memory errors in supported compiler environments.
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer can detect classes of undefined behavior in supported compiler environments.
Code review complements automated testing.
Resource ownership should be visible from interfaces where practical.
RAII should be preferred for resource lifetime management where appropriate.
Raw owning pointers should be avoided when an ownership type expresses the requirement more clearly.
Global mutable state can make systems harder to test and reason about.
Small interfaces reduce unnecessary coupling.
Small functions with clear responsibilities are easier to understand and test.
Dependencies should be introduced deliberately.
Additional libraries increase dependency and deployment considerations.
The standard library should be considered before introducing a third-party dependency for functionality it already provides adequately.
Correctness is more important than clever syntax.
Readable code is easier to maintain than unnecessarily complicated code.
Modern language features should be used when they improve correctness or clarity.
Modern C++ does not mean using every available language feature.
Simple code can still be modern C++.
A deterministic system should make important behavior explicit.
A deterministic system should explicitly define serialization.
A deterministic system should explicitly define protocol behavior.
A platform abstraction should isolate operating-system-specific behavior.
The common interface should define behavior rather than merely hide filenames.
Platform-specific backends should produce equivalent required results where platform independence requires equivalence.
C++ can be used for command-line applications.
C++ can be used for desktop applications.
C++ can be used for libraries.
C++ can be used for embedded systems where an appropriate implementation is available.
C++ can be used for performance-sensitive software.
C++ can interoperate with C through carefully designed interfaces.
C++ can provide higher-level resource-management abstractions while retaining access to lower-level facilities.
C++ does not automatically make a program object-oriented.
C++ does not require classes for every problem.
C++ does not automatically prevent memory corruption.
C++ does not automatically provide garbage collection.
C++ does not automatically provide thread safety.
C++ does not automatically provide deterministic behavior.
C++ provides language and library mechanisms from which those properties can be deliberately engineered.
RAII, standard containers, value semantics, smart pointers, and strong interfaces can reduce classes of resource-management defects.
A well-designed C++ program makes ownership and lifetime understandable.
A well-designed C++ system separates platform-specific implementation from platform-independent behavior where portability is required.
A well-designed C++ binary interface treats ABI compatibility as an explicit engineering constraint.
Reliability is the ability of a system to perform its required functions correctly under defined conditions for a defined period of time.
Reliability is not the absence of observed failure.
Reliability is not the same as availability.
Reliability is not the same as performance.
Reliability is not the same as durability.
Reliability is not the same as resilience.
These properties can interact.
Correctness describes whether behavior satisfies defined requirements.
Reliability describes whether required correct behavior continues under defined operating conditions.
Availability describes whether a system is able to provide a required service when needed according to the availability definition.
Resilience describes the ability to withstand, adapt to, or recover from failures.
A system can be available but non-durable if acknowledged data is lost after restart.
Terms should be defined before claims are made.
A failure occurs when a system does not provide required behavior.
A fault is a condition that can cause a failure.
An error is an incorrect internal state that can result from a fault.
Terminology varies between engineering disciplines.
Use the terminology defined by the system or governing specification.
Do not rely on ambiguous use of the word error.
Failures can originate in software.
Failures can originate in storage.
Failures can originate in networks.
Failures can originate in dependencies.
Failures can originate in resource exhaustion.
Failures can originate in invalid input.
Failures can originate in operator action.
Failures can originate in environmental conditions.
Reliability engineering begins by identifying possible failure modes.
A failure mode describes a way in which a component or system can fail.
A process can crash.
A process can hang.
A process can deadlock.
A process can return incorrect data.
A process can reject valid work.
A process can accept invalid work.
A dependency can become unavailable.
A dependency can become slow.
A dependency can return malformed data.
A network connection can fail.
A network connection can stall.
A message can be delayed.
A message can be duplicated.
A message can be reordered where the transport permits it.
A storage device can become full.
A storage operation can fail.
Configuration can be invalid.
A certificate can expire.
A clock can be wrong.
A machine can restart.
Power can fail.
Network calls can fail.
Storage operations can fail.
Process creation can fail.
Thread creation can fail.
File access can fail.
Name resolution can fail.
Authentication can fail.
Authorization can fail.
Dependencies can fail.
A system should define behavior for expected failures.
Failure handling should preserve required invariants.
A failed operation should not leave authoritative state partially modified unless partial modification is explicitly permitted.
A failed transaction should not be reported as successful.
A failed validation should not become accepted state.
A failed authentication should not create authenticated authority.
A failed persistence operation should not be reported as durable.
Failure handling is part of correctness.
A failure can be transient.
A failure can be permanent.
A transient failure can disappear without changing the request.
Temporary network interruption can be transient.
Unsupported protocol version is normally permanent until one participant changes.
Invalid credentials are normally permanent until credentials change.
Retrying a permanent failure wastes resources.
Not every transient failure should be retried automatically.
Retry safety depends on operation semantics.
An idempotent operation can be repeated without changing the intended final state beyond the first successful application according to its semantics.
A read operation is often idempotent.
Setting a value to a fixed value can be idempotent.
Creating an external effect can be non-idempotent.
Retries must account for uncertain outcomes.
If a request is sent and the connection fails before the response arrives, the requester might not know whether the remote operation occurred.
A timeout does not prove failure.
A disconnect does not prove rollback.
Unknown outcome should remain unknown until evidence resolves it.
Do not convert uncertainty into certainty for convenience.
Request identifiers can support deduplication.
Idempotency keys can allow repeated submissions to represent one logical operation.
Deduplication state must be retained long enough to cover expected retries.
Deduplication itself must be reliable.
Exactly-once effect is a strong guarantee.
Exactly-once effect requires a defined failure model and coordination mechanism.
A unique identifier alone does not provide exactly-once behavior.
At-most-once behavior attempts to avoid duplicate execution.
At-most-once behavior can lose operations.
At-least-once behavior can produce duplicate delivery.
Delivery semantics should be explicit.
Retries should be bounded.
Unlimited retries can overload a recovering dependency.
Retry count should be limited.
Retry duration should be limited.
Backoff delays repeated attempts.
Exponential backoff increases delay between attempts.
Jitter varies retry timing.
A retry storm occurs when many clients repeatedly retry a failing dependency.
Retry storms can prevent recovery.
A circuit breaker temporarily stops attempts to a dependency after defined failure conditions.
A circuit breaker can have closed state.
A circuit breaker can have half-open or probing state.
Circuit breakers are not a replacement for correct timeout handling.
Circuit breakers are not a replacement for capacity planning.
Circuit breakers are not a replacement for fixing a failed dependency.
They are one containment mechanism.
Timeouts bound waiting.
Every remote operation should have appropriate timeout behavior where indefinite waiting is unacceptable.
Read timeout bounds selected waiting for incoming data.
Write timeout bounds selected waiting for outgoing progress.
Request timeout bounds an application operation.
One timeout value is not necessarily correct for every stage.
A timeout should produce a defined state.
Resources associated with timed-out operations should be cleaned up.
Timeout does not automatically cancel remote work.
Cancellation semantics must be defined separately.
A cancellation request can race with operation completion.
Do not claim an operation did not occur merely because cancellation was requested.
Graceful degradation allows a system to preserve available capabilities when selected capabilities fail.
A required persistence subsystem failure can require rejecting operations that need persistence.
Degradation should follow architecture.
Do not invent degraded behavior during failure.
Degraded state should be visible.
A degraded system should not report full health.
A system can be operational.
A system can be degraded.
A system can be unavailable.
A system can be recovering.
Operational state names should have defined meanings.
A process running does not prove the service is healthy.
