When designing an allocation, determine who owns the memory.
Determine how long it must live.
Determine who can access it.
Determine who can modify it.
Determine whether ownership can transfer.
Determine whether ownership is shared.
Determine what happens if allocation fails.
Determine maximum size.
Determine whether the size can be controlled by external input.
When accepting a buffer, establish its capacity.
Establish whether it is mutable.
Establish how long it remains valid.
Establish whether the callee can retain it.
Establish whether ownership transfers.
Do not infer these properties from a pointer alone.
When returning memory, document whether the caller owns it.
Document how it must be released.
Document its lifetime.
Document whether it can be modified.
Document size separately when the representation does not inherently contain it.
When releasing memory, ensure the current component owns the release responsibility.
Ensure no required borrowers remain.
Release through the correct allocator.
Invalidate local ownership state.
Do not continue accessing the released object.
Do not assume aliases disappeared.
