Encoding changes representation.
Base64 does not provide confidentiality.
Hexadecimal is an encoding.
URL encoding is an encoding.
Hashing is not encoding.
Encryption is not encoding.
A checksum algorithm detects accidental changes according to its error-detection properties.
CRC algorithms are commonly used for transmission or storage error detection.
A checksum matching expected value provides integrity evidence against accidental corruption according to its model.
It does not generally provide protection against an intentional attacker.
Use cryptographic integrity mechanisms when adversarial modification matters.
Two semantically identical structures can serialize differently if field order or formatting is not defined.
Hashing those representations produces different digests.
Consensus systems require particularly careful canonicalization.
All participants should hash the same bytes for the same logical object.
Platform-dependent structure padding must not affect canonical data.
Local pointer values must not affect canonical data.
