Ignoring return values discards evidence.
A function reporting failure should not have its output consumed as though success occurred.
Error handling is part of the algorithm.
Cleanup is part of the algorithm.
A robust algorithm defines what happens when operations fail.
If a file read fails, define behavior.
If a network send is partial, define behavior.
If a database transaction fails, define behavior.
If a module rejects input, define behavior.
Success paths alone do not define a complete algorithm.
Resource cleanup should occur on failure paths.
A failed algorithm should not leak memory.
A failed algorithm should not leak file handles.
A failed algorithm should not leave locks held.
A failed algorithm should not leave corrupted state.
Structured cleanup can simplify error handling.
C code often uses a cleanup path to release acquired resources.
Correct cleanup is more important than stylistic purity.
Algorithms that modify state should consider atomicity.
Atomicity means an operation appears complete or not applied according to the defined observer and mechanism.
A database transaction can provide atomicity for database changes.
