Compare-and-swap is commonly abbreviated CAS.
CAS can support concurrent algorithms.
CAS algorithms require careful memory-order reasoning.
Do not use low-level atomic algorithms unnecessarily.
A mutex is often simpler and safer.
Algorithm sophistication should be justified by requirements.
Cached results can become incorrect when input dependencies change.
A pure function depends only on its explicit inputs and produces no externally visible side effects according to the functional model.
