A function reading a mutable global is not pure.
A function performing network I/O is not pure.
Pure components are easier to test deterministically.
Not every function needs to be pure.
Side effects are necessary for I/O and state changes.
Separate calculation from side effects where useful.
A pipeline processes data through ordered stages.
Each stage consumes input and produces output.
Pipelines make boundaries explicit.
A pipeline should not report final success when an earlier required stage failed.
A later stage should not execute on invalid earlier output unless recovery rules explicitly permit it.
Module discovery, validation, qualification, loading, initialization, and activation form a pipeline when architecture defines them that way.
Lesson ingestion can form a pipeline.
Locate lesson.
Read lesson.
Parse units.
Validate units.
Store units.
A failure in one stage should not be reported as failure in another stage.
An ingestion algorithm should reconcile counts.
Processed units should equal stored units plus rejected units when those are the only possible outcomes.
If processed is 195, stored is 195, and rejected is 0, the arithmetic reconciles.
This does not prove semantic quality.
Counting and correctness are different.
A retrieval pipeline can normalize a question.
It can classify intent.
It can search indexes.
It can retrieve candidate facts.
It can rank facts.
It can filter by authority.
It can generate a response.
It can validate response grounding according to architecture.
Each stage should have a defined contract.
A response generator should not answer from unrelated evidence merely because some context exists.
An algorithm should optimize for the requested objective.
If the objective is current ABI, historical ABI progression is secondary.
If the objective is ABI history, historical progression is primary.
If the objective is compatibility, supported-version rules are primary.
The same data can support different algorithms depending on the question.
Question interpretation is part of correct computation.
A decision tree represents decisions through branches.
Each internal decision evaluates a condition.
Each branch represents an outcome.
Leaves represent results or actions.
Decision trees can make bounded policy logic explicit.
Decision trees can become difficult to maintain when rules overlap heavily.
Tables can sometimes represent policy more clearly.
A lookup table maps defined input categories to defined output behavior.
Lookup tables can replace long chains of conditionals when behavior is truly tabular.
Choose representation according to the rule structure.
Rule ordering matters when multiple rules can match.
First-match algorithms select the first applicable rule.
Best-match algorithms select according to specificity or score.
All-match algorithms apply every applicable rule.
The intended semantics should be explicit.
Firewall rules, routing rules, and policy rules can use different matching semantics.
