Maximum collection size should be defined where required.
Maximum recursion depth should be defined where required.
Maximum retry count should be defined where required.
Maximum message size should be defined.
Maximum lesson size can be defined.
Maximum response context can be defined.
Bounds improve predictability.
Bounds should derive from operational requirements.
Arbitrary bounds can reject legitimate work.
No bounds can permit resource exhaustion.
Zero elements is a valid collection size.
Zero matches is a valid search result.
Zero-byte input can be valid or invalid according to specification.
Zero affected database rows can be valid.
Zero active modules can be valid during startup or configuration.
Zero is not automatically an error.
Algorithms should handle maximum supported values.
Boundary cases should be deliberate.
Empty state is still state.
An empty list is not NULL.
An empty string is not NULL.
A zero-length buffer can have different semantics from no buffer.
Absence and emptiness should remain distinct when the interface distinguishes them.
A sentinel value is a special value representing control information.
Sentinel values can terminate sequences.
Sentinel values can represent absence.
Using -1 as "not found" works only when -1 cannot be a valid result according to the interface.
Structured result types can avoid sentinel ambiguity.
In C, separate status and output parameters can distinguish success from returned data.
Algorithms should preserve provenance when provenance matters.
