TLS protects them in transit.
Secure cookie configuration reduces selected exposure.
Application security reduces script-based theft.
Server-side lifecycle controls limit duration.
Logout invalidation limits continued use.
No single control eliminates every possibility.
Deleting a browser cookie without invalidating the server-side session can leave the session usable if the identifier was copied elsewhere.
Server-side sessions should be invalidated where required.
Token-based systems require their own invalidation or expiration strategy.
Do not assume removing the local token destroys every copy.
Unauthenticated.
Authentication attempt.
Authenticated.
Session active.
Session expired or invalidated.
Logged out.
Possibly reauthenticated.
Transitions should be explicit.
Do not allow ambiguous state to grant access.
A failed authentication attempt should remain unauthenticated.
Authentication failure should not create partially trusted identity.
Do not populate privileged session state before credential verification completes.
Authenticate first.
Then establish authenticated state.
