Software testing evaluates whether software behaves according to defined expectations.
A test provides evidence about behavior under tested conditions.
A passing test does not prove that software has no defects.
A failing test provides evidence that an expected condition was not satisfied.
Testing and debugging are different activities.
Verification asks whether a system satisfies specified requirements.
Validation asks whether the resulting system satisfies its intended use and requirements.
Testing can contribute evidence to verification and validation.
A test should have a defined purpose.
A test should have defined inputs.
A test should have defined expected results.
A test should have observable results.
A test without a meaningful expected result provides weak evidence.
A test should not silently convert failure into success.
A test should not report success merely because execution completed.
Execution completion and correctness are different.
A program compiling successfully does not prove that it works correctly.
A program linking successfully does not prove that it works correctly.
A program starting successfully does not prove that it works correctly.
A module loading successfully does not prove that the module works correctly.
A network connection succeeding does not prove that the protocol works correctly.
A database connection succeeding does not prove that a query is correct.
A query executing successfully does not prove that its result is correct.
A function returning a value does not prove that the value is correct.
Each claim requires evidence appropriate to that claim.
A test case is a defined set of conditions used to evaluate behavior.
A test suite is a collection of test cases.
A test runner executes tests and reports results.
A test fixture establishes state required by a test.
A test oracle determines the expected result against which observed behavior is compared.
A deterministic test produces the same expected result when executed under the same defined conditions.
Nondeterministic tests are harder to diagnose.
A flaky test passes and fails inconsistently without an intentional change to the tested behavior.
Flaky tests reduce confidence in test results.
A flaky test should be investigated.
Repeatedly rerunning a flaky test until it passes does not repair the underlying problem.
A test should fail for the behavior it is intended to test.
A test failing because of unrelated infrastructure provides weaker evidence about the target behavior.
Test environments should control relevant dependencies where practical.
Tests should minimize irrelevant dependencies.
A unit test evaluates a small unit of software behavior.
A unit is commonly a function, method, class, module component, or similarly bounded piece of behavior.
A unit test should isolate the behavior being evaluated where practical.
A unit test does not necessarily require complete system startup.
A unit test can test valid input.
A unit test can test invalid input.
A unit test can test boundary conditions.
A unit test can test error handling.
A unit test can test state transitions.
A unit test can test deterministic transformations.
Unit tests are useful for fast local feedback.
Unit tests do not replace integration tests.
An integration test evaluates interactions between components.
An integration test can test an application and database together.
An integration test can test a host and module together.
An integration test can test a client and server together.
Integration testing can discover problems that isolated unit tests cannot.
Correct components can interact incorrectly.
Interface assumptions can disagree.
Serialization can disagree.
Version expectations can disagree.
Integration tests evaluate these boundaries.
A system test evaluates a complete or substantially complete system.
A system test can exercise behavior through public interfaces.
A system test can include multiple services.
A system test can include persistent storage.
A system test can include platform-specific behavior.
System tests provide broader behavioral evidence.
System tests can be slower and harder to diagnose than unit tests.
Different test levels answer different questions.
An end-to-end test evaluates a complete operational path.
An end-to-end test begins from an external or high-level entry point and verifies the resulting system behavior.
An end-to-end test can verify a request from client through server to persistence and back.
An end-to-end test can verify a module from discovery through activation and behavior.
An end-to-end test can verify a transaction from submission through final acceptance.
A smoke test checks a small set of essential functionality.
A smoke test determines whether a build is sufficiently functional for deeper testing.
A smoke test is intentionally limited.
Passing a smoke test does not mean every feature works.
Terminology around smoke and sanity testing can vary.
The important property is the defined scope of the test.
A regression test checks that previously working behavior remains correct after changes.
A regression occurs when a change breaks behavior that previously worked.
A defect repair should often receive a regression test.
A regression test preserves evidence of the expected repaired behavior.
Without a regression test, the same defect can return unnoticed.
A regression suite should include important historical failures.
Historical defects can become future test cases.
Testing can preserve lessons learned from failures.
Negative testing verifies rejection of invalid, prohibited, unsupported, or unauthorized behavior.
Both positive and negative tests are important.
A parser should be tested with malformed input.
Authentication should be tested with valid credentials.
Authentication should be tested with invalid credentials.
Authorization should be tested with insufficient authority.
A module loader should be tested with a compatible module.
A module loader should be tested with an incompatible module.
A protocol should be tested with supported messages.
A protocol should be tested with unsupported messages.
Security boundaries require negative testing.
A system that accepts valid input correctly but also accepts prohibited input is not correct.
Boundary testing evaluates values at and around defined limits.
If a buffer permits 256 bytes, useful tests can include 0 bytes.
Useful tests can include 1 byte.
Useful tests can include 256 bytes.
Useful tests can include 257 bytes.
The exact boundaries depend on the requirement.
Off-by-one errors commonly occur near boundaries.
Numeric limits should be tested.
String-length limits should be tested.
Collection-size limits should be tested.
Message-size limits should be tested.
Timeout boundaries should be tested.
Date boundaries should be tested where relevant.
Version boundaries should be tested.
Boundary tests should derive from actual defined constraints.
Equivalence partitioning groups inputs expected to behave similarly.
Representative values can be selected from each partition.
Equivalence partitioning reduces redundant testing while preserving useful coverage.
A property describes behavior that should remain true across a class of inputs.
Sorting output should preserve the input elements according to the sorting contract.
Serialization followed by deserialization can preserve values according to the format contract.
A parser should reject input exceeding defined maximum size.
Generated tests still require meaningful properties.
Random input without meaningful assertions provides limited evidence.
Fuzz testing supplies generated or mutated input to discover failures.
Fuzzing is especially useful for parsers.
Fuzzing is useful for network protocol handlers.
Fuzzing is useful for memory-unsafe software.
A fuzzer can discover crashes.
A fuzzer can discover assertion failures.
A fuzzer can discover memory errors when combined with instrumentation.
The triggering input should be minimized where useful.
A reproducible fuzz failure can become a regression test.
A crash does prove that execution reached an incorrect or unsupported state when crashing is outside the specification.
Static analysis can identify suspicious code patterns.
Static analysis can identify some type errors.
Static analysis findings require interpretation.
Dynamic analysis can observe resource behavior.
Dynamic analysis can observe timing behavior.
Dynamic analysis can observe actual input-output behavior.
Code review is a form of human or assisted examination.
Code review can identify logic errors.
Code review can identify unclear assumptions.
Code review can identify security problems.
Code review can identify maintainability problems.
Code review does not replace execution testing.
Different methods detect different classes of defects.
Test coverage measures which code or conditions were exercised according to the selected coverage metric.
Line coverage measures executed source lines.
Statement coverage measures executed statements according to tooling.
Function coverage measures executed functions.
One hundred percent branch coverage does not prove correctness.
A test can execute code without meaningfully verifying it.
Coverage should not become the sole definition of test quality.
Assertions define expected conditions.
An assertion should express a meaningful requirement.
An assertion that always evaluates true provides no useful evidence.
An assertion against the wrong expected value can make incorrect behavior appear correct.
Expected values should derive from requirements.
A test should not derive its expected result using the same defective algorithm being tested when independent verification is required.
Independent test oracles provide stronger evidence.
Golden files contain known expected output.
Golden files are useful for deterministic structured output.
Golden files can become difficult to review when extremely large.
Updating a golden file merely because a test failed can hide a regression.
Expected output should be changed only when the intended behavior actually changed.
Snapshot updates should be reviewed.
Tests should not train developers to approve changes blindly.
A mock replaces a dependency with controlled behavior for testing.
A fake provides a simplified working implementation of a dependency.
Terminology can vary between testing frameworks.
Test doubles allow controlled dependency behavior.
Mocks can make tests too dependent on implementation details when overused.
Changing internal implementation should not require rewriting unrelated tests when behavior remains unchanged.
Tests should protect contracts rather than accidental implementation details.
An interface contract defines expected behavior between components.
Contract tests verify that implementations satisfy interface expectations.
A module ABI is an interface contract.
ABI compatibility can be tested.
A module declaring ABI 1.5 can be tested against a host requiring ABI 1.5.
A module declaring ABI 1.4 should be rejected when the host requires ABI 1.5 and no compatibility mechanism permits it.
The test should verify the actual rejection behavior.
Documentation text is not the runtime ABI contract unless architecture explicitly makes it so.
Tests should use the authoritative interface definition.
A module qualification test can verify manifest presence.
A module qualification test can verify manifest syntax.
A module qualification test can verify module name.
A module qualification test can verify version.
A module qualification test can verify required exports.
A module qualification test can verify activation.
Each stage should be tested independently where practical.
A module activation test should not report activation before initialization succeeds.
A hot-load test should verify the module is usable after loading.
A functional test should additionally verify the module performs its intended function.
Logs and behavior provide complementary evidence.
A module can function correctly while logging incorrectly.
API testing evaluates defined program interfaces.
An API test should verify valid requests.
An API test should verify invalid requests.
An API test should verify response structure.
An API test should verify authorization.
An API test should verify boundary conditions.
HTTP API tests should verify appropriate status codes.
HTTP 200 indicates successful HTTP-level handling according to server response.
HTTP 200 does not automatically mean application data is correct.
HTTP 400 indicates a client request problem according to HTTP application behavior.
HTTP 401 commonly indicates authentication is required or failed according to the application's HTTP authentication semantics.
HTTP 403 commonly indicates the request is understood but not permitted.
HTTP 404 commonly indicates the requested resource was not found.
HTTP 500 indicates a server-side error condition.
Tests should verify both status and relevant response content.
Returning HTTP 200 with an error hidden in arbitrary text can make automated testing harder.
Interfaces should expose machine-readable success and failure states where appropriate.
Database tests should distinguish connectivity from query behavior.
A database authentication test verifies that credentials are accepted.
A database authorization test verifies that required operations are permitted.
A query test verifies SQL behavior.
A constraint test verifies integrity rules.
A transaction test verifies commit and rollback behavior.
A database returning zero rows can be a passing test when zero rows are expected.
A test expecting zero rows should not fail merely because no records were returned.
Database tests should clean up their test data.
Test data should not contaminate production data.
A test database should be isolated where practical.
Test fixtures should create known state.
A test should not pass merely because an unrelated record happens to exist.
Known initial state makes tests reproducible.
Network testing should distinguish layers.
A DNS test verifies name resolution.
A TLS test verifies TLS behavior.
A protocol handshake test verifies application protocol compatibility.
A functional request test verifies actual service behavior.
Ping does not prove application health.
A successful TCP connect does not prove protocol health.
Tests should verify the layer relevant to the requirement.
Timeout behavior should be tested.
Connection-refusal behavior should be tested.
Unexpected peer closure should be tested.
Partial writes should be handled according to API semantics.
Malformed network messages should be tested.
Oversized network messages should be tested.
Wrong protocol versions should be tested.
Wrong network identifiers should be tested.
Wrong peer identity should be tested when identity validation exists.
Reconnect behavior should be tested.
Backoff behavior should be tested when required.
A reconnect test should verify that temporary failure can recover.
A reconnect test should also verify that permanent failure does not create uncontrolled retry loops.
Testing failure behavior is as important as testing normal operation.
Filesystem testing should verify successful reads.
Filesystem testing should verify missing files.
Filesystem testing should verify malformed files.
Filesystem testing should verify maximum supported file sizes.
Filesystem testing should verify path handling.
A writable directory should be tested using the identity that actually performs the write.
Root being able to write does not prove a web-service user can write.
The interactive developer account being able to write does not prove the service account can write.
Permissions should be tested under the operational identity.
A test executed under the wrong identity can produce misleading results.
Code compiling on Windows does not prove it compiles on Linux.
Code compiling on Linux does not prove it compiles on Windows.
Platform-independent architecture requires platform-specific implementation testing.
The same protocol input should produce equivalent protocol-visible results across supported platforms where the specification requires equivalence.
Serialization should be tested across platforms.
Integer widths should be tested.
Byte order should be tested.
File-path assumptions should be tested.
Line endings should be tested where relevant.
Socket behavior should be tested.
Platform abstraction should be tested at its boundary.
Common code should not silently depend on one platform's implementation behavior.
Cross-platform tests provide evidence that abstraction actually works.
A clean build removes dependence on stale build artifacts.
Incremental builds are useful during development.
A clean build provides stronger evidence of build reproducibility.
A build succeeding because an old object file remained is not evidence that current source compiles completely.
Build directories should be cleaned when clean-build evidence is required.
Compiler warnings should be reviewed.
A warning-free build can be a test requirement.
A warning-free build does not prove correctness.
Testing with multiple compilers can improve portability evidence.
Compiler optimization can expose undefined behavior differently.
Sanitizers instrument programs to detect classes of runtime errors.
AddressSanitizer can detect many memory errors.
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer can detect many forms of undefined behavior.
ThreadSanitizer can detect many data races.
A sanitizer not reporting an error does not prove absence of all memory defects.
A sanitizer finding should be investigated.
Memory leak detectors can identify unreleased allocations.
Leak testing should account for deliberate process-lifetime allocations when architecture permits them.
A leak growing with repeated operations is especially important.
Resource testing should include memory.
Resource testing should include sockets.
Resource testing should include threads.
Resource testing should include handles on platforms that use them.
A long-running service should be tested over time.
A soak test runs a system for an extended period.
Soak testing can reveal leaks.
Soak testing can reveal cumulative state errors.
Soak testing can reveal reconnect problems.
Soak testing can reveal timing-dependent failures.
Soak testing can reveal performance degradation.
A soak test should have defined success criteria.
Stress testing pushes a system toward or beyond expected capacity.
Load and stress testing are related but different.
A load test can verify normal operational capacity.
A stress test should observe how the system fails.
Graceful degradation is preferable to uncontrolled corruption.
A server reaching capacity should reject or defer work according to defined behavior.
Performance testing measures timing and throughput characteristics.
Performance tests require controlled conditions for meaningful comparisons.
One fast execution does not prove performance.
One slow execution does not prove a regression.
Repeated measurements provide stronger evidence.
Baseline measurements allow comparison over time.
Performance regression testing detects meaningful degradation.
Performance requirements should specify acceptable thresholds.
A benchmark is not necessarily a correctness test.
Fast incorrect software is still incorrect.
Concurrency testing evaluates behavior when operations occur simultaneously.
Race conditions may not appear during single-threaded tests.
Concurrent tests can repeat operations to increase the chance of exposing races.
A passing concurrency test does not prove absence of races.
Shared state should be tested under contention.
Database transactions should be tested under concurrent access.
File creation should be tested under concurrent access when races matter.
Identity generation should be tested for concurrent invocation if multiple processes can invoke it.
Two simultaneous identity-generation operations should not silently create inconsistent identities when architecture requires one identity.
Atomic operations can simplify concurrency correctness.
Concurrency tests should verify final state.
Merely surviving concurrent execution is not enough when state can become incorrect.
A system can have states such as INITIALIZING.
A system can have states such as ACTIVE.
A system can have states such as DEGRADED.
The exact states depend on architecture.
Tests should verify prohibited transitions.
A component should not report ACTIVE before required qualification.
Recovery transitions should be tested.
Repeated recovery should be tested when relevant.
A missing file should produce the defined missing-file behavior.
A timeout should produce timeout.
A connection refusal should produce connection refusal.
A malformed response should produce malformed-response failure.
An empty result should remain an empty result.
Specific failures should not collapse unnecessarily into "internal service unavailable."
Generic errors destroy diagnostic information.
Tests should verify error preservation.
Tests should verify that user-facing errors remain bounded when security requires it.
Internal logs can preserve more detailed diagnostics.
Security testing verifies security requirements.
Authentication tests verify identity mechanisms.
Input tests verify validation.
Injection tests verify that data cannot become unintended instructions.
CSRF tests verify state-changing request protection where applicable.
Signature tests verify cryptographic validation.
Security testing should include successful authorized behavior.
A security mechanism that blocks everyone is not necessarily correct.
Correct security permits authorized operations and rejects unauthorized operations.
A negative security test should verify rejection.
A positive security test should verify permitted behavior.
A failing security test should lead to investigation.
Removing the security check is not a repair unless requirements explicitly changed.
Backward compatibility means newer software supports defined older interfaces or data according to policy.
Forward compatibility means older or current software can tolerate defined newer information according to policy.
Compatibility should be specified.
Compatibility should not be guessed.
An ABI test should use explicit supported ABI values.
A protocol compatibility test should use explicit protocol versions.
A file-format compatibility test should use defined format versions.
A historical version appearing in documentation does not automatically mean it remains supported.
Support status should come from current policy or interface definition.
A module ABI history of 1.3, 1.4, and 1.5 does not mean a host using ABI 1.5 must accept modules built for all three.
If current policy requires exact ABI 1.5, compatibility testing should verify 1.5 is accepted and unsupported versions are rejected.
Current requirements govern current tests.
Historical requirements remain useful for testing historical releases.
Release testing evaluates a candidate build before release.
A release candidate is a build considered potentially suitable for release.
A release candidate should have an identifiable source revision.
A release candidate should be tested as the actual artifact intended for distribution.
Testing source code alone does not test packaging.
Testing the installer does not automatically test every installed feature.
Installation testing should verify required files.
Installation testing should verify permissions.
Installation testing should verify configuration.
Installation testing should verify startup.
Installation testing should verify uninstallation when supported.
Upgrade testing should verify transitions from supported previous versions.
Upgrade tests should preserve required user data.
Upgrade tests should preserve required configuration.
Upgrade tests should apply required migrations.
Upgrade tests should reject incompatible states according to policy.
Rollback testing should verify supported rollback procedures.
Rollback can require data migration.
Rollback can be impossible after irreversible schema changes.
A test plan describes intended testing scope.
A test plan can identify features.
A test plan can identify risks.
A test plan can identify test levels.
A test plan can identify acceptance criteria.
A test plan can identify exclusions.
A test plan should not claim unperformed testing.
A test report records observed test results.
A test report should identify the environment when relevant.
A test report should identify passes.
A test report should identify failures.
A test report should identify blocked tests.
A test report should identify skipped tests.
Skipped is not passed.
Blocked is not passed.
Not executed is not passed.
Unknown is not passed.
A test result should represent what actually happened.
A known defect does not become correct behavior merely because it is known.
When the defect is repaired, the expected-failure designation should be reconsidered.
Test automation executes repeatable tests using software.
Automation reduces repetitive manual work.
Automation improves consistency.
Automation does not make incorrect tests correct.
Automated tests require maintenance.
A test suite can become stale.
A stale test can enforce obsolete behavior.
Requirements changes can require test changes.
Tests should not be changed merely because implementation disagrees with them.
Tests encode expectations.
A requirement can be wrong or obsolete.
An implementation can be wrong.
Evidence and governing requirements should resolve the discrepancy.
Test-first development writes tests before implementation according to a development method.
TDD uses a cycle commonly described as red, green, refactor.
A failing test is written for required behavior.
Implementation is added until the test passes.
Code is then improved while preserving behavior.
TDD is one development method.
Tests written after implementation can still be valuable.
The important requirement is meaningful verification.
Manual testing is testing performed through human interaction or observation.
Manual testing is useful for exploratory behavior.
Manual testing is useful for interfaces.
Manual testing is useful for unexpected workflows.
Manual testing can be inconsistent.
Important repeatable findings should often become automated regression tests.
Exploratory testing investigates software without restricting every action to predefined cases.
Exploratory testing uses tester judgment.
Exploratory testing can discover unexpected interactions.
Exploratory testing should still record meaningful defects and reproduction steps.
A defect report should describe observed behavior.
A defect report should describe expected behavior.
A defect report should include reproduction steps when known.
A defect report should include logs or evidence when useful.
A defect report should avoid unsupported conclusions about root cause.
"The application returned HTTP 500" is an observation.
Debugging establishes cause.
A minimal reproduction reduces a failure to the smallest practical conditions that still demonstrate it.
Minimal reproductions simplify debugging.
Removing unrelated components can identify boundaries.
If a failure remains without the network, the network is less likely to be necessary to reproduce it.
If a failure disappears when a module is removed, the module or its interaction becomes relevant evidence.
Changing many variables simultaneously makes conclusions weaker.
Tests should control variables.
A baseline can be a known-good configuration.
A baseline can be expected performance.
"Yesterday's build" is less precise than a version or commit identifier.
Reproducibility requires identifying inputs.
Compiler version can matter.
Build flags can matter.
Dependency versions can matter.
Operating system can matter.
Environment variables can matter.
Test data can matter.
Time can matter.
Random seeds can matter.
A reproducible test should control or record relevant variables.
Randomized tests should record the random seed when possible.
A recorded seed can reproduce a failure.
Time-dependent tests should control time where practical.
Tests should not unnecessarily depend on the wall clock.
Sleeping for arbitrary durations can make tests slow and flaky.
Synchronization should observe the condition being waited for when possible.
A test waiting for a service should verify readiness rather than merely sleeping five seconds.
Five seconds can be too short on one machine.
Five seconds can be unnecessarily long on another machine.
Timeouts should still bound waiting.
A test that can wait forever can hang an entire test suite.
Every asynchronous test should have a defined termination condition.
A network connection can be interrupted.
A file can be made unreadable.
A disk-full condition can be simulated where practical.
A malformed message can be supplied.
A timeout can be induced.
Recovery testing verifies behavior after failure.
A failed operation can remain failed when retry would be unsafe.
Recovery should preserve state integrity.
A system recovering availability while corrupting data has not recovered correctly.
Data integrity should be checked after recovery.
Crash testing evaluates abrupt process termination.
A partially written file should not silently become accepted valid state.
Atomic file replacement can be tested by interrupting writes.
Restart testing verifies initialization from existing state.
A program working only from a fresh empty directory is not enough when normal operation resumes saved state.
Corrupted saved state should be tested.
Missing saved state should be tested.
Current saved state should be tested.
Persistence should not depend on accidental memory state from the previous process.
A cold start begins without previously initialized runtime memory.
A warm restart can reuse persistent state but not process memory unless architecture explicitly provides it.
Restart behavior should be deterministic according to persistent inputs.
Data-ingestion testing verifies parsing and storage behavior.
An ingestion test should verify accepted units.
An ingestion test should verify stored units.
Counts should reconcile according to ingestion rules.
If 195 units were processed, 195 stored, and 0 rejected, the counts are internally consistent.
Content retrieval should be tested separately.
Successful retrieval does not prove correct reasoning over retrieved content.
Ingestion, storage, retrieval, ranking, and response generation are separate stages.
Each stage can be tested independently.
A lesson ingestion test can verify the lesson file exists.
A lesson ingestion test can verify the file is readable.
A lesson ingestion test can verify parsing.
A lesson ingestion test can verify unit count.
A lesson ingestion test can verify storage.
A retrieval test can query known facts from the lesson.
A retrieval test should verify the expected fact is returned.
A reasoning test can ask a question requiring combination of multiple stored facts.
A reasoning test should distinguish retrieval failure from reasoning failure.
If the correct source facts were retrieved but the answer was wrong, retrieval succeeded and reasoning failed.
If no facts were retrieved despite matching stored facts, retrieval failed.
If a downstream service was unavailable, infrastructure failed.
These are different failure categories.
A response should not claim lack of knowledge when knowledge exists but retrieval failed.
A response should not claim internal service failure when retrieval returned a valid empty result unless another service actually failed.
Question intent matters.
Tests can evaluate relevance.
A relevance test supplies a question with a known target concept.
The expected retrieval should contain evidence directly related to that concept.
Retrieving a fact about sessions for a question about equality operators is a relevance failure.
Returning "PHP is dynamically typed" to a question asking the difference between == and === is insufficient even though the fact is related to PHP typing.
Related is not the same as responsive.
Answer testing should evaluate factual correctness.
Answer testing should evaluate completeness according to expected scope.
A concise answer can be correct.
A long answer can be wrong.
Answer length is not a correctness metric.
A system should prefer a supported narrow answer over an unsupported elaborate answer.
No speculation means claims should be grounded in available evidence.
Tests can deliberately ask questions not covered by available knowledge.
The expected behavior can be a bounded statement that available information is insufficient.
Inventing an answer should fail such a test.
Knowledge boundaries should be tested.
Source precedence should be tested.
If current authoritative source says ABI 1.5 and historical documentation mentions ABI 1.4, a current-state question should produce ABI 1.5.
If asked for ABI history, historical versions can be returned.
If asked what changed between 1.4 and 1.5, the system should require evidence describing the change.
The same corpus can support different answers depending on question intent.
Retrieval should not treat every matching source as equally authoritative.
Current state and historical state should be distinguishable.
A test can contain intentionally conflicting sources.
Conflicting evidence should not be silently merged into nonsense.
If no authority rule resolves a conflict, the conflict should be reported.
Testing retrieval only with clean unambiguous facts hides ranking problems.
Duplicate facts should be tested.
Ten copies of an incorrect statement do not make it correct.
Testing should include duplicate sources where relevant.
Testing should include stale documentation.
Testing should include current source.
Testing should include irrelevant high-similarity text.
Testing should include paraphrased questions.
Testing should include questions requiring two related facts.
Testing should include questions requiring refusal to speculate.
A knowledge system should be tested like any other engineered system.
Its inputs are questions and source material.
Its internal stages should be observable enough to diagnose failures.
A wrong answer should be traceable.
Which sources were retrieved?
Which source was ranked highest?
Which module produced the response?
Which service failed?
Which fallback was used?
Observability improves testing.
Observability should not expose secrets unnecessarily.
Audit logs can support test diagnosis.
Tests should avoid depending on decorative log wording when structured events are available.
Machine tests should prefer machine-readable state.
Human-readable logs can change without behavioral change.
Stable interfaces make tests more durable.
Tests should not become coupled to irrelevant formatting.
Exact formatting should be tested when formatting itself is part of the contract.
Protocol bytes require exact tests.
JSON schema can require exact fields.
Human prose often allows variation.
The assertion should match the requirement.
A test that is too loose can accept invalid behavior.
Test precision should match specification precision.
Qualification is stronger than mere execution.
A component can execute without being qualified.
Qualification testing verifies required evidence before declaring a component approved for operation.
A skipped required test should not count as a pass.
An unexecuted required test should not count as a pass.
A known failure should not count as a pass.
Qualification status should derive from actual test results.
A GREEN status should mean defined qualification criteria were satisfied.
Additional states can represent pending or incomplete qualification.
Status definitions should be explicit.
Testing is evidence generation.
A test runner should return a nonzero process exit status when required tests fail according to conventional command-line automation.
A successful process exit status should represent success according to the tool's contract.
Automation can use exit status to stop failed builds.
Ignoring failed exit status can allow defective builds to continue.
Build pipelines should fail when required stages fail.
Test evidence should remain factual.
"PASS" means the tested expectation was satisfied.
"FAIL" means the tested expectation was not satisfied.
"SKIP" means the test was intentionally not executed.
"BLOCKED" means required conditions prevented execution.
"ERROR" can mean the test itself could not complete according to framework definitions.
These states should not be conflated.
A test report can include failure messages.
A test report can include source revision.
A test report can include platform.
A test report can include compiler.
A test report can include dependency versions.
A test report can include configuration.
Enough context should be preserved to reproduce important failures.
Testing should begin with requirements.
Testing should identify observable behavior.
Testing should exercise normal behavior.
Testing should exercise boundaries.
Testing should exercise failure behavior.
Testing should exercise recovery where recovery is required.
Testing should verify state after operations.
Testing should preserve reproducible failures.
Testing should convert repaired defects into regression tests where useful.
Testing should distinguish layers.
Testing should distinguish evidence from inference.
Testing should never redefine failure as success merely because failure is inconvenient.
A system that cannot be tested is difficult to trust.
A system with deterministic interfaces is easier to test.
A system with bounded behavior is easier to test.
A system with explicit errors is easier to test.
A system with small components is easier to test.
A system with clear authority boundaries is easier to test.
A system with observable state is easier to test.
A system with stable protocols is easier to test.
A system with explicit versioning is easier to test.
Testing does not create correctness.
Testing creates evidence about correctness.
Evidence should support the exact claim being made.
If a test proves a module loaded, say the module loaded.
If a test proves a function returned the expected value, say the function returned the expected value.
If a test proves 195 lesson units were stored, say 195 lesson units were stored.
Do not expand that evidence into claims the test did not establish.
If there is no test for a claim, do not claim the test proved it.
Precise testing produces precise engineering knowledge.
Architecture identifies responsibilities.
Architecture identifies boundaries.
Architecture identifies relationships between components.
Architecture identifies how data and control move through the system.
Architecture identifies important constraints.
Architecture is not merely a directory structure.
Architecture is not merely a diagram.
Architecture is not merely a list of technologies.
Architecture describes how parts of a system are organized to satisfy requirements.
Implementation exists within architecture.
Architecture guides implementation.
Architecture should follow requirements.
Architecture should not exist merely to imitate another system.
A small system can have a simple architecture.
A large system can require more architectural boundaries.
Complexity should be justified by requirements.
More modules do not automatically create better architecture.
More abstraction does not automatically create better architecture.
Every abstraction has a cost.
Every boundary has a cost.
Every dependency has a cost.
Every network boundary has a cost.
Every persistent state boundary has a cost.
Architecture should make necessary complexity manageable.
Accidental complexity should be minimized.
Essential complexity comes from the problem being solved.
Accidental complexity comes from the chosen implementation or organization.
Good architecture attempts to avoid unnecessary accidental complexity.
Separation of concerns assigns different responsibilities to appropriate components.
A component should have a clear responsibility.
Persistence logic is different from transport logic.
Business logic is different from platform adaptation.
These responsibilities can interact without becoming the same responsibility.
A boundary separates responsibilities.
A boundary can be a function interface.
A boundary can be a module interface.
A boundary can be a process interface.
A boundary can be a network protocol.
A boundary can be a storage format.
A boundary can be an organizational ownership boundary.
Different boundaries have different costs.
An in-process function boundary is generally cheaper than a network boundary.
A network boundary introduces communication failure.
A process boundary introduces serialization or IPC requirements.
A persistence boundary introduces durability and recovery requirements.
Do not introduce expensive boundaries without a requirement.
An interface defines how components interact.
An interface should avoid exposing unnecessary implementation details.
A stable interface allows implementation to change without requiring every consumer to change.
Encapsulation hides implementation details behind a controlled interface.
Encapsulation does not mean hiding behavior that consumers must understand.
A component can be internally complex while exposing a small interface.
A small interface is easier to test.
A small interface is easier to secure.
A small interface is easier to maintain.
An abstraction represents a concept while hiding selected implementation details.
An abstraction should simplify reasoning.
Do not abstract merely to remove repeated syntax.
Do not abstract before the common behavior is understood.
Premature abstraction can encode incorrect assumptions.
Repeated stable patterns can justify abstraction.
An abstraction should represent a meaningful boundary.
Hidden dependencies make systems difficult to understand.
Implicit initialization order can create hidden dependencies.
Network availability can create hidden dependencies.
Time can create hidden dependencies.
Architecture should identify important dependencies.
High-level policy should not unnecessarily depend on low-level implementation details.
Low-level implementations can satisfy interfaces defined by higher-level requirements.
Dependency inversion separates policy from implementation.
Dependency inversion does not require a dependency-injection framework.
An interface and explicit construction can be sufficient.
Dependency injection means supplying a dependency rather than having a component secretly construct or locate it.
Constructor injection is one technique.
Function parameters are another technique.
Explicit initialization is another technique.
A service locator is another pattern but can hide dependencies when overused.
The goal is clarity of dependency relationships.
A module is a bounded unit of software responsibility.
A module can contain code.
A module can contain configuration.
A module can expose an interface.
A module can maintain private state.
A module can depend on other modules according to architecture.
Module boundaries should be meaningful.
Combining unrelated responsibilities into one module can create excessive coupling.
Modules should be cohesive.
Cohesion describes how closely related the responsibilities within a component are.
High cohesion means a component's responsibilities belong together.
Coupling describes dependencies between components.
Zero coupling is not a realistic goal for cooperating software.
Components must interact to accomplish work.
The objective is controlled coupling.
Internal implementation details should not become external dependencies accidentally.
A public interface is a commitment.
Changing a public interface can affect consumers.
Internal interfaces can evolve more freely.
Architecture should distinguish public from private boundaries.
A module API defines operations available to module consumers.
A module ABI defines binary interaction when modules communicate at the binary level.
API and ABI are different.
A stable source API does not guarantee a stable ABI.
A plugin architecture allows separately developed components to extend a host through defined interfaces.
A plugin is not automatically trusted.
Discovery means finding a potential plugin.
Loading means placing plugin code into the process.
Qualification means establishing that the plugin satisfies required conditions.
Initialization means allowing the plugin to establish operational state.
A discovered module should not automatically execute.
A loaded module should not automatically receive unrestricted authority.
A module contract should define required entry points.
A module contract should define supported versions.
A module contract should define ownership.
A module contract should define lifecycle.
A module contract should define failure behavior.
A module contract should define capabilities where applicable.
The host owns the host architecture.
A core component contains behavior considered foundational to the system architecture.
Placing everything in core increases coupling.
A feature belongs in core when its responsibility is foundational according to the architecture.
Core stability can reduce system-wide regression risk.
Core boundaries should be protected intentionally.
Modules can provide optional behavior outside core.
If core cannot operate without an optional module, that module is not operationally optional.
Terminology should match actual dependency relationships.
A layered architecture organizes responsibilities into conceptual layers.
An application layer can coordinate use cases.
A domain or business layer can contain application rules.
A persistence layer can manage stored data.
Exact layers depend on the system.
A layer should not exist merely because a textbook lists it.
Layering should simplify dependencies.
A lower layer should not depend arbitrarily on higher-layer presentation details.
Strict layering permits interaction only through adjacent layers according to the architecture.
Relaxed layering can permit selected direct dependencies.
Neither is universally correct.
MVC stands for Model-View-Controller.
MVC separates selected application responsibilities.
A model represents or manages application data and related behavior according to the implementation.
A view presents information.
A controller handles input or coordinates application behavior.
MVC implementations vary.
MVC does not have one universal directory structure.
MVC does not require one specific framework.
MVC does not imply that every business rule belongs in a controller.
Controllers can become excessively complex when they accumulate unrelated responsibilities.
Views should not become hidden persistence layers.
Models should not become arbitrary collections of unrelated functions.
The useful property is separation of responsibility.
A monolith deploys an application primarily as one unit.
A monolith can contain well-separated internal modules.
Monolith does not mean unstructured.
A modular monolith combines one deployment unit with strong internal module boundaries.
A modular monolith can be appropriate for many systems.
Microservices divide a system into independently deployable networked services.
Microservices introduce distributed-system complexity.
Microservices introduce service discovery.
Microservices introduce deployment coordination.
Microservices introduce observability requirements.
Microservices are not automatically more scalable.
Microservices are not automatically more maintainable.
A local function call is simpler than a remote call.
A local transaction is simpler than a distributed transaction.
Use distribution when its benefits justify its costs.
A service should own its behavior.
A service should have a defined interface.
Service boundaries should not be arbitrary file boundaries converted into network boundaries.
A client-server architecture separates requesting participants from service-providing participants.
Peer-to-peer does not imply identical authority.
Topology and authority are different architectural concepts.
Event-driven architecture coordinates components through events.
An event should describe a fact according to its semantics.
A command requests that something occur.
"Create account" is command-like.
"Account created" is event-like.
Names should reflect semantics.
Event-driven systems introduce asynchronous behavior.
Asynchronous behavior introduces ordering concerns.
Asynchronous behavior introduces duplicate-delivery concerns.
Asynchronous behavior introduces eventual consistency concerns.
It changes how dependencies are expressed.
Consumers still depend on event schemas and semantics.
Event contracts should be versioned and controlled.
Publish-subscribe allows publishers to emit information without directly selecting every consumer.
Publish-subscribe can improve extensibility.
It can make execution paths harder to trace.
Observability becomes important.
Message-driven architecture uses messages to coordinate components.
Messages should have explicit contracts.
Queues should be bounded.
Retry semantics should be defined.
Message delivery does not automatically mean processing succeeded.
A pipeline architecture processes data through stages.
Each stage performs a defined transformation or operation.
Pipelines can simplify sequential processing.
Stages should have clear input and output contracts.
One stage should not depend on undocumented side effects from another stage.
Pipeline failure behavior should be defined.
A failed stage can stop the pipeline.
A failed stage can reject one item.
A failed stage can retry.
The correct behavior depends on requirements.
State machines are useful when valid operations depend strongly on current state.
Explicit state reduces ambiguous behavior.
A state transition should have defined preconditions.
A state transition should have defined effects.
Invalid transitions should be rejected.
State machines can exist within any larger architecture.
Architecture patterns can be combined.
A system can be a modular monolith using MVC and state machines.
A service can use event-driven processing internally.
A distributed system can contain layered services.
Patterns are tools.
Patterns are not exclusive identities.
Architecture should describe actual behavior rather than collect pattern names.
State ownership is an architectural decision.
Every authoritative piece of mutable state should have defined ownership.
Multiple uncontrolled writers create coordination problems.
Single-writer ownership can simplify reasoning.
Shared state requires synchronization.
Distributed shared state requires distributed coordination.
A cache should not become authoritative accidentally.
A replica should not become authoritative accidentally.
A view should not become authoritative accidentally.
Application state can be volatile.
Application state can be persistent.
Volatile state disappears when the process terminates.
Persistent state survives according to its storage guarantees.
Architecture should identify which state must survive restart.
Required persistent state should not exist only in process memory.
Persistent state requires schema or format management.
Persistent state requires migration or compatibility strategy.
Persistent state requires recovery behavior.
A database is an architectural dependency when application correctness depends on it.
A remote API can be an architectural dependency.
A compiler or runtime can be an architectural dependency.
Dependencies should be recognized rather than treated as invisible infrastructure.
External dependencies can fail.
A dependency can be mandatory.
A dependency can be optional.
A mandatory dependency failure can make a capability unavailable.
Graceful degradation preserves available capabilities when selected dependencies fail.
Degraded operation should be explicit.
A degraded system should not pretend to be fully healthy.
Health should represent required capabilities.
A failure domain is a boundary within which one failure can affect multiple components.
One machine can be a failure domain.
One network can be a failure domain.
One database can be a failure domain.
One datacenter can be a failure domain.
One software defect can create a shared failure domain across many machines.
Redundancy should cross relevant failure domains.
Running two machines on one power source does not protect against power-source failure.
Running multiple identical implementations can share software defects.
Fault isolation prevents one failure from unnecessarily affecting unrelated components.
Process boundaries can provide fault isolation.
Module boundaries alone do not necessarily provide runtime isolation.
A crash in one in-process module can terminate the host process.
Memory corruption in one native module can corrupt host state.
Stronger isolation generally has greater communication cost.
Choose isolation according to risk.
A sandbox restricts a component's capabilities.
Sandboxing can limit filesystem access.
Sandboxing can limit network access.
It limits potential effects according to the sandbox model.
A component that only reads configuration should not require write authority without reason.
A component that does not use the network should not require network authority without reason.
Authority should follow responsibility.
Trust boundaries identify where data or control crosses between different trust levels.
External input crosses a trust boundary.
Plugin input can cross a trust boundary.
Network input crosses a trust boundary.
Previously stored data should not automatically be considered safe.
Validation belongs at trust boundaries.
Validation should occur before untrusted data affects authoritative state.
Authentication architecture determines how identity is established.
Authorization architecture determines how permissions are enforced.
Key-management architecture determines how cryptographic keys are handled.
Audit architecture determines how security-relevant actions are recorded.
Security should not be added only at the user interface.
A disabled button is not authorization.
Architecture should enforce rules at authoritative boundaries.
Platform independence means common required behavior does not depend on one platform's implementation details.
Platform independence does not mean platform-specific code cannot exist.
The common layer defines required semantics.
A Windows backend can implement those semantics using Windows APIs.
A Linux backend can implement those semantics using Linux or POSIX APIs.
The externally required result should remain equivalent where architecture requires it.
Platform abstraction isolates platform-specific behavior.
A platform interface should expose required capabilities.
The common core should not need to know unnecessary platform details.
Compile-time selection can choose a platform backend.
Runtime selection can choose a platform backend.
The correct mechanism depends on deployment requirements.
Platform detection should not become business logic.
A platform backend is an implementation mechanism.
The application mission remains common.
Portability requires more than successful compilation.
Equivalent source can behave differently when assumptions are platform dependent.
Path syntax can differ.
Filesystem semantics can differ.
Case sensitivity can differ.
Socket behavior can differ.
Thread scheduling can differ.
Clock behavior can differ.
Compiler behavior can differ.
Architecture should isolate or explicitly define these differences.
Do not serialize native structures.
Do not rely on host byte order.
Do not rely on compiler padding.
Do not rely on local timezone.
Do not rely on filesystem enumeration order.
Deterministic systems require deterministic architecture at relevant boundaries.
Determinism means defined inputs produce defined outputs according to system rules.
Nondeterministic implementation details should not affect deterministic outputs.
Thread scheduling should not affect consensus-visible state.
Filesystem ordering should not affect canonical output.
Randomness should be controlled when deterministic output is required.
Time should be an explicit input when time affects deterministic behavior.
Hidden inputs weaken determinism.
Architecture should identify sources of nondeterminism.
Configuration is an input.
Configuration should have defined precedence.
Configuration should have defined validation.
Configuration should have defined defaults.
Configuration errors should fail clearly.
A default should be used only when the architecture defines a default.
Missing required configuration should not be guessed.
Configuration and runtime state are different.
Runtime state describes current operation.
Changing runtime state does not necessarily change configuration.
Do not confuse them.
Feature flags enable or disable behavior through configuration or control state.
Feature flags can support staged rollout.
Feature flags increase state combinations.
Permanent piles of feature flags create hidden architecture.
A feature flag should not silently change security invariants.
A feature flag should not silently create protocol incompatibility.
Feature activation should respect architectural boundaries.
Initialization establishes component state before normal operation.
Initialization order should follow dependencies.
Initialization failure should be explicit.
Startup success and readiness are different.
A process can be running but not ready.
Liveness and readiness are different.
Liveness means the process is operating sufficiently to respond according to the liveness definition.
A healthy process can be temporarily not ready.
Shutdown is part of architecture.
Components can need time to stop safely.
A server can stop accepting new work.
Resources can be released.
Shutdown should be bounded.
A component can be forced to terminate.
Restart is a normal operational event in many systems.
A restart should not corrupt persistent state.
A restart should not duplicate non-idempotent operations without defined handling.
A restart should not depend on stale process memory.
Recovery architecture should define sources of truth.
A source of truth is the authoritative source for defined information.
Derived data can be reconstructed from authoritative data.
Architecture should distinguish authoritative data from derived data.
Duplicating authoritative state creates synchronization requirements.
Avoid multiple sources of truth for the same fact unless the system defines reconciliation.
Data should be validated at appropriate boundaries.
Data transformations should be explicit.
Derived values should retain enough provenance where required.
A component should not silently reinterpret data owned by another component.
Control flow describes how execution responsibility moves.
A controller can invoke a service.
A service can invoke a repository.
An event can trigger a handler.
A scheduler can invoke a job.
A network request can invoke a protocol handler.
Architecture should make major control paths understandable.
Hidden callbacks can complicate reasoning.
Excessive indirection can obscure execution.
Indirection is useful when it decouples meaningful responsibilities.
Indirection is harmful when it only makes code harder to trace.
Direct calls are not architecturally inferior when direct dependency is appropriate.
Simple is a valid architectural property.
A repository pattern abstracts persistence operations.
A repository can separate application behavior from storage details.
A repository is not required for every application.
Adding a repository that merely renames every database operation can add little value.
Abstractions should earn their existence.
A controller coordinates requests according to its responsibility.
A service can contain reusable application behavior.
A service class containing unrelated helper methods is not cohesive merely because it is called a service.
Names do not create architecture.
Utility modules can become dumping grounds.
If unrelated functions accumulate in one utility module, responsibilities should be reconsidered.
Helpers should remain bounded.
A factory creates objects according to defined construction logic.
Factories can hide complex construction.
Factories can select implementations.
Factories are unnecessary when ordinary construction is simple.
A singleton provides one globally accessible instance according to the pattern.
Singletons can create hidden global dependencies.
Global convenience can create architectural coupling.
Immutability reduces possible state transitions.
Immutable objects cannot be modified after construction according to their model.
Immutable data can be easier to share safely.
Not every object should be immutable.
Mutable state is necessary for many systems.
Mutable state should have clear ownership.
Concurrency architecture determines how work executes simultaneously.
Threads can share process memory.
Processes have stronger memory isolation.
Worker pools bound concurrent work.
Architecture should select a concurrency model based on workload.
More threads do not automatically mean more performance.
Shared mutable state requires synchronization.
Concurrency boundaries should align with state ownership where practical.
A worker should not modify state owned exclusively by another worker without a defined coordination mechanism.
Message passing can reduce shared-memory coupling.
Message passing introduces message contracts.
Locks can coordinate shared memory.
Locks introduce blocking and deadlock risks.
Architecture should choose the simplest correct coordination model.
A scheduler decides when work runs.
Scheduling policy can affect latency.
Critical correctness should not depend on one task always running immediately.
Real-time requirements are different from ordinary performance requirements.
A real-time system requires operations to satisfy defined timing constraints.
Fast average execution is not equivalent to real-time guarantees.
Architecture must account for worst-case timing when real-time behavior is required.
Optimization should target demonstrated bottlenecks.
Architectural performance includes communication cost.
Architectural performance includes serialization cost.
Architectural performance includes storage cost.
Architectural performance includes synchronization cost.
Architectural performance includes algorithmic cost.
Architectural performance includes cache behavior.
Premature distribution can harm performance.
Premature abstraction can harm performance.
Premature optimization can harm maintainability.
Correctness comes before unnecessary optimization.
Performance requirements should still influence architecture early when they are genuine constraints.
Vertical scaling adds resources to one system.
Horizontal scaling adds additional systems or workers.
Horizontal scaling usually requires stronger coordination architecture.
Stateless request handlers are easier to scale horizontally.
Stateful services can also scale but require state coordination.
Stateless does not mean the entire application has no state.
It means the component does not depend on local session state that prevents interchangeable handling according to the architecture.
Scaling choices have costs.
Reliability is the ability to perform required behavior consistently under defined conditions.
Retries can improve reliability for transient failures.
Retries can also amplify failure.
Timeouts prevent indefinite waits.
Redundancy can improve availability.
Redundancy can create consistency requirements.
Reliability mechanisms should be matched to failure modes.
Do not add a retry because an operation failed once without understanding whether retry is safe.
Resilience is the ability to continue or recover under failures.
Graceful degradation is one resilience strategy.
Redundancy is another.
Backpressure is another.
Circuit breaking is another.
Persistence is another.
Architecture should define expected failures.
Expected failures should have designed behavior.
Unexpected failures should fail safely and observably.
Observability is an architectural concern.
Logs provide event records.
Metrics provide measurements.
Traces provide operation flow across boundaries.
Health endpoints provide selected operational status.
Audit records preserve defined historical evidence.
These mechanisms have different purposes.
Logs should not be the only state database.
Metrics should not replace audit history.
Audit history should not be rewritten to make current state appear cleaner.
Observability should respect component boundaries.
Distributed operations should have correlation identifiers where useful.
Errors should identify the failed boundary.
"Something failed" is weak evidence.
"Protocol handshake rejected unsupported version" is stronger evidence.
Architecture should make failures diagnosable.
A system that cannot explain its state is difficult to operate safely.
Error handling should follow responsibility.
A low-level component can report a low-level failure.
A higher-level component can translate it into application meaning.
Translation should preserve useful cause information.
Do not expose low-level secrets or internals unnecessarily.
Do not discard evidence needed for diagnosis.
Error messages should distinguish expected rejection from internal failure.
An unavailable dependency is not necessarily malformed input.
Error taxonomy improves recovery.
Return codes are another.
Callbacks are another.
No mechanism is universally superior.
Use the mechanism appropriate to the language and architecture.
Errors should not be silently ignored.
Ignoring an error is a decision.
That decision should be justified.
A fallback is alternative behavior used when preferred behavior is unavailable.
Fallbacks should preserve required correctness.
A fallback that returns incorrect authoritative data is not resilience.
A fallback can provide reduced functionality.
Fallback behavior should be visible.
Silent fallback can hide outages.
Compatibility is an architectural concern.
File formats can outlive code versions.
Network protocols can connect different versions.
Module ABIs can connect separately built components.
Architecture should define compatibility boundaries.
Versioning should occur where compatibility requires it.
Do not version every internal function unnecessarily.
Do version persistent or external contracts when changes can affect independent consumers.
Migration transforms persistent state from one representation to another.
Migrations should be controlled.
Migrations should preserve required data.
Migration failure should have recovery procedures.
A migration should not be confused with ordinary runtime behavior.
Backward-compatible migrations can support rolling upgrades.
Breaking migrations can require coordinated deployment.
A rolling deployment replaces instances gradually.
Different versions can coexist temporarily.
Protocols and storage formats must tolerate that coexistence if rolling deployment is required.
An atomic deployment replaces a unit as one controlled change.
It can increase interruption depending on infrastructure.
Deployment strategy should match availability requirements.
Build documentation should include every required artifact.
Artifact identity should be traceable.
A hash can identify exact artifact bytes.
A version identifies a logical release.
Version and hash serve different purposes.
Deployment should know which artifact is running.
Architecture should not rely on operators guessing.
Configuration deployment should be controlled separately from binary deployment where appropriate.
A binary can be correct while configuration is wrong.
Configuration can be correct while the binary is incompatible.
Architecture exists in operation, not only in source code.
A diagram showing redundant servers is meaningless if both depend on one unprotected database.
A diagram showing modules is meaningless if every module modifies global state directly.
Architecture should describe reality.
Architecture drift occurs when implementation diverges from intended architecture.
Architecture drift can accumulate gradually.
Code review can detect drift.
Dependency analysis can detect drift.
Tests can enforce selected boundaries.
Documentation should be updated when architecture intentionally changes.
Do not preserve an obsolete diagram merely because it is official-looking.
Documentation should describe the current controlled architecture.
A decision record can state the selected approach.
A decision record can state consequences.
A decision record can preserve why a decision was made.
Decision history prevents repeated debates based on forgotten context.
A later decision can supersede an earlier decision.
Superseding history should not erase earlier history.
Architecture evolves.
Evolution should be controlled.
Refactoring changes internal structure while preserving required external behavior.
Refactoring can improve architecture.
Refactoring should not silently change protocol behavior.
Refactoring should not silently change persistent format.
Refactoring should not silently change authorization.
External behavior should remain protected by tests.
A rewrite replaces substantial implementation.
Rewrite decisions should be justified.
Old code being unattractive is not sufficient evidence that a rewrite is necessary.
Incremental replacement can reduce risk.
Legacy code means existing code that must be maintained.
Legacy does not automatically mean bad.
Old software can be stable.
New software can be defective.
Evaluate evidence.
Technical debt represents future cost created by implementation decisions.
Not every simple implementation is technical debt.
Complexity introduced without requirement can itself be technical debt.
Architecture should favor the smallest design that correctly satisfies current known requirements while leaving reasonable paths for change.
Do not build hypothetical future systems without evidence they will be needed.
Extensibility should be intentional.
A plugin boundary can support expected extension.
A stable protocol can support independent implementations.
A configuration mechanism can support known variation.
Excessive genericity can make current behavior harder to understand.
"You might need it someday" is weak justification for complexity.
YAGNI is a software design principle meaning You Aren't Gonna Need It.
It warns against implementing speculative requirements.
YAGNI does not mean ignore foreseeable requirements.
Known requirements should be designed for.
Speculative requirements should not dominate architecture.
KISS is a design principle commonly expanded as Keep It Simple.
A correct simple system is preferable to an unnecessarily complicated equivalent system.
A simpler design is easier to test.
A simpler design often has fewer failure modes.
DRY is commonly expanded as Don't Repeat Yourself.
Two similar pieces of code can represent different responsibilities.
Combining them prematurely can create coupling.
Duplicate knowledge is more dangerous than duplicate syntax.
SOLID is a group of object-oriented design principles.
SOLID can guide selected object-oriented designs.
SOLID is not a universal architecture law.
Non-object-oriented systems can have excellent architecture.
Patterns and principles are tools.
Requirements remain primary.
A design pattern describes a recurring solution structure.
A pattern should be used when its problem exists.
Do not introduce a pattern merely because its name is familiar.
Pattern names can improve communication.
A factory that does nothing beyond calling a constructor may add unnecessary indirection.
An adapter converts one interface into another.
Adapters are useful at boundaries.
A platform adapter can translate common operations to platform APIs.
A protocol adapter can translate between protocol representation and internal representation.
An adapter should not silently change semantics.
A facade can reduce coupling to subsystem details.
A facade should not become a second implementation of the subsystem.
A proxy can provide caching.
A proxy can provide access control.
A proxy should preserve the semantics expected by its interface.
A decorator adds behavior around another component.
Decorators can add logging.
Decorators can add metrics.
Decorators can add caching.
Middleware can authenticate.
Middleware can log.
Middleware can transform requests.
Middleware ordering matters.
One middleware can depend on work performed by earlier middleware.
Hidden middleware ordering dependencies create fragile architecture.
Metrics are a cross-cutting concern.
Authentication can be cross-cutting.
Tracing can be cross-cutting.
Cross-cutting does not mean every component should implement the concern independently.
Shared mechanisms can provide consistency.
Shared mechanisms should not obscure local responsibility.
A framework provides reusable architecture and execution structure.
A library provides functionality called by application code.
The distinction is often described through inversion of control.
Application code commonly calls a library.
The boundary is not always absolute.
Using a framework creates architectural dependency on framework behavior.
Framework upgrades can affect application architecture.
Do not let framework conventions replace understanding of system responsibilities.
The application's requirements remain authoritative.
Domain terminology should be consistent.
One concept should not have many accidental names.
Different concepts should not share one ambiguous name.
Clear terminology improves architecture.
Names establish mental boundaries.
If a component discovers peers, do not call discovery admission.
If a component stores a cache, do not call it authoritative state.
Precise naming is engineering.
Data ownership should follow domain ownership where practical.
One component should not directly modify another component's internal persistence without a defined interface.
Bypassing ownership creates hidden coupling.
Shared databases can create coupling between otherwise separate services.
Two services using the same tables are not fully independent merely because they have separate executables.
Storage boundaries matter.
Changing a schema can break consumers.
Direct database access can bypass application validation.
Architecture should define who may write which data.
Read access and write access can have different boundaries.
Command-query separation distinguishes operations that change state from operations that retrieve state.
A query requests information.
Separating them can clarify behavior.
Not every function must strictly follow command-query separation.
The principle is useful when it makes side effects clear.
A query that secretly modifies authoritative state is surprising.
Side effects should be explicit.
Idempotency is architectural when operations can be retried.
An idempotent operation can be repeated without changing the intended final state beyond the first successful application according to its semantics.
Local initialization can benefit from idempotency.
Deterministic initialization is useful.
Running initialization twice should not corrupt state.
A migration should not accidentally repeat destructive actions.
A module discovery pass should not duplicate module registration.
Lifecycle operations should define repetition behavior.
Resource ownership is architectural.
Memory has ownership.
Sockets have ownership.
Threads have ownership.
Database connections have ownership.
Locks have ownership.
Temporary files have ownership.
Cryptographic keys have ownership.
Every acquired resource should have a defined release path.
A component should not release resources owned by another component without contract.
A host can own a module instance.
The host can request module shutdown.
The module can release its resources.
This is clearer than both independently freeing shared resources.
Lifecycle boundaries reduce ambiguity.
A subsystem is a collection of components providing a larger responsibility.
Subsystem boundaries can reduce cognitive load.
A subsystem should expose a controlled interface.
Consumers should not depend on arbitrary subsystem internals.
A subsystem can contain its own layers.
Architecture can be hierarchical.
A system contains subsystems.
Subsystems contain modules.
Modules contain functions or classes.
Not every level needs formal ceremony.
If a change has one obvious responsible component, boundaries are probably useful.
If every change requires touching unrelated components, coupling can be excessive.
If no one knows where new behavior belongs, responsibilities can be unclear.
If the same rule is implemented independently in many places, authority can be fragmented.
One authoritative implementation of important rules reduces disagreement.
Do not duplicate consensus rules across platform-specific backends.
Do not duplicate protocol semantics independently unless implementations are intentionally separate and tested for equivalence.
Centralize rules when one authority is required.
Decentralize implementation where independent responsibility is useful.
Centralization and decentralization are tools.
Neither is universally superior.
Centralized architecture can simplify authority.
Centralized architecture can create bottlenecks or single points of failure.
Decentralized architecture can increase coordination complexity.
Requirements determine the tradeoff.
Architecture should distinguish control plane from execution plane where useful.
A control-plane failure should not necessarily corrupt already valid execution state.
Execution components should not silently rewrite control policy.
Exact boundaries depend on the system.
Policy and mechanism should be distinguishable.
Mechanism determines how it is accomplished.
One mechanism can implement multiple policies.
One policy can be implemented through different platform mechanisms.
Separating policy from mechanism improves portability.
Platform-specific code should implement mechanism.
Common architecture can define policy.
Policy should not accidentally become dependent on one platform's API.
A scheduler mechanism can differ between operating systems.
The scheduling policy can remain common.
The event semantics can remain common.
A networking implementation can differ.
The protocol semantics can remain common.
Architecture should preserve semantic equivalence.
A boundary should validate assumptions.
When data crosses from external input to internal representation, validate it.
When data crosses from common code to platform code, validate interface requirements.
When data crosses from volatile state to persistent state, validate persistence invariants.
When data crosses from one trust domain to another, validate authority.
Boundaries are natural enforcement points.
Repeated defensive validation can still be appropriate across trust boundaries.
Each authoritative boundary should enforce its own requirements.
Architecture should minimize invalid states.
Types can represent valid distinctions.
State machines can restrict transitions.
Separate functions can represent separate authority.
Configuration schemas can reject invalid configuration.
Database constraints can enforce persistent invariants.
Protocol validation can reject invalid messages.
Multiple layers can enforce different parts of correctness.
Defense in depth can apply to correctness as well as security.
A database constraint can protect an invariant even if application validation contains a defect.
A protocol validator can protect application logic from malformed messages.
An operating-system permission can limit damage if application authorization fails.
Independent controls reduce reliance on one assumption.
Controls should remain understandable.
Redundant controls with contradictory rules create confusion.
One policy should have consistent enforcement.
A module should be testable through its interface.
A parser should be testable without a live network.
A state machine should be testable with explicit transitions.
Testability is often evidence of clear boundaries.
Testability should not force unnatural architecture.
Production requirements remain primary.
Mocks replace dependencies with controlled test implementations.
Mocks can isolate behavior.
Excessive mocking can test assumptions instead of real integration.
Integration tests verify components working together.
End-to-end tests verify complete paths.
Architecture needs tests at multiple boundaries.
Unit tests cannot prove integration.
Integration tests cannot efficiently cover every internal edge case.
End-to-end tests can be expensive.
Use each level for appropriate evidence.
A core can be tested to ensure optional modules are not required.
A module loader can be tested to reject invalid modules.
A platform layer can be tested for equivalent behavior.
A protocol can be tested with shared vectors.
A persistent format can be tested for compatibility.
Static dependency checks can enforce forbidden imports.
Filesystem permissions can enforce runtime ownership.
Architecture can be enforced by tools where useful.
Documentation alone does not enforce behavior.
Tooling alone does not explain intent.
Use both where appropriate.
Architecture should remain understandable without requiring one original developer to explain it.
Code should reveal responsibilities.
Documentation should explain non-obvious constraints.
Interfaces should communicate contracts.
Operational procedures should describe required state.
Institutional knowledge should not exist only in one person's memory.
A bus factor describes risk created when critical knowledge is concentrated in too few people.
Documentation and simple architecture reduce that risk.
Complex architecture increases training cost.
A system that only its creator can safely modify has maintainability risk.
Architecture should support diagnosis.
When an operation fails, identify the responsible boundary.
Protocol failure belongs at protocol handling.
Persistence failure belongs at persistence.
Platform failure belongs at platform implementation.
Clear boundaries make failures easier to classify.
Do not report every failure as a network error.
Preserve actual cause.
A degraded architecture should isolate failure.
If one peer fails, other peers can continue when the protocol permits it.
If a platform backend cannot initialize, dependent capability should report unavailable.
Do not invent fallback behavior that violates architecture.
Architecture should define what cannot happen.
A module cannot bypass core authority.
A view cannot directly rewrite authoritative storage if the architecture forbids it.
A platform backend cannot change consensus semantics.
A cache cannot become source of truth.
Negative constraints are useful architecture.
Architectural authority should be explicit.
Human governance determines project requirements.
Software architecture implements those requirements.
An automated component operates within delegated authority.
A subsystem does not expand its own authority merely because it can technically access more resources.
Technical capability and authorized responsibility are different.
Least authority reduces accidental behavior.
Architecture should not confuse possibility with permission.
Code can technically open a file.
That does not mean the component owns the file.
Code can technically call an endpoint.
That does not mean the operation is authorized.
Code can technically alter global state.
That does not mean the architecture permits it.
Boundaries define responsibility beyond raw technical capability.
Architectural review should ask what problem is being solved.
What are the requirements?
What state exists?
Who owns that state?
Which components are authoritative?
Which dependencies are required?
Which dependencies are optional?
Where are the trust boundaries?
Where are the platform boundaries?
Where are the persistence boundaries?
Where are the network boundaries?
What must survive restart?
What must remain deterministic?
What must remain compatible?
What must remain secure?
What must remain auditable?
What must remain simple?
Only then choose structure.
Requirements come first.
Do not begin with a message broker because asynchronous systems sound scalable.
Do not begin with containers because deployment exists.
Choose mechanisms after identifying requirements.
A plain file can be correct storage.
Memory can be correct storage.
A network service can be correct storage.
JSON can be sufficient for small structured state.
A key-value store can be appropriate for simple keyed state.
An append-only log can be appropriate for historical events.
No storage technology is universally superior.
Use the smallest mechanism that satisfies correctness, scale, durability, concurrency, and operational requirements.
Every third-party dependency introduces compatibility risk.
Every dependency introduces update responsibility.
Every dependency can introduce security exposure.
Every dependency can become unavailable.
Do not reimplement complex solved problems without reason.
Do not import large dependencies for trivial functionality without reason.
External services reduce local implementation.
External services create external dependency.
Local implementation increases ownership.
Local implementation can increase control.
Architecture should recognize operational ownership.
Someone must deploy the system.
Someone must update it.
Someone must observe it.
Someone must recover it.
Someone must rotate credentials.
Someone must handle storage growth.
A design that is elegant in source but impossible to operate is incomplete.
Operational simplicity is architecture.
Installation complexity is architecture.
Upgrade complexity is architecture.
Recovery complexity is architecture.
Architecture includes the system after compilation.
A system's architecture includes code.
A system's architecture includes runtime processes.
A system's architecture includes authority boundaries.
Do not confuse source organization with complete architecture.
Do not confuse directory layout with architecture.
Do not confuse classes with components.
Do not confuse components with processes.
Do not confuse processes with services.
