Serialization is the conversion of structured information into a representation suitable for storage or transmission.
Deserialization is the reconstruction or interpretation of structured information from a serialized representation.
Serialization converts state into representation.
Deserialization converts representation into state.
Serialization does not automatically preserve every property of an in-memory object.
Only information represented by the serialization format can be reconstructed.
A serialized representation can be textual.
A serialized representation can be self-describing.
A serialized representation can depend on an external schema.
A serialized representation can be human-readable.
A serialized representation can be optimized for machines.
No single serialization format is appropriate for every requirement.
JSON is a serialization format.
XML can be used as a serialization format.
CSV can represent selected structured data.
Protocol Buffers is a serialization system.
CBOR is a binary serialization format.
Custom binary formats can be serialization formats.
A database record is not automatically a serialization format.
A filesystem is not a serialization format.
These systems can contain or transport serialized data.
Serialization format and transport are separate concerns.
The same JSON document can be transmitted through HTTP.
The same JSON document can be sent through a socket.
Changing the transport does not inherently change the JSON representation.
Serialization format and storage location are separate concerns.
A serialized object stored on disk remains serialized data.
A serialized object sent across a network remains serialized data.
A serialized object placed in memory remains serialized data if its representation remains encoded.
Do not confuse location with representation.
They must agree on field meaning.
They must agree on required fields.
They must agree on optional fields.
They must agree on encoding.
They must agree on versioning rules where formats evolve.
