PHP stands for PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor.
PHP source code is normally executed on a server rather than in the user's web browser.
PHP can generate HTML and other response content dynamically.
PHP code is commonly embedded within HTML documents.
PHP statements normally end with a semicolon.
PHP comments can use # for single-line comments.
PHP comments can use /* and */ for block comments.
PHP variable names are case-sensitive.
PHP is dynamically typed.
PHP supports integer values.
PHP supports boolean values.
PHP supports arrays.
PHP supports objects.
PHP constants represent named values that are not intended to change during execution.
PHP constants can be defined using the const keyword.
PHP constants can also be defined using the define function.
PHP strings can be enclosed in single quotes.
PHP strings can be enclosed in double quotes.
PHP double-quoted strings can interpolate variables.
PHP strings can contain escape sequences when appropriate quoting rules are used.
PHP arrays can contain multiple values.
PHP arrays can use numeric indexes.
PHP arrays can use string keys.
An indexed PHP array normally uses numeric keys.
An associative PHP array uses named string keys.
PHP arrays can contain values of different types.
PHP arrays can contain other arrays.
The count function returns the number of elements in a PHP array.
The foreach statement can iterate over PHP arrays.
PHP supports assignment operators.
PHP supports comparison operators.
PHP supports logical operators.
PHP uses == for loose equality comparison.
PHP uses === for strict equality comparison.
PHP uses != for loose inequality comparison.
PHP uses !== for strict inequality comparison.
Strict comparison in PHP compares both value and type.
PHP supports else and elseif branches.
PHP supports switch statements.
PHP supports match expressions in modern PHP versions.
PHP supports do-while loops.
PHP supports for loops.
PHP supports foreach loops.
The break statement exits a loop or switch.
The continue statement advances execution to the next loop iteration.
PHP functions are declared using the function keyword.
PHP functions can accept parameters.
PHP functions can return values.
A PHP function can explicitly declare parameter types.
A PHP function can explicitly declare a return type.
PHP supports optional function parameters with default values.
PHP supports variable-length argument lists.
PHP functions have their own local variable scope by default.
PHP can include another PHP file using include.
PHP can include another PHP file using require.
PHP provides include_once to prevent repeated inclusion of the same file through that statement.
A failed require produces an error that stops normal execution of the requiring script.
Namespaces help organize PHP classes, interfaces, functions, and constants.
PHP can process HTTP requests received by a web server.
PHP provides superglobal arrays containing request and server information.
The $_GET superglobal contains query-string parameters supplied to PHP.
The $_SERVER superglobal contains server and request information.
The $_COOKIE superglobal contains cookies supplied with the request.
The $_FILES superglobal contains information about uploaded files.
The $_REQUEST superglobal may combine input from multiple request sources depending on PHP configuration.
PHP can send HTTP response headers using the header function.
HTTP headers must normally be sent before response body output begins.
HTML forms can submit data to PHP applications.
Input sanitization modifies or removes unwanted data.
Validation and sanitization are different operations.
Output escaping helps prevent untrusted data from being interpreted as executable or structural content.
HTML output containing untrusted text should be escaped for the HTML context.
The htmlspecialchars function can encode characters with special meaning in HTML.
Security decisions should not rely solely on client-side validation.
PHP provides built-in session support.
A PHP session can preserve server-side state between HTTP requests.
The session_start function starts a new session or resumes an existing session.
PHP session data is accessible through the $_SESSION superglobal after session initialization.
Cookies are stored by the client and returned with applicable later requests.
PHP can create cookies using the setcookie function.
Session identifiers must be protected from unauthorized disclosure and reuse.
Authentication state should not be trusted solely because a client supplied a cookie.
PDO stands for PHP Data Objects.
PDO provides a consistent database-access interface for PHP.
PDO supports prepared statements.
Prepared statements separate SQL structure from bound data values.
Bound parameters should be used instead of concatenating untrusted input into SQL statements.
Database operations can use transactions when supported by the database and storage engine.
A transaction can group related database operations into a single logical unit.
A transaction can be rolled back when its operations must be discarded.
PHP supports object-oriented programming.
PHP classes are declared using the class keyword.
Objects are instances of classes.
PHP classes can contain properties.
PHP classes can contain methods.
PHP constructors use the __construct method.
PHP supports class inheritance.
PHP supports interfaces.
PHP supports traits.
An interface defines a contract that implementing classes must satisfy.
A trait provides reusable method and property declarations for classes.
PHP supports public visibility.
PHP supports protected visibility.
PHP supports private visibility.
PHP supports static class members.
PHP supports abstract classes.
PHP supports final classes and methods.
PHP supports exceptions.
Exceptions can be handled using try and catch.
A finally block can execute after try and catch processing regardless of whether an exception was caught.
Production applications should log useful diagnostic information without unnecessarily exposing internal details to users.
Displaying detailed application errors to public users can disclose sensitive implementation information.
PHP can read files from the filesystem when operating-system permissions allow access.
PHP can write files to the filesystem when operating-system permissions allow access.
The fopen function opens a file or stream.
The fclose function closes an opened file handle.
The file_put_contents function can write data to a file.
Filesystem paths supplied by users must not be trusted without validation.
File upload handling must validate the uploaded content and destination rather than trusting the supplied filename.
PHP application security depends on both application code and runtime configuration.
Untrusted input must not be directly concatenated into SQL queries.
Prepared statements with bound parameters reduce SQL injection risk when used correctly.
Cross-site scripting can occur when untrusted content is inserted into executable browser contexts without appropriate handling.
Cross-site request forgery protections are important for state-changing web requests authenticated through ambient credentials such as cookies.
A CSRF token should be unpredictable and validated by the server.
Passwords should not be stored as plaintext.
PHP provides password_hash for creating password hashes using supported password-hashing algorithms.
PHP provides password_verify for checking a password against a compatible stored password hash.
Authentication establishes who a user is.
Authorization determines what an authenticated identity is permitted to do.
Application secrets should not be exposed through public web content.
MVC stands for Model-View-Controller.
MVC separates application responsibilities into models, views, and controllers.
A view is responsible for presenting application output.
A controller commonly receives an application request and coordinates the work required to produce a response.
Routing determines which application handler or controller receives a request.
MVC implementations vary between frameworks and applications.
MVC is an application architecture pattern rather than a feature of the PHP language itself.
PHP web applications require a mechanism for the web server to execute PHP code.
Apache HTTP Server can execute PHP through supported PHP integration methods.
PHP-FPM provides FastCGI process management for PHP.
PHP runtime configuration is commonly controlled through php.ini and related configuration mechanisms.
Filesystem ownership and permissions affect what PHP processes can read and write.
A PHP process cannot normally write to a filesystem location unless its operating-system identity has appropriate permission.
The json_decode function parses JSON into PHP values.
JSON decoding can produce associative arrays when requested.
JSON parsing errors should be detected rather than assuming supplied JSON is valid.
The DateTime class represents date and time values.
The DateTimeImmutable class provides date and time objects whose modification methods return new objects instead of changing the original object.
PHP application behavior should be deterministic where deterministic behavior is required.
Errors should be handled deliberately rather than silently ignored.
Database failures should not be assumed to have succeeded.
Filesystem operations should be checked for failure.
Security-sensitive operations should fail safely.
Application logs should provide enough information to diagnose failures without exposing secrets.
Configuration should be separated from application behavior where practical.
Code should favor clear behavior over unnecessary complexity.
Documentation should describe the behavior actually implemented by the software.
HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language.
HTML describes the structure and meaning of web content.
HTML is a markup language, not a programming language.
A web browser parses HTML to construct a representation of the document.
The browser represents a parsed HTML document as the Document Object Model, commonly called the DOM.
HTML elements represent parts of a document.
Most HTML elements are represented using tags.
An HTML element can contain text, other elements, or both.
HTML elements can be nested inside other HTML elements.
Proper nesting helps maintain a valid and understandable document structure.
A closing tag ends most non-void HTML elements.
Some HTML elements are void elements and do not contain child content.
The img element is a void element.
The br element is a void element.
The hr element is a void element.
HTML attributes provide additional information about an element.
Attributes are normally written inside an element's opening tag.
Attribute values should normally be enclosed in quotation marks.
An element can have multiple attributes.
The id attribute identifies an element within a document.
An id value should be unique within an HTML document.
The class attribute associates an element with one or more class names.
Multiple elements can share the same class.
Classes are commonly used by CSS and JavaScript to select groups of elements.
The DOCTYPE tells the browser which general HTML parsing mode the document expects.
The html element is the root element of an HTML document.
The title element defines the title of the document.
The document title commonly appears in the browser tab or window title.
The charset metadata declares the character encoding used by the document.
UTF-8 is a commonly used character encoding for HTML documents.
A typical UTF-8 declaration is <meta charset="UTF-8">.
The viewport metadata helps browsers size pages appropriately on different devices.
The link element can associate external resources with an HTML document.
The link element is commonly used to load an external CSS stylesheet.
The script element is used to associate executable script code with an HTML document.
JavaScript is commonly loaded using the script element.
The style element can contain CSS rules within an HTML document.
External stylesheets are generally preferable when styles are shared across multiple pages.
HTML headings represent headings and subheadings within document content.
HTML provides heading elements from h1 through h6.
The h1 element normally represents the highest-level heading of a document or major content region.
Heading levels communicate document structure and should be chosen according to meaning rather than appearance alone.
The p element represents a paragraph.
The br element creates a line break.
The hr element represents a thematic break between sections of content.
The strong element represents content with strong importance.
The em element represents emphasized content.
The i element can represent text distinguished from surrounding prose for reasons such as terminology or alternate voice.
The div element is a generic block-level container.
Div and span are useful when no more meaningful semantic element is appropriate.
Semantic HTML uses elements whose names describe the purpose or meaning of their content.
Semantic HTML can improve accessibility and document maintainability.
The header element represents introductory or navigational content for a page or section.
The footer element represents footer information for a page or section.
The main element represents the primary content of a document.
A document should normally have only one visible main content region represented by the main element.
The nav element represents a section containing navigation links.
The section element represents a thematic section of content.
The aside element represents content indirectly related to surrounding content.
A link can point to another resource.
A link can point to a fragment within the current document.
A fragment link commonly references an element by its id.
An absolute URL includes sufficient information to identify a resource independently of the current document location.
A relative URL identifies a resource relative to the current document or configured base URL.
Relative URLs are useful for resources located within the same website.
The target attribute can influence where a linked resource is opened.
Opening untrusted links in a new browsing context can require additional security considerations.
Images are embedded in HTML using the img element.
The src attribute identifies the image resource.
The alt attribute provides a text alternative for an image.
Meaningful images should normally have useful alternative text.
Decorative images can use an empty alt attribute when appropriate.
Alternative text is important for accessibility and when an image cannot be displayed.
The width and height attributes can provide intrinsic dimensions for an image.
The li element represents an item within a list.
Lists can contain nested lists.
The dl element represents a description list.
The dt element represents a term or name in a description list.
The dd element represents a description or value associated with a term in a description list.
HTML tables represent tabular data.
The table element contains a table.
The tr element represents a table row.
The tfoot element groups table footer content.
Tables should be used for tabular data rather than general page layout.
HTML forms collect information from users.
The action attribute identifies where form data is submitted.
The method attribute identifies the HTTP method normally used when submitting a form.
GET and POST are commonly used HTTP methods for HTML forms.
GET form submissions commonly encode submitted data into the request URL.
Sensitive information requires appropriate transport security such as HTTPS.
An input with type="text" accepts general single-line text.
A password input does not encrypt the password merely because its characters are visually obscured.
An input with type="email" represents an email-address input.
An input with type="number" represents numeric input.
An input with type="checkbox" represents an independent on-or-off choice.
An input with type="radio" represents a choice commonly grouped with related radio controls.
An input with type="hidden" submits a value that is not normally presented as an editable control.
Hidden form values are still controlled by the client and must not automatically be trusted by the server.
The textarea element accepts multi-line text.
The select element provides a selection control.
The option element represents a choice within a select element.
A submit button can initiate form submission.
The name attribute determines the name associated with a successful form control during submission.
The value attribute provides or represents a value associated with a form control.
The label element associates descriptive text with a form control.
Labels improve form usability and accessibility.
A label can be associated with a control using the for attribute and the control's id.
The required attribute can request browser-side validation before form submission.
HTML form validation can improve usability.
Client-side HTML validation is not a security boundary.
A user can bypass or modify client-side validation.
Server-side applications must independently validate untrusted form data.
Data submitted by an HTML form must be treated as untrusted by the server.
HTML attributes such as maxlength can constrain normal browser interaction but do not establish a server-side security limit.
Disabled form controls are generally not submitted with a form.
Readonly controls can prevent normal editing while still allowing their values to be submitted.
HTML comments begin with <!-- and end with -->.
Secrets must not be placed in HTML comments.
Anything sent as HTML to a browser should be considered visible to the client.
Server-side secrets should remain on the server and should not be embedded in generated HTML.
The entity &lt; represents a less-than sign.
The entity &gt; represents a greater-than sign.
The entity &amp; represents an ampersand.
Escaping special characters is important when inserting untrusted text into HTML.
Untrusted data inserted into HTML without appropriate output escaping can create cross-site scripting vulnerabilities.
Output escaping should be appropriate for the context in which data is inserted.
HTML text content and HTML attribute values can require different output-handling considerations.
URLs and JavaScript contexts have security considerations different from ordinary HTML text.
HTML itself should not be relied upon to sanitize untrusted data.
Server-side software should validate input and encode output appropriately for its destination.
The DOM is a structured representation of a document exposed to software.
JavaScript can inspect and modify the DOM.
Changes to the DOM can change what a user sees without changing the original HTML response received from the server.
HTML provides document structure and semantics.
CSS controls the visual presentation and layout of HTML content.
JavaScript commonly provides client-side behavior and interactivity.
HTML, CSS, and JavaScript have distinct responsibilities even though they commonly work together.
PHP can generate HTML on the server.
A PHP program can produce an HTML document as its HTTP response.
The browser does not normally receive the PHP source code executed by the server.
The browser receives the output produced by PHP, which can include HTML.
HTML does not execute PHP.
PHP is executed by the server before generated HTML is sent to the browser.
An HTML form can submit data to a PHP application.
A PHP application can process submitted form data and return another HTML document.
A PHP application can retrieve information from a database and represent that information in generated HTML.
Database credentials should not be embedded in HTML delivered to a browser.
HTML cannot directly protect a server-side database.
Authorization decisions must be enforced by trusted server-side software rather than HTML controls.
Disabling a button in HTML does not establish authorization.
Security-sensitive operations must be protected on the server.
HTTP is the protocol commonly used to transfer HTML documents between web clients and web servers.
HTTPS is HTTP protected using TLS.
HTTPS protects data while it travels between communicating endpoints when correctly configured.
HTTPS does not make unsafe HTML or unsafe server-side application logic automatically secure.
A web server can serve static HTML files directly.
A browser sends an HTTP request for a resource.
A web server returns an HTTP response.
An HTTP response can contain an HTML document.
The Content-Type response header tells the client what media type the response represents.
HTML responses commonly use the text/html media type.
HTML document structure and HTTP transport are separate concerns.
Declaring document language assists accessibility tools and other software.
Accessibility means designing content so people with differing abilities can perceive and interact with it.
Semantic elements can improve accessibility.
Images conveying information should have appropriate alternative text.
Interactive controls should use elements appropriate for interaction when possible.
Keyboard accessibility is important for interactive web interfaces.
Visual appearance alone should not be the only means used to communicate important meaning.
HTML source order can affect reading and keyboard navigation order.
Valid and meaningful document structure can make accessibility easier to maintain.
HTML should be validated during development when practical.
HTML validation can detect malformed markup and structural mistakes.
Valid HTML does not guarantee that a page is accessible.
Valid HTML does not guarantee that an application is secure.
Browser developer tools can inspect HTML and the DOM.
Client-side HTML must never be treated as inaccessible to the user.
A user can inspect and modify HTML in their own browser.
An id identifies a particular element within a document.
A class categorizes one or more elements.
CSS can select elements by element type, id, class, attributes, and other relationships.
JavaScript can locate DOM elements using selectors and DOM APIs.
HTML provides the structure on which CSS and client-side JavaScript commonly operate.
Separating HTML structure, CSS presentation, and JavaScript behavior generally improves maintainability.
HTML files commonly use the .html or .htm filename extension.
A web server can return HTML regardless of the server-side filename or technology that generated it.
A URL does not have to end in .html for its response to contain HTML.
Dynamic web applications often generate HTML without exposing implementation filenames in URLs.
Document structure should describe content rather than merely reproduce a desired visual appearance.
HTML is one layer of a web application and does not replace server-side application logic.
HTML is not an authentication system.
HTML is not an authorization system.
HTML is not a server-side programming language.
HTML is the structural and semantic foundation of content presented on the Web.
CSS stands for Cascading Style Sheets.
CSS is a style sheet language used to control the presentation of documents.
CSS is commonly used to style HTML documents.
CSS is not HTML.
CSS is not JavaScript.
CSS is not a server-side programming language.
CSS can control colors, typography, spacing, sizing, borders, backgrounds, and layout.
A CSS rule normally consists of a selector and a declaration block.
A selector identifies the elements to which a CSS rule applies.
A CSS declaration consists of a property and a value.
CSS declarations are normally separated by semicolons.
A property identifies the presentation characteristic being configured.
The syntax selector { property: value; } represents a basic CSS rule.
CSS comments begin with /* and end with */.
CSS can be written inline using the style attribute.
CSS can be embedded in an HTML document using the style element.
CSS can be stored in an external stylesheet.
An HTML document can load an external stylesheet using the link element.
External stylesheets allow multiple HTML documents to share presentation rules.
Separating CSS from HTML generally improves maintainability.
The cascade determines which CSS declarations apply when multiple declarations target the same element.
The cascade considers factors including origin, importance, specificity, and source order.
Source order can determine the winning declaration when otherwise competing declarations have equal precedence.
Using !important unnecessarily can make styles harder to maintain.
Inheritance allows some CSS property values to pass from an element to its descendants.
Not every CSS property is inherited.
Layout-related properties are commonly not inherited.
A user agent stylesheet is a browser's built-in default stylesheet.
A type selector selects elements by HTML element name.
A class selector selects elements according to their class attribute.
A class selector begins with a period.
An ID selector selects an element according to its id attribute.
An ID selector begins with a hash character.
The selector #header selects the element whose id is header.
Selectors can be combined to express relationships between elements.
A descendant combinator selects elements nested within another matching element.
A child combinator selects direct children of another matching element.
The child combinator is represented by >.
The general sibling combinator is represented by ~.
A selector list allows the same declarations to apply to multiple selectors.
Selectors in a selector list are separated by commas.
A pseudo-class represents an element in a particular state or relationship.
Pseudo-classes begin with a colon.
:hover can select an element while a pointing device is positioned over it.
:focus can select an element that currently has focus.
:checked can select a checked form control.
:first-child can select an element that is the first child of its parent.
A pseudo-element represents a stylable part or generated portion of an element.
Pseudo-elements are commonly written using two colons.
::before can generate a pseudo-element before an element's content.
::after can generate a pseudo-element after an element's content.
Generated CSS content should not be relied upon as the sole source of essential document information.
CSS specificity is influenced by the components of a selector.
ID selectors have greater specificity than class selectors under ordinary specificity comparison.
Type selectors and pseudo-elements contribute to specificity.
The box model describes how CSS represents the dimensions and spacing of an element.
The CSS box model consists of content, padding, border, and margin areas.
The content area contains an element's content.
A border surrounds the padding and content areas.
The height property can specify the height of an element's box according to its box-sizing behavior.
The box-sizing property controls how specified dimensions are calculated.
With box-sizing: content-box, specified width and height apply to the content box.
The margin property controls outer spacing.
Top, right, bottom, and left sides can be styled independently.
CSS shorthand properties can set multiple related values in one declaration.
The padding shorthand can specify padding for multiple sides.
The border shorthand can specify border width, style, and color.
The display property affects how an element participates in layout.
display: inline-block combines characteristics of inline and block layout.
display: none removes an element from normal rendering.
An element using display: none does not occupy its normal layout space.
Hiding an element with CSS does not provide security.
CSS must not be used as an authorization mechanism.
visibility: hidden hides an element while generally preserving its occupied layout space.
The position property controls an element's positioning scheme.
position: static is the normal default positioning scheme for most elements.
position: relative keeps an element in normal flow while allowing positional offsets and establishing positioning context for certain descendants.
position: absolute removes an element from normal flow and positions it relative to an applicable containing block.
position: fixed positions an element relative to the viewport or another applicable fixed-position containing block.
position: sticky can switch between relative-like and fixed-like behavior according to scrolling conditions.
The z-index property can influence stacking order within applicable stacking contexts.
A larger z-index value does not universally place an element above every other element because stacking contexts affect comparison.
CSS can modify how elements participate in normal document flow.
Floats allow content to flow around an element.
The float property was historically used for page layouts but is primarily intended for floated content.
Modern layouts commonly use Flexbox or Grid instead of float-based page layout.
The flex-direction property controls the main axis direction.
flex-direction: column arranges the main axis vertically.
align-items controls alignment of flex items along the cross axis.
align-content affects distribution of multiple flex lines when applicable.
The gap property can create spacing between flex or grid items.
flex-wrap controls whether flex items can wrap onto additional lines.
flex-grow controls how available positive free space can be distributed to a flex item.
flex-shrink controls how a flex item can shrink when space is insufficient.
flex-basis specifies an initial main-size basis for a flex item.
grid-template-columns defines the columns of an explicit grid.
grid-template-rows defines the rows of an explicit grid.
The fr unit represents a fraction of available space in a grid layout.
The repeat() function can define repeated grid tracks.
The gap property can define spacing between grid tracks.
Flexbox and Grid solve different layout problems and can be used together.
Flexbox is commonly useful for arranging content primarily along one dimension.
Grid is commonly useful for layouts involving both rows and columns.
CSS supports absolute and relative length units.
The px unit represents a CSS pixel.
The em unit is relative to an applicable font size.
Percentage values are relative to a property-specific reference.
The vw unit represents one percent of the viewport width.
Relative units can help create adaptable layouts.
The rgb() function specifies colors using red, green, and blue components.
Modern rgb() syntax can also represent alpha transparency.
The hsl() function specifies colors using hue, saturation, and lightness.
The color property controls the foreground color of text and related content.
The background-color property controls an element's background color.
The background property is a shorthand for multiple background properties.
CSS can use background images.
The background-image property can reference an image resource.
CSS can load external resources such as images and fonts.
External CSS resources can generate additional network requests.
A font-family list can contain fallback fonts.
The font-size property controls text size.
The font-weight property controls font weight.
The line-height property controls line box height.
The text-decoration property controls decorative lines and related text decoration.
CSS presentation does not change the underlying semantic meaning of HTML text.
Web fonts can be defined using @font-face.
A web font can be downloaded by the browser when referenced by CSS.
Font availability can affect how text is rendered.
Fallback fonts should be considered when designing layouts.
A media query begins with @media.
Media queries can test characteristics such as viewport width.
CSS breakpoints are conditions chosen by the developer at which layout rules change.
Responsive design should not assume one fixed screen size.
Flexible layouts can reduce dependence on device-specific dimensions.
Images can be made responsive using appropriate sizing rules.
max-width: 100% can prevent an image from exceeding the width of its containing block in common layouts.
The max-width property specifies an upper bound for width.
The min-width property specifies a lower bound for width.
The max-height property specifies an upper bound for height.
The min-height property specifies a lower bound for height.
The overflow property controls handling of content that exceeds an element's box.
overflow: auto can provide scrolling when needed.
CSS transitions can animate changes between property values.
The @keyframes rule defines stages of a CSS animation.
The animation property is a shorthand for animation-related properties.
CSS transforms can visually translate, rotate, scale, or skew rendered elements.
A transform can change visual presentation without changing ordinary document flow.
Opacity controls the transparency of an element and its rendered descendants as a group.
opacity: 0 makes an element fully transparent but does not necessarily remove its layout or interactivity.
Visual invisibility does not establish security.
CSS custom properties are author-defined reusable property values.
CSS custom property names begin with two hyphens.
A custom property can be referenced using the var() function.
Custom properties participate in the cascade.
Custom properties can reduce repetition in stylesheets.
The :root selector commonly provides a convenient location for document-wide custom properties.
CSS inheritance and custom properties can be used together.
CSS supports calculations using the calc() function.
calc() can combine compatible values and units according to CSS rules.
CSS supports minimum and maximum selection using functions such as min() and max().
CSS supports bounded responsive values using clamp().
CSS files are text resources.
A CSS syntax error can cause a declaration or rule to be ignored depending on where the error occurs.
Browsers generally attempt to continue processing CSS after unsupported or invalid declarations.
A valid stylesheet can still produce an undesirable layout.
CSS validation can identify some syntax and standards errors.
CSS validity does not guarantee accessibility.
CSS validity does not guarantee security.
CSS validity does not guarantee correct visual design.
Browser developer tools can inspect applied CSS rules.
A user can override or remove CSS in their own browser.
CSS cannot enforce authorization.
CSS cannot securely hide confidential information delivered to the client.
If sensitive content is included in the HTML response, CSS does not make that content secret.
Text and background colors should provide sufficient contrast.
Content should remain understandable when stylesheets fail to load where practical.
CSS should not make keyboard focus impossible to identify.
Removing focus indicators without providing an accessible replacement can make an interface difficult to use.
Layouts should account for text resizing and varying content lengths.
Fixed dimensions can cause content to overflow when text size increases.
Color alone should not be the sole indicator of important state or meaning.
CSS can visually reorder content in some layouts.
Visual order and document order can differ.
Document order remains important for accessibility and keyboard navigation.
CSS selectors operate on document structure and state.
CSS does not modify server-side application state by itself.
PHP can generate HTML containing class and id attributes that CSS subsequently styles.
CSS is interpreted by the client browser rather than by PHP in the ordinary web request model.
A PHP application can choose which CSS resources or class names appear in generated HTML.
CSS cannot make unsafe PHP application logic secure.
HTML provides structure and semantics.
CSS provides presentation and layout.
PHP can provide server-side application behavior and generate HTML.
A browser can combine HTML, CSS, and JavaScript when rendering and operating a web page.
The web server can send HTML and CSS as separate HTTP resources.
CSS resources are commonly served with the text/css media type.
Changes to a cached stylesheet might not immediately appear if a client continues using an older cached response.
Stylesheet URLs can be versioned or otherwise changed to assist cache invalidation.
CSS specificity should not be increased unnecessarily.
Overly specific selectors can make styles difficult to override and maintain.
Reusable classes can provide predictable styling across multiple elements.
Class names should preferably describe useful roles or concepts rather than depend entirely on temporary visual appearance.
Large stylesheets can be organized into logical sections or separate files.
Consistent naming conventions can improve stylesheet maintainability.
Duplicated CSS declarations can make maintenance harder.
CSS is declarative: authors describe desired presentation rather than writing an imperative sequence for rendering each element.
The browser's rendering engine determines how applicable CSS rules are used to present the document.
CSS can adapt presentation without requiring the semantic HTML content to be rewritten.
CSS is a core technology of web presentation.
C is a general-purpose programming language.
C is a compiled programming language.
C was designed for efficient systems programming.
C provides relatively direct access to memory and machine-level resources.
C source files commonly use the .c filename extension.
C header files commonly use the .h filename extension.
ISO C refers to standardized versions of the C programming language.
Portable C code avoids unnecessary dependence on implementation-specific behavior.
The main function returns an int.
Returning zero from main conventionally indicates successful program termination.
A nonzero program exit status conventionally indicates some form of failure.
C is case-sensitive.
Uppercase and lowercase identifiers are distinct in C.
Statements in C commonly end with a semicolon.
A block of C statements is enclosed by braces.
Whitespace generally separates tokens but is otherwise flexible in many contexts.
C comments can use /* and */.
Modern C also supports // line comments.
An identifier names an object, function, type, label, macro, or other program entity.
C identifiers can contain letters, digits, and underscores subject to language rules.
C has reserved keywords that cannot be used as ordinary identifiers.
A variable is a named object whose stored value can change during its lifetime.
A declaration introduces information about an identifier.
A definition provides the entity described by a declaration where required.
A function declaration describes a function's interface.
A function definition provides a function's implementation.
A function prototype declares the types of a function's parameters and return value.
Calling a function with an incompatible declaration can produce incorrect behavior or a diagnostic.
C is statically typed.
Every object and expression in C has a type.
The short type is an integer type.
The int type is an integer type.
The long type is an integer type.
The long long type is an integer type.
Unsigned integer arithmetic has defined modulo behavior.
Signed integer overflow has undefined behavior.
The float type represents floating-point values.
The double type represents floating-point values with at least the precision requirements specified for double.
The long double type provides an implementation-defined extended floating-point type.
The _Bool type represents boolean values in C.
The stdbool.h header provides the bool, true, and false convenience macros in versions of C where they are defined by that header.
The sizeof operator reports the size of an object or type in bytes.
sizeof returns a value of type size_t.
A C byte is the size of char.
C does not require a byte to contain exactly eight bits.
uint8_t is an exact-width unsigned 8-bit integer type when provided.
int16_t and uint16_t represent exact-width 16-bit integer types when provided.
int32_t and uint32_t represent exact-width 32-bit integer types when provided.
int64_t and uint64_t represent exact-width 64-bit integer types when provided.
size_t is an unsigned integer type suitable for representing object sizes.
ptrdiff_t is a signed integer type suitable for representing pointer differences.
A constant is a value that does not change during evaluation.
Floating-point literals represent floating-point constants.
Character constants represent integer values associated with characters.
A string literal normally includes an implicit terminating null character.
A C string is conventionally a sequence of characters terminated by a null character.
The null character has numeric value zero.
The null character is commonly written as '\\0'.
A C string does not intrinsically store its own length.
Functions processing C strings commonly search for the terminating null character.
strlen returns the number of characters before the terminating null character.
strlen does not count the terminating null character.
strlen requires a valid null-terminated string.
A character array is not necessarily a valid C string.
A pointer stores an address or pointer value referring to an object, function, or permitted special location.
The address-of operator & obtains the address of an object where permitted.
The dereference operator * accesses the object designated by a pointer where permitted.
A null pointer does not designate an object or function.
The NULL macro can represent a null pointer constant in appropriate contexts.
Using a pointer after the lifetime of its referenced object has ended can have undefined behavior.
Pointer arithmetic is defined only within constrained relationships to arrays and objects.
A pointer can point to an element of an array.
Adding one to a pointer to an array element advances it to the next element.
Pointer arithmetic is scaled according to the pointed-to type.
C arrays contain a fixed number of elements once defined.
Array elements have the same element type.
The expression array[index] accesses an array element when the index is valid.
Array indexing outside the permitted bounds has undefined behavior.
C does not automatically perform array bounds checking.
An array expression often converts to a pointer to its first element.
An array and a pointer are not the same type of object.
After an array parameter is adjusted to a pointer parameter, sizeof no longer reports the original array size.
A multidimensional array is an array whose elements are themselves arrays.
C stores ordinary multidimensional arrays in row-major order.
A structure groups multiple named members into one type.
The struct keyword declares or defines structure types.
The . operator accesses a member of a structure object.
The -> operator accesses a structure member through a pointer to a structure.
The expression ptr->member is equivalent to (*ptr).member when ptr validly points to the structure.
A union allows multiple members to share overlapping storage.
Only appropriate access to a union's stored representation should be relied upon according to C rules.
An enumeration defines a set of named integer constants.
The enum keyword declares enumeration types.
typedef creates an alias for an existing type.
typedef does not create a fundamentally new distinct type.
The const qualifier indicates that an object should not be modified through that qualified access path.
Attempting to modify an object defined as const through an inappropriate cast can have undefined behavior.
The volatile qualifier indicates that accesses to an object have observable behavior relevant to the implementation.
volatile is not a general thread-synchronization mechanism.
The restrict qualifier can express pointer aliasing assumptions for optimization and semantics where applicable.
The static keyword has different meanings depending on context.
A static local variable retains its stored value between function calls.
A file-scope identifier declared static has internal linkage where applicable.
A static function at file scope is visible by name only within its translation unit.
The extern keyword can declare an identifier whose definition exists elsewhere.
External linkage allows appropriately declared entities to be referenced across translation units.
A translation unit is the source processed by the compiler after preprocessing.
A C project can contain multiple translation units.
Header files commonly contain declarations shared between translation units.
Function definitions are normally placed in source files.
Header guards prevent repeated inclusion from causing unwanted duplicate declarations or definitions.
A conventional header guard uses #ifndef, #define, and #endif.
The C preprocessor operates before ordinary compilation.
Preprocessor directives begin with #.
A macro performs preprocessing substitution rather than behaving exactly like a C function.
Function-like macros can evaluate their arguments more than once if written carelessly.
Macro parameters should often be parenthesized appropriately when used in expressions.
Conditional compilation uses directives such as #if, #ifdef, #ifndef, #elif, #else, and #endif.
Conditional compilation can select platform-specific implementation code.
Platform-specific implementation code can be isolated behind a common interface.
Portable interfaces allow different platform implementations to provide equivalent behavior.
The compiler translates C source into lower-level program representation or object code.
A compile error prevents successful translation of a translation unit.
A linker error occurs when required definitions or symbol relationships cannot be resolved during linking.
A runtime error occurs while an executing program is running.
Warnings should be reviewed rather than automatically ignored.
-Wextra enables additional warnings in GCC and compatible compilers.
The -std option can select a particular C language standard mode in compilers that support that option.
Optimization options can affect generated machine code without changing the intended defined behavior of a correct program.
Undefined behavior permits the C implementation to impose no requirements on the resulting behavior.
A program containing undefined behavior cannot rely on a particular result from that operation.
Implementation-defined behavior is behavior where the implementation selects and documents a permitted choice.
Unspecified behavior permits one of multiple possibilities without requiring the implementation to document which occurs.
Portable C programs distinguish defined behavior from undefined, implementation-defined, and unspecified behavior.
The assignment operator = stores a value into an object where permitted.
The equality operator == compares two values for equality according to their types.
The inequality operator != compares two values for inequality.
The relational operators <, <=, >, and >= compare values according to C rules.
The logical AND operator is &&.
The logical OR operator is ||.
The logical NOT operator is !.
The bitwise AND operator is &.
The bitwise XOR operator is ^.
The bitwise complement operator is ~.
The left shift operator is <<.
The right shift operator is >>.
Logical operators and bitwise operators are not interchangeable.
The && operator uses short-circuit evaluation.
The || operator uses short-circuit evaluation.
The conditional operator ?: selects between two expressions according to a condition.
The increment operator ++ increases an applicable scalar value by one.
The decrement operator -- decreases an applicable scalar value by one.
Prefix and postfix increment have different value computation timing.
Operator precedence determines how expressions are grouped when parentheses do not override that grouping.
Parentheses can make intended expression grouping explicit.
The if statement conditionally executes code.
The switch statement selects among cases based on an integer or enumeration expression.
A case label identifies a value handled by a switch statement.
The break statement can terminate the nearest enclosing loop or switch.
Missing break statements in switch cases can intentionally or accidentally allow fallthrough.
Intentional fallthrough should be made clear to maintainers.
The for statement provides a looping construct.
The do statement executes its body before testing its loop condition.
The continue statement skips to the next applicable loop iteration.
A function can accept parameters.
C passes function arguments by value.
Passing a pointer allows a function to access an object owned by its caller.
Passing a pointer is still passing the pointer value by value.
C does not provide pass-by-reference semantics as a separate language mechanism.
A function returning void does not return a value.
A void pointer can hold a converted pointer to an object type.
void * is commonly used for generic object pointers.
A void pointer must be converted or assigned appropriately before dereferencing as a typed object.
Function pointers can be used to implement callbacks.
A callback allows one component to invoke behavior supplied by another component through an agreed function interface.
An ABI is an Application Binary Interface.
An ABI defines binary-level conventions required for independently compiled components to interact correctly.
An ABI can define calling conventions.
An ABI can define symbol naming and binary interface expectations.
A source-level API and a binary ABI are related but distinct concepts.
An API defines interfaces used by source code.
An ABI defines compatibility at the compiled binary level.
Changing a public structure layout can break ABI compatibility.
Changing a function signature can break API and ABI compatibility.
Modules loaded dynamically must satisfy the binary interface expected by their host.
A dynamically loaded module can expose a known entry point used by its host.
The host and module must agree on structures passed across their binary boundary.
Versioned ABIs can prevent incompatible modules from being accepted.
ABI validation should occur before an incompatible module is allowed to operate.
Dynamic libraries contain code that can be loaded and linked at runtime or program startup depending on the platform.
Shared libraries on Unix-like systems commonly use the .so filename extension.
Dynamic libraries on Windows commonly use the .dll filename extension.
Platform-specific dynamic loading APIs differ.
C itself does not define a standard dynamic-library loading API.
Memory for ordinary local automatic objects is managed according to their scope and lifetime.
Dynamic memory can be requested using malloc.
malloc returns a pointer to allocated storage or a null pointer when allocation fails.
calloc allocates storage and initializes its bytes to zero.
free releases dynamically allocated storage obtained through compatible allocation functions.
Passing an invalid pointer to free has undefined behavior.
Freeing the same allocation more than once has undefined behavior.
Failing to release no-longer-needed dynamic allocations can cause memory leaks.
A dangling pointer refers to storage whose lifetime has ended or that is otherwise no longer valid.
Setting a pointer to NULL after free can help prevent accidental reuse of that particular pointer but does not repair other aliases.
Every successful allocation should have clearly defined ownership and lifetime.
Memory ownership conventions make C systems easier to reason about.
A buffer has a finite capacity.
Writing beyond the bounds of a buffer has undefined behavior and can create security vulnerabilities.
Buffer sizes must be tracked explicitly when the program cannot otherwise derive them safely.
A destination buffer must have enough capacity for data written into it.
strcpy does not know the capacity of its destination buffer.
strcat does not know the capacity of its destination buffer.
Unbounded string copying can cause buffer overflows.
snprintf accepts a destination size and can be used for bounded formatted output.
The return value of snprintf should be checked when truncation matters.
memcpy copies a specified number of bytes between non-overlapping memory regions.
Using memcpy with overlapping source and destination regions has undefined behavior.
memmove supports overlapping source and destination regions.
memcmp compares byte sequences.
strcmp compares null-terminated strings lexicographically.
strncmp compares strings up to a specified maximum number of characters.
String comparison using == compares pointer values rather than string contents.
strcmp should be used when ordinary C string contents need to be compared.
stdio.h declares standard input and output facilities.
stdlib.h declares general utility functions including memory allocation and conversions.
stddef.h declares common types and macros such as size_t and ptrdiff_t.
limits.h declares implementation limits for integer types.
float.h declares characteristics of floating-point types.
ctype.h declares character classification and conversion functions.
time.h declares date and time facilities.
errno.h provides access to errno and related error macros.
assert.h provides the assert diagnostic macro.
FILE represents a stream used by standard I/O facilities.
fopen opens a file stream.
fclose closes a file stream.
fread reads binary data from a stream.
fwrite writes binary data to a stream.
fgets reads a bounded line or character sequence from a stream.
fputs writes a string to a stream.
fprintf writes formatted output to a stream.
printf writes formatted output to standard output.
stderr is the standard error stream.
stdout is the standard output stream.
stdin is the standard input stream.
File operations can fail.
Programs should check the results of operations that can fail.
fopen returns a null pointer when it cannot open the requested file.
Binary and text stream behavior can differ across platforms.
Opening a file with mode "rb" requests binary reading.
Writing a file is not guaranteed to place all data permanently on physical storage immediately.
Buffered I/O can delay underlying writes.
fflush requests that buffered output for an applicable stream be transmitted to its host environment.
Closing a writable stream flushes buffered output as part of normal stream closure.
Error handling should be explicit.
A function can return a status value indicating success or failure.
A caller should check error status when failure matters.
errno can provide additional error information for functions documented to set it.
errno should only be interpreted according to the contract of the function that failed.
perror can print a message associated with the current errno value.
Defensive C programming validates assumptions at boundaries.
Lengths received from external input should be validated before being used for memory operations.
Integer overflow can cause size calculations to produce unsafe results.
Network protocols should define serialization explicitly.
Serialization converts structured data into a defined transferable representation.
A protocol should define field sizes and encoding rather than depending on compiler-specific structure layout.
Writing a C struct directly to a network connection does not automatically create a portable protocol.
Structure padding can vary according to implementation and ABI requirements.
Integer byte order can vary between systems.
Network byte order conventionally uses big-endian ordering for many Internet protocols.
Portable protocol implementations explicitly encode and decode multibyte fields.
Deterministic serialization produces the same defined byte representation for the same logical input.
Consensus-sensitive software should not depend on unspecified or undefined behavior.
Consensus-sensitive software should define serialization precisely.
Consensus-sensitive calculations should produce identical required results across supported platforms.
Floating-point behavior can vary in ways that make it unsuitable for some deterministic consensus calculations.
Fixed-width integer arithmetic is often easier to specify deterministically than platform-dependent integer representations.
Platform independence does not require every implementation file to be identical.
Compile-time platform selection can choose an implementation backend.
Runtime platform detection can choose an implementation backend when architecture permits.
Common code should avoid unnecessary operating-system dependencies.
A module interface should define ownership of memory passed across module boundaries.
A module interface should define the lifetime of pointers passed across module boundaries.
A module interface should validate version compatibility.
A module should not assume host internals that are not part of its declared interface.
A host should reject a module that fails required qualification or compatibility checks.
A test verifies expected program behavior.
A negative test verifies correct handling of invalid or prohibited input or behavior.
Regression tests preserve previously established behavior against future changes.
Tests should be deterministic when testing deterministic behavior.
A passing test demonstrates that the tested condition passed for that execution.
A passing test does not prove the absence of every possible defect.
Compiler warnings and tests provide different kinds of evidence.
A clean compilation does not prove runtime correctness.
Static analysis can identify classes of defects without executing the program.
Dynamic analysis examines behavior while a program executes.
Code review can identify design and implementation problems not captured by automated tests.
Small functions with clear responsibilities are generally easier to test.
Initialization should establish valid program state before dependent operations execute.
Cleanup should release resources acquired during execution.
Resource acquisition and release paths should account for intermediate failures.
A program should not continue using a resource after acquisition failed.
The ISO C standard library does not define sockets.
Socket programming requires platform-specific networking facilities or an abstraction.
TCP does not preserve application message boundaries.
An application protocol running over TCP must define its own message framing.
A single send operation does not guarantee a single corresponding receive operation.
Network timeouts prevent some operations from waiting indefinitely.
Ports identify transport-layer service endpoints.
Binding a server socket to a port can fail if the address is unavailable or permission is denied.
HTTP is an application protocol commonly transported over TCP.
A C program can implement an HTTP server using appropriate networking facilities.
HTTP requests contain a request method, target, headers, and optionally a body according to protocol rules.
HTTP responses contain a status, headers, and optionally a body according to protocol rules.
A 404 HTTP status indicates that the requested resource was not found.
A 503 HTTP status indicates that a service is unavailable.
An application should use HTTP status codes according to the actual result of request handling.
A JSON response is text formatted according to JSON syntax.
C does not include JSON processing in the ISO C standard library.
JSON support requires program code or an additional library.
External libraries add dependencies that must be understood and managed.
Minimizing dependencies can reduce deployment complexity.
Avoiding unnecessary dependencies can make systems easier to audit and maintain.
make is not part of the ISO C language.
A Windows batch file can invoke a C compiler and linker directly.
Build scripts should make compilation steps repeatable.
Repeatable builds reduce accidental differences between development runs.
Compiler options should be documented as part of a project's build procedure.
Comments should explain information that is not already obvious from the code.
Comments that contradict the implementation are harmful.
Consistent formatting improves readability.
Braces can make control flow clearer even when the language permits an unbraced statement.
Combining multiple unrelated statements on one line can reduce readability.
C permits highly compact code but compact code is not automatically better code.
Correctness is more important than cleverness.
Defined behavior is more reliable than behavior that merely happens to work on one compiler.
Portable behavior is more reliable across supported platforms than undocumented assumptions.
A deterministic system should make important behavior explicit.
C provides mechanisms rather than automatic safety.
The programmer is responsible for many memory, lifetime, bounds, and resource-management decisions.
Careful validation is essential when C processes untrusted input.
C can provide efficient and predictable systems when its interfaces and invariants are carefully defined.
C++ is a compiled programming language.
C++ was originally developed as an extension of ideas and capabilities associated with C.
C++ and C are distinct programming languages.
Valid C code is not necessarily valid C++ code.
Valid C++ code is not necessarily valid C code.
C++ supports procedural programming.
C++ supports object-oriented programming.
C++ supports generic programming.
C++ supports functional programming techniques.
C++ is case-sensitive.
C++ source files commonly use the .cpp, .cc, or .cxx filename extensions.
C++ header files commonly use the .h, .hpp, .hh, or .hxx filename extensions.
A C++ implementation includes a compiler and supporting implementation facilities.
The linker combines object files and libraries into an executable or library.
A translation unit is source code processed after preprocessing.
A C++ program can consist of multiple translation units.
ISO C++ refers to standardized versions of the C++ programming language.
Portable C++ avoids unnecessary dependence on implementation-specific behavior.
The -std option can select a C++ standard mode in GCC and compatible compilers.
Returning zero from main conventionally indicates successful program termination.
Statements commonly end with a semicolon.
Blocks are enclosed by braces.
Comments can use /* and */ for a block comment.
An identifier names a program entity.
A definition provides an entity where a definition is required.
A variable is an object with a name.
An object is a region of storage associated with a type and lifetime according to C++ rules.
Every expression has a type and a value category.
C++ provides fundamental types.
bool represents boolean values.
char is a character and integer type.
short is an integer type.
int is an integer type.
long is an integer type.
long long is an integer type.
float is a floating-point type.
double is a floating-point type.
long double is a floating-point type.
void represents the absence of a value in applicable contexts.
std::size_t is an unsigned integer type suitable for representing object sizes.
The sizeof operator reports the size of an object or type in bytes.
Fixed-width integer types such as std::uint32_t are provided through <cstdint> when supported.
Signed integer overflow has undefined behavior.
Unsigned integer arithmetic has defined modulo behavior.
C++ does not automatically make arithmetic immune to overflow.
auto can request type deduction from an initializer.
auto does not mean that an object has no type.
constexpr indicates that an entity can participate in constant evaluation when its requirements are satisfied.
consteval requires a function invocation to produce a compile-time constant where the language requires immediate invocation.
A reference provides an alias to another object or function.
An rvalue reference is written using && in a type declaration.
A reference must be initialized.
A reference is not normally reseated to refer to a different object after initialization.
Pointers and references are different language mechanisms.
A pointer stores a pointer value.
The address-of operator & can obtain the address of an object where permitted.
The dereference operator * accesses an object through a valid pointer.
nullptr is the C++ null pointer literal.
nullptr should generally be preferred over integer zero or NULL for null pointer values in C++.
Using an object after its lifetime has ended can have undefined behavior.
An array contains a fixed number of elements once its bound is established.
Out-of-bounds array access has undefined behavior.
Built-in arrays do not automatically perform bounds checking.
std::string manages its own storage.
std::string knows its current size.
std::string is different from a null-terminated C string.
A C-style string is conventionally a null-terminated sequence of characters.
std::string can interoperate with C-style strings when required.
The c_str member function provides access to a null-terminated character representation of a std::string.
The lifetime of pointers obtained from a std::string must obey the string object's invalidation rules.
C++ provides namespaces.
A namespace groups names and helps prevent naming collisions.
std::cin is a standard input stream.
std::cerr is a standard error stream.
The insertion operator << is commonly used with output streams.
The extraction operator >> is commonly used with input streams.
std::getline can read a line from an input stream into a string.
<iostream> declares standard stream facilities.
<string> declares std::string.
<vector> declares std::vector.
<memory> declares standard smart pointer facilities.
<algorithm> declares many standard algorithms.
<filesystem> declares standard filesystem facilities in applicable C++ versions.
<thread> declares standard thread facilities.
<mutex> declares mutual exclusion facilities.
<optional> declares std::optional.
<map> declares std::map.
<unordered_map> declares std::unordered_map.
C++ classes define user-defined types.
The class keyword defines a class type.
The struct keyword can also define a class type in C++.
Class members are private by default when declared using class.
Class members are public by default when declared using struct.
The public access specifier makes members accessible according to public access rules.
The protected access specifier provides access associated with inheritance relationships.
Encapsulation combines data and operations behind a defined interface.
Encapsulation can prevent callers from depending directly on internal representation.
A constructor initializes an object.
A constructor has the same name as its class.
A destructor performs cleanup associated with the end of an object's lifetime.
A destructor is named using a tilde followed by the class name.
An object can have multiple constructors with different parameter lists.
Function overloading allows multiple functions with the same name when their parameter declarations distinguish the overloads.
The compiler performs overload resolution to determine which applicable function is called.
A default constructor can construct an object without caller-supplied arguments.
A copy constructor constructs an object from another object of the same type.
A move constructor can transfer resources from an eligible source object.
A move assignment operator can transfer resources during assignment.
The Rule of Zero recommends designing types so resource management is handled by members that already manage their own resources.
The Rule of Three describes the relationship among destructor, copy constructor, and copy assignment in resource-managing classes.
RAII stands for Resource Acquisition Is Initialization.
RAII associates resource ownership with object lifetime.
An RAII object acquires or represents a resource through its lifetime and releases it during destruction.
RAII can manage memory.
RAII can manage files.
RAII can manage locks.
RAII can manage sockets through suitable wrapper types.
RAII reduces the need for manually paired cleanup operations.
Stack unwinding destroys applicable automatic objects when an exception propagates through their scopes.
Destructors are therefore important to exception-safe resource management.
C++ supports dynamic memory allocation.
The new expression can allocate and construct an object dynamically.
The delete expression can destroy and release an object allocated by a compatible new expression.
new[] allocates an array dynamically.
delete[] releases an array allocated by a compatible new[] expression.
Using mismatched allocation and deallocation mechanisms has undefined behavior.
Manual new and delete are often unnecessary in modern C++ application code.
Standard containers and smart pointers can manage dynamic storage automatically.
std::unique_ptr represents exclusive ownership of a dynamically managed object.
A std::unique_ptr is normally not copyable.
A std::unique_ptr can be moved.
std::make_unique constructs an object managed by a std::unique_ptr.
A dynamically managed object owned by shared_ptr objects remains alive while qualifying shared ownership remains.
std::weak_ptr provides a non-owning relationship to an object managed by std::shared_ptr.
std::weak_ptr can help break shared ownership cycles.
std::make_shared constructs an object managed through shared ownership.
Shared ownership should be used when shared lifetime is actually required.
A raw pointer does not inherently express ownership.
A reference does not inherently express ownership.
Resource ownership should be clear at interface boundaries.
C++ supports inheritance.
Public inheritance commonly represents an is-a relationship.
Inheritance should be used when the modeled relationship warrants it.
Composition allows an object to contain objects of other types.
Composition is often preferable when the relationship is has-a rather than is-a.
A virtual member function supports dynamic dispatch.
Dynamic dispatch can select an overriding function according to the runtime type of an object.
The virtual keyword declares applicable virtual behavior.
A derived class can override a virtual function.
The override specifier asks the compiler to verify that a member function overrides a virtual base-class function.
The final specifier can prevent further overriding or derivation in applicable contexts.
A pure virtual function is declared using = 0.
A class containing an unimplemented pure virtual function is abstract.
An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly.
A base class intended for polymorphic deletion should generally provide an appropriate virtual destructor.
Deleting a derived object through a base pointer without an appropriate virtual destructor can produce undefined behavior.
C++ supports templates.
A function template describes a family of functions.
A class template describes a family of classes.
std::vector is a class template.
std::array is a class template.
Template code is instantiated for applicable template arguments.
A template is not the same mechanism as a preprocessor macro.
Templates participate in C++ type checking and language semantics.
A concept expresses requirements on template arguments.
The C++ standard library provides containers.
A container manages a collection of objects.
std::array stores a fixed-size contiguous sequence.
std::deque provides a double-ended sequence container.
std::list provides a doubly linked list.
std::forward_list provides a singly linked list.
std::map stores key-value pairs ordered by key according to its ordering relationship.
std::unordered_map stores key-value pairs using hashing.
std::set stores unique keys in ordered form.
std::unordered_set stores unique keys using hashing.
Container choice should reflect required operations and behavior rather than habit.
std::vector is often a useful default sequence container when contiguous dynamic storage is appropriate.
Standard containers manage their own storage.
Iterators can be invalidated by container operations.
Using an invalidated iterator can produce undefined behavior.
A range-based for loop can iterate through a standard container.
Using a reference in a range-based for loop can avoid copying each element.
Using const references can provide read-only iteration without unnecessary copies.
The standard library provides algorithms.
std::sort sorts an applicable range.
std::find searches an applicable range.
std::copy copies elements between appropriate ranges.
std::transform applies an operation while transforming elements.
Algorithms commonly operate using iterators or ranges.
Modern C++ ranges provide additional mechanisms for composing operations in applicable standards.
A lambda expression creates an unnamed function object.
Lambdas can capture values or references from their surrounding scope.
Capture behavior should be chosen deliberately.
Capturing by reference requires the referenced object's lifetime to remain valid when the lambda uses it.
C++ supports exceptions.
The throw expression raises an exception.
A try block defines code for which exceptions can be handled.
A catch clause handles matching exceptions.
Exceptions can propagate through multiple function calls.
Exception propagation performs stack unwinding.
Not every C++ project uses exceptions.
Error handling policy should be consistent within an interface.
std::exception is a standard base class used by many standard exceptions.
C++ also supports error reporting through return values.
std::optional can represent the presence or absence of a value.
std::variant can represent one value selected from a defined set of alternative types.
An interface should make failure behavior clear.
C++ has deterministic object lifetime rules for many scoped objects.
Automatic objects are destroyed when their lifetime ends according to language rules.
RAII uses deterministic destruction to manage resources.
Garbage collection is not the normal automatic memory-management model of C++.
A memory leak occurs when allocated resources become unreachable or are otherwise never released when they should be.
A dangling pointer or reference refers to an object whose lifetime has ended or an invalid location.
Use-after-free is an invalid access to storage after its lifetime has ended.
Buffer overflows can occur in C++ when unsafe memory access exceeds valid bounds.
Using C++ does not automatically prevent memory-safety defects.
Bounds-checked access is available through facilities such as the at member function of standard containers.
std::array::at provides checked element access.
Undefined behavior means the C++ standard imposes no requirements on the behavior of that operation.
Out-of-bounds memory access can have undefined behavior.
Dereferencing invalid pointers can have undefined behavior.
Data races can have undefined behavior.
Unspecified behavior allows one of several possibilities without requiring a particular choice.
Portable C++ distinguishes defined behavior from implementation-dependent assumptions.
C++ supports function overloading.
Operator overloading cannot create arbitrary new operator symbols.
Operator overloading should preserve understandable semantics.
The == operator represents equality comparison according to the applicable built-in or overloaded semantics.
The = operator represents assignment according to the applicable semantics.
Assignment and equality comparison are different operations.
The === operator is not a C++ operator.
C++ does not use === for strict equality.
The != operator represents inequality comparison.
The logical AND operator is &&.
The logical OR operator is ||.
The logical NOT operator is !.
The bitwise AND operator is &.
The bitwise XOR operator is ^.
The bitwise complement operator is ~.
Logical and bitwise operators are not interchangeable.
&& uses short-circuit evaluation.
|| uses short-circuit evaluation.
The -> operator accesses a member through a pointer-like expression according to applicable language rules.
The . operator accesses a member through an object expression.
The expression std::string uses the scope-resolution operator to identify string within namespace std.
C++ supports references in function parameters.
A parameter declared T& receives an lvalue reference to an object of type T.
Passing large objects by const reference can avoid copying when ownership is not transferred.
Passing by value can be appropriate when a function needs its own copy.
std::move performs a cast that enables move semantics where the target operation supports moving.
std::move does not itself move bytes or resources.
Code should not assume a specific value for a moved-from object unless its type guarantees one.
Perfect forwarding preserves relevant value-category information through generic interfaces.
C++ supports compile-time programming.
Templates can perform compile-time computation.
Concepts can express compile-time interface constraints.
static_assert checks a condition during compilation.
A failed static_assert prevents successful compilation.
Compile-time validation can prevent invalid configurations from reaching runtime.
Macros are not type-safe language functions.
Macros can cause surprising behavior through textual substitution.
constexpr variables and inline functions can often replace simple constant or function-like macros.
A conventional header guard uses #ifndef, #define, and #endif.
Templates are commonly defined in headers because their definitions must be available where required for instantiation.
The inline specifier participates in language linkage and definition rules and does not guarantee machine-code inlining.
Internal linkage restricts applicable names to a translation unit.
External linkage permits applicable names to refer to entities across translation units.
Namespaces organize source-level names.
An ABI can define calling conventions.
An ABI can define object layout.
An ABI can define symbol naming and name mangling conventions.
An ABI can define exception and runtime conventions.
C++ source compatibility does not guarantee C++ binary compatibility.
C++ ABI compatibility can depend on compiler, compiler version, standard library, build options, architecture, and platform ABI.
Changing the layout of a class exposed across a binary boundary can break ABI compatibility.
Changing function signatures can break binary compatibility.
C++ name mangling encodes information about entities into linker symbol names according to an implementation ABI.
extern "C" can request C language linkage for applicable declarations.
extern "C" is commonly used when exposing a C-compatible interface from C++.
A stable C interface can sometimes provide a simpler binary boundary between independently built components.
C++ classes should not be exposed across an ABI boundary without deliberately controlling compiler and ABI compatibility.
C++ standard library types should not be assumed binary-compatible across arbitrary compilers or runtimes.
A module system should define its binary contract explicitly.
Dynamically loaded modules must satisfy the ABI expected by their host.
ABI version validation can prevent incompatible modules from loading.
C++ itself does not define a universal operating-system dynamic-library API.
Dynamic loading commonly requires platform facilities or an abstraction layer.
Unix-like systems commonly use shared objects with the .so extension.
Windows commonly uses dynamic-link libraries with the .dll extension.
Platform-specific loading code can be isolated behind a common interface.
Platform independence does not require every implementation file to be identical.
Platform-independent behavior requires defined externally observable behavior to remain consistent where the system requires consistency.
std::filesystem::path represents filesystem paths.
std::thread represents a thread of execution.
std::jthread provides a joining thread abstraction in applicable modern C++ standards.
std::mutex provides mutual exclusion.
std::lock_guard manages a lock using RAII.
std::unique_lock provides a more flexible lock ownership wrapper.
A data race occurs when conflicting unsynchronized accesses meet the language's data-race conditions.
Mutexes and atomic operations serve different synchronization purposes.
Thread-safe design requires explicit synchronization of shared mutable state.
Immutable data can reduce synchronization requirements.
Concurrency does not automatically improve performance.
Thread creation and synchronization have costs.
C++ provides time utilities through std::chrono.
std::chrono provides clocks, durations, and time points.
System wall-clock time can change while a program is running.
Network programming commonly uses operating-system APIs or additional libraries.
TCP does not preserve application message boundaries.
C++ network code using TCP must define application-level framing.
A send operation does not imply one matching receive operation.
Partial reads and writes must be handled.
Connections can fail or close.
Network operations can block.
Timeouts can bound waiting behavior.
Protocol serialization should be explicitly defined.
Writing the raw bytes of a C++ object to a network connection does not automatically create a portable protocol.
Object layout can include padding.
Object layout can depend on ABI rules.
Byte order can differ across platforms.
Pointer values are not portable serialized data.
Virtual table pointers are not portable serialized data.
std::string's internal representation is not a portable serialization format.
std::vector's internal representation is not a portable serialization format.
Portable serialization defines the bytes independently of in-memory implementation details.
Deserialization must validate untrusted input before using lengths, indexes, or allocations derived from it.
Integer overflow should be considered when calculating buffer and allocation sizes.
Consensus-sensitive behavior should avoid undefined behavior.
Consensus-sensitive behavior should not depend on unspecified iteration order unless the protocol explicitly accounts for it.
std::unordered_map iteration order should not be treated as a portable deterministic ordering.
An ordered representation should be explicitly defined when deterministic ordering is required.
Floating-point calculations can have properties that make them unsuitable for some cross-platform consensus calculations.
Fixed-width integer representations can be easier to define precisely for deterministic protocols.
C++ exceptions crossing arbitrary module or ABI boundaries can create compatibility problems.
Memory allocated by one runtime should not automatically be freed by an incompatible runtime.
Ownership across binary boundaries should be explicitly specified.
An ABI should define who allocates and who releases transferred resources.
Opaque handles can hide C++ implementation details behind a stable interface.
A C-compatible ABI can use opaque pointers or handles to represent internal C++ objects.
Implementation details can remain private behind an ABI boundary.
A compiler warning identifies a condition the compiler considers noteworthy.
Warnings do not necessarily indicate an error.
Warnings should be reviewed rather than ignored automatically.
-Wall enables a useful set of warnings in GCC and compatible C++ compilers.
-Wextra enables additional warnings.
-Wpedantic requests diagnostics concerning strict language conformance.
Treating warnings as errors can be useful in controlled build environments.
A warning-free build does not prove that software is correct.
Tests provide evidence about tested behavior.
A unit test exercises a bounded unit of functionality.
A regression test protects previously established behavior.
A negative test verifies correct handling of invalid or prohibited conditions.
Tests should be deterministic when the behavior being tested is deterministic.
A passing test does not prove that every possible defect is absent.
Static analysis examines source or program properties without ordinary execution.
AddressSanitizer can detect classes of memory errors in supported compiler environments.
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer can detect classes of undefined behavior in supported compiler environments.
Code review complements automated testing.
Resource ownership should be visible from interfaces where practical.
RAII should be preferred for resource lifetime management where appropriate.
Raw owning pointers should be avoided when an ownership type expresses the requirement more clearly.
Global mutable state can make systems harder to test and reason about.
Small interfaces reduce unnecessary coupling.
Small functions with clear responsibilities are easier to understand and test.
Dependencies should be introduced deliberately.
Additional libraries increase dependency and deployment considerations.
The standard library should be considered before introducing a third-party dependency for functionality it already provides adequately.
Correctness is more important than clever syntax.
Readable code is easier to maintain than unnecessarily complicated code.
Modern language features should be used when they improve correctness or clarity.
Modern C++ does not mean using every available language feature.
Simple code can still be modern C++.
A deterministic system should make important behavior explicit.
A deterministic system should explicitly define serialization.
A deterministic system should explicitly define protocol behavior.
A platform abstraction should isolate operating-system-specific behavior.
The common interface should define behavior rather than merely hide filenames.
Platform-specific backends should produce equivalent required results where platform independence requires equivalence.
C++ can be used for command-line applications.
C++ can be used for desktop applications.
C++ can be used for libraries.
C++ can be used for embedded systems where an appropriate implementation is available.
C++ can be used for performance-sensitive software.
C++ can interoperate with C through carefully designed interfaces.
C++ can provide higher-level resource-management abstractions while retaining access to lower-level facilities.
C++ does not automatically make a program object-oriented.
C++ does not require classes for every problem.
C++ does not automatically prevent memory corruption.
C++ does not automatically provide garbage collection.
C++ does not automatically provide thread safety.
C++ does not automatically provide deterministic behavior.
C++ provides language and library mechanisms from which those properties can be deliberately engineered.
RAII, standard containers, value semantics, smart pointers, and strong interfaces can reduce classes of resource-management defects.
A well-designed C++ program makes ownership and lifetime understandable.
A well-designed C++ system separates platform-specific implementation from platform-independent behavior where portability is required.
A well-designed C++ binary interface treats ABI compatibility as an explicit engineering constraint.
C# is a general-purpose programming language.
C# is pronounced C sharp.
C# is a statically typed programming language.
C# is commonly used with the .NET platform.
C# and .NET are related but are not the same thing.
C# is the programming language.
.NET provides runtimes, libraries, tools, and application frameworks used by C# and other languages.
C# source files commonly use the .cs filename extension.
C# is case-sensitive.
C# supports procedural programming.
C# supports generic programming.
Modern .NET normally compiles C# source into Common Intermediate Language and associated metadata.
Common Intermediate Language is commonly abbreviated CIL.
The .NET runtime can use just-in-time compilation to translate CIL into native machine code.
Just-in-time compilation is commonly abbreviated JIT.
.NET can also support ahead-of-time compilation in applicable deployment models.
Ahead-of-time compilation is commonly abbreviated AOT.
The Common Language Runtime is commonly abbreviated CLR.
The CLR is the execution environment used by .NET implementations that provide the CLR.
The runtime provides services including managed memory, type handling, exception handling, and execution support.
Managed code executes under services provided by the .NET runtime.
Managed code does not mean that application logic is automatically correct or secure.
A C# application can still use unmanaged resources.
C# can interoperate with native code.
C# blocks are enclosed by braces.
C# line comments begin with //.
C# block comments begin with /* and end with */.
XML documentation comments commonly begin with ///.
An identifier names a program entity.
C# identifiers are case-sensitive.
A variable stores a value according to its type.
A local variable must be definitely assigned before its value is read.
C# is statically typed.
The type of an expression is determined according to C# language rules.
The var keyword requests local variable type inference.
A variable declared with var still has a statically determined type.
The dynamic type defers certain operations to runtime binding.
dynamic and var have different meanings.
bool represents Boolean values.
sbyte represents a signed 8-bit integer.
short represents a signed 16-bit integer.
int represents a signed 32-bit integer.
long represents a signed 64-bit integer.
ulong represents an unsigned 64-bit integer.
char represents a UTF-16 code unit.
decimal represents a decimal floating-point value designed for high-precision decimal calculations.
string represents a sequence of characters.
object is the root type from which other .NET types ultimately derive according to the .NET type system.
C# distinguishes value types from reference types.
A reference type variable holds a reference to an object or can hold null where nullable.
Enumerations are value types.
Classes are reference types.
Arrays are reference types.
Delegates are reference types.
Strings are reference types.
A class defines a reference type.
A struct defines a value type.
The class keyword declares a class.
The struct keyword declares a structure.
The enum keyword declares an enumeration.
An enum defines a set of named constants backed by an integral type.
A namespace organizes related types.
Namespaces help prevent naming collisions.
The namespace keyword declares a namespace.
A using directive does not copy a library into a program.
Types can contain fields.
Types can contain properties.
Types can contain methods.
Types can contain events.
A field stores data associated with a type or object.
A property exposes value access through accessors.
A property can define a get accessor.
A property can define a set accessor.
An init accessor permits initialization in applicable object initialization contexts while restricting later assignment.
A method defines executable behavior.
A method can accept parameters.
A method can return a value.
C# passes arguments by value by default.
Passing a reference-type variable by value passes the reference value by value.
Passing a reference value by value does not copy the referenced object.
The ref modifier supports passing an argument by reference.
The out modifier supports passing a variable by reference for output.
The in modifier supports readonly reference passing in applicable contexts.
A ref parameter and an ordinary value parameter have different semantics.
A constructor has the same name as its containing type.
A class can define multiple constructors.
The new operator can create an object or value according to the target type.
Creating a managed object normally allocates storage managed by the runtime.
C# uses automatic garbage collection for managed memory.
Garbage collection reclaims managed objects that are no longer reachable according to runtime rules.
Garbage collection does not automatically release every type of unmanaged resource promptly.
IDisposable defines a standard pattern for releasing resources.
The Dispose method releases resources according to an object's implementation.
The using statement can ensure IDisposable resources are disposed when control leaves the applicable scope.
The using declaration can associate disposal with the enclosing scope.
Garbage collection and deterministic resource disposal solve different problems.
A finalizer can participate in cleanup of unmanaged resources when a type requires one.
Finalizers are not a substitute for deterministic disposal.
A finalizer's execution time is not deterministic.
The public access modifier allows access according to public accessibility rules.
The private access modifier restricts access to the containing type.
The internal access modifier provides access within the same assembly according to C# accessibility rules.
protected internal and private protected provide combined accessibility forms.
Class members are private by default when no applicable access modifier is supplied.
A namespace-level type is internal by default when no applicable access modifier is supplied.
C# supports inheritance.
A class can derive from another class.
A class can have only one direct base class.
A class can implement multiple interfaces.
Inheritance and interface implementation are different mechanisms.
The base keyword accesses members of the base class in applicable contexts.
The virtual modifier permits applicable members to be overridden.
The override modifier provides a new implementation of an inherited virtual member.
The abstract modifier can declare incomplete behavior requiring implementation by a derived non-abstract type.
An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly.
A sealed override prevents further overriding of that member.
Polymorphism allows code to operate through a base type or interface while runtime dispatch selects applicable overridden behavior.
An interface defines a contract that implementing types can satisfy.
The interface keyword declares an interface.
Interfaces are commonly used to separate contracts from implementations.
Composition allows one object to contain or reference other objects.
Composition and inheritance model different relationships.
Composition can reduce coupling when inheritance is not required.
C# supports method overloading.
Method overloading allows methods to share a name when their parameter signatures distinguish the overloads.
C# does not permit arbitrary new operator symbols to be invented.
The assignment operator is =.
The equality operator is ==.
The inequality operator is !=.
The behavior of == depends on the types being compared and any applicable overloaded operator.
ReferenceEquals can determine whether two reference variables refer to the same object.
Value equality and reference identity are different concepts.
The Equals method participates in value equality conventions.
Types overriding Equals should follow the associated equality contract.
GetHashCode should be consistent with Equals when objects are used in hash-based collections.
The relational operators include <, <=, >, and >= where supported.
The logical AND operator is &&.
The logical OR operator is ||.
The logical NOT operator is !.
The bitwise AND operator is &.
The bitwise XOR operator is ^.
The bitwise complement operator is ~.
&& uses short-circuit evaluation.
|| uses short-circuit evaluation.
The null-coalescing operator is ??.
The null-coalescing assignment operator is ??=.
The member access operator is a period.
The null-forgiving operator is ! in applicable nullable-reference contexts.
A nullable value type can represent either a value or null.
The syntax int? represents a nullable int.
Nullable reference types provide compile-time annotations and analysis concerning possible null references.
Nullable reference type analysis does not make null reference errors impossible.
A non-nullable reference annotation expresses an intended nullability contract to compiler analysis.
A nullable reference annotation such as string? indicates that null is an expected value according to nullable analysis.
A NullReferenceException can occur when code dereferences a null reference.
Null input should be validated where an interface does not permit it.
The is operator can test type and pattern relationships.
A switch expression can produce a value according to matching arms.
The if statement conditionally executes code.
The else clause provides an alternative branch.
The switch statement selects behavior among cases.
The foreach statement iterates over an enumerable sequence.
The while statement repeats while its condition remains true.
The continue statement advances to the next applicable loop iteration.
The return statement exits a method and can return a value.
The throw statement raises an exception.
C# supports exception handling.
A catch block handles matching exceptions.
A finally block executes cleanup behavior when control leaves the associated try construct according to language rules.
Exceptions can propagate through multiple method calls.
System.Exception is the base type for standard exception objects.
Exception messages can provide diagnostic information.
Sensitive information should not be exposed through exception messages presented to untrusted users.
An exception can contain an inner exception.
C# supports generics.
Generics allow types and methods to operate with type parameters.
A generic type can be reused with different type arguments.
List<T> is a generic collection type.
Dictionary<TKey,TValue> is a generic key-value collection.
Queue<T> is a generic first-in-first-out collection.
Generic constraints restrict the types that can be supplied as type arguments.
Generics differ from storing all values as object.
Boxing converts a value type to an object or implemented interface representation.
Unboxing extracts a value type from a compatible boxed representation.
Unboxing requires compatible type information.
Unnecessary boxing can create allocations and overhead.
Accessing an array outside its valid bounds throws an IndexOutOfRangeException.
The Length property reports an array's number of elements.
List<T> provides a dynamically sized sequence.
The Count property reports the number of elements in many collection types.
Count and capacity are different concepts.
A collection's capacity can exceed its current element count.
Dictionary<TKey,TValue> associates keys with values.
Dictionary keys should have stable equality and hash behavior while used as keys.
Collections are not automatically thread-safe.
System.Collections.Concurrent provides concurrent collection types.
C# supports delegates.
A delegate represents references to methods with a compatible signature.
Delegates can reference static or instance methods.
Action represents a delegate that returns no value.
Func represents a delegate that returns a value.
Predicate<T> represents a delegate returning a Boolean result for a value.
Lambda expressions use the => syntax.
A lambda can capture variables from its surrounding scope.
Captured variables can outlive the immediate execution of the declaring method depending on the resulting closure.
Closures can introduce additional object lifetimes and allocations.
C# events provide a publish-and-subscribe mechanism based on delegates.
The event keyword restricts direct invocation and assignment according to event semantics.
Subscribers register handlers with an event.
Publishers raise events.
C# supports Language Integrated Query.
Language Integrated Query is commonly abbreviated LINQ.
LINQ operations can be written using method syntax.
Where filters a sequence.
Select projects elements into another form.
OrderBy orders elements according to a key.
GroupBy groups elements according to a key.
Any determines whether a sequence contains an element satisfying applicable conditions.
All determines whether all applicable elements satisfy a condition.
First returns the first applicable element and throws when no element exists according to its contract.
FirstOrDefault returns the first applicable element or a default value when none exists.
Single requires exactly one applicable element according to its contract.
SingleOrDefault permits zero or one applicable element according to its contract.
LINQ queries can use deferred execution.
Deferred execution means evaluation can occur when results are enumerated rather than when the query is constructed.
Not every LINQ operation uses deferred execution.
ToList materializes a sequence into a List<T>.
ToArray materializes a sequence into an array.
Repeated enumeration can repeat underlying work for deferred queries.
A LINQ expression used with a database provider is not necessarily executed as ordinary in-memory C# code.
The async modifier identifies methods that can contain await according to language rules.
async and await do not automatically create a new thread.
Task represents an asynchronous operation.
Task<T> represents an asynchronous operation that produces a value.
An async method commonly returns Task, Task<T>, ValueTask, or ValueTask<T> according to its requirements.
An async void method should generally be limited to scenarios such as event handlers where the contract requires void.
Blocking synchronously on asynchronous operations can cause performance or synchronization problems.
Asynchronous I/O can allow a thread to perform other work while waiting for external operations.
CancellationToken communicates cancellation requests to cooperating operations.
Cancellation is cooperative.
A CancellationToken does not forcibly terminate arbitrary executing code.
CancellationTokenSource creates and controls cancellation tokens.
Timeouts and cancellation are related but distinct concepts.
C# supports multithreading.
Thread represents an operating-system thread abstraction provided by .NET.
The thread pool manages reusable worker threads.
Task-based programming commonly uses the thread pool for CPU-bound scheduled work.
lock provides mutual exclusion around a critical section.
The lock statement uses a synchronization object.
Code should not normally lock on publicly accessible objects.
Deadlock can occur when execution paths wait indefinitely for resources held by one another.
Shared mutable state requires synchronization when accessed concurrently.
Interlocked provides atomic operations for supported scenarios.
volatile affects certain memory-access semantics but is not a replacement for all synchronization.
A race condition occurs when program results depend improperly on timing or ordering between operations.
Repeated string concatenation can create multiple intermediate strings.
StringBuilder provides mutable string-building functionality.
String interpolation can embed expressions into formatted strings.
An interpolated string commonly begins with $.
The syntax $"Hello {name}" is an interpolated string.
String comparisons should use an appropriate comparison rule.
StringComparison can specify ordinal or culture-sensitive comparison behavior.
Ordinal comparison compares string data according to ordinal rules.
Culture-sensitive comparisons can vary according to cultural rules.
Security-sensitive identifiers should not casually depend on culture-sensitive string comparison.
C# supports records.
A record is a type designed to support value-oriented data modeling.
Records can provide compiler-generated value equality behavior.
Record classes are reference types.
Records do not make contained mutable objects immutable.
with expressions can create modified copies of records and other supported types.
C# supports tuples.
Tuple elements can have names.
Tuples are useful for small grouped return values when a dedicated domain type is unnecessary.
C# supports extension methods.
Extension methods do not actually add instance members to the extended type.
Extension method resolution follows compile-time rules.
Attributes attach metadata to program elements.
Attributes derive from System.Attribute.
Reflection can inspect metadata and type information at runtime.
Reflection can discover types and members.
Heavy reflection can increase complexity and runtime cost.
Assembly metadata describes compiled .NET components.
An assembly is a deployment and versioning unit containing .NET code and metadata.
A .NET .dll is not necessarily equivalent in binary interface behavior to an unmanaged native DLL.
Managed assemblies contain metadata understood by the .NET runtime.
Namespaces and assemblies are different concepts.
An assembly can contain types from multiple namespaces.
NuGet is a package-management system commonly used by .NET projects.
A NuGet package can contain libraries, build assets, analyzers, tools, or other package resources.
Dependencies should be selected deliberately.
The .NET SDK provides command-line development tools.
dotnet is the primary command-line driver for modern .NET SDK workflows.
dotnet run builds and runs an applicable project.
dotnet publish prepares an application for deployment.
A .csproj file describes a C# project using MSBuild project format.
MSBuild is the build system used by .NET project tooling.
Project files can define target frameworks.
A target framework identifies the .NET API surface and runtime family targeted by a project.
Target frameworks are commonly represented using target framework monikers.
A project can target more than one framework.
Targeting a framework does not mean every machine has that runtime installed.
Framework-dependent deployment requires an appropriate runtime on the target system.
Single-file deployment can package application components into a reduced number of deployment files according to .NET publishing behavior.
Trimming can remove code considered unused during publishing.
Reflection and dynamic loading can complicate trimming because required code may not be statically obvious.
Native AOT can compile applicable .NET applications ahead of time to native code.
AOT deployment has different compatibility and runtime characteristics from ordinary JIT deployment.
C# can be used for command-line applications.
C# can be used for web applications.
C# can be used for web APIs.
C# can be used for libraries.
C# can be used for cloud services.
C# can be used for games with frameworks and engines that support C#.
C# can be used for mobile and cross-platform applications with applicable .NET frameworks.
ASP.NET Core is a framework for building web applications and services using .NET.
ASP.NET Core can be used with C#.
A web application receives HTTP requests and produces HTTP responses.
Middleware forms a processing pipeline for HTTP requests and responses in ASP.NET Core.
Middleware can inspect, modify, handle, or pass requests to later middleware.
An endpoint performs application behavior associated with a matched request.
Dependency injection is built into common ASP.NET Core application patterns.
Dependency injection supplies dependencies to components rather than requiring those components to construct every dependency themselves.
Dependency injection and interfaces can reduce coupling when used appropriately.
Dependency injection does not automatically make a design correct.
Configuration provides application settings.
Secrets should not be committed directly into source code.
Environment variables can provide configuration values.
Logging records application events and diagnostics.
Logs should not unnecessarily contain passwords, tokens, private keys, or other sensitive data.
Structured logging records fields in a machine-processable form.
Database access can be performed using database providers or libraries.
ADO.NET provides core .NET database-access abstractions.
DbConnection represents a database connection abstraction.
DbDataReader provides forward-only reading of query results according to provider behavior.
Database commands should use parameters rather than constructing SQL by concatenating untrusted input.
Parameterized SQL does not automatically make dynamically constructed SQL identifiers safe.
Database input remains untrusted input.
Entity Framework Core is an object-relational mapping framework for .NET.
Entity Framework Core is commonly abbreviated EF Core.
An object-relational mapper is commonly abbreviated ORM.
Using an ORM does not eliminate the need to understand database behavior.
Database transactions group operations into an atomic unit according to database transaction semantics.
Applications should handle database failures explicitly.
Database connections are resources and should be released appropriately.
Connection pooling can reuse underlying database connections.
Disposing a pooled connection object normally returns its underlying connection to the pool according to provider behavior rather than necessarily closing the physical connection immediately.
C# applications can interact with MySQL through an appropriate .NET database provider.
C# applications can interact with MariaDB through an appropriate compatible provider.
C# applications can interact with PostgreSQL through an appropriate .NET provider.
C# applications can interact with SQLite through an appropriate .NET provider.
Database-provider behavior is not defined by the C# language.
C# supports file operations through .NET libraries.
System.IO provides common file and stream APIs.
File provides static convenience methods for common file operations.
FileStream provides stream-based file access.
