Assertions should not replace validation of untrusted production input.
Logging can provide operational evidence.
Logs should provide enough context for diagnosis without exposing unnecessary secrets.
Structured error handling is preferable to silently ignoring failures.
An operation that fails should return or report failure according to its interface contract.
Catching Exception indiscriminately can hide important failures when used carelessly.
A catch block should handle exceptions it can meaningfully address or report.
An empty catch block can conceal defects and operational failures.
Cancellation should not automatically be reported as an unexpected failure when cancellation was requested and is part of the interface contract.
A deterministic operation produces the same required result for the same defined input and state.
Hash-based collection enumeration order should not be used as a protocol-defined ordering unless explicitly guaranteed by the applicable contract.
Current observed iteration order should not be mistaken for a permanent cross-runtime protocol guarantee.
Culture-sensitive formatting can produce different text under different culture settings.
InvariantCulture provides culture-independent formatting behavior for applicable conversions.
Date and time serialization should use explicitly defined formats.
Time zones should not be inferred when protocol semantics require an explicit time basis.
DateTime and DateTimeOffset represent different time concepts.
UTC is commonly used for interoperable timestamps.
A deterministic protocol should specify timestamp representation.
A deterministic protocol should specify string encoding.
C# strings use UTF-16 code units internally according to .NET string representation semantics.
Internal string representation and network encoding are different concepts.
BinaryWriter and BinaryReader can read and write primitive values but protocol byte order and format must still be deliberately specified.
BitConverter behavior can depend on machine endianness for applicable conversions.
BinaryPrimitives provides explicit endian-aware operations for primitive values.
Cross-platform protocols should explicitly define byte order.
Cross-platform behavior should not depend on filesystem path separators.
Cross-platform behavior should not assume Windows-only drive letters.
Cross-platform behavior should not assume Unix-only root paths.
Path manipulation should use platform-aware facilities where filesystem semantics are intended to be platform dependent.
Protocol paths and filesystem paths are different concepts.
A protocol can define its own platform-independent path syntax.
.NET supports multiple operating systems.
C# applications can run on Windows when supported by their target runtime and dependencies.
C# applications can run on Linux when supported by their target runtime and dependencies.
C# applications can run on macOS when supported by their target runtime and dependencies.
Cross-platform .NET code can still contain platform-specific dependencies.
Platform-specific behavior should be isolated when portability is required.
OperatingSystem provides runtime operating-system checks in applicable modern .NET versions.
Conditional compilation can select code according to defined compilation symbols.
Conditional compilation and runtime platform selection solve different problems.
Platform-specific implementations should preserve common required behavior.
Platform independence concerns behavior, not merely whether code compiles on multiple systems.
The partial modifier allows one type definition to be split across multiple source declarations.
Partial methods provide related compile-time extension mechanisms according to C# version rules.
Generated code commonly uses partial types to cooperate with developer-written code.
C# supports indexers.
An indexer allows instances of a type to be accessed using indexing syntax.
The base keyword refers to the base-class portion or members in applicable contexts.
A static class cannot be instantiated.
A static class contains only static members according to C# rules.
Static mutable state is shared across applicable callers within its runtime context.
readonly fields restrict assignment according to readonly field rules.
A const field represents a compile-time constant.
const and readonly have different initialization and runtime semantics.
C# supports local functions.
A local function is declared within another member.
Local functions can help keep bounded helper behavior close to its caller.
Expression-bodied syntax can make simple members concise.
Concise syntax should not be used when it makes behavior harder to understand.
Named arguments identify parameters by name at a call site.
C# supports optional parameters.
Optional parameter default values become part of the calling contract.
Changing an optional parameter default can have versioning implications for already compiled callers.
The params modifier permits a variable number of arguments for an applicable parameter.
params can cause an array or compatible collection representation to be created according to the language and runtime behavior.
C# supports ref returns and ref locals.
Reference-return features expose aliases to storage and require careful lifetime reasoning.
ref struct types have restricted lifetimes designed to prevent unsafe escape of stack-associated data.
C# supports pattern-based disposal and enumeration in addition to interface-based mechanisms in applicable language versions.
await using supports asynchronous disposal.
IAsyncDisposable defines asynchronous cleanup.
await foreach supports asynchronous enumeration.
IAsyncEnumerable<T> represents an asynchronous sequence.
Asynchronous enumeration can produce values over time without materializing the entire sequence first.
Asynchronous code should propagate cancellation when its interface supports cancellation.
Fire-and-forget asynchronous work can lose errors or outlive required state if not deliberately managed.
Application shutdown should account for active background operations.
BackgroundService provides a common abstraction for long-running hosted .NET service work.
A service should handle startup failures explicitly.
A service should not report itself ready before required initialization succeeds.
Health checks can report service health.
A health check is evidence about the checks it performs, not proof that every application function is correct.
A dependency being unavailable can make an operation unavailable without requiring the entire process to crash.
Failure boundaries should be defined according to system architecture.
A 503 response is appropriate when an HTTP service is genuinely unable to handle a request because required service capability is unavailable.
A knowledge failure and a service availability failure are different conditions.
Errors should describe the actual failure condition.
A short supported result is preferable to a detailed unsupported result.
C# provides mechanisms for building strongly typed managed software.
.NET provides the runtime and libraries commonly used to execute C# applications.
Garbage collection manages ordinary managed memory but does not remove the need for resource ownership and disposal.
Classes and structs provide implementations and data models.
Generics provide reusable type-safe abstractions.
Delegates and events provide callback and notification mechanisms.
Exceptions provide structured failure propagation.
Tasks represent asynchronous operations.
The standard .NET libraries provide collections, files, networking, JSON, cryptography, threading, and other facilities.
C# can interoperate with native C and C++ code through deliberately defined native interfaces.
Deterministic C# systems require explicit control of serialization, ordering, culture, time, numeric behavior, and external state.
Go is a general-purpose programming language.
Go is a compiled programming language.
Go is statically typed.
Go is case-sensitive.
Go is designed to support simple and maintainable software.
Go is commonly used for servers, command-line tools, network services, infrastructure software, and distributed systems.
Go source files use the .go filename extension.
Execution of an ordinary Go executable begins with the main function in package main.
A Go library uses a package name other than main according to its design.
The go command provides standard tooling for building, testing, formatting, and managing Go code.
go build compiles Go packages and their dependencies.
go run compiles and runs a Go program.
go test runs tests for selected Go packages.
go fmt formats Go source code.
gofmt provides standardized Go source formatting.
go vet examines Go code for certain suspicious constructs.
A successful build does not prove that a Go program is correct.
A successful test does not prove that every possible defect is absent.
Go modules provide dependency and version management.
A go.mod file defines a Go module.
A Go module can contain multiple packages.
A package and a module are different concepts.
The go directive in go.mod identifies the Go language version associated with module semantics according to Go tooling rules.
Checksums for downloaded module content can be recorded in go.sum.
go mod tidy updates module requirements and sums according to packages used by the module.
Imports make functionality from other packages available to a Go source file.
The import declaration specifies imported packages.
Unused imports are compile-time errors.
Unused local variables are compile-time errors.
An identifier names a program entity.
Identifiers beginning with an uppercase letter are exported from their package when applicable.
Exported identifiers form part of a package's accessible API.
Go does not use public and private keywords for ordinary package-level visibility.
Package visibility is determined by identifier capitalization.
The := syntax provides short variable declaration inside functions where permitted.
Short variable declaration infers types from assigned expressions.
Go does not use := as a general assignment operator in every context.
The = operator performs assignment.
Constants are declared with the const keyword.
Constants can be typed or untyped.
Untyped constants can participate in conversions according to Go constant rules.
The zero value is the default value of a variable when no explicit initialization supplies another value.
The zero value of an integer type is zero.
The zero value of bool is false.
The zero value of a slice is nil.
The zero value of a map is nil.
The zero value of a channel is nil.
A useful Go type can often be designed so its zero value is meaningful.
bool is Go's Boolean type.
string represents an immutable sequence of bytes conventionally containing UTF-8 encoded text.
A Go string can contain arbitrary bytes.
A Go string is immutable.
A rune is an alias for int32.
A rune commonly represents a Unicode code point.
A byte is an alias for uint8.
int is a signed integer type whose size is implementation dependent within Go's requirements.
uint is an unsigned integer type whose size is implementation dependent within Go's requirements.
int8 is a signed 8-bit integer.
uint8 is an unsigned 8-bit integer.
uint16 is an unsigned 16-bit integer.
int32 is a signed 32-bit integer.
uint32 is an unsigned 32-bit integer.
int64 is a signed 64-bit integer.
uintptr is an unsigned integer type large enough to hold the bit pattern of a pointer according to its defined use.
complex64 represents complex numbers with float32 components.
complex128 represents complex numbers with float64 components.
Go requires explicit conversions in many places where some languages perform implicit numeric conversions.
The syntax T(x) converts x to type T when the conversion is permitted.
A conversion and a type assertion are different operations.
Go arrays have fixed length.
An array's length is part of its type.
Accessing an array or slice outside its valid bounds causes a runtime panic.
An array value contains its elements.
A slice describes a portion of an underlying array.
A slice has a length.
A slice has a capacity.
len reports a slice's current length.
cap reports a slice's current capacity.
A nil slice has length zero.
A non-nil empty slice can also have length zero.
A nil slice and a non-nil empty slice are not identical states.
append can allocate a new underlying array.
Code must use the slice value returned by append.
Appending to one slice can affect another slice when both share an underlying array and no reallocation separates them.
A subslice can keep an underlying array reachable.
copy copies elements between slices.
A map stores key-value associations.
Map keys must be comparable.
The make function can initialize a map.
A nil map can be read from.
Writing to a nil map causes a runtime panic.
Reading a missing map key returns the value type's zero value.
The comma-ok form can distinguish a missing map key from a stored zero value.
Map iteration order is not specified.
A program must not depend on map iteration order for deterministic protocol behavior.
A map is not safe for unsynchronized concurrent read and write access.
A struct groups named fields into a single type.
The struct keyword defines structure types.
Struct fields can have different types.
A struct value contains its fields.
Assigning a struct normally copies its value.
A pointer can refer to a struct.
The . operator accesses struct fields and methods.
Go permits convenient field selection through pointers to structs.
Go has pointers.
The & operator obtains an address where permitted.
The * operator dereferences a pointer.
Dereferencing a nil pointer causes a runtime panic.
Go does not support ordinary pointer arithmetic.
The unsafe package provides operations that bypass some normal Go type and memory protections.
unsafe should be bounded and justified when required.
A function is declared with the func keyword.
A function can accept parameters.
A function can return values.
A Go function can return multiple values.
Multiple return values are commonly used to return a result and an error.
A function's signature describes its parameters and results.
Function arguments are passed by value.
Passing a pointer passes the pointer value by value.
Passing a slice passes a slice descriptor by value.
Passing a map passes a map value referring to runtime-managed map state.
Passing a channel passes a channel value.
A variadic function accepts a variable number of trailing arguments.
A variadic parameter uses ... before its element type.
Functions are values in Go.
A function value can be assigned to a variable.
A function can be returned from another function.
A closure is a function value that references variables from an enclosing scope.
Captured variables can remain reachable while a closure requires them.
Methods associate functions with named receiver types according to Go rules.
A method declaration includes a receiver.
Pointer receivers can modify the referenced receiver.
A type does not need to explicitly declare that it implements an interface.
Go interfaces are satisfied implicitly.
An interface defines a set of methods and type constraints according to its context.
A value implements an ordinary interface when its method set satisfies the interface's required methods.
Implicit interface satisfaction reduces direct coupling between an interface and its implementations.
Small interfaces are commonly easier to satisfy and compose.
The empty interface interface{} can hold values of any type.
Using any sacrifices some compile-time knowledge about the contained dynamic value.
A type assertion extracts or tests the dynamic value held by an interface.
The syntax value.(T) performs a type assertion.
A failed single-result type assertion causes a panic.
A type switch selects behavior according to the dynamic type held by an interface.
An interface value conceptually contains dynamic type and dynamic value information.
An interface holding a typed nil pointer is not necessarily itself a nil interface.
Nil interface behavior should be understood when designing error and interface APIs.
The error type is a built-in interface used to represent errors.
An error value is nil when no error is being reported by ordinary convention.
Functions commonly return an error as their final result.
Callers should inspect errors when failure matters.
Errors are ordinary values in Go.
Go does not use exceptions for ordinary error handling.
The errors package provides standard error utilities.
fmt.Errorf can format an error.
errors.Is tests whether an error matches a target through supported wrapping behavior.
errors.As searches an error chain for an assignable error type.
Wrapped errors can preserve causal information.
Error messages should provide useful context.
Error messages should not expose unnecessary secrets.
A function should return errors according to its interface contract.
A caller should not convert every error into a generic service failure without understanding the error.
Different failure conditions should remain distinguishable when system behavior depends on them.
panic stops ordinary execution of the current goroutine and begins panicking.
Deferred functions execute while a panic unwinds the goroutine's stack.
recover can regain control of a panicking goroutine when called in an appropriate deferred function.
panic is not a replacement for ordinary error returns.
Recovering from a panic does not automatically make corrupted application state valid.
Arguments to a deferred function call are evaluated when the defer statement executes.
defer is commonly used for cleanup.
A file can be closed with a deferred Close call after successful opening.
Deferred cleanup should only be registered after the resource has actually been acquired.
Go has garbage-collected memory management.
The runtime automatically reclaims unreachable managed memory.
Garbage collection does not eliminate the need to close files.
Garbage collection does not eliminate the need to close network connections.
Garbage collection does not eliminate the need to release locks.
Garbage collection does not automatically manage every external resource.
Resource ownership should remain explicit.
Keeping references to unneeded objects can prevent their memory from being reclaimed.
Memory leaks can occur logically when programs retain objects that are no longer needed.
The new function allocates storage for a value and returns a pointer to its zero value.
new and make serve different purposes.
Go supports concurrency as a core language feature.
A goroutine is a lightweight concurrent function execution managed by the Go runtime.
A goroutine is not the same abstraction as an operating-system thread.
The Go runtime schedules goroutines onto execution resources.
Creating a goroutine does not guarantee that it immediately executes.
Concurrent execution does not imply parallel execution.
Parallel execution and concurrency are related but distinct concepts.
Channels have an element type.
The make function creates channels.
An unbuffered channel has no value buffer.
Channel capacity is specified when a buffered channel is created.
Sending to a full buffered channel blocks until space becomes available or another control mechanism intervenes.
Receiving from an empty open channel blocks until a value becomes available or another control mechanism intervenes.
Receiving from a closed channel returns remaining buffered values before returning the element type's zero value with a false receive status after the buffer is drained.
The comma-ok receive form can determine whether a channel is closed and drained.
Sending on a closed channel causes a panic.
Closing an already closed channel causes a panic.
Receiving from a nil channel blocks indefinitely.
Sending to a nil channel blocks indefinitely.
The sender is commonly responsible for closing a channel when closure is required.
A channel does not need to be closed merely because no more values happen to be sent if receivers do not depend on closure.
The close function indicates that no more values will be sent on a channel.
A for range loop can receive values from a channel until it is closed and drained.
The select statement waits on multiple channel operations.
A select with a default case can perform a non-blocking channel operation.
When multiple select cases are ready, one ready communication is selected according to Go's selection rules.
A nil channel can be used to disable a select case dynamically.
Channels are one synchronization mechanism.
Mutexes are another synchronization mechanism.
sync.Mutex provides mutual exclusion.
A Mutex must not be copied after first use.
Lock acquires a mutex.
Unlock releases a mutex.
sync.RWMutex supports shared read locking and exclusive write locking.
sync.WaitGroup can wait for a collection of goroutines to finish.
WaitGroup usage must obey its synchronization contract.
sync.Cond provides condition-variable behavior.
sync.Map provides a concurrent map specialized for particular use cases.
An ordinary map with explicit locking is often clearer when sync.Map's specialized behavior is unnecessary.
The sync/atomic package provides low-level atomic memory operations.
Atomic operations and mutexes solve different synchronization problems.
Shared mutable data requires synchronization when accessed concurrently.
A data race occurs when concurrent accesses violate Go's memory synchronization requirements.
Data races can produce incorrect behavior.
The Go race detector can identify many data races during instrumented executions.
The -race option enables race detection on supported platforms and commands.
A race-detector run cannot prove that every possible data race is absent.
Concurrency should be introduced when it serves the system rather than merely because goroutines are inexpensive.
Goroutine lifetimes should be controlled.
A goroutine that waits forever when it should terminate is a goroutine leak.
Cancellation allows concurrent operations to stop when their work is no longer required.
The context package carries deadlines, cancellation signals, and request-scoped values across API boundaries.
context.Context is commonly passed as the first parameter to functions that participate in request-scoped cancellation.
A Context should not normally be stored in a struct merely for convenience.
A cancelled context does not forcibly terminate arbitrary code.
Context deadlines can bound how long operations are permitted to wait.
Context values should be used for request-scoped data rather than as a substitute for ordinary function parameters.
A function accepting Context should propagate cancellation to applicable subordinate operations.
Ignoring cancellation can leave unnecessary work running.
Go supports generic programming.
Type parameters allow functions and types to operate over sets of types.
A generic declaration defines one or more type parameters.
Type constraints define the permitted type arguments.
The any constraint permits any type.
Generic programming should be used when it makes an abstraction clearer or removes meaningful duplication.
The == operator compares values when their types are comparable.
The != operator tests inequality for comparable values.
Maps are not generally comparable with == except against nil.
Functions are not generally comparable with == except against nil.
Arrays are comparable when their element type is comparable.
Interface values can be compared when their dynamic values are comparable.
Comparing interface values containing non-comparable dynamic values can panic.
Go does not have an === operator.
Go equality semantics are not PHP equality semantics.
Go equality semantics are not JavaScript equality semantics.
Programming operators must be interpreted according to the language being discussed.
The logical AND operator is &&.
The logical OR operator is ||.
The logical NOT operator is !.
The bitwise AND operator is &.
The bitwise XOR operator is ^.
The bit-clear operator is &^.
The left shift operator is <<.
The right shift operator is >>.
&& and || use short-circuit evaluation.
Assignment and equality are different operations.
= performs assignment.
== performs equality comparison where permitted.
:= declares at least one new variable in an applicable local scope while assigning values.
-- decrements a variable according to Go statement rules.
Go does not provide prefix increment or decrement expressions.
x++ is a statement in Go, not a general expression.
Go does not provide a ternary ?: operator.
The if statement conditionally executes code.
An if statement can include a short initialization statement.
The switch statement selects among cases.
Go switch cases do not fall through by default.
A switch can omit its expression and operate as a sequence of Boolean cases.
A type switch selects behavior according to dynamic types.
The for statement is Go's looping construct.
Go does not have a separate while keyword.
A for loop with only a condition serves the role commonly called a while loop in other languages.
The range form iterates over supported values.
Range behavior depends on the type being ranged over.
Ranging over a map does not provide a defined deterministic iteration order.
Ranging over a string decodes UTF-8 sequences into rune values and byte indexes.
Ranging over a slice provides indexes and element values.
Loop variables have language-version-specific semantics that should be understood when captured by closures.
The break statement exits an applicable loop, switch, or select.
The continue statement advances an applicable loop.
Labels can identify statements for break, continue, and goto according to Go rules.
goto exists in Go but is constrained by language rules.
Clear structured control flow is generally preferable when goto is unnecessary.
Go supports composition through struct fields and interfaces.
Go does not provide class-based inheritance.
Go does not have classes.
Go does not require classes for object-oriented design techniques.
Methods can be associated with named types.
Interfaces provide behavioral abstraction.
Embedding is not identical to class inheritance.
An embedded field is still part of a containing struct's composition.
Interface embedding combines interface method sets and constraints according to interface rules.
A type can satisfy multiple interfaces without declaring them explicitly.
This allows interfaces to be defined by consumers where appropriate.
Package APIs should expose required behavior without exposing unnecessary implementation details.
Small interfaces can reduce coupling.
Large interfaces require implementations to provide more behavior.
The io.Writer interface represents values that can write bytes.
io.Reader defines a Read method.
io.Writer defines a Write method.
Many Go libraries compose behavior using io.Reader and io.Writer.
An interface should normally represent required behavior rather than being created automatically for every concrete type.
Go uses explicit error values instead of exception-based ordinary error handling.
Errors should be handled near the level where meaningful action can be taken.
Wrapping an error can preserve its underlying identity.
A generic message that discards the underlying error can make diagnosis harder.
Error paths should be tested.
Negative tests verify behavior under invalid or failure conditions.
The testing package provides Go's standard testing support.
Test source files conventionally end with _test.go.
Test functions conventionally begin with Test.
Example functions can provide executable documentation tests.
go test runs package tests.
Table-driven tests represent multiple test cases as data consumed by one test function.
Subtests can be created using t.Run.
Tests should be deterministic when testing deterministic behavior.
Tests should not depend unnecessarily on execution order.
Tests using external networks can fail for reasons unrelated to local program logic.
Unit tests should isolate external dependencies when isolation is part of the test's purpose.
Integration tests intentionally test component interactions.
A regression test protects behavior that previously failed.
Coverage measures which code was exercised according to the selected coverage mechanism.
High coverage does not prove correctness.
Go's standard library provides extensive functionality.
The fmt package provides formatted input and output.
The bytes package provides operations on byte slices.
The strconv package converts strings and basic values.
The unicode package provides Unicode character properties.
The unicode/utf8 package provides UTF-8 operations.
The os package provides operating-system functionality.
The io package provides basic I/O interfaces and utilities.
The bufio package provides buffered I/O.
The path package manipulates slash-separated paths.
The net package provides network I/O.
The net/http package provides HTTP client and server functionality.
The encoding/json package provides JSON encoding and decoding.
The encoding/binary package provides translation between byte sequences and fixed-size values.
The crypto packages provide cryptographic primitives and protocols.
The database/sql package provides a generic interface around SQL databases and drivers.
The log package provides basic logging.
The time package provides time and duration functionality.
The context package provides cancellation and request-scoped context propagation.
The errors package provides error creation and inspection.
The flag package provides command-line flag parsing.
The regexp package provides regular-expression functionality.
The sort package provides sorting functionality.
The slices package provides generic slice operations in applicable Go versions.
The maps package provides generic map operations in applicable Go versions.
The standard library does not provide a driver for every external database.
database/sql requires an appropriate database driver.
Opening a database handle does not necessarily establish a physical database connection immediately.
sql.DB represents a database handle and connection pool abstraction.
A sql.DB is designed to be long-lived and shared according to its contract.
DB.Close releases resources associated with a database handle.
Query parameters should be passed using the database driver's parameter mechanism.
SQL identifiers cannot always be supplied as ordinary query parameters.
Dynamic identifiers require explicit validation or safe construction according to the database and driver.
Rows returned by a database query should be closed when required.
rows.Err should be checked after row iteration.
Transactions group database operations according to database transaction semantics.
A failed transaction operation should be handled explicitly.
Database errors should not automatically be translated into one generic failure if callers require more precise behavior.
Go can interact with MySQL using an appropriate Go database driver.
Go can interact with PostgreSQL using an appropriate driver.
Go can interact with SQLite using an appropriate driver.
Database support comes from packages and drivers rather than the Go language itself.
Go is widely used for network services.
TCP does not preserve application message boundaries.
An application protocol using TCP must define message framing.
A single Write call does not guarantee that the peer receives one corresponding Read.
A Read can return fewer bytes than the caller ultimately needs.
Network code must handle partial reads and writes according to the interfaces it uses.
io.ReadFull can read exactly a requested number of bytes or report an error.
Connections can fail or close at any time.
Network operations can block.
Deadlines can bound network waiting.
net.Conn provides methods for setting deadlines.
A server should handle malformed network input without trusting it.
Network input is untrusted unless an established protocol and validation prove otherwise.
A listener accepts incoming network connections.
net.Listen can create network listeners.
A TCP listener bound to 127.0.0.1 is ordinarily reachable only through the local IPv4 loopback interface.
A TCP listener bound to 0.0.0.0 can accept connections on applicable local IPv4 interfaces when allowed by the operating system and network configuration.
Ports identify transport-layer endpoints.
Binding to a port can fail.
Accepting a connection can fail.
Reading from a connection can fail.
Writing to a connection can fail.
Closing a connection can fail.
Network failure is a normal condition that software must handle.
The net/http package implements HTTP client and server facilities.
http.Handler defines behavior that serves HTTP requests.
The ServeHTTP method handles an HTTP request.
http.HandlerFunc adapts a function to the Handler interface.
http.Server represents an HTTP server.
Unbounded HTTP timeouts can permit connections to consume resources indefinitely.
http.Client sends HTTP requests.
HTTP status codes should describe the actual HTTP result.
HTTP status 200 indicates successful handling according to HTTP semantics.
HTTP status 401 indicates that authentication is required or has failed according to HTTP semantics.
HTTP status 403 indicates that the server understood the request but refuses to authorize it.
HTTP status 500 indicates an internal server error.
HTTP status 503 indicates service unavailable.
A 503 response should represent actual service unavailability.
Lacking information to answer a question is not by itself service unavailability.
An internal dependency failure can make a service unavailable when that dependency is required to perform the requested operation.
Error reporting should distinguish unavailable service from unavailable knowledge.
HTTP handlers should not expose internal secrets through error responses.
Request bodies have finite resource costs.
Servers processing untrusted request bodies should apply appropriate size limits.
Request data should be validated before trusted use.
HTTP headers are untrusted input.
Form values are untrusted input.
Authentication and authorization must be enforced by trusted server-side behavior.
Hiding a client-side control does not enforce authorization.
json.Marshal encodes supported Go values as JSON.
json.Unmarshal decodes JSON into a supplied Go value.
JSON field mapping can be controlled with struct tags.
A struct tag attaches string metadata to a struct field.
A json tag can define a field name.
A json tag can request omission of certain empty values using applicable options.
Decoded input must still be validated.
Unknown JSON fields can be rejected when a decoder is configured accordingly.
JSON numbers require deliberate handling when exact numeric representation matters.
Protocol definitions should specify numeric ranges and representation.
Writing raw struct memory to a network does not define a portable protocol.
Pointers are not meaningful portable serialized values.
binary.BigEndian represents big-endian byte order.
binary.LittleEndian represents little-endian byte order.
Network protocols should define byte order explicitly.
Consensus-sensitive systems should not serialize maps by unspecified iteration order.
Consensus-sensitive systems should not rely on platform-dependent integer sizes.
Consensus-sensitive systems should define overflow behavior.
Consensus-sensitive systems should define ordering.
Consensus-sensitive systems should define string encoding.
Consensus-sensitive systems should define timestamp representation.
Consensus-sensitive systems should avoid dependence on unspecified behavior.
Go's defined semantics help portability, but application-level determinism still requires deliberate protocol design.
Floating-point values can be inappropriate for some deterministic financial or consensus calculations.
Integer or fixed-point representations can provide explicitly defined behavior when their ranges and scaling are specified.
time.Duration represents an elapsed duration in nanoseconds.
time.Time represents an instant with associated time information.
Time zones should be handled explicitly when system behavior depends on them.
UTC is commonly used for interoperable timestamps.
A protocol should define its timestamp format.
Wall-clock time can change.
Monotonic clock readings embedded in applicable time.Time values can support elapsed-time measurement within a process.
Serialized time values do not preserve Go's internal monotonic clock reading.
Timeout logic and calendar-time logic solve different problems.
Go can access files through os and related packages.
os.Open opens a file for reading.
os.WriteFile writes data to a file.
Reading an entire untrusted file can consume substantial memory.
Large inputs should be bounded or streamed where appropriate.
File operations can fail.
A path valid on one operating system might not have identical meaning on another.
path/filepath provides operating-system-aware filesystem path operations.
filepath.Join combines filesystem path elements.
Cleaning a path does not by itself authorize access to the resulting target.
Untrusted paths require boundary and authorization checks.
Directory traversal can occur when untrusted path input escapes an intended root.
Symbolic links can affect filesystem boundary assumptions.
Filesystem security requires more than checking whether a string begins with an expected prefix.
Temporary files can be created with os.CreateTemp.
Temporary directories can be created with os.MkdirTemp.
Temporary resources should be removed when no longer required according to application policy.
Go supports cross-compilation for many supported target combinations.
GOOS identifies a target operating system to Go tooling.
GOARCH identifies a target architecture to Go tooling.
A program compiling for multiple targets does not prove that its runtime behavior is platform independent.
Platform-specific files can use filename suffixes recognized by Go tooling.
Build constraints can select files for particular build conditions.
Platform-specific behavior should not alter protocol-defined results when platform-independent results are required.
The runtime.GOOS value identifies the target operating system at runtime.
The runtime.GOARCH value identifies the target architecture.
Cross-platform code should avoid unnecessary assumptions about path syntax.
Cross-platform code should avoid assumptions about integer size when exact widths matter.
Cross-platform code should avoid assumptions about byte order when protocols define an order.
Go's standard library provides many cross-platform abstractions but cannot erase every operating-system difference.
cgo is not required for ordinary pure-Go programs.
Using cgo introduces native compiler and platform dependencies.
cgo can complicate cross-compilation.
Calls across the Go and C boundary have additional rules and costs.
Go pointers passed to C must obey cgo pointer rules.
C resources must be released according to the C interface contract.
A stable C ABI can provide a language-neutral native boundary.
Go itself does not define one universal dynamic-library plugin ABI across all platforms.
The plugin standard-library package provides dynamic loading on supported systems with significant compatibility constraints.
Go plugins require closely compatible toolchains and dependencies.
A general cross-platform module system should not assume Go's plugin package is universally available.
A process boundary can provide stronger language and ABI separation than in-process dynamic loading.
Protocols can connect components written in different programming languages.
An ABI and a network protocol are different contracts.
An ABI defines binary interaction inside compatible process environments.
A protocol defines communication representation and behavior between components.
A stable protocol can allow a Go component to communicate with components written in C, C++, C#, PHP, or other languages.
Protocol compatibility requires defined serialization and behavior rather than shared source language.
Validation and sanitization are different operations.
Output encoding depends on the destination context.
HTML output requires HTML-appropriate encoding.
SQL data should use parameterized query facilities.
File paths derived from users require boundary validation.
Authentication determines identity.
Authorization determines permitted actions.
Authentication and authorization are different responsibilities.
Authorization must be enforced by trusted service behavior.
Passwords should not be stored as plaintext.
Passwords should use an appropriate password-hashing algorithm.
The crypto/rand package provides cryptographically secure random bytes according to its contract.
The math/rand package is not a substitute for cryptographic randomness.
TLS protects network traffic between endpoints when correctly configured.
The crypto/tls package provides TLS functionality.
TLS does not make unsafe application logic automatically secure.
HTTPS is HTTP transported over TLS.
A server should validate TLS configuration according to its security requirements.
Certificates establish properties according to the trust model and validation performed.
Go does not eliminate all security vulnerabilities.
Logic errors remain possible.
Authorization failures remain possible.
Resource exhaustion remains possible.
Unbounded allocations remain possible.
Unbounded goroutines remain possible.
Denial-of-service vulnerabilities remain possible.
Security requires deliberate system design.
A context timeout can help bound external operations.
Input-size limits can help bound resource consumption.
Worker limits can help bound concurrency.
Connection limits can help bound service resource use.
Backpressure prevents producers from indefinitely overwhelming consumers.
Buffered channels provide finite queues, not unlimited capacity.
A bounded system should define what happens when capacity is reached.
Dropping work, blocking producers, rejecting requests, and applying backpressure are distinct policies.
The correct capacity policy depends on the system's contract.
Go encourages explicit dependencies through ordinary values and interfaces.
Package-level global mutable state can make testing and reasoning harder.
Initialization behavior can be provided by init functions.
An init function runs automatically during package initialization.
A package can contain multiple init functions according to Go rules.
init functions cannot be called directly.
Excessive hidden initialization can make startup behavior difficult to reason about.
Explicit initialization is easier to control when failure must be reported.
An init function cannot return an error.
Operations that can fail and require operator handling are often better represented by explicit initialization functions.
Package initialization order follows Go's defined dependency rules.
Import cycles are not permitted.
A package cannot directly or indirectly import itself.
Avoiding import cycles encourages directional package dependencies.
Packages should have bounded responsibilities.
A package's exported API should be smaller than its internal implementation when practical.
The cmd directory is a common project convention for executable commands but is not a language requirement.
The internal directory behavior is enforced by Go tooling.
Project layout conventions are not all language rules.
Go does not require a src directory for modern module-based projects.
Go does not require object-oriented directory structures.
Simple project layouts are preferable when additional hierarchy provides no useful boundary.
Build automation should remain repeatable.
go build resolves and compiles package dependencies according to module configuration.
go test can build test binaries automatically.
The -count option can control test caching behavior in applicable go test usage.
The -run option selects tests by name pattern.
Benchmarks can measure performance under controlled test conditions.
Benchmark results depend on hardware, environment, workload, and compiler/runtime versions.
Optimization should be based on measured requirements rather than assumption.
pprof provides profiling support for Go programs.
Profiles can identify CPU and memory behavior.
A profile describes observed execution and does not prove all possible performance behavior.
Allocations can create garbage-collection work.
Reducing unnecessary allocations can improve some workloads.
Avoiding every allocation is not automatically a useful optimization.
Clear code should not be sacrificed without evidence that complexity is justified.
Compiler optimizations can change across Go versions.
Go binaries commonly contain substantial runtime support.
A Go executable can often be deployed as a single native binary when its dependencies and build mode permit.
DNS resolution behavior can vary according to build configuration and platform.
Deployment requirements should be verified from the actual build.
Environment variables can configure Go applications.
os.Getenv reads an environment variable.
os.LookupEnv distinguishes an unset variable from a variable set to an empty string.
Environment variables are not automatically secret.
Secrets require appropriate access control regardless of configuration mechanism.
Command-line arguments are available through os.Args.
Sensitive secrets should not automatically be placed on command lines.
The flag package provides basic command-line option parsing.
Configuration should have defined precedence when multiple sources can provide values.
Configuration parsing should reject invalid critical values rather than silently inventing unsafe defaults.
Defaults should be deliberate.
Operational errors should be observable.
Logs provide evidence about application events.
Logs should not contain unnecessary secrets.
Structured logs can make machine processing easier.
A log entry should distinguish informational operation from warning and failure conditions.
Logging an error does not handle the error by itself.
Returning an error and logging an error are separate actions.
Logging the same error repeatedly at multiple layers can create noisy duplicate records.
The layer responsible for operational reporting should be defined.
Graceful shutdown allows a service to stop accepting new work and finish or cancel active work according to policy.
Operating-system signals can request process termination.
os/signal provides signal handling facilities.
Shutdown should have a bounded deadline when indefinite waiting is unacceptable.
A service should report degraded operation according to actual capability rather than generic failure wording.
An unavailable optional dependency does not necessarily make every service capability unavailable.
A required dependency failure can prevent the dependent capability from operating.
System status should reflect actual system state.
A dispatcher coordinates work among components according to defined responsibilities.
A dispatcher should distinguish routing failure from execution failure.
A dispatcher should preserve useful error information returned by downstream components.
A dispatcher should not transform every downstream failure into one generic error.
A module should have a bounded responsibility.
A module should not depend on undocumented internals of another module.
A module's successful qualification establishes only the conditions tested by qualification.
Hot loading a module does not prove every runtime operation will succeed.
Module discovery, qualification, activation, dispatch, and execution are distinct lifecycle stages.
Failures should identify the lifecycle stage in which they occurred.
A system that continues after a nonfatal module failure should report degraded capability accurately.
A system should not claim total service unavailability when only one bounded capability failed.
A response should be grounded in available information.
A system should distinguish not knowing an answer from being technically unavailable.
A knowledge retrieval miss is not inherently an HTTP service failure.
A reasoning failure is not inherently a network failure.
A persistence failure is not inherently a knowledge failure.
Retrieved information should be ranked according to the actual question rather than merely broad topic overlap.
Programming-language operators are meaningful retrieval terms.
Go uses == for equality on comparable values.
Go does not use ===.
PHP defines == and === with different comparison semantics.
C# uses == but does not use === as an equality operator.
A question about an operator should retain enough language context to avoid mixing semantics from different languages.
Knowledge about multiple languages should remain distinguishable by language.
Go favors explicit control flow and explicit error handling.
Go favors composition through packages, structs, and interfaces rather than class inheritance.
Go provides garbage collection but still requires explicit management of external resources.
Go provides goroutines and channels but concurrency still requires deliberate lifecycle and synchronization design.
Go supports cross-platform compilation but cross-platform behavior still requires deliberate engineering.
Go can interoperate with C, but native interoperability introduces additional ownership and portability constraints.
Go can build small command-line programs as well as long-running network services.
Simple Go code is preferable to unnecessary abstraction when both satisfy the same requirements.
Deterministic behavior requires explicit control of inputs, state, ordering, serialization, and external dependencies.
Correctness is more important than merely producing output.
JavaScript is standardized primarily through the ECMAScript specification.
ECMAScript defines the standardized core language used by JavaScript implementations.
JavaScript is case-sensitive.
JavaScript source files commonly use the .js filename extension.
JavaScript modules commonly use the .js or .mjs filename extension depending on the environment and configuration.
JavaScript can execute in web browsers.
JavaScript can execute outside web browsers using server-side and standalone runtimes.
A JavaScript runtime provides facilities beyond the ECMAScript language itself.
Browser APIs are not the JavaScript language itself.
The DOM is a browser-provided API rather than part of the ECMAScript core language.
HTTP networking facilities such as fetch are provided by host environments rather than being fundamental language syntax.
JavaScript is dynamically typed.
Variables do not have permanently fixed value types.
JavaScript primitive types include undefined.
JavaScript primitive types include boolean.
JavaScript primitive types include number.
JavaScript primitive types include bigint.
JavaScript primitive types include string.
JavaScript primitive types include symbol.
Objects are non-primitive JavaScript values.
Functions are callable objects.
Arrays are objects.
JavaScript numbers use IEEE-754 double-precision floating-point representation for the number type.
The bigint type represents integers outside the ordinary safe integer limitations of number.
BigInt and Number are different numeric types.
Arithmetic cannot freely mix BigInt and Number values without explicit conversion where required.
NaN means Not-a-Number.
NaN is a value of the number type.
NaN is not equal to itself using ==.
Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER identifies the largest integer that can be represented exactly and safely for ordinary integer comparisons using the number type.
Number.MIN_SAFE_INTEGER identifies the corresponding smallest safe integer.
Floating-point arithmetic can produce rounding results that differ from exact decimal arithmetic.
The expression 0.1 + 0.2 does not produce an exactly represented mathematical decimal 0.3 in ordinary JavaScript Number arithmetic.
Floating-point values should not be used casually for exact financial or consensus calculations.
A string is an immutable sequence of UTF-16 code units.
JavaScript strings are immutable.
Changing a string operation produces another string value rather than modifying the original string's contents.
String length counts UTF-16 code units rather than necessarily counting user-perceived characters.
let declares a block-scoped binding.
var declares a variable using older function-scoped or global-scoped semantics.
const prevents reassignment of the binding.
const does not make an object stored through that binding immutable.
Properties of an object referenced by const can still be modified unless other mechanisms prevent modification.
let permits reassignment.
Modern JavaScript generally favors const when a binding does not need reassignment.
Modern JavaScript generally favors let when reassignment is required.
var has different scoping and hoisting behavior from let and const.
Block scope is associated with constructs enclosed by braces where lexical declarations apply.
Function scope applies to var declarations inside functions.
Lexical declarations include let and const.
The period before lexical initialization is commonly called the temporal dead zone.
Declarations are processed according to JavaScript execution rules before ordinary statement execution.
The typeof operator returns a string describing certain type categories.
typeof true returns "boolean".
typeof 1 returns "number".
typeof 1n returns "bigint".
typeof Symbol() returns "symbol".
typeof null returning "object" does not mean null is an ordinary object.
null represents an intentional absence of an object value according to common usage.
undefined commonly represents absence of an assigned value or missing result according to language and API context.
null and undefined are distinct values.
The == operator performs abstract equality comparison.
The === operator performs strict equality comparison.
Strict equality does not perform the same cross-type coercions as abstract equality.
The expression 5 == "5" evaluates to true.
The expression 5 === "5" evaluates to false.
The expression null == undefined evaluates to true.
The != operator performs abstract inequality comparison.
JavaScript equality semantics are different from PHP equality semantics.
Operator semantics must be interpreted according to the language being discussed.
Object equality using === compares object identity.
Two separately created objects with identical properties are not strictly equal merely because their contents appear identical.
Two references to the same object compare strictly equal.
Object.is(NaN, NaN) evaluates to true.
Object.is(0, -0) evaluates to false.
The = operator performs assignment.
Assignment and comparison are different operations.
The logical AND operator is &&.
The logical OR operator is ||.
The logical NOT operator is !.
&& and || use short-circuit evaluation.
&& and || return operand values rather than necessarily returning Boolean values.
The nullish coalescing operator is ??.
The ?? operator uses its right operand when its left operand is null or undefined.
Optional chaining uses the ?. syntax.
JavaScript supports arithmetic operators.
JavaScript supports bitwise operators.
Most ordinary JavaScript bitwise operators convert Number operands to 32-bit integer representations according to language rules.
Prefix and postfix increment have different result-value behavior.
Operator precedence affects expression grouping.
Parentheses can make intended grouping explicit.
Implicit type coercion converts values according to language rules.
String concatenation can trigger coercion.
The expression 1 + 2 evaluates numerically.
The expression "1" + 2 produces a string.
The expression 1 + "2" produces a string.
Explicit conversion can make intent clearer.
Number(value) converts a value according to Number conversion rules.
String(value) converts a value to a string according to String conversion rules.
Boolean(value) converts a value according to Boolean conversion rules.
Falsy values convert to false in Boolean contexts.
false is falsy.
0 is falsy.
-0 is falsy.
The empty string is falsy.
null is falsy.
undefined is falsy.
NaN is falsy.
Most other values are truthy.
An if statement executes conditionally according to the truthiness of its condition.
The else clause provides an alternative branch.
The switch statement selects among cases.
JavaScript switch case matching uses strict equality comparison semantics.
A for statement provides looping.
A while statement loops while a condition remains truthy.
A do-while statement executes its body before checking its continuation condition.
A for...of loop iterates values from an iterable.
A for...in loop iterates enumerable property keys.
for...in should not be treated as the ordinary value iterator for arrays.
The continue statement advances an applicable loop.
Functions define reusable behavior.
A function declaration declares a named function.
A function expression creates a function value.
An arrow function uses => syntax.
Arrow functions and ordinary functions have important semantic differences.
Arrow functions do not create their own this binding.
Arrow functions do not create their own arguments object.
Arrow functions cannot be used as constructors with new.
An ordinary function's this value depends on how the function is invoked according to JavaScript rules.
this does not simply mean the function in which the word appears.
Method invocation can establish this from the receiver object.
bind creates a function with a specified this value and optionally pre-supplied arguments.
call invokes a function with an explicitly supplied this value.
JavaScript functions can return values.
A function without an explicit return value returns undefined.
Functions are first-class values.
Functions can be returned from other functions.
A callback is a function supplied for later invocation.
Lexical scope determines which surrounding declarations code can access.
JavaScript uses lexical scoping for ordinary variable resolution.
An inner declaration can shadow an outer declaration with the same name.
Objects store properties.
Numeric-looking property names are represented according to property-key conversion rules.
Object literals commonly use braces.
Property access can use dot notation.
obj.name accesses a property named name.
obj["name"] accesses a property named name.
Objects can contain methods.
A method is a function associated with an object through a property or method definition.
A prototype can provide inherited properties and methods.
Prototype-based inheritance differs from traditional class-based inheritance models.
The class syntax provides a higher-level syntax over JavaScript's object and prototype mechanisms.
A class declaration defines a class.
A constructor method initializes class instances.
Class methods are defined on the class prototype unless declared static.
Static methods belong to the class constructor rather than individual instances.
The extends keyword creates a derived class relationship.
The super keyword accesses base-class construction or methods according to context.
Private class elements can use names beginning with #.
A private class field is enforced by JavaScript private-element semantics.
A property whose ordinary name merely begins with an underscore is not language-enforced private state.
JavaScript arrays are ordered list-like objects.
Array indexes normally begin at zero.
Arrays can grow dynamically.
The length property reports an array's length according to array semantics.
An array can contain values of different types.
typeof [] returns "object".
push adds values to the end of an array.
pop removes and returns the last array element.
unshift adds values to the beginning of an array.
slice returns a selected portion without modifying the original array.
map creates an array containing transformed results.
filter creates an array containing elements satisfying a predicate.
reduce combines elements according to a reducer function.
find returns the first element satisfying a predicate.
findIndex returns the index of the first element satisfying a predicate.
some tests whether at least one element satisfies a predicate.
every tests whether all elements satisfy a predicate.
includes tests whether an array contains a value according to its comparison semantics.
sort sorts an array in place.
The default Array sort compares elements as strings according to its default rules.
Array iteration methods can invoke callback functions.
Mutating an array while iterating it can affect iteration behavior.
An iterable provides values through the iteration protocol.
Strings are iterable.
Maps are iterable.
Sets are iterable.
Map stores key-value pairs.
Map keys can be values of any JavaScript type.
Map preserves insertion order for iteration according to its defined semantics.
Set stores unique values.
Set membership uses SameValueZero comparison semantics.
WeakSet stores weakly held eligible values.
Weak collections are not enumerable.
Weak collections allow entries to disappear when keys are no longer otherwise reachable.
JavaScript uses automatic garbage collection in ordinary implementations.
The language does not provide ordinary deterministic manual memory deallocation.
An object can be reclaimed after it becomes unreachable according to implementation behavior.
Garbage collection timing is not deterministic.
A program should not depend on garbage collection happening at a particular moment.
References retained by event listeners, closures, caches, or global structures can keep objects reachable.
Logical memory leaks can occur when unnecessary references are retained.
JavaScript has exceptions.
The throw statement throws a value.
The Error class represents a generic error.
SyntaxError represents invalid syntax.
ReferenceError represents certain invalid identifier references.
A try block contains code whose exceptions can be caught.
A catch block handles an exception.
A finally block executes cleanup behavior when control leaves the associated try construct according to language rules.
An exception can propagate through multiple function calls.
An empty catch block can hide defects.
Error messages should not expose unnecessary secrets to untrusted clients.
Host environments provide mechanisms for asynchronous operations.
Promises represent eventual completion or failure of asynchronous operations.
A Promise can be pending.
A Promise can become fulfilled.
A Promise can become rejected.
A settled Promise is either fulfilled or rejected.
A Promise settles only once.
then registers behavior for Promise fulfillment and can also register rejection handling.
catch registers Promise rejection handling.
finally registers behavior after settlement without replacing the result unless its own behavior changes the chain.
Promise chaining creates new Promises.
Returning a Promise from a then callback causes the chain to adopt its eventual state according to Promise resolution rules.
Throwing from a Promise callback produces rejection of the resulting Promise.
Unhandled Promise rejections can represent programming or operational failures.
async functions return Promises.
An async function always returns a Promise.
Returning an ordinary value from an async function fulfills its returned Promise with that value.
Throwing from an async function rejects its returned Promise.
The await operator waits for an awaited value according to Promise resolution semantics within an async context.
await does not block the entire JavaScript runtime in the same manner as synchronous busy waiting.
Asynchronous programming and parallel execution are different concepts.
The JavaScript event loop coordinates asynchronous callbacks and jobs in common host environments.
The event loop is substantially defined by the host environment rather than solely by ECMAScript.
Promise reactions use the language's job mechanism and are commonly processed as microtasks by host integrations.
Timers such as setTimeout are host APIs.
setTimeout does not guarantee execution at the exact requested time.
A zero-millisecond timeout does not mean immediate synchronous execution.
Long-running synchronous JavaScript can delay other work on the same execution thread.
CPU-intensive operations can make a browser interface unresponsive when executed on its main thread.
Web Workers can provide additional browser execution contexts.
Worker threads or processes can provide additional concurrency in server-side runtimes depending on the runtime.
JavaScript modules support explicit imports and exports.
The export keyword exposes module bindings.
The import keyword imports module bindings.
ECMAScript modules are commonly abbreviated ESM.
Module imports are statically analyzable in ordinary import declarations.
Dynamic import uses import() and returns a Promise.
Modules are evaluated according to defined dependency relationships.
Circular module dependencies can exist but require understanding initialization behavior.
CommonJS is a module system historically associated with Node.js.
require is associated with CommonJS environments rather than ECMAScript language module syntax.
module.exports is associated with CommonJS.
A runtime can support both module systems according to its rules and configuration.
JSON stands for JavaScript Object Notation.
JSON was inspired by JavaScript object literal syntax.
JSON and JavaScript object literals are not identical syntaxes.
JSON is a data-interchange format.
JSON supports strings.
JSON supports numbers.
JSON supports true.
JSON supports false.
JSON supports null.
JSON does not support undefined.
JSON does not support functions.
JSON does not support comments in standard JSON syntax.
JSON object property names must be strings enclosed according to JSON syntax.
JSON.parse converts JSON text into JavaScript values.
JSON.stringify converts supported JavaScript values into JSON text.
JSON.stringify does not preserve every possible JavaScript value or object relationship.
Functions and undefined values have special omission or conversion behavior during JSON serialization depending on their location.
BigInt cannot be serialized directly by ordinary JSON.stringify without custom handling.
Circular object references cause ordinary JSON.stringify to fail unless custom handling removes or transforms the cycle.
Successful JSON parsing does not establish that input is trusted.
Parsed JSON should be validated against application requirements.
JavaScript is central to browser-side web development.
HTML defines document structure and content semantics.
JavaScript defines programmable behavior and interaction.
JavaScript can read and modify a browser document through the DOM.
DOM stands for Document Object Model.
The DOM represents a document as objects and nodes.
document.querySelector selects the first matching element according to a CSS selector.
document.querySelectorAll selects matching elements according to a CSS selector.
getElementById retrieves an element by its id.
textContent reads or writes textual content of a node.
innerHTML parses or serializes HTML content.
Assigning untrusted data to innerHTML can create cross-site scripting vulnerabilities.
textContent should be preferred when inserting untrusted plain text that should not be interpreted as markup.
Creating DOM nodes explicitly can avoid interpreting arbitrary strings as HTML.
Event listeners register behavior for events.
removeEventListener removes a matching registered event listener according to its contract.
Events can propagate through DOM trees.
Event propagation includes capture and bubble phases.
event.target identifies the originating event target.
event.currentTarget identifies the target whose listener is currently executing.
preventDefault requests prevention of an event's default action where cancelable.
stopPropagation stops further propagation according to event rules.
Event delegation can reduce the number of listeners required for dynamic collections.
HTML forms collect user input.
JavaScript can respond to form submission events.
A submit event can be intercepted with an event listener.
preventDefault can prevent the browser's normal form submission behavior.
JavaScript can send form data using network APIs.
JavaScript is not required for basic HTML form submission.
A server receives submitted form data according to the form's method and encoding.
PHP can process form data sent by a browser.
Client-side JavaScript and server-side PHP execute in different environments.
Browser JavaScript normally executes on the user's device.
PHP normally executes on the web server.
Browser JavaScript cannot directly read arbitrary server-side PHP variables.
JavaScript can send HTTP requests to server endpoints.
The server can return JSON consumed by JavaScript.
A common web flow is browser JavaScript to HTTP to server application to database and back through HTTP to JavaScript.
Client-side validation is not a trusted security boundary.
Server-side applications must independently validate untrusted input.
JavaScript validation does not replace PHP validation on a PHP server.
HTTP is an application protocol.
JavaScript can interact with HTTP through host-provided APIs.
fetch is a commonly available web API for HTTP requests.
fetch returns a Promise.
A fetch Promise can fulfill even when the HTTP response status represents an HTTP error such as 404 or 500.
Code using fetch should inspect the response status when application behavior depends on HTTP success.
response.ok indicates whether the HTTP status is within the successful range defined by the Fetch API.
response.status provides the HTTP status code.
response.json parses a response body as JSON.
response.text reads a response body as text.
A network request can fail before an HTTP response is received.
A network failure and an HTTP error response are different conditions.
An HTTP 404 response is still an HTTP response.
An HTTP 500 response is still an HTTP response.
An HTTP 503 response is still an HTTP response.
A rejected fetch operation can indicate a network-level or Fetch-level failure rather than an HTTP status failure.
HTTP status 200 represents successful handling according to HTTP semantics.
HTTP status 204 represents successful handling with no response content.
HTTP status 401 concerns authentication requirements or failure according to HTTP semantics.
HTTP status 403 indicates that the server refuses authorization.
HTTP status 409 represents a conflict with current resource state.
HTTP status 429 represents too many requests.
HTTP status 500 represents an internal server error.
HTTP status 503 represents service unavailable.
A 503 status should represent actual service unavailability.
HTTP methods include GET.
HTTP methods include POST.
HTTP methods include PUT.
HTTP methods include PATCH.
HTTP methods include DELETE.
HTTP methods include HEAD.
GET is intended for retrieving a representation without requesting unsafe state-changing semantics.
POST submits data for processing according to resource semantics.
PUT requests creation or replacement of a resource representation according to target semantics.
PATCH requests partial modification according to the patch representation and server contract.
DELETE requests removal of a resource according to server semantics.
HTTP method semantics are defined by HTTP standards rather than JavaScript.
Content-Type identifies the media type of a message representation.
Authorization can carry authentication credentials according to the selected scheme.
Credentials should not be exposed unnecessarily to JavaScript.
Browser developer tools can inspect network requests and loaded scripts.
Client-side code is not an appropriate location for secrets that must remain hidden from the client.
Cookies are managed by browsers according to cookie rules.
JavaScript can access some cookies through document.cookie.
HttpOnly cookies are not exposed through document.cookie.
HttpOnly can reduce exposure of cookie values to injected JavaScript.
Secure cookies are restricted to secure transport according to cookie semantics.
SameSite controls aspects of cross-site cookie sending.
Cookie security attributes solve different problems.
Sessions commonly store server-side state associated with a client identifier.
A session identifier can be stored in a cookie.
JavaScript does not need direct access to an HttpOnly session cookie for the browser to send it with eligible requests.
CORS is a browser security mechanism controlling whether scripts can access responses from different origins.
CORS is enforced primarily by browsers.
CORS does not function as server-side authentication.
Allowing a CORS origin does not authorize a user to perform every operation.
The same-origin policy restricts interactions between origins.
An origin consists of scheme, host, and port.
Two URLs can have the same host but different origins because their scheme or port differs.
XSS occurs when attacker-controlled content is executed as script in a trusted web context.
Injecting untrusted strings into HTML can create XSS.
Output encoding should match the output context.
HTML encoding and JavaScript string encoding solve different contexts.
A Content Security Policy can restrict sources and execution of web content.
Content Security Policy is commonly abbreviated CSP.
CSP can reduce the impact of some injection vulnerabilities.
CSP does not replace correct output encoding and input handling.
Cross-Site Request Forgery is commonly abbreviated CSRF.
CSRF defenses can include tokens and appropriate cookie policies.
CORS and CSRF are different security concerns.
XSS and CSRF are different security concerns.
Authentication determines identity.
Authorization determines permitted actions.
Client-side JavaScript must not be the sole enforcement point for authorization.
Disabling a button does not prevent an attacker from manually sending the corresponding HTTP request.
Server-side authorization must protect privileged operations.
The browser environment provides localStorage.
localStorage stores string data associated with an origin.
JavaScript accessible to an origin can access that origin's localStorage according to browser security rules.
Sensitive authentication credentials stored in localStorage can be exposed by successful XSS.
sessionStorage provides origin-associated storage scoped according to browser session rules.
IndexedDB provides a browser database for structured client-side data.
Client-side state must be validated before trusted server operations rely on it.
JavaScript can manipulate URLs.
URL represents and parses URLs in supported host environments.
URLSearchParams provides query parameter operations.
User-provided URLs should be validated according to the application's intended schemes and destinations.
Open redirect vulnerabilities can occur when untrusted input controls redirect destinations without proper restrictions.
JavaScript regular expressions provide pattern matching.
A regular expression is created with literal syntax or RegExp.
Regular expressions are useful for text processing.
Regular expressions are not appropriate parsers for every structured language.
Poorly designed regular expressions can cause excessive computation.
Date represents date and time values using milliseconds relative to the ECMAScript epoch internally.
Date has historically complicated local-time and timezone behavior.
Date.now returns the current timestamp in milliseconds according to the system clock.
System wall-clock time can change.
Performance timing APIs can provide monotonic-style elapsed-time measurements in browser environments.
A protocol should explicitly define timestamp representation.
ISO 8601 formats are commonly used for interoperable timestamps.
A timestamp lacking timezone information can be ambiguous.
UTC is commonly used for machine-to-machine timestamps.
Intl provides internationalization functionality.
Intl formatting can depend on locale and runtime data.
Deterministic protocols should use explicitly defined culture-independent representations.
JavaScript property enumeration order has defined rules for many operations in modern ECMAScript.
A deterministic serialization format should specify canonical ordering when byte-for-byte equality matters.
Cryptographic hashing of structured data requires deterministic serialization if logically identical data must produce identical hashes.
JavaScript itself does not define cryptographic security merely because it can manipulate bytes.
Web Crypto provides cryptographic functionality in supported web environments.
crypto.getRandomValues provides cryptographically strong random values in supported web environments.
Math.random is not intended for cryptographic security.
Passwords should not be generated or protected using Math.random when cryptographic randomness is required.
Password hashing should be performed with algorithms designed for password storage.
A general cryptographic hash is not automatically an appropriate password-storage algorithm.
Private keys require controlled storage.
A private key delivered to browser JavaScript cannot remain secret from the user controlling that browser.
TLS protects transport between endpoints when correctly configured.
TLS does not enforce authorization.
TLS does not validate application data semantics.
JavaScript can use WebSocket APIs in supported browsers.
WebSocket provides bidirectional communication over a persistent connection after its opening handshake.
WebSocket and raw TCP are different abstractions.
A WebSocket connection can close unexpectedly.
WebSocket applications require reconnection policy when reconnection is desired.
Automatic reconnection should use bounded retry behavior.
Immediate infinite retry loops can overload a service.
Exponential backoff can reduce repeated retry load.
An operation should not be retried automatically when repeating it can produce unsafe duplicate effects unless the operation is idempotent or otherwise protected.
Idempotency means repeating an operation has the same intended effect as performing it once according to the operation's contract.
GET, PUT, and DELETE have idempotent semantics under HTTP definitions even though repeated requests can produce different response representations.
POST is not inherently idempotent.
Node.js is a JavaScript runtime built for server-side and general-purpose execution.
Node.js is not the JavaScript language itself.
Other JavaScript runtimes can also execute server-side JavaScript.
Runtime-specific APIs should not be mistaken for universal ECMAScript APIs.
process is a Node.js global API rather than a browser JavaScript global.
window is a browser global object in traditional browser contexts.
document is a browser API and is not normally available in a basic Node.js process.
Package ecosystems provide third-party JavaScript libraries.
npm is a package manager commonly associated with JavaScript and Node.js.
A package.json file describes package metadata and dependencies in common JavaScript tooling.
Third-party packages introduce dependencies and supply-chain considerations.
A dependency should not be added merely because a small operation can be delegated to a package.
Dependency versions affect compatibility and security.
Untrusted dependencies can execute code with developer or build-system privileges.
Dependency provenance matters.
Unused dependencies should be removed when practical.
A framework is not the JavaScript language.
Vue is a JavaScript framework or progressive framework used for user interfaces.
Angular is a web application framework.
Express is a server-side web framework commonly used with Node.js.
Using JavaScript does not require React.
Using JavaScript does not require Node.js.
Using JavaScript does not require npm.
Using JavaScript does not require a framework.
Plain JavaScript can directly use browser APIs.
A framework adds abstractions and conventions.
Framework abstractions can improve development for appropriate applications.
Frameworks also introduce dependencies and complexity.
The simplest adequate implementation is often easier to maintain.
TypeScript is a language that adds a static type system and related features to JavaScript development.
TypeScript source commonly compiles or transforms into JavaScript.
TypeScript and JavaScript are distinct languages.
JavaScript runtimes execute JavaScript rather than TypeScript source unless tooling or runtime-specific support transforms it.
TypeScript type checking does not automatically validate runtime data.
External JSON remains untrusted even when a TypeScript type says what shape the programmer expects.
JavaScript uses automatic semicolon insertion in certain grammatical situations.
Automatic semicolon insertion is commonly abbreviated ASI.
Semicolons can still be written explicitly.
ASI has defined rules and should not be treated as random behavior.
Certain line breaks can change how JavaScript is parsed.
Consistent formatting reduces ambiguity for human readers.
Formatters can standardize source layout.
Linting tools can detect suspicious patterns and enforce project conventions.
ESLint is a commonly used JavaScript linting tool.
A linter is not a proof of correctness.
A formatter is not a validator of program semantics.
A clean lint run does not prove a program is correct.
Testing verifies selected expected behavior.
A unit test exercises a bounded unit of behavior.
An integration test exercises interaction between components.
An end-to-end test exercises a larger application path.
A regression test protects behavior that previously failed or was established.
A negative test verifies behavior under invalid or prohibited conditions.
JavaScript tests should include asynchronous failure paths when asynchronous behavior matters.
A Promise-based test should wait for the Promise to settle.
A test that exits before asynchronous work completes has not tested that work correctly.
Fake timers can provide deterministic control of timer-dependent tests when used appropriately.
Excessive mocking can cause tests to verify the mock arrangement rather than actual component integration.
Tests should assert externally meaningful behavior.
A passing test proves only the conditions exercised by that test.
Browser developer tools can inspect JavaScript execution.
console.log writes diagnostic information to a console provided by the host.
Console output is not a durable audit log.
Production diagnostics should use logging appropriate to the runtime and system requirements.
Sensitive data should not be written casually to browser consoles.
Source maps associate transformed or bundled code with original source locations.
Source maps can aid debugging.
Minification reduces source representation size and can obscure formatting.
Minification is not a security boundary.
Client-side source code should be considered obtainable by the client.
Optimization strategies vary between engines.
Performance should be measured rather than inferred solely from syntax.
Algorithmic complexity can matter more than micro-optimizations.
Repeated DOM operations can cause expensive rendering work in browser applications.
Batching DOM changes can improve performance in applicable cases.
Network latency often dominates small local computation costs in web applications.
Caching can reduce repeated work.
A cache is not the authoritative data source unless the system explicitly defines it as such.
Cached information can become stale.
A cache miss is not necessarily an application error.
A cache miss and service unavailability are different conditions.
A retrieval miss and a network failure are different conditions.
Error categories should preserve the actual failure.
JavaScript supports destructuring.
Array destructuring extracts values from iterable positions.
Object destructuring extracts properties by name.
Default values can be supplied during destructuring.
Rest syntax collects remaining values or properties according to context.
The ... syntax has different meanings depending on whether it is used as rest or spread.
Spreading an object creates a shallow copy of enumerable own properties according to object spread rules.
A shallow copy does not recursively clone nested objects.
Two shallow copies can still refer to the same nested object.
Object.assign performs shallow property assignment.
Structured cloning can copy supported structured data using host-provided structuredClone facilities where available.
Not every JavaScript value can be structured-cloned.
JSON cloning loses or changes unsupported JavaScript values and types.
Mutation changes an existing object.
Immutability avoids changing an existing value or object state according to the selected design.
JavaScript does not make ordinary objects immutable by default.
Object.freeze prevents certain modifications to an object itself.
Object.freeze is shallow.
Nested objects are not automatically frozen by freezing their containing object.
Pure functions produce results without observable side effects according to their defined inputs.
JavaScript does not require functions to be pure.
Pure functions can be easier to test and reason about.
Side effects include operations such as changing external state, performing I/O, or modifying shared objects.
Global mutable state can make software harder to reason about and test.
Browser scripts historically shared global scope in ways that could create name collisions.
ECMAScript modules provide module-scoped bindings.
Strict mode changes certain JavaScript semantics and error handling.
The directive "use strict" enables strict mode in applicable script or function contexts.
ECMAScript modules execute in strict mode automatically.
Strict mode prevents or reports several historically error-prone behaviors.
Strict mode does not make a program automatically secure.
Property descriptors define characteristics of object properties.
Properties can be writable or non-writable.
Properties can be enumerable or non-enumerable.
Properties can be configurable or non-configurable.
Properties can be data properties or accessor properties.
A setter handles assignment to an accessor property.
Reading a property is therefore not guaranteed to be free of side effects when accessors or proxies are involved.
Proxy can intercept certain operations on another object.
Reflect provides functions corresponding to several fundamental object operations.
Symbols provide unique primitive values often used as property keys.
Symbol.for uses a global symbol registry.
Symbols created separately with Symbol() are distinct even when given the same description.
JavaScript supports iterators.
A generator function uses function* syntax.
A generator can pause using yield.
Generators implement iteration protocols.
Async generators can produce asynchronous sequences.
for await...of consumes asynchronous iterables in appropriate contexts.
JavaScript can process binary data.
ArrayBuffer represents a raw binary data buffer.
Typed arrays provide typed views over binary buffers.
Uint8Array provides a view of unsigned 8-bit integer elements.
DataView provides explicit reading and writing of multiple numeric formats and byte orders.
Binary protocol code should explicitly define byte order.
Machine memory representation should not be assumed to be a network protocol.
Protocol field widths should be defined explicitly.
Protocol string encoding should be defined explicitly.
TextEncoder commonly encodes strings as UTF-8 in supported web and runtime environments.
TextDecoder decodes byte sequences using a selected encoding.
Deterministic binary protocols should not depend on object property layout.
A protocol is a contract between communicating components.
An API is a source or service interface contract.
An ABI is a binary interface contract.
API and ABI are different concepts.
Browser JavaScript normally interacts through APIs and protocols rather than native ABIs.
Server-side runtimes can provide native extension mechanisms.
Native extensions must obey the runtime's supported binary interface.
A JavaScript package version is not an ABI version.
A README history is not necessarily the authoritative declaration of a currently active interface.
Current authoritative interface declarations should be distinguished from historical documentation.
Current configuration describes current state.
Evidence about a previous version does not establish that the previous version remains active.
When multiple sources disagree, the source directly defining current executable behavior should be distinguished from secondary descriptive documentation.
A technical answer should state only what available evidence supports.
Relevant evidence is not necessarily sufficient evidence.
Topic overlap does not establish a specific conclusion.
Retrieval should preserve important operators and technical tokens in a question.
The strings == and === carry important meaning in JavaScript questions.
The strings +, ++, &&, ||, ??, ?., =>, and === can be semantically significant JavaScript tokens.
A question asking about == and === is not equivalent to a generic question about equality.
A question asking about Promise rejection is not equivalent to a generic question about errors.
An answer should address the question asked rather than merely return a fact from the same broad topic.
A correct but unrelated fact is not a correct answer to the question.
Insufficient evidence should not be replaced with invented detail.
A concise supported answer is preferable to a detailed unsupported answer.
JavaScript is commonly one layer of a larger web system.
HTML provides structure.
JavaScript provides programmable client behavior.
HTTP carries requests and responses.
A server-side language such as PHP can implement application logic.
SQL can communicate with relational databases.
JSON can carry structured data between client and server.
Each layer has its own syntax and semantics.
Knowledge from one layer should not be substituted for another merely because they participate in the same application.
A JavaScript syntax rule is not a PHP syntax rule.
A PHP runtime behavior is not a browser JavaScript runtime behavior.
An SQL query is not JavaScript merely because JavaScript constructed the query string.
HTML is not a programming language merely because JavaScript can modify HTML.
CSS is not JavaScript merely because JavaScript can modify styles.
Separation of responsibilities makes web systems easier to reason about.
Client behavior belongs on the client when it genuinely concerns client interaction.
Trusted authorization belongs on the server.
Persistent authoritative data belongs in an appropriate trusted datastore according to application architecture.
Server responses should not be assumed correct merely because they are valid JSON.
Every trust boundary requires deliberate validation.
JavaScript can build sophisticated applications.
JavaScript can also build small scripts without a framework.
The appropriate architecture depends on the actual problem.
More code is not automatically more capable code.
Simple, bounded behavior is easier to reason about.
Deterministic behavior requires explicit control of state, ordering, serialization, time, randomness, and external dependencies.
SQL stands for Structured Query Language.
SQL is a language used to work with relational database systems.
SQL is used to define, query, insert, update, and delete relational data.
SQL is standardized, but database systems implement different SQL dialects and extensions.
SQL syntax and behavior can differ between database systems.
MySQL uses SQL.
MariaDB uses SQL.
Microsoft SQL Server uses SQL.
Oracle Database uses SQL.
SQL and MySQL are not the same thing.
SQL and MariaDB are not the same thing.
MariaDB is a relational database management system that implements an SQL dialect.
A relational database organizes data into relations commonly represented as tables.
A table contains rows and columns.
A row represents one record or tuple in a table.
A column represents an attribute of the data stored in a table.
Each column has a defined data type according to the database system.
A schema can define tables.
A schema can define columns.
A schema can define constraints.
A schema can define indexes.
A schema can define relationships.
A schema can define views and other database objects according to the database system.
A table name identifies a table.
A column name identifies a column.
Identifier case sensitivity can vary according to the database system and configuration.
SQL keywords are commonly written in uppercase for readability.
SQL keywords generally do not have to be uppercase in many database systems.
A SQL statement commonly ends with a semicolon in tools and scripts.
The semicolon separates SQL statements where required by the client or environment.
ALTER changes database objects.
DROP removes database objects.
TRUNCATE removes table data according to database-specific semantics.
SELECT is used to query data.
SELECT column_name requests a particular column.
Selecting only required columns can reduce unnecessary data transfer.
The FROM clause identifies the source of queried data.
The WHERE clause filters rows.
A WHERE condition determines which rows qualify.
The = operator commonly tests equality in SQL.
The <> operator represents inequality in standard SQL.
Many SQL implementations also accept != for inequality.
The < operator tests less-than relationships where supported by the operand types.
The > operator tests greater-than relationships.
The <= operator tests less-than-or-equal relationships.
The >= operator tests greater-than-or-equal relationships.
AND combines conditions requiring both to be true.
OR combines conditions requiring at least one to be true.
NOT negates a condition.
Parentheses can make logical grouping explicit.
Operator precedence affects SQL expressions.
WHERE conditions should use parentheses when grouping might otherwise be unclear.
SQL uses three-valued logic involving TRUE, FALSE, and UNKNOWN in applicable expressions.
NULL represents the absence of a known value.
NULL is not the same thing as zero.
NULL is not the same thing as an empty string.
The expression column = NULL does not correctly test for NULL.
IS NULL tests whether a value is NULL.
IS NOT NULL tests whether a value is not NULL.
Three-valued logic affects filtering behavior.
A WHERE clause retains rows whose condition evaluates to TRUE.
Rows for which a WHERE condition is FALSE are not selected.
Rows for which a WHERE condition is UNKNOWN are not selected.
COALESCE returns the first non-NULL expression in its argument list according to SQL semantics.
COALESCE can provide a fallback value for NULL.
NULL handling should be deliberate.
DISTINCT removes duplicate result rows according to the selected expressions.
ORDER BY sorts query results.
ORDER BY can sort ascending.
ASC specifies ascending ordering.
DESC specifies descending ordering.
Without ORDER BY, result row order should not be assumed.
Observed row order without ORDER BY is not a reliable ordering contract.
Ordering by a non-unique column can leave ties without deterministic relative ordering.
Additional ordering columns can break ties.
LIMIT restricts result count in SQL dialects that support LIMIT.
OFFSET skips a number of rows in dialects that support OFFSET.
Pagination syntax differs between database systems.
LIMIT is not universal standard syntax across every SQL implementation.
Pagination without deterministic ordering can produce unstable results.
OFFSET-based pagination can become expensive for large offsets.
Aliases provide alternate names for columns or tables within a statement.
AS can introduce an alias in applicable SQL syntax.
A column alias can make calculated results easier to understand.
A table alias can make joins and long queries easier to read.
COUNT counts rows or non-NULL expression values according to the selected form.
COUNT(*) counts qualifying rows.
COUNT(column) counts qualifying rows where that column expression is not NULL.
SUM calculates a total over numeric values.
AVG calculates an average.
MIN returns a minimum value.
Aggregate functions commonly ignore NULL values except where function semantics specify otherwise.
GROUP BY groups rows for aggregate calculations.
WHERE filters rows before grouping.
HAVING and WHERE serve different purposes.
Using HAVING where WHERE is appropriate can produce unnecessary work or different semantics.
SQL queries can combine tables.
A JOIN combines rows from multiple table expressions according to a join condition.
INNER JOIN returns matching combinations according to the join condition.
LEFT JOIN preserves rows from the left side even when no matching right-side row exists.
RIGHT JOIN preserves rows from the right side in database systems that support it.
FULL OUTER JOIN preserves unmatched rows from both sides in systems that support it.
CROSS JOIN produces combinations of rows from its inputs.
A cross join of A rows and B rows can produce A multiplied by B result rows.
An accidental missing join condition can produce unexpectedly large result sets.
Join conditions commonly compare key columns.
A foreign key relationship and a JOIN are related but distinct concepts.
A foreign key enforces a referential constraint.
A JOIN retrieves related data.
A declared foreign key does not automatically cause every query to join the tables.
A table can have a primary key.
Primary key columns are constrained according to the database's primary-key semantics.
A UNIQUE constraint prevents duplicate values or combinations according to database NULL semantics.
A foreign key references a candidate key, commonly a primary or unique key, in another or the same table according to database rules.
Referential integrity prevents invalid references according to defined constraints.
A foreign key can prevent insertion of a reference to a nonexistent parent row.
A foreign key can affect deletion of referenced rows.
ON DELETE behavior defines what happens when a referenced row is deleted.
ON UPDATE behavior defines what happens when a referenced key is updated.
CASCADE propagates an applicable referential action.
RESTRICT can prevent an operation that would violate references according to database semantics.
SET NULL sets referencing columns to NULL when allowed by the schema and referential action.
Cascading actions should be chosen deliberately.
A careless cascading delete can remove more data than intended.
UNIQUE enforces uniqueness according to its semantics.
CHECK enforces a Boolean condition in database systems that support and enforce it.
PRIMARY KEY establishes primary-key constraints.
FOREIGN KEY establishes referential constraints.
Database constraints protect invariants independently of application code.
Application validation does not replace important database constraints.
Database constraints do not replace all application validation.
Validation and integrity constraints serve related but different layers.
Data types describe permitted stored values.
SQL data types vary between database systems.
INTEGER represents integer values according to implementation rules.
SMALLINT represents a smaller-range integer type according to implementation rules.
DECIMAL represents exact decimal values according to declared precision and scale.
NUMERIC commonly represents exact numeric values.
VARCHAR represents variable-length character data.
TEXT commonly represents larger variable-length text in systems providing that type.
DATE represents calendar dates.
TIME represents times.
TIMESTAMP represents date-and-time information according to implementation semantics.
BOOLEAN represents Boolean values in systems with a Boolean type.
BINARY types store binary data in systems supporting them.
BLOB commonly stores binary large objects.
JSON types are provided by some database systems.
A database-specific JSON type is not part of every SQL implementation.
Exact monetary values should normally use an exact numeric representation rather than binary floating point.
Data type ranges should be chosen according to actual domain requirements.
A larger type is not automatically better.
Schema types should communicate and enforce expected data.
CREATE TABLE creates a table.
A CREATE TABLE statement defines columns and constraints.
ALTER TABLE modifies an existing table according to supported operations.
Dropping a table is destructive.
Database migrations should be versioned when repeatable deployment matters.
A migration should have a defined expected starting state.
A migration should have a defined resulting state.
Migration failure should be detectable.
Production schema changes should account for existing data.
Adding a NOT NULL column to a populated table requires handling existing rows according to the database and migration design.
Database-specific behavior matters for operational schema changes.
An INSERT statement can specify target columns.
Explicitly specifying target columns can make inserts more resilient to schema ordering changes.
A single INSERT can add multiple rows in database systems supporting multi-row syntax.
Generated identifiers can be returned using database-specific or standard-supported mechanisms.
Auto-increment behavior is database-specific.
AUTO_INCREMENT is associated with MySQL and MariaDB.
Identity-column syntax differs between database systems.
Sequence objects can generate numeric values in systems supporting sequences.
Generated numeric identifiers do not automatically provide globally unique identity.
UPDATE changes existing rows.
The WHERE clause restricts rows modified by UPDATE.
An UPDATE without an appropriate WHERE clause can modify every row in a table.
Destructive UPDATE operations should be reviewed carefully.
A DELETE without an appropriate WHERE clause can delete every row in a table.
DROP removes a database object.
TRUNCATE and DELETE have different transactional, logging, trigger, identity, and locking behavior depending on the database system.
TRUNCATE should not be treated as universally equivalent to DELETE without a WHERE clause.
A transaction groups database operations into a transactional unit.
BEGIN or database-specific syntax starts an explicit transaction.
COMMIT makes a successful transaction's changes durable according to the database's transaction guarantees.
ROLLBACK abandons changes made by a transaction according to transactional semantics.
Transactions support atomic groups of operations.
Atomicity means a transaction's operations are treated as a unit with respect to commit or rollback.
Consistency means transactions preserve defined database invariants when used correctly.
Isolation concerns interaction between concurrent transactions.
Durability concerns persistence of committed changes despite failures within the guarantees of the database system.
Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability are commonly abbreviated ACID.
ACID does not mean every application operation is automatically correct.
A transaction does not replace application validation.
A transaction does not automatically prevent logical race conditions.
Isolation levels control classes of interactions visible between concurrent transactions.
REPEATABLE READ provides stronger repeat-read behavior according to database semantics.
SERIALIZABLE provides behavior equivalent to some serial execution according to the database's serialization guarantees.
Isolation-level details differ between database systems.
MySQL or MariaDB behavior at a named isolation level should not automatically be assumed identical to another database engine.
A dirty read observes uncommitted changes from another transaction.
A phantom involves changes in rows satisfying a query predicate between reads according to isolation definitions.
Locks coordinate concurrent access.
A database can use row locks.
A database can use table locks.
Locking implementation is database-specific.
A deadlock occurs when transactions form a cycle of incompatible waits.
Applications should be prepared for transaction failure caused by deadlock resolution.
Retries should be bounded.
Retries should not blindly repeat non-idempotent external side effects.
Database transactions do not automatically include unrelated external systems.
An index is a data structure used by a database to accelerate certain data access patterns.
An index can reduce the work required for some queries.
An index consumes storage.
Maintaining an index adds work to inserts, updates, and deletes.
Indexes should reflect actual query and integrity requirements.
A primary key commonly has an associated index or index-like structure depending on the database.
A UNIQUE constraint commonly uses an index or equivalent enforcement mechanism.
A composite index contains multiple columns.
Column order matters in many composite index access patterns.
A query optimizer determines an execution strategy.
An execution plan describes how a database intends to execute a query.
EXPLAIN displays execution-plan information in many SQL database systems.
EXPLAIN syntax and output differ between database systems.
A query being syntactically short does not mean it is computationally inexpensive.
A query returning one row can still scan millions of rows.
An index can make some lookups faster.
An inappropriate index can be unused.
Statistics help query optimizers estimate data distributions and costs.
Optimization should be based on measured query behavior.
SELECT * can retrieve unnecessary columns.
Retrieving unnecessary data consumes database, network, and application resources.
Filtering at the database can reduce unnecessary transfer.
Applying a function to an indexed column in a predicate can prevent some index access strategies depending on the database and expression.
Implicit type conversions can affect index use and comparison semantics.
Second Normal Form is commonly abbreviated 2NF.
Third Normal Form is commonly abbreviated 3NF.
Boyce-Codd Normal Form is commonly abbreviated BCNF.
Normalization is a design tool rather than a requirement to maximize normalization in every system.
Denormalization deliberately introduces redundancy for defined operational reasons.
Denormalization creates consistency responsibilities.
A normalized design can still perform poorly if queries and indexes are inappropriate.
Database design should reflect data invariants and access patterns.
A one-to-many relationship associates one parent with multiple child rows.
A many-to-many relationship is commonly represented using an associative or junction table.
A junction table stores references to related rows.
A junction table can have a composite primary key.
Relationships should be enforced with constraints when database integrity requires enforcement.
A view does not necessarily store its result data.
Materialized views require refresh behavior.
Views do not automatically improve performance.
Stored procedures contain database-side executable logic in systems supporting them.
Stored functions return values according to database-specific procedural facilities.
Triggers execute automatically in response to defined database events.
Stored procedures and triggers are not portable across every SQL implementation.
Triggers can enforce or automate behavior.
Hidden trigger behavior can make data changes harder to reason about.
SQL injection occurs when untrusted input changes the intended structure of an SQL statement.
Concatenating untrusted values directly into SQL can create SQL injection vulnerabilities.
Escaping is not a universal substitute for parameterized queries.
Prepared statements support parameter binding.
Application code should use parameters for untrusted data values.
A parameter represents a data value.
A parameter does not ordinarily represent an arbitrary table name.
A parameter does not ordinarily represent an SQL keyword.
Dynamic SQL identifiers require validation against an allowed set or another database-specific safe construction mechanism.
A query such as SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ? uses a placeholder in APIs and database systems supporting question-mark parameters.
Placeholder syntax differs between database systems and client libraries.
Some systems use numbered placeholders.
Some systems use named placeholders.
The principle of parameter binding is more important than a particular placeholder syntax.
A parameterized query can still contain a logic flaw.
SQL injection prevention and authorization are different security responsibilities.
Database credentials should have only required privileges.
Read-only operations can use read-only privileges where architecture supports them.
Database users and application users are different identity concepts.
Authentication determines identity.
Authorization determines permitted actions.
Database authorization controls what database identities can do.
Both layers can matter.
Passwords should not be stored as plaintext in database tables.
Password values should be stored using an appropriate password hashing scheme.
SQL does not itself make password storage secure.
Database encryption and password hashing solve different problems.
Encrypted database storage can protect data under certain storage-threat conditions.
TLS can protect database connections in transit when correctly configured.
TLS does not repair SQL injection vulnerabilities.
Backups preserve recoverable copies of database data.
A backup is useful only if it can be restored successfully when required.
Backup procedures should be tested through restoration.
Replication is not the same thing as backup.
Replication copies data changes to other database instances according to replication design.
A replica containing the same accidental deletion is not an independent historical backup.
Point-in-time recovery can restore a database to an earlier state when appropriate backups and logs are available.
Recovery objectives should be defined according to system requirements.
Recovery Point Objective is commonly abbreviated RPO.
Recovery Time Objective is commonly abbreviated RTO.
Durability does not eliminate the need for backups.
A database can become unavailable.
Applications should handle connection failures.
Applications should handle authentication failures.
Applications should handle query failures.
Applications should handle timeouts.
Applications should not convert every database error into the same internal meaning.
A duplicate-key violation is different from a connection timeout.
A syntax error is different from a deadlock.
A missing table is different from a rejected credential.
Preserving error categories improves diagnosis and correct application behavior.
Database error messages can contain sensitive schema or query information.
Raw database errors should not automatically be exposed to untrusted web clients.
Opening a new physical database connection for every small operation can be expensive.
Connection pooling reuses database connections.
Applications should release database resources according to their client library contract.
Long-running transactions can retain locks and old row versions depending on the database.
Transactions should be kept appropriately bounded.
A transaction should not remain open while waiting unnecessarily for user interaction.
A connection timeout limits waiting to establish or acquire a connection according to the client.
A timeout does not prove that the database performed no work.
An application must understand whether an operation may have committed before retrying after ambiguous connection failure.
Idempotency matters when retrying database operations.
A generated request identifier can help correlate database operations with application logs.
Audit logs should be protected against unauthorized modification.
A normal application log and an audit record serve different purposes.
A subquery is a query nested inside another SQL statement.
A scalar subquery returns a single value according to its context.
An EXISTS subquery tests whether qualifying rows exist.
EXISTS can express relationship existence without returning the matching data.
NOT IN involving NULL values can produce unintuitive UNKNOWN results.
NOT EXISTS can be safer for some anti-join logic involving nullable values.
A correlated subquery references values from an outer query.
A Common Table Expression is commonly abbreviated CTE.
WITH introduces a CTE in systems supporting it.
A CTE names a query expression for use by a larger statement.
A CTE does not inherently materialize its result.
Materialization behavior depends on the database and optimizer.
Recursive CTEs can express hierarchical and graph-like traversal.
An incorrectly bounded recursive query can consume excessive resources.
PARTITION BY divides rows into window partitions.
ORDER BY inside a window defines ordering for applicable window calculations.
ROW_NUMBER assigns sequential numbers within its window ordering.
DENSE_RANK assigns ranks without gaps after ties.
Window functions and GROUP BY solve different problems.
UNION combines result sets while removing duplicates according to UNION semantics.
UNION ALL combines result sets without duplicate elimination.
UNION ALL can avoid unnecessary duplicate-removal work when duplicates are permitted.
The combined queries in a UNION must have compatible result structures according to database rules.
INTERSECT returns rows common to result sets in systems supporting it.
Set-operation support varies between database systems.
SQL operates primarily on sets or multisets of rows rather than requiring row-by-row procedural thinking.
Executing one query per returned row is commonly called the N+1 query problem when an initial query causes repeated related queries.
N+1 query patterns can cause excessive database round trips.
Reducing round trips can improve performance.
One enormous query is not automatically better than several bounded queries.
Query architecture should reflect correctness, maintainability, and measured performance.
Database state is persistent state.
Application memory is not automatically persistent.
A variable in PHP is not a database row.
A C structure is not a database row.
A Go struct is not a database row.
An object-relational mapper can map application objects to relational data.
An object-relational mapper is commonly abbreviated ORM.
An ORM is not a database.
An ORM generates or executes database operations through a database interface.
Developers using an ORM still need to understand transactions, relationships, indexes, constraints, and query behavior.
An ORM abstraction can produce inefficient SQL.
Generated SQL should be inspected when performance or correctness requires it.
Raw SQL can provide precise control.
Raw SQL also requires careful parameterization and maintenance.
The correct choice depends on the application.
PHP can communicate with SQL databases using database client extensions and libraries.
PDO is a PHP database access abstraction.
PDO is not SQL.
PDO can execute SQL through supported database drivers.
MySQLi is a PHP extension for interacting with MySQL-compatible databases.
JavaScript server runtimes can communicate with SQL databases using database client libraries.
Browser JavaScript should not normally connect directly to a private relational database.
A browser should communicate with an application server or defined service interface.
Exposing database credentials to browser JavaScript exposes those credentials to the client.
C# can communicate with SQL databases using .NET database providers.
Programming-language database APIs and SQL are separate layers.
The application constructs or selects an SQL operation.
The database returns results or status.
The application interprets those results according to its own contract.
An HTTP request is not an SQL query.
An HTTP endpoint can cause server code to execute SQL.
A database error is not automatically an HTTP 503.
An unavailable database can make an HTTP service or operation unavailable when that database is required.
A constraint violation is not service unavailability.
A query returning zero rows is not service unavailability.
A query returning zero rows can be a valid result.
A missing database record and a database connection failure are different conditions.
Error reporting should identify the actual failure layer.
A SQL query that executes successfully can still return incorrect logical results.
Successful syntax does not establish semantic correctness.
A query returning data does not establish that the caller was authorized to see the data.
Correctness includes both query logic and surrounding application requirements.
Stored data can contain malicious strings.
Database storage does not sanitize data.
Parameterized INSERT statements prevent SQL injection into the statement but do not make stored text safe HTML.
Input validation, SQL parameterization, and output encoding solve different problems.
Block comments commonly use /* and */.
Attackers can use SQL comments as part of injection attempts when vulnerable query construction permits them.
The correct defense is safe query construction and parameterization rather than attempting to blacklist suspicious characters.
Blacklisting quote characters is not a reliable SQL injection defense.
Escaping rules depend on the database, connection configuration, and client API.
Prepared statements avoid requiring application code to manually reproduce SQL literal escaping rules for bound data.
Prepared statements can improve security and can sometimes improve repeated execution efficiency.
A prepared statement separates preparation from execution according to database and driver behavior.
Server-side and client-side emulated prepared statements are not necessarily identical.
Client-library configuration can affect prepared-statement behavior.
Application code should understand the contract of its database library.
Character encoding matters in databases.
UTF-8 is a common text encoding.
A database connection has character encoding settings.
A table or column can have character-set and collation settings depending on the database.
A character set defines how characters are represented.
A collation defines comparison and ordering rules for text.
Collation can affect equality.
Collation can affect ordering.
Collation can affect uniqueness.
Case sensitivity can depend on collation.
Accent sensitivity can depend on collation.
Text equality in a database should not be assumed to mean byte-for-byte equality unless the selected collation and type provide that behavior.
Binary comparison and linguistic comparison solve different requirements.
Identifiers and user-facing text can require different comparison policies.
Database timestamps require defined timezone semantics.
A DATE value represents a date rather than an instant in time.
A timestamp representation should have a defined timezone interpretation.
Storing UTC timestamps is common for interoperable event records.
UTC storage does not remove the need to convert correctly for user-facing local time.
Database server timezone and application timezone can differ.
Implicit timezone assumptions can produce incorrect results.
Database-generated time and application-generated time can differ.
Replication can introduce delay.
A replica can lag behind its source.
Read-after-write consistency should be explicitly considered when replicas are used.
High availability and consistency involve architectural tradeoffs.
A failover changes which database instance provides service.
Failover should preserve required data guarantees.
A successful TCP connection to a database server does not prove that the database is operational for every required query.
A successful authentication does not prove required tables exist.
A successful query does not prove replication is healthy.
Health checks should test the capability they claim to represent.
SELECT 1 does not prove every application schema operation works.
Schema qualification can verify required tables or migrations.
Application startup can validate required database schema version.
Schema version mismatch should be distinguished from database unavailability.
Database migrations and application versions should have defined compatibility.
Backward-compatible schema changes can permit staged deployment.
Dropping a column still used by an older application version can break that application.
Compatibility should be deliberate.
Soft deletion marks data as deleted without physically removing the row.
Hard deletion physically removes data.
Soft deletion and archival are different concepts.
Soft-deleted rows require queries to respect deletion state.
A database retention rule should be explicit.
Permanent historical records should be represented according to their governing lifecycle rather than casually deleted.
Deleting a production row does not necessarily erase every backup copy immediately.
Privacy and retention requirements must account for replicas, backups, logs, and derived data.
A database can enforce immutable-like records through permissions, triggers, append-only design, or application architecture.
An append-only table records new events rather than modifying historical events.
Event logs and current-state tables serve different purposes.
Current state can be represented separately from historical events.
Event sourcing represents state as a sequence of events according to that architecture.
Event sourcing is not required for ordinary relational database applications.
Relational databases can store both current state and historical records.
Database design should follow actual requirements rather than fashion.
SQL queries should answer the question actually being asked.
A query that retrieves related data is not necessarily sufficient evidence for a more specific conclusion.
The presence of a historical row does not prove that the historical state remains current.
A current-status column and a historical event table represent different kinds of evidence.
When answering a question about current state, current authoritative state should be distinguished from historical records.
A version-history row does not establish that an older version is currently active.
A database containing versions 1.3, 1.4, and 1.5 does not imply that all three versions are active.
A query should filter according to the state required by the question.
Version strings do not always sort correctly as ordinary text.
Semantic version comparison is different from lexicographic string comparison.
Database schema should explicitly represent active state when active state matters.
Evidence retrieval and evidence interpretation are separate operations.
A database can return relevant evidence without that evidence supporting the requested conclusion.
Correct SQL retrieval does not guarantee correct reasoning about the retrieved rows.
A query can be syntactically valid and semantically wrong.
A query can be semantically correct and operationally inefficient.
A query can be efficient and still violate authorization.
A secure query can still answer the wrong question.
Database engineering requires correctness, integrity, security, performance, recoverability, and understandable behavior.
Simple schemas are easier to reason about when they satisfy the requirements.
Explicit constraints are preferable to undocumented assumptions.
Explicit ordering is preferable when deterministic ordering matters.
Explicit transactions are preferable when multiple writes must succeed or fail as a unit.
Explicit error handling is preferable to silently discarding database failures.
Parameterized queries are preferable to constructing SQL by concatenating untrusted data.
Tested backups are preferable to assuming backups work.
Defined ownership of schema changes is preferable to uncontrolled production modification.
A relational database is a persistent system of record only when the architecture assigns it that authority.
SQL is a tool for expressing operations over relational data.
HTTP stands for Hypertext Transfer Protocol.
HTTP is an application-layer protocol.
HTTP uses a request-response model.
An HTTP client sends a request.
An HTTP server sends a response.
A request and a response are different protocol messages.
HTTP defines message semantics independently of the programming language implementing the client or server.
PHP can implement HTTP server behavior.
JavaScript can initiate HTTP requests through host APIs.
C can implement HTTP clients and servers.
C++ can implement HTTP clients and servers.
Go can implement HTTP clients and servers.
HTTP is not PHP.
HTTP is not JavaScript.
HTTP is not HTML.
HTTP is not JSON.
HTTP is not TCP.
HTTP can transport HTML.
HTTP can transport JSON.
HTTP can transport binary data.
HTTP commonly operates over TCP in HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 deployments.
HTTP/3 operates over QUIC.
HTTPS is HTTP transported with TLS protection.
TLS and HTTP solve different protocol responsibilities.
TLS provides transport security properties.
HTTP defines application request and response semantics.
Using HTTPS does not make an application logically correct.
Using HTTPS does not provide application authorization by itself.
Using HTTPS does not validate application input.
An HTTP request contains a method.
An HTTP request identifies a target.
An HTTP request can contain header fields.
An HTTP response contains header fields.
An HTTP response can contain a message body.
The request method communicates the requested operation semantics.
GET requests retrieval of a representation.
POST submits data for processing according to the target resource's semantics.
PUT requests creation or replacement of the state of a target resource according to the supplied representation.
PATCH requests partial modification according to the patch representation and server contract.
DELETE requests removal of a resource association according to server semantics.
OPTIONS requests communication options for a target resource or server.
TRACE performs a diagnostic loop-back operation where supported.
CONNECT establishes a tunnel according to HTTP semantics.
Not every HTTP server supports every method.
A server can restrict methods available for a resource.
GET is a safe HTTP method according to HTTP semantics.
HEAD is a safe HTTP method.
OPTIONS is a safe HTTP method.
A safe method requests read-only semantics from the client perspective.
Safe does not mean that a server performs literally no internal activity.
A server can log a GET request without violating GET's safe semantics.
GET should not be designed to request destructive state changes.
HTTP defines idempotent methods.
GET is idempotent.
PUT is idempotent.
DELETE is idempotent according to HTTP method semantics.
OPTIONS is idempotent.
TRACE is idempotent.
POST is not inherently idempotent.
PATCH is not inherently idempotent.
Idempotency does not mean every repeated response must be byte-for-byte identical.
Deleting an already deleted resource can produce a different response while DELETE remains idempotent in intended effect.
HTTP status codes are three-digit integers.
Status codes are grouped into classes.
3xx status codes concern redirection.
5xx status codes indicate server-side failures.
The status class is determined by the first digit.
A 2xx response is not automatically HTTP 200.
A 4xx response is not automatically HTTP 404.
A 5xx response is not automatically HTTP 500.
The specific status code should represent the actual result.
HTTP 100 means Continue.
HTTP 101 means Switching Protocols.
HTTP 200 means OK.
HTTP 201 means Created.
HTTP 202 means Accepted.
HTTP 204 means No Content.
HTTP 206 means Partial Content.
HTTP 301 means Moved Permanently.
HTTP 302 means Found.
HTTP 303 means See Other.
HTTP 304 means Not Modified.
HTTP 307 means Temporary Redirect.
HTTP 308 means Permanent Redirect.
HTTP 401 means Unauthorized.
Despite its historical name, HTTP 401 concerns missing or invalid authentication credentials.
HTTP 403 means Forbidden.
HTTP 403 indicates that the server understood the request but refuses to fulfill it according to authorization or policy.
HTTP 404 means Not Found.
HTTP 405 means Method Not Allowed.
HTTP 406 means Not Acceptable.
HTTP 408 means Request Timeout.
HTTP 409 means Conflict.
HTTP 410 means Gone.
HTTP 412 means Precondition Failed.
HTTP 413 means Content Too Large.
HTTP 415 means Unsupported Media Type.
HTTP 422 means Unprocessable Content.
HTTP 426 means Upgrade Required.
HTTP 428 means Precondition Required.
HTTP 429 means Too Many Requests.
HTTP 431 means Request Header Fields Too Large.
HTTP 500 means Internal Server Error.
HTTP 501 means Not Implemented.
HTTP 503 means Service Unavailable.
HTTP 504 means Gateway Timeout.
HTTP 505 means HTTP Version Not Supported.
A malformed request can produce HTTP 400.
Invalid request syntax can produce HTTP 400.
A missing required request field can produce HTTP 400 when that makes the request invalid.
HTTP 401 is appropriate when valid authentication credentials are required and have not been supplied or accepted.
HTTP 403 is appropriate when the request is understood but the server refuses authorization.
Authentication and authorization are different concepts.
Authentication determines identity.
Authorization determines permitted actions.
A user can be authenticated but not authorized.
HTTP 404 means the target resource was not found or the server is not willing to disclose its existence according to applicable semantics.
A resource not being found is not an internal server error.
HTTP 405 means the target resource exists or is understood but does not permit the request method.
A 405 response should include an Allow header identifying supported methods according to HTTP requirements.
A duplicate or conflicting state transition can produce 409 when that accurately represents the application contract.
HTTP 410 indicates that a target resource is known to be gone and the condition is expected to be permanent.
HTTP 413 indicates that request content exceeds a limit the server is willing or able to process.
HTTP 422 indicates that the server understands the content type and syntax but cannot process the contained instructions according to the request semantics.
HTTP 429 represents rate limiting or excessive request frequency.
HTTP 500 represents an unexpected server condition preventing fulfillment of the request.
A programming error can produce HTTP 500.
An unhandled exception can produce HTTP 500.
An internal operation failing unexpectedly can produce HTTP 500.
HTTP 500 should not be used merely because the requested information does not exist.
HTTP 501 is different from HTTP 405.
501 means the server does not implement the required functionality.
HTTP 502 indicates that a server acting as a gateway or proxy received an invalid response from an upstream server.
HTTP 503 indicates that the server is currently unable to handle the request.
Temporary overload can produce HTTP 503.
Maintenance can produce HTTP 503.
Failure of a required service dependency can produce HTTP 503 when it makes the requested service unavailable.
HTTP 503 represents service availability.
HTTP 503 does not mean "no evidence was found."
HTTP 503 does not mean "the search returned zero results."
HTTP 503 does not mean "the answer is uncertain."
HTTP 503 does not mean "the resource does not exist."
A knowledge miss is not inherently HTTP 503.
A retrieval miss is not inherently HTTP 503.
An unsupported conclusion is not inherently HTTP 503.
An unavailable required internal service can legitimately cause HTTP 503.
A failed optional subsystem does not necessarily make the whole service unavailable.
A server should report availability according to the capability actually required by the request.
HTTP 504 indicates that a gateway or proxy did not receive a timely response from an upstream server.
HTTP 408 concerns the client request timing at the server.
HTTP 504 concerns an upstream response timeout at a gateway or proxy.
A standalone application that is not acting as a gateway should not blindly translate every timeout into 504.
HTTP status describes HTTP request handling.
Internal error categories can be more precise than HTTP status codes.
An application can record a detailed internal error while returning an appropriate HTTP status externally.
Internal diagnostic information should not automatically be exposed to untrusted clients.
A response status and a response body serve different purposes.
The status code communicates protocol-level outcome semantics.
The response body can provide application-specific detail.
A successful HTTP response can contain an application-level result indicating no records were found.
Zero search results can be returned with HTTP 200 when the search itself succeeded.
An empty JSON array can represent a successful query with no matching records.
Unknown is not unavailable.
Unsupported is not unavailable.
Invalid is not unavailable.
Unauthorized is not unavailable.
Forbidden is not unavailable.
Conflict is not unavailable.
Unavailable should mean unavailable.
Error categories should preserve their actual meanings.
Header field values have syntax defined by each header field.
Content-Type identifies the media type of message content.
Content-Length indicates message content length in bytes when present and applicable.
Accept communicates media types acceptable for the response.
Accept-Encoding communicates acceptable content codings.
Content-Encoding identifies applied content coding.
Authorization carries authentication credentials according to an authentication scheme.
WWW-Authenticate communicates authentication challenges.
Location identifies another URI in redirects and certain creation responses.
Cache-Control communicates caching directives.
If-None-Match supplies entity-tag conditions.
If-Match supplies entity-tag preconditions.
Last-Modified communicates a representation modification time.
If-Modified-Since provides a date-based conditional request.
User-Agent identifies information about a requesting user agent according to client behavior.
Server can identify information about server software.
Exposing unnecessary server implementation details can aid fingerprinting.
Host identifies the target host and port information in HTTP/1.1 requests according to protocol rules.
Cookie carries cookies from a client to a server.
Set-Cookie instructs a user agent to store a cookie.
Vary describes request header fields affecting representation selection and caching.
Headers are not inherently trustworthy.
Request headers originate from clients or intermediaries.
A client can forge many ordinary request headers.
User-Agent is not an authentication mechanism.
Referer is not a reliable authentication mechanism.
Origin can be useful in security policy but does not replace authentication.
Reverse proxies can add forwarding headers.
text/plain identifies plain text.
application/json identifies JSON content.
application/x-www-form-urlencoded is commonly used for HTML form data.
multipart/form-data is commonly used for forms containing files or multipart fields.
Accept describes representations the recipient would prefer to receive.
Content-Type and Accept are different headers.
A JSON body should normally be identified using application/json.
Valid JSON syntax does not prove that the JSON satisfies an application's schema.
Media-type validation and semantic validation are different steps.
An HTTP message body is a sequence of bytes interpreted according to representation metadata.
A request body can be empty.
A response body can be empty.
HTTP 204 responses do not contain response content.
HEAD responses do not include response content even though representation metadata can describe what a GET would return.
A response body should not be assumed to contain JSON merely because an application usually returns JSON.
Clients should inspect the protocol contract and applicable Content-Type.
Parsing an HTML error page as JSON can produce a JSON parser error that hides the original HTTP result.
Body parsing failure and HTTP status failure are different conditions.
A URL is a type of URI associated with locating resources.
An HTTP URL contains a scheme.
An HTTP URL contains an authority.
An HTTP URL can contain a path.
An HTTP URL can contain a query.
An HTTP URL can contain a fragment.
Query parameters are part of the URL query component.
Path parameters extracted by an application router are untrusted input.
HTTP headers are untrusted input.
Request bodies are untrusted input.
Client-provided filenames are untrusted input.
Validation should occur before untrusted input controls privileged behavior.
A URL path and a filesystem path are different concepts.
Mapping URL paths directly to filesystem paths without validation can create path traversal vulnerabilities.
Percent-encoding represents certain bytes in URI components.
URL decoding can change the interpreted value of input.
Repeated or inconsistent decoding can create security problems.
Canonicalization should be deliberate.
A server should define accepted URL forms.
HTTP supports persistent connections.
HTTP/1.1 commonly permits multiple request-response exchanges over one TCP connection.
One TCP connection can carry multiple HTTP requests.
Closing one HTTP response does not necessarily mean closing the TCP connection.
HTTP/2 multiplexes multiple streams over a connection.
HTTP/2 allows multiple concurrent request-response exchanges on one connection.
HTTP/3 provides HTTP semantics over QUIC.
Application semantics should not depend unnecessarily on whether HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, or HTTP/3 carries the request.
A server can support multiple HTTP protocol versions.
HTTP version negotiation can occur through TLS and protocol mechanisms.
HTTP is stateless at the protocol application semantics level between requests unless state is represented or associated externally.
Applications can maintain sessions across HTTP requests.
A session is application state associated with requests.
Cookies can carry session identifiers.
A session identifier is not the session data itself when the server stores session state separately.
HTTP does not require PHP sessions.
PHP sessions are one implementation of application session state.
JavaScript does not create server-side session security merely by retaining a token.
Session identifiers should be protected against disclosure.
Session fixation occurs when an attacker can cause a victim to use a session identifier known to the attacker.
Session identifiers can be regenerated after authentication to mitigate fixation risks.
Session expiration limits session lifetime.
Logging out should invalidate or otherwise terminate authenticated session authority according to application design.
Deleting a visible client cookie alone may not invalidate server-side session state.
HttpOnly prevents ordinary browser JavaScript from reading the cookie.
SameSite influences whether cookies are sent in cross-site contexts.
Path limits the URL paths for which a cookie is applicable.
Domain controls host scope according to cookie rules.
Expires can define an absolute cookie expiration.
Max-Age can define a relative cookie lifetime.
Cookie attributes provide different protections.
HttpOnly does not encrypt a cookie.
Secure does not prevent server-side theft of a session identifier.
SameSite does not replace authorization.
HTTP authentication can use multiple schemes.
Basic authentication sends credentials encoded according to the Basic scheme.
Base64 encoding is not encryption.
Basic authentication should be protected by TLS when credentials require confidentiality.
Bearer authentication uses bearer tokens.
Possession of a bearer token is ordinarily sufficient to exercise the authority represented by that token.
Bearer tokens should be protected as credentials.
Authentication credentials should not be placed in URLs without a compelling protocol reason.
URLs can appear in logs, browser history, analytics, and referrer information.
Authorization headers are generally preferable to query strings for bearer credentials.
HTTP caching stores reusable responses.
Caching can also serve stale data.
Cache policy should reflect data semantics.
no-cache requires validation before reuse according to caching semantics.
private restricts response reuse to private caches.
An ETag identifies a selected representation version.
Conditional requests can avoid retransmitting unchanged representations.
If-None-Match can validate a cached representation.
HTTP 304 is not an error.
Caches must respect Vary.
Sensitive responses require deliberate caching policy.
A reverse proxy can cache HTTP responses.
A CDN can cache HTTP responses.
301 represents permanent redirection.
302 represents Found and historically has client behavior considerations.
307 preserves the request method during temporary redirection.
308 preserves the request method during permanent redirection.
Redirect semantics matter for state-changing requests.
An open redirect occurs when untrusted input controls a redirect destination without adequate restrictions.
Redirect targets derived from user input should be validated.
Accept-Encoding can communicate supported content codings.
Content negotiation should not silently change security semantics.
Compression reduces representation size.
gzip is a common HTTP content coding.
br identifies Brotli content coding.
Compression can reduce bandwidth.
Compression consumes CPU resources.
HTTP range requests request portions of representations.
206 Partial Content represents successful partial transfer.
Range handling must validate requested bounds.
HTTP supports conditional modification.
If-Match can require a representation to have a particular entity tag before an operation proceeds.
Conditional modification can prevent lost updates.
Optimistic concurrency uses state versions or equivalent preconditions to detect conflicting changes.
HTTP 412 indicates a failed precondition.
HTTP 428 can require clients to submit preconditions.
Concurrency control and authentication are separate concerns.
CORS is primarily a browser mechanism governing script access to cross-origin responses.
CORS uses HTTP headers.
Access-Control-Allow-Headers identifies permitted request headers.
Access-Control-Allow-Credentials controls credentialed cross-origin behavior according to CORS rules.
A CORS preflight commonly uses OPTIONS.
CORS is not authentication.
CORS is not authorization.
CORS does not prevent non-browser clients from sending requests.
A server exposed to a network remains exposed regardless of whether CORS permits browser JavaScript to read its responses.
Cross-Site Request Forgery is commonly abbreviated CSRF.
CSRF abuses a user's authenticated browser context to cause unwanted requests.
CSRF protections can include unpredictable request tokens.
SameSite cookies can reduce some CSRF exposure.
Checking request methods can support correct design but does not by itself prevent CSRF.
GET should not perform destructive actions.
XSS and CSRF are different vulnerabilities.
Content-Security-Policy controls permitted content sources and execution according to browser CSP processing.
X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff restricts certain MIME sniffing behavior.
Strict-Transport-Security instructs supporting browsers to use HTTPS for a host according to HSTS rules.
Permissions-Policy controls selected browser capabilities.
Security headers complement application security.
Security headers do not repair broken authorization.
Security headers do not repair SQL injection.
Security headers do not make untrusted HTML safe.
HTTP servers must handle malformed input.
Request parsing has security consequences.
Conflicting message-length information can create request-smuggling vulnerabilities when intermediaries interpret requests differently.
Proxies and origin servers must agree on request framing.
HTTP/1.1 message framing must follow protocol requirements.
Servers should use mature protocol parsers rather than ad hoc parsing where practical.
Request limits protect resources.
A server can limit header size.
A server can limit header count.
A server can limit request-line length.
A server can limit concurrent connections.
A server can limit requests per client according to policy.
A server can impose timeouts.
A read timeout limits how long a server waits for request data according to implementation semantics.
A write timeout limits how long a server waits while sending response data according to implementation semantics.
An idle timeout limits unused persistent connections.
A connection timeout limits establishing a connection according to client implementation.
A total request timeout can bound an entire client operation.
Different timeout types protect against different failure modes.
A timeout should report the operation that timed out.
A timeout waiting for an upstream service is not the same as a malformed client request.
A timeout waiting for a local database is not automatically a gateway timeout.
System architecture determines the correct error classification.
Retries can also duplicate state-changing operations.
A client should not blindly retry every failed request.
Idempotent operations are generally safer to retry.
Retry policy should be bounded.
Retry policy should include delays where repeated immediate attempts would create load.
Retry-After can guide retry timing for applicable responses.
Exponential backoff increases delays between repeated attempts.
A retry storm can worsen an overloaded service.
A circuit breaker is an application resilience pattern rather than an HTTP protocol requirement.
Health checks determine whether a service or capability is operating according to defined criteria.
A health endpoint should report what it actually tests.
A process being alive does not prove its dependencies are healthy.
A database connection being healthy does not prove every application capability works.
Liveness and readiness are different concepts.
Liveness indicates whether a process should be considered alive according to deployment policy.
Readiness indicates whether a service is ready to receive applicable traffic.
A temporarily unready service can legitimately be removed from load balancing.
Health checks should not perform unnecessarily destructive operations.
HTTP APIs expose application capabilities through HTTP.
An API endpoint is a defined HTTP target with an application contract.
An endpoint contract can define path.
An endpoint contract can define request headers.
An endpoint contract can define request body.
An endpoint contract can define response status.
An endpoint contract can define response headers.
An endpoint contract can define response body.
API behavior should be explicit.
API versioning manages compatibility across interface changes.
Versioning can appear in paths.
Versioning can appear in media types.
No single API versioning strategy is universally required.
Backward compatibility means existing valid clients continue to work according to their established contract.
Adding an optional response field is often easier to make backward compatible than removing an existing required field.
Changing the meaning of an existing field can break compatibility even when syntax remains unchanged.
An API version and an ABI version are different things.
A REST API is an API designed according to REST architectural constraints to some degree.
HTTP and REST are not synonyms.
An HTTP API does not automatically satisfy REST constraints.
RPC can operate over HTTP.
GraphQL can operate over HTTP.
File downloads can operate over HTTP.
Streaming can operate over HTTP.
WebSocket begins with an HTTP-based opening handshake but then uses the WebSocket protocol.
HTTP and WebSocket are different protocols after a successful protocol switch or extended connection establishment.
HTTP can carry browser traffic.
HTTP can carry public API traffic.
The same protocol does not imply the same trust level.
Internal HTTP requests should still have defined authentication and authorization when required.
Network location alone is not sufficient authorization for many systems.
localhost does not automatically mean trusted.
A reverse proxy can route requests.
A reverse proxy can apply limits.
A reverse proxy can cache responses.
A reverse proxy can modify headers.
An origin application should know which proxy headers it trusts.
A load balancer distributes requests among service instances.
Load balancing can improve availability and capacity.
Load balancing does not make an unhealthy application correct.
Session state can complicate load balancing.
Sticky sessions route a client repeatedly to the same backend according to load-balancer policy.
Shared session storage can permit requests to reach different backend instances.
Stateless application design can simplify horizontal scaling.
Stateless does not mean an application stores no persistent data.
Stateless HTTP application instances can use shared databases or external state services.
Useful request logs can include method.
Useful request logs can include path.
Useful request logs can include status.
Useful request logs can include duration.
Logs should avoid unnecessary credentials.
Authorization headers should not be logged casually.
Session cookies should not be logged casually.
Request bodies can contain secrets.
Response bodies can contain secrets.
Logging entire HTTP messages can create security and privacy risks.
A request identifier can correlate activity across components.
Correlation identifiers should not be mistaken for authentication credentials.
Tracing and logging are complementary observability mechanisms.
Metrics can measure request count.
Metrics can measure latency.
Metrics can measure error rates.
Metrics can measure active connections.
Observability should preserve meaningful failure categories.
Reporting every failed answer as 503 destroys diagnostic information.
Reporting every failure as 500 destroys diagnostic information.
Reporting every missing record as 404 can also be wrong when the endpoint represents a successful search collection.
The endpoint contract determines how application results map to HTTP.
An application reasoning layer should not invent HTTP transport failure.
Transport status and knowledge state are separate dimensions.
A request can succeed technically while producing no answer.
A retrieval subsystem can find no matching evidence while HTTP remains fully operational.
A reasoning subsystem can reject an unsupported conclusion while HTTP remains fully operational.
A storage subsystem can fail while the HTTP server remains alive.
A required storage failure can make a particular endpoint unavailable.
An optional storage failure can leave other endpoints healthy.
Failure scope matters.
An application should degrade according to the capability actually lost.
A module failure does not automatically mean total service failure.
A single unavailable module does not automatically justify HTTP 503 for unrelated capabilities.
A module execution failure should be distinguished from a retrieval miss.
A retrieval miss should be distinguished from insufficient evidence.
Insufficient evidence should be distinguished from contradictory evidence.
Contradictory evidence should be reported as contradiction rather than transport failure.
A service can truthfully say it does not know.
Not knowing is an information condition.
Being unavailable is an operational condition.
Those conditions should not be conflated.
HTTP clients should inspect status codes.
HTTP clients should not assume every response is successful.
A server returning HTTP 404 is reachable.
A server returning HTTP 500 is reachable.
Receiving an HTTP error response proves that an HTTP response was received.
A connection refusal occurs before an HTTP response exists.
A TLS handshake failure occurs before ordinary HTTP exchange completes.
A socket timeout can occur without any HTTP response.
Network-layer failures and HTTP-layer failures are different categories.
DNS resolves names to network information according to DNS.
TCP establishes reliable byte-stream connections for applicable HTTP versions.
TLS can establish encrypted authenticated transport.
HTTP then exchanges application messages over the established transport.
A DNS error should not be mislabeled as an HTTP status.
A TLS error should not be mislabeled as an origin HTTP response.
Layer boundaries matter for diagnosis.
HTTP/1.1 messages are textual in their framing syntax with message content represented as bytes.
HTTP/2 uses binary framing.
HTTP/3 uses HTTP semantics with QUIC transport and binary framing mechanisms.
HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 do not change the fundamental meaning of GET into something different.
Status semantics remain HTTP semantics across protocol versions unless specifications define otherwise.
HTTP clients and servers should follow standards rather than depend on accidental implementation behavior.
Standards compliance improves interoperability.
Interoperability requires both parties to agree on protocol behavior.
An application-specific protocol carried over HTTP should define its own message contract.
JSON over HTTP is a common application protocol pattern.
JSON-RPC can be transported over HTTP.
Using POST does not automatically make an operation unsafe.
Using GET does not make a badly designed destructive endpoint safe.
Application behavior should respect HTTP method semantics.
A response should use the most accurate applicable status.
A successful operation should not return 500 merely because the result was unexpected to a developer.
A failed operation should not return 200 merely to avoid handling errors.
Application-specific error codes can supplement HTTP status.
An application error code should have documented meaning.
An error message should explain the failure without leaking unnecessary implementation details.
Machine-readable errors should have stable fields.
Human-readable messages can provide context.
A machine should not have to parse prose to determine a stable error category when a structured error code can provide it.
Error contracts are part of API design.
Errors should be deterministic where the underlying condition is deterministic.
A server should distinguish invalid input from unavailable dependencies.
A server should distinguish unauthorized action from nonexistent resources according to security policy.
Precise error classification improves testing.
Precise error classification reduces false operational alarms.
HTTP status codes are evidence about request handling.
HTTP 503 has a specific operational meaning.
HTTP 404 has a specific resource-result meaning.
HTTP 400 has a specific request-failure meaning.
HTTP 403 has a specific authorization meaning.
HTTP 429 has a specific rate-limiting meaning.
Choosing a status code should follow the actual condition.
If no HTTP failure occurred, an internal knowledge response should not fabricate one.
If an answer cannot be established from available evidence, the response should say that the evidence is insufficient.
If no matching knowledge exists, the response should report the knowledge miss according to the application's contract.
If an internal service required for the operation is genuinely unavailable, service-unavailable reporting is appropriate.
If a module is active and successfully executes but finds no answer, the module did not become unavailable merely because its result was empty.
If a module crashes or cannot execute a required operation, that is an execution failure.
If module discovery fails, that is a discovery failure.
If module qualification fails, that is a qualification failure.
If routing cannot identify a handler, that is a routing failure.
If evidence exists but does not support a conclusion, that is insufficient evidence.
These failure and knowledge states should remain distinct.
Correct HTTP handling preserves those distinctions at the transport boundary.
JSON stands for JavaScript Object Notation.
JSON is a text-based data interchange format.
JSON is language-independent as a data interchange format.
JSON syntax was derived from a subset of JavaScript object literal syntax.
JSON and JavaScript are not the same thing.
A JavaScript object is not automatically JSON.
JSON can represent objects.
JSON can represent arrays.
JSON can represent strings.
JSON can represent false.
JSON can represent null.
Standard JSON does not represent undefined.
Standard JSON does not represent functions.
Standard JSON does not represent comments.
A JSON document contains one top-level JSON value.
The top-level value can be an object.
The top-level value can be an array.
The top-level value can be a string.
The top-level value can be a number.
The top-level value can be true.
The top-level value can be false.
The top-level value can be null.
A JSON object is enclosed in braces.
A JSON array is enclosed in brackets.
A JSON object contains name-value members.
Object member names are strings.
JSON object member names must use double quotation marks.
A colon separates an object member name from its value.
A comma separates members of an object.
A JSON array contains an ordered sequence of values.
A comma separates values in an array.
JSON does not permit a trailing comma after the final object member according to standard JSON syntax.
JSON does not permit a trailing comma after the final array element according to standard JSON syntax.
JSON strings use double quotation marks.
Single quotation marks do not delimit standard JSON strings.
The empty JSON object is {}.
The empty JSON array is [].
An empty object and an empty array are different values.
JSON whitespace can appear around structural characters where permitted.
JSON whitespace includes space.
JSON whitespace includes horizontal tab.
JSON whitespace includes carriage return.
Whitespace inside a JSON string is part of the string.
Whitespace outside strings generally does not change the represented data structure.
JSON strings support escape sequences.
\" represents a quotation mark inside a JSON string.
\\ represents a backslash inside a JSON string.
\/ can represent a slash inside a JSON string.
\b represents backspace.
\f represents form feed.
\r represents carriage return.
\t represents horizontal tab.
\u followed by four hexadecimal digits represents a UTF-16 code unit escape.
JSON text is defined for Unicode data.
JSON exchanged between systems is commonly encoded as UTF-8.
UTF-8 is the required encoding for JSON exchanged between systems that are not part of a closed ecosystem under current JSON interoperability specifications.
A JSON parser converts JSON text into an implementation's data representation.
A JSON serializer converts supported application data into JSON text.
Parsing and serialization are different operations.
Successful parsing proves that text satisfies the parser's accepted JSON syntax.
Successful parsing does not prove that the data satisfies application requirements.
Successful parsing does not prove that data is trustworthy.
Successful parsing does not prove that data is current.
Successful parsing does not prove that data is true.
Syntax validation and semantic validation are different.
A JSON object can be syntactically valid while containing semantically invalid values.
A JSON document can be syntactically valid while omitting fields required by an application.
A JSON document can be syntactically valid while containing values of the wrong application-defined type.
JSON numbers use decimal syntax defined by JSON.
JSON does not define separate integer and floating-point data types.
A JSON number is a JSON number regardless of whether its textual form contains a fractional component.
Numeric precision can be lost when a JSON number exceeds the exact numeric range of the receiving implementation.
JavaScript commonly represents parsed JSON numbers using the Number type.
JavaScript Number cannot exactly represent every possible integer.
Large integer identifiers should not automatically be transported as JSON numbers when exact representation cannot be guaranteed by every participant.
JSON null is a literal value.
JSON null and an absent object member are different states.
An application can assign different meanings to missing and null values.
A missing field can mean unspecified.
Those meanings must be defined by the application.
JSON Boolean values are lowercase true and false.
TRUE is not the standard JSON true literal.
FALSE is not the standard JSON false literal.
NULL is not the standard JSON null literal.
Object member names are case-sensitive strings.
"name" and "Name" are different member names.
Applications should define exact field names.
A producer and consumer must agree on field names.
A producer and consumer must agree on value meanings.
A producer and consumer must agree on required fields.
A producer and consumer must agree on optional fields.
A producer and consumer must agree on valid ranges.
A producer and consumer must agree on versioning behavior.
JSON provides syntax.
JSON does not by itself provide the application contract.
JSON Schema is a specification family for describing and validating JSON data.
JSON Schema and JSON are different things.
A JSON document does not require a JSON Schema merely to be valid JSON.
A JSON Schema can define expected value types.
A JSON Schema can constrain string values.
A JSON Schema can constrain numeric values.
A JSON Schema can constrain array contents.
A JSON Schema can constrain object properties.
Schema validation provides stronger structural validation than syntax parsing alone.
Schema validation does not establish authorization.
Schema validation does not establish provenance.
Schema validation does not establish currentness.
A value satisfying a schema can still contain malicious application data.
Validation should match the actual application contract.
A schema that rejects valid required evolution can create compatibility problems.
JSON can be used for configuration.
A JSON configuration file is data.
An inaccessible configuration file is an I/O failure.
Malformed JSON is a parsing failure.
A valid JSON configuration with invalid values is a validation failure.
A valid configuration referencing an unavailable service is an operational dependency failure.
These failures are different.
A JSON parser error is not automatically a network error.
A missing field is not automatically a parser error.
An unknown field is not automatically invalid unless the contract says it is invalid.
A configuration version mismatch is not automatically malformed JSON.
Layer boundaries matter.
JSON is commonly used by HTTP APIs.
application/json is the standard media type commonly used for JSON content.
An HTTP response containing JSON should use an appropriate Content-Type.
An HTTP request containing JSON should use an appropriate Content-Type.
HTTP and JSON are separate protocols and formats.
HTTP transports messages.
JSON can represent application data within those messages.
Valid JSON does not imply HTTP success.
HTTP 404 can contain a valid JSON error body.
HTTP 503 can contain a valid JSON error body.
A client should not discard HTTP status information merely because response-body parsing failed.
Attempting to parse an empty response body as JSON can fail.
An empty body and an empty JSON object are different.
An empty body and an empty JSON array are different.
{} is valid JSON.
[] is valid JSON.
An empty byte sequence is not a complete JSON value.
A JSON API should define whether an empty collection is represented by [].
A JSON API should define whether null is permitted.
A JSON API should define whether fields can be omitted.
Consistency makes clients easier to implement.
Browser JavaScript can serialize data using JSON.stringify.
Browser JavaScript can parse JSON using JSON.parse.
PHP can encode values as JSON using json_encode.
JavaScript JSON.stringify and PHP json_encode are implementation APIs rather than the JSON format itself.
JavaScript undefined has no direct JSON representation.
JavaScript functions have no direct JSON representation.
JavaScript BigInt has no ordinary direct JSON.stringify representation without custom handling.
Language-specific values may require deliberate conversion before JSON serialization.
Serialization can fail.
Serialization failure should be handled.
Parsing can fail.
Parsing failure should be handled.
A serializer should not silently invent meanings for unsupported application values.
PHP associative arrays can be encoded as JSON objects or arrays depending on their structure and encoding behavior.
PHP objects can be encoded according to PHP JSON encoding rules.
PHP json_decode can return objects or associative arrays depending on arguments.
The receiving PHP representation is not itself JSON after decoding.
A JavaScript object created by JSON.parse is a JavaScript value.
A PHP array created by json_decode is a PHP value.
A Go struct populated by JSON decoding is a Go value.
A C structure populated by a JSON library is a C value.
The source JSON representation and the in-memory representation are different layers.
Go provides encoding/json.
json.Marshal serializes supported Go values into JSON.
json.Unmarshal parses JSON into a supplied Go value.
Struct tags can control JSON field names and options.
A Go struct tag such as json:"name" can map a field to a JSON property named name.
A Go struct's memory layout is not JSON.
C does not provide a JSON parser in the ISO C standard library.
C++ does not provide one universal JSON representation as part of the language itself.
Programming-language JSON libraries can differ in edge-case behavior.
A protocol should define interoperability requirements independently of one library.
JSON objects are conceptually unordered collections of name-value pairs.
JSON arrays are ordered.
Array element order is semantically significant.
The arrays [1,2] and [2,1] contain the same values in different orders.
Those arrays are not equivalent when order matters.
Object member order should not be used as an implicit priority system without an explicit higher-level contract.
A JSON parser can preserve encountered member order as an implementation behavior.
Deterministic serialization requires deliberate canonicalization.
Two serializers can represent the same logical JSON data using different insignificant whitespace.
The texts {"a":1,"b":2} and { "a": 1, "b": 2 } represent equivalent object data.
Byte-for-byte comparison of ordinary JSON text is not the same as semantic comparison of parsed JSON values.
Whitespace differences change bytes without necessarily changing JSON meaning.
Object member ordering can change bytes without necessarily changing JSON meaning.
Escaping choices can change bytes while representing equivalent string values.
Cryptographic hashing operates on bytes.
Hashing two semantically equivalent but byte-different JSON documents produces different hashes under ordinary cryptographic hashing.
A system requiring stable hashes over JSON must define a canonical representation.
Canonicalization defines one deterministic representation for equivalent data according to a canonicalization specification.
Canonical number representation can be part of a canonical representation.
Canonical string escaping can be part of a canonical representation.
Canonical whitespace rules can be part of a canonical representation.
Consensus-sensitive systems should not hash arbitrary serializer output unless serializer behavior is part of the consensus specification.
Consensus-sensitive systems should not depend on map iteration order when constructing JSON.
Deterministic systems should explicitly define serialization.
Deterministic serialization should produce identical bytes for identical logical input under the defined protocol.
JSON alone does not guarantee deterministic byte serialization.
A verifier must reconstruct or receive the same signed representation.
Reformatting signed JSON can invalidate a byte-level signature.
Adding insignificant whitespace can invalidate a byte-level signature if raw JSON bytes are signed.
Reordering object members can invalidate a raw-byte signature.
A signature scheme should define the signed representation.
A cryptographic hash does not authenticate data by itself.
A digital signature can provide authenticity and integrity properties when correctly designed and verified.
JSON does not provide encryption.
JSON does not provide authentication.
JSON does not provide signatures.
Those capabilities must be added by surrounding protocols or application mechanisms.
JSON input is untrusted when it comes from an untrusted source.
A JSON field containing HTML can cause XSS if later inserted into HTML unsafely.
A JSON field containing SQL text can contribute to SQL injection if later concatenated into SQL.
A JSON field containing a filesystem path can contribute to path traversal if used without validation.
A JSON field containing a shell command fragment can contribute to command injection if used unsafely.
JSON encoding does not sanitize data for other contexts.
JSON escaping is not HTML escaping.
JSON escaping is not SQL parameterization.
JSON escaping is not shell escaping.
Each output context requires appropriate handling.
A value safely represented in JSON can still be dangerous when moved into another interpreter.
Trust should not be inherited merely because parsing succeeded.
Input validation should enforce type.
Input validation should enforce length.
Input validation should enforce range.
Input validation should enforce permitted values.
Input validation should enforce relationships between fields when required.
Input validation should reject impossible or prohibited states.
A string being valid UTF-8 does not mean the string is a valid username.
A number being syntactically valid does not mean it is within an application's permitted range.
An object containing a field does not mean the caller is permitted to set that field.
Authorization should determine which fields or operations a caller may control.
Mass assignment vulnerabilities can occur when applications blindly copy client-supplied object fields into privileged data models.
A server should explicitly control which client-provided fields can modify protected state.
A client-supplied role field should not grant administrative authority merely because the JSON parsed successfully.
A client-supplied user identifier should not override authenticated identity unless the application contract explicitly permits it.
Data and authority are different concepts.
JSON can describe a requested action.
JSON does not authorize the action.
JSON can contain an identity claim.
JSON does not prove the identity claim.
JSON can contain a timestamp.
JSON does not prove the timestamp is truthful.
JSON can contain a signature.
JSON does not prove the signature is valid.
Verification must be performed by the appropriate trusted mechanism.
JSON can represent version information.
A version field should have defined semantics.
A schema version and an application version are different concepts.
A protocol version and an ABI version are different concepts.
A JSON field named version is ambiguous unless the contract defines what is being versioned.
Historical versions can appear in JSON data without being current.
A list containing "1.3", "1.4", and "1.5" does not establish that all versions are active.
A history array describes history if the contract defines it as history.
A current_version field can explicitly identify current state when the contract defines it as authoritative.
Current state and historical state should be represented distinctly when that distinction matters.
A parser should not infer current state merely from the last textual value unless the data contract defines ordering and current-state semantics.
Semantic versions are structured identifiers.
Semantic version ordering is not ordinary lexicographic string ordering.
"1.10.0" can sort before "1.9.0" under naive string ordering even though semantic version precedence places 1.10.0 after 1.9.0.
A module manifest describes module metadata according to the host's contract.
A manifest can identify a module version.
A manifest can identify an interface version.
A manifest can identify capabilities.
A manifest can identify dependencies.
The exact fields depend on the module system.
A host can reject a syntactically valid manifest because required fields are absent.
A host can reject a syntactically valid manifest because a version is incompatible.
A host can reject a syntactically valid manifest because a capability is unsupported.
A host can reject a syntactically valid manifest because qualification fails.
Manifest parsing, manifest validation, compatibility checking, qualification, loading, and activation are different lifecycle stages.
An incompatible interface version is different from failed module qualification.
Failed module qualification is different from runtime module failure.
Runtime module failure is different from a knowledge miss.
Precise state distinctions improve diagnosis.
JSON can be used for persistent data.
Using JSON for persistence can be appropriate for small bounded datasets.
A JSON file can be simpler than a database when relational queries, concurrency, and large-scale indexing are unnecessary.
A database can be more appropriate when concurrent transactions, complex querying, indexing, or integrity relationships are required.
JSON and databases solve different problems.
A JSON file is not automatically a database.
Partial writes can corrupt a file.
Process termination during writing can leave incomplete data.
A common safe-update pattern writes a complete new file and then atomically replaces the previous file where the filesystem provides required rename semantics.
File locking can coordinate writers where supported and correctly implemented.
Secrets stored in JSON are still secrets.
A filename extension does not protect sensitive data.
A .json file can contain passwords, keys, tokens, or personal information.
Public web roots should not expose private configuration JSON.
Web server configuration should prevent unintended access to private files.
Source-control repositories should not contain production secrets merely because those secrets are stored in JSON.
A secret removed from the latest Git revision can remain in repository history.
Secret rotation can be required after accidental disclosure.
JSON configuration can refer to environment variables or external secret stores according to application design.
Configuration and secrets do not have to use the same storage mechanism.
JSON arrays can represent collections.
An empty array can represent a valid collection containing zero items.
Zero items is not an error by itself.
A retrieval operation returning [] can mean the operation succeeded and found no matching items.
A retrieval operation returning null can have a different defined meaning.
A retrieval operation failing to execute should be represented separately from an empty successful result.
A successful empty result and an unavailable service are different states.
A successful empty result and malformed data are different states.
Applications should preserve these distinctions.
A knowledge corpus can use JSON to represent facts.
A fact record can contain source information.
A fact record can contain content.
A fact record can contain timestamps.
The actual fields depend on the corpus contract.
Provenance can help determine authority.
Source identity and content are separate pieces of information.
A source can be authoritative for one question and irrelevant for another.
A JSON record describing old state should not override a current authoritative declaration merely because both parse successfully.
Retrieval relevance and evidentiary authority are different.
A search system can retrieve a JSON record because its text matches a query.
Textual match does not prove that the record answers the question.
Evidence selection should consider the actual meaning of fields.
A field named history should be treated as historical information.
A field named deprecated can identify data that should not be treated as current according to the contract.
A field named status can represent state when its permitted values are defined.
Field names alone should not be assigned undocumented semantics.
The schema or application contract defines meaning.
JSON Pointer defines syntax for identifying a specific value within a JSON document.
A JSON Pointer uses path-like tokens.
JSON Pointer and filesystem paths are different.
JSON Patch commonly uses the media type application/json-patch+json.
JSON Patch operations include add.
JSON Patch operations include remove.
JSON Patch operations include replace.
JSON Patch operations include move.
JSON Patch operations include copy.
JSON Patch operations include test.
A JSON Patch document is not an arbitrary JSON object.
Patch operations should be authorized.
The test operation can support conditional modification.
JSON Merge Patch provides another method for describing changes to JSON documents.
JSON Patch and JSON Merge Patch have different semantics.
An API should define which patch format it accepts.
JSON Lines is a convention where separate JSON values are written one per line.
JSON Lines is not one ordinary JSON array.
A complete JSON Lines file can contain multiple top-level JSON values separated by lines.
Standard JSON expects one top-level JSON value per JSON text.
JSON Lines can be useful for streaming or append-oriented records.
A consumer must know whether input is standard JSON or JSON Lines.
NDJSON is another name commonly used for newline-delimited JSON.
Streaming JSON requires framing.
TCP does not preserve application message boundaries.
Sending two JSON documents over TCP does not automatically tell the receiver where one ends and the next begins.
A protocol can use newline-delimited JSON when its rules permit it.
A protocol can use one JSON value per HTTP message.
Framing and serialization are different responsibilities.
JSON defines representation.
The transport defines how bytes move.
The application protocol defines what messages mean.
A protocol using JSON over TCP must define framing.
A protocol using JSON over HTTP can use HTTP message framing.
A parser should enforce reasonable resource limits for untrusted input.
Extremely deep nesting can consume parser resources.
Extremely large arrays can consume memory.
Extremely large objects can consume memory.
A valid JSON document can still be operationally dangerous because of its size or structure.
Maximum string lengths can be security and reliability controls.
Resource limits should be explicit.
Rejecting oversized JSON is different from reporting malformed JSON.
A valid but semantically prohibited JSON document can produce an application validation error.
A malformed JSON request can produce HTTP 400 when the request cannot be parsed according to the endpoint contract.
HTTP status selection should reflect the actual failure.
JSON parsing should produce deterministic interpretation for the same valid input under the selected parser contract.
Application validation should produce deterministic results for deterministic rules.
Configuration loading should not silently guess missing critical values when guessing could change system behavior.
Optional configuration should have documented defaults.
Defaults should not be invented differently on each run.
Strict configuration validation can reject unknown fields when that improves safety.
Strictness should match the purpose of the JSON document.
Configuration and network protocols can require different unknown-field policies.
Ignoring an unknown network field can support forward compatibility.
Ignoring an unknown security-critical configuration field can conceal an operator error.
JSON objects can contain duplicate member names in textual input.
Interoperable behavior with duplicate names is problematic because parsers can handle duplicates differently.
Some parsers preserve the first duplicate.
Some parsers preserve the last duplicate.
Some parsers report duplicates.
Some parsers expose all duplicates.
Security-sensitive JSON should not rely on ambiguous duplicate-name behavior.
A validator and application parser should agree about duplicate-name handling.
Parser differentials can create security vulnerabilities.
Canonical JSON representations should not contain ambiguous duplicate members.
JSON is simple by design.
Simple syntax does not eliminate the need for a precise data contract.
A large JSON document can still represent a simple structure.
File size does not determine authority.
The source and contract determine authority.
A README represented as JSON would still be documentation.
A runtime manifest represented as JSON would still be a runtime manifest.
A historical record represented as JSON would still be historical.
Serialization format does not determine evidentiary priority.
JSON should represent facts without changing their meaning.
A serializer should not transform historical information into current state.
An empty array means only what the contract defines it to mean.
null means only what the contract defines it to mean.
A field named evidence should not automatically imply that the evidence proves a conclusion.
Meaning comes from the governing application contract and verified state.
JSON is useful because it is small, readable, widely supported, and structurally explicit.
JSON should not be made more complicated than the data contract requires.
Explicit fields are preferable to undocumented positional assumptions when clarity matters.
Explicit version fields are preferable when consumers must distinguish incompatible formats.
Explicit status fields are preferable when multiple states must remain distinguishable.
Explicit error categories are preferable to forcing consumers to infer failure from prose.
Explicit nullability rules are preferable to accidental behavior.
Explicit limits are preferable to unbounded resource consumption.
Explicit serialization rules are preferable when deterministic bytes matter.
What that data means must be defined by the system using it.
Linux is an operating system kernel.
The Linux kernel manages hardware resources and provides operating-system services to user-space programs.
Ubuntu is a Linux distribution.
Debian is a Linux distribution.
Fedora is a Linux distribution.
Linux and Ubuntu are not synonyms.
Ubuntu uses the Linux kernel.
The kernel and user space are different execution environments.
Kernel space contains privileged kernel execution.
User space contains ordinary application processes.
Applications normally request kernel services through system calls.
A system call provides a controlled interface from user space to the kernel.
Programs should not assume that ordinary user-space code can directly access arbitrary kernel memory.
A process is an executing program instance.
Multiple processes can execute the same program.
Each process has a process identifier.
A process identifier is commonly abbreviated PID.
PID 1 has a special role as the initial user-space process within a Linux PID namespace.
On many modern Linux distributions systemd runs as PID 1.
A parent process can create child processes.
A process has a parent process identifier.
A parent process identifier is commonly abbreviated PPID.
Processes have credentials.
Process credentials include group identity information.
A user identifier is commonly abbreviated UID.
Linux groups are identified internally by numeric group identifiers.
A group identifier is commonly abbreviated GID.
User names provide human-readable mappings to UIDs.
Group names provide human-readable mappings to GIDs.
The root user conventionally has UID 0.
Running as root grants substantial authority.
Applications should not run as root merely because doing so avoids permission problems.
Least privilege means granting only the authority required for a task.
A dedicated service account can limit damage from compromise.
Linux files have ownership.
A file has an owning user.
A file has an owning group.
Traditional Unix file permissions contain read, write, and execute bits.
The read permission is represented by r.
The execute permission is represented by x.
For a regular file, read permission permits reading file contents according to applicable access checks.
For a regular file, execute permission permits execution when other requirements are satisfied.
Directory permissions have different practical meanings from regular-file permissions.
Read permission on a directory permits listing directory entries according to applicable access checks.
Write permission on a directory permits creating, removing, or renaming entries according to applicable access checks.
Execute permission on a directory permits traversing or searching the directory according to applicable access checks.
Writing an existing file and creating a new file involve different permission checks.
A process must be able to traverse parent directories to access a path.
chmod changes traditional file mode permissions.
chown changes file ownership.
chgrp changes group ownership.
chmod does not change the owning user.
chown does not inherently change every permission bit.
Numeric chmod modes encode permission bits.
Permission value 4 represents read.
Permission value 2 represents write.
Permission values are combined for owner, group, and other.
Mode 755 commonly means owner read-write-execute and group and other read-execute.
Mode 644 commonly means owner read-write and group and other read-only.
Mode 600 commonly means owner read-write with no group or other permissions.
Mode 700 commonly means owner read-write-execute with no group or other permissions.
Mode 777 grants read, write, and execute permissions broadly.
chmod 777 should not be used as a generic solution to permission problems.
World-writable files and directories increase security risk.
Permissions should reflect actual required access.
The setuid permission can cause an executable to run with the file owner's effective UID under applicable conditions.
On directories, setgid can cause new entries to inherit the directory's group.
The /tmp directory commonly uses the sticky bit.
umask influences permission bits removed from newly created files and directories.
umask is not itself the final file permission.
The creating program's requested mode and the umask together influence resulting traditional permissions.
Access Control Lists can provide permissions beyond traditional owner-group-other mode bits.
Linux ACLs are commonly manipulated with getfacl and setfacl.
Linux capabilities can grant selected privileged kernel operations without granting full root authority.
Capabilities divide some traditional root privileges into smaller units.
Granting a process one required capability can be safer than running the entire process as root.
Capabilities should still be treated as privileged authority.
Linux uses a hierarchical filesystem namespace.
The root of the filesystem namespace is /.
Absolute paths begin with /.
Relative paths are interpreted relative to a process's current working directory.
The current directory is represented by .
Path normalization matters for security.
Untrusted paths can contain traversal components.
The Filesystem Hierarchy Standard describes common directory purposes on Linux systems.
Not every Linux system follows every hierarchy convention identically.
/var commonly contains variable system data.
/var/log commonly contains logs.
/var/lib commonly contains persistent application state.
/var/cache commonly contains application cache data.
/run commonly contains runtime state.
/usr commonly contains installed user-space programs and data.
/usr/local commonly contains locally installed software outside distribution package management conventions.
/opt commonly contains optional or self-contained application software.
/home commonly contains user home directories.
/root is commonly the root user's home directory.
/dev exposes device nodes and related interfaces.
/sys exposes kernel device and subsystem information through sysfs.
/boot commonly contains boot-related files.
Application-specific installation layouts can deliberately use /opt.
Persistent application data should not be confused with temporary runtime data.
Configuration and mutable runtime data should be distinguished when practical.
Logs and configuration serve different purposes.
Executable binaries and writable application state should be separated when practical.
A regular file stores byte data.
A directory maps names to filesystem objects.
A hard link creates another directory entry referring to the same inode where supported.
A symbolic link and a hard link are different mechanisms.
A symbolic link can become dangling when its target no longer exists.
Following symbolic links can have security implications.
A filename is stored in a directory entry rather than being the inode itself.
Different filesystems implement details differently.
A filesystem can be mounted into the filesystem namespace.
umount detaches a mounted filesystem when conditions permit.
A mount point is a directory where another filesystem becomes accessible.
Different mount points can reside on different storage devices or filesystem types.
Atomic file replacement is useful for durable configuration updates.
A common pattern writes complete new content to a temporary file and renames it over the target.
Durability can require additional synchronization beyond rename.
A successful write system call does not necessarily mean data has reached persistent physical storage.
Buffering and caching can delay physical storage.
Durability requirements should be explicit.
A file descriptor is a process-local integer referring to an open resource.
Standard input conventionally uses file descriptor 0.
Standard output conventionally uses file descriptor 1.
Standard error conventionally uses file descriptor 2.
Standard input is commonly abbreviated stdin.
Standard output is commonly abbreviated stdout.
Standard error is commonly abbreviated stderr.
File descriptors can refer to pipes.
File descriptors can refer to devices.
File descriptors are finite process resources.
A process that leaks file descriptors can eventually fail to open additional resources.
Opening a file and closing a file are distinct lifecycle operations.
close releases a file descriptor reference.
Programs should not assume a descriptor number permanently identifies the same resource.
Shell redirection changes where standard streams are connected.
The > operator commonly redirects standard output to a file and truncates or creates it according to shell semantics.
The < operator commonly redirects standard input.
2> commonly redirects standard error in shells such as Bash.
2>&1 commonly redirects standard error to the current destination of standard output according to shell evaluation order.
A pipe connects the output of one process to the input of another.
The | shell operator commonly creates a pipeline.
A pipeline allows programs to compose through streams.
Pipes carry bytes.
A pipe is not a persistent file.
A pipe has finite buffering.
A writer can block when a pipe buffer is full and no reader drains it.
A reader can observe end-of-file when all writing endpoints are closed.
Named pipes are filesystem-visible FIFO objects.
Anonymous pipes are commonly created between related processes.
Linux IPC mechanisms include pipes.
Linux IPC mechanisms include Unix domain sockets.
Linux IPC mechanisms include network sockets.
Linux IPC mechanisms include shared memory.
Linux IPC mechanisms can include message queues.
A socket is an endpoint for communication.
An Internet socket can use IPv4.
A Unix domain socket provides local IPC through the Unix socket API.
Unix domain sockets can be represented by filesystem paths or abstract namespace addresses on Linux.
A Unix domain socket is not a TCP socket.
UDP provides datagram communication.
TCP does not preserve application message boundaries.
An application protocol over TCP must define framing.
UDP preserves datagram boundaries.
UDP does not provide TCP's reliable ordered delivery semantics.
localhost commonly refers to the local host.
127.0.0.1 is the IPv4 loopback address commonly used for localhost.
::1 is the IPv6 loopback address.
Binding a service to 127.0.0.1 restricts ordinary IPv4 network access to the local host.
Listening on all interfaces exposes a service more broadly than listening on loopback.
Network binding should match intended exposure.
A port identifies a transport endpoint in combination with address and protocol.
TCP port numbers and UDP port numbers are separate namespaces.
A process must bind a listening socket before accepting applicable incoming connections.
A successful TCP connection does not prove application qualification.
DNS resolves names according to DNS configuration.
A hostname and an IP address are different identifiers.
One address can host multiple services.
DNS resolution can fail independently of TCP connection behavior.
Network diagnosis should preserve failure layers.
A DNS failure is not a TCP refusal.
A TCP refusal is not an application protocol rejection.
An application timeout is not automatically a DNS failure.
Processes have an environment.
Environment variables can configure applications.
Environment variables can contain secrets.
Environment variables are not inherently secret storage.
Secrets should be handled according to the actual threat model.
PATH is an environment variable containing directories searched for executable commands.
Executing an unqualified command can run the first matching executable found through PATH.
Privileged scripts should use controlled command resolution.
A malicious PATH can cause an unintended executable to run.
The current working directory is not universally or automatically part of PATH.
HOME commonly identifies a user's home directory.
USER commonly contains a user name in many environments but should not be treated as authoritative security identity.
Process credentials from the operating system are authoritative for kernel permission checks.
export marks a shell variable for inclusion in the environment of subsequently executed commands.
A shell interprets command syntax.
sh commonly identifies a POSIX-style shell interface but the implementation can vary.
Commands such as cd can be shell built-ins.
Shell syntax is not C syntax.
Shell syntax is not PHP syntax.
Shell syntax is not Linux kernel syntax.
Quoting matters in shell commands.
Double quotes permit selected expansions.
Shell scripts should quote variable expansions when literal values are intended.
Untrusted strings should not be concatenated into shell commands.
Shell injection occurs when untrusted data changes intended command syntax.
A shell should not be invoked merely to execute a program when shell syntax is unnecessary.
Command exit status communicates success or failure according to program convention.
An exit status of zero conventionally indicates success.
The exact meaning of nonzero statuses is program-specific.
A nonzero exit status does not universally mean the same error.
The shell variable $? commonly contains the most recent foreground pipeline or command status according to shell rules.
Exit status should be checked when command success matters.
A process can terminate normally.
A process can terminate because of a signal.
A process can terminate because of an unrecoverable error.
The shell can encode signal termination into reported status according to shell conventions.
Signals provide asynchronous notifications to processes.
SIGTERM requests process termination.
SIGKILL forces process termination by the kernel.
SIGSTOP stops process execution.
SIGCONT resumes a stopped process.
SIGHUP historically indicates terminal hangup and is also used by many daemons to request configuration reload.
SIGCHLD notifies a parent about child process state changes.
SIGSEGV indicates an invalid memory access condition detected by the system.
SIGABRT commonly indicates process abort.
SIGTERM can be handled by a process.
SIGINT can be handled by a process.
SIGKILL cannot be caught, blocked, or ignored.
SIGSTOP cannot be caught, blocked, or ignored.
A graceful shutdown commonly begins with SIGTERM rather than SIGKILL.
SIGKILL prevents an application from performing ordinary shutdown cleanup.
SIGKILL should not be the first choice merely because termination is desired.
systemd manages units.
A service unit describes a managed service.
Service unit files commonly use the .service suffix.
systemctl start starts a unit.
systemctl stop stops a unit.
systemctl restart stops and starts a unit.
systemctl reload requests a supported configuration reload.
systemctl status displays unit state information.
systemctl enable configures a unit to start according to its installation relationships.
systemctl disable removes corresponding automatic-start enablement links according to unit configuration.
A service can be running without being enabled.
A service can be enabled but currently stopped.
systemctl daemon-reload causes systemd to reload unit definitions.
daemon-reload does not necessarily restart a running service.
Service restart behavior should match the component whose configuration changed.
A systemd service can run under a configured Group.
WorkingDirectory defines a service working directory when configured.
ExecStart defines a command used to start a service.
EnvironmentFile can load environment assignments according to systemd syntax.
Restart controls automatic restart policy.
RestartSec controls delay before applicable restart attempts.
Automatic restart can improve resilience.
An unbounded rapid restart loop can consume resources and flood logs.
Restart policy should distinguish expected termination from failure when appropriate.
systemd can impose resource and security restrictions.
ProtectHome can restrict home-directory access.
PrivateTmp can provide a private temporary namespace.
CapabilityBoundingSet can restrict capabilities.
SystemCallFilter can restrict system calls according to configuration.
Service hardening should preserve functionality actually required by the service.
A service failing under hardening does not automatically mean the hardening feature is defective.
Required access should be identified explicitly.
systemd-journald collects journal records on many distributions.
journalctl queries the systemd journal.
journalctl -u can select records for a unit.
journalctl -f follows new records.
Application-specific logs can be stored in files.
The journal and application log files can contain different information.
A missing log record does not prove an event did not occur unless logging guarantees establish that conclusion.
Log timestamps should have defined timezone interpretation.
UTC is useful for correlating events across systems.
Log levels commonly include debug.
Log levels commonly include informational.
Log levels commonly include warning.
Log levels commonly include error.
Log levels can include critical or fatal states.
Logging severity should reflect actual operational severity.
A module informational message should not automatically be an error.
Degraded state and failed state are different conditions.
A service can remain operational with reduced capability.
An operational alert should describe the capability that failed.
Passwords should not be logged.
Private keys should not be logged.
Authentication tokens should not be logged.
Session identifiers should not be logged casually.
Logs require access controls.
Logs can consume disk space.
Log rotation limits uncontrolled log growth.
logrotate is commonly used to rotate file-based logs.
Rotation can rename old logs.
Rotation can compress old logs.
Rotation can remove logs according to retention policy.
Applications must handle rotation correctly if they keep log files open.
df reports filesystem space usage.
df and du answer different questions.
A filesystem can run out of free blocks.
A filesystem can run out of free inodes.
Available disk capacity is a finite resource.
CPU time is a finite resource.
File descriptors are finite resources.
Process counts can be limited.
Network sockets consume resources.
Threads consume resources.
Applications should place boundaries on resource consumption.
A process can contain multiple threads.
Threads within a process share an address space.
Threads and processes are different execution abstractions.
A race condition occurs when behavior depends incorrectly on timing or ordering between concurrent operations.
A mutex can protect shared state.
A semaphore can coordinate access or counts.
Synchronization should match the data and concurrency model.
Adding threads does not automatically improve performance.
Concurrency concerns multiple activities making progress over overlapping time.
Parallelism concerns simultaneous execution.
Multiple CPU cores can execute tasks in parallel.
Linux uses virtual memory.
Each process operates in a virtual address space.
Processes do not ordinarily access each other's private memory directly.
A segmentation fault commonly results when a process performs an invalid memory access.
A segmentation fault is not an HTTP status.
A segmentation fault is not a JSON parsing error.
A segmentation fault is not a knowledge miss.
Memory can be anonymous.
Memory can be file-backed.
Memory can be shared.
mmap maps memory into a process address space.
Memory mapping can map files.
Memory mapping can create shared-memory regions.
Virtual memory can exceed immediately available physical RAM through paging and other mechanisms.
Swap provides disk-backed storage for certain memory pages according to kernel policy.
Swap is much slower than RAM for ordinary access.
The kernel can invoke the Out-Of-Memory killer under severe memory pressure.
The OOM killer can terminate processes to recover memory.
A process disappearing because of OOM is different from a clean application exit.
dmesg displays kernel ring-buffer messages subject to permissions and system configuration.
Kernel messages can describe hardware and driver events.
Kernel messages can describe OOM activity.
Kernel messages can describe filesystem errors.
Application logs and kernel logs should both be examined when the failure layer is uncertain.
The /proc filesystem exposes process and kernel state.
Each process commonly has a directory under /proc named by PID.
/proc/PID/cmdline exposes process command-line data according to permissions.
/proc/PID/fd exposes file descriptor links according to permissions.
/proc/meminfo exposes memory statistics.
/proc/cpuinfo exposes CPU information.
/proc is virtual rather than an ordinary persistent disk filesystem.
Values in /proc represent live kernel state.
The /sys filesystem exposes kernel objects and device information.
/sys is virtual.
Linux devices can appear under /dev.
/dev/null discards written data and returns end-of-file when read.
/dev/zero produces zero bytes when read.
/dev/random and /dev/urandom expose kernel random interfaces according to Linux semantics.
Cryptographic randomness should use appropriate operating-system or cryptographic APIs.
Linux supports shared libraries.
Shared libraries allow executable code to be loaded and used by multiple programs.
Shared library files commonly use the .so naming convention.
.so commonly means shared object.
A shared library and a loadable application module can use similar mechanisms but are not necessarily the same architectural concept.
The dynamic linker loads required shared libraries.
ldd can display shared-library dependencies for many dynamically linked executables.
ldd should be used cautiously with untrusted executables because implementation and platform behavior can involve security concerns.
ABI compatibility matters for native shared libraries.
ABI stands for Application Binary Interface.
An ABI defines binary-level compatibility requirements.
An ABI can define calling conventions.
An ABI can define data structure layouts.
An ABI can define symbol expectations.
An ABI can define type sizes.
An ABI can define alignment.
An ABI can define binary interface version requirements.
API and ABI are different.
Source compatibility and binary compatibility are different.
A source-compatible change can still break an ABI.
Changing an exported function signature can break ABI compatibility.
A module host should validate the ABI version required by its module contract.
The current ABI should come from the authoritative interface definition used by the running software.
A README can document ABI history.
A header can define the current compile-time ABI contract.
A manifest can declare module ABI compatibility according to architecture.
Discovery, manifest validation, qualification, loading, and activation are separate stages.
A valid shared object does not mean the module is qualified.
A qualified module does not mean every operation it performs will succeed.
A failed optional module should not automatically terminate an otherwise healthy core unless architecture requires it.
Dynamic loading on Linux commonly uses dlopen.
dlsym resolves symbols from a dynamically loaded object.
dlerror reports dynamic loader errors.
Linux executable files can use the ELF format.
ELF stands for Executable and Linkable Format.
ELF is commonly used for executables, shared libraries, and object files on Linux.
A valid ELF file can still be the wrong architecture.
A valid ELF file can still depend on unavailable libraries.
A valid ELF file can still expose the wrong symbols.
A valid ELF file can still implement an incompatible ABI.
File-format validity and module validity are different.
Linux supports multiple CPU architectures.
Common Linux architectures include x86-64.
Common Linux architectures include AArch64.
Platform-independent source does not mean one binary runs natively on every CPU architecture.
Endianness can differ between architectures.
Data type sizes can differ between ABIs.
Binary protocols should define field sizes explicitly.
Binary protocols should define byte order explicitly.
Binary protocols should not transmit raw native structures unless the protocol deliberately specifies and controls the representation.
Network byte order is conventionally big-endian.
A platform-independent protocol should produce identical protocol meaning across supported platforms.
Consensus-visible results should not depend accidentally on host byte order.
Consensus-visible results should not depend accidentally on structure padding.
Consensus-visible results should not depend accidentally on locale.
Consensus-visible results should not depend accidentally on filesystem directory enumeration order.
Directory enumeration order should not be treated as deterministic unless explicitly sorted.
Filesystem metadata timestamps can vary.
Wall-clock time can change.
CLOCK_REALTIME represents wall-clock time.
Elapsed durations should generally use a monotonic clock.
Wall-clock timestamps should use a defined time representation.
NTP can adjust system wall-clock time.
A wall clock can move forward or backward.
Timeout calculations based solely on wall-clock assumptions can be incorrect.
Time zones affect human-readable local time.
Locale affects text formatting.
Locale can affect sorting.
Locale can affect numeric formatting.
Machine protocols should not depend accidentally on user locale.
Linux process execution commonly uses exec-family system interfaces.
exec replaces the current process image with another program when successful.
fork creates a child process on systems supporting fork semantics.
After fork, parent and child execute independently from the fork point with separate process identities.
fork and exec are often combined to launch another program.
system() invokes a shell command according to the C library contract.
system() introduces shell interpretation.
A child process should have defined standard streams and inherited descriptors.
File descriptors can be marked close-on-exec.
Descriptor inheritance can accidentally expose resources to child processes.
Privilege boundaries should account for inherited descriptors.
Processes can change user or group credentials when permitted.
Dropping privileges after privileged initialization can reduce attack surface.
A process should not assume that changing only one credential field removes all privileged authority.
Capabilities and supplementary groups can remain relevant.
Linux namespaces isolate selected system resources.
PID namespaces isolate process identifier views.
Mount namespaces isolate filesystem mount views.
Network namespaces isolate network stacks.
User namespaces isolate user and group identifier mappings.
IPC namespaces isolate certain IPC resources.
UTS namespaces isolate hostname and domain-name views.
Containers use Linux isolation mechanisms including namespaces and cgroups.
A container is not a virtual machine merely because it provides an isolated environment.
Containers share a host kernel unless another virtualization layer is involved.
cgroups control and account for resource usage.
cgroups can limit CPU allocation.
cgroups can organize processes.
systemd uses cgroups to manage services on many systems.
Filesystem paths inside a container can differ from host paths.
Package managers install and maintain distribution software.
apt updates package metadata and installs or removes packages through configured repositories.
apt update refreshes package index information.
apt upgrade upgrades installed packages according to dependency and policy rules.
Installing packages changes system state.
Package installation should be deliberate on production systems.
Distribution packages can receive security updates.
Locally compiled software can fall outside normal package-manager update tracking.
An application under /opt can require its own update lifecycle.
Updating a shared library can affect applications depending on its ABI.
ABI-breaking updates can require rebuilding dependent software.
Backups protect persistent data.
A backup does not need to include reproducible temporary files unless recovery requirements say otherwise.
Configuration can be part of backup requirements.
Private keys can be critical recovery material.
Private-key backups require strong protection.
A backup is not proven useful until restoration has been tested.
Replication is not the same as backup.
Linux security includes discretionary access control.
Traditional Unix permissions are discretionary access control.
Linux can also use mandatory access control frameworks.
A process can have traditional file permissions yet still be denied by a mandatory access-control policy.
A permission-denied error should not automatically be solved with chmod.
Diagnosis should determine which access-control layer denied the operation.
File ownership can be correct while AppArmor denies access.
Traditional mode bits can deny access before another policy is relevant.
systemd sandboxing can deny access.
Filesystem mount options can deny execution or writes.
Read-only mounts prevent writes regardless of ordinary writable mode bits.
noexec mount options restrict direct execution according to mount semantics.
nosuid affects setuid and setgid behavior.
Access failures should be investigated at the actual enforcement layer.
A web server commonly runs under a restricted service account.
Apache HTTP Server commonly uses a dedicated account such as www-data on Debian-derived systems.
The exact account depends on configuration and distribution.
A PHP script cannot write a file merely because the interactive shell user can write it.
The PHP process must have filesystem authority to perform the write.
Giving the PHP process write access to an entire application tree is usually unnecessary.
Writable directories should be limited to locations that actually require runtime modification.
Executable application code should ordinarily not need to be writable by the web service account.
Configuration files containing secrets should have restrictive permissions.
Web server and filesystem permissions together define important security boundaries.
A process listening on a privileged port below 1024 traditionally requires appropriate privilege or capability.
CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE can permit binding low-numbered ports without full root authority.
A reverse proxy can listen on public ports while an application listens on an unprivileged local port.
This can reduce application privilege requirements.
Firewall rules control network traffic.
iptables commands can operate through compatibility layers on modern systems.
A firewall and application authorization solve different problems.
A firewall can restrict which network traffic reaches a service.
A firewall does not validate application-level permissions inside accepted requests.
A firewall rule permitting a port does not prove a service is listening.
Network troubleshooting should check each layer.
ss displays socket information.
curl can make application-protocol requests including HTTP.
curl successfully establishing HTTP communication provides stronger HTTP-layer evidence than ping.
ps displays process information.
top displays dynamic process and resource information.
The kill command sends SIGTERM by default in common implementations when no signal is specified.
grep searches input for matching patterns.
tail displays the end of input or files.
tail -f follows appended file content.
head displays the beginning of input.
cat concatenates and displays file contents.
less provides interactive file viewing.
find searches filesystem trees according to predicates.
grep and find solve different search problems.
grep searches content.
find searches filesystem entries.
wc can count lines, words, and bytes according to options.
wc -l counts line delimiters according to its semantics.
A line count does not necessarily equal a logical record count unless the file format defines one record per line.
awk processes structured text records.
sed performs stream-oriented text transformations.
cut extracts fields or character ranges according to its options.
sort sorts text lines.
uniq identifies or removes adjacent duplicate lines according to options.
sort followed by uniq is a common pattern when global duplicate grouping is intended.
A successful grep match proves matching text was present in the searched input.
A grep match in a README does not override a current interface declaration.
A grep match in a historical log establishes that text was logged at that time.
Historical logs describe historical events.
Live configuration describes current configuration.
Source headers can define compile-time contracts.
Runtime state can differ from source if deployed binaries were built from different source.
Determining current runtime behavior can require examining the running artifact and its configuration.
Evidence should be classified according to what it actually establishes.
The newest file is not automatically the most authoritative file.
The largest version number found in text is not automatically the active version.
A README mentioning an ABI history does not automatically define the current ABI.
An authoritative interface header can define the compile-time ABI when the architecture assigns it that role.
A running module loader can provide runtime evidence about the ABI it accepts.
Source, build artifact, configuration, and runtime state are different layers.
It does not prove unrelated system properties.
A web server's service account can be tested explicitly when diagnosing write permissions.
sudo -u can execute a command under another user's identity when authorized.
Testing access as the actual service user provides stronger evidence than testing as an administrative shell user.
sudo grants controlled privilege according to configuration.
sudo is not part of the Linux kernel.
sudo policy is commonly configured through sudoers.
Administrative access should be used deliberately.
Commands executed with sudo can modify protected system state.
Copying commands without understanding their scope can damage a system.
Configuration should be backed up before risky modification when recovery requires it.
A service should be tested after configuration changes.
A successful syntax check does not prove runtime behavior is correct.
A successful service start does not prove every feature works.
A health test should exercise the capability it claims to validate.
RUNNING means a process is running.
READY means a service can perform its required operation according to readiness criteria.
DEGRADED means some capability is impaired while the system remains operational according to defined state semantics.
A single warning should not automatically make a system failed.
A single optional module failure can justify degraded state when that capability matters but the core remains functional.
System architecture defines how those mechanisms are used.
Correct Linux engineering depends on explicit ownership, permissions, lifecycle, boundaries, failure handling, and observable state.
A small service can be more reliable than an unnecessarily complicated service.
A deterministic service should define configuration.
A deterministic service should define startup behavior.
A deterministic service should define failure behavior.
A deterministic service should define persistent state.
A deterministic service should define resource limits.
A computer network allows systems to exchange data.
Network communication occurs through defined protocols.
A protocol defines rules for communication between participants.
A failure at one network layer should not automatically be reported as a failure at another layer.
Physical connectivity, IP routing, transport connectivity, name resolution, TLS, and application protocols are distinct concerns.
The Internet Protocol is commonly abbreviated IP.
IP provides packet delivery between network interfaces across interconnected networks.
IPv4 is version 4 of the Internet Protocol.
IPv6 is version 6 of the Internet Protocol.
IPv4 and IPv6 use different address formats.
An IPv4 address contains 32 bits.
An IPv6 address contains 128 bits.
An IPv4 address is commonly written as four decimal octets.
192.0.2.1 is an example IPv4 address.
An IPv6 address is commonly written as hexadecimal groups separated by colons.
2001:db8::1 is an example IPv6 address reserved for documentation.
An IP address identifies an interface or addressing endpoint according to network configuration.
An IP address does not identify an application by itself.
Multiple applications can communicate through the same IP address using different transport endpoints.
A host can have multiple IP addresses.
A hostname can resolve to multiple IP addresses.
Multiple hostnames can resolve to the same IP address.
IPv4 address 127.0.0.1 is part of the loopback range.
127.0.0.1 commonly refers to the local IPv4 host.
IPv6 address ::1 is the IPv6 loopback address.
Loopback traffic does not ordinarily leave the local host.
A server binding to 0.0.0.0 commonly listens on all available IPv4 interfaces.
The unspecified IPv6 address is ::.
Binding and connecting are different operations.
A server binds a local endpoint.
A client connects to a remote endpoint.
A socket is an operating-system abstraction for communication.
A network socket can be associated with a protocol family.
A network socket can be associated with a transport protocol.
A socket endpoint can include an IP address and port.
A TCP connection is identified by endpoint information for both participants.
Port numbers range from 0 through 65535.
A port number does not identify a protocol by itself.
TCP port 80 and UDP port 80 are different transport endpoints.
Port assignments are conventions and configuration.
An application can use a nonstandard port when both sides agree.
A service listening on a port does not prove it implements the expected application protocol.
A TCP listener on port 443 does not by itself prove valid HTTPS behavior.
Protocol qualification requires protocol-level evidence.
TCP stands for Transmission Control Protocol.
TCP is connection-oriented.
TCP provides a reliable ordered byte stream between endpoints while the connection remains operational.
TCP detects loss and performs retransmission according to its protocol.
TCP does not preserve application message boundaries.
One send operation does not necessarily correspond to one receive operation.
A receiver can receive part of one sender write.
Applications using TCP must define message framing.
Message framing identifies where one application message ends and another begins.
Delimiter framing separates messages using a defined delimiter.
Fixed-size framing uses messages of a predetermined size.
Protocol-specific framing can combine multiple techniques.
A newline can be used as a delimiter only when the protocol defines how newlines inside content are handled.
JSON alone does not define framing for multiple JSON documents over a raw TCP stream.
HTTP defines its own message framing.
A custom TCP protocol must define its own framing.
Failure to handle partial reads is a common TCP programming error.
A successful write call can report fewer bytes written than requested depending on API and conditions.
A correct application handles the number of bytes actually transferred.
TCP connection establishment uses a handshake.
The TCP handshake establishes transport connectivity.
Successful TCP connection establishment does not prove application compatibility.
Successful TCP connection establishment does not prove authentication.
Successful TCP connection establishment does not prove protocol qualification.
Transport connectivity and application readiness are different states.
TCP connections have state.
A connection can be established.
A connection can be closing.
A connection can be closed.
A connection can be reset.
Applications should define how connection closure affects their protocol.
TCP keepalive can detect some dead peer conditions over long-lived connections.
TCP keepalive is not the same as an application heartbeat.
An application heartbeat can test application-level responsiveness.
A TCP keepalive tests transport-level connection conditions according to operating-system behavior.
A successful application heartbeat provides stronger evidence of application responsiveness than mere socket existence.
A connection can remain established while an application is deadlocked or otherwise unable to perform useful work.
Application health should be tested at the required layer.
UDP stands for User Datagram Protocol.
UDP is connectionless at the protocol level.
UDP sends datagrams.
UDP preserves datagram boundaries.
UDP does not guarantee delivery.
UDP does not guarantee ordering.
UDP does not guarantee duplicate suppression.
UDP does not provide TCP-style retransmission.
A UDP send succeeding does not prove the remote application received the datagram.
A UDP application can receive duplicate messages.
A UDP application can receive messages out of order.
UDP is appropriate when its semantics match application requirements.
TCP is appropriate when reliable ordered byte-stream semantics match application requirements.
Neither TCP nor UDP is universally superior.
Protocol choice should follow requirements.
IP packets can be duplicated.
IP packets can arrive out of order.
Transport protocols handle these conditions according to their semantics.
Networks are not inherently reliable.
Applications should expect communication failure.
Timeouts bound how long an operation waits.
A read timeout limits waiting for incoming data.
An application timeout can limit an entire operation.
Different timeout types should remain distinguishable.
A timeout is not proof that the remote system performed no work.
A request can reach a server and complete even when the client times out before receiving the response.
Retrying after an ambiguous timeout can duplicate an operation.
State-changing operations require deliberate retry design.
Idempotency can make retries safer.
A retry should be bounded.
Infinite immediate retry loops can create failure storms.
Retry delays can reduce pressure on a failing dependency.
Exponential backoff increases delay after repeated failures.
Jitter adds variation to retry timing.
Jitter can reduce synchronized reconnect storms.
Many clients reconnecting simultaneously can overload a recovering server.
A reconnect loop should have bounded and controlled timing.
Repeated disconnect and reconnect behavior can indicate transport instability.
Repeated disconnect and reconnect behavior can indicate application timeout configuration.
Repeated disconnect and reconnect behavior can indicate server overload.
Repeated disconnect and reconnect behavior can indicate an intermediary closing connections.
Repeated disconnect and reconnect behavior can indicate client logic incorrectly treating normal conditions as failure.
The reconnect pattern alone does not prove the cause.
Connection refused is different from connection timeout.
A connection timeout indicates that connection establishment did not complete within the configured time.
A timeout does not prove that the host is offline.
A firewall can silently drop packets.
Routing problems can prevent packets from reaching the destination.
An accepted connection indicates a listener accepted transport establishment.
Neither condition alone proves application correctness.
DNS stands for Domain Name System.
DNS maps names to data including network addresses.
DNS is a distributed naming system.
A DNS lookup can succeed while the resulting service is unavailable.
A DNS lookup can fail while a service remains reachable directly by IP address.
A DNS record can exist while no application listens at the resulting address.
DNS data has caching behavior.
DNS records have TTL values.
TTL stands for Time To Live.
A DNS TTL influences how long applicable DNS data can be cached.
Cached old values can remain until expiration according to resolver behavior.
An A record maps a name to an IPv4 address.
An AAAA record maps a name to an IPv6 address.
An MX record identifies mail exchange information.
A TXT record stores text data used by many higher-level mechanisms.
An NS record identifies authoritative name servers for a DNS zone.
A PTR record supports reverse DNS mappings.
DNS record types have different purposes.
A hostname used by an application should resolve according to the application's network requirements.
IPv4 and IPv6 connectivity can differ.
Successful IPv4 connectivity does not prove successful IPv6 connectivity.
Successful IPv6 connectivity does not prove successful IPv4 connectivity.
Routing determines where IP packets are sent.
A routing table contains route information.
A route can identify a destination network.
A route can identify a gateway.
A route can identify an outgoing interface.
A default IPv4 route is commonly represented as 0.0.0.0/0.
A default IPv6 route is commonly represented as ::/0.
Longest-prefix matching is used to select more specific IP routes.
A gateway forwards packets between networks.
A router operates primarily at the network layer.
A missing route can prevent communication.
A wrong route can send traffic toward the wrong network.
Routing loops can prevent successful delivery.
traceroute can provide evidence about the path packets take where network devices permit the required responses.
A traceroute failure does not necessarily mean the final destination is unreachable.
Network devices can filter traceroute-related traffic.
ICMP communicates network control and diagnostic information.
Ping success proves some IP and ICMP communication occurred.
Ping success does not prove TCP connectivity.
Ping failure does not prove a host is down.
A host can serve TCP applications while refusing ICMP echo requests.
Network diagnosis should test the layer relevant to the problem.
A subnet groups addresses according to a network prefix.
CIDR stands for Classless Inter-Domain Routing.
CIDR notation combines an address with prefix length.
192.0.2.0/24 represents an IPv4 prefix with 24 network-prefix bits.
2001:db8::/32 represents an IPv6 prefix with 32 network-prefix bits.
A subnet mask describes network-prefix boundaries in IPv4 representations.
Hosts determine whether a destination is on-link according to routing and prefix configuration.
Private IPv4 address ranges include 10.0.0.0/8.
Private IPv4 address ranges include 172.16.0.0/12.
Private IPv4 address ranges include 192.168.0.0/16.
Private does not mean secure.
A private network can contain hostile or compromised systems.
Network location is not authentication.
Link-local IPv4 addresses use 169.254.0.0/16.
IPv6 has link-local addressing commonly using fe80::/10.
Link-local addresses have limited routing scope.
Address scope matters.
NAT modifies network addressing information according to translation rules.
NAT commonly allows multiple private hosts to share public IPv4 connectivity.
NAT is not a security model by itself.
A NAT device can incidentally block unsolicited inbound connections when no mapping exists.
NAT and firewalling are different concepts.
Port forwarding creates translation rules allowing traffic toward an internal service.
A firewall can allow traffic.
A firewall can reject traffic.
A firewall can drop traffic.
Reject and drop have different observable behavior.
Reject commonly sends an error response.
Drop commonly discards traffic without an explicit rejection response.
A dropped TCP SYN can lead to a connection timeout.
A rejected TCP connection can produce an immediate failure.
Firewall behavior can therefore affect observed client errors.
An application should not infer too much from one network symptom.
Host firewalls and network firewalls are separate enforcement points.
Cloud-provider firewalls can add another enforcement point.
Multiple firewall layers can exist simultaneously.
Opening a host firewall does not prove an upstream firewall permits traffic.
A listening application does not prove routing reaches it.
Network interfaces connect hosts to networks.
An interface has state.
An interface can be administratively up.
An interface can be administratively down.
An interface can have one or more addresses.
An interface can have routes.
The loopback interface is commonly named lo on Linux.
Ethernet interfaces can have implementation-dependent names.
ip addr displays interface addressing on Linux.
ip link displays link-layer interface information.
ip route displays routing information.
ss displays socket information.
ss -l can show listening sockets according to options.
A listening socket proves that a local socket is listening.
A listening socket does not prove external reachability.
netstat is an older networking utility still present on some systems.
ss is commonly preferred on modern Linux systems.
Network tools provide evidence about particular layers.
Ethernet operates at a link layer.
Ethernet frames carry network-layer packets on Ethernet networks.
A MAC address identifies a link-layer interface according to Ethernet addressing.
MAC addresses and IP addresses operate at different layers.
A router normally forwards based on IP routing rather than remote MAC addresses across routed networks.
IPv6 uses Neighbor Discovery rather than ARP.
Neighbor Discovery uses ICMPv6.
Neighbor Discovery failure can prevent applicable IPv6 communication.
Link-layer problems and IP-layer problems are different.
MTU stands for Maximum Transmission Unit.
MTU limits packet size on a network link.
Different network paths can have different effective MTUs.
Path MTU Discovery attempts to determine usable packet sizes.
Incorrectly filtered ICMP can interfere with Path MTU Discovery.
MTU problems can produce connections that establish but fail when larger data transfers occur.
A successful handshake does not prove all packet sizes will traverse the path.
MSS stands for Maximum Segment Size in TCP.
TCP MSS relates to payload sizing within the path's packet constraints.
Fragmentation and segmentation are different mechanisms.
TLS stands for Transport Layer Security.
TLS provides confidentiality and integrity for application communication when correctly configured.
TLS can authenticate servers through certificates.
TLS can optionally authenticate clients.
TLS commonly operates above TCP.
HTTPS uses HTTP over TLS.
TLS and TCP are different layers.
TCP connectivity can succeed while TLS negotiation fails.
TLS negotiation can succeed while HTTP fails.
Each stage should remain distinguishable.
A TLS certificate binds identity information according to certificate validation rules.
A certificate can be expired.
A certificate can be issued by a trusted certification path.
A certificate can fail hostname validation.
Certificate trust and hostname validation are separate checks.
Disabling certificate verification removes important authentication guarantees.
A client should not disable TLS verification merely to make an error disappear.
TLS protocol versions differ.
TLS cipher suites define cryptographic algorithm combinations according to protocol version.
TLS configuration should use currently appropriate security settings.
TLS protects data in transit.
TLS does not protect a compromised endpoint.
TLS does not provide application authorization by itself.
TLS does not prevent an authorized application from returning incorrect data.
TLS does not prevent application logic errors.
Network security requires multiple layers.
A proxy forwards communication on behalf of clients or servers.
A reverse proxy can route requests.
A reverse proxy can load balance.
A reverse proxy can enforce request limits.
A reverse proxy can alter headers.
Proxy behavior introduces another failure layer.
A client can successfully reach a reverse proxy while the reverse proxy cannot reach the origin.
An upstream connection failure can produce an HTTP gateway error.
HTTP 502 commonly indicates invalid or failed upstream response behavior at a gateway or proxy.
HTTP 504 commonly indicates an upstream timeout at a gateway or proxy.
Architecture determines whether the component is acting as a gateway.
Load balancing distributes traffic among multiple service instances.
A load balancer can use round-robin selection.
A load balancer can use least-connections selection.
A load balancer can use health information.
A load balancer can use other routing policies.
Health checks should test the capability required for traffic.
A process existing is weaker evidence than successful application-level qualification.
A backend can be reachable but unhealthy.
Failover changes service traffic from one endpoint to another.
Failover should occur according to defined qualification rules.
A fallback endpoint should be validated before use.
Merely being listed in configuration does not prove an endpoint is healthy.
A successful TCP connection is one qualification stage.
A successful protocol handshake is a stronger qualification stage.
Network identity can be validated at the application layer.
Protocol version can be validated.
Network identifier can be validated.
Genesis identifier can be validated in a blockchain protocol.
Qualification should reject an endpoint that answers transport connections but belongs to the wrong network.
Transport success is not identity success.
A peer is another participant in a network protocol.
Peer-to-peer is commonly abbreviated P2P.
P2P does not mean that every peer has identical authority.
P2P does not mean that every peer stores identical state at every instant.
P2P protocol rules define participant behavior.
A peer discovery mechanism identifies candidate peers.
Discovery and qualification are different stages.
A discovered peer is not automatically trusted.
A peer handshake can exchange identity information.
A peer handshake can exchange protocol version.
A peer handshake can exchange capabilities.
A peer handshake can exchange current state information.
A peer can send malformed messages.
A peer can send oversized messages.
A peer can send unsupported messages.
A peer can disconnect unexpectedly.
A peer can lie.
Network protocol implementations should treat remote input as untrusted.
Message lengths should be bounded.
Field values should be validated.
State transitions should be validated.
Malformed remote input should not corrupt local state.
A protocol parser should reject invalid messages safely.
Network protocols should define byte order.
Network byte order is conventionally big-endian.
Multi-byte integer serialization should use explicit byte order.
Sending raw native integer memory across a network is not portable.
Sending raw C structures across a network is not portable unless the protocol deliberately fixes layout, alignment, byte order, and sizes.
Structure padding can differ between compilers and platforms.
Protocol serialization should be explicit.
A 32-bit protocol field should have exactly defined width.
Signedness should be defined.
Malformed lengths should be rejected before allocation where possible.
An attacker-controlled length should not cause unbounded memory allocation.
Message framing should be validated before message processing.
Protocol parsing should separate transport receipt from semantic validation.
Receiving bytes does not mean the message is valid.
Parsing a message does not mean the message is authorized.
Validating syntax does not mean the message is truthful.
Authorization and consensus rules can apply after parsing.
Protocol state machines define allowed message sequences.
A message can be syntactically valid but invalid in the current protocol state.
A protocol should not silently guess the meaning of unknown message types.
Unknown protocol versions should be handled according to compatibility policy.
Backward compatibility should be deliberate.
Forward compatibility should be deliberate.
Network protocol behavior should not depend on undefined implementation behavior.
A heartbeat is an application-level message indicating liveness according to protocol rules.
A heartbeat interval defines expected heartbeat timing.
A heartbeat timeout defines when a peer can be considered unresponsive according to policy.
Heartbeat intervals should not be unnecessarily aggressive.
A heartbeat succeeding does not prove every application operation works.
A protocol-specific health request can test stronger capability.
Long-lived connections need lifecycle management.
A connection should have a defined establishment state.
A connection should have a defined qualification state.
A connection should have a defined closing state.
A connection should not be considered qualified merely because a socket descriptor exists.
Reconnect logic should distinguish intentional shutdown from failure.
Intentional shutdown should not trigger endless reconnection.
Authentication failure should not necessarily trigger rapid automatic retry.
Transient transport failure can justify retry.
Permanent configuration failure should be surfaced rather than hidden behind infinite retry.
Retry behavior should depend on failure category.
DNS failure can be retried according to policy.
Connection refusal can be retried according to policy.
Authentication rejection should normally require correcting credentials or authority.
Protocol-version rejection should normally require compatibility correction.
Malformed local configuration should be corrected rather than repeatedly retried.
Deterministic failure classification prevents useless reconnect loops.
A connection pool maintains reusable connections.
A leaked connection can exhaust a pool.
An unhealthy pooled connection should not be reused blindly.
A pool should validate connections according to its contract.
Idle connections can be closed by servers, proxies, firewalls, or NAT devices.
A client should tolerate a pooled connection becoming stale.
Round-trip time is commonly abbreviated RTT.
RTT measures time for a message or packet exchange to travel to a destination and back according to the measurement method.
A high-bandwidth link can still have high latency.
A low-latency link can still have limited bandwidth.
Throughput can be lower than nominal bandwidth.
Packet loss can reduce TCP throughput.
High latency can affect protocols requiring many sequential round trips.
Application design can reduce unnecessary round trips.
Batching can reduce round trips.
Persistent connections can reduce repeated handshake overhead.
Caching can reduce network requests.
Performance optimization should be measured.
A slow request is not necessarily caused by the network.
Server computation can dominate latency.
Database queries can dominate latency.
Queueing can dominate latency.
DNS resolution can contribute latency.
TCP connection establishment can contribute latency.
TLS negotiation can contribute latency.
Network monitoring can observe connection and traffic behavior.
Wireshark analyzes network protocol traffic.
Unencrypted application data can be visible in packet captures.
TLS encrypts application payloads in transit.
TLS metadata can still reveal some communication characteristics.
Packet captures should be protected according to their contents.
A packet capture provides evidence from the capture point.
Traffic absent from one interface capture does not prove it was absent from every network segment.
Logs and packet captures provide different evidence.
Application logs show application interpretation.
Packet captures show observed network traffic.
Kernel socket state shows transport state.
DNS tools show name-resolution behavior.
Each source answers different questions.
netcat can create simple TCP or UDP connections according to implementation.
A successful netcat TCP connection proves basic transport connectivity to the listener.
It does not prove the application protocol is correct.
telnet can establish plain TCP sessions and is sometimes used for basic diagnostics.
telnet is not appropriate for transmitting sensitive credentials over unencrypted networks.
curl is useful for HTTP and related protocol testing.
dig queries DNS.
nslookup queries DNS but has different behavior and output.
ss examines sockets.
tcpdump examines packets.
No single diagnostic command proves every layer is healthy.
Diagnosis should begin with the observed failure.
If a hostname cannot resolve, inspect DNS.
If an address resolves but connection establishment fails, inspect routing, firewall, and listener state.
If TCP connects but TLS fails, inspect TLS configuration and identity.
If TLS succeeds but HTTP fails, inspect HTTP behavior.
If HTTP succeeds but application data is wrong, inspect application logic and data.
If an application protocol handshake fails after TCP connection, inspect protocol compatibility and state.
Layered diagnosis reduces speculation.
"Network error" is less useful than a specific failure category.
"DNS resolution failed" identifies a layer.
"TCP connection refused" identifies a layer.
"TCP connection timed out" identifies a layer.
"TLS certificate validation failed" identifies a layer.
"Read timed out waiting for response" identifies a layer.
"Peer closed connection" identifies a layer.
"Malformed response" identifies a layer.
Specific errors improve debugging.
Specific errors reduce false assumptions.
Windows network error codes and Linux errno values are platform-specific representations.
A platform-independent application can translate platform-specific network errors into defined internal categories.
The original platform error should remain available for diagnostics where useful.
A portable network layer should preserve semantic meaning across operating systems.
A timeout should remain a timeout across platforms.
A DNS failure should remain a DNS failure across platforms.
Network byte serialization should produce identical protocol bytes across supported platforms.
Protocol behavior should not depend on Windows or Linux socket implementation details.
The platform interface can translate operating-system networking APIs into common internal operations.
Common protocol semantics should remain platform-independent.
Platform-specific implementation behavior belongs behind the platform boundary.
A network module should have a defined interface.
The interface can define connect.
The interface can define listen.
The interface can define accept.
The interface can define send.
The interface can define receive.
The interface can define close.
The interface can define timeout behavior.
The interface can define address resolution.
The interface can define error categories.
The exact interface depends on architecture.
An interface should expose enough information for callers to distinguish meaningful failures.
Returning one generic failure for every network condition destroys information.
A Boolean success-or-failure return can be insufficient when callers need recovery decisions.
Network errors should not automatically become knowledge errors.
A failed socket connection does not mean information does not exist.
A knowledge miss does not mean the socket failed.
A protocol response saying no records were found means the remote service responded.
A remote service returning no matching records is evidence that communication succeeded far enough to receive that result.
A service timeout and an empty result are different.
A connection failure and an unsupported request are different.
Operational state and information state should remain distinct.
A service can be online and know nothing about a requested subject.
A service can contain the requested information while temporarily being unreachable.
Distributed systems communicate over networks.
A distributed system should expect partial failure.
One component can fail while others remain operational.
One network path can fail while another remains operational.
One peer can be unreachable while other peers remain reachable.
A DNS server can fail while cached records remain usable.
A primary endpoint can fail while a qualified fallback remains available.
Architecture should define which failures are fatal.
Architecture should define which failures cause degraded state.
Architecture should define which failures permit failover.
Architecture should define which failures should merely be logged.
Failover should preserve protocol identity.
A fallback server belonging to the wrong network is not a valid failover merely because it accepts a connection.
A fallback server using an incompatible protocol is not a valid failover.
Qualification should precede promotion to active use.
Network state changes over time.
A peer qualified earlier can later fail.
A previously failed peer can later recover.
Health should be re-evaluated according to policy.
Re-evaluation should not create excessive traffic.
A cached qualification result should have a defined lifetime if continued validity matters.
Connection establishment itself can provide fresh evidence.
Trust decisions should use the evidence required by the architecture.
A network identifier distinguishes one logical network from another.
A protocol version distinguishes protocol revisions.
A node identifier identifies a protocol participant according to protocol rules.
An address identifies a transport location.
These identifiers are not interchangeable.
A node can change IP address while retaining logical identity if the protocol permits it.
An IP address can host multiple logical nodes or services.
Transport location and protocol identity should remain separate.
Cryptographic identity can bind protocol identity to a key.
A public key can identify an entity according to protocol design.
A network connection alone does not prove cryptographic identity.
TLS server authentication can establish transport endpoint identity according to certificate rules.
Application signatures can establish message-level authenticity according to application rules.
Transport authentication and message authentication are different layers.
Network data should be treated as untrusted until validated according to the protocol.
Length fields should be checked.
Cryptographic hashes should be verified when the protocol defines them.
Signatures should be verified when required.
Network identifiers should be checked.
Protocol versions should be checked.
Sequence numbers should be checked when defined.
Replay protection should be applied when required.
Replay occurs when a previously valid message is transmitted again in a context where repetition is not authorized.
Nonces can help distinguish protocol exchanges.
Sequence numbers can help detect duplicates or ordering violations.
Replay protection must match protocol requirements.
A checksum detects some accidental corruption.
A checksum is not generally a cryptographic authentication mechanism.
A message authentication code can provide integrity and authentication when participants share an appropriate secret.
A digital signature can provide message authenticity using asymmetric cryptography when correctly implemented.
Networking and cryptography solve related but distinct problems.
Broadcast sends traffic to multiple participants within applicable network scope.
IPv4 supports broadcast addressing.
IPv6 does not use broadcast in the same way and relies heavily on multicast.
Multicast delivers traffic to subscribed groups according to network support.
Unicast addresses one destination endpoint.
Broadcast, multicast, and unicast have different scaling and routing behavior.
Public Internet routing does not generally forward local broadcast traffic.
Service discovery on a LAN can use multicast or broadcast protocols.
Service discovery across routed networks requires appropriate mechanisms.
A network daemon should validate discovery responses.
Discovery does not establish trust.
A public service must intentionally bind and be permitted through firewall policy.
Exposing an internal control interface publicly increases attack surface.
Attack surface should be minimized.
Unused listeners should be disabled.
Unused protocols should be disabled when practical.
Default credentials should not be used.
Rate limiting can protect network services from excessive request volume.
Connection limits can protect finite resources.
Per-client limits can reduce abuse.
Timeouts can prevent idle clients from consuming resources indefinitely.
Maximum connection counts can prevent descriptor exhaustion.
Maximum queue sizes can prevent unbounded memory growth.
Backpressure allows overloaded components to slow producers instead of accepting unlimited work.
Dropping work can be appropriate when protocol and application semantics permit it.
Silent failure makes diagnosis difficult.
A server under overload should preserve enough capacity to report useful status where practical.
Denial-of-service conditions can exhaust bandwidth.
Denial-of-service conditions can exhaust CPU.
Denial-of-service conditions can exhaust memory.
Denial-of-service conditions can exhaust application worker pools.
Distributed denial-of-service is commonly abbreviated DDoS.
DDoS uses traffic or requests from multiple sources to disrupt service.
The observed source IP of hostile traffic is not always the true human or organizational origin.
Source addresses can be spoofed in protocols where return traffic is unnecessary.
Proxies and relays can obscure origin.
Attribution requires evidence beyond country or IP address alone.
Traffic source and attacker identity are different claims.
Operational reporting should distinguish observed source addresses from attribution.
A network outage can result from attack.
A network outage can result from overload.
A network outage can result from configuration error.
A network outage can result from provider failure.
A network outage can result from routing failure.
A network outage can result from application failure.
Evidence should establish cause before conclusions are asserted.
Network monitoring should record enough evidence to reconstruct incidents.
Useful network evidence can include source and destination addresses.
Useful network evidence can include source and destination ports.
Useful network evidence can include protocol.
Useful network evidence can include connection state.
Useful network evidence can include request counts.
Useful network evidence can include response status.
Useful network evidence can include duration.
Logs should distinguish observation from inference.
"Address X belongs to attacker Y" is an attribution claim requiring additional evidence.
Evidence-based reporting should preserve that distinction.
A network service should have deterministic startup behavior.
A network service should fail clearly when a required port cannot be bound.
A network service should report the address and port it successfully binds when appropriate.
A network service should define timeout behavior.
A network service should define connection lifecycle.
A network service should define malformed-input behavior.
A network service should define reconnect behavior when acting as a client.
A network service should define retry behavior.
A network service should define failure categories.
A network service should not treat every transient failure as fatal.
A network service should not treat every negative application result as network failure.
A network service should preserve boundaries between transport, protocol, application, and knowledge state.
Networking is communication between systems under imperfect conditions.
Correct networking code assumes connections can close.
Correct networking code assumes reads can be partial.
Correct networking code assumes names can fail to resolve.
Correct networking code assumes routes can change.
Correct networking code assumes dependencies can become unavailable.
Correct networking code bounds resource use.
Correct networking code validates remote input.
Correct networking code reports the layer that actually failed.
Correct networking code retries only when retry is appropriate.
Correct networking code preserves protocol semantics across platforms.
Correct networking code separates reachability from identity.
Correct networking code separates availability from knowledge.
Small protocols are easier to reason about.
Explicit framing is easier to reason about than implicit framing.
Explicit versions are easier to reason about than guessed compatibility.
Explicit error categories are easier to reason about than generic failure.
Explicit peer qualification is easier to reason about than trusting any open socket.
Deterministic networking does not mean the network itself is deterministic.
The network can fail unpredictably.
Security engineering protects systems, data, operations, and authority from unauthorized or unintended actions.
Security is a system property.
Security is not provided by one programming language.
Security is not provided by one firewall.
Security is not provided by encryption alone.
Security depends on architecture, implementation, configuration, operation, and human authority.
A trust boundary separates components or contexts with different levels of trust.
Data crossing a trust boundary should be treated according to the receiving system's validation requirements.
External input is untrusted unless an established mechanism proves otherwise.
Network input is untrusted.
HTTP request input is untrusted.
Command-line input can be untrusted.
Environment variables can be untrusted.
Configuration files can be untrusted when unauthorized parties can modify them.
File content can be untrusted.
Module input can be untrusted.
Plugin input can be untrusted.
Inter-process messages can be untrusted.
Data becoming internal does not automatically make it trustworthy.
Trust should be established through defined mechanisms.
Trust should not be inferred merely from location.
localhost does not automatically mean trusted.
A private network does not automatically mean trusted.
An internal service does not automatically mean trusted.
A file under an application directory does not automatically mean trusted.
A module being present does not automatically mean trusted.
A successful network connection does not automatically mean trusted.
A valid JSON document does not automatically mean trusted.
A syntactically valid request does not automatically mean authorized.
Authentication and authorization are different.
Authorization should be checked where protected actions occur.
A username alone does not prove identity.
A claimed identifier alone does not prove identity.
A request field saying admin does not grant administrative authority.
Least privilege means granting only the authority required for a task.
A process should not run as root merely because root avoids permission errors.
A module should receive only the capabilities required for its mission.
A user should receive only the permissions required for their role.
A service account should have only the resources required by that service.
Reducing privilege reduces the consequences of compromise.
Privilege should have a defined purpose.
Privilege should have a defined scope.
Privilege should have a defined lifetime when temporary authority is sufficient.
Permission to execute is not permission to act without boundaries.
Capability and authority are different concepts.
A program can technically be capable of an action without being authorized to perform it.
Authorization should be explicit.
Input validation determines whether input satisfies defined requirements.
Validation should occur before input controls privileged behavior.
Validation should enforce expected type.
Validation should enforce expected range.
Validation should enforce expected format.
Validation should enforce permitted values.
Validation should reject prohibited states.
Validation should reject malformed input.
Validation should not silently reinterpret malformed input into a different valid request.
Normalization and validation are different operations.
Normalization should not create ambiguity.
Canonicalization produces a defined representation.
Canonicalization can be security-sensitive.
Encoding transformations can change interpreted meaning.
Repeated decoding can create vulnerabilities.
Validation should occur against the representation actually used for the security decision.
A denylist defines values or patterns explicitly prohibited.
Allowlists are often stronger when the set of valid inputs can be precisely defined.
An allowlist should still be designed correctly.
Input validation is not the same as output encoding.
Output encoding protects data when inserted into a particular interpretation context.
HTML output requires HTML-appropriate encoding.
SQL values require parameterized query handling.
Shell arguments require safe process invocation.
URL components require appropriate URL encoding.
JSON values require JSON serialization.
One encoding mechanism should not be assumed safe for another context.
HTML escaping does not make a string safe as SQL.
JSON escaping does not make a string safe as HTML.
SQL escaping does not make a string safe as a shell command.
SQL injection occurs when untrusted data alters intended SQL syntax.
Command injection occurs when untrusted data alters intended operating-system command syntax.
Code injection occurs when untrusted data becomes executable code.
LDAP injection can occur when untrusted data alters LDAP query syntax.
Injection vulnerabilities cross a data-to-instruction boundary.
Data should remain data.
Prepared statements can prevent many SQL injection vulnerabilities when used correctly.
Concatenating untrusted strings into SQL creates risk.
Escaping manually is generally weaker than correct parameter binding.
A parameterized query does not automatically validate business rules.
Untrusted input should not be concatenated into shell commands.
A shell should not be invoked when shell syntax is unnecessary.
If a shell must be used, input handling must follow the shell's actual parsing rules.
A filename is not automatically a safe shell argument.
XSS occurs when attacker-controlled content is executed as script or otherwise interpreted as active content in another user's browser.
Stored XSS persists attacker-controlled content for later delivery.
Reflected XSS returns attacker-controlled content through a request-response path.
DOM-based XSS can result from unsafe browser-side DOM manipulation.
HTML output encoding helps prevent XSS in HTML text contexts.
Different HTML contexts require appropriate handling.
An HTML attribute context differs from an HTML text context.
A JavaScript context differs from an HTML context.
Using textContent is safer than innerHTML when only text is intended.
Content Security Policy does not replace correct output handling.
Cross-Site Request Forgery is commonly abbreviated CSRF.
CSRF causes a user's authenticated client to perform an unwanted action.
CSRF and XSS are different vulnerabilities.
State-changing requests should use appropriate methods.
GET should not perform destructive state changes.
CSRF tokens can protect state-changing requests.
A CSRF token should be unpredictable.
A CSRF token should be validated server-side.
Merely placing a hidden field named csrf does not provide protection if its value is not verified.
SameSite cookies can provide additional CSRF protection.
SameSite cookies do not replace authorization.
Logout can be a state-changing operation.
Passwords are authentication secrets.
Passwords should not ordinarily be stored in plaintext.
Passwords should not ordinarily be reversibly encrypted merely for later comparison.
Passwords should be processed with an appropriate password hashing algorithm.
Password hashing should use a unique salt.
A salt does not need to be secret.
A salt helps prevent efficient reuse of precomputed password hashes.
A password hash is not the same as a general cryptographic hash used without a password-hardening construction.
Password verification should use the password-hashing algorithm's verification mechanism.
Passwords should not be logged.
Passwords should not be exposed in error messages.
Secrets include API credentials.
Secrets include session identifiers when possession grants authority.
Secrets should be protected at rest according to requirements.
Secrets should be protected in transit according to requirements.
Secrets should not be embedded unnecessarily in source code.
Secrets should not be committed to public source-control repositories.
Removing a secret from the latest source revision does not remove it from repository history.
A disclosed secret should be considered for rotation.
Secret rotation replaces credentials that may no longer be trustworthy.
Access to secrets should follow least privilege.
Private keys should remain private.
Possession of a private key can grant cryptographic authority.
Private-key file permissions should be restrictive.
Losing a private key can mean losing the ability to authenticate or sign according to the system design.
Cryptographic hashes map arbitrary input to fixed-size digest values.
Changing input generally changes the hash.
A cryptographic hash is not encryption.
A hash is not designed to be decrypted.
A hash alone does not prove who produced data.
A SHA-256 digest is commonly represented using 64 hexadecimal characters.
A SHA-256 digest can detect changes when compared against a trusted expected digest.
A digest received from the same untrusted source as the data does not independently establish authenticity.
The trusted source of the expected digest matters.
A cryptographic hash can provide integrity evidence.
A digital signature uses asymmetric cryptography.
A private key creates a signature.
Successful signature verification does not prove that the signer was authorized to perform every possible action.
Cryptographic identity and authorization remain separate.
RSA is an asymmetric cryptographic algorithm family.
RSA key size affects security properties.
RSA-3072 uses a 3072-bit RSA modulus.
SHA-256 can be used with appropriate RSA signature schemes.
Signature schemes must define padding and encoding requirements.
Raw RSA operations should not be invented as application signature schemes.
Established cryptographic constructions should be used.
Cryptographic code should use well-reviewed implementations where practical.
Inventing custom cryptography creates unnecessary risk.
Encryption and hashing are different.
Hashing is designed as a one-way digest operation.
Symmetric encryption uses shared secret key material.
Asymmetric encryption uses a public-private key relationship.
TLS uses cryptography to protect network communication.
Encryption does not automatically provide authorization.
Encryption does not make malicious data safe.
Encrypted malicious input remains malicious after decryption.
Authentication should occur according to protocol requirements.
Authorization should occur according to application requirements.
A nonce is a value intended for limited or single use according to protocol design.
A nonce is not automatically secret.
Replay attacks reuse previously valid messages or credentials.
Replay protection can use nonces.
Replay protection can use timestamps combined with appropriate acceptance windows and identity mechanisms.
Replay protection can use protocol state.
No single replay mechanism is correct for every protocol.
A timestamp alone does not prove freshness.
System clocks can differ.
System clocks can be manipulated.
Freshness rules should be explicit.
Random values used for security should come from a cryptographically secure random source.
Predictable random numbers should not be used for authentication tokens.
Predictable random numbers should not be used for cryptographic keys.
Operating systems provide secure randomness interfaces.
Cryptographic libraries provide secure random generation.
A pseudo-random generator can be cryptographically secure when designed and seeded appropriately.
The ordinary language runtime random function may not be suitable for cryptographic use.
Memory safety protects against invalid memory operations.
C permits direct memory manipulation.
C does not automatically enforce array bounds.
C does not automatically prevent use-after-free.
C does not automatically prevent buffer overflow.
C does not automatically prevent null-pointer dereference.
C does not automatically prevent integer overflow.
C does not automatically initialize all memory.
Memory-safe programming in C requires explicit discipline.
A buffer overflow occurs when a program writes beyond the bounds of a buffer.
An out-of-bounds operation can corrupt memory.
An out-of-bounds operation can disclose data.
An out-of-bounds operation can sometimes permit arbitrary code execution.
Copy operations should know destination capacity.
String operations should account for terminating null bytes when C strings are used.
A C string is conventionally terminated by a null byte.
Binary data is not necessarily a C string.
Binary data can contain null bytes.
strlen should not be used on arbitrary binary buffers unless null-termination is guaranteed.
Network message lengths should be tracked explicitly.
File data lengths should be tracked explicitly.
A pointer does not carry buffer length information by itself.
A function receiving a buffer should receive or otherwise know its valid length.
Integer overflow can invalidate size calculations.
Multiplication used for allocation size can overflow.
Bounds checks should account for arithmetic overflow.
Negative signed values converted to unsigned types can become large positive values.
Compiler warnings should be reviewed.
Warnings can identify dangerous conversions.
Use-after-free occurs when memory is accessed after its lifetime has ended.
Double free occurs when dynamically allocated memory is freed more than once.
Memory ownership should be explicit.
Each allocation should have defined ownership.
Each resource should have defined cleanup responsibility.
Ambiguous ownership creates bugs.
Resource leaks can cause denial of service.
Memory leaks consume memory.
File descriptor leaks consume descriptors.
Thread leaks consume execution resources.
Resource exhaustion is a security concern.
Security includes availability.
Availability means authorized users or systems can obtain required service according to requirements.
Confidentiality protects information from unauthorized disclosure.
Integrity protects information from unauthorized modification.
Availability protects required access to systems and information.
Confidentiality, integrity, and availability are commonly called the CIA triad.
The CIA triad is a conceptual model.
Security requirements can extend beyond the CIA triad.
Authenticity concerns confidence in identity or origin.
Accountability concerns associating actions with actors according to system evidence.
Non-repudiation concerns evidence intended to prevent credible denial of certain actions under defined conditions.
Privacy concerns appropriate handling of information about individuals.
Safety and security can overlap but are not identical.
Availability can be attacked through denial of service.
A denial-of-service attack attempts to make a service unavailable.
A distributed denial-of-service attack uses multiple sources or systems.
DDoS is not defined by the attacker's country.
Observed source location does not establish attacker identity.
Compromised machines can participate in DDoS attacks.
Botnets can distribute attack traffic.
Reflection attacks cause third-party systems to send responses toward a victim.
Amplification attacks generate responses larger than triggering requests.
Rate limiting can reduce some denial-of-service effects.
Connection limits can reduce some denial-of-service effects.
Request-size limits can reduce some denial-of-service effects.
Timeouts can reduce some resource-exhaustion attacks.
Upstream filtering can be required when attack traffic exceeds local network capacity.
Security controls should exist at appropriate layers.
A web application firewall can inspect application traffic according to configured rules.
A WAF does not replace secure application code.
A WAF can produce false positives.
A firewall does not repair SQL injection.
A firewall does not repair broken authorization.
A firewall does not repair unsafe memory handling.
Defense in depth uses multiple independent or complementary security controls.
Defense in depth does not mean adding arbitrary complexity.
Each security control should address a defined risk.
Redundant controls can reduce dependence on a single failure.
Security complexity itself can create vulnerabilities.
Small, understandable security boundaries are easier to audit.
Default allow means actions are permitted unless explicitly prohibited.
Default deny is often appropriate for security-sensitive authority boundaries.
Default deny should not be applied blindly where system requirements require open participation.
Security policy defines permitted and prohibited behavior.
Code implements mechanisms.
Policy and mechanism are different.
A mechanism should not silently invent policy.
A service should not grant itself new authority because an operation would otherwise fail.
Failure to obtain authorization means the action should not occur.
An error should not be solved by bypassing security controls.
Permission denied is evidence that an access-control mechanism rejected an operation.
chmod 777 is not a generic solution to permission denied.
Running as root is not a generic solution to permission denied.
Disabling TLS verification is not a generic solution to certificate errors.
Disabling CSRF protection is not a generic solution to failed POST requests.
Disabling authentication is not a generic solution to authorization problems.
Security failures should be diagnosed rather than bypassed.
Secure failure means the system remains within authorized boundaries when an operation fails.
Fail closed means failure results in denying the protected action.
Fail open means failure results in permitting the action.
Security-sensitive authorization commonly should fail closed.
Not every system function should fail closed in the same way.
Availability requirements can require carefully designed degraded behavior.
A logging failure should not necessarily grant administrative access.
An authentication-service failure should not automatically authenticate everyone.
A signature-verification failure should not treat the signature as valid.
An unreadable access-control policy should not silently become allow-all.
Security-critical defaults should be explicit.
A missing required security configuration should produce a clear failure.
A malformed required security configuration should produce a clear failure.
A security subsystem should not silently guess credentials.
Identity creation should be deliberate.
Key creation should be deliberate.
Replacing a key can invalidate established identity relationships.
Existing keys should not be overwritten casually.
File existence should be checked before identity generation when preservation is required.
Atomic creation can prevent concurrent identity-generation races.
A module system creates a trust boundary.
A module can execute code inside or alongside a host process depending on architecture.
Loading native code grants substantial capability within the hosting process.
A native module can potentially access host memory.
A native module can potentially call host-accessible operating-system functions.
Module discovery should identify candidates.
Manifest validation should verify required metadata.
ABI validation should verify binary interface compatibility.
Qualification should verify required module properties.
Loading should occur only after required validation.
Activation should occur only after successful loading and qualification according to architecture.
Discovery does not equal authorization.
Presence does not equal qualification.
Qualification does not equal unrestricted authority.
A module should not invent new host capabilities.
A module ABI defines binary compatibility.
An ABI version mismatch can justify module rejection.
An ABI mismatch is not a network error.
An ABI mismatch is not a knowledge miss.
An ABI mismatch is not a malformed HTTP request.
Failure categories should remain precise.
A module manifest can declare ABI compatibility.
The host's authoritative ABI definition determines the interface it implements.
Historical documentation does not override the active interface contract.
Documentation should be kept current to reduce ambiguity.
Runtime validation should not rely solely on human-readable README history when machine-readable authoritative data exists.
Security decisions should prefer authoritative structured state.
A file hash can detect module modification.
A signature can authenticate an approved module according to signing policy.
Hash verification and signature verification are different.
A matching hash proves equality to a trusted expected hash.
A valid signature proves validity under the corresponding verification key and signature scheme.
A valid signature does not automatically mean a module is compatible.
A compatible module does not automatically mean a module is authorized.
Authorization should still be validated.
Security checks can form a pipeline.
Each stage should answer one defined question.
Is the file present?
Is the file structurally valid?
Is the manifest valid?
Is the ABI compatible?
Is the signature valid?
Is the signer authorized?
Does the module satisfy qualification requirements?
Can the module load?
Can the module activate?
Each question is different.
Collapsing all failures into "module invalid" destroys useful evidence.
Audit logs record security-relevant and operational events.
An audit log should record facts about events.
An audit log should use stable event categories.
An audit log should include useful timestamps.
An audit log can include action.
An audit log can include target.
An audit log can include result.
An audit log can include reason.
An audit log should not contain unnecessary secrets.
Audit records should be protected from unauthorized modification.
Audit records do not automatically prove every event that occurred.
Logging coverage determines what can be concluded from absence.
Security monitoring detects events requiring attention.
An alert is evidence that a detection rule triggered.
Detection rules can produce false positives.
Detection rules can produce false negatives.
An investigation evaluates available evidence.
A confirmed incident should meet defined evidence requirements.
Evidence and probability are different.
A pattern match is evidence of a pattern match.
A pattern match is not automatically evidence of successful exploitation.
An HTTP attack request reaching a server does not prove exploitation succeeded.
A vulnerability scanner request does not prove the vulnerability exists.
A failed login attempt does not prove account compromise.
A successful login proves authentication succeeded according to the system but does not by itself prove the human identity behind the action.
Observed source IP addresses identify observed network sources.
Observed country information is derived from address-location data and can be inaccurate.
Security reports should avoid unsupported attribution.
Security reports should preserve timestamps.
Security reports should preserve affected systems.
Security reports should preserve operational impact.
An event occurring during an outage does not alone prove it caused the outage.
Causation requires supporting evidence.
Security research should preserve historical records.
Historical security records can remain valuable after a threat is resolved.
Status can change while the historical record remains.
Deletion of evidence can damage future analysis.
Retention policy should define how evidence is preserved.
Sensitive evidence should have access controls.
Integrity verification can help detect evidence modification.
Digital evidence should be collected without unnecessary modification.
Analysis can operate on copies.
Hashes can verify that copies match originals.
Time synchronization improves event correlation.
Clock drift can complicate timelines.
A timestamp is evidence of what a system clock reported.
A timestamp is not infallible proof of absolute real-world time.
Multiple independent evidence sources can strengthen conclusions.
If evidence does not establish a conclusion, the conclusion should not be presented as fact.
If there is doubt, the evidence should be described rather than certainty invented.
Contradictory evidence should be reported as contradictory.
An exploit is a method or action that takes advantage of a vulnerability.
A compromise occurs when protected security properties have actually been violated.
An attempted attack is not automatically a compromise.
A vulnerability existing is not proof that it has been exploited.
An exploit attempt is not proof of exploit success.
Evidence of successful unauthorized state change is stronger than evidence of attempted input.
Security severity should reflect potential and actual impact according to the chosen assessment framework.
Risk considers likelihood and impact according to a risk model.
A high-severity vulnerability can have low immediate exposure in one deployment.
A lower-severity weakness can become important in combination with other weaknesses.
Threat modeling identifies assets.
Threat modeling identifies controls.
Threat modeling should occur before incidents where practical.
An asset is something requiring protection.
Assets can include data.
Assets can include credentials.
Assets can include availability.
Assets can include identity.
Assets can include authority.
Assets can include reputation, although reputation is not a technical control.
An attack surface consists of interfaces through which a system can be influenced.
Network listeners contribute to attack surface.
Web endpoints contribute to attack surface.
File parsers contribute to attack surface.
Module loaders contribute to attack surface.
Administrative interfaces contribute to attack surface.
Update mechanisms contribute to attack surface.
IPC interfaces contribute to attack surface.
Reducing unnecessary interfaces reduces attack surface.
Unused functionality should not remain enabled without reason.
Administrative interfaces should receive stronger protection than ordinary public interfaces when their authority is greater.
Administrative actions should require authorization.
Administrative actions should be auditable.
An uncommon filename is not authorization.
Software updates can repair vulnerabilities.
Update authenticity should be verified.
An update should come from an authorized source.
An update package can be hashed.
An update package can be digitally signed.
A valid signature can authenticate the update according to signing policy.
An update should be compatible with the target system.
An authentic update can still be incompatible.
Partial updates can leave inconsistent state.
Atomic replacement can reduce partial-update risk.
Rollback can restore a previous version when supported and authorized.
Rollback itself can create security risk if it permits installation of known-vulnerable versions.
Update policy should define permitted versions.
Automatic updates trade operator control for faster deployment.
Manual updates trade automation for operator scheduling.
Dependencies create inherited risk.
A vulnerable dependency can affect applications using it.
Dependency version information should be known.
Unused dependencies should be removed.
Dependencies should come from trusted sources.
Dependency updates should be tested.
A dependency being popular does not prove it is secure.
A dependency being open source does not prove it is secure.
A dependency being proprietary does not prove it is secure.
Evidence matters more than labels.
Software composition analysis can identify known dependency vulnerabilities.
Known-vulnerability scanning does not detect every vulnerability.
Static analysis examines source or compiled representations without executing the complete target behavior.
Fuzzing supplies generated or mutated inputs to discover failures.
Code review examines implementation.
No single testing method proves complete security.
Multiple methods provide different evidence.
Fuzzing is useful for parsers.
Fuzzing is useful for protocol handlers.
A crash is evidence of incorrect behavior.
A crash is not automatically exploitable.
Regression tests can ensure a repaired vulnerability does not reappear.
Security tests should include negative tests.
Authentication testing should include invalid credentials.
Authorization testing should include insufficient privileges.
Parser testing should include malformed input.
Protocol testing should include unsupported versions.
Signature testing should include modified content.
File-access testing should include denied paths.
Security systems should prove that boundaries reject invalid behavior, not only that valid behavior succeeds.
Error messages should provide enough information for legitimate diagnosis.
Error messages should not unnecessarily disclose secrets.
Stack traces can reveal internal paths and implementation details.
Database errors can reveal schema details.
Detailed diagnostics can be recorded in protected logs.
Operator-facing diagnostics can contain more detail than untrusted client responses when appropriate.
An alert identifier can support investigation.
An alert identifier is not evidence by itself.
Security alerts should have stable identifiers where operational workflows require them.
Security state should be explicit.
NORMAL can represent expected operation according to system policy.
DEGRADED can represent reduced capability.
ALERT can represent detected conditions requiring attention.
FAILED can represent loss of required capability.
COMPROMISED should not be asserted without evidence meeting the system's definition.
State names should have defined criteria.
A warning should not automatically mean compromise.
A module failure should not automatically mean compromise.
A timeout should not automatically mean attack.
A crash should not automatically mean attack.
An attack should not automatically mean successful compromise.
Secure software should validate assumptions.
Secure software should bound resources.
Secure software should separate data from instructions.
Secure software should authenticate identity where identity matters.
Secure software should authorize actions where authority matters.
Secure software should preserve least privilege.
Secure software should protect secrets.
Secure software should verify integrity where integrity matters.
Secure software should log important events without leaking secrets.
Secure software should fail within defined boundaries.
Secure software should distinguish errors accurately.
Secure software should preserve evidence.
Secure software should avoid unsupported conclusions.
Secure software should remain understandable enough to audit.
Unnecessary complexity increases the number of states engineers must reason about.
Security controls should therefore be as simple as their requirements permit.
Simple does not mean weak.
A small explicit interface can be easier to secure than unrestricted internal access.
A deterministic validator can be easier to test than heuristic acceptance.
A deterministic permission model can be easier to audit than undocumented exceptions.
A deterministic module loader can be easier to qualify than arbitrary plugin behavior.
The same invalid signature should be rejected consistently.
The same incompatible ABI should be rejected consistently.
The same malformed request should be rejected consistently.
Security decisions should not depend on irrelevant timing or incidental ordering.
Historical documentation should not override active security configuration.
Old credentials should not remain active merely because they appear in documentation.
Old interface versions should not be accepted merely because they appear in history.
Current authority should come from the system's governing source of truth.
Evidence should come from the source appropriate to the question.
Source code can establish intended implementation.
Compiled artifacts establish what was built from some source and toolchain.
Runtime state establishes what is currently executing.
Configuration establishes configured behavior.
Logs establish recorded historical events.
Documentation explains intended or historical behavior according to its scope.
Security is not certainty produced by confidence.
Security is disciplined control supported by evidence.
SQL stands for Structured Query Language.
SQL is a language used to work with relational database systems.
SQL can update data.
SQL can delete data.
SQL can define constraints.
SQL can define permissions in database systems that support those operations.
MySQL is a relational database management system.
MariaDB is a relational database management system.
PostgreSQL is a relational database management system.
SQLite is a relational database management system.
Different database systems implement SQL with differences.
SQL syntax and behavior are not completely identical across every database system.
A feature supported by one SQL database is not automatically supported by another.
Database-specific behavior should not be assumed to be portable SQL.
A relational database organizes data into relations commonly represented as tables.
A table contains columns.
A row represents one tuple or record according to the table design.
A column represents an attribute according to the table design.
A table has a defined schema.
A schema can define tables.
A schema can define columns.
A schema can define constraints.
A schema can define indexes.
A schema can define relationships.
A database schema and the data stored under that schema are different things.
Changing data does not necessarily change the schema.
Changing the schema can affect how existing data is interpreted or constrained.
A column has a data type.
Data types restrict or describe values that can be stored.
Common SQL data categories include binary values.
Common SQL data categories include dates.
Common SQL data categories include times.
Common SQL data categories include timestamps.
Database systems can provide additional data types.
Data types should match the meaning and required range of the data.
A number stored as text behaves differently from a number stored in a numeric type.
Identifiers should not automatically be treated as quantities.
A numeric-looking identifier can be stored as text when arithmetic has no meaning for it.
A primary key identifies rows according to a table's key definition.
A primary key cannot contain NULL according to relational database primary-key semantics.
A table normally has at most one primary key definition.
A primary key can contain one column.
A primary key can contain multiple columns.
A key containing multiple columns is a composite key.
A surrogate key is an identifier introduced primarily for database identity.
Neither surrogate nor natural keys are universally correct.
Key choice should follow data requirements.
A UNIQUE constraint and a primary key are not identical concepts.
A table can have multiple UNIQUE constraints.
A foreign key expresses a relationship between values in tables.
A foreign key references a candidate key according to database rules.
Foreign-key constraints can enforce referential integrity.
Referential integrity prevents certain invalid relationships between records.
Deleting a referenced parent can be restricted.
Deleting a referenced parent can cascade according to foreign-key configuration.
Deleting a referenced parent can set a child reference to NULL according to supported configuration.
Cascade behavior should be chosen deliberately.
A cascading delete can remove more data than the directly selected row.
Destructive cascade behavior should be understood before execution.
Constraints enforce data rules at the database layer.
UNIQUE enforces uniqueness according to database semantics.
PRIMARY KEY defines the primary identifier.
FOREIGN KEY enforces defined references.
CHECK can enforce expressions according to database support.
DEFAULT supplies a value when one is not otherwise provided according to database semantics.
A default value is not validation of arbitrary supplied values.
Constraints protect integrity even when multiple applications write to the same database.
Database constraints do not replace application authorization.
NULL represents the absence of a known SQL value according to SQL semantics.
NULL is not the empty string.
NULL is not zero.
NULL is not false.
NULL behaves differently from ordinary values.
Comparing a value to NULL with ordinary equality does not behave like comparing ordinary values.
IS NULL tests for NULL.
IS NOT NULL tests for non-NULL.
SQL uses three-valued logic in many expressions.
An SQL condition can evaluate to TRUE.
An SQL condition can evaluate to FALSE.
An SQL condition can evaluate to UNKNOWN.
NULL can cause comparisons to evaluate to UNKNOWN.
UNKNOWN is not the same as FALSE even though filtering can exclude both.
NULL semantics should be understood explicitly.
WHERE filters rows.
GROUP BY groups rows for aggregate processing.
HAVING filters groups.
ORDER BY defines result ordering.
LIMIT can restrict returned rows in database systems that support that syntax.
SQL clauses have defined logical roles.
SELECT * requests all columns from the selected row source.
SELECT * can be convenient.
SELECT * can also retrieve unnecessary data.
Explicit columns make dependencies clearer.
Explicit columns can reduce data transfer.
Explicit columns can reduce accidental exposure of newly added fields.
A WHERE clause restricts affected rows.
An UPDATE without an appropriate WHERE clause can modify every qualifying row in a table.
A DELETE without an appropriate WHERE clause can delete every qualifying row in a table.
Destructive SQL should be reviewed carefully.
A query should not assume a WHERE clause exists merely because one was intended.
SELECT retrieves rows.
ALTER changes database objects.
DROP removes database objects.
TRUNCATE removes table data according to database-specific semantics.
DELETE and DROP are fundamentally different operations.
TRUNCATE and DELETE have different semantics.
DDL commonly refers to Data Definition Language operations.
DML commonly refers to Data Manipulation Language operations.
Database operations should be understood by behavior rather than acronym alone.
A query result can contain zero rows.
Zero rows does not mean the database is unavailable.
Zero rows does not mean the SQL parser failed.
Zero rows does not mean the network failed.
Zero rows does not mean the database connection failed.
Zero rows means the successfully executed query produced no result rows according to its conditions.
An empty result and an execution failure are different states.
A database connection failure means the client could not establish or maintain required database connectivity.
An authentication failure means database credentials were rejected according to the authentication mechanism.
An authorization failure means the authenticated database identity lacks permission for the requested operation.
A deadlock is a concurrency condition in which transactions wait in a cycle that the database resolves by aborting one or more participants according to its behavior.
These failures are different.
Applications should preserve database error categories.
A query returning no rows should not be reported as an internal service failure.
A network timeout should not be reported as zero matching rows.
Failure layers matter.
A JOIN combines rows from multiple row sources according to a relationship condition.
An INNER JOIN returns rows satisfying the join relationship.
A RIGHT JOIN preserves rows from the right side where supported.
A FULL OUTER JOIN preserves unmatched rows from both sides where supported.
Not every database system supports every join syntax identically.
A CROSS JOIN forms combinations of rows according to Cartesian-product semantics.
An accidental Cartesian product can produce a very large result.
Tables should normally be joined using meaningful relationships.
A JOIN does not automatically require foreign-key constraints, but constraints can enforce the relationship represented by the data model.
A self join joins a table to itself through aliases.
Aliases give temporary names to tables or expressions within a query.
Aliases can disambiguate columns with the same name.
Column names should be qualified when ambiguity exists.
Ambiguous SQL should not rely on the database guessing intent.
COUNT counts according to the expression supplied.
COUNT(*) and COUNT(column) can produce different results.
AVG calculates an average.
MAX selects a maximum value.
Aggregate behavior with NULL should follow the database and SQL semantics.
GROUP BY defines groups used by aggregate expressions.
Grouping rules should be explicit.
HAVING filters aggregated groups.
WHERE filters rows before grouping in the logical query model.
WHERE and HAVING solve different problems.
ORDER BY controls output ordering.
Without ORDER BY, row order should not be assumed.
A database can return rows in different orders across executions when no ordering is specified.
Primary-key order is not automatically result order.
Index order is not automatically result order.
If order matters, ORDER BY should specify it.
ASC requests ascending ordering.
DESC requests descending ordering.
Multiple ORDER BY expressions can define deterministic tie-breaking.
Ordering on a non-unique field alone can leave ties with unspecified relative order.
A deterministic result order requires enough ordering expressions to distinguish rows when required.
LIMIT restricts the number of returned rows in systems supporting it.
OFFSET skips rows according to result ordering.
Pagination should use deterministic ordering.
Pagination without stable ordering can return inconsistent page boundaries.
Large OFFSET values can become inefficient.
Keyset pagination can use ordered key values instead of large offsets.
Pagination strategy should follow scale and requirements.
An index is a database structure used to accelerate certain data access patterns.
An index can reduce query search work.
An index consumes storage.
Indexes should support actual query patterns.
A UNIQUE constraint commonly uses an index according to database implementation.
A composite index contains multiple columns.
Column order in a composite index matters.
An index useful for one query may not help another query.
A database query optimizer chooses an execution strategy.
An execution plan describes how the database intends to execute a query.
EXPLAIN can display query-plan information in many SQL databases.
A slow query should be measured.
A slow query should not automatically be blamed on missing indexes.
A query can be slow because it examines too much data.
A query can be slow because of poor join strategy.
A query can be slow because of lock contention.
A query can be slow because of disk pressure.
A query can be slow because of insufficient memory.
A query can be slow because of network latency.
A query can be slow because of inefficient application behavior.
Performance diagnosis should identify the actual bottleneck.
Second Normal Form is commonly abbreviated 2NF.
Third Normal Form is commonly abbreviated 3NF.
Boyce-Codd Normal Form is commonly abbreviated BCNF.
Normalization is not a commandment that every database must maximize.
Database design should reflect data relationships and workload.
A table should represent a coherent concept.
Repeated groups should not be hidden inside arbitrary string fields when relational querying is required.
Comma-separated identifiers inside one column are generally harder to validate and query relationally than a relationship table.
Structured relationships should normally be represented structurally.
JSON columns can be useful for data whose structure fits that storage model.
JSON storage does not eliminate relational design considerations.
Database choice should match requirements.
A relational database is useful when relationships, constraints, transactions, and structured queries matter.
JSON can be appropriate for configuration.
A database should not be introduced merely because persistence exists.
A file should not be used merely to avoid learning database semantics when transactional relational behavior is actually required.
Small, deterministic systems should choose the simplest persistence mechanism satisfying requirements.
A transaction groups database operations into a unit of work.
BEGIN or START TRANSACTION begins an explicit transaction according to database syntax.
COMMIT makes transaction changes durable and visible according to database semantics.
ROLLBACK abandons uncommitted changes according to transaction semantics.
Transactions help maintain consistency across related operations.
A transaction can contain one statement.
A transaction can contain multiple statements.
Multiple related changes can succeed or fail together.
Autocommit automatically commits statements according to database and connection configuration.
An application should not assume it is inside an explicit transaction when it is not.
ACID describes important transaction properties.
ACID stands for Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability.
Atomicity means transaction operations are treated as a unit with respect to commit or rollback.
Consistency means transactions preserve defined database integrity rules when correctly designed.
Isolation concerns interactions between concurrent transactions.
Durability concerns persistence of committed changes according to database guarantees.
ACID does not mean every transaction is authorized.
ACID concerns database transaction properties.
Isolation levels define what interactions concurrent transactions can observe.
Common isolation concepts include dirty reads.
Common isolation concepts include non-repeatable reads.
Common isolation concepts include phantom reads.
Database systems implement isolation using mechanisms such as locks and multi-version concurrency control.
MVCC stands for Multi-Version Concurrency Control.
The same named isolation level can have implementation details that differ between systems.
Applications requiring precise concurrency behavior should understand the target database.
A race condition can occur at the database level.
Two clients can read the same state and attempt conflicting updates.
Transactions can help.
Constraints can help.
Atomic SQL operations can help.
Locks can help.
A transaction should usually remain as short as practical.
Long transactions can retain locks or versions.
Long transactions can increase contention.
Database locks coordinate concurrent access.
A shared lock and exclusive lock provide different access semantics according to database implementation.
Row-level locking can limit contention compared with table-level locking.
Lock behavior is database-specific.
Deadlocks can occur even when each individual transaction is valid.
A database can detect deadlocks.
Applications may need bounded retry logic for transactions aborted by deadlock.
Not every database error should be retried.
Syntax errors should not be retried endlessly.
Authentication failures should not be retried endlessly.
Transient deadlocks can justify retry.
Transient connectivity failures can justify retry depending on operation semantics.
Retry behavior should depend on failure category.
A client losing its connection after sending COMMIT can face an ambiguous result.
The transaction may have committed even if the client did not receive confirmation.
Retrying an ambiguous operation can duplicate application effects.
Application operations can use unique identifiers or idempotency mechanisms when duplicate execution must be prevented.
Database transaction boundaries and application operation boundaries should be designed together.
SQL injection occurs when untrusted input changes intended SQL syntax.
SQL injection is a security vulnerability.
Prepared statements separate SQL structure from bound values.
A parameter placeholder represents a value.
A parameter placeholder generally cannot replace arbitrary SQL syntax such as a table name or ORDER BY keyword.
Dynamic identifiers require separate validation and construction.
Prepared statements do not automatically make dynamic identifiers safe.
An allowlist can validate permitted dynamic table or column names.
Prepared statements help prevent SQL injection.
Prepared statements do not provide authorization.
Prepared statements do not validate business rules.
Prepared statements do not automatically improve every query's performance.
Security properties and performance properties are different.
A database account should follow least privilege.
An application should not connect as the database root administrator merely because doing so is convenient.
A web application should receive only required database permissions.
A service requiring SELECT and INSERT should not automatically receive DROP permission.
Database credentials are secrets.
Database credentials should not be logged.
Configuration containing database credentials should have appropriate filesystem permissions.
Credential rotation should be possible.
Different services can use different database identities.
Separate identities improve accountability and least privilege.
Database authentication proves identity according to the database authentication mechanism.
Database authorization determines permitted database operations.
Authentication success does not grant unlimited authority.
GRANT provides privileges according to database-specific SQL.
REVOKE removes privileges according to database-specific SQL.
Privilege syntax varies among database systems.
Permissions should be explicit.
A database user named root and the Linux root user are not automatically the same identity.
PAM stands for Pluggable Authentication Modules on Linux.
Some database systems can integrate with PAM through authentication plugins or modules.
PAM authentication behavior depends on configuration.
Changing an authentication plugin can affect database login behavior.
Authentication plugins should be evaluated according to security and operational requirements.
A database driver connects an application to a database.
PHP provides database interfaces including PDO.
PDO stands for PHP Data Objects.
PDO provides a consistent PHP interface for supported database drivers.
PDO does not make different databases semantically identical.
A PDO driver implements communication with a specific database system.
PDO can use prepared statements.
PDO exceptions can report database failures according to configuration.
Application code should handle database exceptions.
An exception should not automatically be shown directly to an untrusted web user.
Protected logs can contain diagnostic detail according to logging policy.
User-facing errors can remain bounded.
MariaDB originated as a fork of MySQL.
MySQL and MariaDB share substantial historical compatibility.
MySQL and MariaDB are not identical.
Features and behavior can diverge between MySQL and MariaDB versions.
Compatibility should be verified for the actual feature being used.
A query working in MySQL is not absolute proof it behaves identically in every MariaDB version.
A query working in MariaDB is not absolute proof it behaves identically in every MySQL version.
Database-specific documentation should be consulted when behavior matters.
Version information matters.
Server version and client-library version are different.
Protocol compatibility and SQL feature compatibility are different.
A database connection can succeed while a particular SQL feature is unsupported.
A database can exist while the authenticated user lacks permission to use it.
Connection success is only one stage.
A database connection typically involves network or local transport.
The client locates the server.
The transport connection is established.
Authentication occurs.
A database or schema context can be selected.
SQL statements can then execute according to permissions.
Failure can occur at each stage.
DNS failure is not SQL failure.
TCP refusal is not authentication failure.
Authentication failure is not authorization failure.
Authorization failure is not SQL syntax failure.
SQL syntax failure is not constraint failure.
Constraint failure is not empty result.
Empty result is not database failure.
Keeping these states separate improves diagnosis.
A database server can listen on a TCP socket.
A database server can use a Unix domain socket.
A Unix domain socket and a TCP connection are different transports.
A local socket does not require IP routing.
Network-exposed databases increase attack surface.
A database should not be exposed publicly without a defined requirement and appropriate controls.
A web application and database on the same host can communicate locally.
A database on a separate host requires network connectivity.
Firewall rules should permit only required access.
TLS can protect database network connections when supported and correctly configured.
Database TLS and application HTTP TLS are separate connections.
Protecting browser-to-web-server traffic does not automatically encrypt web-server-to-database traffic.
A database backup should be restorable.
A backup file existing does not prove restoration works.
Logical backups represent database objects and data in a portable logical form according to tooling.
Physical backups copy database storage structures according to database-specific procedures.
Logical and physical backups have different properties.
Backup procedures should follow the database engine's requirements.
mysqldump is a logical backup utility associated with MySQL-compatible systems.
MariaDB provides compatible or related backup tools depending on version.
Backup tools should match the database system and version.
Backups should receive access controls.
Backup retention should follow policy.
Encryption can protect backup confidentiality.
A backup should have an identified creation time.
A backup should have an identified source.
Recovery Point Objective is commonly abbreviated RPO.
RPO describes acceptable data-loss interval according to continuity planning.
Recovery Time Objective is commonly abbreviated RTO.
RTO describes acceptable restoration time according to continuity planning.
RPO and RTO are operational requirements.
Database architecture should support required recovery objectives.
Replication copies database changes to other database instances according to replication design.
Replication is not the same as backup.
A replica can improve read scaling.
A replica does not automatically provide historical recovery.
Reading from a lagging replica can return older state.
Applications requiring current state should understand replica consistency.
Primary and replica terminology describes replication roles.
Automatic failover can promote a replica.
Failover should preserve data consistency requirements.
A database failover can create a period of unavailability.
Connection pools can retain connections to a failed database instance.
Failover-aware clients should handle stale connections.
Connection pooling reuses database connections.
Opening a new database connection for every tiny operation can add overhead.
A connection pool has finite capacity.
Connections should be returned to the pool.
Leaked connections can exhaust the pool.
Long transactions can monopolize pooled connections.
Pool size should reflect database and application capacity.
A larger pool is not automatically faster.
Too many database connections can overload the database server.
Backpressure can prevent application request volume from overwhelming the database.
Database connection errors should be bounded.
A database outage should not cause infinite memory growth in request queues.
An application should define behavior when persistence is unavailable.
Some operations may fail.
Some read operations may use safe cached data according to architecture.
Security-critical writes should not be silently discarded.
A failed database write should not be reported as successful.
Persistence confirmation should match the actual database result.
A successful SQL statement can affect zero rows.
An UPDATE affecting zero rows can mean no rows matched.
An UPDATE affecting zero rows can mean values were already equal depending on database reporting behavior.
Affected-row semantics can vary by database and driver configuration.
Applications should understand the driver's affected-row definition.
A DELETE affecting zero rows is not necessarily an error.
An INSERT normally creates a row or fails according to constraints and statement semantics.
Database-specific upsert syntax can insert or update according to conflicts.
MySQL and MariaDB support INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE.
Other databases can use different upsert syntax.
Portable applications should isolate database-specific SQL where practical.
AUTO_INCREMENT is used by MySQL and MariaDB for automatically generated numeric values.
Deleted identifiers can create gaps.
Rolled-back operations can create gaps depending on database behavior.
Concurrent inserts can create observed ordering differences.
COUNT(*) should be used when an actual row count is required.
Generated identifiers should not be reused merely to eliminate gaps.
Timestamps should have defined semantics.
A created_at field can represent creation time.
An updated_at field can represent modification time.
The database and application should agree on timezone behavior.
Local display time can be derived separately.
A timestamp column does not automatically prove when a real-world event occurred.
It proves what the system recorded according to its clock and transaction behavior.
Database timestamps and application timestamps can differ if different clocks are used.
One authoritative timestamp source can reduce ambiguity where required.
MySQL and MariaDB character sets define text encoding.
Collations define comparison and sorting behavior.
Character set and collation are different concepts.
A collation can be case-sensitive.
A collation can be case-insensitive.
A collation can have language-specific comparison behavior.
String equality can therefore depend on collation.
Identifier equality in application logic should not blindly assume database collation behavior.
Unicode can represent visually similar characters using different code points.
Normalization can matter for identifiers.
Text comparison rules should be explicit when identity depends on them.
Database schemas evolve.
A migration changes database structure or data according to a controlled procedure.
Migrations should have defined ordering.
A migration should not run twice when it is not designed to be repeatable.
Migration state should be recorded.
Application code and schema versions should remain compatible.
Deployment order matters.
Backward-compatible schema changes can simplify rolling deployments.
Dropping a column is more destructive than adding an optional column.
Changing a column type can lose or transform data.
A production migration should not be improvised blindly.
DDL can acquire locks.
DDL can affect availability.
Large migrations can take substantial time.
Existing data should be checked before enforcing a new constraint.
Data cleanup and constraint introduction can be separate stages.
SQL views provide stored query definitions according to database capabilities.
A view can simplify repeated queries.
A view does not necessarily store its own copy of data.
A materialized view stores derived results in systems supporting that feature.
Materialized views require refresh behavior.
MariaDB and MySQL view capabilities differ from systems with native materialized views.
A stored procedure contains database-side procedural logic.
A trigger executes database-defined behavior in response to defined events.
Triggers can enforce or automate behavior.
Database-side logic should be documented.
An INSERT can cause additional changes through triggers.
A DELETE can cause additional changes through triggers or cascades.
Affected behavior should be understood before destructive operations.
Triggers should not be assumed absent.
Database events or schedulers can execute work independently of application requests according to database features.
Operational diagnosis should consider database-side automation when unexplained changes occur.
A query should be deterministic when deterministic results are required.
ORDER BY should define ordering.
Functions used in queries can introduce nondeterminism.
Current-time functions depend on time.
Random functions produce varying values.
Queries depending on collation can vary.
Queries depending on transaction isolation can observe different concurrent state.
A value stored in a database remains data.
Storing HTML in a database does not make it safe HTML.
Storing shell commands in a database does not make them safe to execute.
Storing JSON in a database does not prove the JSON is valid unless validated or constrained.
Stored data should be validated again when crossing into a new interpretation context where required.
Persistent data can remain malicious.
A database is not a trust sanitizer.
Database records can contain historical information.
Historical data should not automatically override current state.
A history table can preserve previous values.
An audit table can preserve events.
A current-state table can represent active state.
Selecting every historical row and choosing an answer by textual similarity can produce incorrect conclusions.
Queries should encode the actual state relationship.
A current flag can identify active records according to schema design.
A valid_from and valid_to interval can represent temporal validity.
A status column can represent lifecycle state.
The application should not guess current state merely from the largest identifier.
The application should not guess current state merely from the newest-looking string.
Authority should be represented explicitly.
Database normalization can preserve facts without conflating them.
One table can represent entities.
Data modeling should reflect what the system needs to know.
A relational database can preserve evidence.
An event record can have a timestamp.
An event record can have a type.
An event record can have a source.
An event record can have a payload.
An event record can have integrity metadata.
Event records should not be silently rewritten when history must be permanent.
Lifecycle changes can be represented as new state or status rather than deletion.
Deletion policy should follow governing requirements.
Soft deletion marks records as deleted without physically removing them.
Soft deletion and permanent historical retention are different concepts.
A soft-deleted record can still be physically deleted later depending on system design.
Terminology should match actual retention guarantees.
Database audit records should be append-oriented when historical integrity requires it.
Application permissions can prohibit UPDATE and DELETE on append-only data paths.
Cryptographic chaining can provide additional tamper evidence when required.
A hash chain links records through cryptographic digests.
Changing an earlier record can invalidate later hash relationships.
A hash chain provides tamper evidence according to its construction.
Off-system replication or signatures can strengthen evidence according to architecture.
Database security should account for administrator authority.
An administrator can have power beyond application users.
Backups can preserve evidence after primary database modification.
Audit systems should define their threat model.
SQL should express what data is required.
Application code should express application behavior.
Security policy should define authority.
These responsibilities can reinforce each other without being confused.
A database is successful when it performs its defined storage and query responsibilities.
A database does not need to contain every form of application state.
A JSON file can be better for small configuration.
A relational database can be better for structured concurrent records.
An append-only text file can be appropriate for simple logs.
A binary format can be appropriate for compact protocol data.
Persistence mechanisms should be chosen deliberately.
SQL is powerful because relational operations can express relationships directly.
Power does not require unnecessary complexity.
A small schema can be correct.
A small database can be useful.
Indexes should follow actual queries.
Transactions should follow actual atomicity requirements.
Constraints should follow actual integrity requirements.
Permissions should follow actual authority.
Database engineering should remain evidence-driven.
Measure query behavior before optimizing.
Verify constraints rather than assuming application code enforced them.
Verify transaction boundaries rather than assuming atomicity.
Verify permissions using the actual database account.
Verify database version before assuming feature support.
Verify backup restoration rather than assuming backup success.
A database returning zero rows is evidence of an empty result for that query.
It is not evidence that the database is broken.
The meaning of zero rows depends on the query.
A query asks a specific question of stored data.
A correct answer can be empty.
A database error is not an answer to the query.
A database timeout is not an empty answer.
A database connection failure is not an empty answer.
A constraint failure is not an empty answer.
Precise failure classification is part of correct database engineering.
SQL provides a structured language for relational data.
The database provides storage, integrity mechanisms, concurrency control, and query execution.
The application provides domain behavior.
Security provides authority boundaries.
Networking provides communication when the database is remote.
The operating system provides the execution environment.
Each layer has a defined responsibility.
Correct systems preserve those boundaries.
Git is a distributed version control system.
Version control records changes to files over time.
Git tracks snapshots of project content.
A Git repository contains version-control history and metadata.
A repository is not the same thing as a working directory.
The working tree contains files currently checked out for editing.
The Git index contains the proposed content for the next commit.
The repository contains committed history.
The working tree, index, and committed repository are distinct states.
A file can differ between the index and the current commit.
Understanding these states is fundamental to Git.
git status reports information about the current working tree and index.
git diff normally shows selected differences involving the working tree.
git diff --staged shows changes staged for the next commit.
git add places selected content into the index.
git commit creates a new commit from the staged snapshot.
A commit does not automatically include every modified file.
Only content staged for the commit is included according to Git operation.
An untracked file is not automatically part of a commit.
A tracked file is a file Git knows through repository history or the index.
An untracked file exists in the working tree but is not currently tracked.
git add can begin tracking a new file.
git rm stages removal of a tracked file.
Deleting a tracked file from the filesystem and committing the deletion removes it from later snapshots.
Deleting a file in a later commit does not erase it from earlier Git history.
Historical content does not automatically represent current project state.
A README in an older commit describes the project according to that historical revision.
A sequence of historical versions does not mean all versions are currently active.
Current state and historical state are different concepts.
A commit is identified by an object identifier.
Git object identifiers are derived from cryptographic hashes.
Modern Git can support different repository object hash algorithms depending on repository format.
SHA-1 has historically been Git's primary object hash algorithm.
A Git object identifier identifies repository content and object metadata according to Git's object model.
A commit hash is not an arbitrary version number.
A commit identifier is not an ABI version.
A commit identifier is not an application version.
A commit identifier is not a protocol version.
Different identifiers answer different questions.
A commit normally contains a reference to one or more parent commits.
A root commit has no parent commit.
A normal linear commit has one parent.
A merge commit commonly has multiple parents.
A commit records author information.
A commit records timestamps.
A commit records a commit message.
A commit message explains a change according to developer convention.
A commit message is not executable code.
A commit message does not override the actual committed content.
Repository evidence should be interpreted according to what it actually establishes.
Git objects include blobs.
Git objects include trees.
Git objects include commits.
A blob stores file content.
A blob does not inherently store the filename.
A commit points to a tree representing a project snapshot.
A branch is a movable reference to a commit.
A branch is not a separate copy of every repository file.
Creating a branch is normally inexpensive because the branch begins as a reference.
A branch name identifies a line of development by reference.
The commit graph represents history.
A branch moves when new commits are made on that branch.
The branch name is not part of the commit's immutable content.
A commit can become unreachable from ordinary references.
HEAD identifies the currently checked-out branch or commit context.
HEAD commonly refers symbolically to the current branch.
Detached HEAD means HEAD refers directly to a commit rather than a normal local branch reference.
Detached HEAD is not inherently an error.
Commits can be created while HEAD is detached.
Such commits can become difficult to find if no persistent reference is created.
A branch can preserve a detached-history commit by referencing it.
git branch lists or manipulates branches according to options.
git switch can change branches.
git switch can create a new branch.
git checkout historically performs branch switching and other operations.
git checkout has multiple behaviors.
Commands should be understood before destructive use.
Git can refuse a branch switch when local changes would be overwritten.
That refusal protects uncommitted work.
Uncommitted work should not be discarded merely to make a command succeed.
git log displays commit history according to selected traversal and formatting.
git show displays information about Git objects, commonly commits.
git show on a commit can display its metadata and patch.
git diff compares selected Git states.
A diff shows changes between two selected states.
A diff is not necessarily the complete project.
A patch describes changes.
Git fundamentally stores snapshots even though differences are commonly displayed as patches.
git blame associates lines with commits according to repository history.
git blame does not establish why a line is correct.
git blame does not establish who currently owns a subsystem.
git blame identifies historical line attribution according to Git's algorithm and available history.
Renames and code movement can complicate historical attribution.
History is evidence, not automatic authority.
The current source determines current implementation.
Project policy can determine which current source is authoritative.
Documentation can explain intent.
Runtime execution can provide current operational evidence.
Git history can explain how current state developed.
These evidence sources should not be conflated.
Divergence means each branch contains commits not contained in the other.
A merge combines histories.
git merge integrates another history into the current branch according to Git's merge algorithm.
A three-way merge uses common ancestry to combine changes.
A merge can succeed automatically.
An automatic merge does not prove the resulting program is correct.
A merge can produce syntactically valid but semantically incorrect code.
A merge conflict occurs when Git cannot automatically reconcile selected changes.
A merge conflict requires resolution.
Conflict markers can identify conflicting content in working files.
