A cryptographic signature can verify that a tag was signed by a key.
Cryptographic identity and organizational authority are different.
A release can reference a commit.
A release can contain built artifacts.
Source commits and built artifacts are different objects.
A binary should be traceable to its source revision when reproducibility or auditability matters.
Build metadata can record a commit identifier.
A commit identifier embedded in a binary can help identify source provenance.
An embedded commit identifier does not prove the binary was honestly built from that commit.
A reproducible build produces identical output from defined identical inputs and environment according to the build specification.
Source integrity and build integrity are separate concerns.
.gitignore specifies patterns for intentionally untracked files.
.gitignore does not remove already tracked files from history.
Adding a secret file to .gitignore after committing it does not remove the secret from Git history.
A tracked file remains tracked even if a later .gitignore pattern matches it.
git rm --cached can remove a path from the index while preserving a working-tree copy according to command usage.
Credentials exposed in Git should be rotated when required.
History rewriting can remove sensitive data from ordinary repository history.
History rewriting does not guarantee every existing clone or cache has lost the old data.
Secret rotation is more reliable than assuming every copy was erased.
.gitignore should exclude generated files when appropriate.
.gitignore should exclude local configuration when appropriate.
.gitignore should exclude secrets when those secrets should never be versioned.
Repository policy should define which artifacts belong under version control.
Build output can often be reproduced and therefore can often be excluded.
Generated artifacts can be committed when project requirements require them.
Dependency directories can be excluded when dependencies are reproducibly restored.
Rules should follow project architecture.
.gitattributes defines path-specific Git attributes.
.gitattributes can control text handling.
.gitattributes can control line-ending normalization.
.gitattributes can identify binary files.
.gitattributes can configure diff behavior.
.gitattributes can configure merge behavior.
.gitattributes can configure export behavior.
Line endings matter for cross-platform development.
LF is a line-feed line ending commonly used on Unix-like systems.
CRLF is a carriage-return plus line-feed line ending commonly used on Windows.
Git can normalize line endings according to configuration and attributes.
Unexpected line-ending conversion can make entire files appear modified.
Line-ending conversion can affect scripts.
Line-ending conversion can affect byte-sensitive files.
Binary files should not undergo text line-ending conversion.
A repository supporting Windows and Linux should define text-file behavior deliberately.
Executable script line endings can affect execution on Unix-like systems.
A shell script containing CRLF can fail unexpectedly in some environments.
A Windows batch file can conventionally use CRLF.
Cross-platform projects should distinguish platform-specific files from platform-independent content.
A Git repository can contain source for multiple platforms.
Branches do not need to correspond to operating systems.
Platform-independent interfaces can remain shared.
Git does not understand programming-language semantics by default.
Git primarily tracks content.
Git can merge text without understanding whether the merged C code compiles.
Git can merge JSON without understanding application schema requirements.
Git can merge a module manifest without understanding ABI compatibility.
Tests and build systems provide semantic validation beyond Git.
Version control records changes.
Version control does not prove correctness.
A clean git status does not mean the software is correct.
A clean git status does not mean the software compiled.
A clean git status does not mean tests passed.
A clean git status does not mean deployment succeeded.
A clean git status does not mean production matches the repository.
Each claim requires its own evidence.
git status should be checked before risky history operations.
Uncommitted changes can be lost through destructive commands.
git restore can replace working-tree or index content according to options.
git reset changes references or index state according to mode.
git reset --soft moves a branch reference while preserving index and working-tree changes according to its semantics.
git reset --mixed changes the branch reference and resets index state while normally preserving working-tree changes.
git reset --hard changes the branch reference and resets index and working tree.
git reset --hard can destroy uncommitted work.
git clean removes untracked files according to options.
git clean -n can preview what would be removed.
Previewing destructive operations reduces mistakes.
A file not tracked by Git may have no recovery path through Git.
Git protects committed history better than uncommitted files.
A stash stores selected working-tree and index changes for later reapplication.
git stash can temporarily clear local modifications.
Stashes can conflict when reapplied.
Stashed work can become difficult to understand if retained without context.
A commit is usually a stronger long-term record than an unnamed stash.
Commits should represent coherent changes when practical.
A coherent commit has one understandable purpose.
Small coherent commits simplify review.
Small coherent commits simplify debugging.
Small coherent commits simplify selective reversal.
A useful commit message should identify what changed.
A useful commit message can explain why the change was necessary.
A useful commit message should not claim tests passed when they did not.
Commit history should remain factual.
A commit can be reverted.
git revert creates a new commit that applies inverse changes from a selected commit according to Git semantics.
Revert preserves history.
Reset moves history references and can rewrite visible branch history.
Revert and reset are different operations.
Revert is often appropriate for undoing a published change.
A revert does not erase the original commit.
The revert records that its effect was later reversed.
History should preserve what happened rather than pretending an earlier event never occurred when auditability matters.
A reverted vulnerability fix can reintroduce the vulnerability.
A reverted database migration can require additional handling.
Version-control reversal and operational rollback are different.
Git bisect helps locate a commit associated with a behavioral change.
git bisect performs a binary search through commit history using good and bad classifications.
A good commit is one known not to exhibit the investigated behavior according to the test.
A bad commit is one known to exhibit the investigated behavior according to the test.
Git bisect can automate testing when a deterministic test command exists.
A bisect result identifies a commit where behavior changed according to the test history.
A behavioral regression can arise from interactions between multiple components.
The first commit where a test fails is strong evidence about the change boundary.
Regression testing and version history complement each other.
git grep searches tracked repository content.
git grep can locate text quickly.
Finding text in a repository does not establish that the text is current authority.
A search can find historical terminology in current documentation.
A search can find examples.
A search can find tests.
A search result must be interpreted according to file role and context.
A header can define interfaces.
A configuration file can define runtime configuration.
A test can define expected behavior.
A changelog can define history.
A build artifact can reflect compiled source.
These file roles differ.
A README containing ABI history can explain previous ABI versions.
A header defining the current ABI constant can establish the compile-time ABI contract when the architecture assigns it that role.
The values 1.3, 1.4, and 1.5 appearing in repository history do not mean the current ABI is "1.3 -> 1.4 -> 1.5."
If the authoritative current definition states ABI 1.5, the current ABI is 1.5.
History explains how the system reached 1.5.
History does not replace 1.5 with the entire sequence.
A changelog records historical changes.
A changelog can contain old version numbers.
A changelog should not be queried as though every version mentioned were simultaneously current.
A README can contain examples from older releases.
Documentation can become stale.
Deployed binaries can become stale relative to source.
Current must always be defined relative to the object being examined.
Current deployed source means the source revision corresponding to the deployed artifact when known.
Current runtime version means the version actually executing.
These can differ.
A production system can run an older commit.
A development branch can contain newer code than production.
A remote repository can contain commits not deployed anywhere.
A local repository can contain commits not pushed anywhere.
Evidence should specify which state it describes.
Git supports cryptographic signing mechanisms according to configuration and version.
A valid commit signature proves the commit signature verifies under the indicated key.
A valid commit signature does not prove the code is safe.
A valid commit signature does not prove tests passed.
A valid commit signature does not prove deployment occurred.
A valid commit signature does not prove the signer had organizational authority unless that authority is separately established.
Signature verification and authorization remain different.
Git hooks execute scripts at defined Git lifecycle points.
Client-side hooks can run before commits.
Client-side hooks can run before pushes.
Server-side hooks can validate received updates.
Server-side enforcement provides stronger centralized policy for remote updates.
A pre-commit hook can run formatting or checks.
A server hook can reject prohibited reference changes.
A hook succeeding does not prove every possible test passed unless the hook actually executes those tests.
Automated pipelines can build and test repository revisions.
Continuous Integration is commonly abbreviated CI.
Continuous Delivery or Continuous Deployment is commonly abbreviated CD depending on workflow.
CI commonly builds and tests changes.
CD can prepare or perform releases and deployments.
Git itself does not provide the entire CI/CD process.
External systems react to Git events.
A triggered pipeline is not the same as a successful pipeline.
A successful build is not the same as successful tests.
Successful tests are not the same as successful deployment.
Successful deployment is not the same as healthy runtime operation.
Each lifecycle stage requires evidence.
A repository can use submodules.
A Git submodule records a reference to a commit in another repository.
A submodule is not ordinary copied directory content.
Updating a submodule reference changes the parent repository's recorded commit pointer.
Submodules support explicit dependency revision control.
Dependencies should use the simplest mechanism satisfying project requirements.
A repository can use tags to identify releases.
Semantic Versioning commonly uses MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH notation.
Git does not enforce Semantic Versioning.
Git does not automatically know whether a change is major, minor, or patch.
Project policy determines release versioning.
A Git tag can reflect project release policy.
Version policy remains outside Git's core object model.
An ABI version can evolve independently from application release version.
Application version 2.0 can use ABI 1.5.
Application version 2.1 can still use ABI 1.5.
A later application release does not automatically require a later ABI.
Git history can record an ABI change.
Git does not decide ABI compatibility.
The module host and interface contract decide ABI compatibility.
Version numbers should not be inferred from unrelated version fields.
A repository can contain multiple version numbers simultaneously for legitimate reasons.
One can identify module ABI.
One can identify schema version.
One can identify file-format version.
Each version should be interpreted according to its field and authority.
Searching for the word "version" and returning every value is not correct reasoning.
Searching for "ABI" and returning every historical ABI mention is not correct reasoning.
Historical ABI values are irrelevant unless the user asks for ABI history or compatibility.
"What ABI versions have existed?" asks a historical question.
The answer can include previous ABI versions when supported by history.
"What changed from ABI 1.4 to 1.5?" asks for comparison between historical interface revisions.
The answer requires evidence describing those changes.
Current-state questions and historical questions require different retrieval strategies.
Current interface declarations should outrank historical narrative for current interface questions.
Historical logs should remain available for historical questions.
Deleting history merely to prevent bad retrieval loses useful evidence.
Better retrieval distinguishes source role.
History should remain history.
Current state should remain current state.
The retrieval layer should understand the distinction.
Git is especially useful because it preserves both.
Git history allows engineers to examine previous states without pretending those states are still active.
A previous commit can be checked out.
Checking out a previous commit changes the working tree to represent that historical state.
Returning to the current branch restores its selected current snapshot.
Time travel through history is an inspection capability.
Git reflog records local movements of references according to Git configuration and retention.
Reflog can help recover commits after accidental reference changes.
Reflog is not the same as shared commit history.
Reflog entries can expire.
Reflog should not be treated as permanent archival history.
A successful git fsck does not prove source-code correctness.
Git garbage collection performs repository maintenance.
git gc can compress objects and remove eligible unreachable data according to retention behavior.
Git object retention should not be mistaken for a permanent backup guarantee.
Important historical records should remain reachable through appropriate references and repository policy.
A Git repository is a development record.
Repository history can support auditability.
Repository history can support debugging.
Repository history can support release management.
Repository history can support source provenance.
Repository history should not be silently rewritten when governing policy requires permanent historical evidence.
Git records source and tracked-file history.
Operational logs record runtime events.
A runtime failure can occur without any source change.
A deployment timestamp is not necessarily a commit timestamp.
A log timestamp is not necessarily a deployment timestamp.
Incident timelines should distinguish these events.
A source repository can prove that a particular source state existed in repository history.
It does not automatically prove that state executed on a particular machine.
Build evidence is required for build claims.
Runtime evidence is required for runtime claims.
Git should be used for what Git actually proves.
git remote -v can display configured remote locations.
Remote configuration is local repository state.
A remote URL does not prove current network availability.
A remote URL does not prove authentication will succeed.
A remote URL does not prove the repository still exists.
git fetch provides stronger evidence that remote communication and Git protocol operations succeeded at that time.
A failed fetch can result from networking.
A failed fetch can result from authentication.
A failed fetch can result from authorization.
A failed fetch can result from repository absence.
A failed fetch can result from protocol or server failure.
The actual error should be preserved.
A Git network failure is not a source-code failure.
