Correcting documentation reduces future ambiguity.
Deleting legitimate historical information is not necessary when it is clearly labeled as historical.
History and current state can coexist safely when their roles are explicit.
Git exists in part to make that coexistence practical.
Version control preserves the past without requiring the past to masquerade as the present.
Software testing evaluates whether software behaves according to defined expectations.
A test provides evidence about behavior under tested conditions.
A passing test does not prove that software has no defects.
A failing test provides evidence that an expected condition was not satisfied.
Testing and debugging are different activities.
