A program linking successfully does not prove that it works correctly.
A program starting successfully does not prove that it works correctly.
A module loading successfully does not prove that the module works correctly.
A network connection succeeding does not prove that the protocol works correctly.
A database connection succeeding does not prove that a query is correct.
A query executing successfully does not prove that its result is correct.
A function returning a value does not prove that the value is correct.
Each claim requires evidence appropriate to that claim.
A test case is a defined set of conditions used to evaluate behavior.
A test suite is a collection of test cases.
A test runner executes tests and reports results.
A test fixture establishes state required by a test.
A test oracle determines the expected result against which observed behavior is compared.
A deterministic test produces the same expected result when executed under the same defined conditions.
Nondeterministic tests are harder to diagnose.
A flaky test passes and fails inconsistently without an intentional change to the tested behavior.
Flaky tests reduce confidence in test results.
A flaky test should be investigated.
Repeatedly rerunning a flaky test until it passes does not repair the underlying problem.
A test should fail for the behavior it is intended to test.
A test failing because of unrelated infrastructure provides weaker evidence about the target behavior.
Test environments should control relevant dependencies where practical.
Tests should minimize irrelevant dependencies.
A unit test evaluates a small unit of software behavior.
A unit is commonly a function, method, class, module component, or similarly bounded piece of behavior.
A unit test should isolate the behavior being evaluated where practical.
A unit test does not necessarily require complete system startup.
A unit test can test valid input.
A unit test can test invalid input.
A unit test can test boundary conditions.
A unit test can test error handling.
A unit test can test state transitions.
A unit test can test deterministic transformations.
Unit tests are useful for fast local feedback.
Unit tests do not replace integration tests.
An integration test evaluates interactions between components.
An integration test can test an application and database together.
An integration test can test a host and module together.
An integration test can test a client and server together.
Integration testing can discover problems that isolated unit tests cannot.
Correct components can interact incorrectly.
Interface assumptions can disagree.
Serialization can disagree.
Version expectations can disagree.
Integration tests evaluate these boundaries.
A system test evaluates a complete or substantially complete system.
A system test can exercise behavior through public interfaces.
A system test can include multiple services.
A system test can include persistent storage.
A system test can include platform-specific behavior.
System tests provide broader behavioral evidence.
System tests can be slower and harder to diagnose than unit tests.
Different test levels answer different questions.
An end-to-end test evaluates a complete operational path.
An end-to-end test begins from an external or high-level entry point and verifies the resulting system behavior.
An end-to-end test can verify a request from client through server to persistence and back.
An end-to-end test can verify a module from discovery through activation and behavior.
An end-to-end test can verify a transaction from submission through final acceptance.
A smoke test checks a small set of essential functionality.
A smoke test determines whether a build is sufficiently functional for deeper testing.
A smoke test is intentionally limited.
Passing a smoke test does not mean every feature works.
Terminology around smoke and sanity testing can vary.
The important property is the defined scope of the test.
A regression test checks that previously working behavior remains correct after changes.
A regression occurs when a change breaks behavior that previously worked.
A defect repair should often receive a regression test.
A regression test preserves evidence of the expected repaired behavior.
Without a regression test, the same defect can return unnoticed.
A regression suite should include important historical failures.
Historical defects can become future test cases.
Testing can preserve lessons learned from failures.
Negative testing verifies rejection of invalid, prohibited, unsupported, or unauthorized behavior.
Both positive and negative tests are important.
A parser should be tested with malformed input.
Authentication should be tested with valid credentials.
Authentication should be tested with invalid credentials.
Authorization should be tested with insufficient authority.
A module loader should be tested with a compatible module.
A module loader should be tested with an incompatible module.
A protocol should be tested with supported messages.
A protocol should be tested with unsupported messages.
Security boundaries require negative testing.
A system that accepts valid input correctly but also accepts prohibited input is not correct.
Boundary testing evaluates values at and around defined limits.
If a buffer permits 256 bytes, useful tests can include 0 bytes.
Useful tests can include 1 byte.
Useful tests can include 256 bytes.
Useful tests can include 257 bytes.
The exact boundaries depend on the requirement.
Off-by-one errors commonly occur near boundaries.
Numeric limits should be tested.
String-length limits should be tested.
Collection-size limits should be tested.
Message-size limits should be tested.
Timeout boundaries should be tested.
Date boundaries should be tested where relevant.
Version boundaries should be tested.
Boundary tests should derive from actual defined constraints.
Equivalence partitioning groups inputs expected to behave similarly.
Representative values can be selected from each partition.
Equivalence partitioning reduces redundant testing while preserving useful coverage.
A property describes behavior that should remain true across a class of inputs.
Sorting output should preserve the input elements according to the sorting contract.
Serialization followed by deserialization can preserve values according to the format contract.
A parser should reject input exceeding defined maximum size.
Generated tests still require meaningful properties.
Random input without meaningful assertions provides limited evidence.
Fuzz testing supplies generated or mutated input to discover failures.
Fuzzing is especially useful for parsers.
Fuzzing is useful for network protocol handlers.
Fuzzing is useful for memory-unsafe software.
A fuzzer can discover crashes.
A fuzzer can discover assertion failures.
A fuzzer can discover memory errors when combined with instrumentation.
The triggering input should be minimized where useful.
A reproducible fuzz failure can become a regression test.
A crash does prove that execution reached an incorrect or unsupported state when crashing is outside the specification.
Static analysis can identify suspicious code patterns.
Static analysis can identify some type errors.
Static analysis findings require interpretation.
Dynamic analysis can observe resource behavior.
Dynamic analysis can observe timing behavior.
Dynamic analysis can observe actual input-output behavior.
Code review is a form of human or assisted examination.
Code review can identify logic errors.
Code review can identify unclear assumptions.
Code review can identify security problems.
Code review can identify maintainability problems.
Code review does not replace execution testing.
Different methods detect different classes of defects.
Test coverage measures which code or conditions were exercised according to the selected coverage metric.
Line coverage measures executed source lines.
Statement coverage measures executed statements according to tooling.
Function coverage measures executed functions.
One hundred percent branch coverage does not prove correctness.
A test can execute code without meaningfully verifying it.
Coverage should not become the sole definition of test quality.
Assertions define expected conditions.
An assertion should express a meaningful requirement.
An assertion that always evaluates true provides no useful evidence.
An assertion against the wrong expected value can make incorrect behavior appear correct.
Expected values should derive from requirements.
A test should not derive its expected result using the same defective algorithm being tested when independent verification is required.
Independent test oracles provide stronger evidence.
Golden files contain known expected output.
Golden files are useful for deterministic structured output.
Golden files can become difficult to review when extremely large.
Updating a golden file merely because a test failed can hide a regression.
Expected output should be changed only when the intended behavior actually changed.
Snapshot updates should be reviewed.
Tests should not train developers to approve changes blindly.
A mock replaces a dependency with controlled behavior for testing.
A fake provides a simplified working implementation of a dependency.
Terminology can vary between testing frameworks.
Test doubles allow controlled dependency behavior.
Mocks can make tests too dependent on implementation details when overused.
Changing internal implementation should not require rewriting unrelated tests when behavior remains unchanged.
Tests should protect contracts rather than accidental implementation details.
An interface contract defines expected behavior between components.
Contract tests verify that implementations satisfy interface expectations.
A module ABI is an interface contract.
ABI compatibility can be tested.
A module declaring ABI 1.5 can be tested against a host requiring ABI 1.5.
A module declaring ABI 1.4 should be rejected when the host requires ABI 1.5 and no compatibility mechanism permits it.
The test should verify the actual rejection behavior.
Documentation text is not the runtime ABI contract unless architecture explicitly makes it so.
Tests should use the authoritative interface definition.
A module qualification test can verify manifest presence.
A module qualification test can verify manifest syntax.
A module qualification test can verify module name.
A module qualification test can verify version.
A module qualification test can verify required exports.
A module qualification test can verify activation.
Each stage should be tested independently where practical.
A module activation test should not report activation before initialization succeeds.
A hot-load test should verify the module is usable after loading.
A functional test should additionally verify the module performs its intended function.
Logs and behavior provide complementary evidence.
A module can function correctly while logging incorrectly.
API testing evaluates defined program interfaces.
An API test should verify valid requests.
An API test should verify invalid requests.
An API test should verify response structure.
An API test should verify authorization.
An API test should verify boundary conditions.
HTTP API tests should verify appropriate status codes.
HTTP 200 indicates successful HTTP-level handling according to server response.
HTTP 200 does not automatically mean application data is correct.
HTTP 400 indicates a client request problem according to HTTP application behavior.
HTTP 401 commonly indicates authentication is required or failed according to the application's HTTP authentication semantics.
HTTP 403 commonly indicates the request is understood but not permitted.
HTTP 404 commonly indicates the requested resource was not found.
HTTP 500 indicates a server-side error condition.
Tests should verify both status and relevant response content.
Returning HTTP 200 with an error hidden in arbitrary text can make automated testing harder.
Interfaces should expose machine-readable success and failure states where appropriate.
Database tests should distinguish connectivity from query behavior.
A database authentication test verifies that credentials are accepted.
A database authorization test verifies that required operations are permitted.
A query test verifies SQL behavior.
A constraint test verifies integrity rules.
A transaction test verifies commit and rollback behavior.
A database returning zero rows can be a passing test when zero rows are expected.
A test expecting zero rows should not fail merely because no records were returned.
Database tests should clean up their test data.
Test data should not contaminate production data.
A test database should be isolated where practical.
Test fixtures should create known state.
A test should not pass merely because an unrelated record happens to exist.
Known initial state makes tests reproducible.
Network testing should distinguish layers.
A DNS test verifies name resolution.
A TLS test verifies TLS behavior.
A protocol handshake test verifies application protocol compatibility.
A functional request test verifies actual service behavior.
Ping does not prove application health.
A successful TCP connect does not prove protocol health.
Tests should verify the layer relevant to the requirement.
Timeout behavior should be tested.
Connection-refusal behavior should be tested.
Unexpected peer closure should be tested.
Partial writes should be handled according to API semantics.
Malformed network messages should be tested.
Oversized network messages should be tested.
Wrong protocol versions should be tested.
Wrong network identifiers should be tested.
Wrong peer identity should be tested when identity validation exists.
Reconnect behavior should be tested.
Backoff behavior should be tested when required.
A reconnect test should verify that temporary failure can recover.
A reconnect test should also verify that permanent failure does not create uncontrolled retry loops.
Testing failure behavior is as important as testing normal operation.
Filesystem testing should verify successful reads.
Filesystem testing should verify missing files.
Filesystem testing should verify malformed files.
Filesystem testing should verify maximum supported file sizes.
Filesystem testing should verify path handling.
A writable directory should be tested using the identity that actually performs the write.
Root being able to write does not prove a web-service user can write.
The interactive developer account being able to write does not prove the service account can write.
Permissions should be tested under the operational identity.
A test executed under the wrong identity can produce misleading results.
Code compiling on Windows does not prove it compiles on Linux.
Code compiling on Linux does not prove it compiles on Windows.
Platform-independent architecture requires platform-specific implementation testing.
The same protocol input should produce equivalent protocol-visible results across supported platforms where the specification requires equivalence.
Serialization should be tested across platforms.
Integer widths should be tested.
Byte order should be tested.
File-path assumptions should be tested.
Line endings should be tested where relevant.
Socket behavior should be tested.
Platform abstraction should be tested at its boundary.
Common code should not silently depend on one platform's implementation behavior.
Cross-platform tests provide evidence that abstraction actually works.
A clean build removes dependence on stale build artifacts.
Incremental builds are useful during development.
A clean build provides stronger evidence of build reproducibility.
A build succeeding because an old object file remained is not evidence that current source compiles completely.
Build directories should be cleaned when clean-build evidence is required.
Compiler warnings should be reviewed.
A warning-free build can be a test requirement.
A warning-free build does not prove correctness.
Testing with multiple compilers can improve portability evidence.
Compiler optimization can expose undefined behavior differently.
Sanitizers instrument programs to detect classes of runtime errors.
AddressSanitizer can detect many memory errors.
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer can detect many forms of undefined behavior.
ThreadSanitizer can detect many data races.
A sanitizer not reporting an error does not prove absence of all memory defects.
A sanitizer finding should be investigated.
Memory leak detectors can identify unreleased allocations.
Leak testing should account for deliberate process-lifetime allocations when architecture permits them.
A leak growing with repeated operations is especially important.
Resource testing should include memory.
Resource testing should include sockets.
Resource testing should include threads.
Resource testing should include handles on platforms that use them.
A long-running service should be tested over time.
A soak test runs a system for an extended period.
Soak testing can reveal leaks.
Soak testing can reveal cumulative state errors.
Soak testing can reveal reconnect problems.
Soak testing can reveal timing-dependent failures.
Soak testing can reveal performance degradation.
A soak test should have defined success criteria.
Stress testing pushes a system toward or beyond expected capacity.
Load and stress testing are related but different.
A load test can verify normal operational capacity.
A stress test should observe how the system fails.
Graceful degradation is preferable to uncontrolled corruption.
A server reaching capacity should reject or defer work according to defined behavior.
Performance testing measures timing and throughput characteristics.
Performance tests require controlled conditions for meaningful comparisons.
One fast execution does not prove performance.
One slow execution does not prove a regression.
Repeated measurements provide stronger evidence.
Baseline measurements allow comparison over time.
Performance regression testing detects meaningful degradation.
Performance requirements should specify acceptable thresholds.
A benchmark is not necessarily a correctness test.
Fast incorrect software is still incorrect.
Concurrency testing evaluates behavior when operations occur simultaneously.
Race conditions may not appear during single-threaded tests.
Concurrent tests can repeat operations to increase the chance of exposing races.
A passing concurrency test does not prove absence of races.
Shared state should be tested under contention.
Database transactions should be tested under concurrent access.
File creation should be tested under concurrent access when races matter.
Identity generation should be tested for concurrent invocation if multiple processes can invoke it.
Two simultaneous identity-generation operations should not silently create inconsistent identities when architecture requires one identity.
Atomic operations can simplify concurrency correctness.
Concurrency tests should verify final state.
Merely surviving concurrent execution is not enough when state can become incorrect.
A system can have states such as INITIALIZING.
A system can have states such as ACTIVE.
A system can have states such as DEGRADED.
The exact states depend on architecture.
Tests should verify prohibited transitions.
A component should not report ACTIVE before required qualification.
Recovery transitions should be tested.
Repeated recovery should be tested when relevant.
A missing file should produce the defined missing-file behavior.
A timeout should produce timeout.
A connection refusal should produce connection refusal.
A malformed response should produce malformed-response failure.
An empty result should remain an empty result.
Specific failures should not collapse unnecessarily into "internal service unavailable."
Generic errors destroy diagnostic information.
Tests should verify error preservation.
Tests should verify that user-facing errors remain bounded when security requires it.
Internal logs can preserve more detailed diagnostics.
Security testing verifies security requirements.
Authentication tests verify identity mechanisms.
Input tests verify validation.
Injection tests verify that data cannot become unintended instructions.
CSRF tests verify state-changing request protection where applicable.
Signature tests verify cryptographic validation.
Security testing should include successful authorized behavior.
A security mechanism that blocks everyone is not necessarily correct.
Correct security permits authorized operations and rejects unauthorized operations.
A negative security test should verify rejection.
A positive security test should verify permitted behavior.
A failing security test should lead to investigation.
Removing the security check is not a repair unless requirements explicitly changed.
Backward compatibility means newer software supports defined older interfaces or data according to policy.
Forward compatibility means older or current software can tolerate defined newer information according to policy.
Compatibility should be specified.
Compatibility should not be guessed.
An ABI test should use explicit supported ABI values.
A protocol compatibility test should use explicit protocol versions.
A file-format compatibility test should use defined format versions.
A historical version appearing in documentation does not automatically mean it remains supported.
Support status should come from current policy or interface definition.
A module ABI history of 1.3, 1.4, and 1.5 does not mean a host using ABI 1.5 must accept modules built for all three.
If current policy requires exact ABI 1.5, compatibility testing should verify 1.5 is accepted and unsupported versions are rejected.
Current requirements govern current tests.
Historical requirements remain useful for testing historical releases.
Release testing evaluates a candidate build before release.
A release candidate is a build considered potentially suitable for release.
A release candidate should have an identifiable source revision.
A release candidate should be tested as the actual artifact intended for distribution.
Testing source code alone does not test packaging.
Testing the installer does not automatically test every installed feature.
Installation testing should verify required files.
Installation testing should verify permissions.
Installation testing should verify configuration.
Installation testing should verify startup.
Installation testing should verify uninstallation when supported.
Upgrade testing should verify transitions from supported previous versions.
Upgrade tests should preserve required user data.
Upgrade tests should preserve required configuration.
Upgrade tests should apply required migrations.
Upgrade tests should reject incompatible states according to policy.
Rollback testing should verify supported rollback procedures.
Rollback can require data migration.
Rollback can be impossible after irreversible schema changes.
A test plan describes intended testing scope.
A test plan can identify features.
A test plan can identify risks.
A test plan can identify test levels.
A test plan can identify acceptance criteria.
A test plan can identify exclusions.
A test plan should not claim unperformed testing.
A test report records observed test results.
A test report should identify the environment when relevant.
A test report should identify passes.
A test report should identify failures.
A test report should identify blocked tests.
A test report should identify skipped tests.
Skipped is not passed.
Blocked is not passed.
Not executed is not passed.
Unknown is not passed.
A test result should represent what actually happened.
A known defect does not become correct behavior merely because it is known.
When the defect is repaired, the expected-failure designation should be reconsidered.
Test automation executes repeatable tests using software.
Automation reduces repetitive manual work.
Automation improves consistency.
Automation does not make incorrect tests correct.
Automated tests require maintenance.
A test suite can become stale.
A stale test can enforce obsolete behavior.
Requirements changes can require test changes.
Tests should not be changed merely because implementation disagrees with them.
Tests encode expectations.
A requirement can be wrong or obsolete.
An implementation can be wrong.
Evidence and governing requirements should resolve the discrepancy.
Test-first development writes tests before implementation according to a development method.
TDD uses a cycle commonly described as red, green, refactor.
A failing test is written for required behavior.
Implementation is added until the test passes.
Code is then improved while preserving behavior.
TDD is one development method.
Tests written after implementation can still be valuable.
The important requirement is meaningful verification.
Manual testing is testing performed through human interaction or observation.
Manual testing is useful for exploratory behavior.
Manual testing is useful for interfaces.
Manual testing is useful for unexpected workflows.
Manual testing can be inconsistent.
Important repeatable findings should often become automated regression tests.
Exploratory testing investigates software without restricting every action to predefined cases.
Exploratory testing uses tester judgment.
Exploratory testing can discover unexpected interactions.
Exploratory testing should still record meaningful defects and reproduction steps.
A defect report should describe observed behavior.
A defect report should describe expected behavior.
A defect report should include reproduction steps when known.
A defect report should include logs or evidence when useful.
A defect report should avoid unsupported conclusions about root cause.
"The application returned HTTP 500" is an observation.
Debugging establishes cause.
A minimal reproduction reduces a failure to the smallest practical conditions that still demonstrate it.
Minimal reproductions simplify debugging.
Removing unrelated components can identify boundaries.
If a failure remains without the network, the network is less likely to be necessary to reproduce it.
If a failure disappears when a module is removed, the module or its interaction becomes relevant evidence.
Changing many variables simultaneously makes conclusions weaker.
Tests should control variables.
A baseline can be a known-good configuration.
A baseline can be expected performance.
"Yesterday's build" is less precise than a version or commit identifier.
Reproducibility requires identifying inputs.
Compiler version can matter.
Build flags can matter.
Dependency versions can matter.
Operating system can matter.
Environment variables can matter.
Test data can matter.
Time can matter.
Random seeds can matter.
A reproducible test should control or record relevant variables.
Randomized tests should record the random seed when possible.
A recorded seed can reproduce a failure.
Time-dependent tests should control time where practical.
Tests should not unnecessarily depend on the wall clock.
Sleeping for arbitrary durations can make tests slow and flaky.
Synchronization should observe the condition being waited for when possible.
A test waiting for a service should verify readiness rather than merely sleeping five seconds.
Five seconds can be too short on one machine.
Five seconds can be unnecessarily long on another machine.
Timeouts should still bound waiting.
A test that can wait forever can hang an entire test suite.
Every asynchronous test should have a defined termination condition.
A network connection can be interrupted.
A file can be made unreadable.
A disk-full condition can be simulated where practical.
A malformed message can be supplied.
A timeout can be induced.
Recovery testing verifies behavior after failure.
A failed operation can remain failed when retry would be unsafe.
Recovery should preserve state integrity.
A system recovering availability while corrupting data has not recovered correctly.
Data integrity should be checked after recovery.
Crash testing evaluates abrupt process termination.
A partially written file should not silently become accepted valid state.
Atomic file replacement can be tested by interrupting writes.
Restart testing verifies initialization from existing state.
A program working only from a fresh empty directory is not enough when normal operation resumes saved state.
Corrupted saved state should be tested.
Missing saved state should be tested.
Current saved state should be tested.
Persistence should not depend on accidental memory state from the previous process.
A cold start begins without previously initialized runtime memory.
A warm restart can reuse persistent state but not process memory unless architecture explicitly provides it.
Restart behavior should be deterministic according to persistent inputs.
Data-ingestion testing verifies parsing and storage behavior.
An ingestion test should verify accepted units.
An ingestion test should verify stored units.
Counts should reconcile according to ingestion rules.
If 195 units were processed, 195 stored, and 0 rejected, the counts are internally consistent.
Content retrieval should be tested separately.
Successful retrieval does not prove correct reasoning over retrieved content.
Ingestion, storage, retrieval, ranking, and response generation are separate stages.
Each stage can be tested independently.
A lesson ingestion test can verify the lesson file exists.
A lesson ingestion test can verify the file is readable.
A lesson ingestion test can verify parsing.
A lesson ingestion test can verify unit count.
A lesson ingestion test can verify storage.
A retrieval test can query known facts from the lesson.
A retrieval test should verify the expected fact is returned.
A reasoning test can ask a question requiring combination of multiple stored facts.
A reasoning test should distinguish retrieval failure from reasoning failure.
If the correct source facts were retrieved but the answer was wrong, retrieval succeeded and reasoning failed.
If no facts were retrieved despite matching stored facts, retrieval failed.
If a downstream service was unavailable, infrastructure failed.
These are different failure categories.
A response should not claim lack of knowledge when knowledge exists but retrieval failed.
A response should not claim internal service failure when retrieval returned a valid empty result unless another service actually failed.
Question intent matters.
Tests can evaluate relevance.
A relevance test supplies a question with a known target concept.
The expected retrieval should contain evidence directly related to that concept.
Retrieving a fact about sessions for a question about equality operators is a relevance failure.
Returning "PHP is dynamically typed" to a question asking the difference between == and === is insufficient even though the fact is related to PHP typing.
Related is not the same as responsive.
Answer testing should evaluate factual correctness.
Answer testing should evaluate completeness according to expected scope.
A concise answer can be correct.
A long answer can be wrong.
Answer length is not a correctness metric.
A system should prefer a supported narrow answer over an unsupported elaborate answer.
No speculation means claims should be grounded in available evidence.
Tests can deliberately ask questions not covered by available knowledge.
The expected behavior can be a bounded statement that available information is insufficient.
Inventing an answer should fail such a test.
Knowledge boundaries should be tested.
Source precedence should be tested.
If current authoritative source says ABI 1.5 and historical documentation mentions ABI 1.4, a current-state question should produce ABI 1.5.
If asked for ABI history, historical versions can be returned.
If asked what changed between 1.4 and 1.5, the system should require evidence describing the change.
The same corpus can support different answers depending on question intent.
Retrieval should not treat every matching source as equally authoritative.
Current state and historical state should be distinguishable.
A test can contain intentionally conflicting sources.
Conflicting evidence should not be silently merged into nonsense.
If no authority rule resolves a conflict, the conflict should be reported.
Testing retrieval only with clean unambiguous facts hides ranking problems.
Duplicate facts should be tested.
Ten copies of an incorrect statement do not make it correct.
Testing should include duplicate sources where relevant.
Testing should include stale documentation.
Testing should include current source.
Testing should include irrelevant high-similarity text.
Testing should include paraphrased questions.
Testing should include questions requiring two related facts.
Testing should include questions requiring refusal to speculate.
A knowledge system should be tested like any other engineered system.
Its inputs are questions and source material.
Its internal stages should be observable enough to diagnose failures.
A wrong answer should be traceable.
Which sources were retrieved?
Which source was ranked highest?
Which module produced the response?
Which service failed?
Which fallback was used?
Observability improves testing.
Observability should not expose secrets unnecessarily.
Audit logs can support test diagnosis.
Tests should avoid depending on decorative log wording when structured events are available.
Machine tests should prefer machine-readable state.
Human-readable logs can change without behavioral change.
Stable interfaces make tests more durable.
Tests should not become coupled to irrelevant formatting.
Exact formatting should be tested when formatting itself is part of the contract.
Protocol bytes require exact tests.
JSON schema can require exact fields.
Human prose often allows variation.
The assertion should match the requirement.
A test that is too loose can accept invalid behavior.
Test precision should match specification precision.
Qualification is stronger than mere execution.
A component can execute without being qualified.
Qualification testing verifies required evidence before declaring a component approved for operation.
A skipped required test should not count as a pass.
An unexecuted required test should not count as a pass.
A known failure should not count as a pass.
Qualification status should derive from actual test results.
A GREEN status should mean defined qualification criteria were satisfied.
Additional states can represent pending or incomplete qualification.
Status definitions should be explicit.
Testing is evidence generation.
A test runner should return a nonzero process exit status when required tests fail according to conventional command-line automation.
A successful process exit status should represent success according to the tool's contract.
Automation can use exit status to stop failed builds.
Ignoring failed exit status can allow defective builds to continue.
Build pipelines should fail when required stages fail.
Test evidence should remain factual.
"PASS" means the tested expectation was satisfied.
"FAIL" means the tested expectation was not satisfied.
"SKIP" means the test was intentionally not executed.
"BLOCKED" means required conditions prevented execution.
"ERROR" can mean the test itself could not complete according to framework definitions.
These states should not be conflated.
A test report can include failure messages.
A test report can include source revision.
A test report can include platform.
A test report can include compiler.
A test report can include dependency versions.
A test report can include configuration.
Enough context should be preserved to reproduce important failures.
Testing should begin with requirements.
Testing should identify observable behavior.
Testing should exercise normal behavior.
Testing should exercise boundaries.
Testing should exercise failure behavior.
Testing should exercise recovery where recovery is required.
Testing should verify state after operations.
Testing should preserve reproducible failures.
Testing should convert repaired defects into regression tests where useful.
Testing should distinguish layers.
Testing should distinguish evidence from inference.
Testing should never redefine failure as success merely because failure is inconvenient.
A system that cannot be tested is difficult to trust.
A system with deterministic interfaces is easier to test.
A system with bounded behavior is easier to test.
A system with explicit errors is easier to test.
A system with small components is easier to test.
A system with clear authority boundaries is easier to test.
A system with observable state is easier to test.
A system with stable protocols is easier to test.
A system with explicit versioning is easier to test.
Testing does not create correctness.
Testing creates evidence about correctness.
Evidence should support the exact claim being made.
If a test proves a module loaded, say the module loaded.
If a test proves a function returned the expected value, say the function returned the expected value.
If a test proves 195 lesson units were stored, say 195 lesson units were stored.
Do not expand that evidence into claims the test did not establish.
If there is no test for a claim, do not claim the test proved it.
Precise testing produces precise engineering knowledge.
Debugging seeks to identify the cause of an observed problem.
Debugging is not guessing.
Debugging is not randomly changing code until symptoms disappear.
A symptom is observable incorrect behavior.
A cause is the condition responsible for producing that behavior.
An error message is evidence.
A warning is evidence.
A warning is not automatically the root cause.
A log entry is not automatically the root cause.
A crash location is evidence.
Debugging begins with observation.
Record what actually happened.
Record what was expected to happen.
Identify the difference between expected and observed behavior.
Preserve relevant timestamps.
Preserve relevant logs.
Preserve relevant configuration.
Preserve the software version.
Preserve the source revision when known.
Preserve the operating environment when relevant.
Do not replace observed facts with assumptions.
A reproducible failure is easier to debug than an intermittent failure.
Reproduction means producing the same relevant failure under defined conditions.
A reproduction should identify required initial state.
A reproduction should identify inputs.
A reproduction should identify expected results.
A minimal reproduction removes unrelated conditions while preserving the failure.
Reducing a reproduction can identify the failing boundary.
Change one relevant variable at a time when practical.
Changing many variables simultaneously weakens causal conclusions.
If five things are changed and the problem disappears, the responsible change is unknown.
A hypothesis is a proposed explanation.
A hypothesis is not evidence.
A hypothesis should produce testable predictions.
Reject hypotheses contradicted by evidence.
Prefer explanations supported by observed behavior.
Do not become attached to a hypothesis.
The first plausible explanation is not necessarily correct.
The most complicated explanation is not necessarily correct.
The simplest explanation is not automatically correct.
Evidence determines which explanation survives.
Two events occurring together does not prove one caused the other.
A network attack occurring during an outage makes the attack relevant evidence.
It does not automatically prove the attack caused the outage.
Causation requires evidence connecting cause and effect.
Debugging should identify the failing layer.
A user-interface failure can originate in the interface.
A user-interface failure can originate in application logic.
A user-interface failure can originate in a network request.
A user-interface failure can originate in a remote service.
A user-interface failure can originate in persistence.
Trace execution through boundaries.
Module interfaces create boundaries.
Process communication creates boundaries.
Network protocols create boundaries.
Database interfaces create boundaries.
Filesystem interfaces create boundaries.
Platform abstractions create boundaries.
Each boundary provides an opportunity to inspect inputs and outputs.
If correct data enters a component and incorrect data leaves it, that component becomes relevant.
If incorrect data already enters a component, investigate upstream.
If correct data leaves a component but the next component receives incorrect data, investigate the boundary or transport.
Debugging can be treated as narrowing a search space.
Begin with the largest known failing path.
Identify a midpoint.
Determine whether behavior is correct before or after that midpoint.
Continue narrowing.
This resembles binary search when the investigation permits it.
git bisect applies this idea across source history.
Logging can expose execution state.
Logs should identify important transitions.
Logs should identify failures.
Logs should preserve useful context.
Logs should not expose unnecessary secrets.
Adding targeted diagnostic logging can test a hypothesis.
Diagnostic logging should be removed or reduced when it is no longer appropriate.
Excessive logging can hide important events.
Useful logging is structured and purposeful.
A log saying "network error" is weaker than a log identifying timeout, refusal, DNS failure, TLS failure, or protocol failure.
Specific errors narrow investigation.
Error propagation should preserve useful cause information.
Wrapping an error can add context.
Wrapping an error should not destroy the underlying cause.
An internal error can contain a platform-specific code.
A higher layer can translate that code into a stable application error category.
Both can be useful for debugging.
Windows error codes are platform-specific.
Linux errno values are platform-specific.
Connection refusal is different from DNS failure.
DNS failure is different from malformed protocol data.
Empty data is different from service failure.
Do not collapse distinct failures during debugging.
A debugger can set breakpoints.
A debugger can inspect variables.
A debugger can inspect call stacks.
A debugger can step through instructions or source lines according to available symbols.
Conditional breakpoints are useful for failures occurring only under specific state.
A watchpoint can pause when memory changes according to debugger capability.
A call stack records active function calls.
The top stack frame identifies the current execution location.
Earlier frames show how execution arrived there.
A stack trace is valuable crash evidence.
A stack trace should be preserved before restarting a failed process when practical.
The crashing function is not necessarily the function that corrupted state.
Memory corruption can occur earlier and become visible later.
A buffer overflow can corrupt memory.
The crash can occur far from the original overflow.
Use-after-free can corrupt or read invalid state.
The visible failure can depend on memory layout.
Uninitialized memory can produce inconsistent behavior.
Undefined behavior can change when optimization changes.
A bug appearing only in a release build can indicate undefined behavior, timing, optimization, or another build-dependent condition.
Compare build settings.
Compare compiler versions.
Compare optimization settings.
Compare preprocessor definitions.
Compare linked libraries.
Build configuration is part of the debugging environment.
Stale object files can create misleading behavior.
A successful incremental build does not prove every source file was rebuilt.
When build-state uncertainty exists, perform a clean build.
Do not make clean builds a ritual without reason.
Use them when stale artifacts are plausible.
Compiler warnings can identify defects.
Do not suppress warnings merely to obtain a clean build.
A warning can reveal implicit conversions.
A warning can reveal uninitialized variables.
A warning can reveal incompatible types.
Not every warning is a defect.
A crash should be reproduced when possible.
Record the stack trace.
Record the binary version.
Record the source revision.
Record whether the crash is deterministic.
A deterministic crash is easier to isolate.
Intermittent failures can result from uninitialized state.
Intermittent failures can result from timing.
Intermittent failures can result from resource exhaustion.
Intermittent does not mean random.
A race condition depends on relative timing between operations.
Adding logging can alter timing.
A race can disappear when observed.
A debugger can also alter timing.
Timing-sensitive defects require careful evidence.
Thread sanitizers can help identify data races.
Stress testing can increase occurrence frequency.
Controlled delays can test timing hypotheses.
Synchronization should use defined coordination mechanisms.
Deadlocks occur when execution participants wait indefinitely on one another according to a dependency cycle or equivalent condition.
A deadlocked process can remain alive.
A process being alive does not prove it is responsive.
CPU usage can provide evidence.
Zero CPU usage does not prove deadlock.
High CPU usage does not prove useful work.
A busy loop can consume CPU while making no progress.
Progress should be measured by meaningful state transitions.
A watchdog can detect lack of expected progress.
A heartbeat does not prove complete functional health.
Debugging should test the capability that failed.
If a database query fails, test database behavior.
If DNS fails, test DNS.
If TCP connection fails, test TCP connectivity.
If TLS fails, test TLS.
If protocol qualification fails, test the protocol handshake.
Do not use ping as proof that an application protocol works.
Do not use a successful TCP connection as proof that the application works.
Do not use application startup as proof that every module works.
A network timeout can occur because packets were dropped.
A network timeout can occur because a service was overloaded.
Inspect both endpoints when possible.
Inspect server logs.
Inspect packet behavior when necessary.
Inspect timing.
Do not immediately increase the timeout.
A larger timeout is appropriate only when the required operation legitimately needs more time.
A connection repeatedly disconnecting and reconnecting should be investigated as a lifecycle problem.
Record why each disconnect occurs.
Determine which side closes the connection.
Determine whether closure is graceful.
Determine whether a timeout triggers closure.
Determine whether protocol validation triggers closure.
Determine whether an intermediary triggers closure.
Determine whether the application intentionally reconnects.
Do not assume the network is unstable merely because reconnect messages appear.
The reconnect logic itself can be defective.
A reconnect loop can amplify a minor failure.
A retry loop should record its failure category.
A retry loop should have bounded timing.
Authentication failure is not normally repaired by reconnecting repeatedly.
Wrong-network identity is not repaired by reconnecting repeatedly.
Transient connection refusal can justify retry according to policy.
Transient timeout can justify retry according to policy.
Failure classification determines recovery behavior.
Database debugging should separate connection from query execution.
Can the application reach the database server?
Can the application authenticate?
Does it have permission?
Does the SQL parse?
Does it return expected rows?
Does the application interpret those rows correctly?
Each question identifies a different stage.
Zero rows is not a database failure.
A successful query returning zero rows should be debugged as data or query-condition behavior when zero rows are unexpected.
Inspect data types.
Inspect NULL behavior.
Inspect collation when relevant.
Inspect transaction visibility.
Do not assume the application connected to the intended database merely because connection succeeded.
Configuration can point to the wrong database.
Development and production databases can contain different data.
A query can be correct against the wrong database and still produce an unexpected result.
Record connection target during diagnosis without exposing secrets.
Prepared statements should be inspected with their parameter values represented safely.
Do not reconstruct SQL by unsafe concatenation merely for debugging.
The executed SQL structure and bound values can be logged separately.
Database transactions can hide state from other connections according to isolation.
An uncommitted insert can exist in one transaction but remain invisible elsewhere.
A rollback can remove changes that appeared temporarily successful.
A COMMIT failure or ambiguous connection loss requires careful investigation.
Do not infer persistence merely because an INSERT call initially succeeded.
Filesystem debugging should use the actual runtime identity.
A file being writable by root does not prove it is writable by the service.
Inspect permissions.
Inspect parent-directory permissions.
Inspect filesystem mount options where relevant.
Inspect access-control mechanisms where relevant.
Test using the actual service identity.
Permission denied is not repaired by blindly granting everyone write access.
Determine which identity requires access.
Grant the minimum required access according to policy.
Directory write permission and file write permission are different.
Modifying an existing file requires appropriate file and path permissions.
Renaming files requires directory permissions.
Atomic replacement often requires directory write permission.
A program can read a file but be unable to replace it.
Exact operation matters.
Path bugs can result from incorrect working directories.
Relative paths depend on process working directory.
Interactive shell working directory can differ from service working directory.
A path working from a terminal does not prove it works from a service.
Log resolved paths when diagnosing path problems.
Absolute paths can remove some ambiguity.
Hard-coded absolute paths reduce portability.
Architecture should determine path strategy.
Module debugging should preserve loader stages.
Discovery identifies a candidate.
Manifest validation validates metadata.
Qualification evaluates required properties.
Loading maps or attaches executable module code.
Initialization establishes module state.
Activation makes module functionality available according to architecture.
Failure at one stage should not be mislabeled as failure at another.
"module.conf missing" is a manifest-stage failure.
A missing export is a loading or interface-validation failure according to architecture.
Initialization failure occurs after loading when initialization does not complete.
Functional failure can occur after successful activation.
A manifest error should first be investigated at the manifest boundary.
Do not modify a valid module manifest merely because another component failed.
Do not touch unrelated configuration without evidence.
A module's module.conf can be authoritative configuration.
If module.conf is known correct and evidence points elsewhere, preserve it.
Changing known-good configuration introduces a new variable.
It does not prove every module command works.
Test the module's actual interface.
If "ingest php" fails, inspect command routing.
If routing succeeds, inspect lesson lookup.
If lookup succeeds, inspect file access.
If file access succeeds, inspect parsing.
If parsing succeeds, inspect storage.
If storage succeeds, inspect reporting.
The failing stage should be isolated.
A narrow defect should receive a narrow repair when architecture permits.
A broad rewrite increases regression risk.
Response debugging should distinguish retrieval from generation.
Did the request reach the response component?
Was the question parsed?
Was relevant knowledge retrieved?
Was irrelevant knowledge retrieved?
Was source authority applied?
Did an internal dependency fail?
Each stage can fail independently.
A wrong answer is not necessarily a generation defect.
The wrong evidence may have been retrieved.
Correct evidence can be retrieved but ranked below irrelevant evidence.
Correct output can be produced but lost during transport.
Trace the pipeline.
If Digit answers an unrelated PHP fact to a specific PHP question, inspect retrieval evidence before changing the PHP lesson.
The lesson can contain the correct fact.
A retrieval failure does not mean the lesson is wrong.
A reasoning failure does not mean ingestion failed.
A successful retrieval does not prove response quality.
Keep stages separate.
Source provenance is useful debugging information.
Record which source produced a retrieved fact.
Record source type.
Record current versus historical status when relevant.
This can explain why an answer was produced.
If an old README outranks a current interface header, the retrieval problem is source precedence.
Deleting historical evidence is one possible workaround but can destroy useful history.
When current state is requested, current authoritative sources should win according to architecture.
When history is requested, historical sources become relevant.
Debugging retrieval requires understanding question intent.
A current-state question and a historical question are not equivalent.
"What ABI do your modules use?" requests current state.
"What ABI versions have your modules used?" requests history.
A retrieval system should not answer all three with the same evidence selection.
A debugging test should include all three.
If the first returns "1.3 -> 1.4 -> 1.5," source-role handling is defective.
If the second returns only "1.5," historical retrieval may be incomplete.
If the third invents differences not present in evidence, grounding is defective.
Different failures require different repairs.
Configuration debugging should establish actual loaded configuration.
The configuration file on disk is not necessarily the configuration currently in memory.
Changing a file does not guarantee a running process reloaded it.
Some systems support live reload.
Some systems require restart.
Some systems watch files.
Some systems cache configuration.
Determine actual behavior.
Log configuration source where safe.
Do not log secrets.
Environment variables can override files.
Command-line options can override defaults.
A stale environment variable can override a corrected file.
A service manager can provide environment variables different from an interactive shell.
A service can run under a different working directory.
A service can run under a different user.
A service can have different PATH settings.
A program working interactively but failing as a service often indicates environment differences.
Compare environments systematically.
Do not immediately rewrite the application.
Deployment debugging should identify the actual executing binary.
The source directory can contain new code while an old binary remains running.
A binary can be rebuilt but not deployed.
A binary can be deployed but the service not restarted.
A service can restart but execute a different path.
Multiple copies of an executable can exist.
Determine the process executable path.
Determine version output.
Determine process start time.
Determine source revision associated with the binary when available.
"Source is fixed" does not mean "running service is fixed."
"Build succeeded" does not mean "deployment succeeded."
"Deployment succeeded" does not mean "runtime behavior is correct."
Each stage requires evidence.
Version strings are useful diagnostic evidence.
Build identifiers are useful diagnostic evidence.
ABI versions are useful diagnostic evidence.
Do not confuse them.
Application version 2.0 can use ABI 1.5.
Protocol version 1 can coexist with application version 2.0.
A commit hash does not replace any of these semantic versions.
Record identifiers with labels.
Unlabeled version numbers create ambiguity.
A client log shows client observations.
A proxy log shows proxy observations.
A packet capture shows traffic visible at the capture point.
No single perspective necessarily contains the complete event.
Correlate using timestamps and identifiers.
Request identifiers can connect events across components.
Transaction identifiers can connect related operations.
Peer identifiers can connect protocol events.
Alert identifiers can connect monitoring events.
Clock synchronization improves timeline reconstruction.
Clock drift can make event order appear incorrect.
Use monotonic clocks for measuring elapsed duration when appropriate.
Monotonic time is useful for durations because it is not affected by ordinary wall-clock adjustments.
Time source matters.
Performance debugging should measure before optimizing.
Identify where time is spent.
Measure DNS time.
Measure connection time.
Measure TLS time.
Measure server processing.
Measure database time.
Measure serialization.
Measure response transfer.
The bottleneck may be external.
A memory profiler identifies allocation behavior.
An I/O profiler can identify blocking or heavy input-output behavior.
Profiling provides evidence.
Optimize measured bottlenecks.
Premature optimization can increase complexity without solving the actual problem.
A high CPU process should be profiled before assuming a loop is defective.
High CPU can represent legitimate computation.
Low CPU can represent deadlock.
Memory debugging should observe growth over time.
Caches intentionally retain memory.
A memory leak occurs when memory that is no longer needed remains unreachable for reuse or is otherwise improperly retained according to the program's ownership model.
Measure the same operation repeatedly.
Observe whether memory stabilizes.
Use memory-analysis tools where available.
A leak can exist in a library.
File descriptor exhaustion can look like unrelated I/O failures.
Socket exhaustion can look like network failure.
Thread exhaustion can prevent new work.
Disk exhaustion can cause write failures.
Disk-full errors should remain disk-full errors.
Permission errors should remain permission errors.
Resource exhaustion should remain resource exhaustion.
Generic "internal service unavailable" messages can obscure actionable causes.
A core dump captures process state around a crash according to operating-system configuration.
Core dumps can contain sensitive information.
A core dump can be examined with a debugger.
Debug symbols improve stack traces.
Debug symbols map machine addresses to source information.
Stripped binaries can be harder to debug.
Production binaries can keep separate debug-symbol files.
Debug information does not need to change program semantics.
Debug builds and production builds can differ.
Reproduce against the actual failing build where possible.
A repair should address the identified cause.
A workaround changes conditions so the symptom does not occur without necessarily correcting the cause.
Workarounds can be legitimate temporary measures.
A workaround should not be mislabeled as a root-cause repair.
Increasing a timeout can be a workaround.
Restarting a process can be a workaround.
Clearing a cache can be a workaround.
Deleting state can be a workaround.
Reinstalling software can be a workaround.
These actions can restore operation while destroying evidence about the cause.
Preserve evidence before destructive recovery when investigation matters.
Operational urgency can require recovery before complete investigation.
When recovery must occur, capture available evidence first when practical.
Incident response and debugging have overlapping but different priorities.
Debugging prioritizes understanding defects.
After service restoration, root-cause analysis can continue using preserved evidence.
A root cause is a condition whose correction prevents recurrence within the defined causal model.
There can be multiple contributing causes.
Complex failures can have more than one necessary condition.
Identify contributing factors when evidence supports them.
A root-cause analysis should distinguish trigger from underlying weakness.
Unsafe parser behavior can be the underlying defect.
The request is not necessarily the software defect.
A network outage can trigger reconnect behavior.
Unbounded reconnect logic can amplify the outage.
A regression is behavior that previously worked and became incorrect after change.
Find a known-good revision.
Find a known-bad revision.
Compare them.
git bisect can narrow the change boundary.
The first bad commit identifies where the tested behavior changed.
Inspect that change.
Debugging concerns cause, not blame.
Blame does not repair software.
A patch should be as narrow as practical.
A patch should preserve unrelated known-good behavior.
A patch should not modify files unrelated to the identified defect without reason.
A patch should not modify a module manifest when the manifest is known-good and the defect lies elsewhere.
A patch should not introduce speculative architectural changes.
Fix the demonstrated problem.
Test the repair.
Run the original reproduction.
Run related regression tests.
Verify that neighboring behavior still works.
A repaired symptom without regression testing can conceal new defects.
A fix should include a regression test when practical.
The regression test should fail before the fix.
The regression test should pass after the fix.
This provides evidence that the patch addresses the observed defect.
If the test passed before the patch, it does not demonstrate the patch repaired that failure.
Do not merely chase green indicators.
Debugging can use assertions.
Assertions expose violated assumptions.
An assertion failure identifies an assumption that was false.
The assertion itself can be wrong.
Investigate both the state and the assumption.
Do not simply delete an assertion because it fired.
If the assertion expresses a valid invariant, the state-producing defect should be repaired.
An invariant is a condition expected to remain true during defined system states.
Examples include a pointer being non-null before dereference.
Examples include an active module having completed qualification.
A state machine makes legal transitions explicit.
An impossible transition indicates either corrupted state, a defective transition, or an incorrect state model.
Inspect the evidence.
Silent coercion hides defects.
Production behavior can require controlled degradation.
Development and production failure policies can differ while preserving the same underlying correctness rules.
Defensive programming anticipates invalid conditions.
Defensive programming does not mean hiding every defect.
Returning a safe error can protect production state.
Logging the invariant violation can preserve debugging evidence.
When state validity is uncertain, fail according to defined policy.
Do not continue blindly.
Do not immediately rewrite the corrupted file.
Make a copy.
Compare with known-good examples.
Identify the first invalid field or region.
Determine which writer could produce that region.
Trace write paths.
A corrupted file can be caused by partial writes.
A corrupted file can be caused by incorrect serialization.
Do not assume malicious modification without evidence.
Write temporary content.
Flush according to durability requirements.
Rename atomically where filesystem semantics permit.
The exact durability requirements determine whether additional synchronization is required.
Serialization debugging compares encoded and decoded representations.
Serialize a known value.
Inspect exact bytes.
Deserialize the bytes.
Compare the result.
Test boundary values.
Test zero.
Test maximum values.
Test signed values when permitted.
Byte-order defects often appear only for values larger than one byte.
Never assume raw C structure memory is portable protocol serialization.
Inspect bytes.
Protocol debugging should use known messages.
Capture received bytes.
Compare against protocol specification.
Verify version.
Verify identifiers.
Verify checksums or hashes.
Priority does not guarantee immediate execution.
Real-time scheduling has stronger timing requirements and different policies.
A clock measures time.
Operating systems expose multiple clock types.
Wall-clock time represents civil time.
Monotonic time measures elapsed progression unaffected by ordinary wall-clock adjustments.
Timeout measurement should generally use a monotonic clock when available.
Wall-clock time can change.
A user can change system time.
Daylight-saving transitions affect civil time representation.
Elapsed-time calculations should not assume wall-clock time always increases smoothly.
UTC is a time standard commonly used for machine timestamps.
Local time applies timezone rules to represent civil time.
Store and compare time according to application requirements.
A timestamp without timezone context can be ambiguous.
Operating systems expose clocks through platform APIs.
Portable code should use an appropriate abstraction when clock semantics must remain consistent.
A boot is the process of starting the operating system.
Firmware initializes hardware before or during the boot process according to platform architecture.
BIOS is an older firmware interface.
UEFI is a modern firmware interface.
UEFI stands for Unified Extensible Firmware Interface.
The bootloader loads or starts the operating-system kernel according to platform design.
User-space initialization starts system services.
Boot stages should not be confused.
A bootloader is not the kernel.
The kernel is not systemd.
systemd is not the shell.
The shell is not the terminal.
The terminal is not the operating system.
Keeping layers distinct makes debugging easier.
A device driver allows the operating system to control hardware according to the device interface.
Drivers commonly execute with elevated privilege.
A defective kernel-mode driver can destabilize the entire operating system.
User-space application failure is normally more isolated.
Different operating systems expose hardware information differently.
Portable hardware-detection logic should separate common classification from platform-specific enumeration.
A GPU is a Graphics Processing Unit.
A GPU being present does not mean a particular compute API is available.
A driver being installed does not prove a workload can execute successfully.
Hardware detection, capability detection, and successful execution are different stages.
USB stands for Universal Serial Bus.
USB devices are enumerated by the operating system.
USB device identity can include vendor and product identifiers.
A USB device's presence does not establish its intended application role.
Application logic should qualify devices according to required capabilities.
Storage devices are exposed through operating-system block or filesystem abstractions.
A block device provides addressable blocks according to storage interfaces.
A filesystem provides files and directories above storage.
Formatting creates a filesystem or storage structure according to the selected tool and format.
Mounting attaches a filesystem into the accessible filesystem namespace on Unix-like systems.
Windows commonly exposes mounted volumes through drive letters or mount points.
A mounted filesystem is not necessarily local physical storage.
Filesystem semantics can differ.
Atomic operations on one filesystem may not have identical guarantees on another.
Portable software should not assume every filesystem behaves identically.
Case sensitivity differs among filesystems.
Linux filesystems commonly distinguish filename case.
Windows filesystems commonly provide case-insensitive filename lookup by default while preserving case.
file.txt and File.txt can be distinct files on a case-sensitive filesystem.
Line endings differ by platform convention.
Windows text files traditionally use CRLF.
Protocol serialization should specify line endings independently from local operating-system conventions.
Network protocols should not inherit arbitrary local newline behavior.
Text encoding is also independent of operating-system identity.
UTF-8 can be used on Windows.
Locale affects language and formatting behavior.
Locale can affect sorting.
Locale can affect numeric formatting.
Machine protocols should avoid unintended dependence on user locale.
A decimal point in a machine protocol should not become a comma merely because the host locale changed unless the protocol explicitly defines locale behavior.
Deterministic systems should control locale-sensitive behavior where it affects outputs.
Containers provide an additional isolation mechanism using operating-system features.
A container is not necessarily a virtual machine.
Containers commonly share the host kernel.
Virtual machines normally run a guest operating system using virtualized hardware.
A container image contains application filesystem and configuration layers according to its container system.
A container does not provide a separate physical computer.
Security assumptions should reflect the actual isolation mechanism.
Privilege escalation means obtaining greater authority than currently permitted.
Applications should not require privilege escalation for routine operations unless necessary.
Running as Administrator to avoid configuring Windows ACLs is usually excessive.
Correct installation should grant the runtime identity exactly the access it requires.
Service identities should be explicit.
File ownership should be explicit.
Privileged operations should be explicit.
Operating-system interaction should be bounded.
A program should know which files it owns.
A program should know which ports it may bind.
A program should know which subprocesses it may launch.
A program should know which environment configuration it consumes.
A program should know which privileges it requires.
Implicit operating-system dependencies make deployment fragile.
A platform abstraction provides a common interface over operating-system-specific implementation.
The Linux implementation satisfies that behavior using Linux or POSIX interfaces as applicable.
Externally required results should remain consistent where architecture requires consistency.
Platform abstraction should not erase meaningful native errors.
Platform abstraction should normalize behavior that is intended to be common.
Do not put Windows-specific behavior into common semantics unless it is actually a common requirement.
Do not put Linux-specific behavior into common semantics unless it is actually a common requirement.
Platform independence means common system behavior is not determined by one operating system.
Platform independence does not mean platform-specific code cannot exist.
Platform-specific code is often necessary.
Runtime detection can select platform backends where architecture supports it.
Build-time and runtime platform selection are different strategies.
A single source tree can support multiple platforms.
Separate platform directories can isolate native implementation.
Shared headers can define common interfaces.
The linker can select the required implementation during build.
A runtime loader can select implementations dynamically when required.
The correct design depends on deployment requirements.
Do not hard-code one operating system when runtime portability is required.
Operating-system APIs return evidence.
Check return values.
Check error codes.
Check resource limits.
Close resources.
Bound retries.
Protect shared state.
Use least privilege.
Separate common behavior from platform implementation.
Do not infer success from silence.
Do not infer file content from extension alone.
Do not infer process success from process creation alone.
Do not infer remote success from a local network send.
Do not infer durability from a buffered write alone.
Do not infer portability because code compiled on two machines.
Do not infer platform independence when observable results differ where they are required to match.
The operating system is an execution environment and resource manager.
Applications operate within the boundaries it establishes.
Small operating-system interfaces are easier to reason about.
Explicit resource ownership is easier to reason about.
Explicit platform boundaries are easier to maintain.
Explicit permissions are easier to audit.
Explicit process lifecycle is easier to operate.
Explicit failure handling is easier to debug.
The application defines its required behavior.
Correct engineering keeps those responsibilities distinct.
A database is an organized system for storing and retrieving persistent data.
Persistent data remains available beyond the lifetime of the process that created it.
A database management system is commonly abbreviated DBMS.
A relational database organizes data primarily using relations commonly represented as tables.
MySQL is a relational database management system.
MariaDB is a relational database management system.
PostgreSQL is a relational database management system.
SQLite is a relational database management system implemented primarily as an embedded library.
Different database systems can implement different features and behavior.
SQL stands for Structured Query Language.
SQL is a language family with standards and implementation-specific dialects.
A table represents a collection of rows according to a defined schema.
A row represents one record or tuple according to the relational model and practical database terminology.
A column represents a defined attribute.
A column has a data type.
A schema defines database structure.
A schema can define tables.
A schema can define columns.
A schema can define constraints.
A schema can define indexes.
A schema can define relationships.
A database schema is not the same thing as application data.
Rows contain stored data.
A primary key must be unique.
A primary key cannot contain NULL according to relational database primary-key semantics.
A table normally has one primary-key constraint.
A primary key can contain one column.
A primary key can contain multiple columns.
A surrogate key is an identifier created primarily to identify a database record.
An automatically generated integer ID is a common surrogate key.
A natural key uses data having meaning outside the database as the identifier.
Neither surrogate nor natural keys are universally superior.
A foreign key represents a relationship to a key in another table or the same table.
Foreign-key constraints can enforce referential integrity.
A foreign key does not automatically create every index an application might need.
Index behavior depends on the database system.
A constraint defines a rule the stored data must satisfy.
A NOT NULL constraint prohibits NULL values in a column.
A UNIQUE constraint requires defined values to remain unique according to database semantics.
A CHECK constraint validates values according to an expression where supported.
A FOREIGN KEY constraint enforces a defined relationship.
Application validation does not necessarily replace database constraints.
Database constraints do not necessarily replace application validation.
They operate at different boundaries.
NULL is not the string "NULL".
SQL uses three-valued logic in many expressions involving NULL.
IS NULL tests for NULL.
IS NOT NULL tests for non-NULL values.
NULL behavior should be understood explicitly.
SELECT can retrieve selected columns.
SELECT can retrieve rows satisfying conditions.
SELECT * requests all columns produced by the selected table or query structure.
Selecting only required columns can make interfaces clearer.
Column order should not be assumed unless explicitly defined by the query or interface.
ORDER BY defines result ordering.
Deterministic result ordering requires sufficient ordering criteria.
ORDER BY on a non-unique value can still leave ties.
Add a deterministic tie-breaking column when stable total ordering matters.
LIMIT restricts the number of returned rows in database dialects supporting that syntax.
OFFSET skips a defined number of rows according to query semantics.
Pagination using OFFSET can become inefficient for large result sets.
Pagination strategy should follow scale and requirements.
Permanent historical records should not be deleted when policy requires their preservation.
A status field can represent lifecycle state without destroying history.
Data retention requirements should determine deletion behavior.
An UPDATE statement without an appropriate WHERE clause can update every applicable row.
A DELETE statement without an appropriate WHERE clause can delete every applicable row.
State-changing SQL should be treated carefully.
A transaction groups database operations according to transactional semantics.
A transaction can begin.
A transaction can perform operations.
A transaction can commit.
A transaction can roll back.
A transaction should represent one coherent unit of work.
If several changes must either all occur or none occur, a transaction can provide that atomic boundary.
ACID is an acronym associated with relational transaction properties.
ACID stands for Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability.
Atomicity means the transaction's changes are applied as a unit according to transactional guarantees.
Consistency means transactions preserve defined database rules and invariants when correctly designed.
Isolation describes how concurrent transactions interact and observe one another.
Durability means committed changes survive failures according to the database's durability guarantees.
Isolation levels define permitted interactions between concurrent transactions.
READ UNCOMMITTED permits weak isolation according to SQL terminology.
READ COMMITTED prevents selected dirty reads.
SERIALIZABLE provides behavior intended to correspond to some serial transaction execution according to its model.
Higher isolation can impose greater concurrency costs.
Isolation level should follow correctness requirements.
A dirty read observes another transaction's uncommitted changes.
A phantom describes changes in the set of rows matching a predicate between reads according to isolation terminology.
Two application requests can attempt to modify the same data concurrently.
A race condition can exist at the database layer.
Reading a value and later updating it based on the earlier read can race with another transaction.
Transactions can help.
Locks can help.
Version columns can support optimistic concurrency control.
Optimistic concurrency assumes conflicts are relatively uncommon and detects conflicting changes.
Pessimistic concurrency locks data before performing conflicting operations according to the database mechanism.
Neither strategy is universally superior.
A deadlock can occur when transactions wait on resources held by each other.
Database systems can detect deadlocks.
A database can abort one transaction to resolve a deadlock.
Applications should handle retryable transaction failures according to policy.
A deadlock is not repaired by ignoring the error.
Retries should be bounded.
A transaction retry should repeat the entire logical transaction when required, not an arbitrary fragment that can violate assumptions.
An index is a data structure maintained to accelerate selected database access.
A database index is not the same as an array index.
An index can accelerate searches.
An index can accelerate joins.
An index can enforce uniqueness when used for a unique constraint or unique index.
Indexes require maintenance when data changes.
Indexes can improve reads while increasing write cost.
An index should support actual query patterns.
A query planner selects an execution strategy.
A query plan describes how the database intends to execute a query.
The database can choose a table scan.
The database can choose an index scan.
The database can choose different join strategies.
Query planners use schema information and statistics.
A query containing an indexed column does not guarantee the database will use that index.
The planner can determine that another strategy is cheaper.
EXPLAIN can display query-plan information in many relational database systems.
Use query plans as evidence when diagnosing query performance.
Do not guess that a query is slow because an index is missing without examining evidence.
A full table scan reads applicable table data without using a selective index path.
A full table scan is not automatically wrong.
Scale matters.
Selectivity describes how narrowly a condition identifies rows.
An index on a low-selectivity column can provide limited benefit for some queries.
Composite indexes contain multiple columns.
Column order in a composite index matters.
An index on columns A and B is not necessarily equivalent to separate indexes on A and B.
An INNER JOIN returns matching rows according to the join condition.
A LEFT JOIN preserves rows from the left side even when no matching right-side row exists.
A RIGHT JOIN preserves rows from the right side where supported.
A FULL OUTER JOIN preserves unmatched rows from both sides where supported.
Not every database dialect supports every join syntax identically.
A CROSS JOIN produces combinations of rows according to Cartesian-product semantics.
An accidental Cartesian product can create extremely large result sets.
Relationships should follow schema semantics.
Normalization organizes relational data to reduce selected redundancy and anomalies.
Normalization uses normal forms.
Second Normal Form is commonly abbreviated 2NF.
Third Normal Form is commonly abbreviated 3NF.
Boyce-Codd Normal Form is commonly abbreviated BCNF.
Normalization is not a command to split every piece of data into the maximum possible number of tables.
Denormalization deliberately introduces redundancy for defined operational reasons.
Denormalization can improve selected read patterns.
Denormalization increases synchronization responsibility.
Do not denormalize without understanding which value is authoritative.
Duplicate data can diverge.
One source should be defined as authoritative where consistency requires it.
A database view presents a query as a database object.
A view can simplify repeated query logic.
A view does not necessarily store its result.
A materialized view stores derived results where supported.
Refresh policy determines when materialized results are updated.
A stored procedure is executable database-side logic where supported.
A trigger executes database-side behavior in response to defined events.
Triggers can also hide side effects from application code.
An application developer should know whether triggers can modify additional data.
Invisible side effects make debugging harder.
SQL injection is a security vulnerability.
String concatenation of untrusted input into SQL can create SQL injection.
Parameterized queries separate SQL structure from supplied values.
Prepared statements support parameter binding.
A parameter placeholder represents a value.
A parameter placeholder does not normally substitute arbitrary SQL identifiers.
Table names and column names cannot generally be treated as ordinary bound values.
Dynamic identifiers should come from controlled allowlists when required.
Do not let arbitrary user input select SQL structure.
A prepared statement can improve security.
A prepared statement can also support repeated execution.
The entire query construction path matters.
Database credentials are secrets.
Database passwords should not be committed to public source repositories.
Database credentials should not be exposed in web responses.
Database credentials should not be written to ordinary logs.
A web application normally should not connect using a database administrative account.
Least privilege applies to database users.
An account requiring SELECT should not automatically receive DROP privileges.
An account requiring selected table modifications should not automatically receive global administrative authority.
Database authentication determines who is connecting.
Database authorization determines what the authenticated identity can do.
Authentication and authorization are different.
A valid database password does not mean the account should have unrestricted privileges.
A database server can listen on a network interface.
A database server bound only to loopback is accessible only locally through that network listener.
A database server bound externally can increase attack surface.
Do not expose a database server publicly merely because an application needs database access.
Firewalls can restrict database access.
Database user permissions provide another control layer.
Transport encryption can protect database traffic.
TLS can protect client-server database connections where supported and configured.
TLS does not repair SQL injection.
TLS does not replace database authentication.
TLS does not replace authorization.
Each control addresses a different boundary.
A connection establishes a client session with a database server or database engine.
Opening a database connection can fail.
Authentication can fail.
The server can be unavailable.
DNS can fail.
The network can time out.
The database can reach a connection limit.
Applications should handle connection failure explicitly.
A connection pool maintains reusable database connections.
Connection pooling reduces repeated connection establishment overhead.
A pool can become exhausted.
Leaked connections can exhaust a pool.
A connection can become stale or invalid.
Pools should validate or recover connections according to their implementation.
A database connection should not remain in an uncommitted transaction when returned to a pool.
Connection state must be reset according to pool behavior.
PHP applications can access databases through database extensions and libraries.
PDO stands for PHP Data Objects.
PDO provides a database-access abstraction in PHP.
PDO supports prepared statements.
PDO supports transactions where the underlying driver supports them.
PDO does not make different database SQL dialects identical.
A PDO application can still contain database-specific SQL.
mysqli is a PHP extension for MySQL-compatible database access.
PDO and mysqli provide different APIs.
Application architecture determines which interface is appropriate.
Using PDO is not a requirement for every PHP application.
Using a database is not a requirement for every PHP application.
Files can be sufficient for small persistent datasets.
JSON can be sufficient for small structured datasets.
SQLite can be sufficient when relational queries and transactional persistence are needed without a separate database server.
A client-server DBMS can be appropriate when concurrency, scale, centralized access, or operational requirements justify it.
Choose the smallest persistence mechanism satisfying requirements.
A database adds operational complexity.
A database requires schema management.
A database can require credentials.
A database can require backups.
A database can require upgrades.
A database can require monitoring.
A database can require network configuration.
Do not introduce a database merely because data exists.
Use a database when database capabilities solve actual requirements.
JSON stores structured data using objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans, and null.
Two writers modifying the same JSON file can overwrite one another without coordination.
Atomic replacement can improve single-file update safety.
Locking can coordinate writers.
As concurrency grows, a database can become more appropriate.
A database can query selected records without rewriting an entire dataset.
A database can maintain indexes.
A database can coordinate concurrent transactions.
These capabilities come with additional complexity.
A migration changes database schema or stored data according to a controlled process.
Schema changes should be versioned when deployment requires repeatability.
A migration can create a table.
A migration can create an index.
A migration can transform data.
A migration can remove obsolete structure when policy permits.
A migration should have a defined relationship to application versions.
Application code should not assume a schema change succeeded merely because deployment began.
Schema compatibility should be validated.
A destructive migration can permanently lose data.
Backups should exist according to recovery requirements before destructive changes.
A backup is a recoverable copy of data.
A backup is useful only if it can actually be restored.
Backup procedures should be tested.
A successful backup command does not prove recovery will succeed.
Restore testing provides evidence.
A database dump represents database content in an export format.
A dump can contain schema.
A dump can contain sensitive information.
Database backups should be protected according to the sensitivity of the original data.
Encryption of production storage does not automatically encrypt every exported backup.
Backup security is a separate requirement.
Replication copies database state to additional database instances according to a replication mechanism.
Replication can improve availability.
A mistaken DELETE can replicate to replicas.
Corrupted logical changes can replicate.
A backup preserves recoverable historical state according to retention policy.
Replication and backup solve different problems.
A primary database can accept authoritative writes according to a replication architecture.
Replicas can receive replicated state.
Terminology varies among database systems.
Replication can be synchronous.
Replication can be asynchronous.
Asynchronous replicas can lag behind the primary.
Reading from a lagging replica can return stale data.
Current-state requirements should account for replication lag.
A replica returning an older value does not necessarily mean the primary lost the newer value.
Source and timing matter.
Database caching can improve performance.
Cached database results can become stale.
Cache invalidation should follow application requirements.
The database is not automatically authoritative either.
Authority is determined by application architecture.
An external source can be authoritative while the database stores a local copy.
A database can be authoritative for application-owned state.
Define authority explicitly.
A database transaction ID is not automatically an application record ID.
A row ID is not automatically a user identity.
A username is not automatically a primary key.
A primary key is not automatically suitable for public exposure.
Internal identifiers and public identifiers can have different requirements.
That can be acceptable.
That can also be undesirable.
Identifier design should follow requirements rather than fashion.
UUIDs can be used as database identifiers.
Different UUID versions have different generation properties.
UUIDs consume more storage than small integer identifiers.
UUIDs can simplify distributed identifier generation.
UUIDs do not make authorization unnecessary.
An unguessable identifier is not a permission system.
Knowing a record identifier should not grant access unless policy explicitly defines that behavior.
Data types matter.
An integer column stores integer values according to the database type.
A decimal type can represent exact fixed-point numeric values according to its precision and scale.
Floating-point types represent approximate binary floating-point values.
Money calculations often require exact decimal or integer representations according to application requirements.
A string type stores textual or byte data according to type and encoding.
CHAR and VARCHAR have different storage and comparison semantics depending on the database.
TEXT types support larger textual values according to the database.
BLOB stands for Binary Large Object.
A BLOB stores binary data.
Storing a file in a database is different from storing a filesystem path to the file.
Large binary data can increase database size and backup cost.
Filesystem storage introduces synchronization between filesystem state and database metadata.
Choose according to requirements.
Character encoding matters.
Database character sets determine how text is stored.
Two strings can compare equal under one collation and different under another.
Text search and identity comparison are different requirements.
Timestamp semantics should be defined.
UTC is commonly useful for machine-stored event times.
A date is not a timestamp.
Timezone information should not be discarded when required.
Database systems have different date and time types.
CURRENT_TIMESTAMP obtains database current time according to database semantics.
Application time and database-server time can differ.
Distributed systems should define which clock is authoritative for a given operation.
Some systems map boolean concepts onto integer-like representations.
Application code should use the database driver's defined conversions rather than assumptions.
An enum constrains a value to a defined set where supported.
Database enum types can make allowed values explicit.
Changing allowed enum values can require schema migration.
A lookup table can provide a more extensible set of states.
The correct representation depends on lifecycle requirements.
Status values should have defined meaning.
"active" should mean one defined state.
"pending" should mean one defined state.
Do not overload one status value with several unrelated meanings.
State transitions can be validated in application logic.
State transitions can also be constrained through database logic where appropriate.
Historical status changes can be recorded separately when audit history is required.
An audit log records meaningful actions or state changes.
An audit log should preserve actor where known.
An audit log should preserve timestamp.
An audit log should preserve action.
An audit log should preserve relevant target identity.
Audit data should not be silently rewritten to make history match current state.
History and current state are different.
A database query should ask the correct source for the question.
A current-state question should query current state.
A historical question should query historical records.
A comparison question can query both.
A higher row count does not establish authority.
The newest timestamp does not automatically establish authority.
Database selection logic must implement actual application rules.
SQL aggregates summarize multiple rows.
COUNT counts rows or non-NULL expressions according to its form.
COUNT(column) counts non-NULL values of that expression.
SUM calculates a total.
AVG calculates an average.
MIN returns a minimum value.
GROUP BY groups rows for aggregate calculation.
HAVING filters groups after grouping according to SQL semantics.
An aggregate result is derived data.
Derived data can sometimes be stored for performance.
Stored derived data must remain synchronized with its source.
Do not store redundant totals without a reason.
A transaction can maintain derived values consistently when storing them is required.
Database triggers can maintain derived values.
Application logic can maintain derived values.
Each approach has tradeoffs.
A query can contain a subquery.
A subquery is a query nested within another query context.
A Common Table Expression is commonly abbreviated CTE.
CTEs can improve readability.
A recursive query should have a termination condition.
Recursive database queries can consume substantial resources.
Bound recursion where requirements permit.
Aggregation should occur where appropriate.
The database engine is designed to perform data operations.
The database should also not become a dumping ground for unrelated business logic.
Keep responsibilities clear.
An ORM is an Object-Relational Mapper.
An ORM maps application objects or models to relational data according to its framework.
An ORM can reduce repetitive database-access code.
An ORM does not eliminate SQL concepts.
An ORM can generate inefficient queries.
Developers using an ORM should still understand transactions, indexes, joins, and query behavior.
Abstraction does not remove the underlying system.
Active Record is one database-access pattern.
Data Mapper is another pattern.
MVC does not require one particular database-access pattern.
A model can use a database.
A model can use files.
A model can use an external service.
A model represents application data and domain behavior according to the framework architecture.
Database and model are not synonyms.
Separation of responsibilities improves maintainability.
A web request can begin a database operation.
The request should validate input.
The request should authenticate the user where required.
The request should authorize the action.
The application should perform database work using safe query construction.
The application should handle database failures.
The application should return an appropriate response.
Database errors should not expose credentials or sensitive internal details to remote users.
Detailed errors can be recorded in protected logs.
A database exception is not necessarily an HTTP 500 response in every situation.
Duplicate user input can map to a validation or conflict response.
Missing data can map to a not-found response.
Unavailable database infrastructure can map to a server-side availability failure.
Preserve the underlying internal error for diagnosis.
SQL syntax errors are developer or query-construction defects.
Constraint violations are data-rule failures.
Deadlocks are concurrency failures.
Authorization failures are permission failures.
Disk-full errors are storage-resource failures.
Do not collapse every database failure into "database unavailable."
Query latency can be monitored.
Transaction failure rate can be monitored.
Connection count can be monitored.
Storage usage can be monitored.
Replication lag can be monitored.
Slow queries can be monitored.
Deadlocks can be monitored.
Monitoring provides evidence.
Monitoring should not execute destructive test operations against production data.
Health checks should be small and bounded.
A database server consumes memory.
A database server consumes CPU.
A database server consumes storage.
A database server can cache data in memory.
Database engines deliberately use memory for caching.
Operational diagnosis requires understanding configured behavior.
Database growth should be understood.
Indexes consume disk space.
Logs consume disk space.
Temporary query data can consume disk space.
Backups consume disk space.
Binary logs or transaction logs can consume disk space.
Unbounded storage growth can cause outages.
Retention policy should be explicit.
Transaction logs support recovery and replication according to the database.
Application logs record application behavior.
Audit logs record security or governance events.
These are not interchangeable.
A database administrator is commonly abbreviated DBA.
Database administration includes configuration.
Database administration includes recovery.
Database administration includes access control.
Database administration includes upgrades.
Database administration includes monitoring.
Automation does not remove responsibility for recovery.
A managed database service can operate infrastructure.
A managed service does not own application correctness.
The application remains responsible for schema and query behavior according to service boundaries.
SQL modes can change behavior.
Collations can change behavior.
Defaults can change behavior.
Deprecated features can be removed.
Test application compatibility before production upgrades.
Do not assume MariaDB and MySQL remain behaviorally identical because they share historical ancestry.
They are distinct database systems.
Features and versions can diverge.
Consult the applicable system documentation when behavior matters.
SQLite differs significantly from client-server database systems.
SQLite executes within the application process through a library.
SQLite does not require a separate database-server daemon for ordinary use.
SQLite still provides SQL and transactions.
SQLite has concurrency characteristics different from client-server databases.
SQLite can be excellent for small and medium local applications.
"Embedded" does not mean toy.
Architecture should follow workload.
A flat file can outperform a database in simplicity when the dataset is tiny and access patterns are simple.
A database can outperform flat files in correctness and capability when concurrent structured access grows.
Data persistence should be deterministic.
Schema should be explicit.
Identifiers should be explicit.
Relationships should be explicit.
Constraints should be explicit.
Transactions should be explicit where atomicity matters.
Backup policy should be explicit.
Access should be explicit.
Errors should be checked.
Queries should be parameterized.
Credentials should be protected.
Privileges should be minimal.
Indexes should solve demonstrated query requirements.
Transactions should represent coherent units of work.
Caches should not be mistaken for authoritative storage.
Database success should not be mistaken for factual correctness.
A database stores what software tells it to store.
A database retrieves according to the queries it receives.
A database enforces the rules actually defined in its schema and permissions.
A database does not infer missing governance.
A database does not decide which human decision is authoritative.
Use it when its capabilities are required.
Do not use it merely because it exists.
Keep the schema small.
Keep queries understandable.
Keep permissions narrow.
Keep transactions bounded.
Keep ownership clear.
Keep current state distinguishable from history.
The simplest persistence mechanism that correctly satisfies the requirements is usually the best starting point.
Software engineering is the disciplined design, construction, testing, deployment, operation, and maintenance of software systems.
A program can compile successfully and still be incorrect.
A program can run successfully and still produce incorrect results.
A program can pass one test and still contain defects.
Correctness must be established against defined requirements.
A requirement describes behavior, capability, constraint, or property a system must satisfy.
Requirements should be explicit.
Requirements should be testable when practical.
Requirements should avoid unnecessary ambiguity.
A requirement should distinguish required behavior from optional behavior.
A requirement should distinguish implementation constraints from desired outcomes.
Requirements can change.
Changes to requirements should be controlled.
A changed requirement can invalidate previously correct implementation behavior.
Engineering begins by understanding the problem.
Unknown requirements should remain unknown rather than being invented.
Assumptions should be identified as assumptions.
Evidence should be distinguished from inference.
Observed behavior should be distinguished from intended behavior.
Existing behavior should not automatically be interpreted as correct behavior.
Documentation can describe intended behavior.
Tests can provide evidence about expected behavior.
Source code can provide evidence about implemented behavior.
Runtime observation can provide evidence about actual behavior.
Authority must be established by the project.
Architecture should serve requirements.
Architecture should not exist merely to demonstrate complexity.
A small system does not require a complicated architecture.
Complexity should be justified by requirements.
A component is a bounded part of a software system.
Components should have clear responsibilities.
A component should expose defined interfaces.
An interface defines how another component interacts with it.
Implementation details should remain behind interfaces when they do not need to be exposed.
Encapsulation limits knowledge of implementation details.
Coupling describes dependencies between components.
High coupling means components depend heavily on one another.
Low coupling reduces unnecessary dependency.
Cohesion describes how closely related a component's responsibilities are.
High cohesion means a component has a focused purpose.
Good modular design generally favors high cohesion and controlled coupling.
A module should have a defined purpose.
A module should have a defined boundary.
A module should not silently assume authority belonging to another component.
A module should not modify unrelated state merely because it can access it.
Capability does not establish authority.
An API is an Application Programming Interface.
An API should define relevant state behavior.
An API contract is a behavioral agreement between provider and consumer.
Changing an API can break consumers.
Backward compatibility means newer software continues supporting defined behavior expected by older consumers.
Forward compatibility describes the ability of existing software to tolerate or interact with newer representations according to the protocol design.
Do not assume compatibility merely because two versions compile.
Protocol compatibility and implementation compatibility are different.
A dependency is software or functionality required by another component.
Dependencies should solve actual problems.
Dependencies require maintenance.
Dependencies can introduce vulnerabilities.
Dependencies can change behavior.
Dependencies can become unavailable.
Dependencies can create licensing obligations.
Do not reimplement complex specialized functionality merely to avoid a mature dependency when doing so increases risk.
Dependency decisions are engineering tradeoffs.
A dependency should have a defined reason for existing.
Unused dependencies should be removed when safe and authorized.
A build transforms source code and resources into executable or distributable artifacts.
A successful build proves that the build process completed according to its criteria.
A successful build does not prove correctness.
A successful build does not prove security.
A successful build does not prove compatibility.
A successful build does not prove runtime success.
Compiler warnings provide engineering evidence.
Warnings should be examined.
Warnings should not be ignored merely because compilation succeeded.
Treating warnings as errors can improve build discipline where appropriate.
Not every warning indicates a defect.
Suppressing a warning should have a reason.
A build should be reproducible when reproducibility is a project requirement.
A reproducible build produces equivalent required output from defined inputs under defined conditions.
Build inputs should be known.
Compiler versions can affect output.
Library versions can affect output.
Build flags can affect output.
Environment variables can affect output.
Build systems should avoid hidden dependencies.
A clean build starts without relying on stale previous build artifacts.
Clean builds help expose undeclared dependencies.
Incremental builds improve development speed.
An incremental build should not produce different required behavior from a clean build.
Source control records changes to project files.
A commit should represent coherent work when practical.
A commit message should describe the change sufficiently for project use.
Version control history is engineering evidence.
History should not be rewritten merely to make past development appear cleaner unless project policy explicitly permits the history operation.
A branch provides an independent line of development according to version-control semantics.
Branches can isolate work.
Merging combines development histories or changes.
Merge conflicts require deliberate resolution.
A conflict means automatic reconciliation was not possible.
A conflict does not indicate which side is correct.
Resolve conflicts according to intended project state.
Do not choose a conflict side merely because it is newer.
A patch is a change applied to existing software.
A patch should be bounded.
Unrelated cleanup increases patch scope.
Increased scope increases regression risk.
Do not refactor unrelated code during a narrowly scoped repair unless authorized or required.
A regression is a previously working behavior that becomes defective after a change.
Regression testing checks that established behavior remains correct after changes.
Every change has regression potential.
Small changes can cause large effects.
Before changing code, identify the failure.
Collect evidence.
Identify the responsible component.
Change the smallest appropriate boundary.
Build the changed software.
Test the changed behavior.
Test relevant surrounding behavior.
Inspect resulting logs or outputs.
Do not declare a defect fixed solely because compilation succeeded.
Debugging is the process of finding and understanding defects.
Begin with observed behavior.
Determine expected behavior.
Identify the difference.
Reduce the problem.
Test hypotheses.
Reject hypotheses contradicted by evidence.
Random changes can hide defects.
A symptom is not necessarily the root cause.
A root cause is the underlying condition producing the failure.
One root cause can produce multiple symptoms.
One symptom can have multiple possible causes.
Do not claim a root cause without evidence.
Logs provide evidence about execution.
Logs can show state transitions.
Logs can show failures.
Logs can show timing.
Logs can show component interaction.
Logs can also be incomplete.
Absence of a log entry does not automatically prove an operation did not occur unless logging is guaranteed for that operation.
Diagnostic output should be precise.
"Failed" is less useful than identifying the operation that failed.
An error should preserve relevant context.
An error should not expose secrets.
Error propagation allows lower-level failures to reach a level capable of handling or reporting them.
Do not silently discard meaningful errors.
A function returning failure should be checked when the interface requires checking.
Ignoring return values can convert detectable failures into later corruption.
Fail fast means stopping when continuing would violate required assumptions.
Fail safe means failure leaves the system in an acceptable state.
Fail fast and fail safe describe different properties.
A system can fail fast while failing unsafely.
A system can fail safely without terminating the entire process.
Failure policy should match the component's responsibility.
Unrecoverable failures should be reported accurately.
Retry can recover transient failures.
Retry is not appropriate for every failure.
Invalid input generally does not become valid through repetition.
Permission denial generally does not disappear through immediate repeated attempts.
Retries should be bounded.
Retries should have defined conditions.
Repeated retry without limits can create an infinite failure loop.
Backoff increases delay between retry attempts according to a defined strategy.
A retry storm occurs when many clients repeatedly retry failing operations and increase system load.
Jitter can distribute retry timing.
State-changing operations require careful retry semantics.
An operation is idempotent when repeating it produces the same intended state according to its defined semantics.
Setting a value to a specific value can be idempotent.
Creating a payment twice is not equivalent to creating it once.
Retry safety should be established before automatically repeating state-changing operations.
Testing evaluates software behavior.
A test should have a defined purpose.
A test should establish conditions.
A test should perform an operation.
A passing test is evidence for the behavior covered by that test.
A failing test is evidence that expected and actual behavior differ according to the test.
When a test fails, examine both implementation and test assumptions.
A unit test evaluates a small bounded component.
An integration test evaluates interaction between components.
An acceptance test evaluates behavior against acceptance requirements.
A regression test protects previously established behavior.
A smoke test checks basic viability after a build or deployment.
A load test evaluates behavior under defined workload.
A stress test evaluates behavior under conditions approaching or exceeding expected capacity.
A soak test evaluates behavior over extended operation.
A security test evaluates security properties.
Different tests answer different questions.
Do not treat a unit test as an end-to-end test.
Do not treat a successful smoke test as proof of production readiness.
Test boundaries should match claims.
If a test proves only parsing, do not report that networking passed.
If a test proves only local behavior, do not report distributed behavior as proven.
Negative testing checks how software handles invalid or unexpected conditions.
Negative tests are important.
Valid input is only part of an interface.
Invalid input should produce defined behavior.
Malformed input should not cause undefined behavior.
Missing input should be handled according to interface rules.
Oversized input should be bounded.
Testing should include boundaries.
One can be a boundary.
Maximum values can be boundaries.
Empty input can be a boundary.
Maximum permitted length can be a boundary.
Boundary-value testing finds defects that ordinary examples can miss.
Test data should be controlled.
Stubs provide controlled responses.
Fakes provide simplified functional implementations.
A test passing against a mock does not prove the real service behaves identically.
Integration testing verifies actual boundaries.
Deterministic tests produce repeatable results from controlled inputs.
Tests depending unnecessarily on network availability can become unstable.
Tests depending on random uncontrolled input can become unstable.
A flaky test passes and fails without a relevant code change.
Flaky tests reduce confidence.
Do not normalize flaky tests as acceptable.
Find the uncontrolled condition.
Randomized testing can be useful when randomness is deliberate and failures can be reproduced.
Record the random seed when reproducibility matters.
A test should not pass because an error path was silently skipped.
Assertions verify expected conditions.
Assertions used for development diagnostics should not necessarily replace runtime validation of untrusted input.
Production behavior should not depend on assertions that can be disabled unless the platform guarantees their execution.
Static analysis examines software without executing the complete program.
Static analysis can identify defects.
Static analysis can identify suspicious patterns.
Static analysis can identify type problems.
Static analysis findings require evaluation.
Dynamic analysis examines executing software.
Memory checkers can identify invalid memory operations.
Profilers measure runtime behavior.
Debuggers inspect program execution.
Sanitizers instrument programs to detect selected defects.
No single analysis tool proves software correct.
Code review evaluates changes before or after integration according to project process.
Review should examine correctness.
Review should examine interfaces.
Review should examine error handling.
Review should examine security implications.
Review should examine maintainability.
Review should not become an excuse to redesign unrelated components.
Review comments should distinguish defects from preferences.
A defect violates required behavior or engineering correctness.
A preference describes one acceptable alternative among several.
Do not present style preference as correctness failure.
Coding standards define project conventions.
Consistency reduces cognitive load.
Naming conventions should be followed within the project.
Formatting conventions should be followed within the project.
Existing project architecture should be respected.
Do not impose another project's conventions without authorization.
Project rules outrank generic stylistic preference within that project.
Comments should explain information that code alone does not express clearly.
Comments should not merely repeat obvious syntax.
Comments can explain why a decision exists.
Comments can explain constraints.
Comments can explain protocol requirements.
Outdated comments are dangerous.
Update comments when behavior changes.
Documentation should match actual behavior.
Documentation can describe installation.
Documentation can describe configuration.
Documentation can describe interfaces.
Documentation can describe architecture.
Documentation can describe operations.
Documentation can describe recovery.
Documentation should distinguish examples from requirements.
An example is not automatically normative.
A normative requirement states what must occur according to the governing specification.
Documentation should identify defaults.
Defaults should be safe and predictable.
Configuration should have defined syntax.
Configuration should have defined defaults.
Configuration should be validated.
Invalid configuration should produce a precise error.
Unknown configuration fields can be rejected or ignored according to defined compatibility policy.
Configuration precedence should be explicit.
Command-line configuration can override file configuration if defined.
Do not create hidden precedence rules.
Configuration files can contain secrets.
Secret configuration requires appropriate protection.
Configuration should not be confused with runtime state.
Configuration defines intended operating parameters.
Generated runtime state should not overwrite human-controlled configuration unless explicitly designed to do so.
State ownership should be clear.
A state machine represents behavior as states and transitions.
A state should have defined meaning.
A transition moves from one state to another according to defined conditions.
Invalid transitions should be rejected.
State-machine design can make lifecycle behavior deterministic.
Explicit state improves diagnosis.
A finite-state machine has a finite set of defined states.
Protocols commonly use state machines.
Service lifecycles can use state machines.
State transitions can be logged.
A lifecycle describes the states an entity passes through over time.
Activation can be a lifecycle event.
Suspension can be a lifecycle event.
Deletion can be a lifecycle event when deletion is permitted.
Not every lifecycle should include deletion.
Historical requirements can require permanent records.
Status changes can preserve history without deletion.
Under Semantic Versioning conventions, a major version commonly indicates incompatible API changes.
A patch version commonly indicates backward-compatible fixes.
Projects can define their own versioning policy.
Do not assume every project follows Semantic Versioning merely because its version contains three numbers.
A version number should identify a defined software state.
Two different artifacts should not casually claim the same version if they contain materially different controlled code.
Build metadata can identify build-specific information.
Release candidates are pre-release versions intended for release evaluation.
An RC is not automatically a final stable release.
Alpha versions generally represent early development.
Beta versions generally represent more mature pre-release testing.
Exact meanings belong to project policy.
A release should have identified source state.
A release should have identified version.
A release should have relevant documentation.
A release should have validation appropriate to the project.
Release notes describe meaningful changes.
Release notes should distinguish enhancements.
Release notes should distinguish compatibility changes.
A changelog records changes across versions.
A changelog is historical documentation.
Do not rewrite historical release descriptions merely because later understanding changed unless correction policy requires it.
Deprecation marks functionality as intended for future removal or replacement.
Deprecated functionality can remain operational.
Deprecation is not immediate removal.
Consumers should be given migration information when appropriate.
Removal should follow compatibility policy.
Maintenance is continued engineering after initial release.
Maintenance includes defect repair.
Maintenance includes security updates.
Maintenance includes compatibility updates.
Maintenance includes dependency updates.
Maintenance can include performance improvements.
Maintenance is part of software engineering, not an afterthought.
Technical debt represents future cost created by implementation or design decisions.
Not every imperfect design is technical debt.
A deliberate simple implementation can be correct engineering.
Complex abstraction added without need can itself create technical debt.
Refactoring changes internal structure while intending to preserve external behavior.
Refactoring should have a defined reason.
Refactoring can reduce duplication.
Refactoring can improve boundaries.
Refactoring introduces regression risk.
Optimization improves resource use or performance.
Optimization should be driven by measured requirements.
Measure before optimizing.
Identify the bottleneck.
Optimize the bottleneck.
Do not optimize code merely because it looks inefficient.
Readable correct code is often preferable to complicated speculative optimization.
Performance requirements should be measurable.
Latency measures elapsed time for an operation.
Throughput measures completed work over time.
CPU utilization measures processor usage.
Memory usage measures memory consumption.
Disk I/O measures storage activity.
Network I/O measures network activity.
Reducing latency can sometimes reduce throughput.
Performance engineering involves tradeoffs.
Scalability describes how system behavior changes as workload or resources increase.
Scalability is not synonymous with speed.
A slower system can scale predictably.
Do not design for imaginary scale without requirements.
Premature distributed architecture can increase failure modes dramatically.
A single process can be sufficient.
A single server can be sufficient.
A file can be sufficient.
Distributed systems should be introduced when their benefits justify their complexity.
Distributed systems experience partial failure.
One component can fail while another remains operational.
Messages can be delayed.
Messages can be duplicated.
Messages can be lost according to transport and protocol.
Clocks can differ.
Network partitions can occur.
Distributed software must define behavior under these conditions.
Do not treat a remote call as a local function call.
Remote calls can time out.
Remote calls can partially succeed.
Remote calls cross trust boundaries.
A timeout does not establish whether the remote operation occurred.
Idempotency and request identifiers can help manage uncertain outcomes.
Logs support observability.
Metrics support observability.
Traces support observability.
Health checks support observability.
Audit records support observability.
Debugging production systems without evidence is guesswork.
A metric is a numeric measurement over time.
A counter increases as events occur.
A gauge represents a value that can increase or decrease.
Metrics should have defined meaning.
A metric without units can be ambiguous.
A process being alive does not necessarily mean it is healthy.
A health check should test meaningful capability without causing harmful side effects.
A liveness check determines whether a component is operating sufficiently to remain running according to orchestration policy.
Health policy should understand dependency behavior.
Graceful degradation means continuing reduced but defined functionality when a dependency or capability is unavailable.
A degraded system should not claim full health.
A degraded system can remain operational.
Critical functionality should be distinguished from optional functionality.
An optional module failure should not necessarily terminate the core system.
A required component failure can prevent safe operation.
Component criticality should be defined.
A core system should know which dependencies are required.
A module system should validate modules before activating them.
Discovery means finding candidate modules.
Discovery does not establish qualification.
Qualification evaluates whether a candidate satisfies requirements.
Activation makes a qualified component operational.
Discovery, qualification, and activation are separate stages.
A discovered module can be invalid.
An invalid module should not automatically crash unrelated core functionality unless architecture requires it.
A module manifest describes module metadata according to the module system.
A missing required manifest is a qualification failure.
A malformed manifest is a qualification failure.
Version validation can be part of qualification.
ABI validation can be part of qualification.
Required symbol validation can be part of qualification.
Security validation can be part of qualification.
Qualification should produce evidence.
Hot loading activates a component without restarting the host process according to the module architecture.
Hot loading introduces lifecycle concerns.
A hot-loaded module must initialize correctly.
A hot-loaded module must not assume process startup state unless defined.
A module can require shutdown behavior.
A module can own resources.
Module unloading must account for active references and resources when unloading is supported.
Not every module system needs unloading.
Do not add unload complexity unless required.
Plugins should not bypass core boundaries.
A plugin implements the contract.
An extension point should be deliberate.
Exposing internal implementation accidentally as an extension interface creates fragile coupling.
Public interfaces should be smaller than internal implementation where practical.
The smaller an interface, the easier it is to reason about.
The smaller a trusted computing boundary, the easier it is to audit.
Simple systems are not simplistic when they satisfy requirements.
Complexity is not evidence of sophistication.
Abstraction is useful when it removes meaningful duplication or isolates meaningful variation.
Three similar lines of obvious code can sometimes be clearer than an elaborate generic framework.
Duplication should be evaluated by maintenance cost, not ideology.
A function should have a coherent responsibility.
A function should have clear outputs.
A function should have defined failure behavior.
Hidden global state makes functions harder to reason about.
Global state is not automatically forbidden.
Global state should be controlled when it is appropriate.
Constants can safely represent global immutable configuration or definitions where appropriate.
A variable name should describe what the value represents.
A function name should describe the operation.
A module name should describe responsibility.
Misleading names create defects.
Names should follow project conventions.
Short names are appropriate when context makes them obvious.
Long names are appropriate when additional specificity prevents ambiguity.
Code readability matters because software is maintained by humans.
Readable code reduces misunderstanding.
Readable code supports review.
Readable code supports debugging.
Readable code supports future changes.
Clever code can be expensive code.
Prefer obvious behavior when obvious behavior satisfies requirements.
Magic behavior is behavior occurring through hidden conventions or side effects.
Some frameworks use convention productively.
Hidden behavior should still be documented and predictable.
A developer should be able to determine why an operation occurred.
Determinism means the same defined inputs and state produce the same defined result where the system is intended to be deterministic.
Determinism improves testing.
Determinism improves reproducibility.
Determinism improves debugging.
Network timing is not deterministic.
Thread scheduling is not deterministic.
Random generation is not deterministic unless controlled.
External services can change.
A deterministic core can isolate nondeterministic boundaries.
Nondeterministic inputs should be made explicit.
Time is an input.
Randomness is an input.
Network responses are inputs.
Filesystem state is input.
Environment variables are input.
Configuration is input.
Treating these values as inputs makes behavior easier to reason about.
Validation establishes whether input satisfies defined requirements.
Parsing and validation are different.
Parsing converts representation into structure.
Validation determines whether that structure is acceptable.
Successfully parsed input can still be invalid.
Validation should occur at trust boundaries.
Internal components can rely on established invariants when architecture guarantees them.
Repeated redundant validation can increase complexity.
Never omit required validation merely because another layer probably performed it.
Trust boundaries should be explicit.
A trust boundary exists where data or authority crosses between domains with different trust assumptions.
Network input crosses a trust boundary.
Files supplied externally cross a trust boundary.
Configuration can cross a trust boundary depending on who controls it.
Validate according to the actual boundary.
Security should not be bolted on after implementation.
Least privilege reduces authority.
Input validation reduces malformed input risk.
Output encoding prevents selected interpretation problems.
Authorization determines permitted action.
Backups support recovery.
No single security mechanism makes software secure.
Reliability describes the ability to perform required behavior consistently under defined conditions.
Availability describes whether a system is accessible when required.
Reliability and availability are related but different.
A system can be reliable when running but unavailable too frequently.
Correctness, reliability, availability, security, and performance are different quality properties.
Engineering should identify which properties matter.
A safety-critical operation can fail closed.
A noncritical informational service can fail open according to requirements.
Failure policy should not be invented during an incident.
Operational behavior should be designed.
Deployment moves software into an execution environment.
Deployment should be repeatable.
Deployment should identify the artifact being installed.
Deployment should establish required configuration.
Deployment should establish permissions.
Deployment should establish dependencies.
Deployment should provide rollback or recovery where required.
Copying files manually can be a deployment method.
Manual deployment becomes error-prone as complexity grows.
Automation can improve repeatability.
A deployment script is software.
Deployment scripts require testing.
An installer is software.
Installers require testing.
Build scripts require maintenance.
Rollback restores a previous known state according to release strategy.
Rollback can be difficult when database schema or persistent state changed.
Forward repair can sometimes be safer than rollback.
Never assume replacing an executable restores the entire system.
State can survive software replacement.
An upgrade changes installed software or data to a newer version.
Skipping versions can require special handling.
Downgrades can be unsupported.
A migration should record completion when required.
Repeated migration execution should have defined behavior.
Maintenance windows provide controlled periods for changes when required.
Zero-downtime deployment is not necessary for every system.
Operational complexity should match availability requirements.
A release requiring five minutes of planned downtime can be preferable to a complicated zero-downtime architecture when requirements permit it.
Engineering is the management of tradeoffs under constraints.
Constraints can include time.
Constraints can include hardware.
Constraints can include compatibility.
Constraints can include staffing.
Constraints can include security.
Constraints can include regulation.
Constraints can include user requirements.
Ignoring a constraint does not solve it.
Technical elegance does not override an explicit requirement.
A simpler correct system is preferable to a complicated correct system when both satisfy the same requirements and constraints.
A system should do what it claims.
A system should report what it actually did.
A system should not claim success when an operation failed.
A system should not claim failure when an operation was never attempted.
A system should not invent evidence.
Unknown should remain unknown.
Failed should remain failed.
Successful should mean the defined success criteria were met.
Degraded should mean the system remains operational with defined capability loss.
Status words should have stable meanings.
Precise status supports automation.
Precise status supports operators.
Human operators establish project intent and authority.
Software executes within delegated authority.
Automation should not expand its authority merely because an operation is technically possible.
A tool should not modify files outside its assigned scope.
A module should not modify core components unless its contract permits it.
A repair should not become an unauthorized redesign.
A diagnostic operation should not become a destructive operation.
Read authority and write authority are different.
Permission to modify one component is not permission to modify another.
Engineering boundaries are part of correctness.
Source ownership matters.
Component ownership matters.
Data ownership matters.
State ownership matters.
Interface ownership matters.
Changes should occur at the boundary that owns the behavior.
If a module owns behavior, repair the module when appropriate.
Do not patch the wrong layer merely because it is easier to edit.
Determine responsibility first.
A workaround bypasses or mitigates a defect without necessarily correcting its root cause.
A workaround can be appropriate temporarily.
A workaround should be identified as a workaround.
Do not silently convert temporary workarounds into permanent architecture.
Emergency repairs can prioritize restoration.
Follow-up engineering can address deeper issues after service restoration.
Incident response and normal development have different priorities.
During an incident, preserve evidence.
Restore required service safely.
Avoid unrelated changes.
Document what changed.
After stabilization, investigate root cause.
Timestamps matter.
Observed facts should be separated from interpretation.
Do not rewrite incident history to match a later theory.
New evidence can change conclusions.
Historical observations remain historical observations.
Post-incident review examines what happened and how the system can improve.
The purpose is engineering improvement.
Useful questions include what failed.
Useful questions include why controls did or did not work.
Useful questions include how recovery behaved.
Useful questions include what change would prevent recurrence.
Do not assume every incident requires architectural redesign.
Sometimes the correct repair is small.
Sometimes a small symptom reveals a large architectural defect.
Evidence determines scope.
A specification defines required behavior.
An implementation realizes the specification.
Documentation explains the system.
Operations run the system.
These activities form a lifecycle.
Software engineering manages that lifecycle deliberately.
Good engineering preserves working behavior while changing required behavior.
Good engineering limits unnecessary scope.
Good engineering produces evidence.
Good engineering recognizes uncertainty.
Good engineering uses clear interfaces.
Good engineering handles failure.
Good engineering validates assumptions.
Good engineering respects authority boundaries.
Good engineering keeps systems understandable.
Do not confuse more code with more capability.
Do not confuse abstraction with architecture.
Do not confuse architecture with complexity.
Do not confuse execution with success.
Do not confuse a symptom with root cause.
Do not confuse a workaround with a repair.
Do not confuse absence of evidence with evidence of absence.
Do not confuse newer with correct.
Do not confuse automated with authoritative.
Do not confuse current state with history.
Do not confuse retry with recovery.
Do not confuse availability with correctness.
Do not confuse performance with scalability.
Do not confuse source compatibility with binary compatibility.
Do not confuse an API with its implementation.
Do not confuse a module with the core that hosts it.
Do not confuse a successful build with a successful release.
When changing software, establish what is wrong.
Establish what correct behavior should be.
Establish what evidence demonstrates the defect.
Make the smallest appropriate change.
Build it.
Test it.
Test relevant boundaries.
Observe the result.
Report what actually happened.
If the result failed, say it failed.
Software engineering is controlled change applied to software systems.
The objective is not merely to make code work once.
The objective is to make required behavior understandable, testable, maintainable, reproducible, and dependable.
API stands for Application Programming Interface.
An API defines how one software component can interact with another.
An API is a contract.
An API can define operations.
An API can define inputs.
An API can define data formats.
An API can define authentication requirements.
An API can define authorization requirements.
An API can define compatibility requirements.
An API is not necessarily a network service.
A library can expose an API.
An operating system exposes APIs.
A network service can expose an API.
An HTTP service can expose an API.
A command-line program can provide an interface consumed by other software.
An API describes interaction rather than implementation.
Consumers should not need to know unnecessary implementation details behind an API.
An API provider is software that implements an API.
An API contract should be explicit.
A contract should define valid requests.
A contract should define valid responses.
A contract should define failure behavior.
A contract should define relevant limits.
A contract should define version behavior where versions exist.
A consumer should not rely on undocumented behavior.
Observed behavior is not automatically guaranteed behavior.
An implementation detail is not automatically part of the API contract.
Changing an implementation detail should not affect consumers when the contract remains unchanged.
Changing the contract can affect consumers.
API stability reduces unnecessary coupling between components.
A local API operates within the same machine or process environment.
A remote API crosses a communication boundary.
A remote request can time out.
A network can fail.
A server can be unavailable.
A response can be lost.
A request can arrive more than once.
A response can arrive later than expected.
A remote service can return malformed data.
A remote service can return an unexpected version.
A remote service can partially complete an operation.
HTTP stands for Hypertext Transfer Protocol.
HTTPS protects transport according to TLS properties.
HTTPS does not automatically make an API logically correct.
HTTPS does not automatically provide application authorization.
HTTPS does not automatically validate request data.
An HTTP request contains a method.
An HTTP request contains a target.
An HTTP request can contain a body.
An HTTP response contains headers.
An HTTP response can contain a body.
The HTTP method communicates request semantics.
GET is commonly used to retrieve a representation.
POST is commonly used to submit data or request processing.
PUT is commonly used to create or replace a resource representation according to API semantics.
PATCH is commonly used to modify part of a resource.
HEAD requests response metadata corresponding to GET without the normal response body.
HTTP methods have defined protocol semantics.
GET should normally be safe according to HTTP semantics.
A safe method is intended not to request a state-changing action.
Safe does not mean the server performs no internal activity.
A GET request can be logged.
A GET request should not ordinarily perform an application action such as deleting a record.
Idempotency means repeating an operation has the same intended effect as performing it once according to the operation semantics.
GET is intended to be idempotent.
PUT is intended to be idempotent.
DELETE is intended to be idempotent according to HTTP method semantics.
POST is not inherently idempotent.
PATCH is not inherently idempotent.
An API can implement additional idempotency mechanisms.
A client can time out after the server successfully processes a request.
The client may not know whether the operation occurred.
Blindly repeating a non-idempotent operation can duplicate state changes.
A resource is an entity exposed through an API according to the API model.
A resource can have an identifier.
A resource can have a representation.
A representation describes resource state in a particular format.
JSON is commonly used for API representations.
JSON is not required for an API.
APIs can use XML.
APIs can use plain text.
APIs can use binary formats.
APIs can use protocol-specific encodings.
The format should be explicitly defined.
application/json identifies JSON content.
A client sending JSON should use the appropriate Content-Type when the API requires it.
A server should validate content type according to the API contract.
Accept can communicate response media types acceptable to a client.
Content negotiation selects a representation according to supported media types and HTTP rules.
An API does not need multiple formats merely because HTTP supports content negotiation.
Keep the interface as small as requirements permit.
JSON parsing and JSON validation are different.
Parsing determines whether input is syntactically valid JSON.
A valid JSON document can be an invalid API request.
Required fields should be validated.
Field types should be validated.
Allowed values should be validated.
Length limits should be validated.
Numeric ranges should be validated.
Unknown fields should be handled according to API policy.
Malformed requests should not be silently interpreted as something else.
Input validation should occur before trusted processing.
An API schema can formally describe data structures.
JSON Schema can describe JSON structure and constraints.
OpenAPI can describe HTTP APIs.
An API description can document methods.
An API description can document request bodies.
An API description can document authentication mechanisms.
The implementation should be tested against the contract.
An endpoint identifies an API interaction target according to the API design.
An HTTP endpoint commonly consists of a path combined with supported methods.
The same path can support different methods.
GET /items and POST /items can represent different operations.
The method is part of the interface.
A URL can contain path parameters.
A request can contain body parameters.
These serve different purposes.
Path parameters commonly identify resources or hierarchy.
Query parameters commonly modify retrieval, filtering, sorting, or pagination.
Request bodies commonly carry structured input for state-changing or complex operations.
These are conventions rather than universal laws.
The API contract determines actual meaning.
Do not accept the same security-sensitive parameter from multiple locations without a defined precedence rule.
Ambiguous parameter precedence creates unpredictable behavior.
HTTP status codes are grouped by class.
1xx status codes represent informational responses.
2xx status codes represent successful processing.
3xx status codes represent redirection.
4xx status codes represent client-side request conditions or failures.
5xx status codes represent server-side failures.
200 OK indicates successful handling with an applicable response.
201 Created indicates successful creation of a resource.
202 Accepted indicates a request was accepted for processing but processing is not necessarily complete.
204 No Content indicates successful processing with no response content.
A 202 response does not mean the requested operation has completed.
An asynchronous operation should expose completion state when consumers need it.
400 Bad Request indicates the server cannot or will not process the request because of a client request problem according to the interface.
401 Unauthorized is commonly used when valid authentication credentials are absent or insufficient for authentication.
Despite its name, HTTP 401 primarily concerns authentication.
404 Not Found indicates the requested resource was not found or is not exposed as existing according to API policy.
413 Content Too Large indicates request content exceeds accepted limits.
422 Unprocessable Content can indicate syntactically acceptable content that cannot be processed according to defined semantics.
429 Too Many Requests indicates rate limiting or request-frequency limits.
500 Internal Server Error indicates an unexpected server-side failure.
502 Bad Gateway indicates a gateway or proxy received an invalid response from an upstream server.
503 Service Unavailable indicates temporary service unavailability.
504 Gateway Timeout indicates a gateway or proxy did not receive a timely upstream response.
Status codes should describe the actual result.
Do not return 200 merely because the server generated a response.
An error response is still a response.
A successful TCP connection does not establish API success.
A successful TLS connection does not establish API success.
An HTTP response does not establish application success.
A 2xx status normally communicates application-level success according to the API contract.
An API error body can provide structured diagnostic information.
Error responses should be predictable.
An error representation can contain an error code.
An error representation can contain a human-readable message.
An error representation can contain relevant field errors.
An error representation can contain a request or correlation identifier.
Internal stack traces should not ordinarily be exposed to remote clients.
Database credentials should not appear in API errors.
Filesystem paths should not be exposed without reason.
Tokens should not appear in API errors.
Machine-readable error codes are more stable than parsing human-readable messages.
Error codes should have stable meanings.
Do not reuse one error code for unrelated conditions.
A request identifier identifies one request for tracing or diagnostic purposes.
A correlation identifier can associate activity across multiple components.
Identifiers can make distributed debugging easier.
A request ID is not automatically an authentication credential.
A request ID should not grant authority.
Authorization determines what an authenticated identity can do.
Authentication and authorization are separate.
An API can require authentication.
An API can require authorization for selected operations.
Authentication should occur before relying on identity.
Authorization should occur before protected action.
A valid credential does not grant unlimited authority.
API keys can authenticate or identify clients according to the API design.
API keys are secrets when possession grants access.
API keys should not be embedded in publicly delivered client-side code when secrecy is required.
API keys should not be written into public repositories.
API keys should not be exposed in logs.
Bearer tokens grant defined access through possession.
Bearer tokens must be protected.
An Authorization header commonly carries authentication credentials in HTTP APIs.
Bearer tokens are commonly transmitted using the Authorization header with the Bearer scheme.
Credentials should not normally be placed in query strings.
URLs can be stored in logs.
URLs can be stored in browser history.
URLs can be exposed through monitoring systems.
Sensitive credentials belong in appropriate protected request mechanisms.
Basic authentication transmits a username and password-derived credential encoding according to HTTP authentication rules.
Base64 encoding used by Basic authentication is not encryption.
Basic authentication should use protected transport when credentials require confidentiality.
OAuth is an authorization framework.
OAuth is not one single authentication protocol.
OAuth defines flows for delegated authorization according to its specifications.
OpenID Connect adds an identity layer on top of OAuth 2.0.
JWT stands for JSON Web Token.
A JWT is a token format.
A JWT can contain claims.
JWT payload data can often be decoded by anyone possessing the token.
Do not store confidential data in a merely signed JWT unless disclosure is acceptable.
A token signature must be verified before trusting signed claims.
Parsing a JWT is not verification.
Verification is not authorization.
Claims must be validated according to the application.
An expiration claim can define token expiration.
An audience claim can identify intended recipients.
Applications should validate claims required by their security model.
An expired token should not be treated as current merely because its signature remains cryptographically valid.
API sessions can use cookies.
Cookies can carry session identifiers.
Session identifiers should be unpredictable when they function as credentials.
A session identifier is not the same as user identity.
Cookies have security attributes.
HttpOnly prevents ordinary client-side script access to the cookie according to browser rules.
Cookie security settings should follow application requirements.
Cookie-authenticated APIs can be exposed to CSRF risks.
CSRF stands for Cross-Site Request Forgery.
CSRF causes a browser to send an authenticated request the user did not intend.
State-changing browser requests should use appropriate CSRF defenses when the authentication model requires them.
Using POST alone does not prevent CSRF.
A CSRF token can bind a state-changing request to an authorized user interaction according to the application mechanism.
CORS is a browser security mechanism governing selected cross-origin web requests.
CORS is not authentication.
CORS is not authorization.
Allowing an origin through CORS does not grant that origin application permissions by itself.
Disabling CORS restrictions does not fix an API authentication problem.
CORS should be configured according to actual browser-client requirements.
A REST-style API commonly models resources and uses HTTP semantics.
An API can use HTTP without being RESTful.
An API can be well designed without strictly following REST.
Architecture should serve requirements rather than terminology.
If an operation is naturally a command, forcing it into artificial CRUD terminology can reduce clarity.
CRUD stands for Create, Read, Update, and Delete.
CRUD describes common data operations.
Validation can be an operation.
Compilation can be an operation.
Submission can be an operation.
Signing can be an operation.
Processing can be an operation.
RPC stands for Remote Procedure Call.
An RPC API models remote operations as procedure or method calls.
JSON-RPC is an RPC protocol using JSON.
gRPC is an RPC framework using Protocol Buffers and HTTP/2 or related supported transport behavior.
RPC is not inherently inferior to REST.
REST is not inherently superior to RPC.
The appropriate interface depends on system requirements.
A protocol should be deterministic where deterministic behavior is required.
Given the same valid request and relevant state, a deterministic operation should produce the defined result.
An API should not return arbitrary different meanings for identical status fields.
Field semantics should remain stable.
A boolean should not sometimes mean success and sometimes mean availability.
One field should have one defined purpose.
Response schemas should be explicit.
Optional fields should be identified as optional.
Required fields should be identified as required.
Missing and null are not necessarily the same.
A missing field means the field is absent.
A null field explicitly carries a null value.
The API contract determines whether these states are equivalent.
Consumers should not invent values for missing fields unless the contract defines defaults.
Defaults should be explicit.
A default is part of behavior.
Changing a default can be a compatibility change.
Pagination divides large result sets into bounded responses.
An API should not return unbounded data when the dataset can grow without limit.
Pagination can use page numbers.
Pagination can use offsets.
Pagination can use cursors.
Pagination can use continuation tokens.
Clients should not parse opaque continuation tokens.
A consumer should return them unchanged when instructed.
Sorting determines result ordering.
Without defined ordering, clients should not rely on accidental database or filesystem order.
A unique tie-breaker can make ordering deterministic.
Rate limiting bounds request frequency or resource use.
Rate limits protect service capacity.
Rate limiting is not a replacement for authorization.
A rate limit can apply per identity.
A rate limit can apply per address.
A rate limit can apply per token.
A rate limit can apply globally.
The rate-limit policy should be defined.
A 429 response commonly indicates rate limiting.
Retry-After can tell clients when retry may be appropriate according to HTTP semantics.
Repeated aggressive retry can worsen an outage.
Timeouts should be bounded.
A client should not wait forever for a remote API.
Connection timeout limits connection establishment time.
Read timeout limits waiting for response data according to the client implementation.
Overall request timeout limits total operation duration.
Different timeout categories should not be confused.
A timeout does not prove the server did nothing.
A timeout establishes that the expected result was not received within the defined time.
Backoff delays repeated retries.
Exponential backoff increases retry delay progressively.
Jitter varies retry timing.
Backoff and jitter reduce synchronized retry storms.
Retries should be bounded.
Permanent errors should not be retried indefinitely.
A 401 authentication failure normally requires valid credentials rather than immediate identical retry.
A 403 authorization failure normally requires changed authority or policy rather than identical retry.
A 404 normally does not become successful through immediate repeated requests unless resource creation or eventual consistency is expected.
A 429 can be retryable according to rate-limit policy.
A 503 commonly indicates temporary unavailability.
A circuit breaker temporarily stops requests to a failing dependency according to defined thresholds.
Circuit breakers can prevent cascading failure.
A circuit breaker has states.
An open circuit rejects or redirects calls without repeatedly contacting the failing dependency.
Bulkheads isolate resources so one failing workload does not consume all capacity.
A separate worker pool can isolate one dependency.
Isolation can limit cascading failures.
API gateways sit between clients and backend services.
A gateway can route requests.
A gateway can enforce rate limits.
A gateway can terminate TLS.
A gateway can transform requests.
A gateway does not remove the need for correct backend authorization when backend trust boundaries require it.
Reverse proxies forward client requests to backend services.
A reverse proxy can provide TLS termination.
A reverse proxy can provide load balancing.
Forwarded client-address headers should not be blindly trusted from arbitrary clients.
A load balancer distributes requests among service instances.
Load balancing improves capacity or availability according to architecture.
Load balancing does not guarantee application state consistency.
Stateful applications can require session affinity or shared state.
Stateless API design makes horizontal distribution easier.
Stateless does not mean the system stores no data.
Stateless means each request contains or references sufficient context so the server does not require hidden per-client conversational state according to the interface model.
Persistent database state is compatible with stateless HTTP request handling.
Caching stores reusable results.
HTTP defines caching mechanisms.
Cache-Control communicates caching directives.
If-None-Match can request conditional retrieval using an ETag.
A server can return 304 Not Modified when the client's cached representation remains valid according to the condition.
Caching can reduce latency and load.
Caching can return stale data if invalidation is incorrect.
Sensitive responses should not be cached where policy forbids it.
Cache behavior should be explicit.
ETags can support optimistic concurrency.
