Do not assume the optimizer is wrong without evidence.
Debug builds commonly include debugging information.
These are project conventions rather than language requirements.
A release should be tested using the configuration actually intended for release.
Source-code instruction order is not necessarily machine instruction order.
Compiler optimization can inline functions.
Inlining replaces or integrates a function call with equivalent generated behavior according to optimization decisions.
The inline keyword does not simply command the compiler to inline a function.
inline also participates in language linkage and definition rules.
The compiler can inline functions not marked inline.
Constant folding evaluates constant expressions during compilation when permitted.
Dead-code elimination removes code whose effects cannot affect required observable behavior.
Common-subexpression elimination can avoid repeated equivalent calculations.
Loop optimizations can transform iteration.
Vectorization can use SIMD instructions for suitable operations.
Optimization should preserve defined program semantics.
Undefined behavior is commonly abbreviated UB.
Undefined behavior is not the same as an ordinary runtime error.
Undefined behavior means the language standard imposes no requirements for that situation.
Accessing outside valid array bounds can produce undefined behavior.
Using an object after its lifetime ended can produce undefined behavior.
Undefined behavior should be removed rather than depended upon.
Behavior observed from one compiler does not define undefined behavior.
Behavior observed from one optimization level does not define undefined behavior.
Behavior observed repeatedly does not make undefined behavior valid.
Implementation-defined behavior is behavior where the implementation chooses among permitted possibilities and documents the choice.
Unspecified behavior allows an implementation to choose among permitted possibilities without necessarily documenting the choice.
Undefined behavior imposes no requirements.
These categories are different.
Portability requires understanding which behavior the language guarantees.
Compiler extensions provide behavior beyond the language standard.
Extensions can reduce portability.
If ISO C portability is required, compiler-specific extensions should be avoided or isolated.
A compiler flag can select a language standard.
