After compilation, an assembler can translate assembly language into machine-code object representation.
A compiler can invoke the assembler automatically.
Developers do not always invoke the assembler directly.
Assembly language represents processor instructions and related directives.
Machine code is encoded instructions executed by a processor.
x86-64 assembly differs from ARM64 assembly.
Architecture targeting matters.
The host is the system running the toolchain.
The target is the system for which output is generated.
Cross-compilation is common for embedded systems.
Cross-compilation is common for operating-system development.
