Trace backward.
Memory errors can be nondeterministic in appearance.
Allocator layout can change between runs.
Thread scheduling can change timing.
ASLR can change addresses.
Optimization can change object placement.
Different input can change allocation order.
A defect disappearing after adding a log statement can indicate timing or layout sensitivity.
It does not mean the logging fixed the defect.
Heisenbug is an informal term for a defect whose behavior changes when observed or instrumented.
Memory and concurrency defects commonly exhibit this behavior.
Testing should verify memory behavior under realistic builds.
Debug allocators can mask layout-sensitive defects.
Optimized builds can expose undefined behavior.
Memory limits can be deliberate.
Containers can impose memory limits.
Operating systems can impose process limits.
Job-control systems can impose memory limits.
A program can run successfully on a developer machine and fail under a constrained deployment.
Test within expected resource limits.
A memory budget defines how much memory a component is expected to consume.
Estimate fixed memory.
Estimate per-connection memory.
Estimate per-request memory.
Estimate queue memory.
Estimate cache memory.
Estimate peak temporary memory.
Many simultaneous operations can multiply temporary memory.
Memory complexity describes how storage requirements grow with input size.
An algorithm using O(1) auxiliary memory uses bounded additional storage independent of input size under the model.
Big-O notation describes growth behavior.
Constant factors still matter.
Measure when memory matters.
