Define length.
Define lifetime.
Define release mechanism.
Define mutability.
Do not force callers to infer these properties.
When debugging memory corruption, find the earliest invalid operation.
Check ownership.
Check initialization.
Check allocation sizes.
Check concurrent access.
Check module boundaries.
Use tools to establish evidence.
Do not guess from the final crash alone.
Bound workers.
Bound queues.
Bound request sizes.
Bound message sizes.
Bound parser depth.
Bound retries that retain memory.
Bound retained history where policy permits.
Measure peak memory.
Test sustained operation.
Memory is finite.
Address space is finite.
Allocator metadata consumes memory.
Threads consume memory.
Queues consume memory.
Connections consume memory.
Caches consume memory.
Logs consume memory.
Temporary operations consume memory.
Every unbounded structure is a potential resource-exhaustion path.
Simple ownership reduces memory defects.
Explicit lengths reduce bounds defects.
Explicit lifetimes reduce dangling references.
Explicit allocation limits reduce exhaustion.
Explicit serialization reduces platform dependence.
Explicit synchronization reduces shared-memory races.
Memory should be treated as a controlled resource.
Allocate deliberately.
Own explicitly.
Bound precisely.
Synchronize when shared.
Release deterministically when the ownership model requires it.
Persist only what must survive.
A protocol defines rules governing communication between participants.
A protocol can define message structure.
A protocol can define valid message sequences.
A protocol can define error behavior.
A protocol can define compatibility requirements.
A protocol can define security requirements.
A protocol is more than a message format.
A message format describes representation.
Two implementations can use identical message encoding while implementing different protocol semantics.
Two implementations can implement equivalent protocol semantics using different internal code.
A protocol participant is an entity communicating according to the protocol.
Participants can be peers.
Participants can have symmetric roles.
Participants can have asymmetric roles.
Roles determine which operations a participant is permitted or expected to perform.
Transport and protocol are different layers.
TCP can carry an application protocol.
UDP can carry an application protocol.
A local socket can carry an application protocol.
Shared memory can carry protocol-like messages.
A serial connection can carry a protocol.
The protocol defines what that information means.
Transport connectivity does not establish protocol compatibility.
A successful TCP connection proves that transport establishment succeeded according to the observed connection.
It does not prove that the remote endpoint speaks the expected application protocol.
It does not prove that the remote endpoint uses a compatible protocol version.
It does not prove that the remote endpoint belongs to the expected network.
It does not prove that the remote endpoint is authorized.
Protocol qualification requires protocol-level evidence.
A protocol message has defined semantics.
Message syntax describes how the message is represented.
Valid syntax does not guarantee valid semantics.
A syntactically valid message can contain an invalid state transition.
A syntactically valid message can contain an unauthorized operation.
A syntactically valid message can contain contradictory fields.
Validation must include semantics where required.
A protocol should define message boundaries.
Message framing determines where one message ends and another begins.
TCP is a byte stream.
TCP does not preserve application message boundaries.
One send operation can be received through multiple receive operations.
Multiple send operations can be returned through one receive operation.
Application protocols using TCP must implement framing.
A fixed-length protocol uses messages of predetermined size.
A length-prefixed protocol includes information describing message length.
A self-describing encoding can indicate structural boundaries.
Each framing method has requirements.
A receiver should not assume one network receive equals one complete protocol message.
A receiver should accumulate bytes until a complete frame exists.
A receiver should preserve excess bytes belonging to subsequent frames.
Length fields must be validated.
A length must not exceed the protocol maximum.
A length must not cause arithmetic overflow.
A length must not cause unbounded allocation.
A message claiming an impossible length should be rejected.
A truncated message should be rejected or treated as incomplete according to protocol state.
A zero-length message should have defined semantics if it is permitted.
Maximum message size should be explicit.
Bounded messages protect memory.
Bounded messages protect processing time.
Bounded messages simplify validation.
Unbounded protocol input is a resource-exhaustion risk.
Protocol fields should have defined types.
Protocol fields should have defined sizes where binary representation requires them.
Protocol fields should have defined encoding.
Protocol fields should have defined valid ranges.
Protocol fields should have defined optionality.
Protocol fields should have defined default behavior if defaults exist.
Do not infer field meaning from implementation convenience.
The protocol definition is authoritative for protocol behavior.
Binary protocols encode messages as bytes according to a defined layout.
Text protocols encode messages using textual representations.
Binary protocols are not inherently more secure than text protocols.
Text protocols are not inherently easier to validate correctly.
Security depends on implementation and protocol design.
A binary protocol should not serialize native structures directly unless native representation is explicitly the protocol format.
Native structures can contain padding.
Native structures can depend on compiler ABI.
Native structures can depend on architecture.
Native structures can depend on endianness.
Protocol serialization should be explicit.
Canonical serialization produces one defined byte representation for equivalent logical protocol data.
Canonical serialization matters when bytes are hashed.
Canonical serialization matters when bytes are signed.
Canonical serialization matters when bytes determine identifiers.
Canonical serialization matters when consensus depends on exact representation.
If multiple byte representations are valid for the same logical value, signatures and hashes can differ.
A protocol state machine represents valid protocol states and transitions.
A connection can begin in an initial state.
Successful qualification can move it into a ready state.
A shutdown request can move it into a closing state.
Connection termination can move it into a closed state.
Exact states depend on the protocol.
State names should describe actual protocol conditions.
Messages valid in one state can be invalid in another.
A data message received before required handshake completion can be rejected.
An authentication response received before an authentication challenge can be invalid.
A commit acknowledgment received when no operation is pending can be invalid.
State validation prevents messages from being interpreted outside their intended context.
Protocol state should be explicit.
Implicit state hidden across unrelated variables makes validation difficult.
A state transition should have defined preconditions.
A state transition should have defined effects.
A state transition should have defined failure behavior.
Invalid transitions should be rejected.
Protocol parsers and protocol state machines solve different problems.
The state machine determines whether the candidate message is valid in the current protocol state.
Parsing success does not establish protocol acceptance.
Protocol acceptance does not automatically establish authorization.
A handshake is an initial protocol exchange used to establish required communication properties.
A handshake can establish protocol identity.
A handshake can establish protocol version.
A handshake can establish network identity.
A handshake can establish participant identity.
A handshake can negotiate capabilities.
A handshake can establish cryptographic state.
A handshake can authenticate participants.
Not every handshake performs every function.
Handshake success means only that the handshake's defined criteria succeeded.
Do not claim authentication if the handshake did not authenticate.
Do not claim authorization if the handshake only authenticated.
Do not claim compatibility if required capabilities were not checked.
A magic value can help detect incorrect input.
A magic value is not authentication.
Anyone who knows the value can reproduce it.
Network identifiers distinguish protocol networks.
A network identifier can prevent accidental cross-network communication.
A network identifier is not necessarily a secret.
A genesis identifier can distinguish independently initialized histories where a protocol uses genesis state.
A version field identifies protocol version according to the protocol's versioning scheme.
Version numbers require defined semantics.
Version 2 is not automatically compatible with version 1.
A larger version number is not automatically better.
Unsupported versions should be rejected or negotiated according to protocol rules.
Version negotiation allows participants to select a mutually supported version.
A downgrade attack attempts to cause participants to use weaker protocol behavior than they otherwise support.
Version negotiation should therefore be authenticated or protected where the threat model requires it.
Backward compatibility means a newer implementation can interact with defined older behavior according to compatibility policy.
Forward compatibility means an older implementation can tolerate defined newer information according to compatibility policy.
Backward compatibility and forward compatibility are different.
Unknown fields can be ignored only when the protocol permits them to be ignored.
Unknown fields can be critical.
Unknown message types can be rejected.
Unknown message types can be ignored.
Unknown message types can be forwarded.
The protocol must define the behavior.
Silently guessing how to handle unknown messages creates interoperability risk.
Capabilities describe optional participant functionality.
Capability negotiation allows participants to identify supported features.
A capability should have defined semantics.
A capability should not be assumed merely because a protocol version commonly includes it.
Validate advertised capabilities.
An advertised capability does not prove the participant will perform it correctly.
Capability advertisement establishes a claim.
Required capabilities can be qualification criteria.
Optional capabilities should not become accidental requirements.
A protocol extension adds behavior beyond an earlier protocol definition.
Extensions should preserve compatibility according to the protocol's rules.
An extension should have a defined identifier.
An extension should define what happens when a peer does not support it.
Extensions should not silently change existing field meaning.
Changing existing semantics without a version or capability boundary can break interoperability.
Protocol evolution should preserve determinism where deterministic interpretation is required.
The same valid message under the same protocol version should have the same defined meaning.
Implementation-specific interpretation should not alter protocol semantics.
A request asks another participant to perform or provide something according to protocol semantics.
Not every protocol is request-response.
Some protocols are event-driven.
Some protocols use streaming.
Some protocols use publish-subscribe.
Some protocols combine these patterns.
Request identifiers associate responses with requests.
A request identifier should be unique within its defined scope.
The scope can be one connection.
The scope can be one participant.
The scope can be one session.
The scope can be globally unique according to the protocol.
The scope must be defined.
A response should identify the request it answers when multiple requests can be outstanding.
Without correlation, concurrent request-response traffic can become ambiguous.
A protocol can allow only one outstanding request.
That simplifies correlation.
It can reduce concurrency.
Protocol complexity should match requirements.
Sequence numbers establish order within a defined scope.
Sequence numbers can detect missing messages.
Sequence numbers can detect duplicates.
Sequence numbers can detect reordering.
