A checksum is not normally cryptographic authentication.
Cryptographic validity does not establish protocol validity.
A correctly signed malformed message should still be rejected.
A correctly signed unauthorized operation should still be rejected.
Protocol validation remains necessary after cryptographic validation.
Authorization determines whether an authenticated or otherwise identified participant may perform an operation.
Authentication and authorization are different.
Network location should not substitute for authorization unless explicitly justified by the trust model.
An ACK is an acknowledgment.
ACK semantics must be defined.
An ACK can mean bytes were received.
An ACK can mean a message was parsed.
An ACK can mean an operation was accepted.
An ACK can mean an operation was persisted.
An ACK can mean an operation was applied.
These are different facts.
Do not interpret an ACK as stronger evidence than its definition provides.
A NACK is a negative acknowledgment.
A NACK reports rejection or failure according to protocol semantics.
A NACK should include a defined reason when useful and safe.
Error codes should have stable meanings.
Unsupported operation is different from temporary unavailability.
Version mismatch is different from internal failure.
