A protocol error means communication violated protocol expectations.
An application error means the protocol exchange was valid but the requested application operation failed.
Transport errors, protocol errors, and application errors are different.
A timeout is a protocol or application waiting condition depending on context.
Timeouts should be bounded.
Connect timeout differs from read timeout.
Handshake timeout differs from request timeout.
One generic timeout value can be inappropriate for every stage.
The remote participant can receive and complete a request while the response is delayed or lost.
Operations with uncertain outcomes require defined recovery behavior.
Read operations are often idempotent.
Increment operations are generally not idempotent.
Creating a unique external action can be non-idempotent.
Request identifiers or idempotency keys can allow a receiver to recognize repeated logical operations.
Deduplication requires state.
Deduplication state requires retention.
Retention duration should cover expected retry behavior.
A retry using a new identifier can appear to be a new operation.
Retry policy and identifier policy must agree.
At-most-once processing attempts to avoid duplicate effects but can lose operations under failures.
