Signedness matters.
A negative length should not become a large unsigned length through conversion.
Integer multiplication can overflow.
Integer addition can overflow.
Offset plus length must be validated safely.
Never validate an overflowed result as though the arithmetic itself were trustworthy.
Variable-length integer encodings should have maximum encoded lengths.
Malformed variable-length values should not cause infinite loops.
Recursive message structures should have depth limits.
Nested data can exhaust stack or memory.
Compression can create decompression bombs.
Define decompressed-size limits.
Protocol input is a resource-consumption surface.
Connection count should be bounded.
Pending requests should be bounded.
Parser work should be proportional to acceptable input size.
Attackers can send valid-looking expensive messages.
Semantic validation can itself require resource controls.
Rate limiting restricts operations over time according to policy.
Rate limiting can protect services.
Rate limiting is not authentication.
Rate limiting is not authorization.
Rate limiting is not protocol validation.
A malformed-message limit can disconnect abusive peers.
Policy should distinguish malicious behavior from ordinary transient errors.
Do not permanently condemn a participant merely because one packet was corrupted unless policy requires it.
Protocol implementations should fail safely.
Malformed input should not crash the process.
Rejected input should not partially mutate authoritative state.
Fail closed where authority cannot be established.
Protocol logging should record useful evidence.
Record connection termination.
Record handshake result.
Record negotiated version.
Record protocol rejection reasons.
Record malformed-message events.
Record request identifiers where useful.
Record state transitions where useful.
Do not log secrets.
Do not log authentication tokens unnecessarily.
Protocol logs should distinguish local and remote events.
"Connection failed" is less useful than a defined failure stage.
DNS resolution failure differs from TCP connect failure.
TCP connect failure differs from handshake failure.
Version rejection differs from authentication failure.
Authentication failure differs from authorization failure.
Authorization failure differs from operation failure.
Report the stage that failed.
A protocol invariant is a condition that must always hold for valid protocol state.
An invariant can require one active leader per term according to a protocol.
An invariant can require message length to remain within limits.
An invariant can require request identifiers not to be reused within a session.
An invariant can require authentication before privileged operations.
An invariant can require monotonically increasing sequence numbers.
Invariants should be enforced at boundaries.
Assertions can detect internal invariant violations during development.
Untrusted input should not be allowed to trigger unsafe assumptions.
A protocol implementation should not assert that peer-controlled data is valid and then terminate when it is not.
Validate external data.
Reserve assertions primarily for internal conditions that should be impossible if the implementation is correct.
Protocol state should not be inferred from log text.
State should exist explicitly in the implementation.
A protocol implementation can be table-driven.
A table can map message types and states to handlers.
A protocol implementation can use generated parsers.
Implementation style is secondary to correctness and clarity.
Small protocols can benefit from straightforward code.
Protocol code should be deterministic where protocol semantics require deterministic behavior.
Given the same valid state and same valid message, the resulting protocol state should be defined.
Local clock differences should not alter deterministic validation unless time rules explicitly account for them.
Windows and Linux implementations should agree on message semantics.
Different CPU architectures should agree on protocol semantics.
Protocol numbers should not use native integer representation directly.
Define widths.
Define byte order.
Define overflow behavior.
A protocol can use text decimal representation when exact parsing rules are defined.
Deterministic parsing requires one defined interpretation.
Whitespace handling should be defined for text protocols.
Line endings should be defined.
Empty lines should have defined meaning.
Duplicate fields should have defined behavior.
Field ordering should have defined behavior.
Do not accept ambiguous messages differently across implementations.
