A protocol can define authoritative responses.
Authority derives from protocol rules.
Authority does not derive merely from a familiar hostname.
A protocol endpoint should be qualified before authoritative operations when qualification is required.
Transport connects candidates.
Handshake identifies candidates.
Validation qualifies candidates.
Authorization permits operations.
These are separate stages.
A peer can pass discovery and fail connection.
A peer can connect and fail handshake.
A peer can pass handshake syntax and fail network identity.
A peer can pass identity and fail version compatibility.
A peer can pass compatibility and fail authentication.
A peer can authenticate and fail authorization.
A peer can be authorized for one operation and unauthorized for another.
A protocol can use client-server architecture.
The client initiates defined operations.
The server provides defined services.
A protocol can use peer-to-peer architecture.
Peer-to-peer does not mean every peer has identical authority.
Roles can still exist.
Voting rights can still differ.
Peer-to-peer topology and governance authority are different.
A protocol can use publish-subscribe architecture.
