Subscribers receive selected messages.
Ordering guarantees must be defined.
Retention guarantees must be defined.
Subscription state must be defined.
A subscriber reconnecting can miss events unless replay or durable subscription is supported.
A protocol can use streaming.
Stream termination should be defined.
Backpressure should be defined.
Partial stream failure should be defined.
A stream being established does not prove every future item will arrive.
Each stream needs identification.
Errors can be per stream.
Connection-level errors can affect every stream.
Use it only when requirements justify it.
Flow control limits how quickly a sender transmits relative to receiver capacity.
Transport protocols such as TCP provide transport-level flow control.
Application-level flow control can still be required.
A receiver can accept network bytes faster than it can process application messages.
Bounded application queues protect memory.
Backpressure should propagate where possible.
Dropping messages should follow protocol semantics.
Priority can determine processing order.
Priority should have bounded semantics.
Low-priority traffic should not be starved indefinitely unless policy permits it.
High-priority traffic should not bypass security validation.
Priority affects scheduling, not validity.
A protocol can support cancellation.
Cancellation requests should identify the operation being cancelled.
