A transaction identifier should associate transaction messages.
A participant should reject operations for unknown or completed transactions according to protocol rules.
Timeout cleanup should not silently violate transaction guarantees.
A protocol can support leases.
A lease grants temporary authority according to defined rules.
Lease duration should be explicit.
Clock assumptions should be explicit.
A stale lease holder should not retain authority beyond protocol rules.
Fencing can prevent stale holders from modifying protected resources.
An epoch distinguishes generations of authority or state.
Epoch values should be monotonically managed according to protocol rules.
Persistent protocol counters must survive restart when correctness depends on them.
Protocol restart behavior matters.
A participant can restart with no session state.
A participant can rejoin a cluster.
A participant can need resynchronization.
A restarted peer can reuse the same stable identity if the identity persists.
A new identity should be treated as new when protocol rules require it.
State synchronization transfers state between participants.
Synchronization should identify the state version or position.
State transfer requires validation.
Checksums can detect corruption.
Hashes can identify exact content.
Signatures can authenticate approved state where the architecture uses them.
The protocol should define which mechanism determines acceptance.
A protocol can contain administrative operations.
Administrative operations should have stronger authorization where required.
Administrative messages should not be enabled merely because a peer is local.
Localhost is a network location.
Localhost is not an identity.
Processes on the same machine can still be unauthorized.
A local protocol endpoint should validate authority according to its threat model.
Protocol security should be layered.
Transport encryption can protect communication confidentiality.
Message validation can protect state.
Replay protection can protect freshness.
Rate limits can protect resources.
Audit logging can preserve evidence.
No one layer replaces all others.
TLS does not define application authorization.
TLS does not validate application message semantics.
A valid TLS connection can carry malicious application messages.
Application protocol validation remains necessary.
Plaintext protocols expose message contents to observers with network visibility.
Whether encryption is required depends on the threat model and environment.
Do not assume private networks are automatically safe.
Do not add cryptography without defining key management.
Security mechanisms without key lifecycle design can create false confidence.
Protocol keys can require generation.
Protocol keys can require storage.
Protocol keys can require distribution.
Protocol keys can require revocation.
Protocol keys can require recovery procedures.
The protocol should not transmit private keys as ordinary messages.
Secrets should not appear in diagnostic messages.
A parser error does not need to expose process memory.
A server error does not need to expose internal paths.
Return enough information for legitimate diagnosis without exposing unnecessary internals.
Protocol denial-of-service resistance begins with cheap rejection.
Validate fixed headers before expensive operations where possible.
Validate network identity before expensive state transfer.
Validate basic syntax before cryptographic work where safe.
Bound work per unauthenticated connection.
Attackers can open many connections.
Connection establishment consumes resources.
Handshake state consumes resources.
Half-open or idle connections consume resources.
Limits should account for legitimate workload.
Protocol resource policy should be measurable.
Observe active connections.
Observe rejected messages.
Observe message sizes.
Observe processing latency.
Observe retry rates.
Observe timeout rates.
Observe version distribution.
Metrics should not become protocol authority.
Metrics describe behavior.
The protocol state determines behavior.
Serialization can be tested independently.
Parsing can be tested independently.
State transitions can be tested independently.
Transport adapters can be tested separately.
The protocol layer should interpret bytes.
The application layer should perform authorized operations.
Exact architecture can vary, but responsibilities should remain clear.
Protocol code should avoid hidden global state.
Explicit connection context improves reasoning.
A connection context can contain negotiated version.
