Running multiple identical implementations can share software defects.
Fault isolation prevents one failure from unnecessarily affecting unrelated components.
Process boundaries can provide fault isolation.
Module boundaries alone do not necessarily provide runtime isolation.
A crash in one in-process module can terminate the host process.
Memory corruption in one native module can corrupt host state.
Stronger isolation generally has greater communication cost.
Choose isolation according to risk.
A sandbox restricts a component's capabilities.
Sandboxing can limit filesystem access.
Sandboxing can limit network access.
It limits potential effects according to the sandbox model.
A component that only reads configuration should not require write authority without reason.
A component that does not use the network should not require network authority without reason.
Authority should follow responsibility.
Trust boundaries identify where data or control crosses between different trust levels.
External input crosses a trust boundary.
Plugin input can cross a trust boundary.
Network input crosses a trust boundary.
Previously stored data should not automatically be considered safe.
Validation belongs at trust boundaries.
Validation should occur before untrusted data affects authoritative state.
Authentication architecture determines how identity is established.
Authorization architecture determines how permissions are enforced.
Key-management architecture determines how cryptographic keys are handled.
Audit architecture determines how security-relevant actions are recorded.
Security should not be added only at the user interface.
A disabled button is not authorization.
Architecture should enforce rules at authoritative boundaries.
Platform independence means common required behavior does not depend on one platform's implementation details.
Platform independence does not mean platform-specific code cannot exist.
The common layer defines required semantics.
A Windows backend can implement those semantics using Windows APIs.
A Linux backend can implement those semantics using Linux or POSIX APIs.
The externally required result should remain equivalent where architecture requires it.
Platform abstraction isolates platform-specific behavior.
A platform interface should expose required capabilities.
The common core should not need to know unnecessary platform details.
Compile-time selection can choose a platform backend.
Runtime selection can choose a platform backend.
The correct mechanism depends on deployment requirements.
Platform detection should not become business logic.
A platform backend is an implementation mechanism.
The application mission remains common.
Portability requires more than successful compilation.
Equivalent source can behave differently when assumptions are platform dependent.
Path syntax can differ.
Filesystem semantics can differ.
Case sensitivity can differ.
Socket behavior can differ.
Thread scheduling can differ.
Clock behavior can differ.
Compiler behavior can differ.
Architecture should isolate or explicitly define these differences.
Do not serialize native structures.
Do not rely on host byte order.
Do not rely on compiler padding.
Do not rely on local timezone.
Do not rely on filesystem enumeration order.
Deterministic systems require deterministic architecture at relevant boundaries.
Determinism means defined inputs produce defined outputs according to system rules.
Nondeterministic implementation details should not affect deterministic outputs.
Thread scheduling should not affect consensus-visible state.
Filesystem ordering should not affect canonical output.
Randomness should be controlled when deterministic output is required.
Time should be an explicit input when time affects deterministic behavior.
Hidden inputs weaken determinism.
Architecture should identify sources of nondeterminism.
Configuration is an input.
Configuration should have defined precedence.
Configuration should have defined validation.
Configuration should have defined defaults.
Configuration errors should fail clearly.
A default should be used only when the architecture defines a default.
Missing required configuration should not be guessed.
Configuration and runtime state are different.
Runtime state describes current operation.
Changing runtime state does not necessarily change configuration.
Do not confuse them.
Feature flags enable or disable behavior through configuration or control state.
Feature flags can support staged rollout.
Feature flags increase state combinations.
Permanent piles of feature flags create hidden architecture.
A feature flag should not silently change security invariants.
A feature flag should not silently create protocol incompatibility.
Feature activation should respect architectural boundaries.
Initialization establishes component state before normal operation.
Initialization order should follow dependencies.
Initialization failure should be explicit.
Startup success and readiness are different.
A process can be running but not ready.
Liveness and readiness are different.
Liveness means the process is operating sufficiently to respond according to the liveness definition.
A healthy process can be temporarily not ready.
Shutdown is part of architecture.
Components can need time to stop safely.
A server can stop accepting new work.
Resources can be released.
Shutdown should be bounded.
A component can be forced to terminate.
Restart is a normal operational event in many systems.
A restart should not corrupt persistent state.
A restart should not duplicate non-idempotent operations without defined handling.
A restart should not depend on stale process memory.
Recovery architecture should define sources of truth.
A source of truth is the authoritative source for defined information.
Derived data can be reconstructed from authoritative data.
Architecture should distinguish authoritative data from derived data.
Duplicating authoritative state creates synchronization requirements.
Avoid multiple sources of truth for the same fact unless the system defines reconciliation.
Data should be validated at appropriate boundaries.
Data transformations should be explicit.
Derived values should retain enough provenance where required.
A component should not silently reinterpret data owned by another component.
Control flow describes how execution responsibility moves.
A controller can invoke a service.
A service can invoke a repository.
An event can trigger a handler.
A scheduler can invoke a job.
A network request can invoke a protocol handler.
Architecture should make major control paths understandable.
Hidden callbacks can complicate reasoning.
Excessive indirection can obscure execution.
Indirection is useful when it decouples meaningful responsibilities.
Indirection is harmful when it only makes code harder to trace.
Direct calls are not architecturally inferior when direct dependency is appropriate.
Simple is a valid architectural property.
A repository pattern abstracts persistence operations.
A repository can separate application behavior from storage details.
A repository is not required for every application.
Adding a repository that merely renames every database operation can add little value.
Abstractions should earn their existence.
A controller coordinates requests according to its responsibility.
A service can contain reusable application behavior.
A service class containing unrelated helper methods is not cohesive merely because it is called a service.
Names do not create architecture.
Utility modules can become dumping grounds.
If unrelated functions accumulate in one utility module, responsibilities should be reconsidered.
Helpers should remain bounded.
A factory creates objects according to defined construction logic.
Factories can hide complex construction.
Factories can select implementations.
Factories are unnecessary when ordinary construction is simple.
A singleton provides one globally accessible instance according to the pattern.
Singletons can create hidden global dependencies.
Global convenience can create architectural coupling.
Immutability reduces possible state transitions.
Immutable objects cannot be modified after construction according to their model.
Immutable data can be easier to share safely.
Not every object should be immutable.
Mutable state is necessary for many systems.
Mutable state should have clear ownership.
Concurrency architecture determines how work executes simultaneously.
Threads can share process memory.
Processes have stronger memory isolation.
Worker pools bound concurrent work.
Architecture should select a concurrency model based on workload.
More threads do not automatically mean more performance.
Shared mutable state requires synchronization.
Concurrency boundaries should align with state ownership where practical.
A worker should not modify state owned exclusively by another worker without a defined coordination mechanism.
Message passing can reduce shared-memory coupling.
Message passing introduces message contracts.
Locks can coordinate shared memory.
Locks introduce blocking and deadlock risks.
Architecture should choose the simplest correct coordination model.
A scheduler decides when work runs.
Scheduling policy can affect latency.
Critical correctness should not depend on one task always running immediately.
Real-time requirements are different from ordinary performance requirements.
A real-time system requires operations to satisfy defined timing constraints.
Fast average execution is not equivalent to real-time guarantees.
Architecture must account for worst-case timing when real-time behavior is required.
Optimization should target demonstrated bottlenecks.
Architectural performance includes communication cost.
Architectural performance includes serialization cost.
Architectural performance includes storage cost.
Architectural performance includes synchronization cost.
Architectural performance includes algorithmic cost.
Architectural performance includes cache behavior.
Premature distribution can harm performance.
Premature abstraction can harm performance.
Premature optimization can harm maintainability.
Correctness comes before unnecessary optimization.
Performance requirements should still influence architecture early when they are genuine constraints.
Vertical scaling adds resources to one system.
Horizontal scaling adds additional systems or workers.
Horizontal scaling usually requires stronger coordination architecture.
Stateless request handlers are easier to scale horizontally.
Stateful services can also scale but require state coordination.
Stateless does not mean the entire application has no state.
It means the component does not depend on local session state that prevents interchangeable handling according to the architecture.
Scaling choices have costs.
Reliability is the ability to perform required behavior consistently under defined conditions.
Retries can improve reliability for transient failures.
Retries can also amplify failure.
Timeouts prevent indefinite waits.
Redundancy can improve availability.
Redundancy can create consistency requirements.
Reliability mechanisms should be matched to failure modes.
Do not add a retry because an operation failed once without understanding whether retry is safe.
Resilience is the ability to continue or recover under failures.
Graceful degradation is one resilience strategy.
Redundancy is another.
Backpressure is another.
Circuit breaking is another.
Persistence is another.
Architecture should define expected failures.
Expected failures should have designed behavior.
Unexpected failures should fail safely and observably.
Observability is an architectural concern.
Logs provide event records.
Metrics provide measurements.
Traces provide operation flow across boundaries.
Health endpoints provide selected operational status.
Audit records preserve defined historical evidence.
These mechanisms have different purposes.
Logs should not be the only state database.
Metrics should not replace audit history.
Audit history should not be rewritten to make current state appear cleaner.
Observability should respect component boundaries.
Distributed operations should have correlation identifiers where useful.
Errors should identify the failed boundary.
"Something failed" is weak evidence.
"Protocol handshake rejected unsupported version" is stronger evidence.
Architecture should make failures diagnosable.
A system that cannot explain its state is difficult to operate safely.
Error handling should follow responsibility.
A low-level component can report a low-level failure.
A higher-level component can translate it into application meaning.
Translation should preserve useful cause information.
Do not expose low-level secrets or internals unnecessarily.
Do not discard evidence needed for diagnosis.
Error messages should distinguish expected rejection from internal failure.
An unavailable dependency is not necessarily malformed input.
Error taxonomy improves recovery.
Return codes are another.
Callbacks are another.
No mechanism is universally superior.
Use the mechanism appropriate to the language and architecture.
Errors should not be silently ignored.
Ignoring an error is a decision.
That decision should be justified.
A fallback is alternative behavior used when preferred behavior is unavailable.
Fallbacks should preserve required correctness.
A fallback that returns incorrect authoritative data is not resilience.
A fallback can provide reduced functionality.
Fallback behavior should be visible.
Silent fallback can hide outages.
Compatibility is an architectural concern.
File formats can outlive code versions.
Network protocols can connect different versions.
Module ABIs can connect separately built components.
Architecture should define compatibility boundaries.
Versioning should occur where compatibility requires it.
Do not version every internal function unnecessarily.
Do version persistent or external contracts when changes can affect independent consumers.
Migration transforms persistent state from one representation to another.
Migrations should be controlled.
Migrations should preserve required data.
Migration failure should have recovery procedures.
A migration should not be confused with ordinary runtime behavior.
Backward-compatible migrations can support rolling upgrades.
Breaking migrations can require coordinated deployment.
A rolling deployment replaces instances gradually.
Different versions can coexist temporarily.
Protocols and storage formats must tolerate that coexistence if rolling deployment is required.
An atomic deployment replaces a unit as one controlled change.
It can increase interruption depending on infrastructure.
Deployment strategy should match availability requirements.
Build documentation should include every required artifact.
Artifact identity should be traceable.
A hash can identify exact artifact bytes.
A version identifies a logical release.
Version and hash serve different purposes.
Deployment should know which artifact is running.
Architecture should not rely on operators guessing.
Configuration deployment should be controlled separately from binary deployment where appropriate.
A binary can be correct while configuration is wrong.
Configuration can be correct while the binary is incompatible.
Architecture exists in operation, not only in source code.
A diagram showing redundant servers is meaningless if both depend on one unprotected database.
A diagram showing modules is meaningless if every module modifies global state directly.
Architecture should describe reality.
Architecture drift occurs when implementation diverges from intended architecture.
Architecture drift can accumulate gradually.
Code review can detect drift.
Dependency analysis can detect drift.
Tests can enforce selected boundaries.
Documentation should be updated when architecture intentionally changes.
Do not preserve an obsolete diagram merely because it is official-looking.
Documentation should describe the current controlled architecture.
A decision record can state the selected approach.
A decision record can state consequences.
A decision record can preserve why a decision was made.
Decision history prevents repeated debates based on forgotten context.
A later decision can supersede an earlier decision.
Superseding history should not erase earlier history.
Architecture evolves.
Evolution should be controlled.
Refactoring changes internal structure while preserving required external behavior.
Refactoring can improve architecture.
Refactoring should not silently change protocol behavior.
Refactoring should not silently change persistent format.
Refactoring should not silently change authorization.
External behavior should remain protected by tests.
A rewrite replaces substantial implementation.
Rewrite decisions should be justified.
Old code being unattractive is not sufficient evidence that a rewrite is necessary.
Incremental replacement can reduce risk.
Legacy code means existing code that must be maintained.
Legacy does not automatically mean bad.
Old software can be stable.
New software can be defective.
Evaluate evidence.
Technical debt represents future cost created by implementation decisions.
Not every simple implementation is technical debt.
Complexity introduced without requirement can itself be technical debt.
Architecture should favor the smallest design that correctly satisfies current known requirements while leaving reasonable paths for change.
Do not build hypothetical future systems without evidence they will be needed.
Extensibility should be intentional.
A plugin boundary can support expected extension.
A stable protocol can support independent implementations.
A configuration mechanism can support known variation.
Excessive genericity can make current behavior harder to understand.
"You might need it someday" is weak justification for complexity.
YAGNI is a software design principle meaning You Aren't Gonna Need It.
It warns against implementing speculative requirements.
YAGNI does not mean ignore foreseeable requirements.
Known requirements should be designed for.
Speculative requirements should not dominate architecture.
KISS is a design principle commonly expanded as Keep It Simple.
A correct simple system is preferable to an unnecessarily complicated equivalent system.
A simpler design is easier to test.
A simpler design often has fewer failure modes.
DRY is commonly expanded as Don't Repeat Yourself.
Two similar pieces of code can represent different responsibilities.
Combining them prematurely can create coupling.
Duplicate knowledge is more dangerous than duplicate syntax.
SOLID is a group of object-oriented design principles.
SOLID can guide selected object-oriented designs.
SOLID is not a universal architecture law.
Non-object-oriented systems can have excellent architecture.
Patterns and principles are tools.
Requirements remain primary.
A design pattern describes a recurring solution structure.
A pattern should be used when its problem exists.
Do not introduce a pattern merely because its name is familiar.
Pattern names can improve communication.
A factory that does nothing beyond calling a constructor may add unnecessary indirection.
An adapter converts one interface into another.
Adapters are useful at boundaries.
A platform adapter can translate common operations to platform APIs.
A protocol adapter can translate between protocol representation and internal representation.
An adapter should not silently change semantics.
A facade can reduce coupling to subsystem details.
A facade should not become a second implementation of the subsystem.
A proxy can provide caching.
A proxy can provide access control.
A proxy should preserve the semantics expected by its interface.
A decorator adds behavior around another component.
Decorators can add logging.
Decorators can add metrics.
Decorators can add caching.
Middleware can authenticate.
Middleware can log.
Middleware can transform requests.
Middleware ordering matters.
One middleware can depend on work performed by earlier middleware.
Hidden middleware ordering dependencies create fragile architecture.
Metrics are a cross-cutting concern.
Authentication can be cross-cutting.
Tracing can be cross-cutting.
Cross-cutting does not mean every component should implement the concern independently.
Shared mechanisms can provide consistency.
Shared mechanisms should not obscure local responsibility.
A framework provides reusable architecture and execution structure.
A library provides functionality called by application code.
The distinction is often described through inversion of control.
Application code commonly calls a library.
The boundary is not always absolute.
Using a framework creates architectural dependency on framework behavior.
Framework upgrades can affect application architecture.
Do not let framework conventions replace understanding of system responsibilities.
The application's requirements remain authoritative.
Domain terminology should be consistent.
One concept should not have many accidental names.
Different concepts should not share one ambiguous name.
Clear terminology improves architecture.
Names establish mental boundaries.
If a component discovers peers, do not call discovery admission.
If a component stores a cache, do not call it authoritative state.
Precise naming is engineering.
Data ownership should follow domain ownership where practical.
One component should not directly modify another component's internal persistence without a defined interface.
Bypassing ownership creates hidden coupling.
Shared databases can create coupling between otherwise separate services.
Two services using the same tables are not fully independent merely because they have separate executables.
Storage boundaries matter.
Changing a schema can break consumers.
Direct database access can bypass application validation.
Architecture should define who may write which data.
Read access and write access can have different boundaries.
Command-query separation distinguishes operations that change state from operations that retrieve state.
A query requests information.
Separating them can clarify behavior.
Not every function must strictly follow command-query separation.
The principle is useful when it makes side effects clear.
A query that secretly modifies authoritative state is surprising.
Side effects should be explicit.
Idempotency is architectural when operations can be retried.
An idempotent operation can be repeated without changing the intended final state beyond the first successful application according to its semantics.
Local initialization can benefit from idempotency.
Deterministic initialization is useful.
Running initialization twice should not corrupt state.
A migration should not accidentally repeat destructive actions.
A module discovery pass should not duplicate module registration.
Lifecycle operations should define repetition behavior.
Resource ownership is architectural.
Memory has ownership.
Sockets have ownership.
Threads have ownership.
Database connections have ownership.
Locks have ownership.
Temporary files have ownership.
Cryptographic keys have ownership.
Every acquired resource should have a defined release path.
A component should not release resources owned by another component without contract.
A host can own a module instance.
The host can request module shutdown.
The module can release its resources.
This is clearer than both independently freeing shared resources.
Lifecycle boundaries reduce ambiguity.
A subsystem is a collection of components providing a larger responsibility.
Subsystem boundaries can reduce cognitive load.
A subsystem should expose a controlled interface.
Consumers should not depend on arbitrary subsystem internals.
A subsystem can contain its own layers.
Architecture can be hierarchical.
A system contains subsystems.
Subsystems contain modules.
Modules contain functions or classes.
Not every level needs formal ceremony.
If a change has one obvious responsible component, boundaries are probably useful.
If every change requires touching unrelated components, coupling can be excessive.
If no one knows where new behavior belongs, responsibilities can be unclear.
If the same rule is implemented independently in many places, authority can be fragmented.
One authoritative implementation of important rules reduces disagreement.
Do not duplicate consensus rules across platform-specific backends.
Do not duplicate protocol semantics independently unless implementations are intentionally separate and tested for equivalence.
Centralize rules when one authority is required.
Decentralize implementation where independent responsibility is useful.
Centralization and decentralization are tools.
Neither is universally superior.
Centralized architecture can simplify authority.
Centralized architecture can create bottlenecks or single points of failure.
Decentralized architecture can increase coordination complexity.
Requirements determine the tradeoff.
Architecture should distinguish control plane from execution plane where useful.
A control-plane failure should not necessarily corrupt already valid execution state.
Execution components should not silently rewrite control policy.
Exact boundaries depend on the system.
Policy and mechanism should be distinguishable.
Mechanism determines how it is accomplished.
One mechanism can implement multiple policies.
One policy can be implemented through different platform mechanisms.
Separating policy from mechanism improves portability.
Platform-specific code should implement mechanism.
Common architecture can define policy.
Policy should not accidentally become dependent on one platform's API.
A scheduler mechanism can differ between operating systems.
The scheduling policy can remain common.
The event semantics can remain common.
A networking implementation can differ.
The protocol semantics can remain common.
Architecture should preserve semantic equivalence.
A boundary should validate assumptions.
When data crosses from external input to internal representation, validate it.
When data crosses from common code to platform code, validate interface requirements.
When data crosses from volatile state to persistent state, validate persistence invariants.
When data crosses from one trust domain to another, validate authority.
Boundaries are natural enforcement points.
Repeated defensive validation can still be appropriate across trust boundaries.
Each authoritative boundary should enforce its own requirements.
Architecture should minimize invalid states.
Types can represent valid distinctions.
State machines can restrict transitions.
Separate functions can represent separate authority.
Configuration schemas can reject invalid configuration.
Database constraints can enforce persistent invariants.
Protocol validation can reject invalid messages.
Multiple layers can enforce different parts of correctness.
Defense in depth can apply to correctness as well as security.
A database constraint can protect an invariant even if application validation contains a defect.
A protocol validator can protect application logic from malformed messages.
An operating-system permission can limit damage if application authorization fails.
Independent controls reduce reliance on one assumption.
Controls should remain understandable.
Redundant controls with contradictory rules create confusion.
One policy should have consistent enforcement.
A module should be testable through its interface.
A parser should be testable without a live network.
A state machine should be testable with explicit transitions.
Testability is often evidence of clear boundaries.
Testability should not force unnatural architecture.
Production requirements remain primary.
Mocks replace dependencies with controlled test implementations.
Mocks can isolate behavior.
Excessive mocking can test assumptions instead of real integration.
Integration tests verify components working together.
End-to-end tests verify complete paths.
Architecture needs tests at multiple boundaries.
Unit tests cannot prove integration.
Integration tests cannot efficiently cover every internal edge case.
End-to-end tests can be expensive.
Use each level for appropriate evidence.
A core can be tested to ensure optional modules are not required.
A module loader can be tested to reject invalid modules.
A platform layer can be tested for equivalent behavior.
A protocol can be tested with shared vectors.
A persistent format can be tested for compatibility.
Static dependency checks can enforce forbidden imports.
Filesystem permissions can enforce runtime ownership.
Architecture can be enforced by tools where useful.
Documentation alone does not enforce behavior.
Tooling alone does not explain intent.
Use both where appropriate.
Architecture should remain understandable without requiring one original developer to explain it.
Code should reveal responsibilities.
Documentation should explain non-obvious constraints.
Interfaces should communicate contracts.
Operational procedures should describe required state.
Institutional knowledge should not exist only in one person's memory.
A bus factor describes risk created when critical knowledge is concentrated in too few people.
Documentation and simple architecture reduce that risk.
Complex architecture increases training cost.
A system that only its creator can safely modify has maintainability risk.
Architecture should support diagnosis.
When an operation fails, identify the responsible boundary.
Protocol failure belongs at protocol handling.
Persistence failure belongs at persistence.
Platform failure belongs at platform implementation.
Clear boundaries make failures easier to classify.
Do not report every failure as a network error.
Preserve actual cause.
A degraded architecture should isolate failure.
If one peer fails, other peers can continue when the protocol permits it.
If a platform backend cannot initialize, dependent capability should report unavailable.
Do not invent fallback behavior that violates architecture.
Architecture should define what cannot happen.
A module cannot bypass core authority.
A view cannot directly rewrite authoritative storage if the architecture forbids it.
A platform backend cannot change consensus semantics.
A cache cannot become source of truth.
Negative constraints are useful architecture.
Architectural authority should be explicit.
Human governance determines project requirements.
Software architecture implements those requirements.
An automated component operates within delegated authority.
A subsystem does not expand its own authority merely because it can technically access more resources.
Technical capability and authorized responsibility are different.
Least authority reduces accidental behavior.
Architecture should not confuse possibility with permission.
Code can technically open a file.
That does not mean the component owns the file.
Code can technically call an endpoint.
That does not mean the operation is authorized.
Code can technically alter global state.
That does not mean the architecture permits it.
Boundaries define responsibility beyond raw technical capability.
Architectural review should ask what problem is being solved.
What are the requirements?
What state exists?
Who owns that state?
Which components are authoritative?
Which dependencies are required?
Which dependencies are optional?
Where are the trust boundaries?
Where are the platform boundaries?
Where are the persistence boundaries?
Where are the network boundaries?
What must survive restart?
What must remain deterministic?
What must remain compatible?
What must remain secure?
What must remain auditable?
What must remain simple?
Only then choose structure.
Requirements come first.
Do not begin with a message broker because asynchronous systems sound scalable.
Do not begin with containers because deployment exists.
Choose mechanisms after identifying requirements.
A plain file can be correct storage.
Memory can be correct storage.
A network service can be correct storage.
JSON can be sufficient for small structured state.
A key-value store can be appropriate for simple keyed state.
An append-only log can be appropriate for historical events.
No storage technology is universally superior.
Use the smallest mechanism that satisfies correctness, scale, durability, concurrency, and operational requirements.
Every third-party dependency introduces compatibility risk.
Every dependency introduces update responsibility.
Every dependency can introduce security exposure.
Every dependency can become unavailable.
Do not reimplement complex solved problems without reason.
Do not import large dependencies for trivial functionality without reason.
External services reduce local implementation.
External services create external dependency.
Local implementation increases ownership.
Local implementation can increase control.
Architecture should recognize operational ownership.
Someone must deploy the system.
Someone must update it.
Someone must observe it.
Someone must recover it.
Someone must rotate credentials.
Someone must handle storage growth.
A design that is elegant in source but impossible to operate is incomplete.
Operational simplicity is architecture.
Installation complexity is architecture.
Upgrade complexity is architecture.
Recovery complexity is architecture.
Architecture includes the system after compilation.
A system's architecture includes code.
A system's architecture includes runtime processes.
A system's architecture includes authority boundaries.
Do not confuse source organization with complete architecture.
Do not confuse directory layout with architecture.
Do not confuse classes with components.
Do not confuse components with processes.
Do not confuse processes with services.
Do not confuse services with machines.
Do not confuse modules with microservices.
Do not confuse interfaces with implementations.
Do not confuse abstraction with complexity.
Do not confuse indirection with decoupling.
Do not confuse duplication with automatically bad design.
Do not confuse reuse with automatically good design.
Do not confuse framework convention with system requirement.
Do not confuse layering with having many folders.
Do not confuse MVC with one framework's MVC implementation.
Do not confuse modularity with dynamic loading.
Do not confuse dynamic loading with qualification.
Do not confuse plugin discovery with plugin authority.
Do not confuse core importance with permission to place every important feature in core.
Do not confuse local with trusted.
Do not confuse reachable with authorized.
Do not confuse cached state with authoritative state.
Do not confuse replicated state with independent authority.
Do not confuse volatile state with durable state.
Do not confuse ready with healthy forever.
Do not confuse retry with resilience.
Do not confuse redundancy with independence.
Do not confuse distribution with scalability.
Do not confuse microservices with modernity.
Do not confuse platform-specific implementation with platform-dependent architecture.
Do not confuse identical implementation with equivalent behavior.
Do not confuse portability with successful compilation.
Do not confuse security controls with architecture being secure.
Do not confuse a diagram with evidence that implementation follows it.
Do not confuse technical capability with architectural authority.
Identify who owns the relevant state.
Identify which layer has authority.
Identify whether it is required or optional.
Identify whether it affects external contracts.
Identify whether it affects protocol behavior.
Place the behavior at the narrowest correct boundary.
When modifying architecture, preserve existing contracts unless change is intentional.
Check module interfaces.
Check protocol interfaces.
Check persistent formats.
Check build artifacts.
Check security boundaries.
Check platform behavior.
Check tests.
Do not let a local change silently become a system-wide semantic change.
Remove unused layers.
Remove dead feature flags.
Remove abandoned interfaces.
Remove unnecessary dependencies.
Combine boundaries that no longer serve a purpose.
Separate responsibilities that have become entangled.
Refactoring can simplify architecture.
Simple architecture is not primitive architecture.
A good architecture makes invalid behavior harder to introduce.
A good architecture makes authority visible.
A good architecture makes dependencies visible.
A good architecture makes failure boundaries visible.
A good architecture makes platform boundaries visible.
A good architecture makes state boundaries visible.
A good architecture makes change understandable.
The architecture should be no larger, no smaller, and no more complicated than the requirements demand.
Performance describes how efficiently a system performs required work under defined conditions.
Performance is not one measurement.
Performance can include latency.
Performance can include throughput.
Performance can include CPU usage.
Performance can include memory usage.
Performance can include storage activity.
Performance can include network activity.
Performance can include concurrency.
Performance can include resource utilization.
Performance can include scalability.
Performance can include responsiveness.
Performance requirements should be defined.
"Low latency" is not precise without a target.
"High throughput" is not precise without a workload.
A performance requirement should identify the operation being measured.
A performance requirement should identify the workload.
A performance requirement should identify relevant resource limits.
A performance requirement should identify an acceptable result.
Observation is stronger than assumption.
A slow-looking function is not necessarily a bottleneck.
A large function is not necessarily slow.
A small function is not necessarily fast.
Measure before optimizing.
Optimization without measurement can waste engineering effort.
Optimization without measurement can make software more complex without improving meaningful performance.
Optimization can introduce defects.
Optimization can increase memory usage.
Optimization can reduce portability.
Optimization can change timing.
Optimization can expose concurrency defects.
Performance changes should therefore be justified by evidence.
Latency is the time required to complete an operation.
Latency can be measured from request start to response completion.
Latency can be measured for one internal operation.
Latency can be measured across an entire end-to-end workflow.
The measurement boundary must be defined.
Network latency is not the same as application latency.
Storage latency is not the same as total request latency.
One operation can contain multiple latency components.
End-to-end latency includes all work inside the measured boundary.
Requests per second is a throughput measurement.
Transactions per second is a throughput measurement.
Messages per second is a throughput measurement.
Operations per second is a throughput measurement.
A system can have high throughput and poor latency.
A system can have low latency and low total throughput.
Batching can improve throughput while increasing latency for individual items.
Performance claims should identify which property improved.
Response time is commonly the elapsed time observed by a requester.
Service time is the time a resource actively spends servicing work according to the measurement model.
An operation can be computationally cheap but wait behind other work.
Queueing matters.
A queue stores work waiting for processing.
A growing queue can increase latency.
An unbounded queue can consume unbounded memory.
Queues should be bounded where resource control matters.
Backpressure limits incoming work when downstream capacity is constrained.
Backpressure can protect latency.
Backpressure can prevent overload from spreading.
Rejecting excess work can be safer than accepting work that cannot be completed within requirements.
CPU capacity is finite.
Storage bandwidth is finite.
