Storage is the preservation and retrieval of data.
Storage can be volatile.
Storage can be persistent.
Volatile storage does not guarantee data survives loss of power or process termination.
Persistent storage is intended to preserve data beyond the lifetime of the process that created it according to the storage system's guarantees.
Persistence is not the same as durability.
Persistence is not the same as availability.
Persistence is not the same as correctness.
Persistence is not the same as integrity.
Persistence is not the same as backup.
Persistence describes data surviving beyond a particular execution boundary according to the storage mechanism.
Durability describes whether acknowledged data survives specified failures according to the storage guarantee.
Integrity describes whether data remains valid and uncorrupted according to defined rules.
Availability describes whether stored data can currently be accessed.
Backup provides a recoverable copy according to backup policy.
Memory is storage.
Files are storage.
Databases are storage.
Object stores are storage.
Block devices are storage.
Queues can contain stored state.
Logs can contain stored state.
Choose storage according to requirements.
State is information representing a system at some point in its operation.
State can be transient.
State can be authoritative.
State can be derived.
State can be cached.
State can be replicated.
Authoritative state is the state designated by system architecture as the source used to determine a particular fact.
Derived state is calculated from other state.
Cached state is a stored copy used to avoid repeated retrieval or computation.
Replicated state is a copy maintained in another location.
Historical state records prior events or values.
One piece of data can have more than one property.
A replicated copy can be authoritative according to a distributed protocol.
A cache should not silently become authoritative unless architecture explicitly assigns it that role.
If data cannot be reconstructed and no other authoritative source exists, it is not merely disposable derived data.
Know who owns state.
State ownership defines which component is responsible for authoritative modification.
Multiple components can read state.
Multiple components should not independently modify authoritative state unless coordination semantics explicitly permit it.
Uncontrolled multiple writers can create conflicting state.
One writer can simplify consistency.
Multiple writers can improve availability or throughput but require coordination.
Architecture determines acceptable ownership.
A file does not define its own authority.
A database table does not define its own authority.
Authority comes from system design.
A copy is not automatically authoritative because it is newer.
A copy is not automatically authoritative because it is local.
A copy is not automatically authoritative because it is available.
A copy is not automatically authoritative because another copy is unavailable.
Storage has boundaries.
Filesystem caches can be another boundary.
Storage-controller caches can be another boundary.
Physical media is another boundary.
Remote storage introduces network boundaries.
A successful write at one boundary does not automatically prove completion at every deeper boundary.
Understand the acknowledgment contract.
A write call can return success after data is accepted by the operating system.
