Buffered writes can remain in memory.
Storage devices can contain volatile write caches.
Databases can buffer operations.
Durability requirements determine which boundary matters.
Do not claim durable persistence without evidence that the required durability boundary was reached.
A flush requests that buffered data move toward a defined persistence boundary according to the API.
Flush semantics depend on the storage system.
A language runtime flush can move data to the operating system.
An operating-system synchronization call can request filesystem or device persistence.
These are not interchangeable.
Know the contract of the storage interface being used.
Closing a file is not universally equivalent to durable synchronization.
A successful file close means the close operation succeeded according to the platform API.
