Permission models can differ.
Do not construct paths through assumptions that are not portable.
Use appropriate path handling.
A relative path is interpreted relative to a defined working or base location.
An absolute path identifies a location from a filesystem root or platform-defined absolute namespace.
Relative paths can become ambiguous when working directory changes.
Critical persistent state should use a known base location.
Do not depend accidentally on the process working directory.
Normalize paths only according to safe platform semantics.
Path normalization can resolve selected redundant components.
A path inside an allowed directory before symbolic-link resolution can resolve elsewhere.
Filesystem security requires more than string prefix checks.
Symbolic links can redirect path resolution.
Junctions and other platform-specific mechanisms can alter resolution.
Do not assume path text uniquely identifies underlying storage.
File identity and path identity are different concepts.
A file can be renamed.
A file can have multiple links.
A path can later refer to a different file.
Permissions control filesystem access according to operating-system rules.
Read permission permits selected reading.
Write permission permits selected modification.
