Directory permissions have directory-specific semantics.
Ownership affects permission evaluation.
Access-control lists can provide additional rules.
Application storage should use least privilege.
A process needing read access does not automatically need write access.
A process needing one writable directory does not automatically need write access to the entire application tree.
Writable code directories increase risk.
Separate code from writable state where practical.
Configuration can be read-only during normal operation.
Runtime data can use dedicated writable locations.
Logs can use dedicated writable locations.
Temporary files can use controlled temporary locations.
Permissions should match actual responsibilities.
