Persistent formats can choose any defined byte order.
The choice matters less than consistency and specification.
Readers should validate lengths before allocation.
A stored length can be malicious.
Do not allocate arbitrary memory solely because a file claims an enormous length.
Bounds protect storage parsers.
Integer overflow can occur when calculating storage sizes.
Validate before multiplication and addition.
A count multiplied by element size can overflow.
An offset plus length can overflow.
Overflow can bypass bounds checks.
Storage parsing is a security boundary when data is untrusted.
Treat persistent input as input.
Do not trust a file merely because your own application created it previously.
It can be truncated.
It can be modified.
It can be from an older version.
It can be restored from backup.
It can be copied from another system.
Validate on load.
Validation should be proportional to risk.
Magic values can identify file type.
Version fields can identify format.
Length fields can identify expected size.
Checksums can detect corruption.
Signatures can authenticate content.
Schemas can validate structure.
Semantic checks can validate relationships.
No one check replaces all others.
Corruption should have defined handling.
If a cache is corrupted, discard and rebuild when safe.
If derived indexes are corrupted, rebuild from authoritative state when safe.
If authoritative state is corrupted, stop unsafe processing and recover from a trusted source according to policy.
Do not silently reset authoritative state to empty.
An empty database is not equivalent to a corrupted database.
A default configuration is not equivalent to missing required configuration.
