Permanent records should remain permanent according to governance.
Archival does not have to mean disappearance.
Archived records can remain queryable.
Storage lifecycle should follow data policy.
Temporary data should have a defined lifetime.
A temporary file can survive a crash.
Startup cleanup can identify stale temporary files.
Cleanup should verify that files are truly temporary.
Filename patterns alone can be dangerous.
A cleanup bug can delete authoritative data.
Validate paths.
Use explicit ownership markers where useful.
A cache stores data that can be obtained elsewhere according to cache design.
Cache entries can expire.
Cache entries can be evicted.
Cache entries can become stale.
Cache correctness depends on invalidation and freshness semantics.
A cache should not be trusted beyond its defined validity.
Cache warming populates cache before demand.
Cache warming improves selected initial performance.
Cache warming should not establish authority.
A cache can be rebuilt from its source.
