Do not call every copied directory a consistent snapshot.
Copying files while they are changing can produce mutually inconsistent state.
Coordinate snapshots when consistency across files matters.
A database dump reads and serializes database contents.
A dump can be logically consistent according to database mechanisms.
Replication copies data between storage locations.
Asynchronous replication allows acknowledgment before replicas necessarily receive the change.
Asynchronous replication can improve availability and latency.
Asynchronous replication permits lag and selected data-loss windows.
Choose according to requirements.
Replication lag is the delay between authoritative change and replica application.
A lagging replica can return stale data.
Conflict resolution must be defined.
Do not silently choose whichever value arrived last unless last-write-wins is the explicit policy.
Distributed storage introduces network partitions.
A replica can be unreachable.
A primary can be unreachable.
Different participants can see different network conditions.
Storage authority should not be transferred casually during uncertainty.
Failover should follow defined protocol.
Split-brain can corrupt distributed state.
Quorum, fencing, leases, consensus, or other coordination can prevent selected split-brain conditions according to architecture.
Do not use distributed consensus where simple single-writer storage is sufficient.
Complexity creates failure modes.
Storage should be as simple as requirements permit.
A relational database can be sufficient.
An append-only log can be sufficient.
A distributed database can be necessary.
Choose the smallest mechanism satisfying requirements.
Do not add a database merely because databases exist.
Do not avoid a database when transactional coordination is genuinely required.
JSON files can be effective for small structured state.
JSON files are easy to inspect.
JSON files can be version controlled when appropriate.
JSON files can be atomically replaced.
