SQLite can provide transactional local storage.
Server databases can provide concurrent multi-client storage.
Flat files can provide simple deterministic storage.
Each has different operational costs.
Storage architecture should reflect scale and concurrency, not fashion.
A database is not automatically more reliable than a file.
A correctly designed atomic file can be extremely reliable for small state.
Reliability depends on guarantees and implementation.
A database is not automatically authoritative.
The application architecture determines authority.
A database can contain cached data.
A file can contain authoritative data.
A remote API can be authoritative for selected external facts.
Identify authority explicitly.
Storage APIs should expose errors.
A function returning data should distinguish valid empty data from failure where the distinction matters.
An empty list can be a valid result.
