Encryption does not replace integrity validation unless the cryptographic mode provides authenticated integrity according to its design.
Encryption keys must be stored and managed.
Encrypting data and storing the key beside it with identical access can provide limited protection against selected threats.
Threat model matters.
Database credentials should not grant unnecessary privileges.
A read-only component should use read-only access where practical.
A writer should have only required write access.
Administrative credentials should not be used for routine application access.
Storage interfaces should validate authorization before modification.
Possession of a path does not imply permission to modify it.
Possession of a database identifier does not imply authority to update it.
Application authorization remains required.
Storage engines enforce storage-level permissions.
Application policy enforces domain-level authority.
Validate authorization before opening an irreversible transaction where practical.
Revalidate state when concurrent changes can invalidate assumptions.
