Requirements come first.
Storage should be boring when possible.
Simple formats are easier to inspect.
Single-writer designs are easier to coordinate.
Atomic replacement is easier to recover than complicated in-place mutation for small files.
Explicit versions are easier to migrate.
Bounded data is easier to operate.
Complex storage should be introduced only when requirements justify it.
