Storage is not merely where bytes are placed.
Storage defines how state survives, how state is identified, how state changes, who may change it, how concurrent changes are controlled, how corruption is detected, and how valid state is recovered after failure.
A reliable storage design knows which state matters.
It knows which copy is authoritative.
It knows what can be rebuilt.
It knows what must never be silently discarded.
It validates what it reads.
It preserves evidence when something goes wrong.
And when the required state cannot be established from evidence, it reports that state as unknown or unavailable rather than inventing one.
Memory management includes obtaining memory.
Memory management includes using memory.
Memory management includes tracking memory ownership.
Memory management includes controlling memory lifetime.
Memory management includes releasing memory when it is no longer required.
Memory management affects correctness.
Memory management affects reliability.
Memory management affects performance.
Memory management affects security.
Memory is finite.
Memory exhaustion can cause allocation failure.
Memory exhaustion can cause severe performance degradation.
Memory usage should be bounded where practical.
Repeated leaks can exhaust available memory.
Long-running processes are particularly affected by persistent leaks.
Memory growth is not automatically a memory leak.
A cache can intentionally retain memory.
A memory pool can intentionally retain memory.
An allocator can retain released memory for later reuse.
A runtime can retain memory without returning it immediately to the operating system.
Determine ownership and reachability before declaring a leak.
Memory lifetime describes how long a memory object remains valid.
Using memory outside its valid lifetime is an error.
Ownership describes responsibility for controlling an object's lifetime.
Ownership should be clear.
A function can own memory.
An object can own memory.
A subsystem can own memory.
A caller can transfer ownership to a callee.
A callee can transfer ownership to a caller.
Shared ownership can exist.
Borrowed access can exist without ownership.
Ownership rules should be explicit.
