A pointer identifies or refers to a memory location according to the programming language and runtime model.
A pointer is not the memory itself.
Multiple pointers can refer to the same memory.
Destroying one pointer variable does not necessarily destroy the memory it referenced.
A null pointer represents no valid object according to the language's null-pointer semantics.
A non-null pointer is not automatically valid.
A non-null pointer can be dangling.
A non-null pointer can point outside an intended object.
A non-null pointer can refer to memory with an incompatible type.
A dangling pointer refers to an object whose lifetime has ended or memory that is otherwise no longer valid for that access.
Dereferencing a dangling pointer is invalid.
Use-after-free can cause incorrect results.
Use-after-free can cause crashes.
Use-after-free behavior can appear to work temporarily.
Apparent success does not make the access valid.
Released memory can retain old bytes until reused.
Those bytes no longer belong to the released object.
Do not rely on them.
A double-free occurs when code attempts to release the same allocation more than once without a valid intervening allocation establishing a new object.
Double-free can corrupt allocator state.
Double-free can create security vulnerabilities.
Setting one pointer to null after freeing memory can prevent accidental reuse through that pointer.
