Aliasing occurs when multiple references or pointers provide access to the same underlying object or memory.
Aliasing can be intentional.
Aliasing complicates lifetime management.
Aliasing can complicate concurrency.
An owner should not release an object while valid borrowers still require it.
Borrowing rules should define how long borrowed access remains valid.
Automatic storage duration commonly provides memory whose lifetime is tied to execution of a block or function scope according to language rules.
Dynamic storage duration commonly provides memory whose lifetime is controlled through allocation and release mechanisms.
Static storage duration commonly provides memory lasting for the program's execution according to language rules.
Exact terminology and semantics depend on the programming language.
Do not treat stack and heap as exact synonyms for language storage duration.
Language semantics determine object lifetime.
The stack commonly stores function call state and selected local objects.
Stack allocation is typically fast.
Stack memory is typically reclaimed automatically when the associated execution frame ends.
Stack capacity is finite.
Large local objects can exhaust stack capacity.
