XML has its own escaping rules.
HTML has its own escaping rules.
Shells have their own quoting rules.
Programming languages have their own string-literal escaping rules.
The correct transformation depends on destination grammar.
Input encoding and output encoding are different concerns.
Validate and decode input according to its actual format.
Encode output according to its destination context.
Security should occur at the boundary where data enters a syntax.
An HTTP request can carry percent-encoded URL components.
It can carry headers represented according to HTTP rules.
It can carry a body containing JSON.
The Base64 can represent binary data.
These are multiple representation layers.
Decode each layer once and in the correct order.
Do not decode layers repeatedly without protocol justification.
Repeated decoding can create security vulnerabilities.
A path traversal sequence can be percent encoded.
