Multiple encodings can create ambiguous input.
Reject unnecessary ambiguity.
A protocol should define whether encoded forms are accepted.
Do not accept every representation merely for convenience.
A canonical input representation reduces parser differences.
UTF-8 has canonical encoding rules.
A Unicode code point has one valid shortest UTF-8 representation.
Historical overlong UTF-8 forms are invalid.
Accepting noncanonical forms can create security inconsistencies between parsers.
Use conforming decoders.
Encoding boundaries can affect buffer sizing.
Hexadecimal output requires twice the binary length before terminators or surrounding syntax.
Check for integer overflow.
Do not allocate using unchecked multiplication or addition.
A maximum decoded size and maximum encoded size can differ.
A 4 MB Base64 string decodes to less binary data than 4 MB.
A 4 MB binary object becomes a larger Base64 string.
Resource limits should specify which representation they measure.
Compression changes size independently of encoding.
Compressed binary data can then be Base64 encoded.
The Base64 text can be larger than the compressed bytes but still smaller than Base64 of the original bytes.
Encoding compressed text before compression can produce different results.
The protocol should define the sequence.
Encryption also operates on bytes.
Text is encoded into bytes before encryption.
Ciphertext is binary.
Ciphertext can then be Base64 or hex encoded for textual transport.
Conceptually:
text -> character encoding -> plaintext bytes -> encryption -> ciphertext bytes -> Base64 -> transport text.
Reversing the operation:
transport text -> Base64 decode -> ciphertext bytes -> decrypt -> plaintext bytes -> character decode -> text.
Each stage has a distinct purpose.
Do not call the Base64 stage encryption.
Do not attempt to decrypt before decoding the ciphertext representation.
Cryptographic keys are bytes.
Displaying a key as hexadecimal or Base64 does not change the underlying key bytes if decoded correctly.
The textual representation must be interpreted using the correct encoding.
A hex key and Base64 key can look different while representing the same bytes.
Representation must be decoded before use when the specification says it is encoded.
Hashes are bytes.
Hash displays are often hexadecimal.
Some APIs return hexadecimal text.
Do not compare raw bytes to ASCII hexadecimal characters.
Know the representation returned by the API.
Identifiers can be encoded.
A binary identifier can be represented in hex.
A binary identifier can be represented in Base64.
The textual representation is not necessarily the underlying identity.
Leading zeros can matter.
For numeric interpretation, 001 and 1 can represent the same number.
Define whether the identifier is numeric or textual.
Do not normalize identifiers without authority.
Case can matter in encoded identifiers.
Hexadecimal can be case-insensitive by definition while application identifiers containing arbitrary letters can be case-sensitive.
Base64 is case-sensitive.
Changing Base64 letter case changes the decoded bytes.
Do not lowercase encoded tokens indiscriminately.
URLs can be case-sensitive in selected components.
Do not apply one case-normalization rule to an entire URL blindly.
File paths have platform-specific encoding and character models.
Unix-like systems commonly treat filenames as byte sequences with restrictions at the kernel filesystem interface, while user environments commonly interpret names through locale or UTF-8 conventions.
Windows commonly exposes filenames through Unicode APIs.
Do not assume every filename can be represented losslessly in every legacy encoding.
Do not assume arbitrary filesystem names are valid UTF-8 on every system.
Filesystem representation and application text representation can differ.
Network protocols define byte representation.
Textual network protocols should define encoding.
Binary network protocols should define field encoding.
Network byte order is an encoding rule for multi-byte numeric fields.
It does not define text encoding.
Text and numeric byte-order rules are separate.
Integers can be encoded in fixed width.
Integers can be encoded in variable length.
Variable-length integer encodings can reduce size for small values.
A variable-length encoding must define canonical forms if multiple byte sequences could otherwise represent the same value.
Reject noncanonical forms where the protocol requires one representation.
Signed integers can use different representations.
Modern systems commonly use two's complement in memory.
A protocol can define signed magnitude.
A protocol can define zigzag encoding.
A protocol can define another mapping.
Do not assume native signed representation is the protocol representation.
Floating-point values can be encoded using IEEE 754 binary formats when a protocol defines them.
Byte order must still be defined.
NaN values can have multiple bit patterns.
If exact canonical representation matters, NaN handling requires rules.
Do not assume numerical equality implies byte equality.
Decimal text is another encoding of numeric values.
The integer value ten can be represented as the ASCII text "10".
That text contains two encoded characters.
It is not the same byte representation as a binary integer value 10.
Converting between textual and binary numeric representation requires parsing or formatting.
Do not reinterpret ASCII digits as binary integer memory.
Leading signs matter.
Whitespace can be permitted or forbidden by the parser.
"10" means ten in decimal.
"10" means sixteen in hexadecimal.
"10" means two in binary.
A numeric representation requires a base.
Prefixes such as 0x can indicate hexadecimal according to selected grammars.
They are not universal.
Base32 is another binary-to-text encoding.
Different Base32 alphabets exist.
Base58 is used by selected systems to avoid visually ambiguous characters.
Base58 variants can use different alphabets.
Base85 and Ascii85 provide other binary-to-text representations.
The name of an encoding is not enough if multiple incompatible variants exist.
Define the alphabet and rules.
Binary-to-text encoding can include checksums in selected application formats.
Do not assume every Base58 string contains a checksum.
Do not assume a checksum provides cryptographic authenticity.
Understand the complete format.
Encoding can be reversible.
Hexadecimal is reversible.
UTF-8 encoding of valid Unicode text is reversible to the original code-point sequence.
Some transformations are lossy.
Lowercasing can be lossy.
Compatibility normalization can remove distinctions.
Replacing unsupported characters is lossy.
Lossy transformations should not be applied to identifiers or authoritative data unless the specification explicitly requires them.
Transliteration converts text from one writing representation toward another, often approximating characters using a different script.
Transliteration can lose information.
Do not use transliteration as a canonical identity transformation without explicit rules.
Escaping is not encryption.
Escaping is not validation.
Escaping is not sanitization in every context.
Escaping should occur for the specific output grammar.
A value correctly escaped for JSON can still be dangerous if later inserted unescaped into HTML.
Each interpretation layer requires correct handling.
Decoding should not automatically imply trust.
Base64-decoding an untrusted payload does not make it trusted.
HTML entity decoding does not make text safe for SQL.
Security properties do not emerge from decoding.
