At the same time, validate the encoded representation itself where malformed encodings are forbidden.
Both layers can matter.
An input pipeline can therefore be:
Receive encoded representation.
Enforce encoded-size limit.
Decode.
Enforce decoded-size limit.
Parse structure.
Validate semantics.
Authorize requested operation.
Encoding detection is inherently uncertain for many legacy encodings.
Some byte sequences are valid under multiple encodings.
Statistical detectors provide guesses.
A guess is not deterministic evidence.
Do not use probabilistic encoding detection where the protocol requires exact interpretation.
Require explicit encoding or use a format with defined encoding.
When best-effort import is acceptable, detection can be used with clear uncertainty handling.
Do not silently convert a guess into authoritative data without policy.
Therefore identifying such content as UTF-8 can be compatible with its bytes.
A decoder can be strict or permissive.
Strict decoding is generally preferable at protocol and security boundaries.
Permissive decoding can be useful for human-facing recovery tools.
Do not let a validation component and execution component use different decoding strictness.
Parser differentials create vulnerabilities.
Reject alternative encodings if the protocol requires canonical form.
Encoding libraries should be used according to their contracts.
A conversion function can stop at invalid input.
A conversion function can report required output size.
A conversion function can include terminators.
Read the API contract.
Do not assume every encoding API behaves like another language's API.
C libraries can use explicit buffers and lengths.
PHP can expose strings as byte sequences with library-specific text handling.
Go strings contain bytes and commonly hold UTF-8 text by convention.
C# strings use .NET's Unicode string representation.
Cross-language systems must not assume native string length means the same thing.
A JavaScript string length commonly reflects UTF-16 code units.
A Go string length returns bytes.
A C# string length counts UTF-16 code units.
Define protocol lengths independently of language-native string length.
If the protocol length is bytes, encode first and count bytes.
If the protocol length is Unicode code points, use a code-point-aware operation.
If the requirement is grapheme clusters, use Unicode segmentation.
Do not use whichever length function happens to be convenient.
Null termination is a string representation convention used by C and related APIs.
A zero byte terminates a conventional C string.
The terminator is not part of the logical text length.
Adding a null terminator changes the byte sequence if the buffer is being hashed, transmitted, or persisted with explicit length.
Do not include terminators in protocol data unless the protocol defines them.
Do not omit terminators when passing data to APIs requiring them.
Length-prefixed strings use an explicit length field.
The length can count code units.
The protocol must define which.
A receiver should validate the length before reading.
A terminator appearing inside length-prefixed binary data does not necessarily end the value.
Representation rules determine boundaries.
Text encodings can contain control characters.
Newline is a control concept represented according to the encoding and text convention.
Line endings differ across systems and formats.
LF is commonly used on Unix-like systems.
CRLF is commonly used by Windows text conventions and selected network protocols.
Some protocols require CRLF regardless of operating system.
Binary-mode I/O preserves bytes.
When exact bytes matter, use binary-safe I/O.
A cryptographic hash of a text file can differ solely because line endings differ.
Canonical text formats should define line endings if exact bytes matter.
Normalization can be useful.
It also changes bytes.
Do not apply line-ending conversion to binary files.
Correctly classify content.
It can normalize Unicode.
It can replace invalid characters.
Therefore opening and saving a file is not always byte-preserving.
Use binary-safe tools when exact byte preservation matters.
Encoding metadata should travel with data where ambiguity would otherwise exist.
A database column can define a character set.
An XML declaration can define encoding.
A file format can mandate UTF-8.
Explicit rules remove guessing.
Database character encoding affects storage and comparison.
A database can convert between client and storage encodings.
Incorrect connection encoding can corrupt text.
Collation defines comparison and ordering rules.
Character encoding defines representation.
They can therefore sort or compare text differently.
Do not use database collation ordering as protocol canonical ordering unless explicitly defined.
A URL is represented as a structured identifier.
Internationalized domain names can involve Unicode and ASCII-compatible encoding mechanisms.
Display representation and network representation can differ.
Do not implement internationalized domain conversion by hand.
Visually deceptive names remain a security consideration.
Email and other Internet protocols have their own encoding rules.
Do not assume every Internet text field accepts arbitrary UTF-8 merely because modern systems often use UTF-8.
Follow the protocol specification.
Legacy encodings still exist.
ISO-8859 family encodings exist.
Windows code pages exist.
Other historical and regional encodings exist.
A robust import system can need explicit conversion support.
New internal systems generally benefit from one defined Unicode encoding.
Reducing the number of accepted encodings reduces ambiguity.
Do not retain legacy encoding complexity without a requirement.
Encoding conversion can fail.
Record the failing stage.
Do not overwrite the only original copy with partially converted output.
Convert into new state.
Then publish.
