Structured object.
Encode JSON text as UTF-8.
Compress the UTF-8 bytes.
Encrypt the compressed bytes.
Encode ciphertext as Base64.
Each layer should be reversible according to defined rules.
Envelope parse.
Base64 decode.
Decrypt.
UTF-8 decode.
JSON parse.
Semantic validation.
Document layered formats.
A magic prefix can identify an encoded container format.
A version can identify transformation rules.
A content type can identify representation.
Metadata can reduce ambiguity.
An attacker changing an encoding declaration can alter interpretation.
Trust boundaries apply to metadata too.
Encoding is often invisible when it works.
That does not make it unimportant.
Many apparent text corruptions are decoding failures.
Many security bugs are wrong-context encoding mistakes.
Reason about the exact representation.
Ask:
What is its current representation?
What encoding produced that representation?
What representation does the next layer expect?
Is the transformation reversible?
Is it canonical?
Is it bounded?
Is it safe for this destination grammar?
Does exact byte identity matter?
Do not confuse code points with bytes.
Do not confuse code points with code units.
Do not confuse code units with grapheme clusters.
Do not confuse grapheme clusters with display width.
Do not confuse UTF-8 with ASCII.
Do not confuse text encoding with serialization.
